const WEUST = {"Mark":[{"c1":1,"v1":2,"c2":1,"v2":2,"label":"Mark 1:2","text":"\"Isaiah\" is used loosely by Mark — the quotation actually blends Malachi 3:1 and Isaiah 40:3, a common practice among Jewish teachers who let the more prominent passage govern the citation. \"It is written\" translates a perfect tense (gegraptai), meaning not just \"was once written\" but \"stands written, and stands written still\" — the record is permanent, on the books. \"I send my messenger\" uses apostellō, the verb behind our word \"apostle\": not a casual errand but a formal commission, an envoy sent with authority to act on the sender's behalf. His job, \"prepare the way,\" draws on the language of advance men who leveled roads ahead of a king's procession. The Baptist's whole ministry was that — getting the road ready for someone greater walking behind him.","translation":"As it stands written in Isaiah the prophet: Behold, I send forth my messenger, on formal commission, before your face, who shall make ready your way."},{"c1":1,"v1":3,"c2":1,"v2":3,"label":"Mark 1:3","text":"\"The voice\" carries no definite article in the Greek — John is \"a voice,\" not \"the voice\"; he points past himself to the greater One coming behind him. \"Crying\" (boáo) is the verb for an emotional, full-throated shout, distinct from a cooler word simply meaning \"to call out\" — the Baptist's preaching came from the heart and struck the heart. \"Prepare\" (hetoimázō) is aorist imperative, plural — a blunt, one-time command issued to the whole nation, with the clipped urgency of a military order and no room for delay. \"Make his paths straight,\" by contrast, shifts to a present imperative — not a single act of welcome but a continuous, habitual readiness. \"Straight\" (euthús) describes the level, well-graded roads built for a king's procession; \"paths\" (tríbolos) is a worn trail, not a highway — the image is modest and personal, pointing to the ordinary business of a heart kept ready rather than one grand gesture. \"Lord\" appears without the article, throwing the weight onto quality rather than bare identity: a road fit for Jehovah himself.","translation":"A voice of one crying: Make ready the road of the Lord; be continually making his paths straight."},{"c1":1,"v1":4,"c2":1,"v2":4,"label":"Mark 1:4","text":"\"There arose\" translates egeneto, the verb used elsewhere for something stepping onto the stage of history — not merely \"John showed up,\" but \"John's appearance marked an epoch.\" \"Unto the remission of sins\" is where most translations mislead. The preposition (eis) doesn't always carry the cause-and-effect sense English \"unto\" implies — elsewhere in the New Testament the same preposition means \"because of.\" John's baptism didn't produce forgiveness; it testified to forgiveness already given. \"Repentance\" (metanoia) is literally a change of mind, not sorrow — the New Testament has a separate word for regret without change (used of Judas).","translation":"John the baptizer stepped onto the scene in the wilderness, proclaiming a baptism of a change of mind, because sins had already been put away."},{"c1":1,"v1":5,"c2":1,"v2":5,"label":"Mark 1:5","text":"\"There went out\" renders an imperfect tense (ekporeuomai) — not a single mass movement but a steady, continuous stream, people arriving one after another over time. The preposition \"unto\" (pros) implies more than direction; it pictures people coming face to face with John, deliberately, each transacting personal business with the God he represented. \"Baptized in the Jordan\" uses en, not eis — \"in,\" not \"into\" — picturing submersion in the river itself. \"Confessing\" is a present participle running alongside the main verb \"baptized,\" meaning the confessing happened at the same moment as the baptizing — proof the repentance had already taken place before the rite, and baptism simply announced it publicly. The word for \"confess\" (homologeō) literally means \"to say the same thing\" — to agree with God's verdict on one's sin, not merely admit a fact about oneself.","translation":"And there kept going out to him, to meet him face to face, all the country of Judea, and all they of Jerusalem, and were being baptized by him in the river Jordan, agreeing with God about their sins even as they were baptized."},{"c1":1,"v1":6,"c2":1,"v2":6,"label":"Mark 1:6","text":"\"Clothed\" is a perfect participle — this was John's regular, habitual dress, not a costume assumed for effect. And \"hair\" is plural in the Greek: the garment was a coarse cloth woven from camel hairs, not a camel's hide draped over his shoulders. His diet of locusts and wild honey reflects the ordinary fare of a wilderness dweller living off what the desert supplied — honey gathered from wild bee colonies nesting in the limestone cliffs of Judea, a food source desert hunters in that region still relied on.","translation":"Now John was habitually clothed in a garment woven of camel's hairs, with a leather belt about his waist; and he ate locusts and wild honey."},{"c1":1,"v1":7,"c2":1,"v2":7,"label":"Mark 1:7","text":"\"There cometh one mightier than I\" carries the definite article in Greek — not merely \"someone,\" but \"the One,\" a specific, expected figure. The sandal-thong detail is deliberate: untying a guest's sandals was the lowest task assigned to a household slave, and John places himself beneath even that — he won't claim the dignity of a servant in the presence of the One coming. That same self-effacement shows up later in his own words, \"He must increase, but I must decrease\" (John 3:30): \"must\" translates dei, a term for what is necessary by the very nature of the situation. The forerunner recedes as the King advances — not so much an act of humility as the built-in shape of the relationship between herald and sovereign.","translation":"And he kept proclaiming, saying, There comes after me the One who is mightier than I, the thong of whose sandals I am not fit to stoop down and untie."},{"c1":1,"v1":8,"c2":1,"v2":8,"label":"Mark 1:8","text":"John frames the contrast with emphatic pronouns — \"as for myself\" (egō) against \"he himself\" (autos) — sharpening the difference between his own baptism and the Messiah's. Both baptisms use the same preposition en (\"in\"), but the grammar shifts: John's is locative of place — a physical location, the Jordan River — while the Messiah's is locative of sphere, one idea confined within another rather than a location. John didn't apply water to people the way Jesus applies the Spirit to people; the parallel is baptism as immersion into a new realm, not substance poured on. What John announced here found its fulfillment at Pentecost, when the Spirit placed believers into the body of Christ.","translation":"As for myself, I baptized you in water; but he himself shall baptize you in the Holy Spirit."},{"c1":1,"v1":9,"c2":1,"v2":9,"label":"Mark 1:9","text":"\"In Jordan\" translates eis — \"into,\" a preposition of motion — while the next verse uses ek, \"out from within.\" The picture is immersion: Jesus went down into the river and came back up out of it. He had no sin of his own to confess, yet he took his place among the repentant of Israel rather than standing apart from them — identifying with the people he had come to save before he ever began saving them.","translation":"And it came to pass in those days that Jesus came from Nazareth of Galilee, and was baptized by John into the Jordan."},{"c1":1,"v1":10,"c2":1,"v2":10,"label":"Mark 1:10","text":"\"Opened\" translates schízō — not a door swinging wide but heaven being torn or rent apart, a violent, decisive image. And the Spirit descends \"into\" him — eis, not the epi (\"upon\") one might expect — language suggesting the Spirit taking up residence within the Messiah rather than simply alighting on him for a moment. This was the anointing that equipped him for his threefold work as prophet, priest, and king.","translation":"And immediately, coming up out of the water, he saw the heavens rent asunder, and the Spirit, as a dove, descending into him."},{"c1":1,"v1":11,"c2":1,"v2":11,"label":"Mark 1:11","text":"\"From heaven\" uses ek, not apo — \"out from within,\" not merely \"from the edge of.\" The voice comes from inside heaven itself, a place with real boundaries, capable of being opened and shut. \"Thou art my beloved Son\" places heavy emphasis on the pronoun \"thou\" (sú) — as for you, in contrast to everyone else. Elsewhere Jesus calls God his own Father using a word for what belongs to someone privately and uniquely, not shared sonship but singular sonship. \"Beloved\" is agapē, love called out by the sheer worth of what is loved — the Father's delight in the Son. That delight is stated in the aorist tense, gathering all of time into one unbroken whole: a pleasure with no beginning and no end, not a reaction to this one moment.","translation":"And there came a voice out from within heaven: Thou art my Son, the beloved, in whom I was well pleased."},{"c1":1,"v1":12,"c2":1,"v2":12,"label":"Mark 1:12","text":"\"Driveth him\" is ekbállō — a forceful word, \"to throw out from within, to drive out,\" the same verb Mark uses elsewhere for casting out demons. Matthew and Luke use gentler terms (\"led up,\" \"led\"), but Mark's choice pictures something closer to compulsion than a leisurely walk — the Spirit thrusting Jesus into the wilderness under the weight of an intense, singular purpose, not reluctant obedience.","translation":"And immediately the Spirit throws him forcibly out into the wilderness."},{"c1":1,"v1":13,"c2":1,"v2":13,"label":"Mark 1:13","text":"\"Tempted\" is peirazomai, a word whose root sense is \"to test, to probe for what's inside\" — used first of examining someone to see what strength or weakness is really there, and only secondarily of soliciting someone toward evil. Both senses are active here: Satan is testing what stands ready in the Last Adam, and at the same time trying to talk him into sin. \"Of Satan\" uses hupo, marking personal agency — this was a real adversary, not an abstract force. The name \"Satan\" is a Hebrew word for adversary; \"devil\" (diabolos) comes from a verb meaning to slander or maliciously misrepresent — the accuser's business is distortion. \"The angels ministered\" — with the definite article, specific angels assigned to the task — uses an imperfect tense: not a single visit but continuous attendance through the whole forty days.\n\nThe proclamation that follows (\"the time is fulfilled\") uses a perfect passive verb — not simply \"the time has arrived\" but \"the time has arrived, and that arrival still stands\": the moment has come and remains epochal. \"Time\" here is kairos, a marked, appointed moment, not the ordinary ticking of chronos. And \"gospel\" (euaggelion) is simply \"good news\" — news that originates with God, not merely news about him.","translation":"And he was in the wilderness forty days, being put to the test by Satan; and he was with the wild beasts, and the angels were continually ministering to him.\n\nThe time stands fulfilled, and remains so, and the kingdom of God has drawn near: keep on changing your minds, and keep on believing in the good news."},{"c1":1,"v1":16,"c2":1,"v2":16,"label":"Mark 1:16","text":"\"By the sea\" is para, \"alongside,\" used twice in the verse — a deliberately close, physical picture of Jesus walking right along the water's edge where the fishermen worked. \"Casting a net\" (amphibállō) combines the verb \"to throw\" with a word for \"on both sides\" — the practiced, back-and-forth motion of a net cast first one way, then another.","translation":"And passing alongside the Sea of Galilee, he saw Simon and Andrew his brother casting a net on both sides, back and forth, in the sea; for they were fishermen."},{"c1":1,"v1":17,"c2":1,"v2":17,"label":"Mark 1:17","text":"\"Come\" (deute) is the same summons Jesus later uses to call Lazarus out of the tomb — an urgent, personal \"come here.\" Paired with \"after,\" it carries the sense of joining someone's company, not just walking behind them. \"I will make you to become fishers of men\" is telling in its phrasing — \"make... become\" points to a gradual process, not an instant transformation. Discipleship, from the first invitation, was framed as something that would take time.","translation":"And Jesus said to them, Come here, join yourselves to me, and I will make you to become, by degrees, fishers of men."},{"c1":1,"v1":18,"c2":1,"v2":18,"label":"Mark 1:18","text":"\"Forsook\" (aphiēmi) carries a prefix implying separation — not simply setting the nets aside for the day but breaking away from that whole way of life — and the aorist tense marks it as a decisive, complete, one-time break, not a tentative pause. \"Followed\" (akoloutheō) literally means to walk the same road as someone, and it's used here with a case ending implying \"following with\" rather than \"following behind\": companionship, shared travel, not trailing at a distance.","translation":"And straightway they broke away from their nets, once for all, and walked the same road with him."},{"c1":1,"v1":19,"c2":1,"v2":19,"label":"Mark 1:19","text":"\"The ship\" is ploion, the substantial fishing vessel, not the small tender boat sometimes towed behind it. \"Mending\" (katartízō) means restoring what's torn so it's ready for use again — ordinary maintenance, not repair after some disaster. And since a boat of that size would typically be moored some distance offshore, Jesus's call to James and John was likely shouted — the word for \"called\" carries that sense of calling out loudly — across open water.","translation":"And going on a little farther, he saw James the son of Zebedee and John his brother, who also were in their boat, restoring their nets to order, and he called out to them at once, loudly, across the water."},{"c1":1,"v1":20,"c2":1,"v2":20,"label":"Mark 1:20","text":"\"Went after him\" is apēlthon, built on the same separating prefix as the previous verse's \"forsook\" — the word itself carries the idea of leaving one thing behind in the very act of going toward another.","translation":"And immediately he called them; and they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired servants, and went off, away from that life, after him."},{"c1":1,"v1":21,"c2":1,"v2":21,"label":"Mark 1:21","text":"\"Synagogue\" is built from a word for \"going\" plus \"with\" — literally, the place people gather by coming together. \"Taught\" uses an inchoative imperfect, a tense that marks the beginning of an ongoing action: the moment Jesus entered, he set into extended teaching, not a brief remark. Mark uses both kērussō (\"to proclaim,\" the herald's public announcement) and didaskō (\"to teach,\" patient instruction) of Jesus elsewhere — he suited his method to the moment, sometimes announcing, sometimes explaining.","translation":"And they go into Capernaum; and immediately on the Sabbath day he entered into the synagogue, the gathering place, and set at once into teaching, and kept on teaching."},{"c1":1,"v1":22,"c2":1,"v2":22,"label":"Mark 1:22","text":"\"Astonished\" (ekplēssō) is a violent word — to be struck out of one's composure, shocked to the edge of panic — and it's passive: something outside the crowd hit them and produced this reaction. The verb \"he taught\" is periphrastic, stretching the action out: \"he was teaching,\" ongoing, not a single remark. \"Authority\" (exousia) is built on a root meaning \"to be out of\" — properly, delegated authority, power lent by someone else. The striking thing Mark records is that the crowd sensed Jesus speaking with authority that was his own, not borrowed the way the scribes borrowed the rulings of earlier rabbis.\n\nWhat happened next confirmed it. Mark's phrase for the possessed man, \"with an unclean spirit,\" is literally \"in a spirit, an unclean one\" — describing someone living within the grip and control of the presence inhabiting him. \"He cried out\" is a cry wrenched up from deep in the throat — visceral, not measured speech.","translation":"And they were struck clean out of their composure at his teaching: for he was continually teaching them as one having authority of his own, and not as the scribes, who taught on authority borrowed from others.\n\nAnd there was in their synagogue a man in a spirit, an unclean one; and he cried out, a cry wrenched up from deep in the throat."},{"c1":1,"v1":25,"c2":1,"v2":25,"label":"Mark 1:25","text":"Greek has two words for \"rebuke\": one describes a correction that actually brings the offender to admit wrongdoing, the other (epitimaō, used here) simply silences without producing any acknowledgment of sin — fitting, since demons never repent. \"Hold thy peace\" is far blunter in the original: the verb means literally \"to muzzle,\" the same word used of muzzling an ox — something closer to \"shut your mouth\" than the polished English suggests. Both verbs in the command are aorist imperative — sharp, immediate orders meant to be obeyed on the spot, with no room for delay.","translation":"And Jesus rebuked him sharply, saying, Be muzzled, shut your mouth, and come out of him, at once."},{"c1":1,"v1":26,"c2":1,"v2":26,"label":"Mark 1:26","text":"\"Had torn him\" (sparassō) is a medical term for violent convulsion — the demon's parting shot, a fit of vindictiveness on the way out rather than any lingering struggle for control.","translation":"And the unclean spirit, convulsing him violently and crying with a loud voice, came out of him."},{"c1":1,"v1":27,"c2":1,"v2":27,"label":"Mark 1:27","text":"Mark reaches for a second, distinct word for amazement here — thamébeō, blending astonishment with real fear, alongside the ekplēssō he used back in verse 22 — the crowd's reaction was deepening. \"They questioned among themselves\" (\"to seek together\") is present tense, picturing an animated, ongoing debate rather than a passing murmur. Their question about \"new\" doctrine uses kainos, not neos — Greek distinguishes newness in time (neos) from newness in kind or quality (kainos): this wasn't simply teaching nobody had heard before, but teaching of an entirely different character than what they were used to. \"He commands\" (epitássō) is military language, the term for drawing up troops in formation — the crowd's real shock wasn't the exorcism itself but that unclean spirits fell into line under his command like soldiers under orders.","translation":"And they were all struck with astonishment and dread, so that they kept on debating together among themselves, saying, What is this? A teaching, new in kind! For with authority he gives command even to the unclean spirits, and they fall into line under him."},{"c1":1,"v1":28,"c2":1,"v2":28,"label":"Mark 1:28","text":"\"Fame\" (akoē) is simply \"the thing heard\" — a report, the same word elsewhere translated \"rumor.\" Word of him spread the ordinary way news spreads, by mouth, and it simply moved fast.","translation":"And the report of him went out immediately everywhere into all the surrounding region of Galilee."},{"c1":1,"v1":29,"c2":1,"v2":29,"label":"Mark 1:29","text":"Mark's brief aside — that Peter was married, his mother-in-law living in his household, and that Jesus made this home his base in Capernaum — sets the domestic scene for the healing that follows. It's a small window into how ordinary and rooted in family life the disciples' world remained even after they left their nets.","translation":"And immediately, coming out of the synagogue, they went into the house of Simon and Andrew, with James and John."},{"c1":1,"v1":30,"c2":1,"v2":30,"label":"Mark 1:30","text":"\"Lay sick\" (katakeimai) is colloquial for being laid up, bedridden — and the imperfect tense means she'd been down for some time already, not freshly taken ill. \"Of a fever\" uses a present participle built on the Greek word for fire — she was, quite literally, burning. Luke, the physician among the gospel writers, adds his own diagnostic touch, describing Jesus as standing over her the way a doctor examines a patient.","translation":"Now Simon's mother-in-law was lying there, having been sick of a fever for some time, continually burning; and at once they tell him about her."},{"c1":1,"v1":31,"c2":1,"v2":31,"label":"Mark 1:31","text":"\"He came\" (proselyōn) carries a prefix meaning \"facing\" — Jesus approached and stood facing her, the same posture Luke describes as a physician bending over a patient. \"Took her by the hand and lifted her up\" reads in the Greek order as \"he lifted her up, having taken her hand\" — the grasp preceding and enabling the lift. \"She ministered unto them\" is imperfect, picturing ongoing service rather than one clean gesture — there was a meal to prepare, and she set about doing it.\n\nThat evening, once the sun had \"gone under\" (the same verb used for the sun sinking into the sea) and the Sabbath had ended, people carried their sick to the door in a steady stream — \"brought\" is imperfect, continuous traffic rather than a single crowd arriving at once. \"Gathered together\" is a perfect participle: the crowd had assembled and settled in, camped at the door, waiting until their business was finished.","translation":"And he came and stood facing her, and, having taken hold of her hand, lifted her up; and the fever left her, and she set about serving them, continually.\n\nAnd at evening, when the sun had gone down under, they kept bringing to him all who were sick and those under the power of demons, in a steady stream. And the whole city was gathered together, and stood camped, at the door."},{"c1":1,"v1":34,"c2":1,"v2":34,"label":"Mark 1:34","text":"\"Divers diseases\" translates poikilos, \"variegated\" — every different kind. \"Suffered [them] not to speak\" is imperfect, describing an ongoing, repeated refusal rather than one command — the demons kept trying, and Jesus kept silencing them, precisely because they recognized who he was and he had no use for that kind of testimony.","translation":"And he healed many who were sick with diseases of every different kind, and cast out many demons; and he kept on not permitting the demons to speak, because they kept on knowing him."},{"c1":1,"v1":35,"c2":1,"v2":35,"label":"Mark 1:35","text":"\"In the morning\" marks the last watch of the night, roughly three to six a.m., and \"a great while before day\" narrows it further — still dark, early in that watch. \"Prayed\" (proseuchomai) adds a prefix meaning \"toward\" to the ordinary word for prayer, sharpening it into consciously directed address to God rather than vague devotion — and the imperfect tense pictures him praying on through those early hours, not offering a brief word and rising.","translation":"And rising up very early, while it was yet dark, in the last watch of the night, he went out and departed into a desert place, and there he kept on praying, directing himself toward the Father."},{"c1":1,"v1":36,"c2":1,"v2":36,"label":"Mark 1:36","text":"\"Followed after him\" is katadiōkō, a hunting term — the prefix adds the sense of pursuit carried through to its conclusion. Peter and the others didn't casually go looking for Jesus; they tracked him down.","translation":"And Simon and those who were with him hunted him down, tracking him to the end."},{"c1":1,"v1":37,"c2":1,"v2":37,"label":"Mark 1:37","text":"\"All men seek for thee\" uses a present tense verb — everyone is seeking, right now, an ongoing search rather than a single inquiry. \"All\" is the disciples' own hyperbole, but it captures something real: demand for him had already outrun the town.","translation":"And when they found him, they said to him, Everyone is continually searching for you, right now."},{"c1":1,"v1":38,"c2":1,"v2":38,"label":"Mark 1:38","text":"\"Let us go\" (agōmen) is a present hortatory subjunctive — \"let us keep going,\" a sustained journey rather than one trip. \"Next towns\" means unwalled country towns, modest places, not larger walled cities. \"I came forth\" points back to why he left Capernaum in the first place — the purpose was never to settle in one town but to keep moving outward.","translation":"And he says to them, Let us keep going on into the neighboring country towns, that I may keep proclaiming there also; for it was for this purpose I came forth."},{"c1":1,"v1":39,"c2":1,"v2":39,"label":"Mark 1:39","text":"\"He came\" opens the verse in the Greek text but goes untranslated in older English versions — a small reminder that Mark tracks Jesus's continuous movement from town to town, carrying forward the resolve just voiced in the previous verse.","translation":"And he kept coming, preaching in their synagogues throughout all Galilee, and casting out the demons."},{"c1":1,"v1":40,"c2":1,"v2":40,"label":"Mark 1:40","text":"\"There came\" is a historic present, lending a past event the immediacy of something happening right now before the reader. \"To him\" again pictures a face-to-face confrontation, not a passing encounter. \"Beseeching\" (parakaleō) is an urgent plea — \"please, I beg you.\" And Greek has two words for \"willing\": one rooted in reason, the other in feeling. The leper uses the emotional one — he isn't appealing to Jesus's logic but to his heart.","translation":"And there comes to him a leper, face to face, pleading with him, and kneeling down to him, and saying to him, If you are willing, from your heart, you are able to make me clean."},{"c1":1,"v1":41,"c2":1,"v2":41,"label":"Mark 1:41","text":"\"Moved with compassion\" (splagchnizomai) is a gut-level word for pity, compassion felt in the body, not merely decided upon — Mark's eye for Jesus's inner life shows here. The sequence in the Greek matters: the decision to heal and the healing itself both preceded the touch and the words — Jesus touched the leper not in order to cleanse him, but to demonstrate to onlookers that he already had, since Jewish law forbade touching a leper and Jesus, who kept the law, would not have touched him while he remained unclean. \"Be thou clean\" is aorist passive imperative — a command for something to happen immediately and completely, not by degrees.","translation":"And, moved with compassion in the depths of his being, he stretched forth his hand and touched him, and says to him, I am willing; be made clean, at once and completely."},{"c1":1,"v1":42,"c2":1,"v2":42,"label":"Mark 1:42","text":"\"The leprosy departed\" carries the same separating prefix seen elsewhere in Mark — a complete, clean break between the man and his disease, nothing left behind. Jesus's charge that follows is unusually forceful — the verb originally described the snort of a horse, and in this context conveys stern, forceful insistence rather than literal anger. The urgency makes sense: an uncontrolled report before the priest had officially certified the cure risked provoking hostile scrutiny before the healing could even be verified through the proper channels — so Jesus pushed him out and toward the one authority who could make the cleansing official.","translation":"And immediately the leprosy went off from him, completely, and he was made clean."},{"c1":1,"v1":45,"c2":1,"v2":45,"label":"Mark 1:45","text":"\"To publish\" and \"to blaze abroad\" are both continuous in force — the man didn't make one announcement but kept proclaiming, kept spreading the story, until Jesus could no longer enter a town openly. \"Came\" is imperfect too: people kept arriving, from everywhere, in an unbroken stream.","translation":"But he went out and began to keep proclaiming it freely, and to keep spreading the matter abroad, so that Jesus could no longer openly enter into a city, but was without, in desert places; and they kept coming to him from every quarter, in an unbroken stream."},{"c1":2,"v1":2,"c2":2,"v2":2,"label":"Mark 2:2","text":"The house could not hold the crowd, and Mark says there was no longer room even at the door (using a construction, hōste, that means \"so much so that\") — the picture is of the crowd overflowing the threshold itself. For \"preached\" Mark chooses laleō rather than kērussō, the word for a formal, authoritative public proclamation. Laleō is the everyday word for conversational speech — used elsewhere of birds chattering and children prattling — and its use here suggests Jesus talking with this crowd rather than orating at them, an intimate, unhurried address rather than a formal sermon. The verb is imperfect: not a single burst of speech but an ongoing conversation, He kept on talking to them.","translation":"And many were gathered together, so that there was no longer room, no, not even near the door; and he kept on talking with them, ordinary and unhurried, the word."},{"c1":2,"v1":3,"c2":2,"v2":3,"label":"Mark 2:3","text":"Mark switches into the present tense to narrate this past event — \"they come\" rather than \"they came\" — the same device we use telling a story today for dramatic immediacy: you can watch it happening. The word behind \"sick of the palsy,\" paralutikos, is built from a root meaning \"to loosen\" plus \"alongside\" — literally a slackening of the nerves along one side of the body, which is exactly our English \"paralytic.\" \"Borne of four\" uses a participle from airō, \"to lift up and carry what has been lifted\" — the man is described as someone raised up and held aloft by the four men rather than merely accompanied by them, emphasizing his complete helplessness and their labor.","translation":"And they come, bringing to him a paralyzed man, lifted up and carried by four men."},{"c1":2,"v1":4,"c2":2,"v2":4,"label":"Mark 2:4","text":"\"They uncovered the roof\" translates a cognate construction in Greek — literally \"they unroofed the roof,\" a rare verb that occurs nowhere else in the New Testament. This wasn't shingles lifted off; a flat oriental roof of that era was packed mortar, tar, ash, and sand over cross-laid timbers or stone slabs, so the verb for \"broken up\" means to dig or scoop out — real excavation, not a quick patch removed. \"Let down\" pictures ropes lowering the pallet from that opening to the floor below, and the word for the paralytic's condition as he \"lay\" there is the same verb used of someone bedridden — he was dead weight, wholly dependent on the four men's effort to get him into the room at all.","translation":"And when they could not get near him because of the crowd, they unroofed the roof where he was; and having dug it out, they let down the pallet on which the paralyzed man lay, dead weight."},{"c1":2,"v1":5,"c2":2,"v2":5,"label":"Mark 2:5","text":"\"Their faith\" is significant — the pronoun points back to the four men and whoever else tore open that roof, not necessarily to the paralytic himself. Their determined, dirty, expensive labor was the outward proof of an inward trust; Jesus reads the effort as the evidence. He addresses the man as \"child\" (teknon) rather than the more formal \"son\" (huios) — a warm, familiar term of address used even toward grown men in everyday Greek of the period, not baby talk but affection. \"Be forgiven\" is a present-tense verb stating a settled fact, better rendered \"your sins are put away\" — not a wish or a future promise but a pronouncement of something already accomplished. The verb itself, aphiēmi, has the root sense of releasing or sending off; it's the word used elsewhere for cancelling a debt outright — an obligation simply let go, no longer held against the debtor.\n\nThe scribes' silent objection is described with dialogizomai, \"to weigh and turn over in the mind\" — a private, wordless calculation, though evidently visible enough on their faces that Jesus reads it instantly. Their charge, \"he is blaspheming,\" uses a verb meaning to speak in a way that injures or slanders — in their theology, entirely correct reasoning, since only God can cancel sin. Their logic was sound; what they'd missed was who stood in front of them.","translation":"And Jesus, seeing their faith, the faith of those who carried him, says to the paralyzed man, Child, your sins are, here and now, put away, cancelled like a debt released.\n\nBut there were certain of the scribes sitting there, weighing it over in their hearts, silently: Why does this man speak thus? He injures God with slanderous speech: who is able to put away sins but God alone?"},{"c1":2,"v1":8,"c2":2,"v2":8,"label":"Mark 2:8","text":"\"Perceived\" translates a compound built on the verb for experiential knowing, sharpened by a prefix that adds the sense of \"fully\" or \"thoroughly\" — Jesus doesn't merely sense something is wrong, He is instantly and completely aware of exactly what the scribes are thinking. \"In his spirit\" points to the inner faculty of thought, will, and feeling — the seat of reasoning itself — so the picture is of Jesus reading their unspoken deliberation directly, without anyone having said a word.","translation":"And immediately Jesus, knowing in his spirit, fully and at once, that they were reasoning thus within themselves, says to them, Why are you reasoning these things in your hearts?"},{"c1":2,"v1":9,"c2":2,"v2":9,"label":"Mark 2:9","text":"The command to the paralytic stacks up three imperatives with different force. \"Arise\" is present tense, \"be arising\" — get up and keep getting up, a process. \"Take up your bed\" is aorist, snapped out with military curtness — a single decisive action, done at once. \"Walk\" is present again — start walking and keep walking, not a single test step but a standing ability restored for good.\n\nJesus explains the healing was proof of \"authority\" — exousia, not the word for raw power (dunamis) but for delegated right to act, the authority a person has when someone else has commissioned them to act in his name. The Son of Man exercises on earth an authority that belongs properly to God the Father who sent Him — which is exactly the scribes' own logic turned back on them: if only God forgives sins, and this man visibly holds that authority, their objection answers itself. \"Go your way\" uses a verb for a decisive parting — the word used of someone ending their attendance on another and departing for good — hinting that Jesus wanted the healed man to slip quietly home rather than linger and draw a crowd that would only inflame the opposition further.","translation":"Which is easier, to say to the paralyzed man, Your sins are put away; or to say, Keep on arising, take up your pallet at once, and keep on walking? But that you may know that the Son of Man has authority, delegated to him, on earth to put away sins, he says to the paralyzed man, I say to you, keep on arising, take up your pallet, at once, and go off to your own home, for good."},{"c1":2,"v1":12,"c2":2,"v2":12,"label":"Mark 2:12","text":"\"They were amazed\" uses the verb behind our English word \"ecstasy\" — literally \"to stand outside oneself.\" It describes a state so overtaken by wonder that ordinary awareness shuts down; for a moment the crowd's whole attention is swallowed by what they've just watched, as though they'd stepped outside their own routine perception of the world. That's the intensity Mark is reaching for — not polite surprise but people momentarily beside themselves.","translation":"And immediately he arose, took up the pallet, and went out before them all, so that they were all put outside of themselves, standing beside themselves in wonder, and glorified God, saying, We never saw it in this fashion."},{"c1":2,"v1":13,"c2":2,"v2":13,"label":"Mark 2:13","text":"\"By the seaside\" uses a preposition meaning \"alongside,\" suggesting Jesus wasn't just heading to the shore but walking its length — a stretch of quiet, moving water and open air that a fully human Messiah, worn from constant demand, would have found restorative. Both verbs describing the crowd and His teaching are imperfect: the people kept coming, and He kept right on teaching them — an ongoing scene, not a single event.","translation":"And he went out again alongside the sea, walking its length; and all the crowd kept coming to him, and he kept on teaching them."},{"c1":2,"v1":14,"c2":2,"v2":14,"label":"Mark 2:14","text":"Levi was seated at the customs post on the Capernaum road, the crossing point for traffic and shipping on the Sea of Galilee — a job that made him wealthy and despised in equal measure, a Jew collecting Roman tax from fellow Jews. \"Follow\" (akoloutheō) is built on the idea of walking the same road as another, joining him as a companion and a disciple, and Jesus gives it as a command, not a suggestion — present tense, meaning \"start now and keep doing it as a way of life.\" The pronoun rendered \"me\" is in a case that signals accompaniment rather than mere pursuit: this isn't \"follow behind me\" but \"walk this road with me,\" side by side rather than trailing single file. Levi got up and did exactly that — leaving a lucrative desk for an itinerant teacher, on nothing more than the force of that call.","translation":"And passing by, he saw Levi the son of Alphaeus sitting at the tax office, and says to him, Start following, and keep on walking this road with me. And he arose and walked the road with him."},{"c1":2,"v1":15,"c2":2,"v2":15,"label":"Mark 2:15","text":"\"Sat at meat\" translates the ordinary word for reclining — the posture of the meal itself, since diners in that culture lay along low couches around the table rather than sitting in chairs. The word rendered \"meat\" simply means food in general, not animal flesh specifically — the same word Jesus uses later of His true nourishment, \"to do the will of him that sent me.\" \"Disciples\" (mathētēs) is simply \"one who learns\" — a description of a following, not a guarantee of salvation; the word covers everyone attached to Jesus as a student, faithful or not, which is worth remembering given how the story of one particular disciple among the Twelve ends.","translation":"And it came to pass, as he reclined at table in his house, that many tax collectors and sinners were reclining together with Jesus and his learners: for there were many, and they were following him."},{"c1":2,"v1":16,"c2":2,"v2":16,"label":"Mark 2:16","text":"The best manuscripts read \"the scribes of the Pharisees\" rather than \"the scribes and the Pharisees\" — meaning specifically the scribes who belonged to the Pharisaic party, essentially trained legal specialists attached to that particular school of thought. Their scandal wasn't abstract: reclining at a meal in that culture was a statement of fellowship and acceptance, so for Jesus to share a couch with tax collectors was, in their eyes, to identify with them.","translation":"And when the scribes who belonged to the Pharisees saw that he was eating together, in fellowship, with sinners and tax collectors, they said to his learners, Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?"},{"c1":2,"v1":17,"c2":2,"v2":17,"label":"Mark 2:17","text":"\"Whole\" translates a word meaning simply \"strong,\" and \"sick\" repeats the word Mark already used back in 1:32 for the diseased crowds brought to Jesus at evening — literally \"those having it badly.\" The Greek word order puts the emphasis exactly where Jesus wants it: no need have the strong of a doctor, but the sick. That's His answer for being found at Levi's table among tax collectors — not that He enjoyed the company of open sin, but that a doctor's place is with the ill, not the well.","translation":"And hearing it, Jesus says to them, Those who are strong have no need of a physician, but those who have it badly. I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners."},{"c1":2,"v1":18,"c2":2,"v2":18,"label":"Mark 2:18","text":"\"Used to fast\" is a periphrastic imperfect construction, stressing that John's disciples and the Pharisees were in the middle of observing a fast at that very moment, not merely that fasting was their general habit. There's an irony buried here: John himself had called the Pharisees a brood of vipers, yet his own disciples are now standing shoulder to shoulder with them, using shared ritual observance to needle Jesus over a table He shared with sinners.","translation":"Now John's disciples and the Pharisees were, at that very time, in the midst of fasting; and they come and say to him, Why do John's disciples and the Pharisees' disciples fast, but your disciples do not fast?"},{"c1":2,"v1":19,"c2":2,"v2":19,"label":"Mark 2:19","text":"\"Children of the bridechamber\" is literally \"sons\" — not the groomsmen but the invited wedding guests, since Galilee didn't follow the Judean custom of formal groomsmen. Jesus is reaching back to John the Baptist's own image of himself as \"friend of the bridegroom\" and recasting it — He is the bridegroom, and mourning has no place at a wedding feast while the groom is present. The question is framed with a negative particle that signals Jesus already expects the answer \"no\" — He isn't wondering aloud, He's stating the obvious and daring anyone to disagree.","translation":"And Jesus said to them, The sons of the bridechamber cannot fast while the bridegroom is with them, can they? As long as they have the bridegroom with them, they are not able to fast."},{"c1":2,"v1":20,"c2":2,"v2":20,"label":"Mark 2:20","text":"\"The days will come\" has no article on \"days\" in the Greek — the stress falls on quality, not a calendar date: days of a certain grim character are coming. The word order puts \"taken away\" first for emphasis, and the reference is to the cross. The better manuscripts then shift from plural \"days\" to a solitary \"in that day\" — a single, weighted moment rather than a stretch of time, marking the crucifixion off as uniquely severe.","translation":"But days shall come, days of a certain grim character, when the bridegroom shall be taken away from them, wrenched away, and then, in that day, they shall fast."},{"c1":2,"v1":21,"c2":2,"v2":21,"label":"Mark 2:21","text":"\"New cloth\" is agnaphos, \"unfulled\" — cloth that hasn't yet gone through the fuller's process of washing, shrinking, and pressing that stabilizes it. Sewn onto a worn garment, it will still shrink after the fact and tear the weakened fabric around it. And \"old\" here isn't the word for old in time (archaios) but for old through use — worn out, threadbare, no longer able to hold a strong new patch. The image fits the larger point: Jesus's message isn't a repair grafted onto the old system, it's something new that the old system cannot contain without tearing further.","translation":"No man sews a patch of unfulled cloth on a garment worn threadbare through use: else the new piece that fills it up takes away from it, the new from the old, and a worse tear is made."},{"c1":2,"v1":22,"c2":2,"v2":22,"label":"Mark 2:22","text":"\"Bottles\" misleads modern ears — these are wineskins, made of animal hide, which can burst under the pressure of fermenting wine in a way glass containers with a cork wouldn't. Two different words for \"new\" appear: the wine is neos, new in time, freshly made; the skins needed are kainos, new in kind or condition, not previously stretched and dried out. Put fresh wine in a wineskin already worn stiff with age (palaios again) and the pressure of fermentation splits it — both wine and skin are ruined. It's the same lesson as the patch: grace poured into the old container of the law destroys both.","translation":"And no man puts new wine, fresh-made, into skins grown old and stiff with use: else the wine bursts the skins, and the wine is spilled out, and the skins are destroyed: but new wine must go into skins new in condition."},{"c1":2,"v1":23,"c2":2,"v2":23,"label":"Mark 2:23","text":"\"Went through the cornfields\" pictures Jesus and His disciples on a footpath cutting alongside and through standing grain — the verb combines both ideas, journeying along the edge and through the field itself. \"Ears of corn\" is misleading to modern readers: the Greek word means simply a head of grain, wheat or barley, and \"corn\" here is just old English usage for grain generally, the way the King James also renders \"a grain of wheat\" with the same word. His disciples are picking heads of grain by hand as they walk, satisfying hunger on the move, nothing more.","translation":"And it came to pass that he went, on a Sabbath day, along the path and through the standing grain; and his disciples began, as they went, to pick the heads of grain by hand."},{"c1":2,"v1":24,"c2":2,"v2":24,"label":"Mark 2:24","text":"\"The Pharisees said\" uses an imperfect verb, not the simple aorist Mark could have chosen if he only meant to report a single remark — the tense signals they kept pressing the point, repeating the accusation. And notice the shift: earlier they complained to His disciples about His table company; now they address Jesus directly, treating the disciples' hand-picked grain as reaping in miniature, and therefore a Sabbath violation.","translation":"And the Pharisees kept saying to him, See, why are they doing on the Sabbath day that which is not lawful?"},{"c1":2,"v1":25,"c2":2,"v2":25,"label":"Mark 2:25","text":"\"Have you never read\" expects the answer \"yes\" — Jesus isn't testing their Bible knowledge, He's reminding scholars of the law of something they already know cold, and turning it back on them. The verbs throughout stay singular, keeping David alone in the spotlight as the one who acted, with his companions mentioned only as an afterthought — which sharpens the parallel Jesus is drawing: a recognized, God-approved leader broke a ceremonial rule under pressing need, and no one condemned him for it.","translation":"And he says to them, Have you never read what David did, when he had need and was hungry, he and those who were with him?"},{"c1":2,"v1":26,"c2":2,"v2":26,"label":"Mark 2:26","text":"\"House of God\" here means the tabernacle, since the temple didn't yet exist in David's day. \"Shewbread\" translates a phrase built from \"before\" and \"to set\" — literally \"the loaves of the setting-forth,\" bread set out before the Lord, which the Jews themselves called \"the bread of the face,\" meaning God's presence. Twelve loaves were baked and set on the table each week, normally reserved for the priests alone to eat once replaced — which is precisely why the incident makes Jesus's point: even bread hedged around by ceremonial restriction gave way to human need.","translation":"How he entered into the house of God, in the days of Abiathar the high priest, and ate the loaves of the setting-forth, the bread of the presence, which it is not lawful to eat except for the priests, and gave also to those who were with him?"},{"c1":2,"v1":27,"c2":2,"v2":27,"label":"Mark 2:27","text":"\"Man\" translates anthrōpos, the generic word for humankind, not a term limited to males — the Sabbath exists for the sake of people in general. \"Was made\" is literally \"came into existence,\" making the Sabbath a created means to a human end, not an end in itself — exactly the inversion the rabbinic tradition had made of it, treating people as existing to serve the day rather than the other way around. The verb for \"he said\" is imperfect too: this wasn't a point made once and dropped, but pressed home repeatedly against minds trained to think ceremonially.","translation":"And he kept saying to them, again and again, The Sabbath came into existence for the sake of man, and not man for the sake of the Sabbath."},{"c1":2,"v1":28,"c2":2,"v2":28,"label":"Mark 2:28","text":"\"Lord\" (kurios) is the word for an owner, someone who possesses and controls a thing outright — but it's also the word the Greek Old Testament uses to render the divine name itself, carrying real overtones of deity here, not just authority over a ritual observance. Mark alone preserves a small word in this saying, better translated \"even\" than \"also\": the Son of Man is Lord even of the Sabbath — striking precisely because the Sabbath was the one institution the formalists treated as untouchable, the last thing they'd have conceded belonged to Him.","translation":"So that the Son of Man is Lord, owner and master, even of the Sabbath."},{"c1":3,"v1":2,"c2":3,"v2":2,"label":"Mark 3:2","text":"\"They watched him\" translates an imperfect tense — not a single glance but a stakeout, the Pharisees keeping our Lord under continuous surveillance to catch him healing on the Sabbath. The verb carries a prefixed preposition meaning \"beside,\" picturing them as side-line observers who wanted no part of him yet couldn't look away — close enough to catch him in the act, careful to stay uninvolved. \"That they might accuse him\" uses the formal legal term for bringing a charge before a tribunal, not casual criticism. This was never curiosity. It was evidence-gathering for a prosecution already decided on.","translation":"And they kept him under continuous watch, from the side, to see whether he would heal him on the Sabbath day, that they might bring a formal charge against him."},{"c1":3,"v1":3,"c2":3,"v2":3,"label":"Mark 3:3","text":"The command is more vivid in the Greek than the English lets on — literally \"arise into the midst,\" step out where everyone can see you. Jesus doesn't quietly heal the man off to the side. He calls him into the center of the room, in full view of the very men watching for a violation, and forces the confrontation into the open before he does anything at all.","translation":"And he says to the man who had the withered hand, Arise, and stand out here, in the midst, where all can see."},{"c1":3,"v1":4,"c2":3,"v2":4,"label":"Mark 3:4","text":"Jesus reframes the whole question before answering it. The Pharisees were thinking in terms of the letter of Sabbath law — is this technically permitted? Jesus asks instead whether it is lawful \"to do good or to do evil, to save life or to kill\" — a category shift from legal compliance to plain ethics. For him there was no third option: refusing to do the good you're able to do is itself a form of doing harm. \"They held their peace\" is imperfect tense too — not a single silence but a held, deepening quiet. They had no answer, because his question wasn't playing by their rules.","translation":"And he says to them, Is it lawful on the Sabbath day to do good, or to do evil? to save life, or to kill? But they kept on holding their peace, in a deepening, unbroken silence."},{"c1":3,"v1":5,"c2":3,"v2":5,"label":"Mark 3:5","text":"Mark pairs two different tenses on purpose. \"He looked round about on them with anger\" is aorist — one swift, sweeping glance, the prefixed preposition indicating it took in the whole circle of onlookers at once. \"Being grieved\" is present tense, durative — a grief that lingered under the anger and outlasted it. Greek actually has three separate words for anger, and Mark reaches for the one denoting a settled, principled response rather than either a passing flare-up or a forbidden, embittered exasperation — anger with an object and a cause, not mere temper. \"Hardness\" translates a word built from the noun for a callus: a heart that has grown a thick, insensible skin over it. The word for \"heart\" is singular in the Greek even though many men are in view, as though the whole company shared one hardened heart between them. \"Restored\" means literally brought back to its former condition — the hand made exactly as it had been.","translation":"And when he had looked around on them all, in one sweeping glance, with anger, being grieved continually at the callused hardness of their heart, he says to the man, Stretch out your hand. And he stretched it out, and his hand was restored, exactly as it had been."},{"c1":3,"v1":6,"c2":3,"v2":6,"label":"Mark 3:6","text":"The alliance in this verse is the real shock. Pharisees and Herodians had nothing in common and ordinarily despised each other — the Pharisees zealous for the law and impatient with Rome, the Herodians attached to a dynasty that owed its throne to Roman favor and wasn't even properly Jewish by descent. Common hostility to Jesus did what nothing else could: it put them in the same room, plotting together. And it happened immediately — the text has them going straight from watching him heal to conspiring to kill him, on the same Sabbath.","translation":"And the Pharisees went out, and immediately took counsel with the Herodians against him, how they might destroy him."},{"c1":3,"v1":7,"c2":3,"v2":7,"label":"Mark 3:7","text":"\"A great multitude\" places the word for \"great\" in an emphatic position in the Greek — this was a crowd out of the ordinary even for Jesus. He withdraws toward open water, which makes practical sense: a lake shore gives room to retreat and a clear line back to a boat if the press of people becomes dangerous, which is exactly the problem the next few verses describe.","translation":"But Jesus withdrew with his disciples toward the sea; and a great multitude, out of the ordinary even for him, followed from Galilee."},{"c1":3,"v1":8,"c2":3,"v2":8,"label":"Mark 3:8","text":"\"What great things he did\" is present tense in the Greek, not simple past — people were continually hearing reports of what he was doing, in real time, as the news kept spreading faster than he could travel. The crowd gathering from Galilee, Judea, Jerusalem, Idumea, and beyond the Jordan wasn't responding to old news; it was chasing a story still unfolding.","translation":"And from Jerusalem, and from Idumea, and beyond Jordan, and around Tyre and Sidon, a great multitude, hearing continually what great things he was doing, came to him."},{"c1":3,"v1":9,"c2":3,"v2":9,"label":"Mark 3:9","text":"Mark is precise about the boat. The word here is for a small rowboat, distinct from the larger fishing vessel used elsewhere — the dinghy that would normally be towed behind the bigger craft. \"Wait on\" translates a verb meaning to stand in constant readiness, and it's in a tense implying continuous action: the disciples kept a small boat rowing along just offshore the whole time, ready to pull Jesus out at a moment's notice. \"Throng\" is the same verb used elsewhere for pressing grapes to extract their juice — not mere crowding but a press capable of crushing a man. The precaution was not excessive.","translation":"And he told his disciples that a small boat should stand in constant readiness for him because of the crowd, lest they press upon him, crushing him like grapes at the press."},{"c1":3,"v1":10,"c2":3,"v2":10,"label":"Mark 3:10","text":"The verb behind \"pressed upon\" means literally to fall upon or collide with — people weren't just crowding close, they were stumbling and shoving into him bodily in their eagerness to get near enough to touch him, hoping healing might come even through an accidental brush. \"Plagues\" translates a word that elsewhere means a stroke or a scourge — the same image behind our own phrase \"a paralytic stroke.\" It's vivid, physical language for affliction, not a clinical term.","translation":"For he had healed many, so that as many as had scourges, physical strokes of affliction, kept falling upon him, colliding with him bodily, to touch him."},{"c1":3,"v1":11,"c2":3,"v2":11,"label":"Mark 3:11","text":"Greek has two verbs for \"to see,\" and Mark chooses the one used of a purposeful, evaluating look rather than a casual glance — the word you'd use of an officer inspecting troops, not a bystander watching a parade pass by. The unclean spirits recognized Jesus with informed, critical scrutiny, not vague unease. Both \"fell down\" and \"cried out\" are imperfect tense — repeated, ongoing action every time this happened, not a single incident. And the declaration \"Thou art the Son of God\" carries an intensive pronoun in the Greek that has no exact English equivalent but lands like \"As for you — you are the Son of God,\" singling him out as utterly unique, in a class of his own. The demons knew exactly who they were dealing with.","translation":"And whenever the unclean spirits, inspecting him with a scrutinizing look, saw him, they kept falling down before him, and kept crying out, saying, As for you, you are the Son of God."},{"c1":3,"v1":12,"c2":3,"v2":12,"label":"Mark 3:12","text":"\"Straightly charged\" translates a verb meaning to rebuke sharply, carrying in the background of its usage a sense of a penalty attached to disobedience — not a mild request but a forceful, binding order. Jesus refused the demons' testimony to his identity. However true their words were, he wanted no endorsement from that source; the truth about him was not going to be established by Satan's own subjects.","translation":"And he kept sternly ordering them, under binding threat, that they should not make him known."},{"c1":3,"v1":13,"c2":3,"v2":15,"label":"Mark 3:13–15","text":"\"Calleth unto him\" is in the middle voice, a form that in Greek marks the subject as acting in his own interest — Jesus called these men for himself, to belong to him. The verb for \"would\" carries its own intensive pronoun as well: \"whom he himself desired.\" No one volunteered; he did the choosing, deliberately and alone. The men \"went off\" to him — the verb implies leaving something behind, stepping away from the crowd below to follow him up the mountain.\n\nThe purpose clauses that follow are worth noticing for their order: first \"that they should be with him,\" a present tense implying ongoing, continuous fellowship — companionship before commission — and only then that he might send them out. \"Send them forth\" is the verb behind our word \"apostle\": to dispatch someone with credentials, as an authorized representative empowered to act on the sender's behalf. \"Power to heal sicknesses and cast out devils\" is not the word for God's own inherent supernatural power but a different term meaning delegated authority — the Twelve weren't given power in themselves, but were authorized to speak and act in Christ's name while the power remained his.","translation":"And he goes up into the mountain, and calls to himself, for his own, those whom he himself desired; and they went off, away from the crowd below, to him. And he appointed twelve, that they might be continually with him, and that he might send them forth as his authorized representatives, to keep on proclaiming, and to have authority, delegated, not their own, to heal sicknesses, and to cast out demons."},{"c1":3,"v1":16,"c2":3,"v2":16,"label":"Mark 3:16","text":"\"Surnamed\" translates a verb meaning literally to place a name upon someone, in addition to what they already had. The name Peter comes from a Greek word for a detached, freestanding stone or fragment — distinct from the related feminine word for a massive bedrock ledge. The distinction matters: Simon is given the name of a solid, weighty stone, descriptive of the rock-like steadiness his character would grow into once transformed, not a description of what he already was. Impulsive, unpredictable Simon becomes, by name and by calling, Peter the rock.","translation":"And upon Simon he placed the name Peter, a detached stone, a freestanding fragment, the man he would grow to become."},{"c1":3,"v1":17,"c2":3,"v2":17,"label":"Mark 3:17","text":"\"Boanerges\" is transliterated straight from a Semitic phrase meaning \"sons of thunder.\" \"Sons of\" is a common Hebrew idiom in which a defining trait is spoken of as though it were a parent — a \"son of thunder\" is simply a thunderous man. The name fits what we know of James and John elsewhere: it was these two who wanted to call down fire on an inhospitable village. A nickname, not a formal title, but an apt one.","translation":"And upon James the son of Zebedee, and John the brother of James, he placed the name Boanerges, that is, Sons of Thunder, men of a thunderous nature."},{"c1":3,"v1":18,"c2":3,"v2":18,"label":"Mark 3:18","text":"The names in this list carry their own small stories. Andrew is a Greek name built on the word for \"man\" and means simply \"manly.\" Philip, also Greek, means \"fond of horses.\" Matthew is Hebrew for \"gift of God.\" Thomas means \"twin.\" And Simon \"the Canaanite\" is better rendered \"the Zealot\" — a member of the fiercely nationalist, anti-Roman party of the day. Putting a Zealot and a Roman tax collector like Matthew in the same band of twelve says something about what following Jesus was already doing to old loyalties and old enemies.","translation":"And Andrew, and Philip, and Bartholomew, and Matthew, and Thomas, and James the son of Alphaeus, and Thaddaeus, and Simon the Zealot."},{"c1":3,"v1":19,"c2":3,"v2":19,"label":"Mark 3:19","text":"\"Iscariot\" most likely means \"man of Kerioth,\" naming Judas's hometown rather than any trait of his — a town in Judah near the border of Edom, according to Joshua's record. \"Betrayed\" translates a compound verb built from \"to give\" and a prefix meaning \"alongside\" — literally, to hand someone over. It's ordinary transactional language, the same word used elsewhere for handing over a prisoner or a piece of property, which is precisely the horror of it here.","translation":"And Judas Iscariot, the man of Kerioth, who also handed him over, as one hands over a piece of property."},{"c1":3,"v1":20,"c2":3,"v2":20,"label":"Mark 3:20","text":"The crowd, which had presumably thinned while Jesus was up the mountain choosing the Twelve, reassembles the moment he comes back down — Mark implies enough time had passed for people to disperse and then regather, which tells us how determined they were not to miss him. The pressure was such that Jesus and his own household couldn't even manage a meal in peace.","translation":"And the crowd comes together again, so that they were not even able to eat bread."},{"c1":3,"v1":21,"c2":3,"v2":21,"label":"Mark 3:21","text":"\"His friends\" is a misleading translation. The Greek phrase means literally \"those from his side\" — his own household, his family, not friends in the ordinary sense. That the same group is identified as his mother and brothers a few verses later confirms it. \"He is beside himself\" translates a verb meaning to be displaced from one's normal state of mind, used elsewhere of being driven out of one's senses entirely. His own family, alarmed by the scale of what they were hearing about his ministry, concluded he had lost his grip and set out to bring him home by force.","translation":"And when those of his own household heard of it, they went out to lay hold of him: for they kept saying, He is displaced from his right mind, out of himself."},{"c1":3,"v1":22,"c2":3,"v2":22,"label":"Mark 3:22","text":"The scribes' accusation goes further than a mere alliance with evil. \"He hath Beelzebub\" implies possession — that the prince of demons is using Jesus as his own instrument, not merely cooperating with him. \"Prince\" translates a word meaning simply \"first\" or \"ruler,\" the one holding chief place among the demons — a title of rank, not nobility. It's a calculated accusation: rather than deny the miracles, which were plainly happening, the scribes tried to reassign their source, crediting Satan instead of God in order to discredit Jesus's claim to be sent from God.","translation":"And the scribes who came down from Jerusalem kept saying, He has Beelzebub within him as his master, and, By the ruler of the demons he casts out demons."},{"c1":3,"v1":23,"c2":3,"v2":23,"label":"Mark 3:23","text":"The word for \"parable\" is built from a verb meaning \"to throw alongside\" — an illustration laid beside a truth so its shape becomes visible by comparison. \"How can Satan cast out Satan\" uses a Greek word for \"how\" that carries the force of \"how is this even possible\" — not a real question but the setup for a logical demonstration. Jesus meets their accusation on its own terms and shows it collapses under its own logic: casting out spirits by the aid of a stronger spirit requires a motive, and no ruler works to dismantle his own kingdom.","translation":"And calling them to him, he began speaking to them in illustrations laid alongside the truth: How is it even possible for Satan to cast out Satan?"},{"c1":3,"v1":24,"c2":3,"v2":26,"label":"Mark 3:24–26","text":"Jesus builds his case in three steps, each one an ordinary observation about how the world works before he applies it to Satan: a kingdom divided against itself cannot stand, a household divided against itself cannot stand, and therefore — the same must be true of Satan's kingdom, if the accusation against Jesus were true. No agent, human or spiritual, works to bring about his own collapse. The logic isn't Jesus defending himself so much as exposing the accusation as self-refuting.","translation":"And if a kingdom is divided against itself, that kingdom is not able to stand. And if a house is divided against itself, that house will not be able to stand. And if Satan has risen up against himself and is divided, he is not able to stand, but has an end."},{"c1":3,"v1":27,"c2":3,"v2":27,"label":"Mark 3:27","text":"\"Spoil\" translates a verb meaning to plunder thoroughly, to ransack completely, and the word behind \"goods\" refers to equipment or tools of a trade rather than mere possessions in general — the very apparatus a strong man depends on for his work. The image is precise: the demons are Satan's tools, and no one raids a strong man's house and strips his equipment from him unless the strong man himself has first been bound. Jesus's exorcisms are proof that the stronger man has already arrived.","translation":"But no one is able to enter into the strong man's house and plunder thoroughly his equipment, unless he first binds the strong man; and then he will ransack his house completely."},{"c1":3,"v1":28,"c2":3,"v2":30,"label":"Mark 3:28–30","text":"\"Blaspheme\" is a transliterated Greek word meaning deliberate, malicious misrepresentation — reproachful speech aimed at discrediting someone, spoken knowing better. That's the precise charge here: the scribes knew the power at work in Jesus's miracles was not evil, and chose anyway to call it Satan's, in order to blunt its force as evidence. \"Eternal damnation\" is a genuinely poor rendering, resting on a corrupted Greek text that read \"judgment\" where the better manuscripts read \"sin.\" The phrase actually means an eternal — or better, an everlasting — sin: not a sin that repeats itself endlessly, but one whose guilt, once incurred in full knowledge and defiance of the truth, is never lifted. It is the deliberate, informed nature of the misrepresentation that makes it what it is, not the category of sin itself.","translation":"Truly I say to you, all their sins shall be forgiven the sons of men, and their deliberate, malicious misrepresentations of the truth, however many they utter; but whoever deliberately misrepresents, in full knowledge, the Holy Spirit, has never forgiveness, but is guilty of a sin that goes on forever, its guilt never lifted."},{"c1":3,"v1":31,"c2":3,"v2":31,"label":"Mark 3:31","text":"Mark switches into present tense here — \"there come his mother and his brethren\" — the kind of vivid present he uses to make a past event feel like it's unfolding as you read it. They stand outside rather than pushing in, and send word through the crowd rather than approaching directly, which suggests some hesitance about how to carry out what they'd come to do.","translation":"And there come his mother and his brothers; and standing outside, they sent word in to him, calling him."},{"c1":3,"v1":32,"c2":3,"v2":32,"label":"Mark 3:32","text":"\"Sat about him\" pictures the crowd seated in a full circle surrounding Jesus, with the message about his family passed inward through the ring to reach him. The mention of sisters alongside mother and brothers, absent from the earlier account of who set out to fetch him, may simply reflect an assumption on the crowd's part rather than a fact — a small, human detail of a message passed along secondhand.","translation":"And a crowd was sitting around him, in a circle; and they say to him, Behold, your mother and your brothers, and your sisters, are outside seeking you."},{"c1":3,"v1":33,"c2":3,"v2":35,"label":"Mark 3:33–35","text":"The verb behind \"looked round about\" is built from the same root as the crowd's circle in the previous verse — Jesus's gaze sweeps the whole ring of people seated around him. It's the same kind of encompassing look he gave the Pharisees back in verse 5, but the temperature is entirely different: no anger here, nothing but warmth. And there's a small but telling grammatical signal in what follows — \"brother,\" \"sister,\" and \"mother\" all appear without the definite article that would normally accompany them in Greek, marking them as figurative rather than literal. Jesus isn't redefining his family in a moment of estrangement; he's naming a family built on obedience to God that anyone in that circle could belong to.","translation":"And sweeping his gaze around the whole circle of those seated about him, he says, Behold my mother and my brothers! For whoever does the will of God, the same is brother, and sister, and mother, to me."},{"c1":4,"v1":2,"c2":4,"v2":2,"label":"Mark 4:2","text":"\"He taught\" renders an imperfect tense — not a single lecture but continuous action, session after session. Mark reaches for this tense often, and it does the work of a moving picture rather than a snapshot. Two verbs cover the range of Jesus's public speech: didaskō, \"to teach,\" the patient unfolding of a subject point by point, and kērussō, \"to proclaim,\" the herald's public announcement. It's worth noting that the New Testament never once calls Jesus a kērux, a \"proclaimer\" — the title people reached for was always didaskalos, \"teacher.\" His parables were exactly what didaskō implies: ordinary pictures set alongside a truth to make it graspable, pitched to what his hearers could actually take in.","translation":"And he kept on teaching them many things in illustrations laid alongside the truth, session after session, and kept saying to them in his teaching."},{"c1":4,"v1":3,"c2":4,"v2":3,"label":"Mark 4:3","text":"\"Hearken\" translates akouete, a present-tense imperative — \"keep on listening\" — rather than the clipped, almost military snap available in the Greek aorist imperative for a blunt command. Jesus chose the gentler form. He needed a shoreline crowd to settle in and stay attentive while he spoke from a boat just offshore, and he got there by patience rather than a bark of authority. \"Behold\" (idou) is a small attention-getting particle with no tidy English equivalent, closer to \"look here\" than a formal \"behold\" — inserted simply to give the moment an extra jolt of immediacy.","translation":"Keep on listening: Look here, the sower went out to sow."},{"c1":4,"v1":4,"c2":4,"v2":4,"label":"Mark 4:4","text":"\"Wayside\" is simply hodos, \"road\" — the hard-packed path running alongside or through the field, not part of the cultivated ground at all. Seed landing there never had a chance to work into soil; it sat exposed on the surface until the birds found it.","translation":"And it came to pass, as he sowed, that some seed fell along the hard-packed path, and the birds came and devoured it up."},{"c1":4,"v1":5,"c2":4,"v2":5,"label":"Mark 4:5","text":"\"Some\" translates allo, \"another seed of the same kind.\" Mark is careful to note that the seed itself never varies from patch to patch in the story. Whatever different fates befall it, the difference traces back to the ground, not the seed — which is the whole point of the parable: the Word sown doesn't change potency from hearer to hearer, but the soil it lands in does.","translation":"And another seed, of that same kind, fell on stony ground, where it did not have much earth; and immediately it sprang up, because it had no depth of earth."},{"c1":4,"v1":7,"c2":4,"v2":7,"label":"Mark 4:7","text":"Again allo marks \"another seed of the same kind,\" this time landing among thorns already established and growing — no farmer sows deliberately into a thicket of briers. \"Choked\" translates sympnigō, the simple verb \"to choke\" with the prefix syn, \"tightly, together,\" added — the compound pictures something squeezed shut, throttled, rather than merely crowded. The same verb elsewhere describes a mob pressing in on someone so tightly it threatens to suffocate him.\n\nThe following two verses shift into an imperfect verb and present-tense participles — \"kept on yielding,\" \"growing up,\" \"increasing,\" \"kept on bearing\" — grammar chosen to stretch a flat fact (\"it bore fruit\") into a process still unfolding as the story is told.","translation":"And another seed of that same kind fell among the thorns already grown up, and the thorns rose up and throttled it, squeezing it shut, and it kept on yielding no fruit.\n\nAnd other seeds fell into the good ground, and kept on yielding fruit, growing up and increasing continually, and kept on bearing, some thirty, and some sixty, and some a hundred."},{"c1":4,"v1":10,"c2":4,"v2":10,"label":"Mark 4:10","text":"\"They that were about him\" names a different circle than \"those closest to him\" back in chapter 3 — not his skeptical family, but the wider ring of followers from which the Twelve had been drawn. \"The mystery\" translates mystērion, built on a root meaning \"to shut, to close\" — the same word the pagan mystery-cults used for truths known only to initiates. Scripture keeps the shape of the word but changes its content: not a truth that's merely hard to work out, but one impossible to grasp until God discloses it — and then perfectly plain.\n\n\"Is given\" is a perfect tense: not \"was handed to you once\" but \"stands given to you, and remains yours.\" The disciples had received the secret of the Kingdom as a lasting possession, theirs to grow into rather than something they'd have to re-earn. \"That\" (hina) introduces a purpose clause, \"in order that\" — the parables are said to function so as to open the truth to the receptive while, at the same time, sealing the willfully closed-minded deeper into their own blindness. It's the same pattern Scripture shows elsewhere: light refused eventually blinds the one who refused it.","translation":"And when he was alone, those about him, along with the twelve, kept asking him about the illustration. And he said to them, To you the secret of the kingdom of God stands given, and remains yours; but to those who are outside, all things come in illustrations laid alongside the truth: in order that seeing they may see and not perceive, and hearing they may hear and not understand."},{"c1":4,"v1":13,"c2":4,"v2":13,"label":"Mark 4:13","text":"The question carries a note of surprised reproach — these men have already been let in on the secret of the Kingdom, and the parable still eludes them. The definite article before \"parables\" points to a specific body of teaching, what Jesus has already given and what is still coming, and the force of the question is that the Sower functions as a kind of master key: miss this one, and the rest stay shut. \"How\" translates pōs, carrying the sense \"how is it even possible\" — not a request for information but Jesus registering, out loud, that without this parable none of the others can be understood.","translation":"And he says to them, Do you not understand this illustration? How, then, will you understand all the illustrations?"},{"c1":4,"v1":15,"c2":4,"v2":15,"label":"Mark 4:15","text":"Two words for \"evil\" sit behind the New Testament's language for the enemy. Kakos names evil in the abstract — a flaw, a defect, something simply bad. Ponēros (the word Matthew's parallel uses for \"the evil one\") names evil that is actively aggressive, not content to be corrupt itself but bent on dragging others down too. Luke's parallel calls him ho diabolos, \"the slanderer, the false accuser.\" All three accounts attach the definite article — not \"an adversary\" but \"the adversary,\" a specific, known figure. \"Taketh away\" (airō) means to seize by force what belongs to, or has been entrusted to, someone else. And \"that was sown\" is a perfect participle — a completed act with a lasting result: the Word had already lodged in the heart and begun its work before it was snatched away.\n\nThe next few verses supply their own vocabulary for the other soils. \"Cares\" (merimna) comes from a root meaning \"pulled in different directions\" — the New Testament's word for anxious distraction, not ordinary concern. \"World\" here is aiōn, \"age,\" the present course of life lived apart from God, not the physical earth. And \"lusts\" is a word whose meaning has drifted in English — the Greek simply means \"strong desire,\" good or bad depending on what it's attached to, only later narrowing to something illicit.","translation":"And these are they by the path, where the word is sown: and when they have heard, immediately the adversary comes and seizes by force the word that had been sown, and had already lodged, in their hearts.\n\nAnd these likewise are they sown among the thorns: such as hear the word, and the anxieties, pulled in different directions, of this present age, and the deceitfulness of riches, and the strong desires for other things entering in, throttle the word, and it becomes unfruitful."},{"c1":4,"v1":21,"c2":4,"v2":21,"label":"Mark 4:21","text":"The question opens with mēti, a negative particle that expects the answer \"no\" — \"a lamp doesn't come..., does it?\" Mark personifies the object with erchetai, \"come,\" as though the lamp walked into the room on its own — part of his habitually vivid way of telling a story. \"Bushel\" is modios, a dry-measure container about the size of a peck basket; clamping a lit lamp under one would smother the flame outright. \"Bed\" is klinē, the low reclining couch set beside a dining table — households of the day ate lying down, not seated — and shoving a lamp under one would set the furniture on fire rather than hide the light. The two images fail in opposite directions to make one point: conceal a lamp and you either extinguish it or destroy something. Its place is the stand (lychnia), out where it can do the one thing it exists to do.","translation":"And he kept saying to them, A lamp doesn't come in to be put under the peck-measure, or under the couch, does it? Doesn't it come in to be set upon the lampstand?"},{"c1":4,"v1":22,"c2":4,"v2":22,"label":"Mark 4:22","text":"The verse is often misread as promising that every secret will eventually leak out. The actual claim runs the other direction: things are hidden for a time precisely so that they can later be revealed — concealment as the means to disclosure, not its opposite. \"Manifested\" translates phaneroō, \"to make visible or known what was previously hidden or unknown.\" The parables' obscurity has a built-in expiration date; what the disciples hold privately now, they'll be responsible to proclaim openly once the time comes.","translation":"For there is nothing hidden, except that it should be made visible; nor was anything kept secret, except that it should come into the open."},{"c1":4,"v1":23,"c2":4,"v2":23,"label":"Mark 4:23","text":"The condition here uses ei, the particle for a condition assumed to be true, rather than ean, which would mark something merely hypothetical. The premise isn't in question — the hearers genuinely do have ears. What's in question is whether they'll use them, and both the infinitive and the main verb are present tense, stressing sustained attentiveness rather than a single act of hearing: \"let him keep on hearing.\"\n\nThe verb behind \"shall more be given\" (prostithēmi) carries the prefix pros, \"toward\" — the sense is of something added on top of what's already due, out of proportion to the effort spent earning it. The measure of attention a person brings to what they hear becomes, almost mechanically, the measure of what comes back to them.\n\nThe parable that follows shifts tenses deliberately. \"Should cast seed\" is an aorist subjunctive, naming the bare act of sowing without dwelling on details. \"Should sleep and rise\" and \"should spring and grow\" are present tense, picturing repeated, ordinary days passing while growth happens unseen — the Greek word order even puts special weight on \"how\": the farmer doesn't know how the seed grows, only that it does.\n\nA final detail is worth keeping: \"of herself\" (automatē, the word behind our \"automatic\") describes something self-moved, proceeding under its own power rather than external control — used elsewhere in the New Testament only of a gate swinging open on its own. The stages of growth get their own precise vocabulary too — chortos, the first blade of grass out of the ground; stachys, the forming ear; sitos, the ripened grain itself — a small botanical ladder tracking a process no farmer can rush and no farmer fully controls.","translation":"If any man genuinely has ears to hear, let him keep on hearing.\n\nTake heed what you hear: with what measure you measure, it shall be measured to you; and to you who hear shall more be added, over and above, out of proportion to what you brought.\n\nAnd he kept saying, So is the kingdom of God, as if a man should cast seed upon the earth, and should sleep and rise, night and day, and the seed keeps springing up and growing, he knows not how. The earth of herself, moved by her own power, bears fruit: first the blade, then the ear, then the full grain in the ear."},{"c1":4,"v1":30,"c2":4,"v2":30,"label":"Mark 4:30","text":"\"Liken\" translates homoioō, \"to compare, to find a resemblance between two things.\" The first-person plural, \"shall we liken,\" draws the hearers themselves into the search for the right picture rather than simply handing them an answer.\n\nThe mustard seed that follows plays on real, verifiable smallness at planting and real, visible size at maturity — the definite article on \"herbs\" specifies cultivated garden plants, not wild ones, sharpening the contrast between the tiniest seed a gardener would plant and the biggest plant that garden would produce. \"Lodge\" is literally \"pitch their tents,\" the same picture language used of nomads camping.\n\nThe closing note on Jesus's method turns on a double sense of akouō, \"to hear\": in certain New Testament contexts (as elsewhere in the epistles) the word slides from mere hearing into understanding, and that's the sense at work here — Jesus pitched his teaching to what his audience could actually take in, not just what they could physically hear. \"Spake he not\" is imperfect, marking habitual practice, not a one-time exception. And \"he expounded\" is epiluō — the simple verb \"to loosen\" with the perfective prefix epi added, \"to loosen further,\" i.e., to unpack and clarify well past the surface meaning. In private, with the Twelve — idios marking them as peculiarly his own circle, not the wider crowd of learners — that loosening amounted to something closer to fresh disclosure than mere explanation.","translation":"And he kept saying, To what shall we liken the kingdom of God? or with what comparison shall we compare it? It is like a grain of mustard seed, which, when it is sown in the earth, is smaller than all the seeds that are upon the earth; but when it is sown, it grows up, and becomes greater than all the garden herbs, and puts out great branches, so that the birds of the air are able to pitch their tents under its shadow.\n\nAnd with many such illustrations laid alongside the truth he kept speaking the word to them, as they were able to hear and understand it; and without an illustration he did not habitually speak to them: but privately, to his own, he loosened everything further, unpacking it all."},{"c1":4,"v1":35,"c2":4,"v2":35,"label":"Mark 4:35","text":"Mark rarely bothers to anchor an episode to a specific time of day, which makes his notice here — \"the same day,\" evening having come — worth pausing on. It ties the crossing of the lake directly onto the end of the long day of parables, the accusation, and the visit from his family: the storm on the water follows hard on a day that had already drained him.","translation":"And on that same day, when evening had come, he says to them, Let us go across to the other side."},{"c1":4,"v1":36,"c2":4,"v2":36,"label":"Mark 4:36","text":"\"They took him\" renders paralambanō — the simple verb \"to take\" with the prefix \"alongside\" attached, giving the sense of taking someone under one's care and protection. The same verb describes the angel telling Joseph to take the child and his mother to Egypt: not a casual pickup, but a deliberate assuming of responsibility. \"Ship\" is ploion, the larger fishing vessel, not a rowboat — the traditional \"little ships\" for the accompanying boats rests on a weaker manuscript reading; the better texts have the same, larger word both times.","translation":"And leaving the crowd, they took him under their care, along with them, in the boat, the larger vessel, even as he was. And other boats also were with him."},{"c1":4,"v1":37,"c2":4,"v2":37,"label":"Mark 4:37","text":"The word behind \"there arose a great storm\" never describes a single strong gust or even a steadily blowing wind, however violent — it names a storm that breaks suddenly out of black thunderclouds, a whirlwind driving rain and chaos in every direction at once, the same word the Greek Old Testament uses for the whirlwind out of which God spoke to Job. \"Beat into\" (epiballō, \"to throw upon\") is imperfect tense — the waves weren't crashing in once but repeatedly, wave after wave. \"Was now full\" uses gemizō, \"to fill up\" — the boat was in the process of swamping as the disciples watched.","translation":"And there arises a great whirlwind of a storm, out of the black clouds, and the waves kept beating into the boat, over and over, so that the boat was now, even as they watched, filling up with water."},{"c1":4,"v1":38,"c2":4,"v2":38,"label":"Mark 4:38","text":"\"The hinder part\" is prymna, the stern, the opposite end from the bow. \"Pillow\" (proskephalaion, literally \"that toward which one puts the head\") was no soft luxury cushion but the steersman's leather headrest or the low bench where he sat — ordinary ship's furniture, not comfort brought along for the trip. \"Master\" in the disciples' cry is didaskale, the vocative of \"Teacher,\" the same title Mark has already shown was the one people instinctively reached for. \"Carest thou not\" uses a verb (mellei) meaning \"it is a concern, a care\" — and \"we perish\" is present tense, \"we are perishing\" right now, not a prediction of what's about to happen. The disciples aren't warning him of coming danger; they're reporting an emergency already underway that he's sleeping through.","translation":"And he himself was in the stern, asleep on the steersman's headrest: and they wake him, and say to him, Teacher, is it no concern to you that we are perishing, right now?"},{"c1":4,"v1":39,"c2":4,"v2":39,"label":"Mark 4:39","text":"\"Peace\" translates siōpaō, \"be silent, hushed, calm,\" in the present imperative — \"be being calmed,\" an ongoing settling rather than an instant. \"Be still\" is phimoō, \"to muzzle\" — literally the verb for putting a muzzle on an ox, used elsewhere of Jesus silencing hostile questioners. Here it's a perfect imperative: \"be muzzled, and stay that way.\" The command to the wind is for a process of quieting; the command to the sea is for an immediate and permanent hush. \"Ceased\" (kopazō) pictures the wind as if it had worn itself out, grown tired of raging — vivid personification matching the storm's own violent introduction two verses earlier.","translation":"And he arose, and rebuked the wind, and said to the sea, Be silent, be continually calmed; be muzzled, and stay muzzled. And the wind grew weary and ceased, and there was a great calm."},{"c1":4,"v1":40,"c2":4,"v2":40,"label":"Mark 4:40","text":"\"Fearful\" is an adjective doing the work of a noun — \"you timid ones, you fearful ones.\" \"How is it\" translates pōs, the same construction used back in verse 13 for \"how is it possible\" — Jesus isn't asking a real question so much as expressing astonishment that men who have watched him this closely could still lack faith. The one who had just quieted a hurricane with two sentences was sitting in the boat with them the whole time.","translation":"And he said to them, Why are you so timid? How is it that you still have no faith?"},{"c1":4,"v1":41,"c2":4,"v2":41,"label":"Mark 4:41","text":"\"They feared exceedingly\" is literally \"they feared a great fear\" — a noun repeating and intensifying its own verb, a Hebraic idiom carried straight into the Greek for maximum force. \"What manner of man\" oversets the original: the word \"man\" isn't in the Greek at all, and Mark's actual wording is simply \"who then is this?\" — with ara, \"then,\" functioning as an inferential particle: given everything that's just happened, who must this be? The demonstrative is masculine, so a person, not a force or a phenomenon, is what's being asked about. Wind and sea taking orders from a human voice is what turns the disciples' fear of the storm into something closer to awe of the man in the boat with them.","translation":"And they feared a great fear, and kept saying to one another, Who then is this, that even the wind and the sea obey him?"},{"c1":5,"v1":2,"c2":5,"v2":5,"label":"Mark 5:2–5","text":"The man's dwelling place uses a compound verb built on the preposition kata, \"down\" — the same idea behind our phrase \"settled down.\" Combined with the imperfect tense of \"had,\" the picture is of a man who had made the tombs his permanent, ongoing residence, not a temporary hideout. The tombs themselves were often natural caves or chambers cut into the rock, roomy enough to be held up by pillars, with recesses along the walls for the dead — set apart from the towns, in lonely country, exactly the kind of place a man fleeing all human company would gravitate to.\n\n\"Chain\" translates halusis, itself built from luo, \"to loose,\" with a negating prefix — literally \"that which cannot be loosed.\" Paul uses the same word of the handcuffs he wore as a prisoner (Ephesians 6:20) and of Onesiphorus's chain (2 Timothy 1:16). Here the irony is that this chain kept failing at its own name: \"bound with fetters and chains\" uses a perfect-tense verb describing careful, thorough work, yet \"plucked asunder\" (diaspao, \"torn in two\") and \"broken in pieces\" (suntribo, \"crushed together\") describe the man's superhuman strength snapping every restraint, so that no one had the power to \"tame\" him — damazo, the word for breaking a wild animal. His cry (krazo) was an inarticulate shriek, and his self-mutilation (katakopto, \"to cut oneself up\") left him gashed and scarred from head to foot.","translation":"And there met him out of the tombs a man with an unclean spirit, who had continually made his dwelling among the tombs; and no one was able to bind him any longer, not even with a chain, because he had often been bound with fetters and chains, and the chains had been torn apart by him and the fetters crushed to pieces, and no one had the strength to tame him. And always, night and day, he was in the tombs and in the mountains, crying out and cutting himself with stones."},{"c1":5,"v1":6,"c2":5,"v2":8,"label":"Mark 5:6–8","text":"The word rendered \"but\" is simply kai, plain \"and\" — Greek has two stronger words for \"but\" (alla and de) that Mark doesn't reach for here. The connection is continuative, not contrastive: this violent man's usual behavior toward strangers and his sudden run toward Jesus are simply two things that happened in sequence, not a dramatic reversal the grammar itself signals. What happens next is the reversal — proskuneo, \"worship,\" describes falling to the knees and touching the forehead to the ground, the full posture of homage paid to a king or to God. A being given over to darkness, with no salvation offered him, bows to the Son of God here just as Paul says every being under the earth will (Philippians 2:10) — though it is the demon controlling the man, not the man himself, who renders this forced homage.\n\n\"What have I to do with thee\" is more literally \"what is there with reference to me and with reference to you\" — an idiom asking, \"what do we have in common?\" The demon then puts Jesus under an oath: \"I adjure thee\" translates horkizo, \"to force someone to swear.\" \"Torment\" is basanizo, a word that originally described testing metal on a touchstone before it came to mean torture — the demon's version of the third degree, in reverse. And \"for He said\" is a progressive imperfect, \"He had been saying\" — Jesus had already commanded the spirit out more than once; this outburst is the demon's response to a command already repeated.","translation":"And seeing Jesus from afar, he ran and worshiped him, and crying out with a loud voice he said, What is there between me and you, Jesus, Son of the Most High God? I put you under oath by God, do not torment me. For Jesus had been saying to him, Come out of the man, you unclean spirit."},{"c1":5,"v1":9,"c2":5,"v2":9,"label":"Mark 5:9","text":"\"He asked him\" is imperfect — \"he kept on asking\" — implying the demon didn't answer on the first try. \"Legion\" was a fixed, well-known term for a Roman military unit of roughly 6,800 men, already borrowed into both Greek and Aramaic in this region where legions were a familiar sight. The name isn't picked for size alone but for what a legion represented: overwhelming, organized force. One man, but many spirits operating as a single unit under that name.","translation":"And he kept on asking him, What is your name? And he answered, saying, Legion is my name, for we are many."},{"c1":5,"v1":10,"c2":5,"v2":10,"label":"Mark 5:10","text":"\"Besought Him much\" is imperfect of parakaleo, \"I beg you, please\" — the demon kept pleading, speaking for the whole company inhabiting the man, that they not be sent out of the region altogether. \"Decapolis\" is simply the transliteration of the Greek for \"ten cities,\" the district just southeast of the Sea of Galilee.\n\nWhen the request narrows to the herd of swine, the verb shifts to the aorist — a single decisive plea rather than ongoing pressure — and \"send\" is an aorist imperative, a sharp command wanting instant compliance. The demons' request itself is telling: it implies they have no rest apart from occupying a body, human or animal, which is why exile from every possible host was the one thing worse to them than entering swine.","translation":"And he kept begging him urgently that he would not send them out of the region."},{"c1":5,"v1":13,"c2":5,"v2":13,"label":"Mark 5:13","text":"Jesus doesn't command the demons into the swine — He permits it; the grammar keeps the initiative with them, not with Him. \"Ran violently\" is hormao, \"to rush headlong, to be driven forward with sudden force\" — the same shock of unwelcome occupation that had made the man himself unmanageable now sent two thousand animals stampeding down the slope. \"Were choked\" is imperfect, picturing the herd disappearing into the water pig by pig rather than all at once; the verb is also used elsewhere of drowning.\n\nThe swineherds \"fled\" (pheugo, to run for safety) and gave a full, detailed report (diegeomai, \"to narrate all the way through\") to their employers — understandably eager to explain a wrecked herd. When the townspeople came to look, \"see\" translates theoreo, the word for a searching, deliberate inspection rather than a passing glance — the kind of scrutiny a general gives troops on review. What they found undid them more than the loss of the pigs: the former demoniac \"in his right mind,\" sophroneo, a word whose dominant sense is self-control, a settled rein on one's own passions and desires, not bare sanity. A man who used to gash himself with stones was now sitting still, dressed, and master of himself — and it was that transformation, even more than the wrecked herd, that sent the town begging Jesus to leave.","translation":"And he permitted them. And the unclean spirits came out and entered into the swine, and the herd, about two thousand, rushed headlong down the steep bank into the sea, and one by one they were being choked in the sea. And those who fed them fled and reported the whole matter fully, both in the town and in the country. And they came to inspect closely what had happened, and came to Jesus and saw the demon-possessed man sitting there, clothed and in full possession of himself, the very man who had had the legion; and they were afraid."},{"c1":5,"v1":18,"c2":5,"v2":18,"label":"Mark 5:18","text":"The verb describing Jesus stepping into the boat is a present participle running alongside the main verb \"prayed\" — the man's pleading happened in the very moment of Jesus boarding, as if he couldn't risk missing the chance. His request to go along looks like a bid to become a disciple, though it's just as easily read as simple fear of being left alone again in the place where the spirits had found him.","translation":"And as he was stepping into the boat, the man who had been demon-possessed kept begging him that he might be with him."},{"c1":5,"v1":19,"c2":5,"v2":19,"label":"Mark 5:19","text":"\"Go home to thy friends\" is more exactly \"go into your home, to your own\" — Jesus sends him first to his own family, the people who had presumably watched his years of torment. \"Tell\" is apaggello, \"to carry a report back\" — bring them word of what happened. \"Hath done\" is perfect tense, marking a complete, settled result: this was not a temporary improvement but a finished cure. \"Had compassion\" translates eleeo, sympathy for another's suffering specifically of the kind that acts, not merely feels.\n\nElsewhere Jesus silences those He heals, wary of stirring the wrong kind of excitement about His mission. Here there's no such danger — the townspeople had just begged Him to leave — and this district needed the testimony badly enough that Jesus sends the man out to give it.","translation":"Go into your home, to your own people, and report back to them how much the Lord has done for you, and stands done, and how he had mercy on you."},{"c1":5,"v1":20,"c2":5,"v2":20,"label":"Mark 5:20","text":"\"Publish\" is kerusso, \"to make a public proclamation,\" the word for a herald's announcement, not a private conversation. \"Decapolis\" again is just \"ten cities\" transliterated — and the man took his testimony across the whole district, so that this healed man became known, by report, throughout all ten towns.","translation":"And he went away and began to proclaim publicly in the Ten Cities how much Jesus had done for him; and everyone was marveling."},{"c1":5,"v1":21,"c2":5,"v2":21,"label":"Mark 5:21","text":"\"Gathered unto Him\" is literally \"after Him\" (epi), not simply \"to Him\" — the crowd forms behind Him as He becomes the center everyone orients toward. The verb \"was gathered\" is passive: the people didn't organize themselves so much as get pulled together by a force outside themselves, drawn by the pull of who He was. \"Nigh unto the sea\" uses para, \"alongside\" — He is right at the water's edge.\n\nA synagogue normally had more than one ruler (compare Acts 13:14-15), whose duties included choosing readers and teachers and keeping order in services — so Jairus held real local standing. His name is Hebrew for \"whom the Lord enlightens.\" \"At the point of death\" is an idiom, literally \"she has it lastly\" — she is breathing her last. And the word Jairus uses isn't the ordinary word for \"heal\" but sozo, \"save\" — the same word used of spiritual salvation, here carrying its equally legitimate sense of rescue from death, exactly as we still say a dying patient's life was \"saved.\"","translation":"And when Jesus had crossed again in the boat to the other side, a great crowd was gathered together after him, and he was alongside the sea. And there comes one of the synagogue rulers, Jairus by name, and seeing him, falls at his feet, and begs him earnestly, saying, My little daughter is at her last gasp; come and lay your hands on her, that she may be saved and live."},{"c1":5,"v1":24,"c2":5,"v2":24,"label":"Mark 5:24","text":"\"Jesus went with him\" more fully reads \"went off with him at once\" — no hesitation. \"Much people followed\" is imperfect, a crowd that kept pressing along behind Him the whole way, and \"thronged\" (sunthlibo) means pressed in from every side — Luke's parallel account uses a word for crowding someone to the point of suffocation.\n\n\"Suffered\" (25-26) is pascho, \"to suffer pain,\" not merely \"to undergo treatment\" — this woman's twelve years were years of real pain, spent \"under\" (hupo) the hands of many doctors who left her worse, not better.\n\nWhen she \"heard of Jesus\" (27-28), the Greek has a definite article that the English drops: she heard about \"the Jesus\" — a small detail that shows how common the name Joshua/Jesus was, and how the article had become a way of marking out this particular Jesus, whose reputation by now needed no further introduction. Coming up in the crowd behind Him, she kept saying to herself — the verb is imperfect — \"if I touch even His garments, I shall be sozo,\" the same word Jairus used: saved, whether from disease or from anything else, one word for both.","translation":"And he went off with him at once. And a great crowd kept following him, and kept pressing in on him from every side."},{"c1":5,"v1":29,"c2":5,"v2":29,"label":"Mark 5:29","text":"\"The fountain of her blood was dried up\" uses pege, \"spring\" — not just that the flow stopped, but that its source was shut off, a picture chosen to say the cure was total, not temporary relief. \"She felt\" translates ginosko, \"to know by personal experience,\" and the tense is the ingressive aorist — the instant of realization, the moment it dawned on her that something had changed. \"She was healed of that plague\" is perfect tense, \"I have been healed and remain so.\" \"Plague\" is mastix, literally \"whip\" or \"scourge\" — the same word used elsewhere of suffering regarded as discipline from God's hand. Twelve years of being scourged by this illness, ended in an instant.","translation":"And immediately the spring of her blood was dried up at its source, and she came to know in her body that she had been healed, and remained healed, of her scourge."},{"c1":5,"v1":30,"c2":5,"v2":30,"label":"Mark 5:30","text":"\"Knowing\" is epiginosko — the simple verb already means experiential, personal knowledge, and the prefix epi sharpens it further, giving the sense of a clear, unmistakable awareness. What He knew was that dunamis — \"power,\" specifically the kind that overcomes resistance and produces change — had gone out from Him. The Greek word order itself puts the emphasis where it belongs: \"the out-from-Him power going out.\" Some measure of what made the miracle happen cost Him something to give; He felt it leave.","translation":"And Jesus, immediately becoming clearly and unmistakably aware within himself that power had gone out from him, turned about in the crowd and kept saying, Who touched my garments?"},{"c1":5,"v1":31,"c2":5,"v2":31,"label":"Mark 5:31","text":"The word for \"thronging\" here (sunthlibo) is a compound of thlibo, the verb Mark uses back in 3:9 for ordinary crowd-pressure — but thlibo is also the word for pressing grapes at the winepress, crushing them. Adding the prefix sun, \"together,\" intensifies it further: a concerted, converging press from every direction, worse than usual. The disciples' surprise is genuine — in a crush like that, why single out one touch? — but their question misses that Jesus was registering something the crowd's random jostling could never produce.","translation":"And his disciples kept saying to him, You see the crowd pressing in on you together from every side, crushing in on you, and yet you say, Who touched me?"},{"c1":5,"v1":32,"c2":5,"v2":32,"label":"Mark 5:32","text":"\"He looked round about\" is imperfect and middle voice together — continuous action, done for His own purpose, in His own interest — Jesus keeps searching the crowd on His own initiative regardless of what the disciples had just objected. The Greek article and participle describing \"her who had done this\" are feminine, marking out a woman specifically. Whatever told Him that, the distinction was real: her touch, however brief, was categorically different from the crowd's shoving — timid, deliberate, and, given what Levitical law said about her condition, genuinely risky for her to attempt at all.","translation":"And he kept looking around to see the woman who had done this."},{"c1":5,"v1":33,"c2":5,"v2":33,"label":"Mark 5:33","text":"\"What was done\" is perfect tense — a completed, lasting change, which is exactly why she couldn't simply melt back into the crowd once she realized she'd been seen. She had touched Him in secret, aware that what she'd done broke ceremonial boundaries, and now falls before Him and discloses \"all the truth\" — not just the touch itself but the whole account behind it, years of illness and every reason she had for reaching out the way she did.","translation":"But the woman, fearing and trembling, knowing what had been done in her and stood done, came and fell down before him and told him all the truth."},{"c1":5,"v1":34,"c2":5,"v2":34,"label":"Mark 5:34","text":"\"Daughter\" is the first word out of His mouth to her, and it's deliberately chosen — not a term for a child but a term of tenderness, spoken to a grown woman probably close to His own age. It reframes the entire encounter: not power dispensed to a stranger, but a father's welcome to his own. \"Hath made thee whole\" is sozo again, perfect tense — a permanent rescue, sealed. \"Go in peace\" uses the preposition eis, \"into,\" not \"in\" — not simply accompanied by peace but sent forward into it, as through a door just opened for her. \"Be whole\" is a present imperative, \"keep on being whole\" — hugies, \"sound of body,\" the root behind our word \"hygienic\" — of her mastix, that same \"scourge\" word from a few verses earlier, now closed for good.","translation":"And he said to her, Daughter, your faith has saved you, and the salvation stands. Go your way into peace, and continue being sound and whole from your scourge."},{"c1":5,"v1":35,"c2":5,"v2":35,"label":"Mark 5:35","text":"\"There came\" is a historical present — Mark's Greek switches to present tense mid-narrative for vividness, dropping the reader into the moment as it happened. \"Thy daughter is dead\" is aorist: a flat, completed fact — \"she died.\" The word \"house\" isn't actually in the Greek; it's supplied because the ruler himself is standing right there with Jesus, so the messengers must be coming from his home. \"Troublest\" is skullo, a vivid word literally meaning \"to skin\" or \"flay\" before it came to mean \"bother, harass, wear out\" — the messengers assume the case is now closed and see no point in dragging out the Teacher's (didaskalos) time any further.","translation":"While he was yet speaking, there come some from the ruler's house who say, Your daughter died. Why do you go on wearing out the Teacher?"},{"c1":5,"v1":36,"c2":5,"v2":36,"label":"Mark 5:36","text":"\"Jesus heard\" is parakouo, \"to hear alongside\" — to overhear something not addressed to Him. \"Be not afraid\" is a present imperative used as a prohibition, and this construction specifically forbids continuing an action already underway: not \"don't start being afraid\" but \"stop the fear you're already in.\" \"Only believe\" is also a present imperative, and it carries the same continuous force in the other direction — \"keep on believing,\" a sustained, ongoing trust Jesus calls for precisely at the moment the news seems to have closed off all reason for it.","translation":"But Jesus, overhearing the word being spoken, says to the ruler of the synagogue, Stop being afraid; only keep on believing."},{"c1":5,"v1":37,"c2":5,"v2":37,"label":"Mark 5:37","text":"The Greek attaches the definite article to Peter's name alone, which has the effect of binding all three men — Peter, James, and John — together as a single, recognized unit, set apart from the rest. These three alone are allowed to witness what follows; the training of the Twelve had its inner circle, and this was one of the moments reserved for it.","translation":"And he permitted no one to accompany him except Peter, and James, and John the brother of James."},{"c1":5,"v1":38,"c2":5,"v2":38,"label":"Mark 5:38","text":"The best manuscripts read \"they come,\" not \"He cometh\" — Jesus, the ruler, and the three disciples arrive together. \"Seeth\" is theoreo, the same word for a deliberate, searching look used earlier of the crowd examining the healed demoniac — Jesus takes in the scene with full understanding, not surprise. \"Tumult\" is thorubos, \"uproar,\" and \"wailed\" (alalazo) is an onomatopoeic word — its very sound imitates the cry it names, the same shout soldiers once raised charging into battle, here worn down into the professional, repetitive keening of hired mourners.","translation":"And they come to the house of the ruler of the synagogue, and he looks closely and takes in an uproar, and people weeping and wailing loudly, again and again."},{"c1":5,"v1":39,"c2":5,"v2":39,"label":"Mark 5:39","text":"\"Why make ye this ado\" uses thorubeo, \"to raise an uproar\" — the same root as the \"tumult\" just described. \"Damsel\" is paidion, \"little girl.\" \"Is dead\" is aorist — a plain statement of the fact, not a denial of it. When Jesus calls it \"sleep,\" He isn't disputing that she died; He's naming what death is about to become in His hands — a condition He is about to reverse as easily as waking someone.","translation":"And going in, he says to them, Why do you raise this uproar, and weep? The little girl did not die, but is sleeping."},{"c1":5,"v1":40,"c2":5,"v2":40,"label":"Mark 5:40","text":"\"Laughed Him to scorn\" is katagelao — the ordinary verb \"to laugh\" with a prefix meaning \"down,\" so literally \"to laugh someone down,\" to jeer. The tense is inceptive imperfect: \"they broke out laughing and kept at it.\" \"Put them all out\" is ekballo, \"to throw out\" — real physical force was apparently needed to clear the professional mourners from the house, much as it had been needed to clear the Temple. \"Taketh\" is paralambano, \"to take alongside,\" of gathering the parents and the three disciples under His own direct charge. And \"entereth\" uses eisporeuomai, a verb elsewhere used of setting out on a journey, rather than one of the plainer words for simply walking — as if the few steps into that room carried the weight of a much longer road, the way a short walk can feel endless when everything about the destination matters.","translation":"And they began laughing him down, and kept at it. But he, throwing them all out, takes along with him the father of the little girl and the mother and those with him, and sets out into where the little girl was lying."},{"c1":5,"v1":41,"c2":5,"v2":41,"label":"Mark 5:41","text":"He took the girl's hand with krateo, \"to grip firmly, to take possession of\" — a strong, deliberate hold, the kind needed to help someone weakened by illness rise. \"Talitha cumi\" preserves Jesus's actual Aramaic words, remembered and passed on — almost certainly through Peter, who was in the room — then translated into Greek for readers who didn't speak the original. The same pattern shows up with Jesus's words from the cross: the original language kept, then rendered for a wider audience. A tender, private moment gets its native words preserved rather than smoothed into translation alone.","translation":"And gripping the little girl's hand firmly, he says to her, Talitha cumi, which is, being translated, Little girl, I say to you, arise."},{"c1":5,"v1":42,"c2":5,"v2":42,"label":"Mark 5:42","text":"\"Arose\" is anistemi, \"to stand up,\" aorist tense — a single completed action, someone getting up from lying down. \"Walked\" shifts to the imperfect of peripateo, \"kept on walking about\" — not one step but sustained, ordinary movement. The note that she \"was twelve years\" old matters because paidion, \"little girl,\" could otherwise suggest a much younger child; the detail confirms she was old enough to be up and walking under her own power. \"Astonished with a great astonishment\" uses existemi, literally \"to stand out of\" or \"to be put out of place\" — the source of our word \"ecstasy,\" though \"amazement,\" people knocked clean out of their ordinary composure, captures the sense better than anything mystical.","translation":"And immediately the little girl stood up, and kept walking about; for she was twelve years old. And at once they were utterly beside themselves with a great amazement."},{"c1":5,"v1":43,"c2":5,"v2":43,"label":"Mark 5:43","text":"\"Charged them straitly\" is diastello, \"to give strict orders.\" The concern wasn't that people would notice the girl was alive and well — that couldn't be hidden — but that word of an actual return from death would spread and stir up the wrong kind of expectation. The instruction to give her something to eat is a small, practical detail: not merely alive, but well enough to need supper, proof that this was a full restoration and not a fragile reprieve.","translation":"And he gave them strict orders that no one should know this, and he told them to give her something to eat."},{"c1":6,"v1":2,"c2":6,"v2":2,"label":"Mark 6:2","text":"\"Astonished\" translates ekplēssō, built from a prefix meaning \"out\" attached to the verb \"to strike\" — the picture is of being struck clean out of one's composure, stunned past self-possession rather than merely startled. The verb is imperfect: this was not a moment's gasp but a lingering daze that hung over the synagogue crowd. Their own neighbor, a man they had watched grow up in a carpenter's shop, was suddenly displaying wisdom and dunamis — the New Testament's word for miracle-working power breaking in from outside the natural order — that nothing about His hometown pedigree could account for. Their question, \"from where does this one get these things?\" is clipped and biting in the Greek, less a genuine inquiry than a demand that He justify Himself.","translation":"And many, hearing him, kept on being astonished, struck out of their composure, saying, From where does this man get these things? And what is this wisdom that has been given to him, that even such works of power come about through his hands?"},{"c1":6,"v1":3,"c2":6,"v2":3,"label":"Mark 6:3","text":"\"Carpenter\" renders tektōn, a word broader than the English suggests — it covered anyone who built or shaped material for a living, in wood, stone, or metal, essentially a skilled tradesman. Jesus had earned His bread with His hands for roughly three decades before anyone heard Him teach. That gap — common laborer one year, extraordinary teacher and healer the next — is exactly what tripped up His neighbors. \"Offended\" translates skandalizō, the word for the trigger of a trap: something that causes a person to stumble over what should have led them to trust. They could not reconcile the carpenter they knew with the authority they were hearing, so instead of bridging the gap with faith, they let it become the very thing that tripped them.","translation":"Is not this the carpenter, the son of Mary, and brother of James, and Joses, and Judas, and Simon? And are not his sisters here with us? And they kept being tripped up over him, stumbling on him as on a trap."},{"c1":6,"v1":4,"c2":6,"v2":4,"label":"Mark 6:4","text":"\"Prophet\" is prophētēs, literally one who speaks forth — a herald of God's message, not primarily a predictor of the future, which is a secondary sense English has layered onto the word. Using it of Himself here, Jesus claims a specific office alongside the other titles He had already claimed elsewhere — Messiah, Son of Man, Son of God. \"Honor\" is timē, the recognition and deference a person is owed. The irony Jesus names is almost proverbial: distance breeds reverence, familiarity breeds contempt. The people with the most exposure to Him — His own household, His own town — extended Him the least of it.","translation":"And Jesus kept saying to them, A prophet is not without honor, except in his own fatherland, and among his own relatives, and in his own house."},{"c1":6,"v1":5,"c2":6,"v2":5,"label":"Mark 6:5","text":"The negation is emphatic in the Greek — not simply \"no\" mighty work, but \"not even one.\" \"Sick\" translates arrōstos, a word for weakness at the constitutional level, a body without strength, rather than any specific ailment. \"Work\" again is dunamis, the vocabulary of supernatural power breaking into ordinary life. The picture is not that Jesus's power failed Him in Nazareth; it's that unbelief had closed off the channel through which it normally flowed — so thoroughly that the townspeople didn't even trouble themselves to bring their sick to Him.","translation":"And he was not able to do there not even one work of power, except that he laid his hands on a few who were without strength, and healed them."},{"c1":6,"v1":6,"c2":6,"v2":6,"label":"Mark 6:6","text":"Jesus \"marvelled\" at their unbelief — the same capacity for astonishment the Gospels elsewhere show Him exercising toward a Gentile centurion's faith, here provoked by its opposite. That a teacher who knows all things could still marvel is worth sitting with: as God, nothing catches Him by surprise, yet in His genuine humanity He came to Nazareth carrying an expectation, and what He met there disappointed it. \"He went about the villages\" pictures a circuit — Jesus working the ring of towns surrounding Nazareth, teaching His way around the very region that had just shut Him out.","translation":"And he kept marveling because of their unbelief. And he went around the villages in a circuit, teaching."},{"c1":6,"v1":7,"c2":6,"v2":7,"label":"Mark 6:7","text":"\"He calls to Himself the Twelve\" — Mark's Greek is present tense even though narrating a past event, his habit of throwing a scene into the present to make it vivid. The verb for sending them out is apostellō, the root of our word \"apostle\": not a casual dispatch but a formal commissioning, envoys sent to represent the Sender's own authority. What they carry is exousia — delegated authority — over the unclean spirits, distinct from dunamis, the raw power itself; the Twelve are given the right to command, backed by a power that isn't their own.\n\nThe travel restrictions are pointed. No pēra — not merely \"no travel bag\" but no beggar's collecting-pouch, the kind itinerant religious hucksters carried to gather offerings from onlookers; Jesus is ruling out professional begging, not just packing light. No chalkos, \"copper\" — no money at all, in the coinage a poor traveler would actually carry. And shaking the dust off their feet when a topos, a town or district, refused to receive them, was a recognized gesture of formal repudiation — a visible verdict pronounced on a place that had heard and refused.\n\nTheir message, that people should repent, uses metanoeō — literally a change of mind about one's former course, not an emotion of regret (the New Testament keeps a separate word for that). And \"anointed with oil\" reflects the ordinary medicinal use of olive oil in the ancient world, the era's basic remedy, used here alongside the disciples' healing ministry — prayer and means working together, not one replacing the other.","translation":"And he calls to himself the Twelve, and began to send them out two by two, and kept giving them authority over the unclean spirits; and he charged them that they should take nothing for the road except a staff only — no bread, no beggar's pouch, no copper coin in their belts; and that they should change their minds. And they kept casting out many demons, and kept anointing with oil many who were without strength, and kept healing them."},{"c1":6,"v1":14,"c2":6,"v2":14,"label":"Mark 6:14","text":"Matthew and Luke both call Herod \"tetrarch,\" his technical title as one of four regional rulers; Mark, writing for a Roman audience where the term was applied loosely to any eastern potentate, calls him \"king\" — not an error, just a difference in audience. \"Shew forth\" translates energeō, the verb for energy actively at work; \"mighty works\" is again dunamis. Herod's theory — that John, raised from the dead, was now channeling a supernatural power he never displayed in life — reads less like careful theology and more like the guesswork of a guilty conscience straining to explain something it can't simply dismiss.","translation":"And King Herod heard of it, for his name had become well known; and he kept saying, John the Baptizer has been raised from the dead, and because of this these powers are actively at work in him."},{"c1":6,"v1":15,"c2":6,"v2":15,"label":"Mark 6:15","text":"The other theories in circulation — that Jesus was Elijah, or a prophet cut from the same cloth as the old prophets — show people groping for a category large enough to hold what they were seeing. Every explanation reached backward, into the past, because nothing in anyone's present experience offered a live comparison. Nobody guessed the truth: they were out of categories, not out of ideas.","translation":"But others kept saying, It is Elijah. And others kept saying, A prophet, like one of the prophets of old."},{"c1":6,"v1":16,"c2":6,"v2":16,"label":"Mark 6:16","text":"Herod's verdict lands with the emphasis Greek word order reserves for the front of a sentence: \"the one whom I beheaded\" comes first, ahead of John's own name — the detail Herod cannot get past. The pronoun \"I\" is stated outright rather than left buried in the verb ending, and the demonstrative \"this one\" adds further weight: Herod isn't hedging, he's certain. \"Said\" is imperfect — he kept repeating the same conclusion every time a fresh theory was floated past him, a man talking himself back into a verdict his conscience had already reached on its own. Mark underscores that it was Herod himself, no proxy, who had ordered John bound — the definite article and an intensive pronoun both drive the point home — because John had told him plainly that his marriage to his brother's wife broke the law.","translation":"But Herod, hearing of it, kept repeating, John, whom I myself beheaded — this one has been raised."},{"c1":6,"v1":19,"c2":6,"v2":19,"label":"Mark 6:19","text":"\"Had a quarrel against him\" translates enechō, a verb for settled, simmering hostility — not a passing irritation but a grudge nursed and kept alive. The tense is imperfect: Herodias's anger toward John never let up; she was simply waiting for her opening. \"She would have killed him, but she could not\" states the situation exactly as it stood — the desire was fully present, only the opportunity was missing.","translation":"And Herodias kept a grudge against him and kept wanting to kill him, but she was not able."},{"c1":6,"v1":20,"c2":6,"v2":20,"label":"Mark 6:20","text":"\"Observed\" undersells the verb, suntēreō, which means to actively guard and preserve — Herod was keeping John safe, standing between him and Herodias's designs, not merely watching him with curiosity. The imperfect tense shows this protection held steady over time. The line usually read \"he did many things\" rests on a textual mix-up; the better reading gives a verb meaning to be at a loss, without resources, unable to find a way through — Herod, torn between a guilty conscience and John's uncompromising preaching, was left perplexed rather than decisive. And \"heard him gladly\" is likewise imperfect: repeated visits to the prison, a man drawn back again and again to a message that unsettled him every single time.","translation":"For Herod kept fearing John, knowing him to be a righteous and holy man, and he kept guarding him safe. And when he heard him, he was thrown into great perplexity; and he kept hearing him gladly."},{"c1":6,"v1":21,"c2":6,"v2":21,"label":"Mark 6:21","text":"\"Convenient\" translates eukairos — built from kairos, a critical, decisive point in time, plus \"good\": a strategically opportune moment, not simply a free afternoon. Herodias had been waiting for exactly this kind of occasion. The birthday banquet gathered Herod's leading men, his military commanders over units of a thousand, and the chief men of Galilee — a room full of witnesses whose presence would make backing out of a rash promise socially ruinous for Herod, which is precisely the leverage Herodias intended to use.","translation":"And a strategically opportune day having come, when Herod on his birthday made a supper for his great men, and the commanders of his thousands, and the chief men of Galilee."},{"c1":6,"v1":22,"c2":6,"v2":22,"label":"Mark 6:22","text":"The Greek marks out \"the daughter of Herodias\" with special emphasis — not just any girl, but Herodias's own daughter, performing a dance of a kind normally reserved for professional entertainers of disreputable standing. That a princess would degrade herself this way before the assembled court was itself part of how the trap worked. Herod's reply — \"ask of me whatever you want\" — uses thelō, desire rooted in feeling rather than sober judgment: a man carried away by the moment, making a rash promise before he has weighed what it might cost him.","translation":"And the daughter of Herodias herself came in and danced, and pleased Herod and those reclining with him. And the king said to the young girl, Ask of me whatever you desire, and I will give it to you."},{"c1":6,"v1":23,"c2":6,"v2":23,"label":"Mark 6:23","text":"\"He sware\" translates omnuō, the verb for formally placing oneself under oath — Herod doesn't just make a promise here, he binds himself to it publicly, raising the stakes of whatever comes next.","translation":"And he swore to her, binding himself with an oath, Whatever you ask me, I will give it to you, up to half of my kingdom."},{"c1":6,"v1":24,"c2":6,"v2":24,"label":"Mark 6:24","text":"\"What shall I ask?\" uses a middle-voice construction that marks the girl as acting in her own interest — she goes to consult her mother expecting some prize of her own choosing for herself. What she gets instead is her mother's long-nursed request, handed straight back to her to carry out.","translation":"And she went out and said to her mother, What shall I ask for myself? And she said, The head of John the Baptizer."},{"c1":6,"v1":25,"c2":6,"v2":25,"label":"Mark 6:25","text":"\"With haste\" and \"straightway\" both press the same point: she wastes no time returning to the king, before the mood of the evening could shift or Herod could reconsider. Exautēs, rendered \"by and by\" in older English, actually means \"this instant\" — she wants her request granted on the spot, not deferred. The demand itself is phrased with a bluntness that manages to sound even more merciless than the mother who sent her — John's head, at once, on a platter — details calculated to leave Herod no room to soften what he had promised.","translation":"And coming in at once with haste to the king, she asked, saying, I want you to give me this instant, on a platter, the head of John the Baptizer."},{"c1":6,"v1":26,"c2":6,"v2":26,"label":"Mark 6:26","text":"\"Exceedingly sorry\" is built from a participle marking entrance into a new state — not \"was sorrowful\" as an ongoing condition, but \"having become sorrowful\" at that instant, the moment the request landed. Herod is genuinely shaken. But what decides the outcome isn't his conscience — it's his oath and the roomful of guests who heard him make it. He would rather kill a man he feared and respected than lose face in front of his own court.","translation":"And the king, having become deeply grieved, yet because of his oaths and those reclining with him, was not willing to refuse her."},{"c1":6,"v1":27,"c2":6,"v2":29,"label":"Mark 6:27–29","text":"\"Executioner\" is spekoulatōr, a Latin military term imported wholesale into Greek — the word behind our \"speculator.\" It named a member of an imperial bodyguard, the kind of armed escort Roman emperors kept close, and by extension came to carry the sense of a hired killer. Herod's household apes the trappings of Roman court life right down to keeping his own company of these guardsmen on hand — one of whom he now sends, without ceremony, to carry out an execution inside the very prison where John had been kept safe until that day.","translation":"And immediately the king, sending out a soldier of his bodyguard, commanded his head to be brought. And going, he beheaded him in the prison, and brought his head on a platter, and gave it to the young girl; and the young girl gave it to her mother."},{"c1":6,"v1":30,"c2":6,"v2":30,"label":"Mark 6:30","text":"\"Apostles\" here is not yet the fixed, technical title it would later become — it simply describes men sent out on a commission to represent the one who sent them, now returning to report on it. They gather back around Jesus and give their account of everything they had done and taught on the road.","translation":"And the sent-out ones gather themselves together to Jesus, and reported to him all things, both what they had done and what they had taught."},{"c1":6,"v1":31,"c2":6,"v2":31,"label":"Mark 6:31","text":"\"Come ye yourselves apart\" piles up an emphasis in the Greek that flattens out in English translation — the pronoun for \"you\" doesn't need to be there grammatically at all, since the verb ending already carries the person, so its presence throws weight onto the disciples specifically: these very men, worn out from the mission they've just completed. \"Apart\" is kat' idian, the language of privacy — what they need is escape from the crowd pressing on them, not simply rest in the abstract. \"Desert place\" is erēmos, meaning uninhabited, not sandy — any quiet, unpopulated spot would serve. Jesus is as careful of His workers' need for recovery as He is of anyone's need for ministry; He takes His own rest seriously enough to insist His disciples take theirs too. But the crowd outruns the boat, converging from every town along the shore faster than a small vessel can outpace a determined crowd circling the lake on foot — proof of how little privacy this particular deserted place was about to offer.","translation":"And he said to them, Come, you yourselves, apart into a deserted place by yourselves, and rest a while. For those coming and going were many, and they did not even have opportunity to eat."},{"c1":6,"v1":34,"c2":6,"v2":34,"label":"Mark 6:34","text":"The construction behind \"began to teach\" marks continuous, ongoing action — Jesus doesn't merely start teaching, He keeps at it hour after hour, despite having come ashore looking for exactly the rest this crowd is now denying Him. \"Moved with compassion\" is one of the rare places where the traditional English earns its keep — it captures a gut-level, visceral response to a crowd Jesus sees as sheep without a shepherd, unprotected and without direction. By the time evening presses in and the disciples suggest sending everyone off to the surrounding farms and villages to buy food, the day has been running for hours on nothing but that compassion.","translation":"And coming out, he saw a great crowd, and he was moved in his inward parts with compassion for them, because they were as sheep not having a shepherd; and he kept on teaching them many things."},{"c1":6,"v1":37,"c2":6,"v2":37,"label":"Mark 6:37","text":"\"Give ye them to eat\" throws heavy emphasis onto \"ye\" — as for you, you feed them — a pointed challenge to disciples who have just spent a whole tour exercising delegated authority over demons and disease, and are now stumped by a logistics problem. Their reply calculates the cost in denarii, the standard day's wage: roughly two hundred days' pay, a figure probably named because it was close to what the Twelve actually had between them — offered less as a genuine plan than as proof of how impossible the request sounded to them.","translation":"But he answered and said to them, As for you, you give them something to eat. And they say to him, Shall we go and buy two hundred denarii worth of bread and give it to them to eat?"},{"c1":6,"v1":38,"c2":6,"v2":38,"label":"Mark 6:38","text":"The command to have the crowd \"sit down\" uses anaklinō, the verb for reclining at a banquet table, not simply taking a seat — the crowd is arranged as if for a formal meal, propped on one elbow on the grass the way a guest would recline on a couch. \"By companies\" translates a Greek idiom formed by doubling the word for a dinner-party — group by group, table-party by table-party — and \"in ranks\" doubles the word for a garden bed — plot by plot. Set side by side, the two phrases paint one picture: block after block of people in their bright, ordinary clothing, arranged across the green Passover-season grass so that it looked, from a distance, like a field broken into flowerbeds.","translation":"And he commanded them all to recline, group by group, table-party by table-party, upon the green grass. And they reclined in groups, plot by plot, by hundreds, and by fifties."},{"c1":6,"v1":41,"c2":6,"v2":41,"label":"Mark 6:41","text":"\"Brake\" and \"gave\" sit in two different tenses in the Greek — the breaking is aorist, a single decisive act, while the giving is imperfect, a continuous, repeated action unfolding again and again. The multiplication itself seems to have happened in that gap: not in one dramatic flash, but somewhere in the ongoing motion of Jesus's hands passing bread to the disciples repeatedly, more coming each time than the last handful could have accounted for.","translation":"And taking the five loaves and the two fish, and looking up to heaven, he blessed and broke the loaves, and kept giving them to his disciples to set before the people; and he divided the two fish among them all."},{"c1":6,"v1":42,"c2":6,"v2":44,"label":"Mark 6:42–44","text":"\"Basket\" is kophinos, the wicker hand-basket a Jewish traveler typically carried — a small, personal container, not a hamper. \"Five thousand men\" uses anēr, the word specifically for adult males, not the generic term that would include women and children — meaning the actual crowd fed that day was considerably larger than the headline number suggests.","translation":"And they all ate and were filled. And they took up twelve small wicker baskets full of fragments, and also of the fish. And those who ate the loaves were five thousand adult men."},{"c1":6,"v1":45,"c2":6,"v2":45,"label":"Mark 6:45","text":"Jesus \"compels\" His disciples onto the boat — a stronger word than merely asking them to go — while He stays behind to dismiss the crowd Himself, sending the Twelve on ahead across the lake toward Bethsaida rather than have them wait on Him. Whatever charged mood the crowd had worked itself into by then, Jesus wanted His disciples clear of it before He dealt with it alone.","translation":"And immediately he compelled his disciples to get into the boat and go before him to the other side, to Bethsaida, while he himself was dismissing the crowd."},{"c1":6,"v1":46,"c2":6,"v2":46,"label":"Mark 6:46","text":"\"Sent them away\" is apotassō, a verb of formal leave-taking, a deliberate goodbye rather than a casual dismissal. \"To pray\" is proseuchomai, built on a preposition meaning \"toward\" or \"facing\" — prayer conceived as a direct approach into Someone's presence, not words simply spoken into the air. Jesus goes up the mountain alone, consciously seeking His Father's face after a day that had cost Him everything He had to give.","translation":"And having said farewell to them, he went off into the mountain to pray, facing toward God."},{"c1":6,"v1":47,"c2":6,"v2":47,"label":"Mark 6:47","text":"Evening has fully come — the later of the two evenings the Jewish day recognized, somewhere around sunset — and the picture the verse leaves is a stark one: the disciples are out in the middle of the lake, and Jesus is entirely alone on the shore, having apparently stayed on the beach for hours after leaving the mountain, still praying, still waiting.","translation":"And when evening had come, the boat was in the middle of the sea, and he was alone on the land."},{"c1":6,"v1":48,"c2":6,"v2":48,"label":"Mark 6:48","text":"\"Toiling in rowing\" translates basanizō, a word that elsewhere means \"to torture\" — the disciples aren't merely working hard at the oars, they're being tormented by a headwind that won't let up. \"Walking on the sea\" uses a preposition of contact: His feet are actually touching the water's surface, the way ours touch pavement. And the phrase usually rendered \"would have passed by them\" is better read the other way — verb and preposition together mean \"to come alongside,\" not \"to go past\": Jesus is heading out into that wind to reach them, not to walk by and leave them to it.","translation":"And seeing them being continually tormented in their rowing, for the wind was against them, about the fourth watch of the night he comes toward them, walking upon the sea, and he was wishing to come alongside them."},{"c1":6,"v1":49,"c2":6,"v2":49,"label":"Mark 6:49","text":"The word for what the disciples think they're seeing is phantasma, not the ordinary word for a departed spirit — phantasma carries associations of magic and sorcery, an apparition conjured up rather than a ghost returned. A man walking on water read to them as witchcraft, not as a soul come back from the dead. \"Cried out\" is a guttural verb, the kind used for a scream torn straight from the throat — sheer, wordless terror.","translation":"But they, seeing him walking upon the sea, supposed that it was an apparition, a thing conjured up, and they cried out with a scream torn from the throat."},{"c1":6,"v1":50,"c2":6,"v2":50,"label":"Mark 6:50","text":"\"Were afraid\" translates tarassō, inward agitation, the mind thrown into turmoil rather than simple fright. \"Be of good cheer\" is tharseō, courage summoned in the face of danger. \"It is I\" carries an emphatic pronoun in the Greek — not just \"it's me\" but \"it is I, and no one else.\" And \"be not afraid\" uses a present-tense prohibition, the grammar for stopping an action already underway: not \"don't start being afraid\" but \"stop being afraid\" — they already were.","translation":"For they all saw him and were thrown into inward turmoil. And immediately he spoke with them, and says to them, Take courage; it is I myself; stop being afraid."},{"c1":6,"v1":51,"c2":6,"v2":51,"label":"Mark 6:51","text":"\"Ceased\" translates kopazō, a verb that pictures the wind growing weary and giving out — the storm doesn't so much stop as run out of energy, not so much halted as spent, the way a runner's stride slows on its own once the drive behind it gives out.","translation":"And he went up to them into the boat, and the wind grew weary and spent itself."},{"c1":6,"v1":52,"c2":6,"v2":52,"label":"Mark 6:52","text":"\"Considered not\" is suniēmi, literally to put things together in the mind — to reason from one fact to the next. The disciples had just watched five loaves feed a multitude and should have reasoned from that miracle straight to this one: the same supernatural power that multiplied bread in Jesus's hands could just as easily quiet a headwind and hold a body up on open water. They didn't make the connection. \"Hardened\" is a perfect-tense passive, describing not a moment of dullness but a settled condition — their hearts weren't merely slow that night, they were, by that point, already callous.","translation":"For they had not put together in their minds the lesson of the loaves; instead their heart had been, and remained, hardened."},{"c1":6,"v1":53,"c2":6,"v2":53,"label":"Mark 6:53","text":"\"Drew to the shore\" is a nautical term for bringing a vessel to its moorings — anchoring, not simply beaching it, which suggests a boat too substantial to run straight up onto the sand. \"Knew him\" uses a verb for recognition built on prior acquaintance — these were people who had seen Jesus before and knew exactly who was stepping off that boat. What follows is frantic: crowds running the whole circuit of the region, carrying their sick from place to place on pallets, chasing down every report of where He'd gone next — an imperfect tense picturing this scrambling search going on continuously, town after town.","translation":"And when they had crossed over, they came to land at Gennesaret and drew the boat to its moorings. And when they had come out of the boat, immediately the people, recognizing him, kept running about that whole region, and began to carry those who were sick on their pallets to wherever they heard he was."},{"c1":6,"v1":56,"c2":6,"v2":56,"label":"Mark 6:56","text":"\"Border\" is kraspedon, the tassel or fringe Jewish law required on the corner of a man's outer garment, meant as a standing visual reminder of the commandments — the same fringe a woman with a chronic hemorrhage had already reached for earlier in this Gospel. Here whole crowds beg simply to touch it, not because there was anything magical in the cloth itself, but because it was the nearest edge of Him they could reach — and everyone who touched it was healed.","translation":"And wherever he entered, into villages, or cities, or the countryside, they kept laying the sick in the marketplaces, and kept begging him that they might touch even the fringe of his garment; and as many as touched it were made whole."},{"c1":7,"v1":2,"c2":7,"v2":2,"label":"Mark 7:2","text":"\"Defiled\" translates koinos, a word that simply meant \"common\" — shared by everybody — before it narrowed in later Greek to mean profane, the opposite of what is set apart as sacred. Applied to hands, it meant ceremonially unclean, not physically dirty; the disciples weren't accused of poor hygiene but of skipping a ritual. The \"bread\" in the Greek is plural and carries the definite article — the loaves, specific ones the Pharisees and Jesus both had in mind, almost certainly leftovers from the feeding of the five thousand still sitting in the baskets. There had been no real opportunity to wash; the Pharisees weren't concerned with that, only with the broken ritual.","translation":"And seeing some of his disciples eating the loaves with common, that is, unwashed, hands, they found fault."},{"c1":7,"v1":3,"c2":7,"v2":3,"label":"Mark 7:3","text":"\"Oft\" is actually pugmē, \"with the fist\" — the verb is middle voice, meaning the Pharisees washed their own hands rather than delegating it to a servant, scrubbing one closed fist against the palm and up the forearm of the other hand to be sure everything that might touch food was ritually clean. \"Tradition\" is paradosis, built on the verb \"to hand over, to deliver into someone's keeping\" — instructions passed down and expected to be kept by each new generation. \"Holding\" translates krateō, which carries the sense of a tight, deliberate grip, not casual observance. And \"the elders\" here doesn't mean the aged in general but a specific class — men of rank, originally drawn from the Sanhedrin — whose oral rulings had, by this point, taken on an authority the Pharisees treated as equal to Scripture.","translation":"For the Pharisees, and all the Jews, except they wash their hands with the fist, do not eat, holding fast, with a tight grip, the tradition handed down from the elders."},{"c1":7,"v1":4,"c2":7,"v2":4,"label":"Mark 7:4","text":"The \"market\" is the agora, the public square where a town's business and gossip both happened — contact with the crowd there was assumed to leave a Jew ceremonially defiled, hence the washing before eating. Two different verbs sit behind the single English word \"wash\" in this passage: niptō, ordinary washing of hands, and baptizō, full immersion, used here of dipping cups and vessels. The \"pots\" are xestēs, a term borrowed from the Latin sextarius, a pint measure — a small detail that shows how deep into the specifics of daily life this scribal system reached, regulating not just people but the exact vessels they ate and drank from.","translation":"And coming from the marketplace, except they immerse themselves, they do not eat. And there are many other things which they have received to hold fast: the immersing of cups, and pitchers, and bronze vessels, and couches."},{"c1":7,"v1":5,"c2":7,"v2":5,"label":"Mark 7:5","text":"Verses 3 and 4 are Mark's own explanatory aside for readers unfamiliar with Jewish custom; verse 5 picks the story back up exactly where verse 2 left off, with the Pharisees and scribes voicing the complaint out loud. Their question is pointed: not \"why don't your disciples wash\" but \"why don't they live according to what has been handed down\" — the tradition itself, not hygiene, is the real issue being pressed.","translation":"Then the Pharisees and the scribes ask him, Why do your disciples not walk according to the tradition handed down by the elders, but eat bread with unwashed, common hands?"},{"c1":7,"v1":6,"c2":7,"v2":6,"label":"Mark 7:6","text":"\"Well\" is kalōs, \"excellently, finely\" — the same word Jesus reaches for again in verse 9, but there it turns to open sarcasm; here it's a straight compliment to Isaiah's accuracy. \"Hypocrites\" comes from hupokritēs, built on \"to judge\" with the prefix \"under\" — originally the word for a stage actor, someone who performed from behind a mask, playing a part rather than being it. \"As it is written\" renders a perfect tense: not merely \"was written once\" but \"stands written, and stands on the record still.\" And the phrase \"is far from me\" uses a verb built from \"to hold\" plus \"away from\" — the heart isn't passively distant here, it's actively holding itself off, at a chosen remove.","translation":"And he said to them, Isaiah excellently prophesied concerning you hypocrites, stage-players behind a mask, as it stands written, and stands written still: This people honors me with their lips, but their heart keeps itself held off, far away from me."},{"c1":7,"v1":7,"c2":7,"v2":7,"label":"Mark 7:7","text":"\"In vain\" is matēn, \"fruitlessly\" — the failure isn't in the sincerity of the worship but in what it accomplishes; effort spent on the wrong object produces nothing, however earnest it feels. \"Worship\" itself, sebomai, is a strong word, carrying real reverence and awe — Isaiah isn't accusing this people of insincerity, only of misdirection. \"Doctrines\" and \"commandments\" are both words for formal, authoritative teaching — precepts handed down and enjoined as binding — which is exactly the problem: human rulings dressed in the authority that belongs only to God's word.","translation":"But fruitlessly do they worship me, teaching as their teachings the commandments of men."},{"c1":7,"v1":8,"c2":7,"v2":8,"label":"Mark 7:8","text":"\"Laying aside\" translates aphiēmi, a word used elsewhere of a speaker or writer setting a subject down in order to move on to something else — deliberately leaving it behind, done with. That's the picture: the Pharisees haven't merely neglected God's commandment, they've set it aside on purpose to make room for something else. That something else they \"hold\" — krateō again, the same tight grip from verse 3 — showing where their real zeal actually lay. The irony is exact: total abandonment of the divine command, matched by white-knuckled devotion to the human one.","translation":"Leaving behind, and done with, the commandment of God, you hold fast, with a tight grip, the tradition of men."},{"c1":7,"v1":9,"c2":7,"v2":9,"label":"Mark 7:9","text":"\"Full well\" repeats kalōs from verse 6, but the tone has flipped into open sarcasm — \"beautifully\" doing bitter work here. \"Reject\" is atheteō, built from the word for something formally \"laid down\" or enacted, with a prefix that negates it — literally \"to un-lay,\" to strip a decree of its force. It's the vocabulary of nullification, not mere disagreement: Jesus isn't saying the Pharisees dislike God's commandment, he's saying their system actively voids it, cancels its effect, so their own tradition can stand in its place.","translation":"And he kept saying to them, Beautifully do you nullify, and strip of its force, the commandment of God, that you may keep your own tradition."},{"c1":7,"v1":10,"c2":7,"v2":10,"label":"Mark 7:10","text":"\"Honour\" is timaō, related to timē, \"a valuation, a price\" — to honor a parent is to assess them at their true worth and treat them accordingly, not simply to be polite. \"Curseth\" is a weaker word than it sounds — kakologeō, \"to speak evil of, to revile\" — ongoing verbal contempt, not the invoking of a divine curse, which is a different word entirely elsewhere in the New Testament. \"Let him die the death\" is emphatic idiom carried straight into Greek: come to his end by death — the full weight of the Mosaic penalty, reserved for habitual, unrepentant abuse, not a single sharp word spoken in anger.","translation":"For Moses said, Honor your father and your mother, valuing them at their true worth; and, Whoever speaks evil of father or mother, let him come to his end by death."},{"c1":7,"v1":11,"c2":7,"v2":13,"label":"Mark 7:11-13","text":"\"Corban\" is a transliterated Hebrew term Mark bothers to explain for his Gentile readers — a formal designation of something as a gift devoted to God. The scribal loophole was simple: a son could declare the money owed to his aging parents \"Corban,\" and by that one word of dedication keep it for himself while looking pious rather than negligent — technically unable to give it away now that it belonged to God, in practice free to spend it however he liked. \"Making of none effect\" is akuroō, \"to strip of legal force\" — the same root idea as verse 9's atheteō, but sharper, almost a legal term: the tradition doesn't just override God's command, it invalidates it. And \"tradition\" and \"delivered\" share the same root verb, \"to hand over\" — the Pharisees weren't only receiving this system, they were actively passing it on, adding their own weight to its authority with each transmission.","translation":"But you say, If a man says to his father or mother, Whatever you might have been profited by me is Corban, that is to say, given to God — you no longer permit him to do anything for his father or his mother, stripping the word of God of its force by your tradition, which you have handed over, and go on handing over."},{"c1":7,"v1":14,"c2":7,"v2":14,"label":"Mark 7:14","text":"Jesus \"calls the people to him again\" — until now the crowd seems to have hung back respectfully while the religious leaders pressed their case, but Jesus deliberately brings them back into the conversation. He isn't finished with a private theological dispute; he wants the ordinary people to see plainly what their teachers' hair-splitting actually amounts to.","translation":"And calling the crowd to himself again, he kept saying to them, Listen to me, all of you, and understand."},{"c1":7,"v1":15,"c2":7,"v2":15,"label":"Mark 7:15","text":"\"Defile\" is koinoō, the verb form of the koinos from verse 2 — \"to make common, to profane.\" Jesus draws the line exactly opposite to where the Pharisees drew it: what a man eats, even with unwashed hands, cannot touch what he is on the inside — food travels one direction, through the body and out again, external to internal never crossing the line that actually matters. What comes from within — a man's own thoughts and choices — is the only thing capable of making him common, unholy, unfit.","translation":"There is nothing outside a man which, going into him, is able to make him common; but the things coming out from within a man are the things that make the man common."},{"c1":7,"v1":16,"c2":7,"v2":16,"label":"Mark 7:16","text":"This verse doesn't appear in the earliest manuscripts behind the modern critical text. It reads like a later addition, echoing the refrain from 4:9 and 4:23 to round the saying off, rather than something Mark originally wrote here.","translation":"If any man has ears to hear, let him keep on hearing."},{"c1":7,"v1":17,"c2":7,"v2":19,"label":"Mark 7:17-19","text":"The disciples' question comes fast — the verb is an inceptive imperfect, \"they began asking,\" fired off the moment they were alone with him in the house, before the crowd's dust had even settled. Jesus' reply is pointed: \"are you also without understanding\" groups the Twelve with the very crowd they'd just left behind, still thinking in categories of ceremonial defilement. He spells out the mechanics almost clinically — koilia, the stomach and bowels, is where food goes, and the word behind \"draught\" was plain classical Greek for a latrine; none of that route passes through the heart, which is the only place real defilement could register. Many take the aside \"thus purging all meats\" to be Mark's own editorial comment rather than Jesus' spoken words — his explanation, in hindsight, of where this teaching was heading: the abolition of the whole clean/unclean food system, the very point Peter would later need a rooftop vision to fully absorb.","translation":"And when he had entered the house, away from the crowd, his disciples began asking him about the parable. And he says to them, Are even you also still without understanding? Do you not perceive that whatever from outside enters into a man is not able to make him common, because it does not enter into his heart, but into the stomach and bowels, and goes out into the latrine? Thus he declared all foods clean."},{"c1":7,"v1":20,"c2":7,"v2":23,"label":"Mark 7:20-23","text":"\"Evil thoughts\" pairs a word carrying an undertone of internal debate or suspicion with kakos, \"wickedness\" in its most basic, almost neutral sense — simply not what it ought to be. The list that follows shifts to a stronger word, ponēria, wickedness actively engaged, and the difference matters: a kakos man is content to ruin himself; a ponēros man isn't satisfied until he's dragged others down with him. \"Lasciviousness\" is aselgeia, broader than mere sexual excess — a general contempt for restraint, insolence that no longer bothers to hide itself. The \"evil eye\" is a single phrase for a deliberately malicious, mischief-working glance, and \"pride\" is built from \"to show oneself above\" — the picture of a man holding his head literally higher than the people around him. Stacking up thirteen items this way, Jesus makes a cumulative point: none of it arrives from outside a man and lodges in him; all of it was already native to the heart, merely waiting for occasion.","translation":"And he kept saying, That which comes out of the man, that is what makes the man common. For from within, out of the heart of men, evil reasonings keep coming out: fornications, thefts, murders, adulteries, covetous desires, wicked schemes, deceit, unrestrained excess, a mischief-working eye, slander, a lifting of oneself above others, foolishness. All these evil things come from within, and keep making the man common."},{"c1":7,"v1":24,"c2":7,"v2":24,"label":"Mark 7:24","text":"The little conjunction usually rendered \"and\" reads better here as adversative — \"but\" — marking a real turn in the story: Jesus leaves Jewish Galilee, where he's just clashed with the religious leadership, and crosses into pagan Phoenicia. The preposition behind \"into the borders of Tyre\" doesn't mean he merely stepped over a boundary line; it signals real depth of entry, well into the heart of the region, not a quick visit to its edge. He wanted privacy there, rest after a punishing stretch of ministry and conflict — and didn't get it.","translation":"But rising up from there, he went off into the region of Tyre, entering deep into it; and going into a house, he wanted no one to know it, and he was not able to escape notice."},{"c1":7,"v1":25,"c2":7,"v2":25,"label":"Mark 7:25","text":"\"Immediately\" doesn't mean the woman heard about Jesus the instant he crossed into the country — it means that as soon as word reached her, she wasted no time getting to him. Mark's word for her daughter is a diminutive, \"little daughter\" — a small, affectionate touch that colors the whole scene with a mother's urgency before a word of dialogue is even spoken.","translation":"But immediately a woman, whose little daughter had an unclean spirit, hearing about him, came and fell down at his feet."},{"c1":7,"v1":26,"c2":7,"v2":26,"label":"Mark 7:26","text":"Mark stacks up three separate identifiers for this woman — Greek by religion (meaning simply Gentile, not ethnically Greek), Syrian by language, Phoenician by race — making sure no reader mistakes her for anything but a complete outsider to Israel's covenant. \"Besought\" is an imperfect tense, describing not a single request but a woman who kept on asking, refusing to let the conversation end; the verb itself carries the sense of an urgent appeal rather than a passing question. \"Cast out,\" by contrast, is an aorist infinitive — she wanted the thing done decisively, in one stroke, not as a process.","translation":"Now the woman was a Greek, a Syrophoenician by race; and she kept begging him earnestly that he would cast the demon out of her daughter, once and for all, decisively."},{"c1":7,"v1":27,"c2":7,"v2":27,"label":"Mark 7:27","text":"The everyday Greek word for \"dog\" — the term Jews commonly used with contempt for Gentiles — is not the word Jesus uses here. He chooses kunarion, \"little dog,\" the diminutive: a household pet under the table, not a scavenger in the street. The distinction matters: Jesus isn't insulting her, he's stating an order of operations — his mission was first to Israel, and only through that chosen channel out to the nations — using the gentlest version of a harsh cultural metaphor to make the point without the sting it would otherwise carry.","translation":"And he kept saying to her, Let the children first be filled, for it is not proper to take the children's bread and throw it to the little dogs under the table."},{"c1":7,"v1":28,"c2":7,"v2":28,"label":"Mark 7:28","text":"The woman answers in kind, picking up his own diminutives and adding more of her own — little dogs, little children, little crumbs — an answer built entirely out of the smallness he offered her, turned into an argument: even the little dogs under the table share what falls from the children's meal. \"Lord\" here is an ordinary term for master, someone with authority to dispose of a thing as he sees fit — not, on this Gentile woman's lips, a confession of Jesus' deity, simply the natural address to a man she's asking a favor of.","translation":"But she answered and says to him, Yes, Master, yet even the little dogs under the table eat of the little children's little crumbs."},{"c1":7,"v1":29,"c2":7,"v2":29,"label":"Mark 7:29","text":"\"For this saying\" is literally \"because of this word\" — her answer itself, not merely her faith in the abstract, is what Jesus credits. \"Is gone out\" is a perfect tense: not \"the demon left\" as a past event only, but \"the demon has gone, and stays gone\" — Mark's grammar itself promises a cure that will hold.","translation":"And he said to her, Because of this word, go your way; the demon has gone out of your daughter, and remains gone."},{"c1":7,"v1":30,"c2":7,"v2":30,"label":"Mark 7:30","text":"The perfect tense repeats here for the same reason — permanence, not just a past exit. And the detail of the child \"laid upon the bed,\" resting quietly rather than thrashing or crying out, is Mark's visual confirmation of the same fact the grammar already states: this deliverance was complete, not partial or temporary.","translation":"And going off to her house, she found the little child laid upon the bed, resting quietly, and the demon gone out of her, and remaining gone."},{"c1":7,"v1":31,"c2":7,"v2":31,"label":"Mark 7:31","text":"The route Mark traces is a wide loop, not a straight line — north through Sidon before doubling back southeast around the Sea of Galilee to reach Decapolis on its far shore. It's a detail worth noticing on a map: Jesus' movement here wasn't a quick detour but an extended, deliberate swing through Gentile territory before he returns to Jewish soil.","translation":"And again going out from the region of Tyre, he came through Sidon to the Sea of Galilee, through the midst of the region of the Ten Cities, having made a wide circuit."},{"c1":7,"v1":32,"c2":7,"v2":32,"label":"Mark 7:32","text":"\"An impediment in his speech\" translates a single compound word, built from \"with difficulty\" and \"speaking\" — this man wasn't mute, he could produce sound, just not clearly. The distinction matters for what follows: Jesus doesn't give speech to someone who had none, he corrects a mechanism that was already partly working.","translation":"And they bring to him a deaf man, one who could speak only with great difficulty; and they beg him to lay his hand upon him."},{"c1":7,"v1":33,"c2":7,"v2":33,"label":"Mark 7:33","text":"\"Aside from the multitude\" is a phrase built on the word for privacy — Jesus deliberately isolates this healing from public view, likely because he had no interest in cultivating a reputation as a wonder-worker that would crowd out his teaching. \"Put his fingers into his ears\" uses a verb that ordinarily means \"to throw\" or \"thrust\" — a vivid, almost forceful gesture, not a gentle touch, chosen precisely because a deaf man couldn't be spoken to and needed something physical to fix his attention and draw out his faith.","translation":"And taking him aside from the crowd privately, he thrust his fingers into his ears, and spitting, he touched his tongue."},{"c1":7,"v1":34,"c2":7,"v2":34,"label":"Mark 7:34","text":"\"He sighed\" is a word for groaning, real emotional weight rather than a formality attached to prayer — Jesus feels the burden of this man's condition even as he moves to heal it. \"Ephphatha\" is Aramaic, and Mark, writing for readers who wouldn't know the language, translates it on the spot: \"be opened.\"","translation":"And looking up to heaven, he groaned deeply, and says to him, Ephphatha, that is, Be opened."},{"c1":7,"v1":35,"c2":7,"v2":35,"label":"Mark 7:35","text":"\"His ears were opened\" puts the Greek word for hearing itself as the object, not merely an anatomical part. \"The string of his tongue\" is literally \"that which bound it,\" loosed the moment his hearing returned. The verb behind \"spake plain\" is an inchoative imperfect — \"he began to be speaking\" — catching the process right as it started, a man discovering mid-sentence that the words were finally coming out right.","translation":"And immediately his hearing was opened, and the bond of his tongue was loosed, and he began speaking, and kept on speaking, plainly."},{"c1":7,"v1":36,"c2":7,"v2":36,"label":"Mark 7:36","text":"\"He charged them\" is diastellō, a verb that originally meant simply \"to separate\" or \"mark off,\" and came to mean \"give a strict, particular order\" — used here in the middle voice, which suggests Jesus had a personal stake in the silence he was requesting, not just procedural caution. The Greek behind \"so much the more\" stacks two comparative words on top of each other for emphasis — the harder he pressed for silence, the louder and wider the news spread anyway.","translation":"And he charged them, for his own sake, that they should tell no one; but the more he kept charging them, all the more abundantly, beyond all measure, they kept proclaiming it."},{"c1":7,"v1":37,"c2":7,"v2":37,"label":"Mark 7:37","text":"\"Beyond measure astonished\" likewise stacks two intensifying words, \"superabundantly\" plus \"above\" — description straining at its own limits to capture a crowd nearly knocked out of their composure. The verb behind their reaction, in fact, originally meant exactly that: to strike someone out of their senses. \"He hath done all things well\" is a perfect tense — not a passing impression but a settled verdict the crowd has arrived at and isn't backing away from.","translation":"And they were astonished, superabundantly, beyond all measure, struck clean out of their senses, saying, He has done, and stands having done, all things well; he even makes the deaf to hear and the mute to speak."},{"c1":8,"v1":4,"c2":8,"v2":4,"label":"Mark 8:4","text":"The disciples' question, “From whence?” translates *pothen* (πόθεν), an adverb regularly used of the source of a food supply — closer to “how could anyone manage to…” than a plain “from where.” Their word for “satisfy,” *chortazō* (χορτάζω), is telling: in ordinary Greek usage it was the word for filling livestock with fodder — hay, grass, feed — and only later got stretched, sometimes with a faint sneer, to cover filling human stomachs. The disciples are thinking in barnyard terms: how do you fill this many mouths out here? “The wilderness” is *erēmia* (ἐρημία), an uninhabited waste with no markets and no houses — and remarkably, this is the second time Jesus has faced this exact problem with a crowd this size; the memory of the five thousand seems to have evaporated completely.\n\nThe Greek of the verses that follow also carries a nuance English flattens out. Jesus “kept giving” the broken bread to the disciples so that they “might keep on setting it forth” — continuous, repeated action, not a single hand-off but an ongoing relay between His hands and theirs as the crowd was fed.","translation":"And his disciples answered him, From where will anyone be able here, in this wasteland with no houses, to fill these people with bread, as one fills cattle with fodder? And he kept giving the loaves to his disciples so that they might keep on setting them before the people."},{"c1":8,"v1":7,"c2":8,"v2":7,"label":"Mark 8:7","text":"When Jesus “blessed” the small fish (*eulogeō*, εὐλογέω), He was doing what any Jew did over a meal — asking God to set His blessing on the food for its intended use. Grace before meals traces straight back to this moment. The “broken pieces” left over are *klasma* (κλάσμα), a word for substantial remnants rather than crumbs — these were sizable leftover portions, not scraps swept off the table. (The KJV's “meat” here simply meant “food” in 1611 English and isn't in the Greek at all; the translators supplied it to smooth the sentence.)\n\nThe location, Dalmanutha, is otherwise unknown, but the region was Decapolis — Gentile territory the Pharisees normally avoided as unclean. That they crossed into it to confront Jesus signals how determined the opposition had become. “Began to question” renders a present-tense verb of continuous action built on *sunzēteō* (συνζητέω), “to examine together, to dispute” — not a single query but a running interrogation that escalated into open argument, with Jesus Himself grammatically marked as an active party rather than merely a target. Their demand was for a *sēmeion* (σημεῖον), a “sign” understood as a certifying credential — proof that the one performing it truly spoke for God. And “tempting” Him is *peirazomai* (πειράζομαι), a word whose root sense is neutral testing — putting something to the proof to see what's really there — before it darkened elsewhere into “tempting to evil.” Here it keeps the neutral sense: they were testing whether His claims held up, not baiting Him to sin.","translation":"And he also blessed the few small fish, asking God's blessing upon them. And they took up what was left over of the broken pieces, seven large baskets full. And the Pharisees came out and began to dispute with him, examining the matter together with him, seeking from him a sign, a certifying proof from heaven, putting him to the test."},{"c1":8,"v1":12,"c2":8,"v2":12,"label":"Mark 8:12","text":"“He sighed deeply in His spirit” intensifies a simple verb for groaning (*stenazō*, στενάζω) with a prefix that pushes it further — not a passing sigh but a groan from the depths, His inner being visibly disturbed. There's real reason for it: this isn't the flat unbelief of an ordinary skeptic but calculated hostility from men who had watched Him work miracles and had already begun explaining them away as demonic. The refusal that follows, “there shall no sign be given,” is built on a Hebrew oath formula — literally “if a sign shall be given,” the truncated form of a self-curse (“may such-and-such happen to me if…”). It isn't a mild decline; it's about as absolute a no as the language allows. “Generation” translates *genea* (γενεά), literally “those of the same stock” — sometimes covering the whole nation, but the context narrows it here to this particular breed of hardened religious leadership.\n\nIn the boat scene that follows, “they had forgotten” uses a verb (*lanthanō*, λανθάνω) carrying a prefix that intensifies it to “completely forgotten” — not an oversight but a total blank. And “He left them” translates *aphiēmi* (ἀφίημι), a verb regularly used of a speaker or teacher breaking off a discussion and walking away from it — Jesus doesn't argue the point further; He simply terminates the exchange.","translation":"And groaning deeply from within his spirit, he says, Why does this generation, this breed, keep seeking a sign? Truly I say to you, if a sign shall be given to this generation — let it be so to me if it is. And they had completely forgotten to take bread. And leaving them, breaking off the exchange, he got into the boat again and went away to the other side."},{"c1":8,"v1":15,"c2":8,"v2":15,"label":"Mark 8:15","text":"“Charged” is an imperfect — Jesus kept repeating the warning, which suggests how badly it was needed. The two verbs for vigilance work as a pair: *horaō* (ὁράω), “take heed,” is rooted in coming to know something by experience, engaging the discerning mind; *blepō* (βλέπω), “beware,” is rooted in ordinary sight but used metaphorically for mental perception — seeing with the mind's eye. Both are present imperatives, commands not for a single glance but for an ongoing, habitual watchfulness. “Leaven” (*zumē*, ζύμη) runs through Scripture as a stock image for corruption — fermentation was viewed as decay working through a batch of dough, so the image consistently stands for evil spreading quietly through something. Here the leaven in view is doctrinal — the Pharisees' externalized religion and the Herodians' worldliness, two different corruptions the disciples are told to keep sniffing out.","translation":"And he kept charging them, saying, Keep on looking, and keep on watching closely, for the leaven of the Pharisees and the leaven of Herod."},{"c1":8,"v1":16,"c2":8,"v2":16,"label":"Mark 8:16","text":"“Reasoned among themselves” is *dialogizomai* (διαλογίζομαι), a word for turning an argument over, weighing reasons back and forth — and it's imperfect tense, so this wasn't a quick exchange but a discussion the disciples kept circling back to. They've entirely missed the point: Jesus has just warned them about doctrine, and they're still stuck on the fact that nobody remembered to pack lunch.","translation":"And they kept discussing and turning it over among themselves, saying, It is because we have no bread."},{"c1":8,"v1":17,"c2":8,"v2":21,"label":"Mark 8:17–21","text":"The word for “disciples” (*mathētēs*, μαθητής) simply means learners, pupils — and Jesus's volley of questions here reads like a teacher exasperated at pupils who still haven't grasped the lesson. The Greek syntax behind “why reason ye, because ye have no bread” isn't a yes-or-no question; it's Jesus quoting their own logic back at them — “why are you reasoning like this: we have no bread?” — as if to say, that's not even the right conversation. “Have ye your heart hardened” uses a perfect participle, the tense for a process completed in the past whose results persist into the present: not “are you being a little slow right now” but “you have arrived at, and now stand in, a settled condition of hardness.” “He said” in this passage is imperfect too — He kept saying it, circling back, which suggests something closer to anguish than simple correction. His reminder of the two feedings, with their two different counts of leftover baskets, presses the point home: they have the evidence sitting in their own memory and still aren't connecting it.","translation":"And knowing it, Jesus says to them, Why are you reasoning like this: We have no bread? Do you not yet perceive nor understand? Have you your heart in a hardened condition, hardened once and now standing so? Having eyes, do you not see? And having ears, do you not hear? And do you not remember? And he kept saying to them, Do you not yet understand?"},{"c1":8,"v1":26,"c2":8,"v2":26,"label":"Mark 8:26","text":"The healing of the blind man at Bethsaida is the only miracle in the Gospels performed in two visible stages, and the Greek marks each stage with a different compound of the verb “to see” (*blepō*, βλέπω): first *anablepō*, “looked up” — the tentative upward squint of someone whose sight is only starting to work; then *diablepō*, “looked through,” as if peering through fog; finally *emblepō*, “looked into,” seeing with the sharp, exact focus of someone tracing the outline of something near at hand. “Clearly,” at the end, is built from the words for “far” and “radiance” — seeing things distinctly even at a distance, full sight restored.\n\nJesus then sends the man straight home rather than back into the village, avoiding the stir a public healing would draw — the same instinct shows up in His choice of destination next: “the villages of Caesarea Philippi” (Mark's word is *kōmē*, small village, not the city itself), well outside Herod Antipas's reach, where He could finally get His disciples alone with barely six months left before the cross. His question there, “whom do men say that I am,” is asked in the imperfect — He kept putting it to them, pressing for a real answer rather than settling for the first thing offered.","translation":"And he looked up and said, I see men, but as trees, walking. Then again he put his hands upon his eyes, and he looked intently through, and was restored, and saw all things clearly, distinctly, even things far off."},{"c1":8,"v1":29,"c2":8,"v2":29,"label":"Mark 8:29","text":"Jesus's question carries a double emphasis in the Greek that flattens out in English: the verb itself (*eperōtaō*, ἐπερωτάω, imperfect — “kept questioning”) already implies persistence, and Mark adds the personal pronoun redundantly for stress — “He Himself kept asking.” The follow-up, “but who do you say,” does the same with the pronoun for “you”: “as for you, in contrast to everyone else's guesses…” Peter's answer gets identical emphatic treatment: “as for you, you are the Christ.” *Christos* (Χριστός) simply means “anointed one,” the exact equivalent of the Hebrew word behind “Messiah” — the king from David's line whom Israel's Scriptures promised, the very figure Psalm 2 pictures the nations conspiring against. Peter isn't offering a vague compliment; he's naming Jesus as that specific, promised King.","translation":"And he himself kept asking them, But as for you — who do you say that I am? And Peter answering says to him, As for you, you are the Christ, the Anointed One, the Messiah."},{"c1":8,"v1":30,"c2":8,"v2":30,"label":"Mark 8:30","text":"“Charged” is *epitimaō* (ἐπιτιμάω), a verb with real force behind it — a sharp, even stern warning, not a casual request. The prohibition is specific: not silence about Jesus in general, but silence about this particular identification, Messiah. The timing wasn't right yet; that announcement would wait for the entry into Jerusalem still to come.","translation":"And he sternly warned them that they should tell no one about him, that he was the Christ."},{"c1":8,"v1":31,"c2":8,"v2":31,"label":"Mark 8:31","text":"“Must” translates *dei* (δεῖ), “it is necessary in the nature of the case” — not fate imposing itself from outside but necessity built into who God is: because He is just, sin's penalty has to be paid; because He is love, He provides the payment Himself. The cross follows necessarily from both. “Rejected” is *apodokimazō* (ἀποδοκιμάζω), a compound built on a verb meaning “to test someone against a standard in order to approve them,” with a prefix that turns it into “reject after testing and finding the person doesn't meet the standard.” That's precisely what the religious leaders did: they measured Jesus against their own specifications for Messiah — a political liberator — and He failed their test, not because He fell short but because their specifications were wrong. Mark also puts the definite article in front of each group named — “the elders, the chief priests, the scribes” — treating them as three distinct parties, each carrying its own share of the responsibility rather than one faceless committee.","translation":"And he began to teach them that it is necessary, in the nature of the case, for the Son of Man to suffer many things, and to be tested and rejected by the elders, and the chief priests, and the scribes, and to be killed, and after three days to rise again."},{"c1":8,"v1":32,"c2":8,"v2":32,"label":"Mark 8:32","text":"“Spake that saying” is imperfect — Jesus didn't drop this in one line and move on; He kept unpacking it in detail. “Openly” translates *parrēsia* (παρρησία), plain and unmistakable speech, deliberately set against the veiled, riddling way He had sometimes spoken earlier — there was no ambiguity left for anyone to hide behind. “Peter took him” uses a verb built to mean taking someone by the hand so as to draw them off to the side, facing you — a private, physical gesture, pulling Jesus away from the others. And “began to rebuke” is the same verb (*epitimaō*) used two verses earlier for Jesus's own stern charge to the disciples — a word that can describe a rebuke whether or not it's deserved. Here it's entirely undeserved: Peter is correcting his Master.","translation":"And he kept speaking the word plainly and openly. And Peter, taking him by the hand and drawing him aside to face him, began to rebuke him."},{"c1":8,"v1":33,"c2":8,"v2":33,"label":"Mark 8:33","text":"“Turned about” (*epistrephō*, ἐπιστρέφω) suggests a sharp, deliberate pivot — Jesus doesn't merely respond, He wheels around, and in doing so faces the rest of the disciples as well, which means Peter's rebuke had been audible to the group; the correction that follows is public precisely because the offense was witnessed. Mark uses the same verb for Jesus's response (*epitimaō*) that he used for Peter's rebuke of Jesus — pointedly not the stronger word available for a rebuke that actually convicts someone and brings them to repentance. Peter isn't yet grasping what he's done.\n\n“Get thee behind me, Satan” reaches back to the wilderness temptation, where Satan offered Jesus the kingdoms of the world in exchange for worship — the same bargain resurfaces here, a shortcut around the cross to power without suffering, and Jesus answers by naming the source directly, using Peter's own mouth as the Tempter's channel. “Savourest” translates *phroneō* (φρονέω), a word less about feeling and more about allegiance — to be aligned with a party, to have your mind set on something (Paul uses this same word in Philippians 2:5 for the mind believers are to have). Peter's fault wasn't a burst of emotion; his whole orientation, in that moment, was aimed at human categories instead of God's.","translation":"But he, wheeling sharply about and seeing his disciples, rebuked Peter, saying, Get behind me, Satan, for your mind is not set on the things of God, but on the things of men."},{"c1":8,"v1":34,"c2":8,"v2":34,"label":"Mark 8:34","text":"“Will come after me” uses *thelō* (θέλω), “desire” — the call isn't to accidental discipleship but to a deliberate wish: whoever desires it. “Come after” (*erchomai*) is the same verb Jesus uses elsewhere for people refusing to come to Him for life; here it means entering His fellowship as a learner. “Deny himself” is *aparneomai* (ἀπαρνέομαι) used reflexively — literally to lose sight of yourself and your own interests — and it stands in the aorist tense, the tense for a decisive, once-for-all entry into a new condition rather than a repeated struggle: a starting point, not a daily renegotiation. The cross was an instrument of execution — carrying one means walking toward death, here death to self.\n\n“Follow” (*akoloutheō*, ἀκολουθέω) means to take the same road as someone else, and the grammar pairs it with a case implying close accompaniment rather than trailing at a distance — walking with Christ, not just behind Him. And where the first two commands are aorist (a decision made once), “follow” shifts to the present imperative — continuous, day-by-day action. The pattern is a single decisive surrender followed by a lifetime of walking it out.","translation":"And calling the crowd to himself with his disciples, he said to them, If anyone desires, of his own deliberate will, to come after me, let him once and for all lose sight of himself and his own interests, and let him take up his cross, and let him keep on following with me, walking the same road at my side, day after day."},{"c1":8,"v1":35,"c2":8,"v2":37,"label":"Mark 8:35–37","text":"“Life” here is not *bios* (βίος), one's physical existence, but *psuchē* (ψυχή) — the soul, the seat of will, thought, and feeling, the whole inner personality with its hopes and drives. Jesus isn't talking about biological survival; He's saying that grasping at self-fulfillment on your own terms is exactly what forfeits it, while surrendering that self-direction to Him is what finally satisfies it — a statement about how a human being is built to function, not a set of terms for earning salvation (self-denial saves no one; only the cross does that). “World” translates *kosmos* (κόσμος), not the physical planet but the whole organized system of life lived apart from God — its values, its pursuits, its priorities. And “lose” (*zēmioō*, ζημιόω) is a word for sustaining real damage or forfeiting something of value, not simple disappearance: trading your soul for the *kosmos* isn't a fair exchange, it's a net loss no accumulation of the world can offset.","translation":"For whoever desires to save his soul shall forfeit it, sustaining real loss; but whoever forfeits his soul for my sake and the gospel's shall save it. For what does it profit a man to gain the whole system of the world, and to sustain the loss and forfeiture of his soul? For what should a man give in exchange for his soul?"},{"c1":8,"v1":38,"c2":8,"v2":38,"label":"Mark 8:38","text":"“Shall be ashamed” can be read as describing a present stance rather than only a future one — whoever is ashamed of Christ now, in this life, determines by that very attitude how Christ will regard him later. The “coming” in view is the visible return to earth in glory, attended by holy angels, not a hidden gathering of believers — open, public vindication answering open, public shame. And “generation” again carries the narrower sense it's had throughout this chapter — not humanity in general but this particular breed, the “adulterous and sinful” stock refusing to receive Him.","translation":"For whoever is ashamed of me and my words in this adulterous and sinful generation, this breed, of him also shall the Son of Man be ashamed, when he comes in the glory of his Father with the holy angels."},{"c1":9,"v1":4,"c2":9,"v2":4,"label":"Mark 9:4","text":"\"Were talking with him\" is a periphrastic construction — the verb \"to be\" in the imperfect plus a present participle — a combination Greek uses to stretch out an action and hold it before the reader. Mark isn't describing a brief exchange; Moses and Elijah were carrying on an extended conversation with Jesus, and the disciples watched it unfold.\n\nPeter's interruption is worth noticing closely. \"Answered\" translates apokrinomai, built from krinō, \"to judge, to weigh and discriminate between things\" — the compound means literally \"to give off a judgment from oneself.\" Nobody had asked Peter anything; he simply inserts his own verdict into a scene he doesn't understand. He addresses Jesus as Rabbei, a transliterated Hebrew title meaning \"my great one\" — the honorific Jewish disciples used for their teachers. And when he calls the moment \"good,\" he reaches for kalos rather than agathos — not moral goodness but the sense of something fitting, impressive, pleasing to behold. His proposal, \"let us make three tabernacles,\" is a hortatory subjunctive — \"let's,\" not \"allow us\" — and the word for tabernacle, skēnē, just means a simple shelter of branches or hides, nothing like Israel's wilderness Tabernacle. Mark's own explanation cuts through any impression that Peter had a plan: he didn't know what to say, because he and the others were ekphobos, thrown into violent terror. This was babble born of fright, not insight.","translation":"And Elijah appeared to them with Moses, and they were continuing to talk with Jesus. And Peter broke in and said to Jesus, Rabbi, it is a fine thing for us to be here; let us put up three shelters — one for you, one for Moses, one for Elijah. For he did not know what to say, since they were terrified."},{"c1":9,"v1":7,"c2":9,"v2":7,"label":"Mark 9:7","text":"\"There came\" is egeneto, an ingressive aorist of \"to become\" — the tense Greek uses for a sudden crossing into a new state. The cloud didn't drift in; it happened, abruptly. And it's the right kind of cloud: nephelē, a cloud with defined edges and shape, not nephos, the shapeless haze that simply blots out the sky. This is the same category of cloud as the pillar that led Israel through the wilderness and the glory that hovered over the mercy seat — a visible sign of God's presence, not weather. \"Overshadowed\" (episkiazō) ordinarily means casting a shadow, but it's also used of a luminous cloud wrapping people in brightness rather than darkness, which fits a moment of glory rather than gloom.\n\n\"Beloved\" comes from agapaō, love drawn out by the worth of the one loved, not mere fondness — \"the dearly-beloved One\" catches it better than \"my beloved Son\" does. And \"hear him\" is a present imperative: not one act of listening but a standing command to keep on hearing him, in the sense that matters most in Greek — hearing that turns into obeying, not just registering sound.","translation":"And suddenly a cloud formed, wrapping them in its brightness; and a voice came out of the cloud: This is my dearly-beloved Son: keep on hearing him."},{"c1":9,"v1":8,"c2":9,"v2":8,"label":"Mark 9:8","text":"\"Suddenly\" (exapina) occurs nowhere else in the New Testament, and it's worth noting what it actually modifies: not the disciples' act of looking around, but what they discovered when they did. The abruptness belongs to the vanishing, not the glance — one moment the mountain was crowded with glory, the next it held only Jesus.","translation":"And suddenly, having looked around, they no longer saw anyone, except Jesus only, with themselves."},{"c1":9,"v1":9,"c2":9,"v2":9,"label":"Mark 9:9","text":"\"Tell\" renders a compound built from \"through\" and \"to lead the way\" — the sense is \"lead someone through a sequence of events,\" essentially \"narrate,\" not just \"mention.\" Jesus is forbidding a story, not a fact. And \"were risen\" is not the perfect tense one might expect (\"has risen, with abiding results\") but an aorist subjunctive — closer to \"until the Son of Man should rise,\" a single future event held out as the boundary on their silence, not yet an accomplished one.","translation":"And as they were coming down from the mountain, he charged them to narrate to no one what they had seen, until the Son of Man should rise from among the dead."},{"c1":9,"v1":10,"c2":9,"v2":10,"label":"Mark 9:10","text":"\"Kept\" translates krateō, a strong verb for holding something with a firm grip — they didn't merely refrain from repeating what they'd seen, they clamped down on it. \"Questioning one with another\" is a compound of \"seek\" and \"with\" — literally \"seeking together,\" the picture of several minds worrying over the same puzzle rather than idle conversation. The puzzle itself was pointed: what could \"rising from the dead\" mean, when it presupposed the death Jesus had just predicted and Peter had so recently resisted? They weren't debating resurrection in the abstract; they were circling a prediction they didn't want to be true.","translation":"And they clamped down on the matter, puzzling together among themselves what this rising from among the dead could mean."},{"c1":9,"v1":11,"c2":9,"v2":11,"label":"Mark 9:11","text":"\"Asked\" is imperfect — not a single question but a running one, the disciples pressing the point repeatedly. \"Must\" translates dei, \"it is necessary in the nature of the case\" — not merely expected, but built into how things have to go. \"Verily\" (mēn, used here without its usual counterpart de) affirms flatly: Elijah does come first, as the scribes teach, and does restore all things.\n\nThe force of Jesus' reply is in the collision he sets up: if Elijah's restoring work has to precede Messiah, and yet Messiah is himself going to suffer and be treated as nothing, then what has actually become of Elijah's role? Jesus answers by identifying John the Baptist's ministry with it — not a literal return of the ninth-century prophet, but a coming in his spirit and power, preparing the nation the way Elijah's future appearance will prepare it again before the second coming. And John's fate — rejected, then killed at the request of a vengeful woman — previews Elijah's own coming ordeal, and the Son of Man's.","translation":"And they kept asking him, Why do the scribes say that Elijah must first come? And he said to them, Elijah indeed, coming first, restores all things; and how is it written of the Son of Man, that he should suffer many things and be treated as nothing? But I tell you, Elijah has indeed come, and they did to him whatever they willed, just as it is written of him."},{"c1":9,"v1":14,"c2":9,"v2":14,"label":"Mark 9:14","text":"The scene Mark paints is deliberately awkward: the nine disciples left behind have just failed to cast out a demon, and the scribes — delighted at the opening — are pressing the failure home in front of a growing crowd. The same root that described the disciples \"seeking together\" over Jesus' words in verse 10 now describes something closer to wrangling: honest inquiry has curdled into public dispute, with the disciples reduced to whatever defense they can manage.","translation":"And coming to the disciples, they saw a great crowd around them, and scribes wrangling with them."},{"c1":9,"v1":15,"c2":9,"v2":15,"label":"Mark 9:15","text":"\"Greatly amazed\" is ekthaumazō, the simple verb \"to be amazed\" intensified by a prefix meaning \"out\" — the picture is of astonishment so complete it exhausts itself, leaving nothing more to draw on. Mark reserves this compound for exactly three scenes: this one, Gethsemane, and the empty tomb — each a moment when reality suddenly outran expectation. Here it's the shock of Jesus reappearing at the very moment his name was being invoked, apparently in vain, by his own disciples.\n\n\"Ran to him and saluted him\" also carries more weight than it looks. A Jewish greeting wasn't a wave — it typically involved embracing, a kiss, and the exchange of \"peace to you,\" enough of a production that Jesus elsewhere tells messengers in a hurry to skip it entirely. That the crowd broke into a run to give him this full greeting says something about how badly they wanted him back.","translation":"And immediately all the crowd, on seeing him, were utterly amazed, and running up to him kept greeting him, embracing him, saying, Peace to you."},{"c1":9,"v1":16,"c2":9,"v2":16,"label":"Mark 9:16","text":"The father's description of his son is clinical and painful. \"A dumb spirit\" uses alalon, \"one who does not speak\" — a word about the sound, not the meaning, so the point is the boy literally cannot make speech sounds; the demon has seized control of his vocal organs. \"Taketh him\" is katalambanō, \"to lay hold of so as to make one's own, to seize\" — Greek writers used this same word for the onset of a seizure, and it's the source of our word \"catalepsy.\" From there the symptoms pile up in quick, ugly verbs: rhēgnymi, \"to convulse,\" for the tearing; trizō, properly \"to squeak or shriek,\" applied to teeth grinding; xērainō, \"to dry up, wither,\" for the boy going rigid in a motionless stupor at the end of each episode. Against all that, the disciples simply \"did not have the power\" — ischuō, the word for the strength it takes to perform an extraordinary feat. They didn't lack technique; they lacked power.","translation":"And he answered him, Teacher, I brought to you my son, who has a spirit that will not let him speak. And wherever it seizes him, it convulses him completely, and he foams and grinds his teeth and grows rigid. And I spoke to your disciples, that they should cast it out, and they did not have the strength."},{"c1":9,"v1":19,"c2":9,"v2":19,"label":"Mark 9:19","text":"\"Faithless\" (apistos) doesn't carry the modern sense of \"treacherous\" — it means simply \"without faith, unbelieving,\" and Jesus' rebuke lands on the disciples along with everyone else; they of all people should have had it. \"How long shall I be with you\" uses pros plus the accusative, a construction that means \"facing\" and implies ongoing relationship, not bare physical presence — closer to \"how long must I remain in fellowship with you like this.\" \"Suffer\" is anechō, \"to hold oneself firm against\" something, i.e., \"put up with, endure.\" And the command \"bring him\" is a present imperative, durative in force — \"keep bringing him,\" which tells us the boy wasn't standing right there but was still being brought from some distance off.","translation":"He answered them, O unbelieving generation, how long must I remain in fellowship with you? How long must I put up with you? Keep bringing him to me."},{"c1":9,"v1":20,"c2":9,"v2":20,"label":"Mark 9:20","text":"The demon, sensing its time was up, made its last stand vivid: \"tare him\" is a compound meaning \"to convulse completely,\" and \"wallowed\" (kyliō, \"to be rolled\") is imperfect — the rolling on the ground went on for a stretch, not a single spasm, and the participle \"foaming\" is equally continuous.\n\nThe father's account of the boy being repeatedly \"cast into the fire and into the waters\" uses ballō, \"to throw\" — in his eyes these weren't accidents but a pattern, the demon driving the boy toward self-destruction. His hedge, \"if thou canst do anything,\" rests on dynamai, \"to have power\" — the verb behind dynamis, the New Testament's word for power that overcomes resistance, elsewhere used of the gospel itself. His faith had been bruised by the disciples' failure and by the sheer severity of the case. And the Greek order of his appeal reverses the English: the request for help comes first, as an aorist imperative — \"help us now\" — with \"having had compassion\" trailing as a participle whose action is assumed already complete. He isn't asking whether Jesus is compassionate; that he takes for granted. His doubt is entirely about ability, and his plural \"us\" quietly makes the boy's suffering his own.","translation":"And they brought him to him. And when the spirit saw him, immediately it convulsed him completely; and falling to the ground, he kept rolling and foaming at the mouth. And Jesus asked his father, How long a time has this been happening to him? And he said, From childhood; and often it has thrown him both into fire and into waters, to destroy him. But if you can do anything, having compassion on us — help us."},{"c1":9,"v1":23,"c2":9,"v2":23,"label":"Mark 9:23","text":"The best manuscripts drop \"believe\" from the father's plea, leaving Jesus simply echoing back \"if thou canst\" — and the Greek marks this off almost like a set of quotation marks, a definite article placed before the phrase to hold the father's own words up for inspection. There's a wordplay underneath: \"canst\" (dynēi) and \"possible\" (dynata) share the same root. Jesus takes the father's tentative \"anything\" and answers it with the Son of God's \"all things\": ability was never the question — the man's own \"if you can\" is thrown back at him, and the real condition is planted on faith, not on Jesus' power.","translation":"And Jesus said to him, As for your 'if you can' — all things are possible to the one who believes."},{"c1":9,"v1":24,"c2":9,"v2":24,"label":"Mark 9:24","text":"\"Cried out, and said\" separates two distinct acts — first an inarticulate cry torn out of him, then the words themselves, \"Lord, I believe.\" \"Help\" is boētheō, literally \"to run at a cry for help\" — the image is of someone rushing toward a person in danger. In verse 22 the same verb appeared as an aorist imperative, one decisive rescue; here it shifts to a present imperative, continuous force — \"keep on helping my unbelief.\" The father has stopped asking only for his son and started asking for himself: he already believes, but his belief is thin, and he wants it reinforced. \"Unbelief\" (apistia) is better read here as \"weakness of faith\" than as its total absence — this is a small, genuine faith asking to be made strong, not a faithless man pretending otherwise.","translation":"Immediately the father of the boy cried out, and said, I believe; keep on helping my weakness of faith."},{"c1":9,"v1":25,"c2":9,"v2":25,"label":"Mark 9:25","text":"\"Came running together\" is a triple compound — \"run\" plus \"with\" plus \"upon\" — painting a crowd converging on the scene from every direction at once, which is why the private exchange between Jesus and the father breaks off. \"Rebuked\" (epitimaō) is a notably different word from the New Testament's other rebuke-word, one that describes a correction leading the offender to conviction and confession. That word is never used of demons; the one used here carries no such expectation, because demons never repent. \"Foul\" (akathartos) is built by placing a negating prefix in front of katharos, \"clean, pure\" — the plainest possible way in Greek to say \"not-clean.\" And \"I charge thee\" uses epitassō, a word with military roots — originally \"to arrange troops in ranks\" — so the order lands with a soldier's sharpness; the personal pronoun \"I\" is spelled out for emphasis, and Jesus adds \"never enter him again\" because this particular possession had been recurring.\n\nAfterward, indoors, the disciples ask why they failed, and Jesus traces it to one thing: \"this kind\" — genos, a specific category or breed — \"comes out by nothing but prayer.\" The words \"and fasting\" are missing from the two oldest manuscripts, which points to the real diagnosis: their failure was prayerlessness, not a missing ritual.","translation":"When Jesus saw that a crowd was rushing together upon them, he rebuked the unclean spirit, saying to it, You dumb and deaf spirit, I myself charge you: come out of him, and never enter him again. And when they had come into the house, his disciples asked him privately, Why could we not cast it out? And he said to them, This kind of spirit can come out by nothing but prayer."},{"c1":9,"v1":30,"c2":9,"v2":32,"label":"Mark 9:30–32","text":"\"Is delivered\" translates paradidōmai — para, \"alongside,\" plus didōmi, \"to give,\" so \"to hand over,\" the standard word for betrayal. It's present tense, and durative: Jesus isn't predicting a future act so much as describing a betrayal already in motion, unfolding even as he speaks, with the betrayer presumably somewhere in the company he's addressing. The disciples' response is telling on its own terms — they didn't understand the saying, and they were afraid to ask. That's not simple confusion; it's the sound of men who suspect the answer and don't want it confirmed.","translation":"The Son of Man is being handed over into the hands of men, and they will kill him; and having been killed, after three days he will rise. But they did not understand the saying, and they were afraid to ask him."},{"c1":9,"v1":33,"c2":9,"v2":33,"label":"Mark 9:33","text":"Once inside, Jesus \"kept on asking\" — the imperfect tense — pressing a question he already half knows the answer to, since he'd evidently caught fragments of the argument on the road. The contrast Mark is building is plain enough without any Greek needed to see it: their teacher walking ahead absorbed in what's coming, the Twelve trailing behind absorbed in each other.","translation":"And being in the house, he kept on asking them, What were you discussing on the road among yourselves?"},{"c1":9,"v1":34,"c2":9,"v2":34,"label":"Mark 9:34","text":"\"They held their peace\" is, more literally, \"they kept on being silent\" — a sustained, awkward quiet, not a brief embarrassed pause. It's easy to see why: Jesus has just told them plainly that he's going to be killed, and their conversation on the road had been about which of them would rank highest in the kingdom. The silence is the sound of that gap becoming visible to them.","translation":"But they kept on being silent, for on the road they had been arguing among themselves who was the greatest."},{"c1":9,"v1":35,"c2":9,"v2":35,"label":"Mark 9:35","text":"The \"if\" in \"if any man desire to be first\" is the Greek construction for a condition already known to be true — Jesus isn't floating a hypothetical, he's naming what he already knows is in their hearts. His answer redefines the ambition rather than condemning it outright: greatness runs through service, and the word for \"servant\" here, diakonos, is the ordinary word for someone who waits on another's needs — the root behind our \"deacon.\"\n\nHe then takes a child into his arms and ties reception of the powerless to reception of himself, and reception of himself to reception of the Father who sent him — a chain that runs from the least significant person in the room straight up to God, collapsing the disciples' whole argument about rank.","translation":"And sitting down, he called the twelve and said to them, If anyone wants to be first, he shall be last of all and servant of all. And taking a child, he set it in their midst; and taking it in his arms, he said to them, Whoever receives one of such children in my name, receives me; and whoever receives me, receives not me, but him who sent me."},{"c1":9,"v1":38,"c2":9,"v2":38,"label":"Mark 9:38","text":"John's report doubles back on itself — \"he does not follow with us... because he was not following with us\" — the repetition betraying that the real objection wasn't theological but territorial: this man wasn't one of the Twelve. The imperfect \"we forbade\" shows it wasn't a single incident but an ongoing policy.\n\nJesus' correction is narrower than it might sound — he doesn't tell them to embrace the man, since his motives are unknown, only to stop hindering him. The logic is practical: someone invoking Jesus' name to perform a miracle is, for the moment at least, not in a position to turn around and speak against him. \"He that is not against us is for us\" sets the default at neutral-leaning-friendly rather than neutral-leaning-hostile — the opposite instinct from the one the disciples had just acted on.","translation":"John said to him, Teacher, we saw someone casting out demons in your name, and we kept forbidding him, because he was not walking the same road with us. But Jesus said, Do not keep forbidding him: for there is no one who shall do a work of power in my name, and be able soon after to speak evil of me. For whoever is not against us is for us."},{"c1":9,"v1":41,"c2":9,"v2":41,"label":"Mark 9:41","text":"The best text omits \"my\" before \"name\" — the reward is tied to giving a cup of water to someone simply \"because ye belong to Christ,\" not because the giver invoked a formula. And the promise is stated with a double negative in Greek, which intensifies rather than cancels — \"he shall in no wise lose his reward\" is about as emphatic a guarantee as the language allows, attached to the smallest possible act of kindness.","translation":"For whoever gives you a cup of water to drink because you belong to Christ — truly I tell you, he shall positively not lose his reward."},{"c1":9,"v1":42,"c2":9,"v2":42,"label":"Mark 9:42","text":"\"Offend\" translates skandalizō, built on skandalon — originally the trigger-stick of a trap, the part that springs and catches. The image is entirely mechanical: to \"offend\" someone here isn't to hurt their feelings but to set something in their path that trips them into a fall. \"Were cast\" is perfect tense, which stresses not the moment of throwing but the resulting condition — he goes down and stays down. Jesus is saying that outcome would be the better one, set against the alternative of causing one of \"these little ones\" to stumble.","translation":"And whoever sets a trap for one of these little ones who believe, it would be better for him to have been thrown into the sea with a great millstone hung about his neck, and to lie there sunk for good."},{"c1":9,"v1":43,"c2":9,"v2":43,"label":"Mark 9:43","text":"\"Maimed\" (kyllon) was the ordinary classical word for a crushed or crippled limb — Jesus isn't reaching for a soft euphemism. \"Life\" here is zōē used in its fuller sense, the life that's actually worth having, not simply a technical label for eternal existence.\n\n\"Hell\" transliterates Gehenna, which names an actual ravine outside Jerusalem — the site of pagan fire-worship under Ahaz, later desecrated by Josiah and turned into the city's garbage dump, where fires burned and worms worked continually through the refuse. That physical image is what stands behind its use as a picture of final judgment — the same place John's Revelation calls the lake of fire. \"Never shall be quenched\" is built the same way \"foul\" was in verse 25: a negating prefix (the source of our word \"asbestos\") attached to the verb \"to quench,\" producing a flat \"unquenchable.\"","translation":"And if your hand traps you into sin, cut it off: it is better for you to enter into life crippled, than having both hands to go into Gehenna, into the fire that shall never be quenched."},{"c1":9,"v1":45,"c2":9,"v2":45,"label":"Mark 9:45","text":"The English can be misread as though it's the two feet that get thrown into hell together; the Greek participle actually modifies the person, not the feet — it's you, having two feet, who would be cast in, not some detached pair of feet. Some manuscripts also drop the closing phrase about unquenchable fire here, since it's already stated fully at verse 48 and doesn't need repeating at every step of the sequence.","translation":"And if your foot traps you into sin, cut it off: it is better for you, having two feet, to be cast into Gehenna, than for you to enter life lame."},{"c1":9,"v1":47,"c2":9,"v2":47,"label":"Mark 9:47","text":"Here the destination shifts from \"life\" to \"the kingdom of God,\" essentially interchangeable ways of naming the same ultimate outcome. The eye is a fitting last item in this sequence — a love of visible beauty is a real and good thing in itself, but the same logic applies: whatever becomes, in a given person, the doorway to sin has to be renounced, even something as central to how we take in the world as sight itself. The escalation from hand to foot to eye tracks a person losing more and more of ordinary function, and still calls it the better bargain.","translation":"And if your eye traps you into sin, pluck it out: it is better for you to enter the kingdom of God with one eye, than having two eyes to be cast into Gehenna."},{"c1":9,"v1":48,"c2":9,"v2":48,"label":"Mark 9:48","text":"\"Their worm dieth not\" pictures ongoing consumption rather than a single blow — decay that never finishes its work, ruin without a floor to it. Verse 49's \"salted with fire\" then reaches back to that unquenchable fire and forward to the self-discipline of verse 50: everyone gets salted by one fire or another, either the fire of judgment or the fire of a disciplined life taken up voluntarily now.\n\nSalt that has lost its savor, Jesus says, cannot be restored — there's no reseasoning it, which is what gives \"have salt in yourselves\" its urgency: whatever preserving, purifying quality is meant to mark them has to be guarded, not assumed. And it's worth noticing where the chapter's argument ends up: \"be at peace with one another\" closes a section that opened with the Twelve arguing on the road about who ranked highest. The command to keep their savor and the command to stop competing land in the very same sentence.","translation":"Where their worm does not die, and the fire is not quenched. For everyone will be salted with fire. Salt is good; but if the salt becomes saltless, with what will you season it? Keep salt in yourselves, and be at peace with one another."},{"c1":10,"v1":2,"c2":10,"v2":2,"label":"Mark 10:2","text":"The Pharisees' question is a test, not a temptation to sin — the verb peirazō carries a broader, more basic sense than our English \"tempt\": it means to put something to the proof, to find out by pressure what is really there, whether strength or weakness, good or evil. Only because such tests usually turned up evil did the word pick up its narrower meaning of enticing someone toward wrongdoing. Here the Pharisees aren't hoping Jesus will do wrong; they're hoping to catch him committing to a position on divorce that will either embarrass him before the crowds or set him against Moses. \"Put away\" translates apoluō, literally \"to release\" — the standard legal term for issuing a divorce, a formal repudiation rather than a mere parting of ways.","translation":"And Pharisees came up and kept asking him, testing him, whether it is permitted for a man to release his wife."},{"c1":10,"v1":3,"c2":10,"v2":3,"label":"Mark 10:3","text":"Jesus answers a question with a question, and the word he chooses is deliberate: \"What did Moses command you?\" Not permit — command. The Pharisees are thinking of Deuteronomy 24, where Moses regulates an existing practice of divorce. Jesus redirects them to Genesis, where Moses recorded what God actually instituted as the ideal for marriage. The distinction between what the law commands and what it merely tolerates is the whole engine of his reply, and his choice of verb sets it running before he ever gets to their quotation.","translation":"And he answered and said to them, What did Moses command you?"},{"c1":10,"v1":4,"c2":10,"v2":4,"label":"Mark 10:4","text":"The Pharisees reach for Deuteronomy 24:1, and the \"bill\" they mention is a biblion — literally a \"little book,\" a small formal document, not an offhand word of dismissal. But the force of their answer lies in what the passage actually does: it's permissive language, written to regulate the fallout of a divorce already happening, not to institute or endorse the practice. Deuteronomy assumes divorce as a given and manages its consequences; it never commands it.","translation":"And they said, Moses permitted a man to write a certificate of divorce — a little book — and to release her."},{"c1":10,"v1":5,"c2":10,"v2":9,"label":"Mark 10:5–9","text":"\"Hardness of heart\" renders sklērokardia, a compound of sklēros — \"hard, harsh, stiff, unyielding\" — and kardia, \"heart.\" It describes a moral callus, an insensitivity built up over time until a person no longer feels the pull of what God is calling for. Moses' concession in Deuteronomy, Jesus says, was aimed at that condition, not at overturning what marriage was created to be.\n\nFor that, he goes back past Moses to creation. \"Leave\" is kataleipō, the simple verb for leaving intensified by the prefix kata — not a casual departure but a decisive breaking away. \"Cleave\" is proskollaō, literally \"to glue to\" — the image is of two surfaces bonded so they no longer come apart. The verb speaks not only of the moment of joining but of remaining joined afterward. And \"one flesh\" uses a construction that stresses the becoming rather than a static fact — two are pictured in the process of turning into one. When Jesus adds that God \"yoked\" them together (suzeugnumi, the same root behind Paul's word for \"yokefellow\" in Philippians 4:3), the picture is of two animals fastened under a single yoke to pull as one — no longer independent, but a working unit that only violence can pull apart.","translation":"But Jesus said to them, Because of your callused, unfeeling hearts he wrote you this commandment. But from the beginning of creation, God made them male and female. For this reason a man shall break away from his father and mother and shall be glued fast to his wife; and the two shall become one flesh: so that they are no longer two, but one flesh. What therefore God has yoked together, let not man separate."},{"c1":10,"v1":10,"c2":10,"v2":12,"label":"Mark 10:10–12","text":"The imperfect tense behind \"asked\" pictures the disciples pressing Jesus with question after question once they're alone with him — this wasn't a single query but a sustained interrogation. His answer extends the principle in both directions: not only the husband who divorces and remarries but the wife who does the same commits adultery. That even-handedness is worth noticing — the legal categories of the period didn't generally give a wife the standing to initiate divorce, so Jesus is stating a principle that reaches beyond the immediate assumptions of his hearers to the marriage bond itself.","translation":"And in the house the disciples kept questioning him again about this matter. And he said to them, Whoever releases his wife and marries another commits adultery against her; and if she herself releases her husband and marries another, she commits adultery."},{"c1":10,"v1":13,"c2":10,"v2":13,"label":"Mark 10:13","text":"\"Brought\" translates a compound verb, prospherō — pherō, \"to carry,\" with the preposition pros, \"to,\" attached — the same word regularly used for bringing an offering or sacrifice. Mark's choice of the compound rather than the simple verb elsewhere in his Gospel suggests these parents saw themselves as presenting their children, almost dedicating them, when they brought them to Jesus. The imperfect tense shows it happening again and again — a steady stream of children being carried up. \"Rebuked\" is epitimaō, paired here with a note of futility: the disciples kept trying to stop it, and it kept not working. Their objection accomplished nothing except revealing what they still didn't understand about the kingdom.","translation":"And they kept bringing children to him, presenting them almost as an offering, that he might touch them: and the disciples kept rebuking those who brought them."},{"c1":10,"v1":14,"c2":10,"v2":14,"label":"Mark 10:14","text":"\"He was much displeased\" undersells it. The verb is aganakteō, built from roots meaning \"to feel pain\" or \"to grieve\" — a strong word for genuine indignation, not mild annoyance. The tense marks the moment Jesus crosses into that state: he becomes angry, on the spot, watching his disciples turn away children. His command has two edges. \"Suffer\" (aphiēmi, \"permit, allow\") opens the door; \"forbid\" (kōluō) is a present-tense prohibition, which in Greek means not \"never do this\" but \"stop what you're already doing\" — Jesus is calling off an action already in progress, not warning against a hypothetical one.","translation":"But when Jesus saw it, he became indignant, and said to them, Permit the children to come to me; stop hindering them: for of such is the kingdom of God."},{"c1":10,"v1":15,"c2":10,"v2":15,"label":"Mark 10:15","text":"The Greek behind \"shall not enter\" stacks two negatives for emphasis — not a simple \"won't\" but something closer to \"will positively not, under any circumstance.\" And \"as\" (hōs) doesn't mean merely \"like\" in a loose sense; it specifies the manner — receive the kingdom in the same way, the same posture, that a small child receives whatever is given to it. The child isn't the model because of innocence but because of dependence: no leverage, no claim, only open hands.","translation":"Truly I tell you, whoever does not receive the kingdom of God in the very manner a little child receives it, shall positively not enter into it."},{"c1":10,"v1":16,"c2":10,"v2":16,"label":"Mark 10:16","text":"\"Blessed\" carries an intensive force in the Greek — not a formal word spoken once over the group but a fervent blessing given to each child individually, and the imperfect tense shows Jesus doing this again and again as child after child was brought to him. He goes further than what was asked: the parents wanted a touch, and Jesus lays his hands on them instead, the fuller gesture of benediction. There's no hurry in the scene, and no limit to his patience with it.","translation":"And taking them in his arms, he laid his hands on them, and kept fervently blessing them, one after another."},{"c1":10,"v1":17,"c2":10,"v2":17,"label":"Mark 10:17","text":"Luke calls the man who runs up an archōn — literally \"a first one,\" a term of rank used elsewhere for a synagogue official or even a member of the Sanhedrin. Matthew calls him a neaniskos, \"young man,\" a word flexible enough to cover anything from late boyhood to early middle age. Whatever his exact standing, the fact that he runs and kneels before Jesus is itself remarkable — this isn't a man seeking material help out of desperation, but someone humbling himself to ask a purely spiritual question. Even his opening address is telling: the Greek word order actually reverses the English, putting the emphasis on the adjective — \"Teacher — good one.\" He's paying Jesus a compliment he hasn't fully thought through.","translation":"And as he was going out on the road, a man ran up and knelt before him, and asked him, Teacher — good one — what shall I do to inherit eternal life?"},{"c1":10,"v1":18,"c2":10,"v2":18,"label":"Mark 10:18","text":"Jesus doesn't deflect the compliment; he interrogates it. \"Why callest thou me good?\" isn't a denial of his own goodness — it's a demand that the man examine the word he just used so casually. If \"good\" means anything, it means measuring up to God's own standard, and Jesus wants the man to feel the weight of that standard before he answers his question about eternal life. The rebuke isn't \"don't call me good\" — it's \"don't hand out that word without thinking about what it costs.\"","translation":"And Jesus said to him, Why do you call me good? No one is good except one — God."},{"c1":10,"v1":19,"c2":10,"v2":19,"label":"Mark 10:19","text":"Of all the commandments Jesus could cite, he lists only the ones governing how a person treats his neighbor — murder, adultery, theft, false witness, fraud, and honoring parents. These are precisely the commandments that feel easiest to claim compliance with; a person can plausibly say he's never murdered or stolen. Jesus starts there because that's exactly where the young man's confidence is about to be tested.","translation":"You know the commandments: Do not murder, Do not commit adultery, Do not steal, Do not bear false witness, Do not defraud, Honor your father and mother."},{"c1":10,"v1":20,"c2":10,"v2":20,"label":"Mark 10:20","text":"\"Observed\" translates phulassō, a word with a military background — it's the term for a sentry standing guard against attack. Applied to the commandments, it means more than technical obedience; it describes a vigilant, protective devotion to keeping them intact, guarding them the way a soldier guards a post. The young man isn't lying when he claims this. His confidence is genuine — which is exactly what makes what comes next so revealing.","translation":"And he said to him, Teacher, all these I have stood guard over from my youth."},{"c1":10,"v1":21,"c2":10,"v2":21,"label":"Mark 10:21","text":"\"Beholding him\" translates emblepō, a verb for fixing the eyes on someone — a deliberate, searching look, not a passing glance. What follows that look is agapaō, and the tense matters: this is the moment Jesus falls in love with him, an entrance into a new state rather than an ongoing condition. Jesus loves what he sees of genuine good in the man, even knowing what will stop him. The word for \"follow,\" akoloutheō, means to take the same road as someone else — not just to obey Jesus' instructions from a distance but to walk where he walks. That's the real ask beneath \"sell what you have\": not merely to relieve himself of wealth, but to become a companion on Jesus' road of self-denial, which was the necessary first step toward the faith that actually saves.","translation":"And Jesus, fixing his eyes on him, fell in love with him, and said to him, One thing you lack: go, sell whatever you have and give to the poor, and you shall have treasure in heaven; and come, take the same road with me."},{"c1":10,"v1":22,"c2":10,"v2":22,"label":"Mark 10:22","text":"\"Was sad\" is stugnazō, a word equally at home describing a storm-darkened sky and a clouded human face — the picture is of something visibly closing over, gathering weight. His expression changes in real time as the answer lands; this isn't quiet disappointment but a face that falls, unmistakably, in front of everyone. The wealth that had made him confident a moment earlier is now the very thing holding him back.","translation":"But his face clouded over at the word, and he went away grieving: for he was one who had great possessions."},{"c1":10,"v1":23,"c2":10,"v2":23,"label":"Mark 10:23","text":"\"How hardly\" is an exclamation, not a statement about the impossible — pōs duskolōs means \"how with difficulty,\" registering astonishment at the size of the obstacle rather than declaring it insurmountable. Jesus isn't saying wealth bars the door; he's saying it makes the door harder to find, because it gives a person so much else to trust in besides God.","translation":"And Jesus looked around and said to his disciples, How hard it is — with what difficulty! — for those who have riches to enter into the kingdom of God!"},{"c1":10,"v1":24,"c2":10,"v2":24,"label":"Mark 10:24","text":"Jesus addresses the startled disciples as teknon, \"children\" — an unusually tender address, softening a hard saying he's about to repeat rather than soften. Their confusion runs deep: in their world, wealth was a mark of God's favor, so a teaching that makes wealth an obstacle to the kingdom upends an assumption they've never had reason to question.","translation":"And the disciples were astonished at his words. But Jesus answered again and said to them, Children, how hard it is to enter into the kingdom of God!"},{"c1":10,"v1":25,"c2":10,"v2":25,"label":"Mark 10:25","text":"The image is a real sewing needle — the Greek word in Mark and Matthew is the ordinary needle used with thread, and Luke's version uses the term for a surgeon's needle. There's no evidence for the popular idea of a small \"needle gate\" in Jerusalem's wall that a camel could squeeze through with effort; that notion is a later invention. The comparison is meant to be flatly impossible, and it wasn't an unfamiliar kind of image — rabbinic literature has its own version of the same proverb, picturing an elephant forced through a needle's eye.\n\nThe disciples' shock is total: \"astonished out of measure\" translates ekplēssō, \"to be struck out of one's self-possession.\" Jesus' answer turns on a single preposition. \"With man it is impossible\" uses para, which pictures standing alongside someone, in their presence. Measured by what a person can do standing next to another person, salvation is out of reach. Measured standing in God's presence, it isn't — \"with God all things are possible.\" The same fact, entirely different depending on whose side you're measuring from.","translation":"It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a sewing needle, than for a rich man to enter into the kingdom of God. And they were struck clean out of themselves with astonishment, saying among themselves, Then who can be saved? Jesus, looking at them, said, Standing alongside man it is impossible, but not standing alongside God: for standing alongside God, all things are possible."},{"c1":10,"v1":28,"c2":10,"v2":28,"label":"Mark 10:28","text":"Peter's \"Lo\" is idou, a word meant to grab attention — \"look at this.\" \"Left\" is aphiēmi in the aorist, a decisive, once-for-all abandonment, and \"followed\" shifts to the perfect tense — a completed action whose results are still standing. Peter isn't describing an ongoing habit; he's reporting a permanent, irreversible decision already made. The question hiding inside his statement — what do we get for this? — shows the disciples still measuring the kingdom by what they've given up rather than what they've gained.","translation":"Peter began to say to him, Look — we have left all things behind, once for all, and we stand having followed you."},{"c1":10,"v1":29,"c2":10,"v2":31,"label":"Mark 10:29–31","text":"Jesus promises a return not just later but \"now, in this time\" — kairos, the word for a critical, hinge-point moment rather than an ordinary stretch of time, the kind of moment that divides one era of God's plan from the next. \"The age to come\" is a different word, aiōn, used for a whole span or dispensation — the Age of Law, the present Age of Grace, the Millennial Age still ahead — and here it points forward to that coming age, in contrast to the present, epoch-making kairos in which Jesus is speaking. The promised hundredfold return, in other words, isn't reserved for eternity — it starts now, in the very turning point Jesus and the disciples are standing in, though not without persecution alongside it.","translation":"Jesus said, Truly I tell you, there is no one who has left house or brothers or sisters or mother or father or children or lands, for my sake and the gospel's, who shall not receive a hundredfold now, in this present critical time — houses and brothers and sisters and mothers and children and lands, with persecutions — and in the age that is coming, life everlasting. But many who are first shall be last, and the last first."},{"c1":10,"v1":32,"c2":10,"v2":32,"label":"Mark 10:32","text":"The Greek makes clear that \"going up to Jerusalem\" describes Jesus and the Twelve, not the road itself — and it's a literal ascent: Jerusalem sits near the high point of the central ridge running the length of Palestine, so the city can't be approached from any direction without climbing. \"Jesus went before them\" uses a construction for habitual action, not a single moment of walking ahead — this was Jesus' repeated practice on this stretch of road, walking alone in front while the Twelve trailed behind, visibly unsettled by something in his bearing they couldn't yet name. \"Those who followed\" and were afraid refers not to the disciples but to the wider crowd of pilgrims traveling the same road toward the Passover — people with no inside knowledge of what Jesus had told the Twelve, picking up on the tension anyway.","translation":"And they were on the road, going up to Jerusalem; and Jesus kept walking on ahead of them: and they were amazed, and those who followed behind were afraid. And taking again the twelve, he began to tell them the things that were about to happen to him."},{"c1":10,"v1":35,"c2":10,"v2":37,"label":"Mark 10:35–37","text":"James and John lead with a blank check — \"Grant us whatever we ask\" — before naming the request, trying to bind Jesus to an answer before he even knows the question. The request itself, for the seats of highest honor, comes from men approaching Jesus as though he already reigns as king, able to hand out favor without limit. The timing makes the moment sting: Jesus has just set his face toward the cross, and his closest followers are jockeying for rank.","translation":"James and John came to him and said, Teacher, we want you to do for us whatever we ask. And he said to them, What do you want me to do for you? And they said to him, Grant us that we may sit, one on your right hand and one on your left, in your glory."},{"c1":10,"v1":38,"c2":10,"v2":38,"label":"Mark 10:38","text":"\"Ye know not what ye ask\" lands harder in the Greek than in English: the verb for \"ask\" is in a middle voice that marks the subject as acting for his own benefit — the grammar itself exposes the self-interest behind the request. Jesus' question about the cup uses the futuristic present, a tense for something not yet happened but so certain that it can be spoken of as already underway. The cup is the one he names later in Gethsemane; the baptism draws on a common metaphorical use of baptizō for being overwhelmed or plunged under crushing circumstance — Jesus is picturing himself submerged in suffering at the cross.\n\nJames and John answer \"we are able\" with more courage than understanding, and events proved the word literal for each of them in different ways. And when Jesus says the seats at his right and left are \"not mine to give,\" he isn't disclaiming his authority so much as pointing past himself to the Father's own arrangement — even the Son operates within it.","translation":"But Jesus said to them, You do not know what you are asking for yourselves. Are you able to drink the cup that I am about to drink, or to be plunged under the baptism with which I am about to be overwhelmed? And they said to him, We are able. And Jesus said to them, The cup that I am about to drink you shall drink, and with the baptism with which I am about to be overwhelmed you shall be baptized: but to sit at my right hand or my left is not mine to give, but it is for those for whom it has been prepared."},{"c1":10,"v1":41,"c2":10,"v2":41,"label":"Mark 10:41","text":"\"Displeased\" is too soft a word for aganakteō — the same term used of Jesus' own anger at the disciples turning children away back in verse 14. This is real indignation, not mild irritation, and it spreads through all ten of the other disciples the moment they hear what James and John asked for. The private ambition of two apostles becomes, almost instantly, a fracture running through the whole group.","translation":"And when the ten heard it, they began to be indignant at James and John."},{"c1":10,"v1":42,"c2":10,"v2":45,"label":"Mark 10:42–45","text":"Jesus draws a sharp line between two Greek words for service. \"Minister\" is diakonos, a servant defined by the act of serving — a term with some dignity to it, describing someone in active service to others. \"Servant\" is doulos, the most servile word available in Greek, the term for an owned slave with no independent standing at all. Jesus doesn't soften the second word to make it more palatable — he escalates deliberately: greatness in his kingdom isn't achieved by becoming somebody's diakonos but by becoming everybody's doulos.\n\n\"Ransom\" is lutron, the specific word for the price paid to free a slave, and \"for\" translates anti, a preposition of exchange — one thing given in place of another. Jesus isn't offering himself as a general benefit; he's offering himself as a payment made instead of the ones who owed it, in the place of many who couldn't buy their own way out of slavery to sin.","translation":"But Jesus called them to him and said, Whoever wants to become great among you shall be your servant; and whoever wants to be first among you shall be slave of all. For even the Son of Man came not to be served, but to serve, and to give his life a ransom in exchange for many."},{"c1":10,"v1":46,"c2":10,"v2":46,"label":"Mark 10:46","text":"Mark's description that Bartimaeus \"was sitting\" by the road carries the sense of habitual routine — begging at this spot was where he could be found, day after day, not a one-time appearance. Jesus enters Jericho openly, moving with the crowds bound for the Passover, publicly and without concealment — a visibility that will matter once he reaches Jerusalem and the authorities already waiting for him there. The blind beggar by the roadside was an unremarkable sight along that route; what happens next is anything but.","translation":"And blind Bartimaeus, the son of Timaeus, was sitting by the roadside begging — as was his habit."},{"c1":10,"v1":47,"c2":10,"v2":47,"label":"Mark 10:47","text":"\"Son of David\" is a deliberately Messianic title, and it matters that Bartimaeus reaches for it here, on Judean soil approaching Jerusalem, where the connection between David's royal line and the coming Messiah carried its fullest weight. Mark's grammar shows Bartimaeus not calling out once but crying out repeatedly, building in volume and persistence — until his actual appeal, \"have mercy on me,\" switches to a sharp, urgent imperative: not mercy someday, but mercy now, at once.","translation":"And when he heard that it was Jesus the Nazarene, he began crying out again and again, saying, Jesus, Son of David, have mercy on me — now!"},{"c1":10,"v1":48,"c2":10,"v2":48,"label":"Mark 10:48","text":"\"Charged\" is epitimaō, a strong verb for sharp rebuke, and \"hold his peace\" is siōpaō in a tense marking entrance into a new state — the crowd isn't asking an already-quiet man to stay quiet, they're demanding a shouting man go silent immediately. It backfires: the more they press him to stop, the louder and more persistent his cry becomes.\n\nThe crowd's tone flips just as fast once Jesus responds. Mark uses the same plain word for \"call\" each time rather than varying his vocabulary — phōneō, the ordinary word for calling out loud enough to be heard at a distance — which gives their relayed message its blunt, unpolished energy: \"Be of good courage, rise, he's calling you.\" No wasted words, exactly the way an actual crowd would talk. Bartimaeus's response matches the urgency: he throws off his outer cloak — the himation, a garment substantial enough that people sometimes slept in it — and springs to his feet rather than rising carefully, leaving behind the one possession a beggar would ordinarily never abandon in a crowd.","translation":"And many kept rebuking him, that he should become silent: but he cried out all the more, a great deal more, Son of David, have mercy on me. And Jesus stopped, and said, Call him. And they called the blind man, saying to him, Be of good courage, rise, he is calling you. And casting away his cloak, he sprang up and came to Jesus."},{"c1":10,"v1":51,"c2":10,"v2":51,"label":"Mark 10:51","text":"\"Lord\" renders Rabbounei, a heightened form of address beyond the ordinary \"Rabbi\" — \"my Master,\" carrying deeper reverence. \"Receive my sight\" is anablepō, which specifically means to recover sight rather than to gain it for the first time — the verb itself tells us Bartimaeus wasn't born blind but is asking to have back something he once had.","translation":"And Jesus answered him, What do you want me to do for you? And the blind man said to him, My Master, that I may recover my sight."},{"c1":10,"v1":52,"c2":10,"v2":52,"label":"Mark 10:52","text":"\"Made thee whole\" translates sōzō, the ordinary New Testament word for \"save\" — used without distinction for physical healing and for spiritual salvation, the same term doing double duty because in the New Testament's thinking the two aren't really separate categories. The perfect tense marks this as a completed act with lasting effect — not a temporary improvement but a durable, permanent cure. And \"followed\" shifts back to the imperfect: Bartimaeus doesn't just say thank you and walk off, he falls in with the procession and keeps walking, joining Jesus on the same road toward Jerusalem that has run through this whole chapter.","translation":"And Jesus said to him, Go your way; your faith has saved you, and the healing stands complete. And immediately he recovered his sight, and kept following him on the road."},{"c1":11,"v1":2,"c2":11,"v2":2,"label":"Mark 11:2","text":"\"On which no one has ever sat\" describes an animal never broken to a rider. Under the Law, things set apart for sacred use were often required to be unused — the heifer for the water of purification (Numbers 19:2) and the cow that pulled the ark on its return (1 Samuel 6:7) both had to be animals that had never worn a yoke. Jesus' choice of a colt that had never carried anyone fits that same pattern of consecration — it marks this ride as no ordinary journey but a deliberately staged, set-apart act. The instructions themselves are given with striking confidence: exactly where to look, and what will be found, before anyone has gone to look.","translation":"Go into the village facing you, and immediately as you enter it you will find a colt tied, on which no one has ever yet sat: untie it and bring it."},{"c1":11,"v1":3,"c2":11,"v2":3,"label":"Mark 11:3","text":"Jesus anticipates the objection before it's raised and hands the disciples the answer in advance: \"The Lord has need of it.\" The title \"the Lord\" is Jesus' own self-designation here, spoken with the expectation that it will be recognized and honored — a quiet claim to authority tucked inside what sounds like an ordinary request. The promise that follows, that the colt will be sent back at once, isn't incidental — it's Jesus taking care that His authority never becomes an excuse to shortchange the owner.","translation":"And if anyone says to you, Why are you doing this? say, The Lord has need of it; and immediately he will send it back here."},{"c1":11,"v1":4,"c2":11,"v2":4,"label":"Mark 11:4","text":"\"In the open street\" translates amphodon, literally \"a road running around\" — the winding lane that curved around a block of houses, distinct from a straight thoroughfare. It's a small, concrete, on-the-ground detail, the kind that shows up when the description comes from someone who was actually there rather than someone summarizing secondhand. The colt turns up exactly where, and exactly as, Jesus had said — confirmed on-site down to the specific street.","translation":"And they went away, and found the colt tied by the door outside, in the winding lane; and they untied it."},{"c1":11,"v1":7,"c2":11,"v2":7,"label":"Mark 11:7","text":"Because the colt had never been ridden, it had never been fitted with any kind of saddle — there was nothing to sit on. The disciples improvise, throwing their own outer cloaks over its back. The scene is makeshift rather than staged: no trappings prepared in advance, only a borrowed animal and garments laid down on the spot.","translation":"And they brought the colt to Jesus, and threw their outer garments on it; and he sat on it."},{"c1":11,"v1":8,"c2":11,"v2":8,"label":"Mark 11:8","text":"\"Way\" translates hodos, the ordinary word for road or path. The gospel writers each reach for a different word for the greenery thrown down before Him — one pictures young cut branches or twigs, another suggests loose foliage or rushes strewn and trampled into a kind of carpet, a third specifies palm fronds by name. Each is recording what he personally saw from his own place in the crowd, not repeating a single fixed account.\n\n\"Hosanna\" is a transliterated Hebrew cry — \"save now,\" a plea drawn from Psalm 118 turned here into acclamation. \"Blessed\" translates eulogeō, \"to speak well of, to praise\" — a verb of speech describing what the crowd is doing with their mouths. A different Greek word, makarios, would describe someone's actual spiritual condition (\"blessed\" in the sense of prosperous); this crowd isn't declaring Jesus prosperous, they're shouting praise at Him as He passes.","translation":"And many spread their garments on the road, and others cut branches from the fields and strewed them on the road. And those who went before and those who followed kept crying out, Hosanna! Save now! Blessed — well spoken of — is he who comes in the name of the Lord!"},{"c1":11,"v1":11,"c2":11,"v2":11,"label":"Mark 11:11","text":"\"Temple\" here translates hieron, the whole complex of courts, colonnades, and outbuildings, including the Court of the Gentiles — not naos, the inner sanctuary that only priests could enter. \"Looked around\" translates periblepō, a compound of the ordinary verb \"to look\" with a prefix meaning \"around, in a circle\" — a comprehensive, sweeping inspection, not a glance. He surveys everything and then leaves for the night without acting. The delay is itself telling: what happens the next day is not an impulsive outburst but something He has already looked over and decided on overnight.","translation":"And he entered Jerusalem, into the temple courts; and having swept his eyes over everything, since the hour was now late, he went out to Bethany with the twelve."},{"c1":11,"v1":12,"c2":11,"v2":14,"label":"Mark 11:12–14","text":"It \"was not the season for figs,\" yet the tree, already in leaf, was advertising fruit that leaves at that time of year would normally promise. Jesus curses it not simply for being barren but for being barren while putting on a display that said otherwise — leaves without any actual figs, appearance without substance. It's an acted parable, staged the day before He walks into the temple and finds the identical pattern there: impressive religious activity with nothing behind it. His hunger, and His initial hope of finding fruit (\"if perhaps He would find anything on it\"), show Him working within the same ordinary limits of information any traveler would have that morning — nothing in the account requires Him to have known in advance that the tree was empty.\n\nWhat follows in the temple carries the same theme forward: Jesus stops not only the selling but the use of the temple courts as a shortcut for hauling goods between the city and the Mount of Olives — a use the authorities had technically banned but stopped enforcing. The money changing itself was profitable business, turning foreign currency into the half-shekels required for the temple tax. Overturning those tables on the eve of Passover, at the height of the trading, wasn't symbolic theater; it was a direct, costly interruption of an operation someone was profiting from.","translation":"And seeing at a distance a fig tree having leaves, he went to see whether perhaps he would find anything on it: and when he came to it, he found nothing but leaves; for it was not the season for figs. And he said to it, Let no one eat fruit from you ever again."},{"c1":11,"v1":17,"c2":11,"v2":17,"label":"Mark 11:17","text":"\"For all the nations\" reflects a dative construction leaning toward \"for the benefit of\" rather than merely \"belonging to\" — the Court of the Gentiles, where the market had taken over, was meant as the one place non-Jews could approach God in that building, not overflow space for commerce. The charge that follows turns on a precise word choice usually flattened in translation: \"den of thieves\" should really read \"den of robbers.\" Greek distinguishes a thief who quietly pilfers (kleptēs) from a robber who takes by force, typically as part of an organized band (lēstēs) — the same word used later for the men crucified alongside Jesus and for Barabbas. The accusation isn't petty pocket-picking; it's organized plunder, carried out in the open, under religious cover.","translation":"And he kept teaching, and saying to them, Is it not written, My house shall be called a house of prayer for all the nations? But you have made it a den of robbers."},{"c1":11,"v1":18,"c2":11,"v2":18,"label":"Mark 11:18","text":"The leaders, hearing of the confrontation, \"began seeking\" a way to destroy Him — an imperfect tense marking the start of an ongoing campaign, not a decision reached in the moment. What holds them back is fear of the crowd, whose reaction to His teaching is described with ekplēssō, a strong compound verb meaning literally \"to strike out of\" — to be struck out of one's senses. It's a visceral, involuntary jolt, not polite admiration: people were being knocked off balance by what they heard, in open contrast to what they were used to hearing from the leaders now plotting against Him.","translation":"And the chief priests and the scribes heard it, and began seeking how they might destroy him: for they feared him, because all the crowd was being struck clean out of themselves with astonishment at his teaching."},{"c1":11,"v1":19,"c2":11,"v2":19,"label":"Mark 11:19","text":"\"Whenever evening came\" uses a word for \"when\" that carries the sense of \"every time\" rather than marking a single occasion — each night that week He made a habit of leaving the city. The verb describing His departure is doubly marked, a prefix meaning \"out of\" reinforced by a separate word meaning \"outside\" — the wording goes out of its way to stress that He put Himself fully clear of Jerusalem each night rather than lingering at its edge. He treated the city as hostile ground well before anyone came to arrest Him there.","translation":"And whenever evening came, he went fully out of the city, clear of it."},{"c1":11,"v1":20,"c2":11,"v2":20,"label":"Mark 11:20","text":"\"Withered\" translates xērainō, \"to dry up,\" and it's cast here in the perfect tense — a form describing not a process underway but a finished state with lasting effect: the tree stands there dead and stays that way. Combined with \"from the roots,\" the phrase rules out anything partial or temporary. What Jesus spoke the day before was, from the moment He said it, already complete.","translation":"And as they passed by in the morning, they saw the fig tree withered away, dried up from the roots."},{"c1":11,"v1":21,"c2":11,"v2":21,"label":"Mark 11:21","text":"Peter's memory is triggered by the sight itself, not by any reminder from Jesus — he's the one connecting yesterday's word to today's result, and his tone (\"Rabbi, look\") reads more like a startled report than settled understanding. He's still catching up to something Jesus had already treated as finished the moment He spoke it.","translation":"And Peter, remembering, said to him, Rabbi, look! The fig tree which you cursed has withered away!"},{"c1":11,"v1":22,"c2":11,"v2":22,"label":"Mark 11:22","text":"\"Have faith in God\" is, in Greek word order, closer to \"have faith of God\" — \"God\" stands in the genitive, marking Him as the object faith is aimed at, not merely its origin. The command is a present imperative, a form carrying ongoing, repeated force in Greek — not \"exercise faith once\" but \"keep on having faith\" as a settled disposition. Jesus takes Peter's amazement at a finished result and turns it back toward the continuous posture that produced it.","translation":"And Jesus answered and said to them, Keep on having faith in God."},{"c1":11,"v1":23,"c2":11,"v2":23,"label":"Mark 11:23","text":"\"Does not doubt\" translates diakrinō, built on a root meaning \"to judge\" or \"divide\" — to be split between two conclusions, pulled one way and then the other. The faith Jesus describes is the opposite condition: undivided. \"It will happen\" is, in the Greek text, a present-tense verb rather than a strict future — what grammarians call a futuristic present, describing what's still ahead with the same certainty normally reserved for what's already in hand.","translation":"Truly I tell you, whoever says to this mountain, Be taken up and be thrown into the sea, and does not become divided in his heart, but believes that what he says is happening, he shall have it."},{"c1":11,"v1":24,"c2":11,"v2":24,"label":"Mark 11:24","text":"Two different Greek verbs lie behind the single English word \"pray\" here. One, proseuchomai, is the general word for prayer addressed to God; the other, aiteō, translated \"ask,\" is the narrower verb for making a specific request — asking that a particular thing be given. \"Received\" is an aorist, and its tense places it logically before \"believe\": the instruction is to believe as already having gotten the thing, not to believe hoping it might eventually arrive. Faith, on this construction, treats the answer as settled before it's seen — the way a signed deed secures ownership of property still out of hand.\n\nThe teaching runs straight on into forgiveness without a break: to pray while nursing a grudge is treated as inconsistent with the very faith just described. \"Trespasses\" translates paraptōma, which pictures a false step, a stumble off the right path — and it's this that has to be let go of before approaching God again.","translation":"Therefore I tell you, whatever things you ask for in prayer, believe that you received them, and they shall be yours. And whenever you stand praying, forgive, if you have anything against anyone; so that your Father also who is in heaven may forgive you your false steps."},{"c1":11,"v1":33,"c2":11,"v2":33,"label":"Mark 11:33","text":"\"We do not know\" is evasion, not honest uncertainty — both available answers to Jesus' question about John's baptism were dangerous for the leaders, so they choose silence over commitment. Jesus' reply mirrors their own question back at them almost word for word: they had asked what sort of authority He was acting under, using poios, a Greek interrogative that presses for the nature or character of a thing rather than just its source — and He now declines, in the same terms, to say what sort of authority stands behind His answer to them. The standoff ends on His terms; His silence functions as a verdict on theirs.","translation":"And they answered and said to Jesus, We do not know. And Jesus said to them, Neither will I tell you by what sort of authority I do these things."},{"c1":12,"v1":2,"c2":12,"v2":2,"label":"Mark 12:2","text":"\"Servant\" translates doulos, a bondslave — not a hired hand but someone wholly owned by the master. The vineyard's rent was paid in kind, a share of the grape harvest, collected at the appointed season. The bondslave sent to collect it stands for the long line of Old Testament prophets God kept sending to Israel to collect what was owed him.","translation":"And at the season he sent to the tenant farmers a bondslave, that he might receive from the tenant farmers a share of the fruit of the vineyard."},{"c1":12,"v1":3,"c2":12,"v2":3,"label":"Mark 12:3","text":"\"Beat\" is derō, a word that originally meant \"to skin\" or \"flay\" but by New Testament times had settled into \"to beat severely, to scourge.\" The tenants' refusal to hand over any fruit pictures Israel's repeated refusal to heed the prophets sent to it.","translation":"And they seized him, and beat him severely, and sent him away empty-handed."},{"c1":12,"v1":4,"c2":12,"v2":4,"label":"Mark 12:4","text":"A second servant fares even worse than the first — struck about the head and heaped with insults. Each servant sent represents another prophetic voice, and each rebuff from the tenants deepens their guilt before the owner finally acts.","translation":"And again he sent to them another bondslave; and that one they struck on the head, and treated shamefully."},{"c1":12,"v1":6,"c2":12,"v2":6,"label":"Mark 12:6","text":"\"Yet he had one\" — after every servant sent had been beaten or killed, only one messenger remained: the owner's own son. The owner assumes, almost naively, that no one would dare harm his son — which is exactly the point of the parable. From any reasonable human vantage point, killing the son should have been unthinkable; that the tenants do it anyway lays bare how far sin will go to protect what it has stolen. Mark's phrase \"beloved son\" echoes the voice at Jesus's own baptism, \"This is my Son, the beloved one, in whom I am well pleased\" — the Father's own words placed here in the mouth of the vineyard owner.","translation":"He had yet one — a son, dearly beloved: he sent him last to them also, saying, They will respect my son."},{"c1":12,"v1":7,"c2":12,"v2":7,"label":"Mark 12:7","text":"The tenants know exactly who is coming: \"This is the heir.\" Their plan isn't confusion about his identity but calculated murder to seize what belongs to him — \"let us kill him, and the inheritance will be ours.\" The parable exposes the reasoning of the religious leaders themselves: Jesus's growing popularity with the crowds threatened their hold on the people, and removing him looked, to them, like the way to keep it.","translation":"But those tenant farmers said among themselves, This is the heir; come, let us kill him, and the inheritance will be ours."},{"c1":12,"v1":8,"c2":12,"v2":8,"label":"Mark 12:8","text":"\"Cast him out of the vineyard\" pictures excommunication — the tenants don't just kill the son, they throw the body outside the vineyard's boundary, treating him as no longer belonging to the community at all. It's a fitting shadow of what actually happened next: Jesus condemned as a blasphemer and crucified outside the walls of Jerusalem.","translation":"And they took him, and killed him, and threw him outside the vineyard."},{"c1":12,"v1":9,"c2":12,"v2":11,"label":"Mark 12:9–11","text":"The owner's response — destroying the tenants and handing the vineyard to others — anticipates Jerusalem's fall and Israel's worldwide scattering, with the vineyard passing for a time into other hands through the church, until Israel's future restoration. The quotation that follows (Psalm 118:22–23, echoed again in Acts 4:11 and 1 Peter 2:4, 7) supplies the verdict on what just happened. \"Rejected\" translates apodokimazō — the simple verb means to test something in order to approve it, and the prefix adds the sense \"away from,\" so the compound describes a rejection that comes only after testing, not instead of it. Israel's leaders did examine Jesus's claims and weigh the evidence of his miracles, and rejected him anyway, because the Messiah he presented himself to be — one who deals with sin rather than Rome — wasn't the Messiah they wanted.","translation":"What therefore will the owner of the vineyard do? He will come and destroy the tenant farmers, and will give the vineyard to others. Have you not even read this scripture: The stone which the builders tested and rejected, this became the head of the corner: this came about from the Lord, and it is marvelous in our eyes?"},{"c1":12,"v1":12,"c2":12,"v2":12,"label":"Mark 12:12","text":"The Sanhedrin's men want to arrest Jesus on the spot but hold back, afraid of the crowd — this is their second failed attempt within the temple courts. They know full well the parable was aimed at them; there's no ambiguity left, only a retreat to plan their next move.","translation":"And they kept seeking to seize him, but feared the crowd; for they knew he had spoken the parable against them: and they left him, and went away."},{"c1":12,"v1":13,"c2":12,"v2":13,"label":"Mark 12:13","text":"Defeated, the different factions regroup and send an unlikely alliance — Pharisees paired with Herodians, two groups that otherwise had little use for each other, now united against a common threat. \"Catch\" translates agreuō, a hunting term for trapping wild game or snaring birds; the men are sent not to ask an honest question but to hunt Jesus down with his own words.","translation":"And they sent to him some of the Pharisees and the Herodians, to snare him in his talk."},{"c1":12,"v1":14,"c2":12,"v2":14,"label":"Mark 12:14","text":"The flattery is calculated to box him in: \"we know that you are true\" uses oida, the verb for settled, certain knowledge rather than a passing impression, and alēthēs for \"true\" carries the sense of someone incapable of dishonesty. \"Is it lawful\" translates exestin, which doesn't point to statute law the way the English suggests — it simply asks \"is it permitted,\" and the permission in view here is religious, not civil: does a Jew's exclusive covenant loyalty to God rule out paying Rome's poll tax? The tax was doubly offensive — it marked subjection to a foreign power, and it had to be paid with a coin stamped with Caesar's image, an image Rome required be honored as divine. A yes trapped him with the crowd; a no trapped him with Rome.","translation":"And when they came, they said to him, Teacher, we know that you are true, and it is not a concern to you about anyone; for you do not look at the face of men, but teach the way of God in truth: Is it permitted to pay the poll tax to Caesar, or not?"},{"c1":12,"v1":15,"c2":12,"v2":15,"label":"Mark 12:15","text":"Jesus sees straight through it — \"knowing their hypocrisy\" names the trap for what it is. \"Tempt\" translates peirazō, \"to put to the test,\" used here in its harder sense of testing someone in hopes they fail, not simply testing to learn the truth. His request for a denarius forces a small delay, since only Jewish coinage was normally used inside the temple itself — the pause before the coin arrives lets the tension of the moment build.","translation":"But he, knowing their hypocrisy, said to them, Why are you testing me, hoping to trap me? Bring me a denarius, that I may look at it."},{"c1":12,"v1":16,"c2":12,"v2":16,"label":"Mark 12:16","text":"\"Image\" is eikōn, a derived likeness — the coin's portrait counts as a copy, not the original. \"Superscription\" is epigraphē, literally \"a writing on\" something, an inscription. The question is airtight: whoever answers has to look at the coin already in hand and admit whose face and name are stamped on it.","translation":"And they brought one. And he said to them, Whose likeness and whose inscription is this? And they said to him, Caesar's."},{"c1":12,"v1":17,"c2":12,"v2":17,"label":"Mark 12:17","text":"Jesus's reply plays on a word switch. The questioners had used didōmi, the ordinary verb \"to give,\" but Jesus answers with apodidōmi, which means to pay something back, to discharge a debt. He isn't describing tribute to Caesar as a gift Israel might withhold — he's describing it as money already owed, since the very coin bearing Caesar's image is already circulating under Caesar's rule. Debt to Caesar and debt to God occupy different accounts; paying one doesn't cancel or compromise the other. \"Marveled\" is thaumazō in the imperfect tense, describing ongoing action — they didn't just react and move on, they stood there stunned, unable to find any opening they could use against him.","translation":"Pay back to Caesar the things of Caesar, and to God the things of God. And they were continually marveling at him."},{"c1":12,"v1":18,"c2":12,"v2":23,"label":"Mark 12:18–23","text":"The Sadducees are marked out by hoitines, a pronoun that identifies a whole class or type rather than a single instance — these are people characteristically defined by denying the resurrection, unlike the Pharisees, who affirmed it. Fewer in number and closely tied to the priestly aristocracy, they test Jesus with a scenario built from the levirate marriage law (a provision meant to keep a family's inheritance from dying out): seven brothers, one wife, no resurrection to sort out the tangle — or so they assume. Their hypothetical is introduced with ean, marking it explicitly as a supposed case rather than a real family history; the whole scenario is a debate tactic, not a report of events.","translation":"And Sadducees came to him — the sort who characteristically say there is no resurrection — and they kept questioning him: Teacher, Moses wrote for us that if a man's brother should die and leave a wife behind with no child, his brother should take the wife and raise up seed for his brother. Suppose there were seven brothers: in the resurrection, when they rise, whose wife will she be? For the seven had her as wife."},{"c1":12,"v1":24,"c2":12,"v2":27,"label":"Mark 12:24–27","text":"Jesus's opening line uses a negative (ou) with a question, a Greek construction that expects the answer \"yes\" — it lands harder than a flat statement would: \"Is it not the case that you are in error?\" Their error is twofold: they don't know the Scriptures, and they don't know the power of God, an ignorance especially inexcusable in men who were largely priests by trade. \"As the angels\" uses hōs, a comparative adverb meaning \"in the same manner as\" — the comparison is deliberately narrow, limited strictly to the point at hand. It says nothing about resurrected people becoming angels; it says only that, like angels, they will no longer marry or reproduce. Jesus then points to Moses at the burning bush, where God says \"I am the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob\" in the present tense, long after all three had died — proof that they were, in some real sense, still alive to him. A God who is God only of the living can't be speaking of men who have simply ceased to exist.","translation":"Jesus said to them, Is it not the case that you are in error, because you know neither the scriptures nor the power of God? For when they rise from among the dead, they neither marry nor are given in marriage; but are, in the very manner of the angels, in the heavens. But concerning the dead, that they are raised — have you not read in the book of Moses how God spoke to him at the bush, saying, I am the God of Abraham, and the God of Isaac, and the God of Jacob? He is not the God of the dead, but of the living: you are therefore greatly in error."},{"c1":12,"v1":28,"c2":12,"v2":28,"label":"Mark 12:28","text":"\"Which is the first commandment\" uses poia, \"of what sort\" or \"what kind,\" rather than a word asking simply \"which one\" — the scribe isn't asking Jesus to rank ten commandments in order but to name the category of commandment that carries the highest priority: ceremonial law or moral law.","translation":"And one of the scribes came, and asked him, Of what sort is the first commandment of all?"},{"c1":12,"v1":29,"c2":12,"v2":33,"label":"Mark 12:29–33","text":"The quotation is the Shema (Deuteronomy 6:4–5), recited daily by every observant Jew and carried, inscribed on parchment, inside the phylacteries scribes wore on forehead and arm — its priority was already assumed before Jesus ever cited it. \"Love\" here is agapaō, love as a matter of will and devotion rather than mere sentiment — deliberately not phileō, the word for ordinary affection or fondness. \"Out of your whole heart\" uses a construction that pictures love flowing outward from the heart as its wellspring, not simply applied with the heart as one tool among several. Loving God and loving one's neighbor stand together as a single, unrivaled command — nothing outranks them.\n\nThe scribe's reply opens with kalōs, an exclamation of hearty agreement — \"right, well said\" — and \"you have said the truth\" uses an adverbial phrase meaning \"truly, in truth,\" endorsing the manner of his statement rather than issuing a formal verdict on doctrine.","translation":"Jesus answered, The first is, Hear, O Israel; The Lord our God is one Lord: and you shall love the Lord your God, out of your whole heart, and out of your whole soul, and out of your whole mind, and out of your whole strength: this is the first commandment. And the second is this, You shall love your neighbor as yourself. There is no other commandment greater than these. And the scribe said to him, Well said, Teacher — you have spoken in truth."},{"c1":12,"v1":34,"c2":12,"v2":34,"label":"Mark 12:34","text":"\"Not far from the kingdom\" credits the scribe with real insight — the word behind \"discreetly\" is nounechōs, literally \"having one's mind in hand,\" someone thinking for himself rather than reciting an expected answer. He grasped not just the two commandments Jesus named but the principle behind them, that moral obligation outweighs ritual. Yet nearness to the kingdom isn't entrance into it — the distance left to close wasn't measured in miles or ceremony but in the will to actually follow through.","translation":"And Jesus, seeing that he answered thinking for himself, said to him, You are not far from the kingdom of God."},{"c1":12,"v1":35,"c2":12,"v2":37,"label":"Mark 12:35–37","text":"Christos transliterates the Greek for \"the Anointed One,\" itself a rendering of the Hebrew behind \"Messiah\" — Israel's expected king from David's line. \"Son\" carries the ordinary Hebrew sense of \"descendant,\" and kurios, \"lord,\" is the word the Greek Old Testament uses to render God's covenant name — its use here carries the weight of deity, not mere politeness. Jesus's question turns on a real puzzle: David, writing under the Spirit's inspiration, calls his own future descendant \"my Lord\" (Psalm 110:1) — language no father uses of an ordinary son. If the Messiah were merely David's human offspring, David wouldn't call him \"Lord.\" The only way both statements hold is if the Messiah is David's descendant by human birth and, at the same time, David's God. The verse quoted also has the Father addressing the Son directly, an Old Testament glimpse of two distinct persons within the Godhead.","translation":"And Jesus, teaching in the temple, said, How do the scribes say that the Christ is the son of David? David himself said, in the Holy Spirit, The Lord said to my Lord, Sit at my right hand, until I put your enemies underneath your feet. David himself calls him Lord; and how then is he his son?"},{"c1":12,"v1":38,"c2":12,"v2":40,"label":"Mark 12:38–40","text":"\"Love\" here is phileō, describing what the scribes are fond of and drawn to, not agapaō's committed devotion. Their long robes (stolē, the word used elsewhere for priestly or royal dress) aren't wrong in themselves — plenty of people wore distinguished clothing on formal occasions — but the scribes wear them to be seen. The same appetite shows up in their taste for public greetings, the title \"Rabbi,\" the front-row seats facing the congregation in the synagogue, and prōtoklisia, \"the first reclining place,\" the seat of highest honor at a banquet where guests ate reclining on couches rather than sitting at a table.\n\nBeneath the display sits something worse: these men managed widows' estates and legal affairs, then quietly siphoned off the proceeds for themselves, dressing the theft up with long, public prayers offered in the widows' own homes. The prayers aren't real prayers at all — they're theater covering a crime, which is why the judgment coming to these men runs heavier than an ordinary thief's.","translation":"And in his teaching he said, Watch out for the scribes, who love to walk about in long robes, and to have greetings in the marketplaces, and the front seats in the synagogues, and the first reclining places at feasts: who devour widows' houses, and for a pretense make long prayers: these shall receive greater condemnation."},{"c1":12,"v1":41,"c2":12,"v2":41,"label":"Mark 12:41","text":"Jesus sits down across from the temple treasury — a colonnade holding a row of collection chests in the Court of the Women — and watches, with a discerning eye, the stream of people dropping in their gifts. Wealthy donors give large sums, visibly.","translation":"And he sat down opposite the treasury, and kept watching how the crowd was dropping money into the treasury: and many rich people were dropping in much."},{"c1":12,"v1":42,"c2":12,"v2":44,"label":"Mark 12:42–44","text":"\"Poor\" translates ptōchos, the stronger word for someone utterly without resources, not the ordinary word for a person simply of modest means — and in the Greek word order, \"poor\" sits in the emphatic position, underlining just how destitute she was. The two coins she drops in are lepta, the smallest denomination minted, worth together only a fraction of a single denarius. Jesus's verdict has nothing to do with the size of the gift and everything to do with what's left afterward — the wealthy gave out of surplus they'd never miss, while she gave everything she had left to live on. God isn't measuring the coin in the box; he's measuring what a person keeps back for themselves.","translation":"And a poor widow came and dropped in two lepta — the smallest of coins — which make a quadrans. And calling his disciples to him, he said to them, Truly I tell you, this poor widow has dropped in more than all those dropping into the treasury: for they all dropped in out of their surplus; but she, out of her lack, dropped in everything she had — her whole living."},{"c1":13,"v1":2,"c2":13,"v2":2,"label":"Mark 13:2","text":"\"Thrown down\" translates kataluo, literally \"to loosen down\" — the picture is of a structure coming apart piece by piece, not toppling in one blow. That matches history: Rome's siege in AD 70 stripped the temple stone by stone rather than flattening it in a single stroke, and today only foundation courses remain. Jesus also stacks two negatives in his answer, which in Greek doesn't cancel out but intensifies — the strongest form of denial the language has. \"Not one stone will be left on another\" is as absolute as a sentence can get.\n\nWhat follows fills out the warning. \"Deceive\" (planao) pictures wandering off a path, not simply being fooled. \"In my name\" is literally \"on the basis of my name\" — claiming his authority as a warrant for their own claims. Strikingly, the word \"Christ\" isn't actually in the Greek of their declaration; it's just an emphatic \"I am he,\" pretenders claiming his identity outright rather than merely borrowing his title. And \"be not troubled\" uses a present-tense command with a negative, the grammar for stopping something already underway — the disciples were anxious before he ever spoke, and Jesus tells them to stand down from a fear already in motion, not to avoid one not yet felt. \"Sorrows\" (odin) is specifically the pain of labor — not chaos but contraction, pains that intensify and space out toward a birth still to come.","translation":"Do you see these great buildings? There shall by no means be left here stone upon stone which shall not be loosened down. ... Watch, that no one lead you astray off the path. Many will come on the basis of my name, saying, I am he, and will lead many astray. ... Stop being thrown into unease; these things must come to pass, but the end is not yet. ... For nation shall rise up against nation, and kingdom against kingdom; these are the beginning of birth pains."},{"c1":13,"v1":9,"c2":13,"v2":9,"label":"Mark 13:9","text":"The pronoun \"you\" is added for emphasis in the Greek — Jesus pivots his disciples' attention from the nation's future to their own: whatever else is coming, watch yourselves. \"Councils\" translates sunedria, literally \"sitting together,\" the local Jewish tribunals modeled on the Sanhedrin in Jerusalem — smaller courts, same pattern.\n\n\"For a testimony against them\" softens under the Greek grammar. The case used is a simple dative, which doesn't carry the sense of \"against\" that the English implies — it reads \"to them.\" The disciples' trials become occasions to testify to the very officials prosecuting them, not just evidence held against the disciples themselves. Being dragged into court was, backhandedly, a platform.","translation":"But watch yourselves. They will hand you over to sitting-together-courts, and in synagogues you will be beaten, and before governors and kings you will stand for my sake, for a testimony to them."},{"c1":13,"v1":10,"c2":13,"v2":10,"label":"Mark 13:10","text":"The same construction behind \"must be\" elsewhere in this chapter shows up again here — a term for necessity built into the nature of things, not just a prediction of what happens to occur. The proclamation of the gospel to the nations is fixed, not incidental to the timeline. And \"first\" places it explicitly before what comes next: this preaching isn't one item in a list of end-time events, it's a marker that has to be crossed before the rest can follow.","translation":"And it is necessary, in the nature of things, that first the gospel be proclaimed to all the nations."},{"c1":13,"v1":11,"c2":13,"v2":11,"label":"Mark 13:11","text":"The \"speaking\" in view here isn't preaching — it's legal defense, testimony given under arrest before a judge, a different scene than the gospel proclamation of the verse before. \"Whenever they lead you\" uses a construction for a recurring, general circumstance rather than one specific future moment — Jesus is describing the whole experience of being marched off into custody, and he tells them not to spend that walk rehearsing a defense. Whatever is needed will be supplied in the moment itself.\n\nWhat follows widens the picture: family turning against family, everyone hating them — no qualifier softens \"all\" in the Greek, it is as total as it sounds. \"Shall be saved\" doesn't carry its usual theological weight here; in a context about enduring to \"the end\" of a defined ordeal, the verb describes coming through alive, rescue at the far side of suffering, not conversion. Context decides which sense a common word carries, and here it's survival, not salvation in the doctrinal sense.","translation":"And whenever they lead you away, handing you over, do not be anxious beforehand what you should speak; but whatever is given you in that hour, speak that — for it is not you who are speaking, but the Holy Spirit. And brother will hand over brother to death, and father child; and children will rise up against parents and have them put to death; and you will be hated by all, on account of my name. But the one who endures to the end, this one will come through alive."},{"c1":13,"v1":14,"c2":13,"v2":16,"label":"Mark 13:14–16","text":"\"Woe\" translates ouai, a plain interjection of grief — a cry of sorrow over a coming hardship, not a curse pronounced on anyone. It's aimed here specifically at those least able to move quickly: women who are pregnant or nursing when the moment to flee arrives.\n\nThe phrasing \"those days shall be affliction\" reads backwards in most English translations. In the Greek, \"those days\" is the grammatical subject — the sentence identifies the days themselves as the tribulation rather than merely locating suffering within them. It isn't \"there will be trouble during that time\"; it's \"that time will be trouble.\" And \"elect\" comes from eklego, a compound built on the idea of choosing out from a larger group — a word that pictures selection, not merely favor.","translation":"But alas for the women having in womb and for the ones nursing in those days! For those days will be trouble, such as has not been from the beginning of creation... and except the Lord had shortened the days, no flesh would have been saved; but for the sake of the chosen-out ones, whom he chose out, he shortened the days."},{"c1":13,"v1":21,"c2":13,"v2":23,"label":"Mark 13:21–23","text":"\"Christ\" is simply the Greek rendering of the Hebrew \"Messiah\" — both mean \"anointed one,\" and both point to a specific, expected figure: the promised king in David's line, not a generic title for a religious leader. The false christs Jesus warns of aren't denying that a Messiah is coming; they're stepping into the role the nation is already watching for.\n\nThe two words for their miracles are worth separating. \"Signs\" (semeion) frames a miracle as evidence — proof meant to back up a claim. \"Wonders\" (teras) frames the identical miracle as spectacle — meant to produce astonishment in whoever's watching. Same event, two different angles: one about what it's supposed to prove, the other about the reaction it's built to provoke. And \"if it were possible\" is a genuinely open condition in the Greek, not a rhetorical concession already settled — the grammar itself leaves the outcome undetermined.","translation":"And then if anyone says to you, Look, here is the Anointed One; look, there — do not believe it. For false anointed ones and false prophets will arise and will give signs and wonders, in order to lead astray, if it should indeed be possible, even the chosen-out ones. But you, watch; I have told you all things beforehand."},{"c1":13,"v1":24,"c2":13,"v2":27,"label":"Mark 13:24–27","text":"\"Coming in clouds\" uses nephele without the definite article — not \"the clouds\" as a specific weather event, but clouds in a more general, qualitative sense. Elsewhere in the New Testament this same word pictures not weather at all but a vast crowd of people — a great surrounding host, the way a stadium of onlookers gets described in similar terms. Read that way, the image isn't meteorological; it's an arrival amid an enormous retinue of the glorified and the angelic, filling the sky the way clouds do, but made of persons rather than vapor.","translation":"But in those days, after that affliction, the sun will be darkened, and the moon will not give its light, and the stars will be falling from heaven. And then they will see the Son of Man coming amid a great cloud — glorified ones and angels thronging around him — with power and glory. And he will send out the angels and gather together the chosen-out ones from the four winds, from the farthest part of earth to the farthest part of heaven."},{"c1":13,"v1":28,"c2":13,"v2":33,"label":"Mark 13:28–33","text":"The hinge of this passage is what \"generation\" (genea) means. Everywhere else Mark and the other Gospels use the word for a specific cohort of people alive at one time, but administrative documents from the period regularly use the same word for \"race\" or \"lineage\" — a family line traced across generations, no particular age-group in view. Context has to settle which sense applies, and here the surrounding material — the Tribulation, the Antichrist, the regathering of Israel, the second coming — points toward the lineage sense. The claim isn't that Jesus's own contemporaries would outlive these events; it's that this people, this line, will still be standing on the earth when they happen. \"Pass away\" (parerchomai) means going off the stage entirely, vanishing from the scene — the same verb applied to heaven and earth two sentences later, and denied of Jesus's own words.","translation":"Now learn the parable from the fig tree. So also you, when you see these things happening, know that he is near, at the very door. Truly I say to you, this race will not go off the stage until all these things take place. Heaven and earth will go off the stage, but my words will by no means go off the stage."},{"c1":13,"v1":34,"c2":13,"v2":37,"label":"Mark 13:34–37","text":"\"A man taking a far journey\" undersells the Greek. The construction pictures someone who has already gone abroad and is now settled in another country — not a man about to leave, but one already gone. The household he's left behind divides into two roles: the general staff (\"servants,\" doulos, the ordinary word for the whole household of bondservants, each given a task) and one figure singled out — the doorkeeper — whose entire job is to stay alert for the knock.\n\nTwo different Greek words sit behind \"watch\" in this short paragraph, and they're not interchangeable. One simply describes staying awake, ordinary wakefulness. The other, used for the doorkeeper's charge, pictures a sleeping man rousing himself — the sharper image of shaking off drowsiness and coming fully alert, not just avoiding sleep in the first place. That's the word Jesus reaches for at the climax, addressed to everyone listening: not merely stay awake, but wake up.","translation":"It is like a man already gone abroad, having left his house and given authority to his bondservants, to each his work, and having commanded the doorkeeper that he keep awake. Keep awake therefore — for you do not know when the master of the house is coming, lest coming suddenly he find you sleeping. And what I say to you I say to all: Wake up!"},{"c1":14,"v1":3,"c2":14,"v2":3,"label":"Mark 14:3","text":"Mark piles up three separate words to describe what Mary poured out: nardos, an oil imported from far up the Ganges and prized across the Greek and Roman world; pistikos, meaning certified genuine, not a cut-rate substitute; and polutelēs, “very costly.” Three words doing the work of one adjective — the writer wants no reader to underestimate the value of the gift.\n\nShe broke the neck of the flask to get at the contents. That's not incidental staging — a broken vessel can't be resealed or reused for anything less. Once opened this way, all of it had to be spent at once, on this, on Him.","translation":"And while he was in Bethany, a woman came having an alabaster flask of ointment of nard, genuine, very costly; and breaking the flask, she poured it down on his head."},{"c1":14,"v1":6,"c2":14,"v2":9,"label":"Mark 14:6–9","text":"The other disciples' anger — Mark says they grumbled “among themselves,” not Judas alone, as John's account narrows it to — is described with embrimaōmai, a strong verb elsewhere used of horses snorting: not a raised eyebrow but an audible, visceral irritation, and the imperfect tense shows them working each other up rather than one sharp outburst. Jesus calls what she did kalos, not agathos. Both mean “good,” but agathos is goodness weighed on the inside — moral, intrinsic — while kalos is goodness that shows, that strikes the eye as beautiful. He isn't only defending the ethics of her extravagance; he's naming its beauty.\n\nJudas's approach to the chief priests uses paradidomai, “hand over” — the ordinary word for handing someone to the authorities, none of the drama English “betray” carries, just flat transactional language. Their reaction uses chairō rather than agalliaō — Greek distinguishes the inward feeling of joy from its outward display, and the writer picks the inward word: their gladness stayed private and calculated, the satisfaction of men who'd rather not tip their hand before the deal is done.","translation":"But they were snorting with irritation against her, working each other up. But Jesus said, Leave her alone; she has done a beautiful thing to me. And Judas Iscariot went away to the chief priests to hand him over to them. And when they heard it, they were glad within themselves, and promised to give him money."},{"c1":14,"v1":12,"c2":14,"v2":12,"label":"Mark 14:12","text":"To pascha, “the passover,” is built directly on the verb “to pass over” — the name itself is a piece of history compressed into a noun, carrying the memory of the night judgment passed over blood-marked doors in Egypt every time it's spoken.\n\n“When they killed the passover” uses a customary imperfect — not a single act but a yearly habit: this is simply what was always done on this day.","translation":"And on the first day of unleavened bread, when it was the yearly custom to kill the pass-over lamb, his disciples said to him, Where do you want us to go and prepare that you may eat the pass-over?"},{"c1":14,"v1":13,"c2":14,"v2":15,"label":"Mark 14:13–15","text":"The householder's willingness to hand over “my guest-chamber” — the pronoun attached in the genitive — suggests this wasn't a stranger's home commandeered on the spot but an arrangement already understood between him and Jesus beforehand.\n\nThe intensive pronoun in “he himself will show you” matters: the master doesn't send a servant to walk Peter and John up to the room, he goes personally. And “furnished” is a perfect participle — the room was already completed and standing ready before they ever arrived, not being tidied as they walked in.","translation":"Go into the city, and a man carrying a pitcher of water will meet you; follow him. And wherever he enters, say to the master of the house, The Teacher says, Where is my guest-room, where I may eat the pass-over with my disciples? And he himself will show you a large upper room, already furnished, made ready; there prepare for us."},{"c1":14,"v1":16,"c2":14,"v2":16,"label":"Mark 14:16","text":"There isn't much grammar to mine here, but the plain narrative detail carries its own weight: the disciples found everything exactly as he had described it — a second confirmation within the same week that his knowledge of what lay ahead wasn't guesswork. And “they prepared the passover” compresses a genuine afternoon's labor — roasting the lamb, baking the unleavened cakes, preparing bitter herbs and wine, readying the room — into three quiet words.","translation":"And the disciples went out and came into the city, and found it exactly as he had told them; and they prepared the pass-over."},{"c1":14,"v1":17,"c2":14,"v2":21,"label":"Mark 14:17–21","text":"Singling out “one that eateth with me” isn't just identification; in a culture where sharing a table created a bond of loyalty, it names the exact shape of the offense — not an enemy's blow but a friend's. The disciples' question, “Is it I?”, carries a Greek negative particle built to expect the answer “no” — grammatically each man is really asking, “It isn't me, is it?”, already leaning toward his own innocence before the words are even out.\n\nThe dish they all dipped into together held a shared sauce eaten in common — the very act of intimacy at that table becomes, in Judas's hand, the mechanism of the betrayal.","translation":"And as they reclined and were eating, Jesus said, Truly I say to you, one of you will hand me over, one who is eating with me. They began to be grieved and to say to him, one by one, It isn't I, is it? But he said to them, It is one of the twelve, the one dipping with me into the same dish."},{"c1":14,"v1":22,"c2":14,"v2":22,"label":"Mark 14:22","text":"The sequence is fourfold and deliberate: took, blessed, broke, gave — the pattern the church has repeated ever since traces this verse precisely. “This is my body” belongs to the same family of statements Jesus makes elsewhere when he identifies himself with an image — not asserting a literal identity but investing an object with what it pictures. The bread doesn't become his body materially; it becomes the physical sign by which what he did with his body can be received and remembered.","translation":"And as they were eating, he took bread, blessed it, and broke it, and gave it to them, and said, Take; this represents my body."},{"c1":14,"v1":23,"c2":14,"v2":25,"label":"Mark 14:23–25","text":"Jewish Passover practice used four cups; the third, following the meal, was known as “the cup of blessing” — almost certainly what's in view here. “Is shed” is present tense describing something still hours away on the calendar — Jesus speaks of the cross as already underway, viewed from inside the moment rather than forecast from outside it.\n\n“New” in “new in the kingdom of God” is kainos, not neos — not new by the clock, a fresher batch of the same thing, but new in kind, a different order of thing altogether. Whatever is drunk in the coming kingdom won't just be aged less; it will belong to a different category of experience.","translation":"And he took a cup, and having given thanks, gave it to them, and he said to them, This is my blood of the covenant, which is being poured out for many. Truly I say to you, I will by no means drink again of the fruit of the vine until that day when I drink it new — new in kind — in the kingdom of God."},{"c1":14,"v1":26,"c2":14,"v2":26,"label":"Mark 14:26","text":"Humneō, “to sing praise,” is the verb behind our word “hymn.” The walk to Olivet that followed was routine enough by that week that none of the Eleven questioned it — Jerusalem offered them no lodging, so the nightly trip out to the hillside had already become habit.\n\nThe word for “offended” in the prediction that follows is skandalizō — literally to trip over an obstacle set in one's path, to stumble at something that ought instead to command respect. What causes the disciples to scatter is essentially that: Jesus becomes, in that moment, an obstacle their expectations weren't built to absorb. The sentence pivots hard on one small word, alla, “but” — grief on one side of it, the promised reunion in Galilee on the other. The turn from desertion to resurrection happens inside a single conjunction.","translation":"And having sung a hymn of praise, they went out to the Mount of Olives. And Jesus said to them, All of you will be caused to stumble over me tonight; but after I am raised, I will go before you into Galilee."},{"c1":14,"v1":29,"c2":14,"v2":29,"label":"Mark 14:29","text":"Peter's “even if” translates ei, the particle Greek uses for a condition treated as true, not doubtful — grammatically he isn't hedging about whether the others will fail, he's conceding it as fact. What follows, alla (“but”), pulls hard against that concession: given that they will all fall away, he sets himself apart from the very premise he's just granted.","translation":"But Peter said to him, Even granting that all will stumble, yet not I."},{"c1":14,"v1":30,"c2":14,"v2":30,"label":"Mark 14:30","text":"Jesus's reply throws the pronoun su, “you,” forward for emphasis — “you, of all people.” He answers Peter's confidence with matching precision: not just “today” (sēmeron) but “this very night,” and not a single denial but three, and not before one cockcrow but two. The specificity mirrors and undercuts the absoluteness of Peter's boast.","translation":"And Jesus says to him, Truly I say to you, that you — you of all people — today, this very night, before the rooster crows twice, will deny me three times."},{"c1":14,"v1":31,"c2":14,"v2":31,"label":"Mark 14:31","text":"Both verb and adverb reinforce each other here: the verb is imperfect, “he kept on saying,” and the adverb ekperissōs itself means “abundantly, with repetition.” Peter's insistence is described, grammatically, by a word about repeating — the sentence enacts what it reports.","translation":"But he kept on saying, with mounting insistence, over and over, If I must die with you, I will by no means deny you."},{"c1":14,"v1":32,"c2":14,"v2":34,"label":"Mark 14:32–34","text":"Three words stack up to describe what came over Jesus in the garden. Ekthambeō, “sore amazed,” is a strong verb for being thrown into terror or shock — closer to overwhelming disorientation than ordinary surprise. Adēmoneō is built on a word meaning “away from home” — the discomfort of being somewhere entirely unfamiliar, facing something never before experienced. And perilupos joins lupē, “grief,” with peri, “around” — grief that doesn't sit in one place but surrounds, saturates. “Unto death” (heōs thanatou) pushes it further still: sorrow heavy enough that it felt, in the moment, almost fatal in itself.","translation":"And they came to a place named Gethsemane, and he began to be thrown into terror and shock, and to be sorely troubled, as one away from home in what he faced. And he says to them, My soul is grief surrounded, grief soaked through, even to the point of death; remain here and keep awake."},{"c1":14,"v1":35,"c2":14,"v2":35,"label":"Mark 14:35","text":"Both main verbs are imperfect — “was falling,” “was praying” — describing repeated, ongoing action rather than a single collapse and a single petition. He kept sinking to the ground and kept praying; this was a sustained struggle, not one dramatic moment. “The hour” had been on his horizon for a long time before this night arrived, referenced again and again earlier in his ministry as something still ahead. Now that it had finally come, even having seen it coming for so long didn't make him dread it any less.","translation":"And going forward a little, he kept falling to the ground, and kept praying that, if it were possible, the hour might pass from him."},{"c1":14,"v1":36,"c2":14,"v2":36,"label":"Mark 14:36","text":"Abba is Aramaic, left untranslated and then immediately glossed with the Greek patēr, “father” — a term of close, familial address rather than formal petition, preserved in its original tongue because the moment was too intimate to render only in translation. The prayer's final line balances two wills against each other with a pronoun placed beside each verb: not what I myself want, but what you yourself want — the grammar keeps both wills distinct even as one yields to the other.\n\nSomething shifts in how Mark names Peter here. Since his call, Mark has consistently used “Peter” — the identity Jesus gave him. Now, waking him from sleep, it's “Simon” again, the old name, the one that belonged to him before that identity was conferred. The lapse in the disciple's role is marked by a lapse in his name. “The spirit indeed is willing, but the flesh is weak” sets pneuma against sarx — the spirit as the part of a person open to God, capable and eager; the flesh as the body in its plain material frailty, willing in theory and unequal to the moment in practice.\n\nBy the third visit Jesus's words shift register — “sleep on now, and take your rest” reads as command but functions as irony, permission granted precisely because it's too late to matter, immediately followed by “it is enough, the hour has come” and “rise, let us be going.” The same imperative mood that a moment ago meant “keep watching” now grants, sardonically, what watching was meant to prevent.","translation":"And he said, Abba, Father — all things are possible to you; remove this cup from me; yet not what I myself will, but what you yourself will. And he comes and finds them sleeping, and says to Simon, Simon, are you sleeping? Were you not strong enough to keep awake one hour? The spirit indeed is eager, but the flesh is without strength. And he comes the third time, and says to them, Sleep on now, and take your rest — it is enough; the hour has come; behold, the Son of Man is being handed over into the hands of sinners. Rise up, let us be going."},{"c1":14,"v1":43,"c2":14,"v2":43,"label":"Mark 14:43","text":"The words announcing the betrayer's approach and Judas's actual arrival happen almost as one sentence — Mark's “immediately, while he was still speaking” leaves no gap between prediction and fulfillment. The crowd itself was a hastily assembled mix — temple police, servants of the high priest, members of the council — armed with swords and clubs, the sort of equipment brought for an expected fight, not the quiet arrest of a man who had taught in public all week.","translation":"And immediately, while he was still speaking, Judas, one of the twelve, arrives, and with him a crowd with swords and clubs, from the chief priests and the scribes and the elders."},{"c1":14,"v1":44,"c2":14,"v2":44,"label":"Mark 14:44","text":"Sussēmon, “token” or “sign,” is a compound built from “together” and “sign” — a signal agreed on in advance between two parties. The kiss itself was the ordinary greeting given to a rabbi, which is exactly what makes it work as betrayal — a gesture of respect turned into a marker for soldiers. Judas doesn't stop at the minimum signal either; he adds “lead him away safely,” tactical advice beyond what was asked, the mark of a man now personally invested in making sure the plan succeeds. When he actually greets Jesus, the verb changes from a plain “kiss” to kataphileō, kiss intensified by a prefix — not a peck but a lingering, deliberately affectionate one, which makes the hypocrisy sharper still.","translation":"Now the one handing him over had given them an agreed-upon sign, saying, Whomever I kiss, he it is; seize him and lead him away under guard, safely. And having come, immediately going up to him, he says, Rabbi, Rabbi, and kissed him fervently, lingering over it."},{"c1":14,"v1":47,"c2":14,"v2":47,"label":"Mark 14:47","text":"The sword carries a definite article in the Greek — “his sword” — implying this wasn't the only weapon in the group; each man may have been carrying one. What actually happened, an ear taken off rather than a wound placed with any precision, reads as a wild, full swing that missed its target. Whoever struck was not attempting a minor injury; he was aiming for the head and caught the ear instead.","translation":"But a certain one of those standing by drew his sword, and struck the servant of the high priest, and cut off his ear."},{"c1":14,"v1":48,"c2":14,"v2":48,"label":"Mark 14:48","text":"Jesus doesn't dispute that he's being arrested — he disputes how. “Brigand” names an armed, violent criminal, the kind of man who requires swords and clubs to bring in. He points out the contrast without needing to state it outright: a public teacher, seized like a highway robber.","translation":"And Jesus answered and said to them, Have you come out, as against a brigand, with swords and clubs to arrest me?"},{"c1":14,"v1":49,"c2":14,"v2":49,"label":"Mark 14:49","text":"The word “daily” underlines just how available he had been — teaching openly in the temple courts on repeated days that same week. The arrest, by contrast, comes at night, by stealth, with an armed crowd — the difference in method says more than the arrest itself, and Mark frames both as the deliberate fulfillment of what had already been written.","translation":"Day after day I was with you in the temple teaching, and you did not seize me; but this is happening that the scriptures might be fulfilled."},{"c1":14,"v1":50,"c2":14,"v2":52,"label":"Mark 14:50–52","text":"The desertion is total — “all of them,” no exception carved out. The detail of the young man fleeing naked, leaving the linen sheet in his captors' hands, appears nowhere else in the Gospels, which has long led readers to suspect it's Mark's own quiet signature on his account — a detail kept not because it mattered to the story but because it mattered to the person who lived it. The cloth wrapped hastily “around his nakedness,” rather than a robe properly worn, suggests someone roused from sleep and thrown on whatever was nearest — which is also exactly why it came off so easily when a hand closed on it.","translation":"And they all left him and fled. And a certain young man was following him, having a linen cloth thrown around his bare body; and they seize him; but leaving the linen cloth behind, he fled naked."},{"c1":14,"v1":57,"c2":14,"v2":59,"label":"Mark 14:57–59","text":"The false testimony misquotes Jesus in a way that changes the grammar, not just the words. What he had actually said used a future statement in the first person — “I will destroy” — a prediction about what would happen to him. The version the witnesses report uses a second-person plural command — “you destroy” — turning a prediction about his own death and resurrection into a threat aimed at the building itself. The added phrases “made with hands” and “without hands” pin the accusation specifically to the Jerusalem temple, in case anyone missed that the witnesses had misapplied a saying that was never about stone and mortar to begin with.","translation":"We heard him say, I will destroy this temple made with hands, and in three days I will build another made without hands. Even so their testimony did not agree."},{"c1":14,"v1":60,"c2":14,"v2":60,"label":"Mark 14:60","text":"Mark notes the physical movement: the high priest doesn't just speak from his seat, he rises and steps forward into the middle of the council toward Jesus. It reads as a man supplying with posture what he lacks in evidence — pressing the moment theatrically because the testimony gathered so far hasn't given him anything solid to work with.","translation":"And the high priest, rising up, stepped into the middle and questioned Jesus, saying, Do you answer nothing? What is it these men testify against you?"},{"c1":14,"v1":61,"c2":14,"v2":61,"label":"Mark 14:61","text":"Jesus's silence is described with an imperfect verb — he kept on maintaining it, an ongoing, deliberate refusal rather than a single non-answer. Answering the charge at all, true or false, would have handed this illegitimate proceeding a legitimacy it hadn't earned. “The Blessed” (eulogētos) is used here on its own, without “God” attached, as a reverent stand-in for the divine name itself — the kind of circumlocution used to avoid pronouncing it directly. The high priest's question, “Are you the Christ, the Son of the Blessed,” is really asking whether Jesus claims to be God's Anointed — but silence toward the charge, absolute silence under a formal oath, was its own kind of answer under Jewish legal custom, one that made a reply almost unavoidable.","translation":"But he kept on remaining silent and answered nothing. Again the high priest questioned him and says to him, Are you the Anointed One, the Son of the Blessed One?"},{"c1":14,"v1":62,"c2":14,"v2":62,"label":"Mark 14:62","text":"The answer comes with the pronoun spelled out for emphasis — “I, in contrast to everyone else here, am.” What follows fuses two Old Testament images: the enthroned figure “at the right hand” from Psalm 110, and the cloud-riding figure “like a son of man” from Daniel 7 — royal authority and apocalyptic arrival braided into a single claim. To anyone in that room who knew those texts, there was no ambiguity about what was being claimed.","translation":"And Jesus said, I — I myself, in contrast to all of you — am; and you will see the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of Power, and coming amid the clouds of heaven."},{"c1":14,"v1":63,"c2":14,"v2":65,"label":"Mark 14:63–65","text":"Tearing his garments, the high priest destroys not one layer of clothing but the plural chitōnas — the undergarments a man of his position wore in pairs. It's an old gesture of horror going back to Genesis, and the law specifically forbade a high priest from tearing his clothes over private grief — which means this wasn't personal emotion breaking through, it was a formal, required legal response to what he judged blasphemy.\n\nPerikaluptō, “cover all around,” describes wrapping something completely over Jesus's head — the blindfold for the mocking game that followed, daring him to name his attacker by something other than sight. Kolaphizō, “buffet,” specifically means striking with a closed fist — not slaps but blows.","translation":"And the high priest, tearing his tunics — both of them — says, What further need do we have of witnesses? And some began to spit on him, and to cover his face completely, and to strike him with their fists, and to say to him, Prophesy! And the officers received him with slaps."},{"c1":14,"v1":66,"c2":14,"v2":68,"label":"Mark 14:66–68","text":"Emblepsasa, “having looked at him,” describes a fixed, searching stare, not a passing glance — the servant girl studies Peter's face before she ever speaks, exactly the kind of attention he was hoping to avoid by sitting at the fire. His denial answers with two separate verbs — “I know not, neither understand” — refusing both awareness of the fact and comprehension of her words, a double-layered disclaimer rather than a simple no.","translation":"And a servant girl of the high priest came, and seeing Peter warming himself, stared fixedly at him and says, You also were with the Nazarene, Jesus. But he denied it, saying, I neither know nor understand what you are saying."},{"c1":14,"v1":69,"c2":14,"v2":71,"label":"Mark 14:69–71","text":"By his third denial Peter escalates into formal legal language. Anathematizō, “to curse,” is the verb for placing oneself under a binding curse — the same word Paul later uses in Galatians for anyone who preaches a false gospel; it's an invocation, “let this happen to me if I'm lying,” not casual language and certainly not profanity in the modern sense. The verb for “swear” is the same one used elsewhere of God swearing an oath on himself — solemn, covenantal language, now pressed into service by a man trying to save his own skin, each denial more binding than the last.","translation":"But he began to invoke a curse upon himself and to swear with a solemn oath, I do not know this man of whom you speak."},{"c1":14,"v1":72,"c2":14,"v2":72,"label":"Mark 14:72","text":"Epiballōn, translated “when he thought thereon,” literally means “having thrown upon” — Mark pictures memory as something hurled onto a target: the second crow of the rooster lands, and Peter's mind snaps immediately onto the prediction made hours earlier. The verb for weeping is an inchoative imperfect, “he began to weep” — a picture of tears starting and continuing, unraveling, where Matthew's account instead uses a single decisive burst. Same moment, two different angles on how the grief actually broke.","translation":"And immediately a rooster crowed a second time. And Peter flung his mind back upon the word Jesus had spoken to him, and throwing himself upon it, he began to weep, and kept on weeping."},{"c1":15,"v1":3,"c2":15,"v2":5,"label":"Mark 15:3-5","text":"The verbs describing the accusation and the questioning are both imperfect tense — not a single volley of charges but a steady battering, one accusation piling onto another while Pilate keeps pressing him, again and again, for a response. Against that barrage, Jesus's silence is total: he answers not even one thing, an emphatic construction in the Greek that leaves no room for a single word slipping through. Pilate's amazement is likewise sustained rather than momentary — a governor who had presided over many trials had never watched a defendant decline every chance to speak in his own defense.","translation":"And the chief priests kept accusing him of many things. And Pilate kept questioning him again, Do you answer nothing? See how many things they accuse you of. But Jesus answered not even one thing more, so that Pilate kept marveling."},{"c1":15,"v1":6,"c2":15,"v2":8,"label":"Mark 15:6-8","text":"The imperfect tense running through this scene signals custom, not a one-time event — releasing a prisoner at Passover was something Pilate regularly did, an expected part of the festival. Barabbas is introduced as \"the one commonly known as\" — the phrasing of a man whose name the crowd already recognized, notorious for having taken part in an insurrection against Rome in which men had died. The crowd's approach to Pilate is described with the verb \"go up\" (anabas), which plants the scene in real geography — a mob climbing toward the judgment seat to make its demand.","translation":"Now at the feast he was accustomed to release to them one prisoner, whomever they asked. And there was one called Barabbas, bound with the insurrectionists who had committed murder in the insurrection. And the crowd, going up, began to ask him to do as he had always done for them."},{"c1":15,"v1":9,"c2":15,"v2":10,"label":"Mark 15:9-10","text":"\"Perceived\" translates an imperfect verb, better rendered \"it was gradually dawning on him\" — Pilate's read on the priests' motive was not a flash of insight but a realization that grew as the proceedings wore on. What dawned on him was envy: the accusations against Jesus, oddly disproportionate for a mere troublemaker, made sense once jealousy of his popularity with the crowds was factored in. Pilate sees through the charge of sedition to the real grievance underneath it.","translation":"But Pilate answered them, saying, Do you want me to release to you the King of the Jews? For it was gradually dawning on him that it was out of envy the chief priests had handed him over."},{"c1":15,"v1":11,"c2":15,"v2":12,"label":"Mark 15:11-12","text":"The chief priests' \"stirring up\" of the crowd pictures active agitation — shaking loose a mob that had come expecting one outcome into demanding another. Pilate's question deliberately repeats their own language back to them — \"him whom you call King of the Jews\" — distancing himself from the title while forcing the crowd to either own it or reject it out loud. Their answer, in the next verse, settles which way they go.","translation":"But the chief priests stirred up the crowd, agitating it, that he should release Barabbas to them instead. And Pilate, answering again, said to them, What then shall I do with him whom you call the King of the Jews?"},{"c1":15,"v1":13,"c2":15,"v2":15,"label":"Mark 15:13-15","text":"The adverb translated \"beyond measure\" marks the crowd's second outcry as louder and more violent than the first — escalation, not repetition. Pilate's question, \"what evil has he done,\" calls for an answer grounded in fact, but a mob has no reasons to give beyond its own will, and volume is the only response it can offer. The scourging that follows was the Roman flagellum, a lash weighted with bone or metal, and Peter later remembers it in the phrase \"by his stripes you were healed\" — the Greek noun there is singular, naming a single bloody welt rather than a series of marks. The picture is not stripes in the plural but one continuous field of torn, raw flesh.","translation":"And they cried out again, still more beyond measure, Crucify him! So Pilate, wishing to satisfy the crowd, released Barabbas to them, and having scourged Jesus with the lash until his flesh was one continuous, torn wound, he handed him over to be crucified."},{"c1":15,"v1":16,"c2":15,"v2":16,"label":"Mark 15:16","text":"\"The courtyard, which is the Praetorium\" names the governor's residence and headquarters, and \"the whole band\" is a Roman cohort — the full unit called together for sport, not a handful of guards. These soldiers were provincial auxiliaries serving under Roman command, not native Jews, which explains why mocking a condemned man as a Jewish king struck them as entertainment rather than sacrilege.","translation":"And the soldiers led him away inside the courtyard, that is, the Praetorium; and they called together the whole cohort."},{"c1":15,"v1":17,"c2":15,"v2":18,"label":"Mark 15:17-18","text":"The \"purple\" was in fact a faded scarlet military cloak, its color close enough to royal purple to carry the joke. The word for \"crown\" here is not the word for a royal diadem but for the victor's wreath awarded for military or athletic triumph — the soldiers are parodying the laurel crown given to a conquering general, and twisting the honor into torment, since the thorny branches woven together would tear the scalp with every movement of his head.","translation":"And they clothe him with a scarlet military cloak, and having plaited a victor's wreath of thorns, they put it on him, and began to salute him, Hail, King of the Jews!"},{"c1":15,"v1":19,"c2":15,"v2":20,"label":"Mark 15:19-20","text":"The mock homage is described with the verb for falling down before a king or a god, and it stands in the imperfect tense — not one gesture but an extended charade of bowed knees and false salutes, repeated and drawn out. The same tense governs the beating and the spitting: this was sustained abuse, not a single blow.","translation":"And they kept striking his head with a reed, and kept spitting on him, and kneeling down, kept doing homage to him, bowing again and again."},{"c1":15,"v1":21,"c2":15,"v2":21,"label":"Mark 15:21","text":"\"Compelled\" translates a loanword from Persian administrative practice, describing the royal couriers who had legal authority to commandeer horses, boats, or men on the spot to keep the king's messages moving. The term had passed into the Greek of Roman-occupied Palestine as the standard word for forced impressment — a soldier's right to conscript a bystander's labor without explanation or appeal, which is exactly what happens to Simon. He is a Jew from Cyrene, a North African city with a substantial Jewish community; nothing in the text marks him as a follower beforehand, only as a man in the wrong place at the wrong time, made to shoulder a stranger's cross.","translation":"And they impress into service a certain passer-by, Simon of Cyrene, coming from the country, that he might carry his cross."},{"c1":15,"v1":22,"c2":15,"v2":23,"label":"Mark 15:22-23","text":"\"Bring\" translates a verb broad enough to mean either \"carry\" or simply \"lead,\" and the ambiguity leaves a real question open: had the scourging left Jesus so weakened that he had to be physically supported, the way the sick were carried to him earlier in the Gospel, or is the word simply describing his being led out to execution like any condemned man? The Greek does not settle it. \"They gave him\" wine mixed with myrrh is a conative imperfect — \"they tried to give,\" \"they offered\" — the customary drugged drink meant to dull a victim's pain before crucifixion. He refused it, choosing to face the cross with unclouded senses.","translation":"And they lead him — or carry him — to the place Golgotha. And they were trying to give him wine mixed with myrrh; but he did not take it."},{"c1":15,"v1":24,"c2":15,"v2":28,"label":"Mark 15:24-28","text":"The \"third hour\" by Jewish reckoning was nine in the morning, and the mockery, the division of his clothing by lot, and the crucifixion itself are compressed into a single terse sentence with no lingering on the physical horror. The \"inscription\" was a board naming the charge against him, customarily carried before the condemned on the way to execution and then fixed above him on the cross — Rome's routine method of publicizing why a man died, turned here into an inscription that told more truth than anyone intended.","translation":"And it was the third hour, and they crucify him, and divide his garments among themselves, casting lots for them, to decide what each should take. And the inscription of the charge against him was written above: THE KING OF THE JEWS."},{"c1":15,"v1":29,"c2":15,"v2":32,"label":"Mark 15:29-32","text":"The verb for their taunting is the same word used elsewhere for reviling God — the crowd's mockery of the dying man carries, without their meaning it to, the very charge the Sanhedrin had leveled against him at his trial. The religious leaders are careful not to address him directly; they mock him in the third person, remarking to one another rather than to him — contempt kept at arm's length. And their version of the inscription reworks Pilate's political title into their own religious vocabulary: not \"King of the Jews\" but \"the Christ, the King of Israel\" — even as mockery, unintentionally naming him exactly what he was.","translation":"And those passing by kept reviling him, blaspheming, wagging their heads. Likewise also the chief priests, mocking among themselves with the scribes, kept saying, He saved others; himself he cannot save. Let the Anointed One, the King of Israel, come down now from the cross, that we may see and believe."},{"c1":15,"v1":33,"c2":15,"v2":34,"label":"Mark 15:33-34","text":"The darkness covers the hours from noon to three, the very stretch of the day when the temple's regular sacrifices would have been proceeding as usual — the sky going dark over the act that would end sacrifice's necessity. \"Forsaken\" translates a compound verb whose root sense is \"to leave down in\" — the picture is of someone left abandoned inside circumstances too heavy for him, helpless, left in the lurch. The cry itself is preserved in its original Aramaic before being translated into Greek, a rare moment where the Gospel keeps the actual sound of his voice rather than only its sense.","translation":"And when the sixth hour came, darkness came over the whole land until the ninth hour. And at the ninth hour Jesus cried out with a loud voice, Eloi, Eloi, lama sabachthani? which is, being translated, My God, my God, why have you left me down, abandoned, in this?"},{"c1":15,"v1":35,"c2":15,"v2":36,"label":"Mark 15:35-36","text":"The bystanders' confusion — mistaking his cry for a call to Elijah — trades on the real similarity between the divine name and Elijah's name in Aramaic, and Elijah was popularly expected to intervene for the righteous in their extremity. The \"vinegar\" offered him was the cheap soured-wine-and-water drink Roman soldiers carried for their own refreshment; whoever offers it seems less interested in mercy than in keeping him alive long enough to see whether Elijah would actually show up.","translation":"And some of those standing by, hearing it, said, Look, he is calling Elijah. And someone ran and filled a sponge with the cheap soured wine, and put it on a reed, and gave him to drink, saying, Let him be; let us see whether Elijah comes to take him down."},{"c1":15,"v1":37,"c2":15,"v2":39,"label":"Mark 15:37-39","text":"\"Gave up the ghost\" represents a single Greek verb meaning simply \"to breathe out,\" \"to expire\" — a plain, almost clinical term for death, with no separate word for \"spirit\" actually present at this point in the text; the older English phrase sounds more mystical than the underlying Greek does. The temple veil that tears is the curtain of the inner sanctuary specifically, not the outer courts — the screen before the Holy of Holies, the one place on earth where God's presence was said to dwell, entered by only the high priest, only once a year. Its tearing from top to bottom marks something done from God's side, not torn by human hands. The centurion's declaration lacks the definite article in Greek — not \"the Son of God\" but \"a son of God,\" about as far as a Roman soldier's categories could reach, recognizing something more than human in what he had just watched without necessarily grasping the full weight later readers hear in the words.","translation":"And Jesus, uttering a loud cry, breathed out his last. And the veil of the sanctuary was torn in two, from top to bottom. And the centurion, who stood facing him, having seen that he breathed out his last in this way, said, Truly this man was a son of God."},{"c1":15,"v1":40,"c2":15,"v2":41,"label":"Mark 15:40-41","text":"\"Looking on\" translates a verb for sustained, attentive watching, not a passing glance — these women kept the cross in steady view from a distance, near enough to see everything, far enough to stay clear of the crowd's cruelty, and they did not look away.","translation":"There were also women looking on from a distance, continually keeping him in view, among them Mary Magdalene."},{"c1":15,"v1":42,"c2":15,"v2":43,"label":"Mark 15:42-43","text":"\"Already\" carries real urgency — Joseph's errand had to be finished before sundown began the Sabbath, or the body would go to the common grave reserved for executed criminals. He is identified as a councillor, a member of the Jerusalem Sanhedrin itself, not merely a local official of his hometown — meaning one Sanhedrist is now petitioning Pilate for the very body another Sanhedrist had pressed him to crucify. \"Taking courage\" is a strong word for daring something genuinely risky: walking in to ask a Roman governor for the corpse of a man just executed for sedition was not a safe thing for a prominent Jew to be seen doing.","translation":"And when evening had already come, since it was the Preparation, that is, the day before the Sabbath, Joseph of Arimathea, a councillor of honorable standing, who himself was also looking for the kingdom of God, came, and taking courage, dared to go in to Pilate and asked for the body of Jesus."},{"c1":15,"v1":44,"c2":15,"v2":45,"label":"Mark 15:44-45","text":"Pilate's astonishment plays across two different tenses: he \"wondered\" — a perfect, framing death as an already-settled state — whether Jesus \"had already died\" — an aorist, asking about the single moment death occurred. Crucifixion typically dragged on for days, so a death within hours needed confirming before the body could be released. Once satisfied, Pilate \"freely gives\" the body over, not from generosity but relief at being rid of an unwelcome case, and the noun handed over is not the ordinary word for a living body but a blunt, clinical word for a corpse.","translation":"And Pilate wondered whether he was, by now, already dead; and summoning the centurion, he asked him whether he had already died. And having learned it from the centurion, he freely gave over the corpse to Joseph."},{"c1":15,"v1":46,"c2":15,"v2":47,"label":"Mark 15:46-47","text":"The linen Joseph buys is cloth by the length, raw material rather than a finished garment — he purchases what's needed and does the wrapping himself. The tomb, freshly cut into rock and never used, and the stone rolled against its opening, complete a proper burial done in haste before the Sabbath closes in. The two women who stay behind to watch where he was laid do so carefully, taking deliberate note of the exact place — witnesses, whether they intended it or not, to precisely which tomb would be found empty three days later.","translation":"And having bought a linen cloth, he took him down, and wrapped him in the linen, and laid him in a tomb which had been cut out of rock, one in which no one had yet been laid; and he rolled a stone against the door of the tomb. And Mary Magdalene and Mary the mother of Joses kept looking on, taking careful note of where he was laid."},{"c1":16,"v1":5,"c2":16,"v2":5,"label":"Mark 16:5","text":"The \"young man\" in white is wearing a *stolē* (στολή) — not everyday clothing but a long, sweeping, trailing robe reserved for occasions of special dignity or solemnity. It's not what an ordinary young man would be wearing at a tomb at dawn, and the description itself is the clue: this is no human visitor. \"Affrighted\" translates *ekthambeō* (ἐκθαμβέω), the ordinary word for amazement with the preposition *ek* prefixed to intensify it — the sense runs closer to \"utterly astounded\" than to simple fear. The women weren't so much frightened as stunned past words by what they were looking at.\n\nIt's also worth noting that the resurrection accounts across the four Gospels don't line up in every detail of who arrived when or saw what first. Rather than undermining the event, that's exactly the texture you'd expect from independent witnesses describing a real, disorienting morning — the core fact holds steady across all four even where each writer's chosen details differ.","translation":"And entering the tomb, they saw a young man sitting on the right side, clothed in a long white robe of dignity; and they were utterly astounded."},{"c1":16,"v1":6,"c2":16,"v2":8,"label":"Mark 16:6–8","text":"The angel's opening words pick up the same verb used for the women's reaction in verse 5 — \"stop being utterly amazed,\" not a fresh \"don't be afraid.\" A negated present-tense command like this points to stopping something already in progress rather than heading off something that hasn't started yet: they're mid-astonishment, and he's telling them to come out of it.\n\nThe scene ends on that same note of overwhelmed silence — the women flee the tomb trembling, and say nothing to anyone, \"for they were afraid.\" Mark's Gospel, in its earliest and best-attested manuscripts, breaks off right there, on fear rather than triumphant announcement. That abrupt ending is almost certainly why later copyists felt the need to round the book out, which is where the material in verses 9–20 comes from.","translation":"But he says to them, Stop being utterly amazed. You are seeking Jesus the Nazarene, the crucified one; he was raised, he is not here. And going out, they fled from the tomb, for trembling and astonishment had seized them; and they said nothing to anyone, for they were afraid."},{"c1":16,"v1":9,"c2":16,"v2":11,"label":"Mark 16:9–11","text":"The name \"Jesus\" that opens verse 9 isn't actually in the Greek — the text simply resumes with \"having risen early on the first day of the week,\" trusting the reader to carry the subject forward from what came before. English can't leave a subject unstated that way, so every translation quietly supplies the name.\n\nThese final twelve verses are the most textually disputed stretch in Mark — many of the oldest surviving manuscripts break off before this point — but since they stand in every widely used translation, they're worth reading through with the same care as the rest of the Gospel rather than passing over in silence. Mary Magdalene, named first among the witnesses to the risen Christ, is identified by her past: the one out of whom seven demons had been driven. Placing that detail here, right before the resurrection appearance, is no accident — the first person to see the risen Lord was the one whose deliverance had been most complete.","translation":"Now having risen early on the first day of the week, he appeared first to Mary Magdalene, out of whom he had driven seven demons. She went and reported it to those who had been with him, as they mourned and wept. And when they heard that he was alive and had been seen by her, they refused to believe it."},{"c1":16,"v1":12,"c2":16,"v2":13,"label":"Mark 16:12–13","text":"\"In another form\" is literally \"in a different outward expression\" — *heterā morphē* — and *morphē* is the same word used earlier in the Gospel for Christ's appearance at the Transfiguration. That verbal echo can mislead: this isn't a return of that dazzling glory. The two on the road simply failed to recognize Him — nothing in His bearing marked Him out from any ordinary traveler, the same way Mary at the tomb had first taken Him for the gardener. The \"different form\" each witness describes turns out to be a description of their own failure to recognize Him, not evidence of some visible change in His body.","translation":"And after these things he was manifested in a different outward form to two of them, as they walked, going into the country. And these went away and reported it to the rest; neither did they believe them."},{"c1":16,"v1":16,"c2":16,"v2":16,"label":"Mark 16:16","text":"The verse's own structure makes a point by what it leaves out. \"The one who believes and is baptized will be saved\" is answered by \"the one who disbelieves will be condemned\" — not \"the one who disbelieves and is not baptized.\" Baptism drops out of the negative half entirely. Belief alone stands against disbelief alone as the deciding factor; baptism appears in the positive statement as belief's natural expression, not as a separate condition tacked onto salvation.\n\n\"Shall be damned\" is also a stronger rendering than the underlying Greek supports. The word is a plain judicial term meaning \"to judge\" or \"to condemn\" — on its own it says nothing about the nature, degree, or duration of the sentence. \"Condemned\" gives the actual sense; any picture of final, eternal punishment has to come from elsewhere in Scripture, not from this word by itself.","translation":"The one who believes and is baptized will be saved; but the one who disbelieves will be condemned."},{"c1":16,"v1":17,"c2":16,"v2":18,"label":"Mark 16:17–18","text":"The \"signs\" here (*sēmeia*) are evidentiary miracles by definition — attesting signs meant to authenticate a message, not spectacles for their own sake. The list sketches the shape of the apostolic age rather than a permanent charter: exorcism and healing continue Christ's own ministry, new tongues mark Pentecost and its immediate aftermath, and the more startling items — handling snakes, drinking poison unharmed — read like markers of a specific missionary moment rather than a promise extended flatly to every believer in every era.\n\nThis is also the stretch of the chapter where textual scholars register the strongest doubts about originality, for the same reasons noted at verses 9–11. Whatever one concludes about how these verses entered the text, within Scripture as we have it the passage describes a particular apostolic moment — it isn't a standing guarantee detached from that setting.","translation":"And these attesting signs will accompany those who believe: in my name they will cast out demons; they will speak with new tongues; they will pick up serpents, and if they drink anything deadly, it will by no means hurt them; they will lay hands on the sick, and they will be well."}],"Romans":[{"c1":1,"v1":2,"c2":1,"v2":4,"label":"Romans 1:2–4","text":"Paul's “prophets” here isn't limited to the major and minor prophets by name — it covers anyone through whom God spoke in advance, Moses and David included, since the promise of good news runs all through the Old Testament, not just the prophetic books. “Holy scriptures” drops the definite article in the Greek, throwing the weight onto quality rather than pointing at a specific known collection — these writings are holy by their very character, because they carry God's news.\n\n“Was made” translates ginomai in an aorist participle, the verb of becoming rather than being — the same verb John uses for “the Word became flesh.” It marks entrance into a new condition: the eternal Son took on a human body and human limits, tracing his ancestry, “according to the flesh,” from David's line. “Declared” is a different verb, horizō, “to mark out the boundary, fix, appoint” — the root of our word “horizon.” It doesn't mean Jesus became Son of God at the resurrection; it means the resurrection is where that sonship, already and always true, was publicly marked out and put on display. Paul is careful with his preposition too — the Greek says only “of the dead” (nekrōn), not “out from among the dead,” tying Christ's rising to the resurrection of the dead as a category he inaugurates, not just his own solitary comeback.","translation":"concerning his Son — who came to be, out of David's seed, according to the flesh; who was marked out as Son of God in power, according to the Spirit of holiness, by resurrection of the dead."},{"c1":1,"v1":5,"c2":1,"v2":7,"label":"Romans 1:5–7","text":"Paul's phrase “obedience of faith” is a genitive construction that cuts both ways in Greek — it can mean the obedience that consists in believing, or the obedience that faith itself produces once a person believes. Paul doesn't disambiguate; his commission from Christ is to bring the nations to whichever it is. “For his name's sake” uses the Old Testament idiom “the Name” — shorthand for the whole of who God has revealed himself to be, his reputation and character bundled into a single word, the way we'd say a man “has a name” for something. Paul's apostleship exists in honor of that whole revealed character.\n\n“Called” (klētoi) is not a title someone earns or a label pinned on afterward — it's the effectual summons itself, the moment God's call reaches in and makes a person willing to receive it. Paul stacks two of these at the end: the Roman believers are “beloved of God” — plural, “God's loved-ones,” love in the sense of the cross — and “called saints,” which in the Greek is simply adjective plus noun, “called holy-ones.” It isn't that they were named and, separately, made holy; the calling itself is what constitutes them set-apart people, cut off from the ordinary and claimed for God.","translation":"Through whom we received grace and a commission as apostle, to bring about among all the nations an obedience that flows from faith, for the sake of his Name — among whom you also are, called ones belonging to Jesus Christ — to all who are in Rome, God's loved-ones, called and thereby made holy ones. Grace to you and peace from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ."},{"c1":1,"v1":8,"c2":1,"v2":8,"label":"Romans 1:8","text":"Paul's “first” here never gets a “second” — the thought runs on without the orderly list he seems to promise, which is just how the man's mind worked when he was excited. “Spoken of” translates kataggellō, a compound that means more than “reported” — it's to broadcast something down the line, hearer to hearer, with an implied note of commendation built in. The world wasn't just hearing gossip about the Roman church; it was hearing them praised. And planting a healthy, visibly faithful church in the capital of the empire — a city famous even among pagan writers for its appetite for vice — was itself a kind of proof that this new faith came from somewhere beyond Rome's own resources.","translation":"First, I am continually giving thanks to my God through Jesus Christ concerning all of you, because your faith is being proclaimed abroad — with praise attached — in the whole world."},{"c1":1,"v1":9,"c2":1,"v2":12,"label":"Romans 1:9–12","text":"“Witness” is martus, the word behind our “martyr” — someone testifying to what he's personally seen or known, even when it costs him. Paul calls on that kind of testimony because his prayer life for these strangers in Rome is invisible to everyone but God. “Serve” is latreuō, priestly language, the word used for the temple ministers' sacred duties — but Paul locates his priesthood “in my spirit,” his own inner life, not in an outward ritual performed at an altar. “Without ceasing” is built from a root meaning “to leave off” with a flat negative stuck on the front — not occasional prayer but prayer with no gaps in it.\n\n“Prosperous journey” renders euodoō, a verb that originally pictured a short, easy road but had broadened by Paul's day to mean simply “to be granted success” in any venture. He piles up qualifiers — if somehow, now, at last — to show just how long he's been wanting this trip and how uncertain he still is that it will happen. And when he explains why, he catches himself mid-sentence: he'd meant to say he wanted to give them a “spiritual gift,” a charisma, but he softens it into something mutual — the verb is sumparakaleō, “to strengthen together with,” not one expert handing down help to novices but two parties leaning on each other's faith.","translation":"For God is my witness, whom I am continually serving in my own spirit in the good news concerning his Son, how without ceasing I keep making mention of you, always asking in my prayers whether somehow, now at last, I might be granted a way to come to you — for I long to see you, so that I may impart to you some gift of the Spirit for your establishing, that is, that we may be strengthened together, each of us by the other's faith, yours and mine alike."},{"c1":1,"v1":13,"c2":1,"v2":13,"label":"Romans 1:13","text":"“Purposed” is protithēmi, literally “to set a thing out in front of yourself” — a deliberate plan, not a passing wish. The old “let” in “was let” is archaic English for “hindered”; the Greek verb, kōluō, means to cut something short before it can happen. And “have,” as in “have some fruit,” is an ingressive aorist — it isn't a state Paul wants to sit in but a result he wants to reach: not “keep having fruit” but “come to obtain it,” a onetime crossing of the finish line.","translation":"I do not want you to be ignorant, brothers, that many times I planned to come to you — and was cut short until now — so that I might obtain some fruit among you too, just as among the rest of the nations."},{"c1":1,"v1":14,"c2":1,"v2":15,"label":"Romans 1:14–15","text":"“Debtor” is opheiletēs, a word for personal moral obligation — Paul owes the world his gospel not because circumstances force it on him but because grace laid a debt on him that only preaching can pay off. “Barbarian,” barbaros, began as pure sound-mimicry — the babbling “bar-bar” the Greeks heard when foreigners spoke — and hardened into the word for anyone outside Greek language and culture. And the pair “wise” and “unwise” carried a moral edge in Greek that's gone from the English: sophos wasn't mere cleverness but wisdom tied to goodness, while anoētos described a lack of understanding rooted in a moral failure, not simple ignorance. Paul's debt runs to every kind of person on both sides of every line the ancient world drew.","translation":"Both to Greeks and to those who only babble, both to the wise and to those wanting in wisdom, I stand as a debtor. So, as far as it depends on me, I am eager to preach the good news to you also, who are in Rome."},{"c1":1,"v1":16,"c2":1,"v2":17,"label":"Romans 1:16–17","text":"Paul had six different Greek words for “power” to choose from, and he reaches for dunamis — inherent, exerted power, the capacity built into a thing to do what it does. It isn't a loanword for explosives (the Greeks had no gunpowder to name); it's the ordinary word for operative strength. There's no definite article in front of it either — Paul isn't calling the gospel “the power of God” as if there were only one, but “a power of God,” a force at work in its own right, not merely a message about one.\n\n“Righteousness of God” turns on a word, dikaiosunē, whose whole shape in Scripture differs from the Greek moral vocabulary around it — righteousness there is a social virtue measured against other people; righteousness here is conformity to God's own character, a status he defines and bestows, not one society negotiates. “Revealed” is apokaluptō, “to uncover,” the same root behind “apocalypse” — something previously hidden is pulled into the light. And the phrase “from faith to faith” traces a single line from source to destination: the righteousness comes by way of faith, its origin, and lands on faith, the only way to receive it — excluding, at both ends, any road through human merit.","translation":"For I am not ashamed of the good news; it is a power of God, resulting in salvation for everyone who keeps believing — to the Jew first, and also to the Greek. For in it a righteousness of God is being uncovered, out of faith, into faith — just as it stands written: the one who is righteous by faith shall keep on living."},{"c1":1,"v1":18,"c2":1,"v2":18,"label":"Romans 1:18","text":"“Wrath,” orgē, isn't a flash of temper in the Greek any more than in the Hebrew behind it — it's the settled, principled opposition of a good being to evil, the necessary flip side of loving what's right. A being incapable of orgē would be a being indifferent to good and evil alike. “Ungodliness” (asebeia) names the vertical failure — irreverence toward God — while “hold down” (katechō) is active, not passive: this isn't people innocently lacking truth, it's people pressing a truth they do have back under the surface so it won't rise up and demand anything of them. The “truth” in view isn't the gospel — plenty of these people never heard it — it's the plain evidence of God visible in the created world, which people actively suppress rather than simply miss.","translation":"For God's wrath is being uncovered from heaven against every irreverence and unrighteousness of men who are holding the truth down in unrighteousness."},{"c1":1,"v1":19,"c2":1,"v2":20,"label":"Romans 1:19–20","text":"“The known thing” (to gnōston) is Paul's phrase, not “the knowable” — this is knowledge people actually possess, not merely knowledge available to them in theory. God did the showing himself (phaneroō, “made it visible”), and the paradox Paul builds is deliberate: the invisible things of God are “clearly seen” — reasoned out backward from the visible creation to the unseen cause behind it, the way anyone infers a builder from a building.\n\nFor “Godhead,” Paul reaches for theiotēs rather than theotēs, the word he uses elsewhere for Christ's full deity. Theiotēs is the broader, vaguer term — not “God-ness” itself but the sum of God's visible attributes, his power and majesty as they can be read off the natural world. That distinction matters: nature shows enough of God to leave everyone without excuse, but it doesn't show God himself the way his own Son does. “Without excuse,” anapologētos, is a courtroom word — literally “without a defense speech” — describing someone who has no argument left to make once the evidence is in.","translation":"Because that which is known about God is plainly evident among them, for God himself made it visible to them. For his invisible qualities — his eternal power and his divine nature — have been, ever since the creation of the world, clearly seen, being perceived through the things that have been made — so that they are left without a defense."},{"c1":1,"v1":21,"c2":1,"v2":23,"label":"Romans 1:21–23","text":"“Became vain” doesn't mean what English “vain” now suggests — not proud, but empty of result. Mataiōō is built on mataios, “futile,” the word for an effort that comes to nothing; Paul is describing minds that thought hard and arrived at nothing worth reaching. “Professing” (phaskō) marks unsupported assertion — claiming something as fact with nothing behind the claim — and it's paired with sophos, “wise” in the fullest classical sense: the Greek ideal of mental excellence, skill, and cultivation. The people Paul describes claimed that highest kind of wisdom for themselves.\n\nWhat they got instead is named with a different word, mōrainō, built from the same root as our “moron” — not moral folly first, but plain stupefaction, a mind gone dull and insipid. And the exchange Paul traces runs on a straight verb, allassō, “to swap one thing for another”: the uncorruptible God traded for eikōn, a derived, secondhand likeness of corruptible man — and then, worse, of birds, four-footed animals, and things that crawl. The list runs downhill on purpose. As worship degrades, so does its object, a straight line from the incorruptible Creator down to a carved snake.","translation":"Because, although they knew God, they did not glorify him as God, nor did they give thanks, but they became futile in their reasonings, and their undiscerning heart was darkened. While claiming to be wise, they turned into fools, and exchanged the glory of the incorruptible God for a likeness — an image of corruptible man, and of birds, and of four-footed animals, and of creeping things."},{"c1":1,"v1":24,"c2":1,"v2":24,"label":"Romans 1:24","text":"“Gave up” is paradidōmi, a handing-over into someone else's control — the same verb used of handing a prisoner over to a jailer. This is judicial, not merely permissive: having chosen the creature over the Creator, humanity was handed over to the very cravings it had preferred. And Paul's preposition matters — the impurity happens “in” their hearts, a locative, meaning the desires themselves are the arena where the dishonoring takes place, not merely the tool used to bring it about.","translation":"Because of this, God handed them over, within the passionate cravings of their own hearts, to impurity — the dishonoring of their bodies among themselves."},{"c1":1,"v1":25,"c2":1,"v2":25,"label":"Romans 1:25","text":"“More than the Creator” undersells the Greek preposition, para — it doesn't mean the creature simply outranked the Creator in their affections, it means the Creator was passed by altogether, bypassed rather than merely demoted. “Worshipped and served” pairs two acts: sebazomai, religious honor, and latreuō, the same cultic-service word Paul used earlier of his own ministry to God — now aimed, tragically, at the created thing instead of at him. “Blessed,” closing the sentence, is eulogētos, built from eu (“well”) and legō (“to speak”) — the source of our word “eulogy” — a doxology Paul can't help dropping in as soon as he's named God as Creator, even mid-indictment.","translation":"They exchanged the truth of God for the lie, and gave religious honor and cultic service to the created thing rather than the Creator, who is to be well-spoken-of forever. Amen."},{"c1":1,"v1":26,"c2":1,"v2":26,"label":"Romans 1:26","text":"“Passions” (pathē) here is a narrower, harder word than the “lusts” (epithumia) of verse 24 — epithumia names the active craving, pathē the underlying diseased condition that produces it, a difference between a symptom and the sickness itself. “Vile,” atimia, is built straight from timē, “honor” or “proper valuation” — with the negating alpha in front, it means a wrong estimate of worth, treating something as less than what it actually is. And Paul's word for “women” here isn't the ordinary gunē but thēlus, “female” — the word that marks the biological distinction of sex rather than womanhood as such, because it's exactly that created distinction, phusis, “nature,” that is being set aside for its opposite, para phusin, “against nature.”","translation":"Because of this, God handed them over to dishonoring passions. Even their females exchanged the natural use for what is against nature."},{"c1":1,"v1":27,"c2":1,"v2":27,"label":"Romans 1:27","text":"“Burned” (ekkaiō) has the intensifying ek- prefix built in — not simply “inflamed” but “burned out,” consumed to the end. Paul switches his word for “lust” too: not the usual epithumia but orexis, a rarer and stronger word for raw appetite. “Working” translates katergazomai, “to work a thing out to its finish” — this wasn't an isolated act but something carried through to completion. And “was meet,” edei, doesn't describe an externally imposed punishment; it names a necessity built into the nature of the case itself — the consequence wasn't handed down afterward, it was baked into the deviation (planē, “a wandering off course”) from the start.","translation":"And likewise also the males, having left behind the natural use of the female, were burned out in their appetite toward one another — males working out with males what is shameful, and receiving back in themselves the payback that was, in the nature of the case, necessary for their wandering off course."},{"c1":1,"v1":28,"c2":1,"v2":28,"label":"Romans 1:28","text":"“Did not like to retain God” hides a courtroom image in English translation. Dokimazō means “to put on trial for the purpose of approving” — humanity tested God against its own specifications and, on that self-appointed jury, disapproved him. Paul's payback is a wordplay: since they rendered God adokimos — failed, disapproved — God in turn handed them over to a nous adokimos, a “disapproved mind,” a mind that itself fails the test a mind is supposed to pass. It's not merely wrong thinking; it's a mind no longer capable of functioning as a mind, its built-in sense of right and wrong short-circuited.","translation":"And just as they, having tested God and found him not to their specifications, disapproved of holding him in full knowledge, God handed them over to a mind that fails the test — to do the things that are not fitting."},{"c1":1,"v1":29,"c2":1,"v2":32,"label":"Romans 1:29–32","text":"“Being filled” is a perfect participle — a completed action whose result has settled into a fixed state. This isn't a momentary lapse Paul is cataloguing; it's a condition people have arrived at and now simply live inside of. Twenty-one separate sins pile up in the list, among them astorgos, “without natural affection” — a compound that names the specific bond of love between parents and children, husbands and wives, now missing entirely. And Paul saves his sharpest observation for last: it's one thing to do these things, another, worse thing to applaud someone else doing them — approval of sin in another is a step further down than the sin itself, because it makes common cause with it on purpose, with a clear conscience.","translation":"Filled with all unrighteousness — wickedness, greed, malice; full of envy, murder, strife, deceit, malignity — whisperers, slanderers, haters of God, insolent, arrogant, boastful, inventors of evil, disobedient to parents, without understanding, faithless, without natural affection, unmerciful — who, though they know full well God's just decree that people who practice such things deserve death, not only keep doing them but applaud those who practice them."},{"c1":2,"v1":1,"c2":2,"v2":1,"label":"Romans 2:1","text":"“Inexcusable” repeats the exact word from 1:20 — anapologētos, “without a defense speech” — Paul is deliberately tying the moralizing critic back to the pagan he's just finished condemning: same word, same verdict. “Judgest” (krinō) here means censorious appraisal, sizing someone up unfavorably, while “condemn” stacks the preposition kata, “down,” onto that same root — to “judge down,” pass sentence. The critic's own sentence on someone else becomes, by the shared principle, a sentence on himself, since he's sinning against the very light by which he judges.","translation":"Therefore you are without a defense, O man, every one of you who judges — for in that in which you judge the other, you condemn yourself, since you, the judge, keep on practicing the very same things."},{"c1":2,"v1":2,"c2":2,"v2":2,"label":"Romans 2:2","text":"“Know” here is oida, the verb for settled, given knowledge rather than knowledge gained by experience — Paul isn't arguing a case, he's stating something both he and his readers already take as certain: God's verdict lines up exactly with the facts, with no gap between what happened and what he rules.","translation":"But we know with certainty that God's judgment is in accordance with truth against those who practice such things."},{"c1":2,"v1":3,"c2":2,"v2":3,"label":"Romans 2:3","text":"“Thinkest” is logizomai, a verb of calculation — Paul pictures the critic actually running the numbers and arriving at the conclusion that he personally will be exempt. And the Greek makes the pronoun emphatic: su, “you” — of all people, you, who can see clearly enough to judge someone else's sin, imagine you'll dodge the very judgment you're so quick to hand down.","translation":"And do you, of all people — you who judge those who practice such things while doing the same yourself — do you reckon that you will escape the judgment of God?"},{"c1":2,"v1":4,"c2":2,"v2":4,"label":"Romans 2:4","text":"“Despisest” is built the same way as our “look down on” — kataphroneō, phroneō (“to direct the mind toward”) plus kata (“down”). “Forbearance,” anochē, is a word borrowed from the language of a military truce — a temporary holding-back of hostilities, not a permanent amnesty, which is exactly Paul's point: God's patience has a term to it, not an open-ended one. And “leads to repentance” is present tense — God's kindness is actively, continuously doing this pulling toward repentance the whole time it's being ignored, not just once, at the start.","translation":"Or are you treating with contempt the riches of his kindness and truce-like restraint and patience — not realizing that God's kindness is continually leading you toward repentance?"},{"c1":2,"v1":5,"c2":2,"v2":5,"label":"Romans 2:5","text":"“Treasurest” is thēsaurizo, “to store something up” — the root behind our word “thesaurus,” a storehouse of words the way this is a storehouse of wrath. Every act of unrepentance makes another deposit. And “hardness” is sklērotēs, the word behind “sclerosis” — a stiffening, an obstinate rigidity that won't bend or break under the very kindness meant to soften it.","translation":"But according to your hardened and unrepentant heart, you are storing up for yourself wrath — for the day of wrath, when God's righteous judgment will be uncovered."},{"c1":2,"v1":6,"c2":2,"v2":10,"label":"Romans 2:6–10","text":"“Render” is apodidōmi, reimbursement language — paying back exactly what's owed, not dispensing an arbitrary reward. “Patient continuance” is hupomonē, built on the idea of remaining under a weight rather than shrugging it off — willingly staying under discipline that the will would naturally resist. And on the other side, “anguish,” stenochōria, is literally “narrowness of place” — the word used for the crush of a besieged city, hemmed in with no room to move, set against the Psalms' opposite image of God bringing a person out “into a broad place.” The vocabulary itself pictures judgment as confinement and reward as spaciousness.","translation":"He will pay back to each one according to his deeds — to those who by patiently remaining under a good work keep seeking glory, honor, and incorruption, eternal life; but to those who are factious and refuse persuasion by the truth while yielding to unrighteousness, wrath and anger. Pressure and confinement on every soul of man who works out evil to the end, the Jew first and also the Greek; but glory, honor, and peace to everyone who works out good to the end, the Jew first and also the Greek."},{"c1":2,"v1":11,"c2":2,"v2":12,"label":"Romans 2:11–12","text":"“Respect of persons” translates a single compound Greek word built from “face” and “receive” — literally “face-receiving,” the practice of favoring someone because of how they look or what standing they hold, rather than judging what's actually true of them. God, Paul says, does no such face-receiving. And the parallel he draws is exact: those without the law who sin will perish without reference to the law; those under the law who sin will be judged by the very law they had. Having the law changes the standard of judgment, not the fact of it.","translation":"For there is no receiving of faces with God. For as many as sinned without law will also perish without law; and as many as sinned within the sphere of law will be judged by that law."},{"c1":2,"v1":13,"c2":2,"v2":13,"label":"Romans 2:13","text":"“Hearers” is akroatēs, not the ordinary word for someone who simply hears — it describes a student, someone whose regular business is sitting under instruction, week after week in the synagogue. Paul's point bites harder for the specificity: it isn't casual listeners he's talking about but people formally, faithfully trained in the law, and even their training doesn't count for anything without action attached to it.","translation":"For it is not the ones who regularly sit under the law's instruction who are righteous before God, but the doers of the law who will be justified."},{"c1":2,"v1":14,"c2":2,"v2":16,"label":"Romans 2:14–16","text":"“When,” hotan, sets up a general, recurring hypothetical — Paul isn't naming specific Gentiles but describing a pattern that shows up whenever it does. And “written on their hearts” plays off the old contrast with the law written on stone tables at Sinai — this is unwritten law, a moral sense built into people rather than handed to them on a document. Paul pictures a small courtroom running inside every person: their conduct testifies, their conscience corroborates the testimony, and their own thoughts take turns as prosecution and defense — apologeomai, literally “speaking in one's own defense” — arguing the case back and forth in a person's own head, with no outside law required to generate the argument.","translation":"For whenever Gentiles, who have no law, do by nature the things the law requires, these, having no law, are a law to themselves — since they demonstrate the work of the law written on their hearts, their conscience bearing joint witness, and their own thoughts alternately accusing or even defending one another — on the day when God judges the hidden things of men, according to my gospel, through Jesus Christ."},{"c1":2,"v1":17,"c2":2,"v2":20,"label":"Romans 2:17–20","text":"“Restest on the law” pictures leaning back on something for support — anapauō, the same verb used for resting one's whole weight on a couch. “Approvest the things that are excellent” is really “test with precision the things that differ” — dokimazō ta diapheronta, fine casuistic discrimination, the skill of drawing careful distinctions, trained by katēcheō, formal instruction — the word that gives us “catechism,” systematic, repeated teaching rather than a one-time lesson.\n\n“Confident” sits in the perfect tense — a settled conviction reached once and never revisited, a closed case in the Jew's own mind. “Guide” is hodēgos, built from “road” and “lead,” while “instructor” is paideutēs, the word for the household slave whose job was walking a child to school and disciplining him along the way — closer to “corrector” than “teacher.” And “babes,” nēpion, literally “the non-speaking one,” was used both of infants and of raw converts still learning to talk the faith. Paul's last jab is “form” — morphōsis, not the full substance of a thing but its outline, a rough sketch of knowledge and truth rather than the finished portrait.","translation":"Granted that you bear the name Jew, and lean your whole weight on the law, and boast in God, and know his will by experience, and — trained by regular catechetical instruction in the law — test with precision the things that differ and approve them; and have persuaded yourself, with a settled conviction, that you are a guide of the blind, a light to those in darkness, a corrector of the senseless, a teacher of infants, holding in the law the rough outline of knowledge and of the truth."},{"c1":2,"v1":21,"c2":2,"v2":24,"label":"Romans 2:21–24","text":"“Abhorrest” (bdelussō) began as a word for physical revulsion — literally to turn away from something because of its stench — before it settled into moral loathing. “Transgressing,” parabasis, is built from “step” plus “alongside/beyond” — stepping over a line that was clearly drawn, not stumbling in the dark but crossing a marked boundary on purpose. And Paul's rapid-fire questions — do you steal, while preaching against stealing; commit adultery, while forbidding it; rob temples, while recoiling from idols like they stink — build to the real charge: it's the transgressor's own conduct, not pagan hostility, that gets God's name spoken with contempt among the very nations he was supposed to be teaching.","translation":"You, then, who teach another — do you not teach yourself? You who preach against stealing, do you steal? You who say a person must not commit adultery, do you commit adultery? You who turn from idols as from a stench — do you rob temples? You who boast in the law, do you dishonor God by overstepping that same law? For, just as it stands written, the name of God is being blasphemed among the nations because of you."},{"c1":2,"v1":25,"c2":2,"v2":25,"label":"Romans 2:25","text":"“Is made” is gegonen, a perfect tense — not a temporary demotion but a settled, standing condition: once the law is broken, the circumcision that once meant something has become, and stays, uncircumcision in God's reckoning. “Keep” is prassō, habitual practice, ongoing conduct, not a single lapse — Paul's whole argument runs on the difference between a badge and a life actually lived out.","translation":"For circumcision is indeed of value, if you are one who habitually practices the law; but if you are a transgressor of the law, your circumcision has become — and remains — uncircumcision."},{"c1":2,"v1":26,"c2":2,"v2":26,"label":"Romans 2:26","text":"“Counted” is logizomai, accounting language — crediting something to a ledger. And “keep” here is a different verb than in verse 25 — phulassō, “to guard,” picturing someone standing watch over the law's requirements so they don't get broken, in the present tense of habitual practice. The uncircumcised person who guards the law this way gets that guarding entered to his account as if it were circumcision itself.","translation":"So if the uncircumcised man is habitually guarding the righteous requirements of the law, will not his uncircumcision be credited to him as circumcision?"},{"c1":2,"v1":27,"c2":2,"v2":27,"label":"Romans 2:27","text":"“By nature” modifies “fulfilling the law” — the point being that this Gentile keeps the law's substance without any of the outward apparatus the Jew has: no written code, no bodily sign. “By” translates dia in the sense of “with the advantage of” — the Jew who transgresses does so in spite of every outward help stacked in his favor, which makes his failure, not his heritage, the thing that actually counts against him.","translation":"And the physically uncircumcised man who, by nature, keeps on fulfilling the law, will judge you — you who, with the letter and circumcision as your advantage, are a transgressor of the law."},{"c1":2,"v1":28,"c2":2,"v2":29,"label":"Romans 2:28–29","text":"“Outwardly” and “inwardly” translate phaneros (“visible, apparent”) and kruptos (“hidden, secret”) — the whole passage turns on relocating the real identity markers from what can be seen to what can't. “In spirit” and “in letter” are both locative — they name a sphere of operation, not a method: true circumcision happens in the territory of a person's inner, God-conscious life, not in the territory of external rule-keeping. There may be a quiet wordplay buried here too — “Jew” descends from “Judah,” which means “praise” — so that the true Jew is, almost by definition, the one whose praise comes from God rather than from the applause of other people.","translation":"For the one who is a Jew merely in outward appearance is not the true Jew, nor is circumcision merely in outward, fleshly form the true circumcision. But the one who is a Jew inwardly is the true Jew, and circumcision is of the heart, in the sphere of the spirit, not the letter — a circumcision whose praise comes not from men, but from God."},{"c1":3,"v1":1,"c2":3,"v2":1,"label":"Romans 3:1","text":"“Advantage” is perisson, “surplus” — something extra, over and above the baseline. Having just argued that circumcision counts for nothing without obedience, Paul anticipates the obvious objection forming in a Jewish reader's mind and states it as bluntly as possible before answering it: if none of that matters, what was the surplus for?","translation":"What, then, is the surplus advantage of the Jew? Or what is the profit of circumcision?"},{"c1":3,"v1":2,"c2":3,"v2":2,"label":"Romans 3:2","text":"\"Chiefly\" translates *prōton*, \"first\" -- Paul is opening a list he never finishes; he names one advantage of being a Jew and moves straight on. \"Oracles\" is *logia*, a term reserved for divine utterances, not writings in general. Israel's advantage was custodial, not moral: they were handed the actual words of God to keep safe, and at the time Paul wrote, that meant the Old Testament.","translation":"Much, in every way -- for first of all, they were entrusted with the oracles of God."},{"c1":3,"v1":5,"c2":3,"v2":8,"label":"Romans 3:5–8","text":"\"Commend\" is *sunistēmi*, \"to set together\" -- proof-language. Paul's point is that human unrighteousness, set side by side with God's righteousness, throws the latter into sharper relief; the contrast demonstrates it rather than excusing the sin that supplies the contrast. \"Taketh vengeance\" is literally \"brings the anger to bear\" (*epipherō orgēn*) -- a controlled, judicial wrath, not a fit of temper. Paul flags his own courtroom analogy as borrowed speech (\"I speak as a man\"), aware that framing God's justice in human legal terms domesticates something too large for the comparison. And the slander in verse 8 -- \"let us do evil that good may come\" -- isn't a hypothetical Paul invents to knock down; it's the actual charge of lawlessness already being circulated against his gospel of grace.","translation":"But if our unrighteousness demonstrates God's righteousness, what shall we say? God is not unrighteous to inflict wrath, is he? -- I speak in merely human terms. Let it not happen! Otherwise how will God judge the world? And if, as is the case, God's truth abounded to his glory by my falsehood, why am I still being judged as a sinner? And why not say -- as some report, slandering us, that we say -- Let us do evil so that good may come? Their condemnation is just."},{"c1":3,"v1":9,"c2":3,"v2":9,"label":"Romans 3:9","text":"\"Are better\" translates *proechō*, \"to hold a place ahead of\" -- a question of relative advantage, not moral superiority. \"Proved\" is a courtroom word (*proaitiaomai*, \"to bring a charge beforehand\"), not a term for logical demonstration. Paul isn't building a syllogism here so much as reading out an indictment already filed: his whole preceding argument has been a charge laid jointly against Jew and Gentile.","translation":"What then? Are we ahead? Not at all -- for we have already brought the charge that Jews and Greeks alike are all under sin."},{"c1":3,"v1":10,"c2":3,"v2":12,"label":"Romans 3:10–12","text":"\"It is written\" is a perfect tense (*gegraptai*) -- not \"was written\" but \"stands written, and stands written still,\" a permanent record rather than a past event. \"Seeketh after\" (*ekzēteō*) describes an active, determined search, not idle curiosity -- and the text's claim is that no one is conducting it. \"Gone out of the way\" is literally \"leaned out\" (*ekklinō*, \"lean\" plus \"out\"), a gradual drift off the road rather than one dramatic wrong turn. And \"become unprofitable\" carries, in its underlying Hebrew sense, the picture of milk gone sour -- something that started good and curdled.","translation":"As it stands written: There is no righteous person, not even one. There is no one who understands; no one who is searching out God. All have leaned off the road; together they have gone sour. There is no one continually doing good -- not so much as one."},{"c1":3,"v1":13,"c2":3,"v2":18,"label":"Romans 3:13–18","text":"The catalog of sins reads like a scan down the body, each organ implicated through its own ordinary function turned toward evil: throat, tongue, lips, mouth, feet. \"Their throat is an opened grave\" uses a perfect participle -- the grave has been opened and stands open, reeking. \"They have used deceit\" is imperfect tense: not a single lie but people continuously smoothing their tongues into deception, habit rather than incident. The poison under the lips belongs specifically to the asp, the Egyptian cobra whose venom sac sits exactly there -- a precise image, not a stock metaphor for snakes in general. The list closes on the root rather than another symptom: \"there is no fear of God before their eyes\" is the cause everything before it is an effect of.","translation":"Their throat is a grave standing open. With their tongues they kept on practicing deceit. The poison of asps is under their lips, whose mouth is full of cursing and bitterness, whose feet run swiftly to pour out blood. Ruin and misery mark their roads, and the road of peace they have not known. There is no fear of God set before their eyes."},{"c1":3,"v1":21,"c2":3,"v2":23,"label":"Romans 3:21–23","text":"\"Now\" here marks a logical turn in the argument, not a moment in time. \"Is manifested\" is a perfect tense: God's righteousness has been disclosed and stands permanently on view, with the law and the prophets continually bearing it witness in the present. \"Have sinned\" is a constative aorist, a single wide-angle snapshot of the whole human race as one completed fact -- all sinned. But \"come short\" shifts to the present tense: not only a past verdict but an ongoing state, like a runner still trailing in a race that hasn't finished. That's the picture behind the verb itself, which properly means falling behind toward a finish line, not simply missing a target once.","translation":"But now, apart from law, God's righteousness stands revealed, the law and the prophets continually bearing it witness -- God's righteousness through faith in Jesus Christ, for all who believe. For there is no distinction: all sinned, and all are still falling short of the glory of God."},{"c1":3,"v1":24,"c2":3,"v2":26,"label":"Romans 3:24–26","text":"Three different Greek verbs get rendered \"redeem\" across the New Testament, and the noun behind \"redemption\" here draws on the fullest of them -- paying a ransom price to set someone permanently free, not merely bidding for them in the slave market but taking them off it for good. \"Set forth\" (*protithēmi*) means displayed publicly, in the open -- a deliberate contrast with the mercy seat in the tabernacle, which sat behind a veil and was approached by one priest, once a year. Christ, by contrast, is put on exhibit for everyone to see. \"Propitiation\" is in fact the technical term (*hilastērion*) for that very mercy seat, the golden cover over the ark where the sacrificial blood was sprinkled -- not language for buying off an angry god, but for the object where blood turns a place of judgment into a place of mercy. And \"remission\" translates a rare word found nowhere else in the New Testament that means \"passing over\" rather than \"putting away\": God didn't excuse sins committed before the cross, he held them in abeyance, forbearing to punish because the reckoning that would settle the account was still ahead.","translation":"Having been declared righteous freely by his grace, through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus -- whom God publicly displayed as a mercy seat, through faith in his blood, to demonstrate his righteousness, on account of his passing over sins previously committed, in the forbearance of God -- to demonstrate his righteousness at the present time, so that he might be both righteous, and the one who declares righteous the one who has faith in Jesus."},{"c1":3,"v1":29,"c2":3,"v2":31,"label":"Romans 3:29–31","text":"Paul switches prepositions mid-sentence for \"faith\" -- *ek pisteōs*, \"out of faith\" as a source, then *dia pisteōs*, \"through faith\" as a means -- almost certainly for rhetorical variety rather than to mark some doctrinal difference in how Jew and Gentile are justified. The real turn is in \"make void\" (*katargeō*, \"render inoperative\"): Paul insists faith doesn't cancel the law's authority but is the one thing that finally puts it on solid ground -- a claim he'll spend chapters 4 and 6–8 making good on.","translation":"Or is he the God of Jews only? Is he not also of Gentiles? Yes, of Gentiles also -- since God is one, and he will justify the circumcised out of faith and the uncircumcised through faith. Do we then render the law inoperative through this faith? Let it not happen! On the contrary, we establish the law."},{"c1":4,"v1":6,"c2":4,"v2":8,"label":"Romans 4:6–8","text":"\"Blessed\" (*makarios*) in ordinary Greek simply meant fortunate or prosperous; David's declaration repurposes it for a condition entirely apart from circumstance -- a man is \"blessed\" while still guilty by nature, because the blessing is a credit entry God makes on his account, not a description of his conduct. \"Iniquities\" is literally \"lawlessnesses\" (*anomia*), deliberate violation rather than wrongdoing in general, and \"are forgiven\" pictures those violations formally put away, struck from the record rather than merely overlooked.","translation":"Just as David also pronounces the man spiritually prosperous to whose account God credits righteousness apart from works: Spiritually prosperous are they whose lawless deeds were put away and whose sins were covered; spiritually prosperous is the man to whose account the Lord will not credit sin."},{"c1":4,"v1":9,"c2":4,"v2":12,"label":"Romans 4:9–12","text":"The order of events in Genesis carries the argument: God credited Abraham with righteousness while he was still uncircumcised, years before the rite was given. Circumcision comes later as a seal stamped onto a righteousness he already possessed, not a condition for earning it. That sequence is what lets Abraham stand as father to both groups -- the uncircumcised who simply believe as he did, and the circumcised who don't merely carry the physical mark but actually walk in the same footsteps of faith he walked while he had no mark at all.","translation":"Is this blessing, then, pronounced upon the circumcised, or also upon the uncircumcised? For we say, his faith was credited to Abraham as righteousness. How then was it credited -- while he was circumcised, or uncircumcised? Not in circumcision but in uncircumcision. And he received the sign of circumcision as a seal of the righteousness of the faith he had while still uncircumcised, so that he might become father of all who believe while uncircumcised, that righteousness might be credited to them also -- and father of the circumcised, not to those who are merely circumcised, but to those who also walk in the footsteps of the faith our father Abraham had while he was still uncircumcised."},{"c1":4,"v1":13,"c2":4,"v2":15,"label":"Romans 4:13–15","text":"Both key verbs sit in the perfect tense: if the inheritance came through law, faith would stand permanently voided and the promise permanently inoperative -- not set back temporarily but disabled for good. The closing line runs almost like a proof: law's one real product is the possibility of transgression, since a violation requires a standard to violate. Remove the law and the category \"transgression\" disappears with it, even though sin as a reality does not.","translation":"For the promise to Abraham and his offspring, that he should be heir of the world, did not come through law but through the righteousness that comes by faith. For if those who are of the law are the heirs, faith has been rendered void, and the promise has been made permanently inoperative. For the law produces wrath; but where there is no law, neither is there transgression."},{"c1":4,"v1":18,"c2":4,"v2":18,"label":"Romans 4:18","text":"The phrase usually smoothed into \"against hope believed in hope\" actually trades on two different prepositions: his situation lay beyond (*para*) anything nature could offer as grounds for hope, yet he staked his expectation upon (*epi*) hope regardless -- not blind optimism but hope deliberately relocated from natural grounds onto God's word alone.","translation":"Who, beyond all hope, believed on the ground of hope, so that he became father of many nations, in keeping with what had been spoken with finality: So shall your offspring be."},{"c1":4,"v1":19,"c2":4,"v2":22,"label":"Romans 4:19–22","text":"\"Considered\" (*katanoeō*) means to look something over carefully, not glance away from it -- the better manuscripts have Abraham staring straight at his situation rather than refusing to face it: a body as good as dead, cast in a perfect tense that pictures a condition already set in and staying set. \"Staggered\" is literally \"to judge between two\" (*diakrinō*), the image of a man torn between two verdicts; Abraham reached no such split decision. And \"was strengthened\" is passive voice -- the strength wasn't something he worked up himself but something worked into him, and it's specifically strength \"with respect to faith,\" not a claim that his body was renewed in advance of the fact.","translation":"And not being weak in his faith, he attentively considered his own body, already as good as dead -- he being about a hundred years old -- and the deadness of Sarah's womb besides. Yet at the promise of God he did not waver between two minds in unbelief, but was made strong in his faith, giving glory to God, and being fully convinced that what God had promised, he was able also to perform. Therefore it was credited to him as righteousness."},{"c1":4,"v1":23,"c2":4,"v2":25,"label":"Romans 4:23–25","text":"\"Shall be imputed\" isn't actually future tense in the Greek; it runs on a verb (*mellei*) that expresses a settled, ongoing divine pattern rather than a one-time future event -- closer to \"it is credited, and keeps being credited\" than \"it will be credited someday.\" \"Delivered\" (*paradidōmi*) is the verb for handing a prisoner over to the courts -- the Father's act toward the Son is cast in exactly the language of a judicial handover, not a private arrangement between the two. And \"offenses\" (*paraptōma*) pictures a straying off the path (*para*, \"aside,\" plus the verb \"to fall\") -- concrete departures from the road, not sin described as an abstract condition.","translation":"Now it was not written for his sake alone, that it was credited to him, but for our sake also, to whom it is to be credited -- to us who have faith in him who raised Jesus our Lord from among the dead, who was handed over because of our transgressions, and was raised because of our justification."},{"c1":5,"v1":2,"c2":5,"v2":2,"label":"Romans 5:2","text":"\"Access\" translates prosagōgē, built from a verb meaning \"to bring\" and a preposition meaning \"toward\" — the picture is of someone ushering a person into the presence of royalty, securing them an audience and standing there in favor. Paul says believers \"have\" this access and \"stand\" in this favor, both in the perfect tense: not a momentary admission but a permanent state, granted once and holding good indefinitely. The hope in view isn't a wish but a settled expectation — the coming glory is itself the guarantee.","translation":"Through him we have obtained, and go on holding, our permanent entrance by faith into this favor in which we now permanently stand; and we exult on the ground of the hope of the glory of God."},{"c1":5,"v1":3,"c2":5,"v2":5,"label":"Romans 5:3–5","text":"\"Glory\" here (kauchaomai) means to exult — and Paul says believers exult in tribulations not merely while enduring them but because of them; the hardship itself is the ground of the boasting. \"Tribulation\" carries the article, marking it as the trouble every believer should expect. The chain that follows runs on a repeating device: tribulation produces endurance (hupomonē, literally \"remaining under\" pressure rather than escaping it), endurance produces \"provenness\" (dokimē — not \"experience,\" but the tested, proven character that survives a trial), and provenness produces hope. \"Shed abroad\" is a perfect-tense verb — the love of God was poured in at one point and floods the heart still, continuing to saturate it.","translation":"And not only that — we are continually exulting in our troubles also, knowing that trouble is producing endurance, and endurance, provenness, and provenness, hope; and this hope does not put us to shame, because the love of God has been poured out into our hearts, and keeps on filling them, through the Holy Spirit who was given to us."},{"c1":5,"v1":6,"c2":5,"v2":8,"label":"Romans 5:6–8","text":"\"For\" (huper) does double duty here — \"on behalf of\" and \"instead of.\" Paul uses the same preposition elsewhere for straightforward substitution, one dying so others need not, and the sense here is the same: Christ died in the place of people who had no reverence for God at all. The timing is called kairos, not just any moment but the fitting, appointed one. Paul then borrows a distinction from ordinary Greek usage, not New Testament theology: the \"righteous\" man simply meets his obligations, while the \"good\" man is the generous, warmly regarded one people might actually die for. Even that rare case falls short of what Christ did — he died for enemies, not for the admirable. \"Commends\" is present tense: God isn't just presenting a past demonstration, he is continually holding this proof up before us.","translation":"For while we were still without strength, at the fitting time, Christ died instead of, in behalf of, ungodly men. For someone will hardly die in behalf of a righteous man — though perhaps for the good man someone might even dare to die. But God keeps on demonstrating his own love toward us, in that, while we were yet sinners, Christ died in our behalf."},{"c1":5,"v1":9,"c2":5,"v2":11,"label":"Romans 5:9–11","text":"Paul argues from the greater case to the lesser: if God did the harder thing — reconciling enemies — while we were still hostile to him, the easier thing, finishing what he started, is guaranteed. \"Wrath\" carries the article: not trouble in general but the specific, final judgment. \"Enemies\" leans toward the passive sense — not simply that we hated God, but that our condition put us at odds with a holy God who could not overlook sin. \"Reconciled\" (katallassō) means, at root, to exchange — to convert a relationship of hostility into one of peace, and the initiative is God's, not ours; we are the ones acted upon. And where the King James has \"atonement,\" the Greek is really \"reconciliation\" — the older English sense of \"at-one-ment,\" making two estranged parties one again, not \"atonement\" in its modern sense of a price paid.","translation":"Much more then, having now been declared righteous by his blood, we will be saved through him from the wrath. For if, while we were enemies, we were reconciled to God through the death of his Son, much more, having been reconciled, we will be saved by his life. And not only so, but we also exult in God through our Lord Jesus Christ, through whom we have now received the reconciliation."},{"c1":5,"v1":12,"c2":5,"v2":14,"label":"Romans 5:12–14","text":"\"World\" (kosmos) means the human race, the same sense it carries in John 3:16. Paul's picture is that when death entered through Adam, it spread \"throughout\" every member of the race — the death is universal because Adam functioned as humanity's representative head, so his sin counted as everyone's sin. Paul proves the point from the period between Adam and Moses: there was no written law yet, and sin is not charged to a person's account where there is no law to break — yet people still died. The only explanation is that they died for Adam's sin, inherited through their union with him, not for sins they had individually committed. Adam is called a \"type\" (tupos), a pattern or mold that foreshadows someone greater to come.","translation":"Therefore, just as through one man sin entered into the world, and through sin, death, so death spread through to all men, because all sinned. For until law came, sin was in the world, but sin is not charged to one's account where there is no law. Yet death reigned as king from Adam until Moses, even over those who had not sinned after the pattern of Adam's transgression — who is a type of the one who was to come."},{"c1":5,"v1":15,"c2":5,"v2":15,"label":"Romans 5:15","text":"\"Offense\" (paraptōma) pictures a stumble off the path — a departure from where one should be walking. \"Free gift\" (charisma) is a favor granted with no strings of merit attached. Paul isn't just comparing degree here, sin's damage against grace's remedy — he's building an argument from certainty: the character of the God who gives freely is a surer basis for confidence than the certainty of sin and death running back to one man's fall. Both \"the one\" transgression and \"the one\" man carry the definite article in the Greek — Paul keeps Adam and Christ each singled out by their singularity.","translation":"But the free gift is not like the trespass. For if by the trespass of the one man the many died, much more did the grace of God, and the free gift by the grace of the one man, Jesus Christ, overflow to the many."},{"c1":5,"v1":16,"c2":5,"v2":16,"label":"Romans 5:16","text":"Paul contrasts sources here. Judgment traces back to a single sin as its source and issues in condemnation; the free gift traces back to many transgressions as its source and issues in justification. One point of origin multiplies into one verdict of guilt; a multiplied problem is answered by one verdict of acquittal.","translation":"And the gift is not like the effect of the one man's sinning. For the judgment came from one transgression, resulting in condemnation; but the free gift came from many transgressions, resulting in a righteous verdict."},{"c1":5,"v1":17,"c2":5,"v2":17,"label":"Romans 5:17","text":"The weight of the sentence falls on \"reigned\" — sin's dominion is pictured as a king ruling through death. Paul's logic demands that the second Adam's effect cannot fall short of the first Adam's: if death reigned as king through one man's fall, then those who receive the abundant grace God offers will all the more certainly reign as kings themselves, through the life that comes by the one man, Christ.","translation":"For if, by the trespass of the one, death reigned as king through that one, much more will those who receive the abundance of grace and of the gift of righteousness reign as kings in life through the one, Jesus Christ."},{"c1":5,"v1":18,"c2":5,"v2":18,"label":"Romans 5:18","text":"Paul distinguishes two related but different words: dikaiōma, the righteous deed itself — Christ's act of satisfying what the law demanded — and dikaiōsis, the legal verdict, God's declaration that acquits a person and grants them standing before him. That verdict is described as belonging to life — a genitive that describes its quality, not merely its result: an acquittal bound up with the gift of life itself. Legally, a verdict changes standing, not character; but Paul's phrase shows the verdict here is inseparable from an actual impartation of life to the one acquitted.","translation":"So then, as through one trespass the result for all men was condemnation, so also through one righteous act the result for all men is a righteous standing that carries life with it."},{"c1":5,"v1":19,"c2":5,"v2":19,"label":"Romans 5:19","text":"\"Disobedience\" (parakoē) is built from the verb \"to hear\" plus a prefix meaning \"alongside\" — literally a hearing that goes astray, an inattentive or careless hearing that shades into refusal. \"Obedience\" (hupakoē) is built the same way but with a prefix meaning \"under\" — a hearing that places itself under authority, a willing submission to what it hears. Paul's word for \"made\" (kathistēmi) is not the ordinary word for manufacturing something, like shaping wood into a spear; it means to appoint or constitute someone to a position by an official, judicial act. By Adam's one act of not-listening, the race was judicially constituted sinful; by Christ's one act of listening and submitting, all who believe are judicially constituted righteous.","translation":"For just as through the one man's disobedience the many were constituted sinners, so also through the one man's obedience the many will be constituted righteous."},{"c1":5,"v1":20,"c2":5,"v2":20,"label":"Romans 5:20","text":"\"Law\" here has no article — Paul means law as a method or principle of God's dealing, not the Mosaic code as a specific document. \"Entered\" (pareiserchomai) is a compound built from \"come,\" \"into,\" and \"alongside\" — the law didn't originate the problem, it came in alongside a sin already present, to expose it. \"Abound\" (pleonazō) means to increase or multiply — the law's effect was to multiply transgression by giving the already-rebellious nature something concrete to defy. But grace's response uses a different, stronger compound, huperperisseuō — not just \"abound\" but \"super-abound,\" to go over and beyond even that increase.","translation":"Now law came in alongside, in order that the trespass might be multiplied; but where sin was multiplied, grace super-abounded, and more besides."},{"c1":5,"v1":21,"c2":5,"v2":21,"label":"Romans 5:21","text":"\"Reigned\" (basileuō) again pictures sin as an enthroned king, and the phrase \"unto death\" describes the sphere in which that reign is carried out and displayed — death is the territory sin rules. Paul sets grace up as the counter-king, ruling not through death but through righteousness, and its rule issues in eternal life, made available through what Christ did.","translation":"In order that, just as sin reigned as king in the sphere of death, so also grace might reign as king through righteousness, resulting in eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord."},{"c1":6,"v1":2,"c2":6,"v2":2,"label":"Romans 6:2","text":"Paul's argument in this chapter hinges on a small grammatical fact: \"sin,\" appearing as a bare noun without the article, points to the sinful nature itself rather than to individual sinful acts — the same personified, enthroned power described as \"reigning\" back in 5:21. The question \"shall we continue in sin\" uses a present tense that asks about an ongoing, habitual relationship, not an isolated lapse. Paul's answer is that believers \"died\" to that nature — an aorist verb, apethanomen, built from a root meaning \"to die\" plus a prefix meaning \"away from\" — describing a single, completed, unrepeatable severing, not a gradual fading. \"Baptized\" is simply transliterated rather than translated, and its everyday Greek use (a smith plunging hot iron into water to temper it, a soldier dipping his spear in blood) points to a decisive change of condition through immersion into something new — here, into Christ's own death, so that the believer's tie to the old ruling power is cut at the root.","translation":"What then shall we say? Are we to go on remaining in sin, that grace may increase? Away with the thought! How shall we, who died once for all to sin, still go on living in it? Do you not know that all of us who were plunged into Christ Jesus were plunged into his death? We were therefore buried together with him through this plunging into his death, in order that, just as Christ was raised from among the dead through the Father's glory, so we too might order our conduct in newness of life."},{"c1":6,"v1":5,"c2":6,"v2":7,"label":"Romans 6:5–7","text":"\"Planted together\" translates a word (sumphutoi) built on the idea of two things growing up joined, sharing one life between them — Paul pictures the believer's union with Christ's death as organic, not merely legal. \"Likeness\" (homoiōma) is a resemblance so close it nearly collapses into identity. Two further word choices matter: the \"old man\" uses palaios, \"old\" in the sense of worn out through use, not archaios, \"old\" in the sense of ancient — Paul means the used-up former self, fit to be discarded, not simply a self from long ago. And \"destroyed\" translates katargeō, which doesn't mean annihilated but rendered inoperative, put out of commission — the body once run by the sinful nature is taken off that nature's payroll, no longer bound to answer its orders.","translation":"For if we have grown into permanent union with him in the likeness of his death, we shall also be in the likeness of his resurrection — knowing this, that our old, worn-out self was crucified with him once for all, in order that the body ruled by sin might be put out of commission, so that we should no longer be rendering sin a slave's habitual obedience. For the one who has died stands free, once for all, from sin's claim."},{"c1":6,"v1":8,"c2":6,"v2":10,"label":"Romans 6:8–10","text":"\"With him\" describes the source the believer's life is now drawn from — not fellowship in some vague sense, but ongoing dependence on Christ's own resurrection life for as long as that life continues, which is forever, since death no longer holds any claim over him. \"He died to sin once\" uses the aorist again — a single, unrepeatable event, done and settled — over against a death that keeps recurring. Since Christ's death dealt with sin's power once for all and his life continues without end, the believer's connection to that life is equally secure and equally permanent.","translation":"Now if we died together with Christ, we believe that we shall also go on living together with him, knowing that Christ, having been raised from among the dead, no longer dies; death no longer lords it over him. For the death he died, he died to sin once for all; but the life he lives, he lives to God."},{"c1":6,"v1":11,"c2":6,"v2":14,"label":"Romans 6:11–14","text":"\"Reckon\" (logizomai) is an accounting term — to count something as a settled fact and act on that basis, not to talk yourself into feeling something. Paul tells believers to reckon themselves dead to sin and alive to God as a fact already accomplished by God, not a feeling to manufacture. \"Let not sin reign\" uses a present imperative with a negative that specifically halts an action already underway — better rendered \"stop letting sin go on reigning\" than a general warning against something not yet happening. \"Yield\" (paristēmi) shifts tense meaningfully: Paul first uses it to forbid continuing to place the body's members at sin's disposal, then switches to the aorist to command a decisive, one-time presentation of oneself to God. \"Instruments\" (hopla) is military vocabulary — weapons, not neutral tools — casting the body's members as armament to be wielded in a real conflict.","translation":"So also, keep reckoning yourselves to be dead ones with reference to sin, but living ones with reference to God, in Christ Jesus. Therefore stop letting sin go on reigning as king in your mortal body, to obey its cravings; and stop presenting your members to sin as weapons for unrighteousness — but present yourselves, once for all, to God as those alive from the dead, and your members to God as weapons for righteousness. For sin will not lord it over you, since you are not under law but under grace."},{"c1":6,"v1":15,"c2":6,"v2":15,"label":"Romans 6:15","text":"The grammar signals a shift from the previous question. In verse 1 the verb is a present subjunctive, asking about a habitual, ongoing pattern of sin; here it switches to an aorist subjunctive, asking about a single, deliberate lapse instead. The objector has downgraded his proposal — not a whole lifestyle of sin, but the occasional, planned exception, on the theory that grace's rule is more lenient than law's. Paul's answer treats that as no better an idea: grace doesn't loosen the reins, it replaces an external code with an internal power that is, if anything, more exacting.","translation":"What then? Are we to sin, on one occasion, because we are not under law but under grace? Away with the thought!"},{"c1":6,"v1":16,"c2":6,"v2":16,"label":"Romans 6:16","text":"The King James \"servants\" undersells the word doulos, the strongest Greek term for a slave — someone born into servitude, whose will is absorbed entirely into the master's, bound by ties nothing but death can break. Paul's point is that believers have not become masterless; they have changed masters. What had been an involuntary bondage to the sinful nature, inherited and inescapable, becomes a devoted bondage to Christ, taken on willingly because the believer's whole orientation has changed.","translation":"Do you not know that to whomever you present yourselves as slaves for obedience, slaves you are to the one you obey — whether of sin, resulting in death, or of obedience, resulting in righteousness?"},{"c1":6,"v1":17,"c2":6,"v2":17,"label":"Romans 6:17","text":"The verb \"delivered\" (paradidōmi) is passive and describes believers as those handed over into a pattern of teaching, shaped by it, rather than as people who simply received information and agreed with it. Paul's picture is less like handing someone a book and more like pouring metal into a mold — the teaching left its shape on them from the inside, which is why the obedience that follows comes \"from the heart\" rather than from external pressure.","translation":"But thanks be to God that, though you were once slaves of sin, you obeyed from the heart that pattern of teaching into whose mold you were delivered."},{"c1":6,"v1":18,"c2":6,"v2":18,"label":"Romans 6:18","text":"\"Made free\" (eleutheroō) is the standard term for manumitting a slave — a legal release from one owner. But Paul's logic won't allow for an unowned, ownerless freedom; the moment one master's claim ends, another's begins. Liberation from sin is, in the same act, enlistment under righteousness.","translation":"And having been set free from sin, you were made slaves to righteousness."},{"c1":6,"v1":21,"c2":6,"v2":23,"label":"Romans 6:21–23","text":"\"Wages\" (opsōnion) is a soldier's term — not a formal salary but rations, the in-kind subsistence handed out to keep a fighting man alive and able to serve. Paul's picture of sin as a commanding officer doling out rations to its troops is grim: the \"pay\" it hands out, keeping its slaves going day to day, terminates in death. Against that he sets charisma, the free gift — nothing earned, nothing doled out as compensation for service, simply given.","translation":"What fruit then were you having at that time, from the things you are now ashamed of? For the end of those things is death. But now, having been set free from sin and made slaves to God, you are having your fruit resulting in holiness, and the end, eternal life. For the wages sin pays out is death; but the free gift of God is eternal life in Christ Jesus our Lord."},{"c1":7,"v1":4,"c2":7,"v2":4,"label":"Romans 7:4","text":"“Are become dead” translates *thanatoō*, not the plainer “died” Paul used back in chapter 6, but a stronger verb meaning “put to death”—a violent death, not a quiet one. He reaches for the sharper word on purpose: the believer's death to the law didn't happen gently, it happened by the same violence that killed Christ, because it happened *in* Christ's death. The mechanism named is “through the body of Christ”—his actual crucified body is what discharges the believer from the law's claim, the way a wife is released from her husband's law only by his death. The purpose clause that follows completes the picture: death first, then a second marriage, entered so that fruit might be produced for God—not law-keeping, but fruit-bearing.","translation":"So that, my brothers, you also were put to death, with violence, with reference to the law, through the body of Christ, resulting in your belonging to another, to him who was raised up out from among the dead, in order that we might go on bearing fruit to God."},{"c1":7,"v1":5,"c2":7,"v2":5,"label":"Romans 7:5","text":"“In the flesh” here doesn't mean “in a body”; it names a condition, total captivity to the fallen nature, the opposite number to “in the Spirit” a chapter later. The “motions of sins” translates *pathēma*, a word for stirred passions or impulses, not neutral instincts but sin already active and pressing for release. “Did work” is *energeō*, the ordinary verb for a force actively operating—the law didn't create these impulses, it triggered them into motion. And the fruit that impulse produced wasn't merely followed by death; the phrasing makes it belong to death, death's own crop.","translation":"For when we were living in the sphere of the flesh, the sinful passions, which were being stirred up through the law, were continually at work in our body's members, resulting in fruit that belonged to death."},{"c1":7,"v1":6,"c2":7,"v2":6,"label":"Romans 7:6","text":"The “dead” in view here isn't the law but the believer's former chained condition—the imperfect tense pictures the evil nature as something that kept holding him down, again and again, before he died with Christ. What follows the discharge isn't a future duty (“that we should serve”) so much as an automatic result: the grammar reports something already happening—present tense, habitual obedience. Paul also reaches for *kainos* rather than *neos* for “new”—not new on the calendar but new in kind, unworn—set against *palaios*, not simply old in years but worn out, obsolete. The contrast running underneath is power source against power source: the Spirit now energizes obedience where the law only ever commanded it and left a person to his own resources.","translation":"But now we have been discharged from the law, having died to that in which we were constantly being held down, so that we are habitually rendering a bondslave's obedience in a sphere new in kind, that of the Spirit, and not in a sphere worn out and useless, that of the written code."},{"c1":7,"v1":7,"c2":7,"v2":7,"label":"Romans 7:7","text":"“God forbid” translates *mē genoito*, literally “may it not come to be”—the strongest recoil the language allows. Paul's actual answer is that the law didn't create sin, it introduced him to it: the verb for “know” is aorist, marking a coming-to-know by experience at a specific moment, not accumulated head knowledge. The same root, *epithumia*, sits behind both “lust” the noun and “covet” the verb—a craving strong enough to seize the will. And Paul pictures the commandment not as a single utterance but as something that kept on saying, repeatedly, “you shall not desire”—the repeated prohibition itself is what woke the craving up.","translation":"What then shall we say? Is the law sin? Away with the thought! But I did not come to know sin experientially, except through the law; for I would not have known evil desire, except that the law kept on saying, You shall not desire."},{"c1":7,"v1":8,"c2":7,"v2":8,"label":"Romans 7:8","text":"“Occasion” translates *aphormē*, a term borrowed from military and business language for a base of operations, a staging point for a campaign. Sin didn't originate in the commandment; it commandeered the commandment as its base, using the prohibition itself as leverage to “work,” *katergazomai*, “accomplish fully,” every kind of craving. Before that, sin was “dead”—not nonexistent, but dormant, lacking the friction it needed to spring into action.","translation":"But sin, seizing a base of operations through the commandment, worked in me every kind of craving. For apart from law, sin lies dead."},{"c1":7,"v1":9,"c2":7,"v2":9,"label":"Romans 7:9","text":"The “life” Paul claims before the law arrived isn't spiritual life—it's the unreflective ease of someone who hasn't yet met a command he could break. The verb behind “revived” is built on the word for “life” with “again” prefixed: sin didn't simply appear when the commandment came, it came back to life, as though sleeping and needing only a prohibition to rouse it. And the “death” that follows isn't physical—it's the collapse of that earlier, unexamined flourishing into misery.","translation":"And I was once alive, apart from law. But when the commandment arrived, sin sprang back to life, and I died."},{"c1":7,"v1":10,"c2":7,"v2":11,"label":"Romans 7:10–11","text":"The Greek adds an emphatic “this very one” that smoother translations drop—Paul isn't just reporting an outcome, he's marking his own surprise at it: the commandment whose whole aim was life turned out, in his hands, to lead to death. “Was found” is passive, a discovery that happened to him rather than one he went looking for. And the verb for what sin did—rendered “beguiled”—pictures deception: sin used the law's own authority as leverage to talk him into disobedience, then killed him through the very command he had trusted.","translation":"And the commandment that pointed to life—this very one, I found, to my surprise, leading to death. For sin, seizing the commandment as its base of operations, deceived me, and through it killed me."},{"c1":7,"v1":12,"c2":7,"v2":12,"label":"Romans 7:12","text":"Three separate adjectives are stacked here, not overlapping synonyms: holy names the law's origin in God's own character, righteous its content matching what God requires, good the beneficial end it serves. Piling up all three closes off any reading of the previous verses as an attack on the law itself.","translation":"So then, the law is holy, and the commandment is holy, and righteous, and good."},{"c1":7,"v1":13,"c2":7,"v2":13,"label":"Romans 7:13","text":"Paul's question—did the good become death to me?—gets the same emphatic refusal as before. His explanation turns on a purpose clause: sin worked death through the good commandment precisely so that sin might be exposed as sin, unmasked for what it actually is. The closing phrase pushes further, into something like “sinful to an extreme degree”—the commandment's role wasn't to make sin worse, but to reveal, using the best possible material, just how bad sin already was.","translation":"Did then that which is good become death to me? Away with the thought! But sin, in order that it might be shown to be sin, was producing death in me through what is good, so that sin, through the commandment, might become sinful beyond measure."},{"c1":7,"v1":14,"c2":7,"v2":15,"label":"Romans 7:14–15","text":"Paul had three separate words available for describing a person, and the choice here matters: not the “natural” man ruled by reason and appetite with no spiritual life at all, not the “spiritual” man walking in the fullness of the Spirit, but the “fleshly” man—a believer who still, in practice, lives under the old nature's pull. “Sold” is a perfect-tense verb, describing not a past event but a settled, standing condition, a debt already foreclosed. And “know,” in “what I do, I do not know,” isn't intellectual assent but experiential understanding—Paul is saying his own actions have gone opaque to him, explainable only by a pull working against his own best desires.","translation":"For we know that the law is spiritual, but I am fleshly, sold, and standing sold, under sin. For what I am doing, I do not understand; for what I want to do, this I am not practicing, but what I hate, this I am doing."},{"c1":7,"v1":16,"c2":7,"v2":20,"label":"Romans 7:16–20","text":"“Consent” translates *sumphēmi*, “to say together with”—an active agreement, stronger than mere acknowledgment: even in the act of disobeying, some part of Paul sides with the law he's breaking. He also has two Greek words for “good” and reaches for the one meaning moral beauty rather than the one meaning usefulness—naming what's genuinely admirable about the very command he can't keep. When he writes “no longer I,” the pronoun is placed for emphasis: his considered, willing self isn't the one driving the behavior; something has moved in and is operating him from the inside. And the verb for “want” is present tense, a standing, continuous desire always switched on, set against a verb meaning “to lie ready at hand” for “do”—the desire to do good is perpetually available to him, but the doing itself never shows up alongside it.","translation":"If then I am doing what I do not want, I am agreeing with the law that it is beautiful and good. So now it is no longer I myself who am doing it, but the sin dwelling in me. For I know that in me—that is, in my flesh—no good thing is dwelling; for the wanting lies constantly ready at hand in me, but the doing of the good does not."},{"c1":7,"v1":21,"c2":7,"v2":23,"label":"Romans 7:21–23","text":"Paul upgrades his vocabulary here: “delight” is a stronger word than the “agree with” of the previous verses, describing genuine moral sympathy rather than bare intellectual assent. He locates that gladness in “the inward man,” his rational, moral self—not identical to the new nature received at the new birth, but the part of a person still oriented toward God. Against it he sets “a different law” operating in his body's members, described in military language: it wages war and takes him prisoner, the verb being the standard one for marching off a captive after a lost battle.","translation":"For I delight in the law of God according to the inward man. But I see a different law in my body's members, waging war against the law of my mind and taking me captive to the law of sin that is in my members."},{"c1":7,"v1":24,"c2":7,"v2":25,"label":"Romans 7:24–25","text":"“Wretched” translates a word that originally described the exhaustion of hard physical labor—not despair as an emotion but the collapse that follows prolonged, fruitless struggle. The Greek word order throws the emphasis onto the pronoun: “wretched man, I”—Paul naming himself first, before the complaint. And when he asks who will deliver him, the interrogative pronoun is grammatically masculine: he isn't asking what method will fix this, he's asking which person will—and framing the question that way already supplies the answer.","translation":"Wretched man that I am! Who will deliver me out of this body of death? Thanks be to God, through Jesus Christ our Lord! So then, I myself, with my mind, am serving the law of God, but with my flesh, the law of sin."},{"c1":8,"v1":1,"c2":8,"v2":1,"label":"Romans 8:1","text":"The earliest manuscripts end this verse after “Christ Jesus”—the clause about walking “not after the flesh but after the Spirit” is a later addition, not part of Paul's original sentence. That detail matters for the logic: Paul isn't grounding “no condemnation” in how well a believer behaves, but in his standing—being “in Christ Jesus” names a position, not a performance record.","translation":"There is, then, now no condemnation at all for those who are in Christ Jesus."},{"c1":8,"v1":2,"c2":8,"v2":4,"label":"Romans 8:2–4","text":"“Law” in verse 2 isn't the Mosaic code but a governing principle—better read “the regulative power of the Spirit, namely, the life that is in Christ Jesus.” That power has already broken the older regulative power of sin and death over the believer, a settled fact rather than an ongoing project. Paul's phrase for the incarnation is built with care: not “in flesh” (too weak a link to sin), not “in the flesh of sin” (too strong, implying Christ shared in sin), but “in the likeness of the flesh of sin”—truly and fully human, wearing sin's own flesh in outward appearance, yet himself without sin. The single preposition behind “for sin” carries the whole weight of the mission, everything done in relation to sin to atone for it and break its hold. And “condemned” names something the law itself never had power to do: the law could pronounce a verdict, but only Christ's death could depose sin from its throne. What follows—the law's righteous requirement “fulfilled in us”—is stated in the passive voice: something done in the believer, God's own accomplishment, not human effort.","translation":"For the regulative power of the Spirit—the life that is in Christ Jesus—has set you free, once for all, from the regulative power of sin and death. For what the law was powerless to do, weakened as it was through the flesh, God did: sending his own Son in the likeness of the flesh of sin, and concerning sin, he condemned—deposed—sin in the flesh, so that the righteous requirement of the law might be brought to completion in us, who order our conduct not under the domination of the flesh, but under the domination of the Spirit."},{"c1":8,"v1":5,"c2":8,"v2":8,"label":"Romans 8:5–8","text":"The verb behind “mind” means to deliberately set one's thinking toward something, a fixed orientation rather than a passing thought. Paul then shifts to the noun form in a construction meaning “the mind possessed by”—the flesh or the Spirit doesn't merely influence the mind, it owns it. The word for “peace” carries its own picture: its root sense is “to bind together what has been separated,” not a calm feeling but a repaired connection. And “subject,” describing what the flesh-dominated mind cannot do toward God's law, is a military term for falling into rank under a commanding officer—such a mind simply refuses the chain of command.","translation":"For those who are habitually dominated by the flesh keep setting their minds on the things of the flesh, but those who are habitually dominated by the Spirit, on the things of the Spirit. For the mind possessed by the flesh is death, but the mind possessed by the Spirit is life and a restored peace; because the mind possessed by the flesh is at war with God—it does not fall into rank under God's law, nor is it able to. And those who are in the sphere of the flesh are not able to please God."},{"c1":8,"v1":9,"c2":8,"v2":9,"label":"Romans 8:9","text":"The Greek verb already carries its own subject in its ending, so when Paul adds a separate pronoun for “you,” it's there for emphasis, a deliberate contrast with the flesh-dominated people just described. “Dwell” comes from the word for “house” and means more than occupying space—it pictures someone thoroughly at home, settled in and functioning as a resident rather than a guest. And “provided that” translates a conditional particle used for something assumed true, offered as a test rather than a doubt: the indwelling Spirit is the diagnostic that settles which side of the earlier contrast a person is actually on.","translation":"But you, you are not in the sphere of the flesh but in the sphere of the Spirit, provided that the Spirit of God is at home, dwelling, in you. But if anyone does not have the Spirit of Christ, this one does not belong to him."},{"c1":8,"v1":10,"c2":8,"v2":10,"label":"Romans 8:10","text":"The “spirit” set against “body” in this verse is the believer's own human spirit, not the Holy Spirit—the contrast only makes sense as the two component parts of a person. The body already carries death in it, a legacy of Adam's sin working itself out physically. But the human spirit is “alive,” and the life-word used isn't just continued existence—it's life with a moral quality attached, shaped by righteousness rather than neutral survival.","translation":"But if Christ is in you, the body, on the one hand, is dead because of sin, but the spirit, on the other hand, is alive because of righteousness."},{"c1":8,"v1":11,"c2":8,"v2":11,"label":"Romans 8:11","text":"“Quicken” is a compound built directly on the words for “life” and “make”—not resuscitation of what nearly died but the impartation of a new order of life to a body still subject to death. The phrase “out from among the dead,” used of both Christ's resurrection and the believer's coming one, pictures a targeted extraction, pulled out from the general company of the dead rather than simply revived. Paul credits the same Spirit already resident in the believer with performing this future act—present indwelling is the guarantee of future resurrection.","translation":"And if the Spirit of the One who raised up Jesus out from among the dead is dwelling in you, he who raised up Christ out from among the dead will also make alive your mortal bodies, through his Spirit who dwells in you."},{"c1":8,"v1":12,"c2":8,"v2":13,"label":"Romans 8:12–13","text":"“Debtors” pictures an obligation owed, and Paul's point is that the believer owes nothing to the flesh—the debt has been cancelled. The two conditional clauses that follow both use present-tense verbs describing habitual, ongoing patterns rather than isolated acts: one who habitually lives under the flesh's dominion is already in motion toward death. The verb for “mortify,” reaching back to the same root used in 7:4 for the believer's own death with Christ, describes a continuous, repeated putting-to-death of the body's deeds—an ongoing campaign carried out through the Spirit's power, not the believer's own effort.","translation":"So then, brothers, we are debtors—not to the flesh, to go on living according to the flesh. For if you are habitually living according to the flesh, you are on your way to dying. But if by the Spirit you are habitually putting to death the deeds of the body, you will live."},{"c1":8,"v1":14,"c2":8,"v2":17,"label":"Romans 8:14–17","text":"Paul has two separate words for “child” elsewhere—one for a born child still under guardians, one for an adult son with full legal standing—and “sons” here is the second: the language of Roman adoption law, where a person became someone's son with full inheritance rights through a formal legal act rather than birth. “Adoption” is literally “son-placing,” a term with no real equivalent in Jewish custom—Paul is reaching for Roman legal vocabulary his readers would recognize. The cry “Abba, Father” repeats the same address in two languages rather than translating one into the other; the doubling itself is the point, a child's instinct to say the word twice. And “bears witness with” is built on the word for testimony plus “together”—two witnesses, the Spirit and the believer's own spirit, independently arriving at, and jointly declaring, the same verdict.","translation":"For as many as are being led by the Spirit of God, these are sons of God. For you did not receive a spirit of slavery, leading again to fear, but you received the Spirit of adoption—of son-placing—by whom we cry out, Abba, Father! The Spirit himself bears joint witness with our spirit, that we are children of God; and if children, also heirs—heirs of God, and joint heirs with Christ, provided that we suffer with him, so that we may also be glorified with him."},{"c1":8,"v1":18,"c2":8,"v2":18,"label":"Romans 8:18","text":"“Reckon” is an accounting term—Paul isn't reporting a feeling but the result of a calculation, weighing one thing against another and stating the conclusion the math produces. “Worthy” continues the image: the word means literally “weighing as much as,” the language of a scale, and Paul's claim is that present suffering simply doesn't register on the same scale as the coming glory. The preposition behind “in us” points outward, toward or upon—the glory isn't a private internal experience but something directed onto believers from outside, made visible.","translation":"For I have calculated that the sufferings of the present time do not weigh as much, are not worthy to be compared, with the glory that is about to be revealed toward us."},{"c1":8,"v1":19,"c2":8,"v2":21,"label":"Romans 8:19–21","text":"“Earnest expectation” is a single compound word built from “away,” “head,” and “watch”—the picture is of someone straining forward, head craned out, eyes fixed on one point, attention pulled away from everything else. Paul applies this to creation itself: the word for “creature” means the whole non-rational created order, animate and inanimate together, personified as a watcher waiting for something specific. The verb for how creation “was made subject” to futility is passive, and Paul is careful about the agency behind it—the construction points to God's will as the occasion rather than naming God as the direct actor, keeping the responsibility for the actual subjecting one step removed even while affirming God ordained the outcome. And the hope in “subjected in hope” belongs grammatically to the thing subjected, not to the one who did the subjecting—creation's own hope, written into the very sentence describing its bondage.","translation":"For the creation, with head craned forward and eyes fixed, is waiting, patiently and expectantly, for the uncovering of the sons of God. For the creation was subjected to futility—not willingly, but on account of him who subjected it—in hope that the creation itself also will be set free from its slavery to decay, into the freedom that belongs to the glory of the children of God."},{"c1":8,"v1":22,"c2":8,"v2":23,"label":"Romans 8:22–23","text":"The compound verbs Paul uses for creation's groaning both carry “together” as a prefix, but the togetherness is among all the parts of creation groaning in unison with each other—the comparison to believers groaning alongside creation comes next, as its own separate clause. “Firstfruits” is a harvest term for the first installment of a crop, guaranteeing the rest is still to come; Paul applies it to the Spirit's present work in believers as a down payment on a fuller inheritance not yet received.","translation":"For we know that the whole creation is groaning together and travailing together, until now. And not creation only, but we ourselves also, who have the first installment of the Spirit—we ourselves also groan within ourselves, waiting eagerly for our adoption, the redemption of our body."},{"c1":8,"v1":24,"c2":8,"v2":25,"label":"Romans 8:24–25","text":"Paul's phrase is literally “we were saved in hope”—hope named as the sphere salvation currently exists in, not yet fully realized. He builds a small paradox out of the word itself: hope that has already been seen fulfilled stops being hope by definition, since the word only applies to what remains outstanding. And the word behind “patience” isn't passive waiting but an active steadfastness, the capacity to hold up and keep going under a sustained load.","translation":"For in hope we were saved. But hope that is seen is not hope; for who hopes for what he already sees? But if we hope for what we do not see, we wait for it through patient endurance."},{"c1":8,"v1":26,"c2":8,"v2":27,"label":"Romans 8:26–27","text":"The verb behind “helps” is a compound built from three pieces—“with,” “over against,” and “take hold”—picturing someone grabbing the other end of a load and lifting it together with the person carrying it, not taking the whole burden away. The weakness in view is specific to prayer: we know the general aim of every prayer but not “the what,” the definite article marking the missing piece as the particular, situation-specific content we can't supply on our own. “Makes intercession” carries the sense of coming upon someone in distress and stepping in on their behalf, a rescue verb rather than a formal petition, and it's paired with groanings that are literally “unspoken”—inarticulate, never becoming language at all.","translation":"And in the same way the Spirit also lends a hand with our weakness; for we do not know the particular thing we should pray for, as is necessary, but the Spirit himself comes to our rescue with wordless groanings. And he who searches the hearts knows what the mind of the Spirit is, because he intercedes on behalf of the saints according to God."},{"c1":8,"v1":28,"c2":8,"v2":28,"label":"Romans 8:28","text":"The oldest manuscripts split on whether this verse names God explicitly as the one doing the working, or leaves the sentence without a stated agent—either way the sense converges on the same actor. The verb itself is present tense and compound, “working together,” describing an ongoing, continuous process rather than a single guaranteed outcome announced after the fact. And “called” carries its narrower Pauline sense here—not a general invitation extended to everyone but the effective call that actually brings a person to faith, tied to God's own purpose rather than the person's initiative.","translation":"And we know that all things are continually working together for good, for those who love God, for those who are the called ones, according to his purpose."},{"c1":8,"v1":29,"c2":8,"v2":30,"label":"Romans 8:29–30","text":"“Foreknew” is built from the ordinary word for “know” with “before” prefixed—its plain lexical sense is a prior knowledge, though how far that knowledge extends (bare foresight, or an active choosing) has been argued for centuries and the word's makeup alone doesn't settle it. “Predestined” is more transparent: it's built on the verb for marking out a fixed boundary, with “before” attached—to set fixed limits on something in advance, here the shape a life will eventually take. “Conformed to his image” sets two words carefully side by side: one for outward form expressing an inner reality, and one for a “derived” likeness, the kind of image a coin bears from the die that struck it, or a child bears from a parent—not a coincidental resemblance between two unrelated things. And the chain of verbs running through these verses—foreknew, predestined, called, justified, glorified—are all in the same simple past tense, including “glorified,” even though the glorification is still future: Paul states it as already settled, certain in God's own accounting before it happens in ours.","translation":"Because those whom he foreknew, he also predestined to be conformed to the image, the derived likeness, of his Son, so that he might be the firstborn among many brothers. And those whom he predestined, these he also called; and those whom he called, these he also justified; and those whom he justified, these he also—already—glorified."},{"c1":8,"v1":31,"c2":8,"v2":32,"label":"Romans 8:31–32","text":"The “if” here is a fulfilled condition—Paul isn't raising the possibility that God might be for us, he's arguing from the settled fact that he is. “His own Son” uses a word for what belongs to someone as a private, particular possession—not just “a son” but this one, peculiarly and intimately his. And the verb for “give” in “give us all things” is built directly on the word for grace—a gift extended freely, not a payment owed, matching the argument's logic: if the costliest gift is already given, the lesser ones follow as a matter of course.","translation":"What then shall we say to these things? Since God is for us, who can be against us? He who did not spare his own—his very own—Son, but gave him up on behalf of us all, how will he not, along with him, freely grace us with all things?"},{"c1":8,"v1":33,"c2":8,"v2":34,"label":"Romans 8:33–34","text":"“Elect” means literally “chosen out”—the word itself pictures a selection made from a larger group. Both rhetorical questions here turn on a logical impossibility rather than an emotional appeal: the same person cannot occupy two contradictory roles at once. God cannot be the one bringing an accusation and the one issuing the justifying verdict simultaneously—the two acts cancel each other out. And Christ cannot be condemning while, in the same breath, he is interceding on the accused person's behalf; the string of verbs describing him—died, raised, seated, interceding—builds a resume that makes condemnation from that quarter simply incoherent.","translation":"Who will bring a charge against God's chosen ones? God is the one who justifies. Who is the one who condemns? Christ Jesus is the one who died—more than that, who was raised—who is at the right hand of God, who also is interceding on our behalf."},{"c1":8,"v1":35,"c2":8,"v2":39,"label":"Romans 8:35–39","text":"“More than conquerors” translates a single compound verb, built by stacking the ordinary word for victory under a prefix meaning “over and above.” Paul isn't claiming a narrow win; he's claiming that the very things threatening to separate believers from Christ's love end up adding to the victory rather than merely failing to stop it. “I am persuaded” is a perfect-tense verb—not a present feeling of confidence but the settled result of a process already completed, a conclusion reasoned to at some point in the past that has stood, unmoved, ever since. And “creature” in the final phrase, “nor any other created thing,” widens the list to its outer limit—every category already named, and then, in case anything was missed, the whole of creation itself, named and excluded.","translation":"Who will separate us from the love of Christ? Will tribulation, or distress, or persecution, or famine, or nakedness, or danger, or sword? Just as it stands written: For your sake we are being put to death all day long; we were regarded as sheep marked for slaughter. But in all these things we are coming off more than victorious, through him who loved us. For I have become, and remain, persuaded that neither death, nor life, nor angels, nor rulers, nor things present, nor things to come, nor powers, nor height, nor depth, nor any other created thing will be able to separate us from the love of God, which is in Christ Jesus our Lord."},{"c1":9,"v1":10,"c2":9,"v2":13,"label":"Romans 9:10–13","text":"The phrase \"purpose according to election\" translates *hē kat' eklogēn prothesis* (hē kat' eklogēn prothesis) — literally \"the according-to-selecting-out purpose,\" with the prepositional phrase functioning like an adjective bolted onto \"purpose.\" *Prothesis* (prothesis) comes from *protithēmi*, \"to set before oneself,\" the ordinary word for a plan a person weighs out and settles on in advance — not an impulse but a deliberated resolve. *Eklogē* (eklogē) is built on *eklegō*, \"to pick out from a group,\" so the purpose in view is one that operates by selecting, not by rewarding merit already present. Paul's point rides on timing: the twins were chosen and rejected before either had been born or had done a single thing, good or bad — so whatever \"election\" means here, it cannot be God ratifying a distinction that human conduct had already created.\n\nThe verb translated \"hated,\" *miseō* (miseō), ordinarily does mean outright hatred, but set against \"loved\" in a comparison like this one it works the way the Hebrew idiom does — naming the weaker of two loves rather than active hostility. \"Esau I hated\" reads more naturally as \"Esau I loved less\" than as a claim that God bore Esau active malice.","translation":"And not only that — when Rebecca also had conceived by one man, our father Isaac — for the children not yet having been born, nor having practiced anything good or bad, in order that the purpose of God, the one according to a selecting-out, might continue standing, not out of a source of works but out of the One who calls, it was said to her: The older shall be servant to the younger; even as it stands written, Jacob I loved, but Esau I loved less."},{"c1":9,"v1":14,"c2":9,"v2":16,"label":"Romans 9:14–16","text":"Paul's reply hinges on two different verb forms of the same promise. The first \"I will have mercy\" is a plain future indicative — a straightforward prediction of what God will do. The second occurrence, inside the relative clause, shifts to a present subjunctive, and the relative pronoun carries the indefinite particle *an* (an), turning \"whom\" into \"whoever, no matter who.\" That grammatical shift is the argument in miniature: God isn't naming favorites in advance so much as asserting that mercy will go wherever he decides to send it, and the decision belongs to him alone, not to any claim a person can lodge against him.\n\n\"Not of him that willeth, nor of him that runneth\" names desire and effort, not identity — \"willeth\" is simple wanting, \"runneth\" pictures a competitor straining toward a finish line, laboring for the prize. Paul denies that either wanting mercy or working for it secures it; only God's own character as the one who shows mercy does. The opening question — \"Is there unrighteousness with God?\" — is phrased in Greek with *mē* (mē), the negative that expects the answer no; Paul isn't entertaining the charge, he's dismissing it before he even argues against it.","translation":"What then shall we say? There is no unrighteousness present with God — is there? Let it not be! For to Moses he is saying: I will have mercy on whomever I am having mercy on, and I will have compassion on whomever I am having compassion on. So then, it is not of the one who is wanting it, nor of the one who is running for it, but of the God who is showing mercy."},{"c1":9,"v1":19,"c2":9,"v2":21,"label":"Romans 9:19–21","text":"\"Resisted\" translates a perfect-tense form of *anthistēmi* (anthistēmi), built from *histēmi*, \"to stand,\" plus the prefix *anti*, \"against.\" The perfect tense doesn't describe an ongoing struggle so much as an arrival — a stand against God's will that has been taken and now simply stands, a settled, finished posture of opposition. Paul isn't picturing someone still pushing; he's picturing someone who has already dug in and stayed there.\n\n\"Repliest against\" is *antapokrinomai* (antapokrinomai), a compound of *apokrinomai*, \"to answer,\" with that same combative *anti* prefix — not a fresh answer but a contradiction thrown back at an answer already given. The objector isn't asking a question; he's talking back. And \"O man\" is deliberately set opposite \"God\" in the sentence — small against great, a reminder of scale before the argument even starts.\n\nThe clay imagery runs on two separate words. \"Lump\" is *phyrama* (phyrama), from a verb meaning \"to knead into dough\" — an undifferentiated mass, not yet shaped into anything in particular. \"Vessel\" is *skeuos* (skeuos), the ordinary word for any household implement or container, useful precisely because it can be shaped toward one purpose or another out of that same mass. \"The thing formed\" is *plasma* (plasma) — our word \"plastic\" descends from it, meaning simply molded material, still pliable in the potter's hands.","translation":"You will say to me then: Why is he still finding fault? For who has taken a stand — and holds it — against his purpose? O man, who indeed are you, the one talking back against God? The molded thing will not say to the one who molded it, Why did you make me like this — will it? Or does the potter not have authority over the clay, out of the same lump to make on the one hand a vessel for honor, and on the other a vessel for dishonor?"},{"c1":9,"v1":22,"c2":9,"v2":24,"label":"Romans 9:22–24","text":"\"Willing to show his wrath\" places the emphasis on God's spontaneous character rather than a settled decree — this is what his holiness disposes him to do, not a hidden plan to magnify judgment later. That's exactly why the sentence pivots to longsuffering: God, though so disposed, held back and endured instead of acting on it.\n\n\"Vessels of wrath\" doesn't mean vessels filled with wrath or built for the purpose of displaying it — it means vessels that belong to wrath, that have, by their own doing, come under it. The participle \"fitted,\" *katartizō* (katartizō), describes a present, ready-made condition — ripe for destruction — without naming who or what produced that condition. Compare \"vessels of mercy,\" where the verb changes to *proetoimazō* (proetoimazō), \"prepared beforehand,\" built with the \"before\" prefix spelled out explicitly. Verse 23 says plainly whose action this is and that it happened in advance; verse 22 leaves the agency unstated and simply names the result. The asymmetry is deliberate: mercy is traced straight to God's prior act, while wrath is described as an achieved condition, its history left for the reader to trace back to human conduct.","translation":"But if, wanting to show his wrath and to make his power known, God bore, with much patient endurance, vessels of wrath, standing ready, ripened for destruction — in order that he might make known the riches of his glory upon vessels of mercy, which he prepared beforehand for glory, even us, whom he called, not only out from among Jews but also out from among Gentiles —"},{"c1":9,"v1":27,"c2":9,"v2":29,"label":"Romans 9:27–29","text":"\"Crieth\" is *krazō* (krazō), a loud, unrestrained cry rather than measured prophetic speech — Isaiah isn't reasoning with Israel here, he's crying out over them. The remnant theme carries the weight of the passage: even if Israel's number rivals the sand of the sea, only a remnant will be saved.\n\nThe word rendered \"word\" in \"finishing and cutting short\" is *logos* (logos), not a term for a legal decree — it means an utterance or reckoning, closer to \"account\" than \"command.\" \"Remnant\" is *hypoleimma* (hypoleimma), simply \"what is left over\" — the surviving portion after everything else has been cut away, echoing the small company preserved out of a nation otherwise as numerous as sand.","translation":"And Isaiah is crying out over Israel: If the number of the sons of Israel should be as the sand of the sea, the remnant will be saved. For the Lord will accomplish his reckoning upon the earth, finishing it and cutting it short. And just as Isaiah has said beforehand: Unless the Lord of hosts had left to us a seed, we would have become like Sodom, and would have been made to resemble Gomorrah."},{"c1":9,"v1":30,"c2":9,"v2":33,"label":"Romans 9:30–33","text":"There's no article before \"Gentiles\" in the Greek — Paul is speaking of Gentiles as a category, not a specific group. \"Followed after\" is *diōkō* (diōkō), the verb for running hard after something, chasing it down — the very picture of the earnest pursuit the Gentiles, ironically, weren't engaged in. \"Attained\" is *katalambanō* (katalambanō), \"to seize, lay hold of and make one's own\" — righteousness came to those not chasing it, and they simply took hold of it.\n\nIsrael gets a different verb for the same idea in verse 31: not *katalambanō* but *phthanō* (phthanō), \"to arrive at, reach\" — and the negative form says Israel, for all its pursuing, never reached the goal it was chasing. The contrast is exact: those not running arrived; those running never did, because they were running on the wrong track — works instead of faith. \"Righteousness\" repeats three times across these verses, underlining that this — not ancestry, not law-keeping — is the real question dividing Paul from his kinsmen. The stone Israel stumbled over is Messiah himself, the demand of faith where they expected achievement.","translation":"What then shall we say? That Gentiles, the ones not chasing after righteousness, laid hold of righteousness — a righteousness, that is, that is out of faith. But Israel, chasing after a law of righteousness, did not arrive at that law. Because of what? Because they pursued it not out of faith but as out of works. They struck their foot against the stone of stumbling, just as it stands written: Behold, I am placing in Zion a stone that trips, a rock that offends, and the one believing upon him will not be put to shame."},{"c1":10,"v1":2,"c2":10,"v2":4,"label":"Romans 10:2–4","text":"\"Zeal of God\" is a genitive of description — zeal that takes God as its object, zeal for him — and Paul concedes it freely; his complaint isn't that it's absent but that it's misdirected. That misdirection traces to a word choice in the next clause: \"knowledge\" here is *epignōsis* (epignōsis), full, accurate, experiential knowledge, not the plainer *gnōsis*. Israel had real zeal running on partial, insufficient knowledge, and partial knowledge sent that zeal down the wrong road.\n\n\"Going about to establish\" uses *histēmi* (histēmi), \"to set up, erect\" — the picture is of building something as one's own monument. \"Their own\" is *idios* (idios): a righteousness that is personally, privately theirs, the product of their own labor rather than a gift received. \"Submitted\" is *hypotassō* (hypotassō), a military term for taking one's place in rank under a commanding officer — better rendered \"subjected themselves.\" Israel's failure wasn't a lack of effort but a refusal to fall in under God's righteousness instead of erecting their own. \"End\" translates *telos* (telos), the point at which something reaches its limit and stops being operative — Christ is the terminus of law as a road to righteousness, not one more law added to the pile.","translation":"For I am testifying about them that a zeal for God they have, but not according to full, accurate knowledge. For, being ignorant of the righteousness of God, and seeking to erect their own private righteousness, to the righteousness of God they did not subject themselves. For Christ is the end of the law, unto righteousness to everyone who is believing."},{"c1":10,"v1":5,"c2":10,"v2":8,"label":"Romans 10:5–8","text":"Paul quotes Leviticus 18:5, \"the man who does the righteousness of the law shall live by it,\" and the verb underneath \"shall live by it\" — *zēsetai en autēi* (zēsetai en autēi) — hangs ambiguous between sphere and means: \"shall live in it\" or \"shall live by it.\" Either way, the demand is achievement — you must do it to live by it — which is exactly why Paul treats law-righteousness as a dead end for anyone trying to be justified by it rather than simply ordering a life already made right by faith.\n\nAgainst that, \"the righteousness of faith\" is personified as speaking for itself, freely adapting Deuteronomy 30:12–14 rather than citing it as a legal proof text. \"Abyss\" translates *abyssos* (abyssos), literally \"bottomless,\" used here for the realm of the dead. The rhetorical questions — who will go up to heaven, who will go down to the abyss — both cash out (Paul spells this out with \"that is,\" *tout' estin*) as absurd errands, since Christ has already come down in the incarnation and already come up in the resurrection; there's nothing left to fetch. \"Word\" in \"the word of faith\" is *hrēma* (hrēma), not *logos* — *hrēma* is the specific spoken utterance, the actual words on someone's lips, rather than the larger, comprehensive idea *logos* carries. Salvation isn't a truth to be quested after; it's a message already sitting on the tongue and in the heart.","translation":"For Moses is writing that the man who does the righteousness that is of the law shall live by it. But the righteousness that is of faith speaks in this way: Do not say in your heart, Who will go up into heaven? — that is, to bring Christ down; or, Who will go down into the abyss? — that is, to bring Christ up from among the dead. But what does it say? The saying is near you, in your mouth and in your heart — this is the saying of faith, which we are proclaiming."},{"c1":10,"v1":11,"c2":10,"v2":13,"label":"Romans 10:11–13","text":"\"Shall not be ashamed\" is better rendered \"will not be put to shame\" — *kataischynō* (kataischynō) carries a Hebrew sense of suffering repulse or defeat, not embarrassment. Paul isn't promising that believers won't feel awkward; he's promising they won't be beaten back, disappointed, left holding nothing after staking their faith on Christ.\n\n\"Difference\" is *diastolē* (diastolē), from a verb meaning \"to draw apart, distinguish\" — a plain distinction, and Paul denies there is one between Jew and Gentile (\"Greek\" standing in for Gentile generally) at the point of faith. \"Lord,\" *kyrios* (kyrios), applied here to Jesus, is the same title the Greek Old Testament uses for the divine name — using it of Christ is itself a claim to his deity, not incidental vocabulary.","translation":"For the scripture says: Everyone who is believing upon him will not be put to shame. For there is no distinction between Jew and Greek, for the same Lord is Lord of all, being rich toward all who are calling upon him. For whoever calls upon the name of the Lord will be saved."},{"c1":10,"v1":18,"c2":10,"v2":18,"label":"Romans 10:18","text":"Paul's question \"did they not hear?\" is framed to force a \"yes\" — of course they heard; he isn't wondering, he's ruling out an excuse. He proves it from Psalm 19:4, a text originally celebrating the heavens' wordless testimony to God, now applied to the gospel's spread. \"World\" renders *oikoumenē* (oikoumenē), the technical term for the settled, civilized world under Roman rule — not the broader *kosmos* — which makes Paul's claim a real, checkable one: by his day the gospel had traveled the empire about as widely as the Jewish diaspora itself.\n\nHe follows with a second question: did Israel fail to *understand*? \"Know\" here is *ginōskō* (ginōskō), knowledge gained through firsthand grasp, not bare information. The question is built with the negative *mē* (mē), which likewise expects \"no\" — Israel understood perfectly well and has no excuse. Moses had already warned that God would provoke Israel to jealousy through \"a no-people\" — Gentiles are literally termed a *no-people*, outsiders with no covenant standing, who would nevertheless receive what Israel spurned. Isaiah goes further still, saying God was \"found by those not seeking\" him — grace arriving before anyone asked.","translation":"But I say, Did they not hear? Certainly they did — into all the earth their voice went out, and their words to the ends of the inhabited world. But I say, Did Israel fail to understand? First Moses says: I will provoke you to jealousy by a no-people, by a nation without understanding I will provoke you to anger. And Isaiah is bold enough to say: I was found by those not seeking me; I became revealed to those not asking for me."},{"c1":10,"v1":21,"c2":10,"v2":21,"label":"Romans 10:21","text":"The image is of hands held out all day long — a patient, unbroken appeal that keeps offering itself even after repeated refusal. \"Disobedient\" translates *apeitheō* (apeitheō), literally \"not allowing oneself to be persuaded\" — a settled refusal to be won over, stronger than simple ignorance. \"Gainsaying\" is *antilegō* (antilegō), \"to speak against,\" picturing active, deliberate opposition rather than passive neglect. Israel's exclusion, Paul insists, isn't due to missed opportunity; it's willful, talked-back-at-God resistance.","translation":"But to Israel he says: The whole day long I stretched out my hands to a people refusing to be persuaded and speaking against me."},{"c1":11,"v1":2,"c2":11,"v2":4,"label":"Romans 11:2–4","text":"\"Foreknew\" translates *proginōskō* (proginōskō); in its handful of New Testament uses about God, it carries more than advance awareness — it marks a people fixed beforehand as God's own, foreordination rather than mere prediction. That's the weight behind Paul's argument: a nation chosen in eternity cannot simply be discarded in time. \"Wot\" is *oida* (oida), certain knowledge, not opinion — Paul assumes his readers already hold this fact as settled. The Elijah episode is introduced with an idiom meaning \"in the case of\" or \"in the history of\" Elijah, pointing to a known precedent. Elijah's complaint uses *entugchanō* (entunchanō) compounded with *kata* (kata, \"against\") — literally \"to plead against\" — and \"digged down\" is *kataskaptō* (kataskaptō), \"to dig under and demolish,\" siege language turned on Israel's own altars. God's reply is a *chrēmatismos* (chrēmatismos), a divine oracle — an authoritative pronouncement, not a private impression. The seven thousand are marked by a qualitative relative pronoun, \"who are of such a sort\" — identified not by a headcount but by character: they had not bent the knee to Baal.\n\nPaul then distinguishes *kairos* (kairos) from *chronos* (chronos): not clock-time but a strategic, decisive season — the present moment of Jew-and-Gentile inclusion is one of those hinge-points in God's plan. \"Remnant\" is *limma* (leimma), from *leipō* (leipō), \"to leave behind\" — what's left over when the rest falls away. \"Election\" is *eklogē* (eklogē), \"a picking out,\" and Paul grounds it explicitly in grace rather than merit. \"Is\" — describing both the remnant's existence and, a moment later, the nature of grace itself — is *ginomai* (ginomai), \"to become,\" a verb of coming-into-being rather than static fact: the remnant has come into permanent existence by grace, and grace, if it required works alongside it, would stop being grace altogether — the two categories cancel each other the moment they're mixed.","translation":"God did not thrust away his people, whom he foreordained. Or do you not know with certainty, in the case of Elijah, what the scripture says — how he pleads against Israel: Lord, they have killed your prophets, they have dug down your altars, and I alone am left, and they are seeking my life. But what does the divine oracle say to him? I have kept in reserve for myself seven thousand men, who are of such a sort as have not bent the knee to Baal. So too, then, at this present decisive season, a remnant has come into being according to a choosing grounded in grace. But if it is by grace, it is no longer on the basis of works — otherwise grace stops being grace."},{"c1":11,"v1":7,"c2":11,"v2":10,"label":"Romans 11:7–10","text":"\"Blinded\" is *pōroō* (pōroō), literally \"to cover with a callus\" — a hardening that grows over the heart the way scar tissue grows over skin. \"Slumber\" is *katanuxis* (katanuxis), a deep insensibility of mind — not drowsiness but a numbness so complete the gospel simply stops registering. The imagery piles up: *trapeza* (trapeza), \"table,\" pictures a settled, feasting prosperity turned into the very trap that catches its owner; *pagis* (pagis) is a hunter's snare for birds and *thēra* (thēra) a hunter's trap for beasts, both describing destruction laid and sprung; *antapodoma* (antapodoma), \"repayment,\" signals that none of this is arbitrary — it's earned consequence. \"Bow down\" is *sunkamptō* (synkamptō), \"to bend together,\" the posture of captives whose backs are bent low under a load. As with Pharaoh, whose heart hardened itself before God hardened it further, the pattern is self-inflicted first and divinely confirmed second — reject the light long enough, and blindness becomes judgment.","translation":"What then? What Israel was continually seeking after, this it did not obtain — but the chosen ones obtained it, and the rest were made calloused, just as it stands written: God gave them a spirit of deep insensibility, eyes for not seeing and ears for not hearing, down to this very day. And David says: Let their table become for them a snare and a trap, a stumbling block and a just repayment; let their eyes be darkened so that they do not see, and keep on bending their backs down continually."},{"c1":11,"v1":11,"c2":11,"v2":11,"label":"Romans 11:11","text":"The question is set up with the negative *mē* (mē), demanding \"no\" — Paul isn't wondering, he's shutting a door. The contrast between \"stumble\" and \"fall\" carries real weight: a stumble is a stagger you recover from, a fall is a collapse you don't get up from, and Paul insists Israel's rejection of the Messiah is the former, not the latter. *Hina* (hina), which normally marks purpose (\"in order that\"), functions here more as result — Israel's stumble wasn't aimed at its own ruin, but God turned it, in his providence, into an opening for the gospel to reach the Gentiles, which was in turn meant to provoke Israel's jealousy and draw it back toward the salvation it had refused.","translation":"I say then, They did not stumble so as to fall, did they? Certainly not! But by their stumbling, salvation has come to the Gentiles, so as to provoke them to jealousy."},{"c1":11,"v1":12,"c2":11,"v2":12,"label":"Romans 11:12","text":"\"Diminishing\" translates *hēttēma* (hēttēma), a word for defeat and loss, not a shrinking headcount — Israel's problem is a forfeited inheritance, not fewer people. Paul argues from lesser to greater: if Israel's defeat already overflowed into riches for the world, \"fullness\" — *plērōma* (plērōma), \"that which has been filled up, completed\" — names Israel's future full restoration, and that completion promises gains far exceeding what the Gentiles have already gained from Israel's failure.\n\nThe connecting word is *de* (de), \"but,\" marking a turn in direction rather than *gar* (\"for\") giving a reason — Paul isn't explaining why Gentiles matter, he's pivoting to his own stake in the matter. \"Magnify\" is *doxazō* (doxazō), \"to glorify\" — Paul honors his Gentile ministry by discharging it faithfully, and he does so with an eye toward his own people: the more fruitful his work among Gentiles, the more likely it is to provoke his kinsmen to jealousy. \"Provoke to emulation\" and \"provoke to jealousy\" translate the same verb, *parazēloō* (parazēloō) — Paul's Gentile mission is deliberately aimed, in part, at making Israel want back what it walked away from.","translation":"Now if their fall means riches for the world, and their defeat and loss means riches for the Gentiles, how much more will their fullness mean? But I am speaking to you Gentiles. Since, then, I am an apostle to the Gentiles, I bring honor to my ministry, if somehow I may provoke to jealousy those of my own flesh, and save some of them."},{"c1":11,"v1":15,"c2":11,"v2":15,"label":"Romans 11:15","text":"The \"if\" running through this argument is *ei* (ei), the \"if\" of a fact already granted, not *ean* (ean), the \"if\" of a mere hypothesis — Paul is reasoning from something established, better rendered \"since\" or \"given that.\" Israel's \"casting away\" is its temporary setting-aside as the channel of salvation, with the Church stepping into that role — a shift that has already brought reconciliation to the world. Israel's future \"receiving,\" by contrast, is pictured as \"life from among the dead\" — not a gentle improvement but a resurrection-scale reversal, out of a nation's spiritual deadness into life.","translation":"For since their rejection means the reconciling of the world, what will their receiving back mean, but life from among the dead?"},{"c1":11,"v1":16,"c2":11,"v2":16,"label":"Romans 11:16","text":"\"For\" here is *de* (de), carrying a continuative \"now\" rather than a strict causal \"for.\" \"First-fruit\" points to the patriarchs, drawing on the law of the first portion of dough set apart for the priests (Numbers 15) — a small part offered on behalf of the whole. The \"if\" is again *ei* (ei), the \"since\" of an established fact. \"Holy\" is *hagios* (hagios), \"set apart,\" a status word rather than a claim about moral perfection — the patriarchs' consecration to God carries the whole nation's future along with it: root and branches share the same set-apart standing.\n\nThe olive-tree picture that follows is worth noting for how it *breaks* the normal agricultural process rather than following it: ordinarily a cultivated shoot is grafted onto wild rootstock to improve it, but here it's the wild branch — the Gentile — grafted into the cultivated tree, an inversion Paul elsewhere calls \"contrary to nature.\" Natural branches (unbelieving Israel) are cut away; the wild branch draws its life from a root it did not grow. The point of the picture is directional: nourishment flows from root to branch, not the reverse, so the grafted-in Gentile owes everything to the root and has no grounds to look down on the branches broken off beside him.","translation":"Now since the first portion is holy, so is the whole lump; and since the root is holy, so are the branches. Now since some of the branches were broken off, and you, a wild olive shoot, were grafted in among them and became a partaker with them of the root and the richness of the olive tree, stop boasting against the branches. But if you do boast — remember, you do not support the root; the root supports you."},{"c1":11,"v1":19,"c2":11,"v2":21,"label":"Romans 11:19–21","text":"\"Well\" translates *kalōs* (kalōs), used here as a term of grudging, partly ironic concession — Paul isn't agreeing, he's letting the objection stand only long enough to correct it. The point he presses is that both being broken off and standing firm are governed by moral conditions, not automatic status: the branches were broken off \"because of unbelief,\" and the one standing now stands \"by faith,\" full stop — no inherited immunity either way. The comparison is corporate throughout — the nation Israel and the Gentile world as collective entities — not a statement about any one believer's grip on salvation. Paul's warning targets presumption in a class of people, unbelieving Gentiles who might coast on privilege, not genuine faith itself: if God did not spare the natural branches for unbelief, he will not spare branches grafted in artificially if they fall into the same unbelief.","translation":"You will say then, Branches were broken off so that I might be grafted in. Granted. They were broken off because of unbelief, and you stand by faith. Do not be high-minded, but be fearing; for if God did not spare the natural branches, neither will he spare you."},{"c1":11,"v1":22,"c2":11,"v2":22,"label":"Romans 11:22","text":"\"Goodness\" is *chrēstotēs* (chrēstotēs), kindness or benignity; \"severity\" is *apotomia* (apotomia), a word built on the image of something cut off sheer — a cliff-face, an abrupt drop — describing rigor without softness. The condition attached is *ean* (ean), a genuine hypothetical: everything depends on \"continuing\" — *epimenō* (epimenō), \"to remain, to abide,\" a word often used of a guest staying on in someone's house. It pictures an ongoing relationship of dependence, not a one-time transaction; the Gentile's standing lasts only as long as he keeps abiding in that kindness rather than presuming on it. \"Cut off\" is literally \"cut out,\" the same pruning image used of the branches removed earlier — a warning that privilege without continuance is no privilege at all.","translation":"See then the kindness and the severity of God — severity toward those who fell, but toward you, kindness, provided you keep on abiding in that kindness. Otherwise you too will be cut out."},{"c1":11,"v1":23,"c2":11,"v2":23,"label":"Romans 11:23","text":"\"They\" is *ekeinos* (ekeinos), \"those,\" a pointed, at-arm's-length pronoun for the unbelieving Jews. The condition is again *ean* (ean), genuinely open — nothing forces their unbelief to continue. Paul's claim that \"God is able\" assumes not just the possibility but the real difficulty of regrafting what was cut away — restoring a broken branch is a harder feat than grafting in a wild one to begin with.\n\n\"Mystery\" is *mustērion* (mustērion), a once-hidden plan of God now disclosed to believers, not a puzzle left unsolved. Paul distinguishes two timelines that are easy to blur: the political dominance of Gentile powers over Israel, running from Babylon to the final defeat of Antichrist, and the \"fullness of the Gentiles,\" the completion of the Church, the body gathered from Pentecost onward. Israel's hardening is explicitly partial — \"in part\" — and time-bound, lasting only until that fullness is reached.\n\n\"And thus\" describes manner more than sequence — not merely \"afterward\" but \"by this means\": through the very jealousy Gentile inclusion was designed to provoke, the nation as a corporate whole will be saved, each living Israelite converted at once at the Messiah's return, fulfilling covenant language about a Deliverer removing Jacob's sin.\n\nFinally, \"concerning\"/\"touching\" render *kata* (kata), marking two respects in which God relates to Israel: hostile as measured by their present rejection of the gospel, beloved as measured by his covenant with the fathers. \"Gifts\" is *charisma* (charisma), a favor given without any merit earning it — a stronger word than the ordinary *dōron* (dōron), \"gift.\" \"Calling\" is *klēsis* (klēsis), the technical term for God's authoritative summons into his kingdom, not a modern \"vocation.\" And \"without repentance\" is *ametamelētos* (ametamelētos), built on *metamelomai* (metamelomai), \"to change one's mind\" — God's gifts and calling to Israel are not subject to a change of his mind; what he has promised the nation remains irrevocably on the books.","translation":"And those also, if they do not continue in their unbelief, will be grafted in; for God is able to graft them in again. For if you were cut out of what is by nature a wild olive tree, and contrary to nature were grafted into a cultivated olive tree, how much more will these, who are the natural branches, be grafted back into their own olive tree? For I do not want you to be ignorant of this mystery, brothers, so that you may not be wise in your own estimation: a hardening in part has come upon Israel, until the fullness of the Gentiles has come in. And by this means all Israel will be saved, just as it stands written: Out of Zion will come the Deliverer; he will turn ungodliness away from Jacob. And this is my covenant with them, when I take away their sins. As regards the gospel, they are enemies for your sake; but as regards God's choice, they are beloved for the fathers' sake. For the gifts and the calling of God are not subject to a change of his mind."},{"c1":11,"v1":30,"c2":11,"v2":32,"label":"Romans 11:30–32","text":"\"Concluded\" (in older English) translates *sugkleiō* (synkleiō), \"to shut up on every side, to enclose completely\" — a picture of being hemmed in with no way out, not merely grouped together. The verb applies evenly to both Gentile and Jew: each shut up, in turn, under the same charge of unbelief. \"Not believed\" and \"unbelief\" both render *apeitheō* (apeitheō), \"to refuse to be persuaded\" — the same stubborn, willful refusal named back in chapter 10. Paul's structure is deliberately reciprocal: Gentile disobedience once opened the door to mercy through Jewish unbelief; Jewish disobedience now will, in the end, open the door to mercy through the mercy already shown the Gentiles. Nobody gets to claim a merit-based exemption — everybody is shut up under the same disobedience, so that everybody's rescue can only ever be traced to mercy.","translation":"For just as you were once disobedient to God, but now have been shown mercy through their disobedience, so also they have now been disobedient, in order that, through the mercy shown to you, they also may now be shown mercy. For God has shut up all people together under disobedience, so that he might show mercy to all."},{"c1":11,"v1":33,"c2":11,"v2":36,"label":"Romans 11:33–36","text":"*Anexichniastos* (anexichniastos), translated \"unsearchable\" and \"past finding out,\" pictures a trail with no scent or footprint left to follow — the same word could describe a bloodhound that loses the trace, or a guide who can't pick out a path through unmarked woods. Paired with *hodos* (hodos), the ordinary word for a road, the phrase says God's ways are a road that can't be traced by walking it backward: human reasoning can follow the argument this far and then the trail runs cold. Paul doesn't answer his own questions — who has known the Lord's mind, who has counseled him, who has ever put him in debt — he lets them stand as rhetorical dead ends. The doxology that follows isn't speculation filling the gap; it's worship: everything traces back to God as its source, moves forward through God as its means, and arrives at God as its goal.","translation":"O the depth of the riches and wisdom and knowledge of God! How unsearchable are his judgments, and how untraceable his ways! For who has known the mind of the Lord, or who became his counselor? Or who has first given to him, that it should be repaid to him? Because from him, and through him, and to him, are all things. To him be the glory forever. Amen."},{"c1":12,"v1":2,"c2":12,"v2":2,"label":"Romans 12:2","text":"\"Conformed\" translates *sunschēmatizō* (sunschēmatizō), built on *schēma* (schēma), an outward shape put on from outside rather than grown from within, with the prefix *sun*- specifying the pattern being copied: this present age, *aiōn* (aiōn) — not the created world but the shifting climate of thought and fashion current in it at any given moment. The verb is a present imperative with the negative *mē* (mē), a construction that in Greek doesn't forbid starting something but orders the stopping of something already underway: quit letting the age keep pressing you into its mold. Set against it is *metamorphoomai* (metamorphoomai), \"transformed\" — the very word used of Christ on the mountain, where the glory already His by nature broke through to the surface. Applied to the believer, it isn't a new costume but a different source: an outward expression rising out of a genuinely changed inner life, sustained through *anakainōsis* (anakainōsis), the ongoing renewal of the mind, not a single decision but a continuing overhaul. The purpose clause leans on *dokimazō* (dokimazō), the verb for testing metal to certify it genuine — as the mind is remade, a believer is able to put God's will to the test and find it approved: good, pleasing, and *teleios* (teleios), lacking nothing needed to be whole.","translation":"And stop being conformed to this age, but keep on being transformed by the renewing of your mind, so that you keep testing and approving what the will of God is — the good and pleasing and complete will."},{"c1":12,"v1":3,"c2":12,"v2":3,"label":"Romans 12:3","text":"Paul stacks three verbs on the same root, *phroneō* (phroneō), \"to form a judgment, to think.\" No one is to *huperphronein* (huperphronein) — to over-think himself, the same *huper*- prefix behind our word \"hyper,\" pushing self-estimate past the mark — but rather to *sōphronein* (sōphronein), literally to think within sound limits, from *sōs* (\"sound\") and *phrēn* (\"mind\"): a calm, accurately sized self-assessment, which has nothing to do with false modesty and everything to do with getting the number right. The standard for that estimate is introduced by *kata* (kata), \"down along, in keeping with\" — each believer's gauge is fixed by the measure of faith God apportioned him, not by comparison with somebody else's. Paul immediately backs the point with the body: *praxis* (praxis), a member's function or mode of operating, differs from part to part, yet every part belongs to one body. Sober self-estimate, in other words, isn't self-erasure — it's seeing your one function clearly inside a body that needs all the others too.","translation":"For I am saying, through the grace given to me, to everyone among you: do not be thinking more highly of yourself than you ought to be thinking, but be thinking so as to be sound-minded, each one according to the measure of faith God apportioned to him."},{"c1":12,"v1":6,"c2":12,"v2":8,"label":"Romans 12:6–8","text":"*Charisma* (charisma) names a grace-gift — an enablement supplied by the Spirit, not a talent a person develops on his own. Prophecy is to run *kata tēn analogian tēs pisteōs*, \"according to the proportion of faith\" — *analogia* (analogia) was a mathematical term for a fitted ratio, so the gift is to be used in step with the faith given for it, not stretched past it. \"Ministry\" is *diakonia* (diakonia), active service, and every gift-word in this list sits in the Greek locative of sphere: stay inside the lane your gift defines instead of drifting into someone else's. Giving is to be done with *haplotēs* (haplotēs), \"singleness\" — no double motive hiding behind the gift, no strings attached. Leading belongs to *ho proistēmi* (ho proïstēmi), literally \"the one stationed out in front,\" and it's to be carried out with *spoudē* (spoudē), the zeal of someone who hurries rather than drags his feet. And showing mercy takes *hilarotēs* (hilarotēs) — our word \"hilarity\" comes straight from it — mercy shown with warmth, not the grudging duty of a chore checked off.","translation":"Having gifts differing according to the grace given to us: whether prophecy, let it be in proportion to the faith; or service, let it be within the service; or the one teaching, within the teaching; or the one exhorting, within the exhortation; the one sharing, with singleness of purpose; the one leading, with eager diligence; the one showing mercy, with cheerful readiness."},{"c1":12,"v1":9,"c2":12,"v2":13,"label":"Romans 12:9–13","text":"*Anupokritos* (anupokritos), \"without hypocrisy,\" comes from the verb for an actor taking a part on stage — the love commanded here has no script and no performance in it. The hatred owed to evil is *apostugeō* (apostugeō), built on a root for hatred that shows itself openly rather than one nursed quietly out of sight, with *apo*- added to press the sense of pulling away, putting real distance between yourself and it. What you hold onto instead takes *kollaō* (kollaō), the verb for gluing something fast — stick to the good the way glue bonds two surfaces. Family warmth inside the church runs on *philostorgos* (philostorgos), built on the verb for the instinctive love of kin: Paul asks believers to feel toward one another the pull that blood relatives feel. Zeal is to *zeō* (zeō), \"boil,\" and the definite article marking \"spirit\" here points to the Holy Spirit rather than mere human enthusiasm — the fuel matters as much as the heat. Endurance is *hupomenō* (hupomenō), literally \"remain under\" — staying put beneath the pressure instead of looking for the exit — and prayer takes *proskartereō* (proskartereō), the word elsewhere used of a soldier's constant post and the apostles' unbroken devotion to prayer. Hospitality gets the strongest verb of the set, *diōkō* (diōkō), \"pursue, chase after\" — not waiting for a need to knock but going out looking for the chance to meet one.","translation":"Love — without acting a part. Be constantly abhorring what is evil, sticking fast to what is good. In brotherly love, be devoted to one another with family affection; in showing honor, be outdoing one another. In zeal, not lagging; boiling in the Spirit; serving the Lord as opportunity comes. Rejoicing in hope; enduring under affliction; persevering unremittingly in prayer; sharing as your own in the needs of the saints; pursuing hospitality."},{"c1":12,"v1":14,"c2":12,"v2":16,"label":"Romans 12:14–16","text":"\"Bless\" is *eulogeō* (eulogeō), literally \"to speak well\" (*eu* + *legō*) — our word \"eulogy\" is this Greek word carried over whole. It's a present imperative ordering a settled habit: keep speaking good over the very people persecuting you. \"Curse,\" *kataraomai* (kataraomai), gets the opposite construction — a present imperative with the negative, which in Greek stops something already in motion rather than warning against its start. Paul isn't guarding against a future temptation; he's telling them to quit a habit some already had. \"Condescend\" translates *sunapagō* (sunapagō), a passive verb meaning to be carried along together with something — not stooping down to lowly people from a position of height, but letting yourself be swept into their company by the same current they're in. And \"in your own conceits\" is literally \"with yourselves\" — wisdom measured against no standard but your own opinion of yourself, a private court where you sit as both defendant and judge.","translation":"Be constantly blessing those who are persecuting you; be blessing and stop cursing. Be rejoicing with those who rejoice, be weeping with those who weep. Be of the same mind toward one another; not setting your mind on the high things, but being carried along together with the lowly. Stop being wise in your own estimation."},{"c1":12,"v1":19,"c2":12,"v2":21,"label":"Romans 12:19–21","text":"\"Avenge\" is *ekdikeō* (ekdikeō), \"to assert one's own right,\" and once again the construction is a present imperative with the negative — an order to end something already underway: stop taking your own justice into your own hands. In its place, \"give place to wrath\" — the definite article marks a specific wrath, God's — meaning leave the space empty rather than crowding in to do His job for Him. The repayment verb, *antapodidōmi* (antapodidōmi), stacks two prepositions onto \"give\" to intensify it into \"pay back in full,\" and that full reckoning belongs to God alone. The coals-on-the-head image draws on an everyday hardship: a hearth fire gone out meant walking to a neighbor and carrying home borrowed embers in a container balanced on the head. Handing an enemy a generous supply of coals wasn't cruelty — it met a real, humbling need and could turn hostility into gratitude. The chapter closes with a matched pair of present imperatives, \"stop being conquered\" by evil and \"keep on conquering\" evil with good, putting the believer on the offensive rather than the defensive.","translation":"Stop avenging yourselves, beloved, but leave room for the wrath; for it stands written, Vengeance belongs to me, I will repay in full, says the Lord. But if your enemy hungers, be feeding him; if he thirsts, be giving him drink; for in doing this you will be heaping coals of fire upon his head. Stop being overcome by evil, but keep on overcoming evil with good."},{"c1":13,"v1":2,"c2":13,"v2":2,"label":"Romans 13:2","text":"The one who \"resists\" is described with a middle-voice participle of *antitassō* (ἀντιτάσσω) — the picture isn't of resistance done to him but resistance he stages himself: he arranges himself in battle order against the authority, like troops drawn up for a fight he started. What he has thereby done to God's ordinance is put in a different verb, *anthistēmi* (ἀνθίστημι), and it's perfect tense — not \"he opposed\" as a completed past act but \"he stands opposed,\" a settled, ongoing condition he has put himself into and continues to occupy. \"Judgment\" translates *krima* (κρίμα), the plain word for a judicial sentence handed down by a magistrate — Paul pictures an actual verdict, not a vague cosmic comeuppance.","translation":"So that the one who is arraying himself in battle against the authority has set himself in permanent opposition to the ordinance of God; and those who are so opposing shall receive judgment for themselves."},{"c1":13,"v1":5,"c2":13,"v2":7,"label":"Romans 13:5–7","text":"Paul grounds submission on two supports at once: the external threat of wrath, and the internal witness of conscience — the believer obeys not merely because refusal is dangerous but because something in him agrees with the arrangement. \"Pay\" translates *teleō* (τελέω), \"to fulfill, complete\" — it casts tax payment as the completion of an obligation already owed, not a favor granted. The verb form is grammatically ambiguous between statement and command, and most take it as a plain statement of fact: submission is already settled for the Christian, and paying taxes simply follows from it. \"Tribute\" is *phoros* (φόρος), literally \"that which is brought in.\"\n\nThe word for \"minister\" changes between verse 4 and verse 7. There it was *diakonos* (διάκονος), a servant considered in what he does. Here it's *leitourgos* (λειτουργός), the word used elsewhere for the sacred service of priests in the temple — Paul quietly elevates the magistrate's office into the category of divinely appointed service. \"Render\" is *apodidōmi* (ἀποδίδωμι), the simple verb \"give\" with a prefix meaning \"off, away from\" — paying something out of a reserve one holds, discharging a debt rather than making a gift. \"Dues\" (*opheilē*, ὀφειλή) is the ordinary word for a debt, carrying real moral weight — what is owed, not merely requested.","translation":"On account of this there is a necessity to keep on subjecting oneself, not only because of the wrath but also because of the conscience — for because of this you keep on paying the taxes, for they are God's public servants, continually devoting themselves to this very thing. Pay off to all what is owed: to the one collecting tax, the tax; to the one collecting toll, the toll; to the one who is owed fear, fear; to the one who is owed honor, honor."},{"c1":13,"v1":8,"c2":13,"v2":8,"label":"Romans 13:8","text":"\"Owe no man anything\" is a present imperative with the negative *mē* (μή) — grammatically this doesn't forbid ever incurring a debt, it forbids the continuation of one already running. Paul isn't outlawing loans; he's telling people who owe money to settle up. The one debt he deliberately exempts is love — the kind the Spirit produces in a believer, self-giving toward the person loved. Unlike every other debt, this is one that grows the more it's paid: discharging it doesn't cancel it, it deepens the capacity for more.","translation":"Stop owing to even one person, even one thing — except to be continually loving one another. For the one who is loving another has fulfilled the law."},{"c1":14,"v1":4,"c2":14,"v2":4,"label":"Romans 14:4","text":"\"Who art thou\" opens with the pronoun thrown forward for emphasis — Paul stops the weaker believer short: you, of all people, judging someone else's household slave (*oiketēs*, οἰκέτης, a servant belonging to a household, not a hired hand)? The force of the word is ownership — this person answers to a master, and that master isn't you. Whether he \"stands or falls\" is settled by that relationship, not by a fellow servant's verdict. The best manuscripts read \"Lord\" rather than \"God\" here, keeping the focus specifically on the believer's relation to Christ as owner. The closing verb is passive and confident — he *shall be made* to stand — the guarantee rests on the master's power to keep him upright, not on the servant's own strength.","translation":"As for you — who are you, judging another's household slave? To his own master he stands or falls. And he shall be made to stand, for the Lord has the power to make him stand."},{"c1":14,"v1":5,"c2":14,"v2":5,"label":"Romans 14:5","text":"\"Alike\" in most English renderings of this verse has nothing behind it in the Greek — drop it and the sense sharpens: the man who esteems every day is subjecting each day, one by one, to his own moral scrutiny. \"Fully persuaded\" translates *plērophoreō* (πληροφορέω), a word built around the idea of being carried to full measure — not a shaky opinion but a complete, settled conviction. Paul's point in both directions is that the conviction has to be one's own, arrived at personally rather than borrowed from someone else.","translation":"The one, on the one hand, is judging one day above another day; the other is subjecting every day alike to his scrutiny. Let each one be fully assured in his own mind."},{"c1":14,"v1":6,"c2":14,"v2":6,"label":"Romans 14:6","text":"\"Regards\" translates *phroneō* (φρονέω), a word for the whole cast of mind, not a bare opinion — how someone thinks and feels about a matter together. \"To the Lord\" puts *kurios* (κύριος) in the dative as a dative of reference: the man's verdict on the day is rendered with the Lord as the standard he's measuring against. The same construction governs the eater and the abstainer — each one's practice is oriented toward Christ, and each backs it with thanksgiving, which is Paul's proof that both, done in faith, land on the same target.","translation":"The one who is regarding the day, with reference to the Lord he regards it. And the one who is eating, with reference to the Lord he is eating, for he gives thanks to God; and the one who is not eating, with reference to the Lord he is not eating, and he gives thanks to God."},{"c1":14,"v1":7,"c2":14,"v2":9,"label":"Romans 14:7–9","text":"\"To himself,\" \"to the Lord\" run through these verses as the same dative of reference each time — a life, and a death, is always lived with reference to someone, and Paul's claim is that for the believer that someone is never the self. The verb for Christ's \"reviving\" is simply *zaō* (ζάω), \"to live,\" but here it's aorist — a completed, punctiliar act rather than an ongoing state — and the word order points it at the resurrection specifically, not at his years walking the earth. That distinction matters for the purpose clause that follows: Christ died and was raised in order that (a genuine *hina*-purpose clause) he might exercise lordship — the verb is *kurieuō* (κυριεύω), \"to rule as *kurios*\" — over both the dead and the living. His death and resurrection weren't merely an event in his own biography; they secured a rule that now covers everyone, on either side of the grave.","translation":"For not one of us is living with reference to himself, and not one is dying with reference to himself. For if we live, with reference to the Lord we live; and if we die, with reference to the Lord we die. Whether then we live or we die, we belong to the Lord. For to this end Christ died and lived again, in order that he might rule as Lord over both dead ones and living ones."},{"c1":14,"v1":10,"c2":14,"v2":12,"label":"Romans 14:10–12","text":"\"Thou\" is emphatic again, echoing verse 4 — Paul circles the same pronoun back at both sides now, weak and strong alike, for judging and for despising each other. The best manuscripts read \"judgment seat of God,\" not \"of Christ,\" keeping the scene deliberately cosmic. \"Confess\" is *exomologeō* (ἐξομολογέω), \"to acknowledge from the heart\" — every knee bowing and every tongue confessing here isn't grudging admission, it's praise wrung out of everyone alike. \"Give account\" renders the standard bookkeeping idiom for closing a ledger — everyone settles their own account, personally, with no one else's column to answer for.","translation":"But you — why are you judging your brother? Or you again — why are you despising your brother? For we shall all stand before the judgment seat of God. For it stands written: As I live, says the Lord, to me every knee shall bow, and every tongue shall confess to God. So then each one of us shall give account concerning himself to God."},{"c1":14,"v1":13,"c2":14,"v2":13,"label":"Romans 14:13","text":"\"Let us not judge\" is present tense — Paul isn't forbidding a single verdict, he's telling the church to break a running habit of criticizing each other. He then pivots the same word to a better use: judge this instead. \"Stumbling block\" is *proskamma* (πρόσκαμμα), built on the verb \"to strike against\" — the image is a stone or obstacle in the road that catches someone's foot. \"Occasion to fall\" is a different word, *skandalon* (σκάνδαλον), originally the trigger-stick of a trap — not just something you trip on but something engineered to catch you. Paul moves from a passive hazard to an active snare within a single sentence.","translation":"Therefore let us no longer be judging one another; but judge this instead — not to be placing a stumbling block before your brother, or a trap in which he may be caught."},{"c1":14,"v1":14,"c2":14,"v2":14,"label":"Romans 14:14","text":"\"I know\" is *oida* (οἶδα), flat, absolute knowledge, no qualification attached. \"Am persuaded\" shifts to *peithō* (πείθω) in the perfect tense — not \"I became convinced\" but \"I have arrived at conviction and stand there still,\" a finished process with a permanent result. \"Unclean\" translates *koinos* (κοινός), \"common\" — the word doesn't mean dirty or immoral, it means undistinguished from ordinary use, the opposite of *hagios* (ἅγιος), \"set apart.\" Nothing is *koinos* in itself; the category is ceremonial, not moral. And Paul roots the conviction \"in the Lord\" — not a private opinion he reasoned his way to, but a settled certainty that belongs to him as a Christian, held in Christ.","translation":"I know — with absolute knowledge — and stand persuaded in the Lord Jesus, that nothing is common in itself; only to the one who reckons a thing to be common, to that one it is common."},{"c1":14,"v1":15,"c2":14,"v2":15,"label":"Romans 14:15","text":"\"Meat\" is the generic word *brōma* (βρῶμα), \"food\" — not flesh specifically, whatever the strong believer is eating over his brother's objection. \"Made to grieve\" doesn't require that the weaker believer be talked into eating against his own conscience — it can simply mean the distress of watching someone he respects do something his own conscience can't approve. Either way, Paul treats causing that pain as already a breach of love. \"Destroy\" is *apollumi* (ἀπόλλυμι), \"ruin\" — a strong word, deliberately jarring, applied to a person for whom Christ died, over something as small as a meal.","translation":"For if because of food your brother is being grieved, you are no longer walking according to love. Stop ruining, by your food, the one on behalf of whom Christ died."},{"c1":14,"v1":16,"c2":14,"v2":18,"label":"Romans 14:16–18","text":"\"Evil spoken of\" is *blasphēmeō* (βλασφημέω) — reviled, given a bad name; Paul warns that careless use of freedom can get something genuinely good slandered. \"The kingdom of God\" here isn't \"eating and drinking\" (*brōsis kai posis*, βρῶσις καὶ πόσις) but righteousness, peace, and joy — and none of the three carry their usual theological weight in this context. Righteousness is practical uprightness toward other people, not justification; peace is concord between believers, not peace with God; joy is the shared gladness that grows out of a community not at war with itself — and all three are located \"in the Holy Spirit,\" his product, not human effort. \"Serve\" is *douleuō* (δουλεύω), slave-service — total, not occasional. \"Acceptable\" (*euarestos*, εὐάρεστος) is what pleases God; \"approved\" (*dokimos*, δόκιμος) is a metals term, the stamp put on something tested and passed.","translation":"Therefore stop letting your good be spoken of as evil. For the kingdom of God is not eating and drinking, but righteousness and peace and joy in the Holy Spirit. For the one who in this is serving Christ as a slave is well-pleasing to God, and approved — having been tested and stamped so — by men."},{"c1":14,"v1":19,"c2":14,"v2":19,"label":"Romans 14:19","text":"\"Follow\" is *diōkō* (διώκω), \"pursue\" — the word for a hunt or chase, not a stroll. Whether it's a command (\"let us pursue\") or a statement (\"we are pursuing\") turns on a single letter in the Greek manuscripts, an omega versus an omicron — a point where textual criticism runs out and the surrounding grammar has to decide it. Since the next verse switches to a flat imperative, the exhortatory reading fits the flow best. \"Edify\" is *oikodomeō* (οἰκοδομέω), literally \"build a house\" — construction language for growing someone in grace. \"One another\" carries a defining article in the Greek easy to lose in translation: Paul means building up specific individuals in the room, not a vague corporate mass.","translation":"So then, let us be eagerly pursuing the things of peace, and the things that build up — with reference to one another."},{"c1":14,"v1":20,"c2":14,"v2":20,"label":"Romans 14:20","text":"\"Destroy\" here isn't the word from verse 15 (*apollumi*, \"ruin\"); it's *kataluō* (καταλύω), \"tear down\" — the demolition word, set deliberately against *oikodomeō*, \"build up,\" from the previous verse. Paul pictures a building crew undoing its own work over a meal. \"Pure\" (*kathara*, καθαρά) keeps the same ceremonial sense running through the chapter — nothing here is a moral judgment on the food itself. \"With offense\" describes not what's eaten but how: eating in a way calculated to become a stumbling block to someone watching.","translation":"Stop tearing down the work of God for the sake of food. All things indeed are pure — but it is evil for the man who is eating so as to cause offense."},{"c1":14,"v1":21,"c2":14,"v2":21,"label":"Romans 14:21","text":"\"Flesh\" here is *kreas* (κρέας), and in this setting it points specifically to meat with a compromised pedigree — the kind that had passed through pagan sacrificial use — not meat in general. Wine and this kind of meat were the two concrete points of the weaker believer's scruple, and Paul's counsel narrows to match: better to forgo either, or anything else that trips a brother, than to insist on the right to have it.","translation":"It is good not to eat flesh, nor to drink wine, nor to do anything by which your brother stumbles."},{"c1":15,"v1":4,"c2":15,"v2":4,"label":"Romans 15:4","text":"\"That\" translates *hina* (ἵνα), a genuine purpose clause — Scripture wasn't written by accident for later readers, it was aimed at them on purpose, \"for our learning.\" \"Comfort\" is *paraklēsis* (παράκλησις), a wide word built on \"calling alongside\" — it covers everything from exhortation to consolation to encouragement, whatever help a person in need requires, not just soothing. Paul pairs it with patience as the twin products Scripture is meant to produce, both feeding into hope.","translation":"For whatever things were written beforehand were written for our instruction, in order that through the patience and through the encouragement that comes from the Scriptures we might keep on having hope."},{"c1":15,"v1":5,"c2":15,"v2":7,"label":"Romans 15:5–7","text":"\"That\" (*hina*) again marks purpose: the unity Paul prays for isn't the goal itself, it's aimed at something further — glorifying God with one voice. The little word sometimes rendered \"even\" is just *kai* (καί), ordinary \"and,\" and the better reading treats it plainly: \"the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ,\" naming God specifically as the Father of Jesus, the same construction Paul uses elsewhere. \"Receive\" is *proslambanō* (προσλαμβάνω) in the middle voice — \"take to oneself,\" the language of drawing someone into one's own circle, granting them access to one's heart. The standard Paul sets is Christ's own reception of each believer — no lower bar is available for how they're to receive one another.","translation":"Now may the God of the patience and the encouragement give to you to be of the same mind with one another according to Christ Jesus, in order that with one accord, with one mouth, you may keep on glorifying the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ. Therefore keep on taking one another to yourselves, just as Christ also took us to himself, for the glory of God."},{"c1":15,"v1":10,"c2":15,"v2":12,"label":"Romans 15:10–12","text":"\"Root\" translates *riza* (rhiza) — not the trunk or branch but the sprout that pushes up out of the root system, the lowest and least visible part of the plant. The picture in Isaiah's quotation trades on that contrast: what comes up out of the hidden root of Jesse's line rises to become the standard the nations rally to. And the verb Paul chooses for the Gentiles' response is not the ordinary New Testament word for believing, *pisteuō* (pisteuo), but *elpizō* (elpizo), \"to hope\" — the Gentiles are pictured fixing their hope on this shoot from Jesse's root, setting up the theme Paul immediately develops in verse 13.","translation":"And again he says, Be glad, Gentiles, with his people. And again, Praise the Lord, all you Gentiles, and let all the peoples praise him. And again Isaiah says, There will be a sprout out of the root of Jesse, even the one who is rising up to rule the Gentiles — on him will the Gentiles set their hope."},{"c1":15,"v1":13,"c2":15,"v2":13,"label":"Romans 15:13","text":"\"In believing\" renders a present infinitive with the article, *en tōi pisteuein* (en to pisteuein) — literally \"in the believing,\" picturing faith not as one decisive act but as an ongoing, habitual exercise. The joy and peace Paul prays for rest on that continuous believing, not on a memory of it. \"Abound\" is *perisseuō* (perisseuo), built on the idea of exceeding a fixed measure — the related noun *perissos* (perissos) means \"more than necessary, superadded.\" Paul is not praying for enough hope to get by; he is praying for a surplus, hope running over the rim. And \"Ghost\" here is simply older English for *pneuma* (pneuma), \"spirit\" — no different sense from \"Spirit\" elsewhere.","translation":"Now may the God of hope keep filling you with all joy and peace in the continual believing, so that you keep overflowing with abundant hope by the power of the Holy Spirit."},{"c1":15,"v1":14,"c2":15,"v2":14,"label":"Romans 15:14","text":"Paul's \"I am persuaded\" is a perfect tense — not a passing impression but a settled conviction reached and still held: \"I have been thoroughly persuaded, and stand persuaded still.\" \"Ye yourselves\" is emphatic in the Greek, stressing that the Roman believers are full of goodness in their own right, not because Paul coached them into it. \"Filled\" is likewise perfect tense — completely filled, with that fullness continuing — and \"all knowledge\" means the whole range of Christian understanding, not omniscience.\n\nHis claim to have \"written\" boldly is an epistolary aorist, a first-century courtesy: Paul describes the letter he is presently composing as though it were already a finished, past event — the vantage point his readers will have once it reaches them. \"The more boldly\" is a true comparative, boldness justified precisely by their fullness of goodness, and \"in some sort\" limits that boldness to certain plain passages rather than the whole letter.\n\nFor \"minister\" Paul reaches past the ordinary word for a servant, *diakonos* (diakonos), and chooses *leitourgos* (leitourgos) — the term for a public officer, and in sacred contexts, a temple priest. The companion verb *leitourgeō* (leitourgeo) is the word used of priests and Levites at their sacred duties. Paul casts his gospel preaching as a priestly service, with the believing Gentiles themselves as the offering he presents, made acceptable through the Spirit's sanctifying work.","translation":"But I myself stand fully persuaded concerning you, my brothers, that you yourselves also are full of goodness, having been filled to completion with all knowledge, so that you are able to keep on admonishing one another. Yet I write to you the more boldly, in some measure, as one reminding you again, because of the grace given to me from God that I should be a minister of Christ Jesus to the Gentiles, serving as a priest in the good news of God, so that the offering up of the Gentiles may become acceptable, having been sanctified by the Holy Spirit."},{"c1":15,"v1":17,"c2":15,"v2":21,"label":"Romans 15:17–21","text":"Paul's \"glorying\" is \"in Christ Jesus\" — the boast belongs entirely to what Christ accomplished through him, not to his own résumé; he immediately shuts down any claim about \"anything which Christ did not work through me.\" The miracles he cites come under two different Greek words with different emphases: *sēmeion* (semeion), \"sign,\" stresses the evidential force of a miracle — the credential proving the message came from God — while *teras* (teras), \"wonder,\" stresses its startling, memorable character. Paul's ministry carried both kinds of force.\n\n\"Fully preached\" translates *plēroō* (pleroo) in the perfect tense with *euaggelion* (euangelion), \"good news\" — Paul does not mean he reached every individual from Jerusalem to Illyricum, but that he brought the gospel's proclamation to completion across that whole territory, leaving nothing undone in his assigned task. \"Strived\" is *philotimeomai* (philotimeomai), literally \"moved by love of honor\" — his refusal to preach where Christ was already named was a matter of honor, not rivalry: he simply would not build on another man's foundation.","translation":"I therefore keep my glorying in Christ Jesus, in the things pertaining to God. For I will not dare to speak of anything except what Christ has worked through me, resulting in the obedience of the Gentiles, by word and deed, by the power of attesting signs and astonishing wonders, by the power of the Holy Spirit — so that from Jerusalem and around as far as Illyricum I have brought the proclaiming of the good news of Christ to completion. And in this way I have made it a point of honor to keep announcing the good news, not where Christ has already been named, so that I would not be building on another man's foundation; but, just as it stands written, Those to whom no report concerning him was made, they shall see, and those who have not heard, they shall understand."},{"c1":15,"v1":22,"c2":15,"v2":24,"label":"Romans 15:22–24","text":"\"Hindered\" is imperfect tense — not a single roadblock but a repeated, ongoing series of hindrances, one thing after another keeping Paul from Rome. \"Place\" is *topos* (topos), meaning \"scope, opportunity for acting\" rather than a literal spot on the map — Paul means there is no more room left for his kind of pioneering work in the regions he has already covered. \"Filled\" is *empimplēmi* (empiplemi), \"to have one's fill, to be satiated,\" and the qualifier \"in part\" deliberately softens that word — Paul admits he would gladly stay far longer than the brief visit he is proposing; only a partial satisfaction is realistic before he presses on toward Spain.","translation":"For this reason I have been repeatedly hindered, by the many things, from coming to you. But now, no longer having scope for work in these regions, and having had a longing to come to you for many years now, whenever I journey to Spain, I hope, as I am passing through, to see you and to be sent on my way there by you — once I have first been in some part satisfied with your company."},{"c1":15,"v1":25,"c2":15,"v2":27,"label":"Romans 15:25–27","text":"\"It pleased\" is *eudokeō* (eudokeo), used of a formal, official resolution by the churches, yet carrying the flavor of something done gladly and freely rather than under compulsion — a formal vote that was also a warm impulse. \"The poor saints\" is better read as \"the poor of the saints\" — not every believer in Jerusalem was poor; this names a subset. \"Contribution\" is *koinōnia* (koinonia), the same root behind \"fellowship\" elsewhere and \"share\" in Hebrews 13:16 — the collection is not merely a gift handed across a gap but the Gentile churches entering into common cause with the Jerusalem believers, making the need their own. Paul frames the exchange as a debt in two currencies: the Gentiles received spiritual riches from the Jews and now owe back material ones.\n\n\"Performed\" (*epiteleō*, epiteleo) means to carry a task through to completion, and \"sealed\" (*sphragizō*, sphragizo) draws on the range of a seal's uses in the ancient world — authenticating, securing as property, finalizing. Paul pictures the delivered collection as something formally certified and closed out, the tangible \"fruit\" grown from the Gentiles' reception of the gospel.","translation":"But now I am journeying to Jerusalem, ministering to the saints. For Macedonia and Achaia were pleased to make a certain joint contribution for the poor of the saints who are in Jerusalem. They were pleased to do it, and they are debtors to them, since the Gentiles have come to share in their spiritual things, and are therefore obligated to serve them in material things. When, then, I have carried this through to completion and have sealed to them this fruit, I will go on through you into Spain — and I know that when I come to you, I will come in the fullness of the blessing of Christ."},{"c1":15,"v1":30,"c2":15,"v2":33,"label":"Romans 15:30–33","text":"The \"for\" in \"for our Lord Jesus Christ\" and \"for the love of the Spirit\" is *dia* (dia) with the genitive, marking agency — \"through,\" not \"for the sake of.\" Paul is not just citing these as reasons; he is naming the actual means by which he expects the Romans to be moved to pray. \"Strive together\" is *sunagonizomai* (sunagonizomai), an athletic term for teammates competing shoulder to shoulder in the games — Paul wants the Romans wrestling alongside him in prayer with the coordinated intensity a team brings to a contest. \"Believe not\" is *apeitheō* (apeitheo), built from *peithō* (peitho), \"to persuade,\" with a negating prefix — literally \"those resistant to persuasion,\" people who have set themselves against being won over. That is the danger Paul is bracing for in Judea.","translation":"Now I beg you, brothers, through our Lord Jesus Christ and through the love that the Spirit produces, to keep contending together with me, as teammates, in your prayers to God on my behalf, that I may be rescued from those resistant to persuasion in Judea, and that my service for Jerusalem may prove acceptable to the saints, so that I may come to you in joy by the will of God and find rest together with you. Now may the God of peace be with you all. Amen."},{"c1":16,"v1":5,"c2":16,"v2":5,"label":"Romans 16:5","text":"\"The church in their house\" reflects the reality of first-century Christian gathering — there were no dedicated church buildings for centuries, so congregations met wherever a well-off believer could open a home. Calling Epaenetus the \"firstfruits of Asia\" locates him among the very first converts in the Roman province of Asia (the western part of what is now Turkey) — a title of honor marking him as an early harvest, the first sign of a larger ingathering still to come.","translation":"Also greet the assembly that meets in their house. Greet Epaenetus, my beloved, who is the firstfruits of Asia to Christ."},{"c1":16,"v1":6,"c2":16,"v2":9,"label":"Romans 16:6–9","text":"The description of Mary's labor uses a verb for wearisome effort pushed to the point of exhaustion — not polite praise but a specific claim about strenuous, costly work on the Romans' behalf. \"Kinsmen\" (*suggenēs*, syngenes) most likely marks Andronicus and Junia as Paul's fellow Jews rather than literal blood relatives — it would be striking if this many people in a Roman congregation were all his actual family. \"Fellowprisoners\" (*sunaichmalōtos*, synaichmalotos) is a military term for a fellow captive taken in war, suggesting they shared one of Paul's imprisonments outright. And \"were in Christ before me\" uses the perfect tense of \"to become\" — not simply \"they became believers earlier\" but \"they came to be in Christ, and remain so\" — the same tense Paul reaches for elsewhere to underline the permanence of a believer's standing.","translation":"Greet Mary, who has labored, and keeps having labored, to the point of exhaustion for you. Greet Andronicus and Junia, my fellow-countrymen and my fellow-prisoners, who are men of note among the apostles, who also have come to be in Christ, and remain so, before me. Greet Ampliatus, my beloved in the Lord. Greet Urbanus, our fellow-worker in Christ, and Stachys, my beloved."},{"c1":16,"v1":10,"c2":16,"v2":16,"label":"Romans 16:10–16","text":"Apelles is called \"approved in Christ\" with a verb that pictures him as already tested and found genuine — not a hope but a verdict already rendered. The greetings to \"those of the household of Aristobulus\" and \"of Narcissus\" point to slaves: household servants commonly kept a former master's name even after being sold or inherited by someone else, so these are greetings to believers within slave households, a quiet witness to how far the gospel had already reached down the social ladder. The \"holy kiss\" takes an ordinary Near Eastern greeting custom and marks it off with a single word — *holy* — as something now belonging distinctly to the family of God rather than mere social courtesy.","translation":"Greet Apelles, the one who has been tested and found genuine in Christ. Greet those who belong to the household of Aristobulus. Greet Herodion, my fellow-countryman. Greet those who belong to the household of Narcissus, who are in the Lord. Greet Tryphaena and Tryphosa, women who keep laboring to the point of exhaustion in the Lord. Greet Persis the beloved, who labored hard in the Lord. Greet Rufus, chosen in the Lord, and his mother, who is also mine. Greet Asyncritus, Phlegon, Hermes, Patrobas, Hermas, and the brothers with them. Greet Philologus, and Julia, Nereus and his sister, and Olympas, and all the saints with them. Greet one another with a holy kiss. All the churches of Christ greet you."},{"c1":16,"v1":17,"c2":16,"v2":20,"label":"Romans 16:17–20","text":"\"Mark\" is *skopeō* (skopeo), a continuous \"keep your eye fixed on\" rather than a single glance — Paul wants ongoing vigilance, not a one-time warning heeded and forgotten. \"Avoid\" is then *ekklinō* (ekklino), built on the idea of leaning away — deliberately angling yourself out of someone's path. The false teachers, Paul says, serve \"their own belly\" (*koilia*, koilia, the whole stomach cavity) — a blunt image for self-interest generally, set against genuine service to Christ. Their method is *chrēstologia* (chrestologia), smooth talk imitating goodness, and *eulogia* (eulogia, the root of our word \"eulogy\"), polished language dressed up to sound fine.\n\nTwo different Greek words lie behind \"simple\" here. In verse 18 it is *akakos* (akakos), literally \"not evil\" — a neutral, undefended innocence, naivety rather than virtue, precisely the quality that leaves someone vulnerable. In verse 19 it is *akeraios* (akeraios), \"unmixed, without foreign admixture\" — a positive purity, moral integrity untouched by corrupting influence, which is what Paul actually wants for the Romans: wisdom paired with an unmixed resistance to evil rather than mere naivety. \"Bruise\" (*suntribō*, syntribo) is a violent verb, \"crush, trample underfoot\" — an echo of the ancient promise that the woman's seed would crush the serpent's head.","translation":"Now I beg you, brothers, keep a constant watch on those who are producing the divisions and the snares that run contrary to the teaching you learned, and keep leaning away from them. For such people are not serving as slaves to our Lord Christ but to their own bellies, and by their smooth talk that imitates goodness, and their polished fine language, they keep deceiving the hearts of the unguarded. Your obedience has reached the ears of everyone; over you, then, I keep rejoicing. But I want you to be wise regarding what is good, and pure, unmixed with evil, regarding what is evil. And the God of peace will soon crush Satan under your feet. The grace of our Lord Jesus be with you."},{"c1":16,"v1":21,"c2":16,"v2":24,"label":"Romans 16:21–24","text":"Timothy's name is built from *timaō* (timao), \"to honor,\" and *theos* (theos), \"God\" — \"one who honors God,\" a name given in hope a child would grow to embody it. Tertius, who pens his own greeting rather than relaying Paul's words, was the secretary taking dictation for the letter — a practical necessity, since Paul's eyesight had been damaged by illness. \"Wrote\" here, like Paul's own \"have written\" earlier in the letter, is an epistolary aorist: Tertius describes his own act of writing from the standpoint of the Roman readers, who will encounter it as something already done, even though for Tertius it is happening in the moment. \"Chamberlain\" translates *oikonomos* (oikonomos), \"manager, steward\" — a term used broadly for someone overseeing property or public affairs, here marking Erastus's civic post managing the city's business.","translation":"Timothy my fellow-worker greets you, and so do Lucius and Jason and Sosipater, my fellow-countrymen. I, Tertius, who am writing this letter, greet you in the Lord. Gaius, host to me and to the whole assembly, greets you. Erastus, the city's steward, greets you, and Quartus the brother."},{"c1":16,"v1":25,"c2":16,"v2":27,"label":"Romans 16:25–27","text":"\"Stablish\" is *stērizō* (sterizo), \"to make firm, set fast\" — the same wish Paul voiced at the letter's opening (1:11), where he longed to impart some gift that would establish the Romans; now, at the close, he hands that task entirely to God, since only God can actually secure it. Paul calls it \"my gospel\" not because he invented it but because it carries his own stamp — the message as he received it directly and characteristically emphasized it: salvation entirely by faith, free and offered without distinction.\n\n\"Mystery\" is *mustērion* (mysterion), something once hidden and unknowable that has now been disclosed and can be understood — not a puzzle still unsolved but a secret now opened. It was \"kept in silence\" (a passive verb, *sigaō*, sigao) through the ages before creation and has now been \"made manifest\" (*phaneroō*, phaneroo, \"to make visible\") through the prophetic writings, by God's own command. \"Everlasting\" is better read as \"eternal\" — *aiōnios* (aionios) means without beginning as well as without end, a stronger claim than \"everlasting,\" which could technically have a starting point.","translation":"Now to him who is able to establish you according to my gospel, even the proclamation of Jesus Christ, according to the uncovering of the mystery that has been kept in silence through times eternal, but now has been made visible, and through prophetic writings, according to the command of the eternal God, has been made known for the obedience of faith among all nations — to the only wise God, through Jesus Christ, be the glory forever. Amen."}],"Galatians":[{"c1":1,"v1":1,"c2":1,"v2":1,"label":"Galatians 1:1","text":"Paul opens with apostolos — from apo (\"from\") and stello (\"send\") — the word for an envoy sent out with credentials, not a volunteer carrying a message. Everything else in the verse is Paul defending those credentials. He is exact with his prepositions: apo marks an ultimate source (\"not from men\"), while dia marks an intermediate channel or agent (\"neither by man\"). So he denies two separate things — that mankind is the fountainhead of his commission, and that any human hand was the pipeline through which it reached him. The word shifts from the plural \"men\" (anthropos, the human race in general) to the singular \"man\" because in Paul's world a body's authority was typically exercised through one representative — the Senate through the emperor, the Sanhedrin through the high priest — so ruling out even a single human intermediary is a sweeping claim. Then dia returns, now positively: \"through Jesus Christ and God the Father,\" both names governed by one preposition, meaning they are not agent-then-source in sequence but a single, joint origin. Tacking on \"who raised him from the dead\" quietly sets Paul's commission apart from the Twelve's: theirs came from Jesus in his humbled, pre-resurrection state; his came from the risen, glorified Christ.","translation":"Paul, a commissioned envoy — not from men as a source, and not even through a man as a channel, but through Jesus Christ and God the Father, who raised him up out from among the dead."},{"c1":1,"v1":2,"c2":1,"v2":2,"label":"Galatians 1:2","text":"Naming \"all the brothers with me\" alongside himself is not sentimental filler. In ancient letters, names added to the opening address functioned as co-signers lending authority to what followed, unlike names tacked onto a closing greeting, which were simply expressions of affection. Paul wants the Galatians to know this isn't one man's private opinion. Then comes the address itself, strikingly bare — no commendation, not even the word \"saints,\" unlike every other Pauline opening, even to the badly-behaved Corinthians. The silence is itself a rebuke. \"Church\" translates ekklesia, built on a verb meaning \"to call out\": in secular Greek it named a citizen assembly summoned for public business, and Paul repurposes it for a called-out gathering of believers.","translation":"And all the brothers with me, to the assemblies of Galatia."},{"c1":1,"v1":3,"c2":1,"v2":3,"label":"Galatians 1:3","text":"The formula \"grace to you and peace\" is Paul's standard opening, but it lands with special weight here, aimed at Galatians being talked out of grace by legalists. Both \"God the Father\" and \"the Lord Jesus Christ\" sit under one governing preposition, apo, marking them as a single joint source — grace and peace do not come partly from one and partly from the other, but from both acting as one. The grace in view is the Spirit's ongoing enabling work in a believer's life, not merely the initial gift of salvation, and the peace is the settled inward peace that flows from that same ministry — exactly what the Galatians were trading away for self-effort under law.","translation":"Grace to you, and peace, from God the Father and the Lord Jesus Christ."},{"c1":1,"v1":4,"c2":1,"v2":4,"label":"Galatians 1:4","text":"\"Gave himself for our sins\" uses hyper, the preposition of substitution — the ordinary word for one person acting in another's place. It's the same word a professional scribe used when signing a document for an illiterate client: \"I wrote in his place.\" Paul reaches for the strongest available term to say Christ stood in for us. \"Deliver\" is exaireo, to pluck out, to rescue by force — not a gentle removal but an extraction. And the \"evil\" of \"this present evil world\" is poneros, not kakos: kakos describes something rotten in itself, content to decay quietly, while poneros describes an active, contagious evil that isn't satisfied until it drags others down too. The present age isn't merely bad — it's aggressively, corrosively bad, and the legalism of the Judaizers belongs to that same corrupting system.","translation":"Who gave himself in place of our sins, so that he might pluck us out from this present, actively corrupting age, according to the will of our God and Father."},{"c1":1,"v1":5,"c2":1,"v2":5,"label":"Galatians 1:5","text":"The doxology is brief, but \"for ever and ever\" repays a glance — literally \"unto the ages of the ages,\" stacking the Greek word for a bounded era of time (aion) on top of itself to push duration past any measurable limit. It is the language of eternity built out of the language of epochs.","translation":"To whom be the glory unto the ages of the ages. Amen."},{"c1":1,"v1":6,"c2":1,"v2":7,"label":"Galatians 1:6–7","text":"\"I marvel\" is thaumazo — real astonishment, the reaction of someone who expected better. \"Removed\" translates metatithemi, elsewhere used for a turncoat switching sides, but Paul deliberately keeps it in the present tense rather than the perfect — a calculated hope, since the defection is still in motion, not yet a settled, finished fact. \"So soon\" (tacheos) underlines the speed of it. Then comes a play on two Greek words for \"another.\" Heteros means another of a different, often inferior kind (our word \"heterodox\" comes from it); allos means simply one more of the same kind. Paul says the Galatians are turning to a heteros gospel — different in kind, and evil in kind — which sets up the paradox that follows: it isn't even an allos gospel, \"not another\" at all, because a message of salvation by works cannot logically be called good news and still count as gospel. \"Trouble\" (tarasso, present tense) describes ongoing mental agitation — these agitators are still actively at work as Paul writes — and \"pervert\" (metastrepho) doesn't mean bend or distort; it means reverse into the opposite.","translation":"I am marveling that so quickly you are shifting away from him who called you in the sphere of Christ's grace, to a different-in-kind gospel — not that there is another gospel of the same kind, only there are some who are continually troubling you and wanting to reverse the gospel of Christ into its opposite."},{"c1":1,"v1":8,"c2":1,"v2":8,"label":"Galatians 1:8","text":"\"But\" is alla, the sharper of Greek's two adversatives — Paul is not softening anything. He includes \"we\" (himself and his co-workers) to show this is not one teacher's pet doctrine against another's, but truth against error itself. \"Preach a gospel\" translates the single verb euangelizomai, \"to announce good news\"; Paul had a plain word for \"proclaim\" available (kerysso) and passed it over for the word that specifically means to evangelize. \"Than\" renders par' ho, literally \"beside which\" — not simply \"against\" but \"overstepping the boundary of,\" moving into new, foreign territory. \"Accursed\" is anathema, a word used in the Greek Old Testament for something devoted to destruction because it is hateful to God — not church discipline (angels cannot be excommunicated) but the language of ultimate spiritual ruin.","translation":"But even if we, or an angel out of heaven, should announce good news to you beyond the boundary of what we announced to you, let him be accursed."},{"c1":1,"v1":9,"c2":1,"v2":9,"label":"Galatians 1:9","text":"\"As we said before\" uses a verb meaning \"to say beforehand,\" pointing to an earlier warning already given, not a restatement of the sentence just written. And where the surrounding verses lean on the perfect tense — completed actions with abiding results — verse 9 shifts its conditional: not the hypothetical ean but ei, the \"if\" of a condition assumed to be actually true. Paul isn't entertaining a possibility; he's stating a fact. \"Received\" is paralambano, elsewhere used of a host warmly welcoming a guest — the Galatians had once taken the true gospel to their hearts eagerly, which makes their present drift worse.","translation":"As we have said before, now again I am saying: if, as is the case, anyone announces good news to you beyond what you took to yourselves so eagerly, let him be accursed."},{"c1":1,"v1":10,"c2":1,"v2":10,"label":"Galatians 1:10","text":"\"Persuade\" is peitho, more precisely \"to win over, to conciliate\" -- Paul is answering a whispered charge that he shapes his message to flatter people. \"Or\" translates a word meaning \"rather than,\" which shows exactly what the slander claimed: that he cared more about pleasing men than pleasing God. Paul then pivots toward the origin of his gospel with \"certify\" (gnorizo), which doesn't mean he's telling the Galatians something new -- it means driving home, emphatically, something they already knew. He calls them \"brothers,\" from adelphos, literally \"of the same womb,\" kin through a shared new birth. \"Is not after man\" uses kata, a preposition whose root idea is \"down,\" carrying the sense of \"measured by\" or \"controlled by\" -- Paul's gospel isn't just non-human in origin, it isn't human in standard or character either.","translation":"For am I now trying to win over men, or God? Or am I seeking to please men? If I were still pleasing men, I would not be a slave of Christ. For I make known to you, brothers, that the gospel announced by me is not, as to its very nature, a human thing."},{"c1":1,"v1":12,"c2":1,"v2":12,"label":"Galatians 1:12","text":"Greek verbs already carry their subject built in, so when a writer adds the separate pronoun \"I\" (ego), it signals deliberate emphasis. Paul underlines his own case against the Twelve's: he \"received\" (paralambano, taking something passed along directly from another person) nothing, and was \"taught\" (didasko, learning by instruction) nothing. Instead he got a \"revelation,\" apokalypsis, the uncovering of something previously hidden, an unveiling accompanied by real understanding, not just a flash of insight. And the phrase \"of Jesus Christ\" is a subjective genitive: Christ is the one doing the revealing, not merely the content revealed.","translation":"For neither did I myself receive it from a man, nor was I taught it, but I got it through an uncovering by Jesus Christ."},{"c1":1,"v1":13,"c2":1,"v2":13,"label":"Galatians 1:13","text":"\"Conversation\" is old English for \"way of life,\" translating anastrophe. \"The Jews' religion\" renders one word, Ioudaismos — likely coined with a note of outsider contempt, much the way \"Christianoi\" was first used of believers before both groups adopted their labels as badges of honor. The verbs \"persecuted\" and \"wasted\" are imperfect tense — not a single episode but sustained, continuous action stretching right up to his conversion. \"Wasted\" (portheo) is a violent word, elsewhere used of the sacking of cities, not mere harassment.","translation":"For you heard of my former way of life in Judaism, that I was, beyond measure, continually persecuting the church of God and continually devastating it."},{"c1":1,"v1":14,"c2":1,"v2":14,"label":"Galatians 1:14","text":"\"Profited\" translates prokopto, literally \"to cut a path forward\" — the image of a pioneer hacking through undergrowth ahead of everyone else, not simply doing well in class. Paul means he was out ahead of his peers, blazing trail in Jewish scholarship and zeal. \"Tradition\" is paradosis, \"something handed down,\" and Paul is careful to root his old zeal in ancestral tradition rather than in the Mosaic law itself, since the Pharisaic traditions had grown up alongside the law and, in places, buried it.","translation":"And I was continually cutting ahead, beyond many of my own age among my people, being more abundantly zealous for the traditions handed down from my fathers."},{"c1":1,"v1":15,"c2":1,"v2":16,"label":"Galatians 1:15–16","text":"\"Separated\" is aphorizo, \"to mark off within a boundary\" — not physical removal from the womb, but being set apart for a purpose before birth, the same idea used elsewhere of Samson and John the Baptist. \"Reveal his Son in me\" uses apokalypto again — an inward, subjective unveiling within Paul, not an outward disclosure of Christ to the world through Paul, which would require the preposition \"through\" rather than \"in.\" This distinguishes the revelation from the earlier, external, ocular vision on the Damascus road; this was an inward apprehension, likely during the silent stretch that followed. \"Conferred\" is prosanatithemi, a word used elsewhere of consulting oracles or soothsayers — Paul says, pointedly, that he did not go looking for anyone else's opinion on what had just happened to him.","translation":"But when it was the good pleasure of him who set me apart from my mother's womb and called me through his grace, to unveil his Son within me, so that I might announce him as good news among the Gentiles, immediately I did not confer with flesh and blood."},{"c1":1,"v1":17,"c2":1,"v2":17,"label":"Galatians 1:17","text":"\"Went up\" is anerchomai, the standard word for approaching Jerusalem, a city on high ground both geographically and religiously, so that journeys toward it were always described as \"going up\" and departures as \"going down.\" \"Before me\" places the temporal marker pro (\"before,\" in time) next to \"apostles,\" quietly granting that the Twelve held the same office Paul claims, just earlier. \"Arabia\" transliterates a word meaning roughly \"an arid, sparsely peopled place\" — Paul went looking for solitude, not an audience.","translation":"Neither did I go up to Jerusalem, to those who were apostles before me; but I went away into Arabia, and returned again to Damascus."},{"c1":1,"v1":18,"c2":1,"v2":18,"label":"Galatians 1:18","text":"\"See\" translates historeo, \"to inquire into, to visit for the purpose of getting acquainted\" — a deliberate, purposeful visit, not a chance encounter. \"Peter\" transliterates the Greek petros, \"rock,\" itself a rendering of the Aramaic Kephas that Paul actually writes — a rock by either name. Fifteen days is mentioned to make the brevity itself the point: nowhere near long enough to have learned the gospel from him.","translation":"Then, after three years, I went up to Jerusalem to get acquainted with Kephas, and I stayed with him fifteen days."},{"c1":1,"v1":19,"c2":1,"v2":19,"label":"Galatians 1:19","text":"The Greek construction counts James among the apostles Paul saw, though he was not one of the Twelve — a natural-born brother of Jesus, son of Joseph and Mary, recognized as an apostle without having been among the original appointees, likely after witnessing the risen Christ himself.","translation":"But I saw none of the other apostles, except James, the brother of the Lord."},{"c1":1,"v1":20,"c2":1,"v2":20,"label":"Galatians 1:20","text":"The oath — \"before God, I do not lie\" — answers a specific slander: the Judaizers had apparently spread word that Paul spent extensive time under the Twelve's tutelage in Jerusalem. The strength of the sworn denial is proportional to how damaging that lie was to his whole argument.","translation":"Now the things I am writing to you — behold, before God, I am not lying."},{"c1":1,"v1":21,"c2":1,"v2":21,"label":"Galatians 1:21","text":"\"Regions\" is klimata, literally the sloping tracts of land running down from the mountains toward the sea, a geographical term for northern Syria and eastern Cilicia. Syria is named first because Paul's work in Antioch preceded his time in Tarsus, and because Cilicia was administratively subordinate to Syria under Rome.","translation":"Then I went into the regions of Syria and Cilicia."},{"c1":1,"v1":22,"c2":1,"v2":22,"label":"Galatians 1:22","text":"\"Was unknown\" describes a continuous state — literally \"I remained unknown\" — not a momentary gap that quickly closed. \"By face\" specifies exactly what kind of ignorance is meant: they simply didn't recognize him on sight. And \"in Christ,\" attached to \"the churches of Judea,\" distinguishes these believing assemblies from the ordinary Jewish synagogues of the same region.","translation":"And I remained, in person, unknown to the churches of Judea that are in Christ."},{"c1":1,"v1":23,"c2":1,"v2":23,"label":"Galatians 1:23","text":"\"Were hearing\" is imperfect — they kept on hearing this report repeatedly, not just once. \"Destroyed\" is portheo again, the same violent word from verse 13, describing not the historical fact of Paul having once wrecked the church but the ongoing process he used to pursue as a persecutor, now flipped completely into ongoing gospel proclamation.","translation":"Only, they kept on hearing: 'He who used to persecute us is now announcing as good news the faith he once was devastating.'"},{"c1":1,"v1":24,"c2":1,"v2":24,"label":"Galatians 1:24","text":"\"Glorified\" is imperfect too — a continuing, repeated response, not a single moment of praise. \"In me\" uses en in the sense of ground or basis: Paul's changed life was the occasion, the reason, for their glorifying God, not merely the setting in which it happened.","translation":"And they kept on glorifying God on account of me."},{"c1":2,"v1":1,"c2":2,"v2":1,"label":"Galatians 2:1","text":"\"Took\" is synparalambano, \"to take along as a companion\" — and bringing Titus, an uncircumcised Gentile, up to Jerusalem was a deliberate provocation, turning him into a living test case for the whole controversy about to be argued.","translation":"Then, after the space of fourteen years, I went up again to Jerusalem with Barnabas, taking Titus along also."},{"c1":2,"v1":2,"c2":2,"v2":2,"label":"Galatians 2:2","text":"\"By revelation\" is kata, \"in accordance with\" — not necessarily the sole cause of the trip, since Acts records the Antioch church commissioning him too, but Paul adds that the Spirit independently confirmed the same direction. \"Communicate\" is anatithemi, \"to lay something out for others' consideration\" — Paul presented his gospel for a hearing, not for correction. \"Of reputation\" translates a participle of dokeo, \"to be reputed, held in esteem,\" a genuine term of honor for James, Peter, and John, with no sarcasm implied by the Greek itself. \"Run in vain\" draws on the footrace, in the present subjunctive, expressing a live, ongoing fear that his mission work could still be undercut — not that it already had failed.","translation":"And I went up in accordance with a revelation, and I laid before them the gospel that I am preaching among the Gentiles — privately, before those of recognized standing — for fear that I might be running, or have run, for nothing."},{"c1":2,"v1":3,"c2":2,"v2":3,"label":"Galatians 2:3","text":"\"Being\" carries concessive force — \"although being\" a Gentile. \"Compelled\" denies not the attempt but its success: real pressure was applied to force circumcision on Titus, and it failed.","translation":"But not even Titus, who was with me, was compelled to be circumcised, Greek though he was."},{"c1":2,"v1":4,"c2":2,"v2":4,"label":"Galatians 2:4","text":"\"Brought in unawares\" is pareisago, \"to bring in alongside\" — used elsewhere of smuggling hostile agents into a city through a friendly faction inside the walls. \"Who\" translates hoitines, a pronoun flagging not mere identity but character: these people were running true to type. \"Came in privily\" (pareiserchomai) is aorist with the force of a pluperfect, describing an earlier, gradual infiltration. \"Spy out\" (kataskopeo) is a straightforward military-espionage word. \"Bring into bondage\" (katadouloo) means to reduce to utter slavery, put in the future tense to mark not a vague wish but a plan its authors expected to succeed.","translation":"It was because of false brothers who had been secretly brought in — the kind who had stolen in to spy out our freedom that we have in Christ Jesus, meaning to reduce us to abject slavery."},{"c1":2,"v1":5,"c2":2,"v2":5,"label":"Galatians 2:5","text":"\"Gave place\" is eiko, \"to yield.\" \"Subjection\" (hypotage) describes an act of voluntary submission, which Paul refuses to make even briefly — the point being that nothing was ever imposed on him, because he never yielded anything to begin with.","translation":"To them we did not yield in submission, not for a single hour, so that the truth of the gospel might continue to abide with you."},{"c1":2,"v1":6,"c2":2,"v2":6,"label":"Galatians 2:6","text":"\"Of reputation\" is the same dokeo-word from verse 2, no irony intended. \"Added\" is prosanatithemi, the same verb Paul used in 1:16 for \"conferred,\" now negated: the Jerusalem leaders imparted nothing to him, exactly as he had imparted nothing to them beforehand. \"Maketh no matter\" is diaphero, \"to matter, to make a difference\" — Paul flatly says their former status changes nothing about his commission, since it came from God directly.","translation":"But from those who were of reputation — whatever they were makes no difference to me; God does not receive a man's face — those of reputation added nothing to me."},{"c1":2,"v1":7,"c2":2,"v2":7,"label":"Galatians 2:7","text":"\"Committed\" is pepisteumai, perfect tense, a technical administrative term used in the imperial bureaucracy for someone formally and permanently entrusted with a responsibility. Paul is not describing two different gospels for two different audiences — \"the gospel to the uncircumcision\" and \"the gospel to the circumcision\" are the same message, entrusted to different messengers for different fields.","translation":"But on the contrary, when they saw that I had been entrusted with the gospel to the uncircumcised, just as Peter had been entrusted with it to the circumcised."},{"c1":2,"v1":8,"c2":2,"v2":8,"label":"Galatians 2:8","text":"\"Wrought\" is energeo, describing God's actual operative power at work — and notably it is not paired here with the preposition \"in,\" as it usually is when Scripture describes the Spirit's inward work in a believer. This is God's outward blessing on their preaching, not an inward transformation being described. \"Peter\" and \"me\" sit in the dative, the case marking whose interest an action serves: God worked for Peter, and God worked for Paul, in the same way.","translation":"For he who worked effectively for Peter with a view to an apostleship to the circumcised, worked effectively for me also, with a view to the Gentiles."},{"c1":2,"v1":9,"c2":2,"v2":9,"label":"Galatians 2:9","text":"\"Pillars\" (stylos) is architectural language borrowed for people — the church pictured as a temple, and these three men as its structural supports. The right hand given in pledge was a widespread ancient gesture of ratified partnership, found on treaties and coins alike. \"Fellowship\" is koinonia, a shared enterprise, a partnership — here specifically a partnership in preaching the same gospel to different fields.","translation":"And when James, Kephas, and John — who were of reputation as pillars — recognized the grace given to me, they gave to me and Barnabas the right hands of partnership, that we should go to the Gentiles and they to the circumcised."},{"c1":2,"v1":10,"c2":2,"v2":10,"label":"Galatians 2:10","text":"\"Remember\" (mnemoneuo) is used here in an unusual sense — not simply \"recall\" but \"care for, provide for.\" \"Forward\" is spoudazo, meaning eager, diligent effort, not just willingness — Paul describes sustained activity, not a passing intention.","translation":"Only, that we should keep on remembering the poor — the very thing I was, in fact, eager and diligent to do."},{"c1":2,"v1":11,"c2":2,"v2":11,"label":"Galatians 2:11","text":"\"Withstood\" is anthistemi, \"to set oneself against,\" a verb that usually implies the other side struck first — in Paul's telling, Peter was the aggressor, even if unintentionally. \"He was to be blamed\" renders a perfect participle with an imperfect verb, literally \"he stood condemned,\" describing an ongoing state of standing under judgment in the eyes of the Antioch believers, not a single accusation.","translation":"But when Kephas came to Antioch, I opposed him to his face, because he stood condemned."},{"c1":2,"v1":12,"c2":2,"v2":12,"label":"Galatians 2:12","text":"\"Did eat\" is imperfect — a settled habit, not a one-time meal. \"Withdrew\" (hypostello) is a military term for pulling troops back under cover, or a sailor's term for furling a sail — imperfect as well, describing a gradual retreat rather than a sudden break. \"Separated\" (aphorizo) marks the final, completed severing that followed the gradual withdrawal.","translation":"For before certain men came from James, he was in the habit of eating with the Gentiles; but when they came, he began drawing back and separating himself, fearing those of the circumcision."},{"c1":2,"v1":13,"c2":2,"v2":13,"label":"Galatians 2:13","text":"\"Dissembled\" is hypokrinomai, literally \"to answer from under [a mask]\" — the root of our word \"hypocrite,\" the language of an actor playing a part that conceals his true face. \"Carried away\" pictures Barnabas swept along with the rest, Paul's own closest partner in Gentile ministry caught in the same pretense.","translation":"And the rest of the Jews also played along with his pretense, so that even Barnabas was swept along with their pretense."},{"c1":2,"v1":14,"c2":2,"v2":14,"label":"Galatians 2:14","text":"\"Walked uprightly\" translates orthopodeo, literally \"walk with straight feet\" — the image of an honest, undeviating course. \"According to\" (pros) narrows the charge precisely: not that Peter's whole life was crooked, but that his stance toward gospel truth, specifically, was not straight. \"Livest\" is present tense, describing a settled pattern rather than his behavior at that literal moment.","translation":"But when I saw that they were not walking a straight course with reference to the truth of the gospel, I said to Kephas in front of everyone: 'If you, a Jew, are habitually living like a Gentile and not like a Jew, how can you compel the Gentiles to live like Jews?'"},{"c1":2,"v1":15,"c2":2,"v2":15,"label":"Galatians 2:15","text":"The pronoun \"we\" is emphatic — Greek verbs already carry their subject, so adding it deliberately draws a sharp line between \"us\" (Jews by birth) and Gentile \"sinners,\" a phrase Paul is using ironically, borrowing the very language of Pharisaic contempt he once wielded himself.","translation":"We ourselves are Jews by nature, and not sinners from among the Gentiles."},{"c1":2,"v1":16,"c2":2,"v2":16,"label":"Galatians 2:16","text":"\"Law\" here means divine law treated purely as a legal system of merit — obey and be approved, disobey and be condemned. Paul's own life had proven the system fails even its most devoted keepers. \"Justified\" is dikaioo, sharing a root with dikaiosyne, \"righteousness\" — the act of declaring, not gradually making, someone righteous. \"By\" before \"faith\" translates dia, marking the channel through which that verdict comes.","translation":"Knowing that a man is not declared righteous by works of law, but only through faith in Jesus Christ, we too put our trust in Christ Jesus, so that we might be declared righteous by faith in Christ, and not by works of law — because by works of law no flesh will be declared righteous."},{"c1":2,"v1":17,"c2":2,"v2":17,"label":"Galatians 2:17","text":"\"If\" is ei, marking a real, fulfilled condition, not a hypothetical. The logic runs: seeking justification in Christ puts a Jew in the very category, \"sinner,\" he'd normally reserve for Gentiles, since the seeking itself admits works never justified him. \"By\" here is en, better read as the sphere in which justification happens — Christ himself is the space within which a believer is declared righteous, not merely the tool by which it is accomplished.","translation":"But if, in seeking to be declared righteous in Christ, we ourselves also were found to be sinners, is Christ then a servant of sin? Away with the thought!"},{"c1":2,"v1":18,"c2":2,"v2":18,"label":"Galatians 2:18","text":"\"Build\" (oikodomeo) and \"destroy\" (kataluo) are architectural opposites applied to legal validity — to build is to declare something standing and binding, to destroy is to strip it of force. Paul pictures himself tearing the law down as a means of acceptance with God, then rebuilding it by going back to it, an act that would prove him the very transgressor he claims not to be. \"Transgressor\" is parabates, someone who crosses a known boundary, sharper than the ordinary word for sinner (hamartolos, \"one who misses a mark\") because it names a willful, informed violation.","translation":"For if I build up again the very things I tore down, I demonstrate myself to be a transgressor."},{"c1":2,"v1":19,"c2":2,"v2":19,"label":"Galatians 2:19","text":"The emphatic ego (\"I,\" myself) singles this out as Paul's own experience, not a general rule for everyone. \"Died to the law through the law\" is a paradox: the law's own penalty, satisfied in Christ's death, is what ends the law's claim on the believer — the law itself supplies the very means of escaping it.","translation":"For I, through the law, died to the law, so that I might live to God."},{"c1":2,"v1":20,"c2":2,"v2":20,"label":"Galatians 2:20","text":"\"Am crucified\" is perfect tense, a completed past event with results still standing in the present — Paul's identification with Christ's death is a settled, finished fact whose benefits he keeps drawing on. \"Yet not I\" marks the swap of a self-directed life for a Christ-indwelt one, not the annihilation of the person but a change in what powers him.","translation":"I have been crucified with Christ; and it is no longer I who live, but Christ who lives in me. And the life I now live in the flesh, I live by faith — the faith of the Son of God, who loved me and gave himself in my place."},{"c1":2,"v1":21,"c2":2,"v2":21,"label":"Galatians 2:21","text":"\"Frustrate\" is atheteo, \"to set aside something established, to void it, to strip it of force\" — the identical verb Paul later uses for annulling a legal covenant in 3:15. Adding works to grace does exactly that to grace, nullifying it, since grace by definition asks nothing in payment. \"In vain\" is dorean, literally \"without a cause, gratuitously, for nothing\" — if law-keeping could justify, Christ's death would have had no reason to happen.","translation":"I do not set aside the grace of God; for if righteousness comes through law, then Christ died for nothing."},{"c1":3,"v1":1,"c2":3,"v2":1,"label":"Galatians 3:1","text":"\"Foolish\" is anoetos — not simple ignorance but a failure to use one's own powers of perception, a moral dullness rather than an intellectual defect. \"Bewitched\" (baskaino) draws on the old superstition of the evil eye, framing their defection as if a malignant spell had been cast over them. \"Set forth\" is prographo, the standard word for posting a public notice, the same verb used of a father publicly disowning responsibility for a son's debts — Paul preached Christ's crucifixion as a placarded public announcement, impossible to miss. \"Crucified\" is perfect tense: not a dead figure frozen on a cross, but the living, risen Christ who still and permanently bears the marks of that crucifixion.","translation":"O senseless Galatians, who has cast an evil eye on you — you, before whose very eyes Jesus Christ was publicly placarded as the crucified one?"},{"c1":3,"v1":2,"c2":3,"v2":2,"label":"Galatians 3:2","text":"\"Hearing of faith\" translates akoe pisteos — akoe can mean either the act of hearing or the message heard, and context favors the latter: \"the message that proclaims faith,\" set against a message of law-obedience. Paul narrows the whole argument down to one decisive test question.","translation":"This only I want to learn from you: did you receive the Spirit by works of law, or by the message that proclaims faith?"},{"c1":3,"v1":3,"c2":3,"v2":3,"label":"Galatians 3:3","text":"\"Made perfect\" is epiteleo, \"to bring to completion\" — full spiritual maturity, not sinless perfection. \"Flesh\" here means the whole natural human capacity operating apart from the Spirit, mind, will, effort, resume, not merely the physical body. Paul's question turns on a jarring mismatch: begun by the Spirit, are they now trying to finish by self-effort?","translation":"Are you so senseless? Having begun by the Spirit, are you now being brought to completion by the flesh?"},{"c1":3,"v1":4,"c2":3,"v2":4,"label":"Galatians 3:4","text":"The construction leaves room for doubt in Paul's own mind — \"if indeed\" — as though he can hardly believe the sufferings they endured for the gospel might really turn out to have been pointless.","translation":"Did you suffer so many things for nothing — if it really was for nothing?"},{"c1":3,"v1":5,"c2":3,"v2":5,"label":"Galatians 3:5","text":"\"Ministereth\" is epichoregeo, a vivid word originally tied to underwriting the costs of a theatrical chorus — \"to supply abundantly, lavishly, at one's own expense.\" Both participles, \"supplying\" and \"working,\" are present tense: this was still happening even as the Galatians drifted, God's hand still visibly active among them in spite of their defection.","translation":"Does he then who is continually, abundantly supplying the Spirit to you, and continually working works of power among you, do this by works of law, or by the message that proclaims faith?"},{"c1":3,"v1":6,"c2":3,"v2":6,"label":"Galatians 3:6","text":"\"Accounted\" is logizomai, a bookkeeping term — to credit something to an account, to enter it on deposit. Abraham's faith was not a meritorious deed that earned righteousness as wages; it was simply credited, entered to his account, as righteousness.","translation":"Just as Abraham believed God, and it was credited to him, resulting in righteousness."},{"c1":3,"v1":7,"c2":3,"v2":7,"label":"Galatians 3:7","text":"\"Therefore\" (ara) draws the logical conclusion straight out of verse 6. \"Children\" is huioi, properly \"sons,\" a status word, not a genealogical one — Paul's point is deliberately non-biological: the family line that matters runs through shared faith, not shared blood.","translation":"You perceive, then, that those who are of faith — these are sons of Abraham."},{"c1":3,"v1":8,"c2":3,"v2":8,"label":"Galatians 3:8","text":"Paul personifies \"the scripture,\" crediting it with foresight, a Jewish idiom for saying that what Scripture records, God himself foresaw and intended. The verb \"would justify\" is present tense, marking a timeless principle rather than a single past event: this is simply how God has always operated.","translation":"And the scripture, foreseeing that God would declare the Gentiles righteous by faith, announced the good news beforehand to Abraham: 'In you all the nations will be blessed.'"},{"c1":3,"v1":9,"c2":3,"v2":9,"label":"Galatians 3:9","text":"\"Faithful Abraham\" singles out the one trait that mattered, that he believed, even though Abraham had every worldly reason to trust his own resources instead; he was no simple nomad but a cultured, wealthy man who nonetheless staked everything on taking God at his word.","translation":"So then, those who are of faith are being blessed together with believing Abraham."},{"c1":3,"v1":10,"c2":3,"v2":10,"label":"Galatians 3:10","text":"\"Under curse\" places the phrase in a kind of legal territory — hupo marking the sphere or jurisdiction a person is bound within. \"Continueth\" pictures the law as a bounded domain: stay inside it perfectly, or you're already outside it and cursed, since no one keeps it perfectly.","translation":"For as many as are of works of law are under a curse; for it stands written: 'Cursed is everyone who does not keep on remaining in all the things written in the book of the law, to do them.'"},{"c1":3,"v1":11,"c2":3,"v2":11,"label":"Galatians 3:11","text":"\"By the law\" is en nomo, literally \"in law,\" the same locative-of-sphere idea from verse 10. \"Just\" functions here as a forensic, legal term, the person accepted and approved by God's verdict, not a description of moral character. \"Shall live\" describes the result of that verdict, a new standing before God, not an infusion of new energy.","translation":"Now that no one is declared righteous before God within the sphere of law is evident, because 'the righteous shall live by faith.'"},{"c1":3,"v1":12,"c2":3,"v2":12,"label":"Galatians 3:12","text":"Paul sets law and faith up as two mutually exclusive operating principles, not two stages of one process. The quotation from Leviticus describes a righteousness that would come from perfectly doing what the law demands, a righteousness no fallen person can actually produce.","translation":"And the law is not of faith; but 'the one who does them shall live by them.'"},{"c1":3,"v1":13,"c2":3,"v2":13,"label":"Galatians 3:13","text":"\"Redeemed\" is exagorazo, not the ordinary word for buying (agorazo, to purchase in the marketplace) but \"to buy someone out of the marketplace\" entirely, so they can never be put up for sale again — a stronger, more final image than a simple purchase. \"For us\" is hyper again, the substitution word from 1:4: Christ took the curse's place, standing exactly where we stood.","translation":"Christ bought us out from under the curse of the law, having become a curse in our place — for it stands written: 'Cursed is everyone who is hanged on a tree.'"},{"c1":3,"v1":14,"c2":3,"v2":14,"label":"Galatians 3:14","text":"Two purpose clauses, each beginning with hina (\"in order that\"), run in parallel: Christ's becoming a curse accomplishes both the extension of Abraham's blessing to the Gentiles and the giving of the Spirit to Jew and Gentile alike, received the same way, through faith.","translation":"So that the blessing of Abraham might come to the Gentiles in Christ Jesus, so that we might receive the promise of the Spirit through faith."},{"c1":3,"v1":15,"c2":3,"v2":15,"label":"Galatians 3:15","text":"\"Covenant\" translates diatheke, from a verb meaning \"to place between,\" an arrangement one party binds himself to, not a will or testament here. \"Confirmed\" is kuroo, \"to ratify,\" in the perfect tense, meaning the ratification is a closed, settled matter. Paul's point rests on ordinary legal custom: once such an agreement is sealed, no one unilaterally cancels it (atheteo, the same verb from 2:21) or adds new terms to it (diatasso).","translation":"Brothers, I am speaking in a human way: even a man's covenant, once it has been ratified, no one sets aside or adds conditions to."},{"c1":3,"v1":16,"c2":3,"v2":16,"label":"Galatians 3:16","text":"\"Seed\" in the singular, in Hebrew idiom, ordinarily just means \"descendants\" collectively, but Paul presses the grammatical singular to make a theological point, reading it as pointing to one person specifically, Christ, rather than to many.","translation":"Now to Abraham the promises were spoken, and to his seed. He does not say, 'and to seeds,' as referring to many, but as referring to one: 'and to your seed,' who is Christ."},{"c1":3,"v1":17,"c2":3,"v2":17,"label":"Galatians 3:17","text":"The 430 years, drawn from Exodus, does real argumentative work: a covenant ratified centuries earlier and left standing that whole time cannot be quietly cancelled by a law that shows up later. The longer the gap, the stronger the point.","translation":"This is what I mean: a covenant already ratified by God, the law which came four hundred and thirty years later, does not annul, so as to make the promise void."},{"c1":3,"v1":18,"c2":3,"v2":18,"label":"Galatians 3:18","text":"\"Gave\" is charizomai, sharing a root with charis, \"grace,\" a gift given purely out of the giver's own generosity, with no reciprocal terms attached, unlike a pledge made under a mutual bargain, for which Greek had an entirely different word. The perfect tense marks this as a past grant whose effect is still standing.","translation":"For if the inheritance is from law, it is no longer from promise; but God has granted it to Abraham, as a gift of grace, through promise."},{"c1":3,"v1":19,"c2":3,"v2":19,"label":"Galatians 3:19","text":"\"Because of transgressions\" leans toward purpose rather than mere cause: the law was added to expose and define transgression. \"Transgression\" (parabasis) means stepping over a known boundary, sharper than the broader word for sin (hamartia, literally \"missing a mark\"), because a boundary has to be marked before it can be crossed — the law turns vague moral failure into a named, specific violation. \"Added\" (prostithemi) marks the law as a later, subordinate supplement, not a rival to the original promise, and its arrival through two intermediaries, angels and a mediator, quietly underscores its lesser standing next to the promise God gave Abraham directly, face to face.","translation":"What then is the law? It was added because of transgressions, until the seed should come to whom the promise had been made — arranged through angels, by the hand of a mediator."},{"c1":3,"v1":20,"c2":3,"v2":20,"label":"Galatians 3:20","text":"\"Mediator\" is mesites, someone who stands in the middle of two or more parties — the very structure of the word implies a go-between, and a go-between only exists where there is a bilateral arrangement. The law needed a mediator because it functions like a two-party contract, obligations on both sides. The promise needed none, because God simply acted, alone, unconditionally.","translation":"Now a mediator is not of one party alone; but God is one."},{"c1":3,"v1":21,"c2":3,"v2":21,"label":"Galatians 3:21","text":"Paul poses the question as a false dilemma and rejects it. If a law existed capable of producing life, righteousness really would come from law-keeping, but no such law exists, since none of us can perfectly keep it. Law and promise aren't opposed; they simply operate in different domains, one exposing guilt, the other offering rescue.","translation":"Is the law then against the promises of God? Away with the thought! For if a law had been given that was able to give life, righteousness would indeed have been by law."},{"c1":3,"v1":22,"c2":3,"v2":22,"label":"Galatians 3:22","text":"\"Concluded\" is synkleio, \"to shut up together, to confine\" — Paul pictures Scripture as a jailer, locking all of humanity without exception into the single cell of universal sin, leaving faith in Christ as the only door out.","translation":"But the scripture has locked up all things together under sin, so that the promise, by faith in Jesus Christ, might be given to those who believe."},{"c1":3,"v1":23,"c2":3,"v2":23,"label":"Galatians 3:23","text":"\"Kept\" is phroureo, a garrison term, to guard under close watch, as a fortress holds prisoners. \"Unto\" is eis, better read \"with a view to\" than as a strict marker of time: the guarding had a purpose, driving people toward faith, not just marking time until it arrived. The definite article before \"faith\" ties this specifically to faith in the historic, already-accomplished Christ, distinct from Abraham's faith, which looked forward to a Christ not yet come.","translation":"But before the faith came, we were kept in custody under law, locked up with a view to the faith that was about to be revealed."},{"c1":3,"v1":24,"c2":3,"v2":24,"label":"Galatians 3:24","text":"\"Schoolmaster\" badly translates paidagogos, which did not mean a teacher (that word was didaskalos) but a household slave assigned to escort and morally supervise a boy from about age six to sixteen, walking him to school, watching his behavior, never instructing him directly. The image grows straight out of \"kept\" (phroureo, \"guard\") in the verse before: the law functioned as a strict, low-ranking guardian for a minor, not a teacher imparting content.","translation":"So that the law became our guardian until Christ, so that we might be declared righteous by faith."},{"c1":3,"v1":25,"c2":3,"v2":25,"label":"Galatians 3:25","text":"The definite article before \"faith\" again ties this to the specific, historic faith already discussed — its arrival ends the guardian's job outright, the way a ward's coming-of-age ends a guardian's authority.","translation":"But now that the faith has come, we are no longer under a guardian."},{"c1":3,"v1":26,"c2":3,"v2":26,"label":"Galatians 3:26","text":"Paul shifts from \"we\" (the Jews, under the guardian) to \"you\" (his readers, Jew and Gentile alike) to signal that the wall dividing them is down. \"Children\" is huios, \"son\" in the sense of one who has reached full legal standing, someone who has outgrown the guardian's supervision entirely, not merely a family member by birth.","translation":"For you are all sons of God, through faith, in Christ Jesus."},{"c1":3,"v1":27,"c2":3,"v2":27,"label":"Galatians 3:27","text":"\"Baptized\" is baptizo, \"to place or put into,\" describing the Spirit's act of placing a believer into living union with Christ, not the rite of water baptism, which never accomplished this. \"Put on\" (endyno) is garment language: not a costume or disguise, but stepping into an actual new identity and relationship.","translation":"For as many of you as were placed into Christ have put on Christ."},{"c1":3,"v1":28,"c2":3,"v2":28,"label":"Galatians 3:28","text":"The three paired opposites, Jew and Greek, slave and free, male and female, name the deepest social divisions of the ancient world and declare them dissolved at the level that matters: shared life \"in Christ Jesus,\" continuing the incorporation language of the previous verse.","translation":"There is neither Jew nor Greek, there is neither slave nor free, there is no male and female; for you are all one in Christ Jesus."},{"c1":3,"v1":29,"c2":3,"v2":29,"label":"Galatians 3:29","text":"Paul closes the chapter's argument with a tight syllogism: belonging to Christ makes you Abraham's seed, since Christ himself is that seed, per verse 16, and therefore an heir, but specifically an heir according to promise, not law.","translation":"And if you are Christ's, then you are Abraham's seed, heirs according to promise."},{"c1":4,"v1":1,"c2":4,"v2":2,"label":"Galatians 4:1–2","text":"\"Child\" translates nepios, a word built from a root meaning \"non-speaking\" — someone too immature to speak for himself, hence a minor rather than an adult. Paul's legal picture is precise: an heir who is still a nepios has no more practical freedom than a doulos, a slave, even though he technically owns the whole estate. He answers to two different overseers — the epitropos, guardian of his person, and the oikonomos, steward of his property — until prothesmia arrives, a term from Greek inheritance law for the date the father himself fixed in advance for the minority to end. Israel under law, Paul is arguing, occupied exactly this position: heir to everything, yet functionally indistinguishable from a slave until the appointed time.","translation":"Now I say that as long as the heir is a child, he differs not at all from a slave, though he is master of everything, but he is under guardians and stewards until the day set beforehand by the father."},{"c1":4,"v1":3,"c2":4,"v2":3,"label":"Galatians 4:3","text":"The same word nepios from verse 1 recurs — \"children\" really means \"immature ones.\" \"Elements\" is stoicheia, a term for the basic, rudimentary units from which a larger system is assembled — alphabet letters, first principles, starting points. Applied to religion, it points to the elementary stage common to fallen humanity generally, whether that meant the ceremonial machinery of Judaism for the Jew or the ritual observances of paganism for the Gentile.","translation":"In the same way we too, when we were immature ones, were held in continual bondage under the elementary principles of the world."},{"c1":4,"v1":4,"c2":4,"v2":4,"label":"Galatians 4:4","text":"\"The fullness of the time\" uses chronos, the Greek word for time simply as duration, a stretch running its course — not kairos, the word for a divinely marked, critical moment. Paul's point is that an appointed span of ordinary time had to run out before God acted. \"Sent forth\" is the double compound exapostello: apostello alone means to commission an envoy with credentials, the root of our word \"apostle\"; the prefixed ex adds \"out from\" — the Son was dispatched out from the Father's own presence, not merely sent on an errand. \"Under the law\" carries no definite article in the Greek — not merely under the Mosaic code as an institution, but under law as a condition, the ordinary status any Jew was born into.","translation":"But when the fullness of the time came, God sent out his Son, born of a woman, made under law."},{"c1":4,"v1":5,"c2":4,"v2":5,"label":"Galatians 4:5","text":"\"Redeem\" translates exagorazo, a marketplace verb meaning to buy someone clear out of the slave market. \"Adoption\" is huiothesia, literally \"son-placing\" — not, as in modern usage, taking in someone else's child, but the formal installing of a minor into the full legal standing of an adult son, with all rights of inheritance activated at once.","translation":"So that he might buy out of slavery those who were under law, so that we might receive the placing as sons."},{"c1":4,"v1":6,"c2":4,"v2":6,"label":"Galatians 4:6","text":"\"Crying\" is krazo, a verb for a loud, unrestrained cry rather than a murmur — in the Greek Old Testament it's often the language of someone crying out to God in urgent prayer. Paul sets the Aramaic Abba directly beside its Greek equivalent, pater — the pairing suggests this may be the very word Jesus himself used in addressing the Father, carried over by his followers with the same warmth and familiarity.","translation":"And because you are sons, God sent forth the Spirit of his Son into your hearts, crying, Abba, Father."},{"c1":4,"v1":7,"c2":4,"v2":7,"label":"Galatians 4:7","text":"Paul switches from plural \"sons\" to singular \"son,\" turning the argument from the group to the individual reader — this is now personal, not general. As son, each believer is also kleronomos, heir, and the inheritance rests entirely on that sonship, not on works performed under law.","translation":"So that you are no longer a slave but a son, and if a son, then also an heir through God."},{"c1":4,"v1":8,"c2":4,"v2":8,"label":"Galatians 4:8","text":"\"Did service\" translates douloo, the verb form of doulos, \"slave\" — the identical slavery vocabulary Paul has been using throughout. He isn't denying that the pagan gods existed; he's denying that they are theoi phusei, \"gods by nature.\" Phusis means what belongs to a being by its very origin and essential character. Real, in Paul's view, but not divine — some other class of being altogether.","translation":"But at that time, not knowing God, you were enslaved to those which by nature are not gods."},{"c1":4,"v1":9,"c2":4,"v2":9,"label":"Galatians 4:9","text":"\"Known\" is ginosko, a verb for personal, relational knowledge rather than bare intellectual acquaintance — to be \"known by God\" means to have been the object of his saving initiative, not merely observed by him. \"How\" opens a genuine cry of astonishment, not a request for information. \"Turn\" is present tense — this defection was happening even as Paul wrote, not something already completed and past. Two different words lie behind the English \"again\": one for simple repetition, and a second, sharper one meaning repetition that returns all the way to where you started — Paul's charge is not just that they're repeating an act but sliding clear back to where they began.","translation":"But now, having come to know God — or rather, having been known by God — how is it that you are turning back again to the weak and beggarly elementary principles, to which you want to be enslaved all over again?"},{"c1":4,"v1":10,"c2":4,"v2":10,"label":"Galatians 4:10","text":"\"Observe\" translates paratereo, a verb for anxious, scrupulous watching — the nervous attentiveness of someone tracking every date lest a prescribed observance slip by unnoticed. The fourfold list — days, months, seasons, years — sweeps in the entire Jewish sacred calendar, from the weekly Sabbath up through the sabbatical and jubilee years.","translation":"Days you are scrupulously watching, and months, and seasons, and years."},{"c1":4,"v1":11,"c2":4,"v2":11,"label":"Galatians 4:11","text":"\"Labour\" is kopiao, labor pushed to the point of exhaustion, and it stands in the perfect tense — Paul isn't describing ongoing toil but looking back at a finished, thorough piece of work that may now be wasted. The grammar of his fear suggests he suspects the worst has already happened, not merely that it might.","translation":"I am afraid for you, that somehow I have labored over you to the point of exhaustion for nothing."},{"c1":4,"v1":12,"c2":4,"v2":12,"label":"Galatians 4:12","text":"\"Be\" translates ginomai, \"become\" — not a static state but a change undergone. Paul isn't asking the Galatians to hold still; he's asking them to make the same move he already made, becoming free of the law, because he himself became like a Gentile in order to reach Gentiles.","translation":"Become as I am, because I also became as you are, brothers, I beg you. You did me no wrong."},{"c1":4,"v1":13,"c2":4,"v2":13,"label":"Galatians 4:13","text":"Paul's phrasing points directly to a physical illness that brought him to preach in Galatia in the first place — the plain sense of the Greek is that sickness, not strategy, put him there.","translation":"But you know that it was because of a weakness of the flesh that I preached the gospel to you on that first occasion."},{"c1":4,"v1":14,"c2":4,"v2":14,"label":"Galatians 4:14","text":"\"Despised\" is ekptuo, literally \"spit out\" — the physical gesture of disgust, not a mild dislike. \"Rejected\" is exoutheneo, to count as absolutely worthless. Paul's condition, whatever it was, was severe enough to invite exactly that reaction, and the Galatians refused it, receiving him instead as though he were a messenger sent from God, even as Christ Jesus himself.","translation":"And the trial that was in my flesh you did not spit out in disgust, nor did you utterly despise it, but you received me as a messenger of God, as Christ Jesus."},{"c1":4,"v1":15,"c2":4,"v2":15,"label":"Galatians 4:15","text":"\"Blessedness\" is makarios, a word that in ordinary Greek usage describes not a pious feeling but a state of flourishing, of prosperity. Paul is asking, quite bluntly, what became of that thriving condition, given how far the Galatians had once been willing to go on his behalf.","translation":"Where then is that prosperous blessing of yours? For I bear you witness that, if it had been possible, you would have dug out your own eyes and given them to me."},{"c1":4,"v1":16,"c2":4,"v2":16,"label":"Galatians 4:16","text":"\"Enemy\" translates echthros, a word for active hostility rather than mere estrangement — and Paul insists the label describes only how the Galatians now perceive him, provoked by nothing more than his telling them the truth.","translation":"So then, have I become your enemy by telling you the truth?"},{"c1":4,"v1":17,"c2":4,"v2":17,"label":"Galatians 4:17","text":"\"Zealously affect\" is zeloo, used elsewhere of a suitor pressing attention on someone he's trying to win — the Judaizers are courting the Galatians, working hard at it, but \"not well\" (kalos), that is, not honorably. The purpose clause exposes the maneuver: they mean to shut the Galatians out — ekkleio, \"to shut out\" — presumably cutting them off from Paul's gospel of grace, precisely so the Galatians will have nowhere left to turn but back to them.","translation":"They are zealously courting you, but not honestly; they want to shut you out, so that you will be courting them."},{"c1":4,"v1":18,"c2":4,"v2":19,"label":"Galatians 4:18–19","text":"\"My little children\" translates teknon, \"born ones,\" a word that stresses the birth relation, and grammatically it sits in apposition rather than as a fresh address — Paul's emotion is overflowing mid-sentence, not starting a new thought. He pictures himself in labor pains again, pointing back to the anguish of first bringing them to faith, until Christ is morphoo — given outward form, outward expression — in them. The passive voice matters: Christ already dwells within them; the problem is that nothing of him shows on the outside, because the Galatians have traded dependence on the Spirit for self-effort under law.","translation":"But it is good to be zealously courted in a good thing at all times, and not only when I am present with you, my little children born of me, for whom I am again undergoing birth pains until Christ is outwardly formed in you."},{"c1":4,"v1":20,"c2":4,"v2":20,"label":"Galatians 4:20","text":"\"Now\" is not the ordinary nun but arti, \"at this very moment\" — a sharper, more immediate marker of time than the plain word would give. \"Stand in doubt\" is aporeo, built from a word for \"a way through\" with a negating prefix — literally \"without a way,\" at a loss which direction to take. The verb's middle voice locates the confusion inside Paul himself, a mind pulled back and forth, not merely a difficult set of circumstances outside him.","translation":"I could wish to be present with you at this very moment, and to change my tone, because I am without a way forward concerning you."},{"c1":4,"v1":21,"c2":4,"v2":21,"label":"Galatians 4:21","text":"\"Law\" here carries no definite article — Paul isn't narrowly pointing at the Mosaic code but at law as a governing principle of life, the very thing the Galatians are eager to place themselves under. His question functions as a challenge: if you're so devoted to law, have you actually listened to what it says?","translation":"Tell me, you who want to be under law, are you not listening to the law?"},{"c1":4,"v1":22,"c2":4,"v2":22,"label":"Galatians 4:22","text":"\"Bondmaid\" translates paidiske, the standard Greek term for a female slave. Paul sets the two mothers up as representative figures before drawing out what each line of descent means.","translation":"For it stands written that Abraham had two sons, one by the slave woman and one by the free woman."},{"c1":4,"v1":23,"c2":4,"v2":23,"label":"Galatians 4:23","text":"\"Was born\" stands in the perfect tense rather than the simple past — Paul isn't just reporting a historical fact but pointing to its lasting result, still standing. Ishmael came kata sarka, \"according to the flesh,\" by nature's ordinary working; Isaac came dia epangelias, \"through promise,\" by God's own intervention when nature alone could not have produced him.","translation":"But the son of the slave woman has been born according to the flesh, while the son of the free woman has been born through the promise."},{"c1":4,"v1":24,"c2":4,"v2":24,"label":"Galatians 4:24","text":"\"Allegory\" doesn't cast doubt on the history — Paul treats the Genesis narrative as literal fact that also carries a further, figurative meaning laid on top of it. He lines up two covenants: the one from Sinai bears children (the same birth-verb used of Ishmael and Isaac) into slavery — and that covenant, he says, is Hagar.","translation":"These things contain an allegory. For these women are two covenants: one from Mount Sinai, bearing children into slavery — this is Hagar."},{"c1":4,"v1":25,"c2":4,"v2":25,"label":"Galatians 4:25","text":"The name Hagar apparently echoed the Arabic name for Sinai itself, so the identification isn't arbitrary wordplay but draws on a real geographic association — tying the mountain of the law to the slave-mother, and by extension to the Jerusalem of Paul's own day, still living under the law's bondage.","translation":"Now this Hagar is Mount Sinai in Arabia, and she corresponds to the Jerusalem that now is, for she is in slavery with her children."},{"c1":4,"v1":26,"c2":4,"v2":26,"label":"Galatians 4:26","text":"\"Jerusalem above\" was already a familiar category among Jewish teachers, who spoke of a heavenly Jerusalem standing as the archetype behind the earthly city. Paul claims that category for the church — free, and \"our mother,\" set over against the enslaved earthly Jerusalem of the previous verse.","translation":"But the Jerusalem above is free, and she is our mother."},{"c1":4,"v1":27,"c2":4,"v2":27,"label":"Galatians 4:27","text":"The verse is a direct quotation of Isaiah 54:1, applied here to Sarah, once barren, who stands for the heavenly Jerusalem.","translation":"For it stands written: Rejoice, barren woman who does not bear; break out and cry aloud, you who are not in labor, because more are the children of the desolate woman than of her who has a husband."},{"c1":4,"v1":28,"c2":4,"v2":28,"label":"Galatians 4:28","text":"The better manuscripts read \"you\" rather than \"we,\" addressing the Galatians directly. \"After the manner of Isaac\" ties their status not to physical descent from Abraham but to the same miraculous promise that produced Isaac.","translation":"Now you, brothers, are children of promise, after the manner of Isaac."},{"c1":4,"v1":29,"c2":4,"v2":29,"label":"Galatians 4:29","text":"\"Persecuted\" is imperfect tense, describing ongoing action rather than a single incident — Ishmael's mockery of Isaac was a sustained pattern, matching the sustained pressure the Judaizers were putting on the Galatians.","translation":"But just as at that time the one born according to the flesh was continually persecuting the one born according to the Spirit, so it is now also."},{"c1":4,"v1":30,"c2":4,"v2":30,"label":"Galatians 4:30","text":"Paul quotes Genesis directly: the rejection of Ishmael becomes, in his reading, the pattern for the rejection of law-keeping as a ground of inheritance.","translation":"But what does the Scripture say? Cast out the slave woman and her son, for the son of the slave woman shall by no means inherit with the son of the free woman."},{"c1":4,"v1":31,"c2":4,"v2":31,"label":"Galatians 4:31","text":"The verse closes the allegory with a plain declaration, setting up the practical exhortation that follows in chapters five and six.","translation":"So then, brothers, we are not children of a slave woman but of the free woman."},{"c1":5,"v1":1,"c2":5,"v2":1,"label":"Galatians 5:1","text":"\"Freedom\" stands in the dative of advantage — Christ's purpose in setting believers free was for their own benefit. \"Stand fast\" is a present imperative, calling for continuous standing, not a single act, while \"be entangled\" is a present imperative that's negated — \"stop being held\" — implying the Galatians were already partway into the net when Paul wrote. \"Entangled\" translates enecho, \"held within,\" the language of something caught and unable to work itself free.","translation":"For this freedom Christ set you free. Keep on standing firm, then, and stop being held again in a yoke of slavery."},{"c1":5,"v1":2,"c2":5,"v2":2,"label":"Galatians 5:2","text":"The conditional verb is present tense, \"if you keep on being circumcised\" — the Galatians had not yet taken the step; Paul is warning them off something still being decided, not rebuking something already done.","translation":"Look, I, Paul, say to you that if you keep on being circumcised, Christ will profit you not at all."},{"c1":5,"v1":3,"c2":5,"v2":3,"label":"Galatians 5:3","text":"\"Testify\" is marturomai, a stronger word than ordinary witnessing — closer to a solemn protest. The participle for \"circumcised\" is present tense, aimed at anyone in the act of receiving the rite, not at those already done — the warning is preventive. \"Debtor\" is opheiletes, someone bound by obligation: submit to circumcision, and the contract is for the whole law, not just its more comfortable parts.","translation":"And I solemnly affirm again to every man who is having himself circumcised, that he is obligated to keep the whole law."},{"c1":5,"v1":4,"c2":5,"v2":4,"label":"Galatians 5:4","text":"\"Become of no effect\" combines katargeo with the preposition \"from\" — the sense is being cut off from a source, losing the benefit that flows through a connection, not being annihilated. \"Fallen from grace\" uses ekpipto, \"to lose one's grip on, to fail of\" — the image is of losing hold of something you had been grasping, not of forfeiting a legal standing already secured once for all.","translation":"You have been severed from Christ, you who are seeking to be justified by law; you have lost your hold on grace."},{"c1":5,"v1":5,"c2":5,"v2":5,"label":"Galatians 5:5","text":"\"Wait\" is the same word used elsewhere of the believer's eager expectation of Christ's return — a straining, forward-leaning anticipation, not passive patience. Paul insists this hoped-for righteousness comes \"through the Spirit,\" \"by faith\" — the opposite path from the self-effort of law-keeping.","translation":"For we, through the Spirit, by faith, are eagerly waiting for the hope of righteousness."},{"c1":5,"v1":6,"c2":5,"v2":6,"label":"Galatians 5:6","text":"\"Availeth\" is ischuo, \"to have power to accomplish something\" — neither the physical mark of circumcision nor its absence carries any power in itself. What has power is faith, and specifically faith actively working itself out through love.","translation":"For in Christ Jesus neither circumcision counts for anything, nor uncircumcision, but faith working through love."},{"c1":5,"v1":7,"c2":5,"v2":7,"label":"Galatians 5:7","text":"\"Did run\" is imperfect tense, picturing a race already underway before something broke its rhythm. \"Hinder\" translates enkopto, literally \"cut in\" — the image of another runner breaking into your lane and disrupting your stride, exactly what the Judaizers did to the Galatians' progress.","translation":"You were running well. Who cut in on you, to keep you from obeying the truth?"},{"c1":5,"v1":8,"c2":5,"v2":8,"label":"Galatians 5:8","text":"\"Persuasion\" is an active-sense noun describing the pressure exerted on the Galatians by the Judaizers' arguments — Paul flatly denies that pressure has any connection to the God who called them.","translation":"This persuasion did not come from the one who is calling you."},{"c1":5,"v1":9,"c2":5,"v2":9,"label":"Galatians 5:9","text":"The verb \"leavens\" is present tense — the corruption was underway but had not yet run its full course. Paul's alarm is at the insidious nature of the influence, not merely at how far it had already spread.","translation":"A little leaven leavens the whole lump."},{"c1":5,"v1":10,"c2":5,"v2":10,"label":"Galatians 5:10","text":"The pronoun \"I\" is emphatic in the Greek, setting Paul's own confidence apart from whatever anyone else may think. \"Confidence\" is a perfect-tense form of the verb \"to persuade\" — a settled conviction already reached and still held, not a fresh guess. \"Bear his judgment\" uses bastazo, the verb for carrying something genuinely heavy.","translation":"I myself have come to a settled confidence in the Lord concerning you, that you will take no other view. But the one who is troubling you will carry his judgment, whoever he is."},{"c1":5,"v1":11,"c2":5,"v2":11,"label":"Galatians 5:11","text":"\"Yet\" implies a contrast with an earlier state of affairs — Paul, as a Pharisee, once did preach circumcision, before his conversion. The condition is contrary to fact: he isn't doing so now, and his ongoing persecution proves it — were he still preaching the law's requirement, the cross would no longer be a skandalon, a \"stumbling block,\" to the Jews who resent it precisely because it sets the law aside.","translation":"But I, brothers, if I am still preaching circumcision, why am I still being persecuted? Then the stumbling block of the cross has been done away with."},{"c1":5,"v1":12,"c2":5,"v2":12,"label":"Galatians 5:12","text":"\"Cut off\" is apokopto, a verb for physical mutilation — Paul's biting wish is that those pressing circumcision on the Galatians would go all the way and mutilate themselves entirely, a jab sharpened by the fact that self-castration was practiced by pagan priests in that very region.","translation":"I wish that those who are upsetting you would even mutilate themselves."},{"c1":5,"v1":13,"c2":5,"v2":13,"label":"Galatians 5:13","text":"\"Occasion\" is aphorme, a military term for a base of operations, a staging ground for a campaign — Paul warns against turning freedom into exactly that kind of springboard for self-indulgence. \"Serve\" is, ironically, slave-vocabulary — freedom from the law's slavery is meant to be exchanged for a willing, loving slavery to one another, and the imperative is present tense: keep on serving, continually.","translation":"For you were called to freedom, brothers — only do not turn your freedom into a base of operations for the flesh, but through love keep on serving one another."},{"c1":5,"v1":14,"c2":5,"v2":14,"label":"Galatians 5:14","text":"\"Fulfilled\" is a perfect-tense verb, \"to fill up, complete\" — the tense signals a settled, standing result: the whole law, Paul says, already stands fully met in this one command.","translation":"For the whole law stands fulfilled in one statement: You shall love your neighbor as yourself."},{"c1":5,"v1":15,"c2":5,"v2":15,"label":"Galatians 5:15","text":"The three verbs — bite, devour, consume — are drawn from the vocabulary of wild animals tearing at one another, a startlingly savage picture for describing conflict among Christians.","translation":"But if you are biting and devouring one another, watch out that you are not consumed by one another."},{"c1":5,"v1":16,"c2":5,"v2":16,"label":"Galatians 5:16","text":"\"Walk\" is ordinary language for conducting oneself day to day. \"Fulfil\" is teleo, \"carry through to completion,\" and the Greek stacks two negatives together, which in Greek intensify rather than cancel each other — this is an emphatic promise, not a bare command: walk by the Spirit, and the flesh's desire simply will not be carried through to completion.","translation":"But I say, keep on walking by the Spirit, and you will positively not carry out the desire of the flesh."},{"c1":5,"v1":17,"c2":5,"v2":17,"label":"Galatians 5:17","text":"\"Against\" carries the sense of \"down upon,\" pressing to suppress. \"Contrary\" is a verb meaning \"to lie opposed to,\" and the reciprocal pronoun shows the opposition runs both directions: flesh presses against Spirit, Spirit presses against flesh, each working to keep the other from getting its way in you.","translation":"For the flesh continually desires against the Spirit, and the Spirit against the flesh, and these lie opposed to one another, so that you do not do the things that you wish."},{"c1":5,"v1":18,"c2":5,"v2":18,"label":"Galatians 5:18","text":"\"Led\" is present tense — ongoing submission to the Spirit's guidance, not a single past transfer of allegiance.","translation":"But if you are being led by the Spirit continually, you are not under law."},{"c1":5,"v1":19,"c2":5,"v2":19,"label":"Galatians 5:19","text":"\"Manifest\" is phaneros, \"out in the open\" — Paul assumes his readers already recognize this list on sight, without needing it explained. \"Lasciviousness\" is a stronger word than simple sensuality — it describes a shameless, brazen disregard for restraint or public decency, the attitude of someone who no longer cares who's watching.","translation":"Now the works of the flesh are plainly evident, and they are things such as these: sexual immorality, impurity, brazen shamelessness."},{"c1":5,"v1":20,"c2":5,"v2":20,"label":"Galatians 5:20","text":"\"Witchcraft\" is pharmakia, the same root behind our word \"pharmacy\" — sorcery practiced by means of drugs and potions. \"Heresies\" is hairesis, built from the verb \"to choose\" — literally a chosen course, a faction gathered around a preferred opinion rather than the truth as given.","translation":"Idolatry, sorcery, enmities, strife, jealousy, outbursts of anger, selfish ambitions, divisions, factions."},{"c1":5,"v1":21,"c2":5,"v2":21,"label":"Galatians 5:21","text":"\"Do\" translates a verb for habitual practice rather than an isolated act — Paul's warning targets settled character, the pattern of a life, not a single lapse. \"Revellings\" describes the raucous, torch-lit drinking processions that wound through the streets late into the night.","translation":"Envyings, drunkenness, carousings, and things like these — about which I warn you in advance, just as I warned you before, that those who habitually practice such things will not inherit the kingdom of God."},{"c1":5,"v1":22,"c2":5,"v2":23,"label":"Galatians 5:22–23","text":"\"Fruit\" is singular, not plural — Paul pictures one unified organic cluster, not nine separate virtues to be cultivated one by one. \"Faith\" here means faithfulness, reliability — a Spirit-produced trait of character, not the initial act of trusting Christ. \"Temperance\" is the same word used elsewhere for an athlete's disciplined mastery over his own body in training — self-control in a literal, muscular sense.","translation":"But the fruit of the Spirit is: love, joy, peace, patient endurance, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-mastery. Against such things there is no law."},{"c1":5,"v1":24,"c2":5,"v2":24,"label":"Galatians 5:24","text":"\"Crucified\" is aorist tense, marking a decisive, completed act rather than an ongoing process — something settled once for all at the moment of faith. Paul pairs two words for the flesh's inner life: one for the passive dispositions and propensities lying within it, and a second for the active reaching-out of those propensities toward gratification.","translation":"And those who belong to Christ Jesus crucified the flesh once for all, together with its passions and its cravings."},{"c1":5,"v1":25,"c2":5,"v2":25,"label":"Galatians 5:25","text":"\"Walk\" here is a different verb from the one in verse 16 — one that means \"to walk in a straight line, to keep in step,\" almost a marching term. Since the Spirit is already the source of the believer's life, Paul's logic follows directly: keep that life in step with the source that produces it.","translation":"Since we are being kept alive by the Spirit, let us also keep in step with the Spirit."},{"c1":5,"v1":26,"c2":5,"v2":26,"label":"Galatians 5:26","text":"\"Vain glory\" is kenodoxos, literally \"empty opinion\" — a self-estimate with nothing solid behind it, the kind of ungrounded pride that is quick to provoke others and quick to envy them, because it is never secure in itself.","translation":"Let us not become those with empty pride, provoking one another, envying one another."},{"c1":6,"v1":1,"c2":6,"v2":1,"label":"Galatians 6:1","text":"\"Overtaken\" is prolambano, \"to catch unawares, to take by surprise\" — the picture is of a believer surprised into sin, not one who planned it. \"Fault\" is paraptoma, \"a false step, a slip beside the path\" — a lighter word than the \"transgression\" used earlier in the letter for a deliberate stepping over a boundary. \"Restore\" is katartizo, the word for setting a dislocated bone or mending a torn net — repair that returns something to full working order. \"Considering thyself\" uses a verb meaning \"to fix the eye on, to keep close watch\" — the one doing the restoring is told to keep just as sharp an eye on his own footing.","translation":"Brothers, even if a man is caught off guard in some false step, you who are spiritual, restore such a one in a spirit of gentleness, watching yourself closely, lest you too be tempted."},{"c1":6,"v1":2,"c2":6,"v2":2,"label":"Galatians 6:2","text":"\"Bear\" is bastazo, the verb for carrying something genuinely heavy — and here the carrying is meant to be willing and sympathetic, not the grudging endurance the same verb can describe elsewhere. \"The law of Christ\" carries the definite article in Greek, giving it an official ring, as though Christ himself issued this one command — love — as his own governing statute.","translation":"Keep on bearing one another's burdens, and so you will fully satisfy the law of the Christ."},{"c1":6,"v1":3,"c2":6,"v2":3,"label":"Galatians 6:3","text":"The self-deception in view is simple: a self-estimate detached from reality collapses the moment it's tested, and the one holding it is the last to notice.","translation":"For if anyone thinks himself to be something when he is nothing, he deceives himself."},{"c1":6,"v1":4,"c2":6,"v2":4,"label":"Galatians 6:4","text":"\"Prove\" is dokimazo, \"to put to the test in order to approve\" — an examination aimed at establishing genuine worth, not a snap judgment. \"Rejoicing\" is kauchema, a legitimate ground for satisfaction measured against oneself alone, not against a neighbor.","translation":"But let each one test his own work, and then he will have a reason for exulting with regard to himself alone, and not with regard to the other person."},{"c1":6,"v1":5,"c2":6,"v2":5,"label":"Galatians 6:5","text":"The word for \"burden\" here differs from the one in verse 2, though the distinction shouldn't be pressed too hard in this passage — the paradox is deliberate: bear each other's heavy loads, and yet each remains answerable for his own.","translation":"For each will carry his own load."},{"c1":6,"v1":6,"c2":6,"v2":6,"label":"Galatians 6:6","text":"\"Taught\" translates katecheo, literally \"to sound down into the ear\" — ordinary language for oral instruction, and the root of our word \"catechize.\" \"Communicate\" is koinoneo, \"to hold in partnership with\" — the one being taught is to stay in active fellowship with the teacher in every good thing taught, not drift off toward another source of instruction.","translation":"Let the one who is being instructed in the word share all good things with the one who instructs him."},{"c1":6,"v1":7,"c2":6,"v2":7,"label":"Galatians 6:7","text":"\"Mocked\" is mukterizo, literally \"to turn up the nose at\" — the picture of a contemptuous sneer, of thinking one can outwit God while wearing a face of outward respect.","translation":"Do not be deceived; God is not mocked. For whatever a man sows, this he will also reap."},{"c1":6,"v1":8,"c2":6,"v2":8,"label":"Galatians 6:8","text":"\"To\" in both phrases translates a preposition meaning \"with a view to\" — not seed literally dropped into soil, but a course of conduct chosen with an eye toward a particular goal, either gratifying the flesh or answering to the Spirit.","translation":"Because the one who sows with a view to his own flesh will reap corruption from the flesh, but the one who sows with a view to the Spirit will reap eternal life from the Spirit."},{"c1":6,"v1":9,"c2":6,"v2":9,"label":"Galatians 6:9","text":"\"Be weary\" pictures a farmer's flagging resolve, worn down by the sheer length of the labor. \"Faint\" is used elsewhere of reapers physically overcome by heat and exhaustion in the field — both verbs describe the temptation to quit not from one crisis but from the accumulated weight of ongoing effort.","translation":"And let us not grow weary in doing good, for in due season we will reap, if we do not give out from exhaustion."},{"c1":6,"v1":10,"c2":6,"v2":10,"label":"Galatians 6:10","text":"\"Opportunity\" is the same word translated \"season\" in the verse before — a fitting, appointed moment. \"Do\" is a verb emphasizing sustained, repeated activity rather than a single deed — the call is to keep working good continually, not perform it once and stop.","translation":"So then, as we have opportunity, let us keep on working what is good toward all, and especially toward those of the household of the faith."},{"c1":6,"v1":11,"c2":6,"v2":11,"label":"Galatians 6:11","text":"Paul draws attention to the size of his own handwriting here — large letters, in contrast to a scribe's smaller hand. Whatever exactly caused it, the detail underlines that these closing words are his own, unmediated, a personal seal on everything the letter has argued.","translation":"See with what large letters I have written to you with my own hand."},{"c1":6,"v1":12,"c2":6,"v2":12,"label":"Galatians 6:12","text":"\"Make a fair show\" translates a compound built plainly from the words for \"good\" and \"face\" — to put on a good face, a pleasing outward appearance. The drive to compel circumcision was cosmetic, aimed at the Judaizers' own standing with a watching Jewish audience, not at the truth of the gospel.","translation":"As many as want to make a good showing in the flesh, these are trying to compel you to be circumcised, only so that they will not be persecuted for the cross of Christ."},{"c1":6,"v1":13,"c2":6,"v2":13,"label":"Galatians 6:13","text":"Paul's charge is pointed: the very people pressing circumcision on the Galatians don't keep the law themselves — their real aim is to boast in the Galatians' flesh, not to honor the law they claim to champion.","translation":"For not even those who are circumcised keep the law themselves, but they want you to be circumcised so that they may glory in your flesh."},{"c1":6,"v1":14,"c2":6,"v2":14,"label":"Galatians 6:14","text":"\"Crucified\" here stands in the perfect tense — not a past event merely remembered but a standing, settled condition: the old order remains, permanently, a crucified thing to Paul, and he to it.","translation":"But far be it from me to glory, except in the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ, through whom the world stands crucified to me, and I to the world."},{"c1":6,"v1":15,"c2":6,"v2":15,"label":"Galatians 6:15","text":"Paul states his reason for glorying only in the cross: the mark on the body settles nothing either way; only a genuinely new creation counts.","translation":"For neither is circumcision anything, nor uncircumcision, but a new creation."},{"c1":6,"v1":16,"c2":6,"v2":16,"label":"Galatians 6:16","text":"\"Rule\" is kanon, literally a measuring rod or standard used to check straightness — Paul sets the cross, and a life ordered by the Spirit, as the one standard defining the true Israel of God.","translation":"And as many as order their conduct by this rule, peace and mercy be upon them, and upon the Israel of God."},{"c1":6,"v1":17,"c2":6,"v2":18,"label":"Galatians 6:17–18","text":"\"Marks\" is stigma, the brand burned into the skin of a slave or temple attendant to show which master owned him. Paul's scars from beatings and stoning are, in his own reading, exactly that kind of brand — visible proof of whose he is.","translation":"From now on, let no one trouble me, for I bear on my body the brand-marks of Jesus. The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with your spirit, brothers. Amen."}],"Ephesians":[{"c1":1,"v1":5,"c2":1,"v2":5,"label":"Ephesians 1:5","text":"\"Predestinated\" translates *proorizō* (προορίζω) — *horizō*, \"to mark out a boundary\" (our word \"horizon\" descends from the noun *horos*), prefixed by *pro*, \"beforehand.\" It's a surveyor's word turned theological: God marked out the limits of our destiny before anything else was drawn on the map. The destination itself is named by *huiothesia*, \"adoption,\" built from *tithēmi*, \"to place,\" and *huios*, the word for a grown, legally-recognized son — not *teknon*, \"a born child.\" Paul is borrowing the Roman legal custom in which an adopted son received full civil standing and inheritance rights regardless of birth. God does the birthing (we become *teknon*, born ones, at regeneration) and then, in the same predestining act, places us as *huios* — sons with legal standing to inherit. The agency clause, \"through Jesus Christ,\" uses *dia* with the genitive, the preposition of the intermediate agent: Christ is the means by which the placement happens, not merely its occasion.","translation":"He marked us out beforehand, with a view to our placement as adult sons for himself through Jesus Christ, according to what pleased his own desire."},{"c1":1,"v1":6,"c2":1,"v2":6,"label":"Ephesians 1:6","text":"\"Freely bestowed\" is *charitoō* (χαριτόω), built on the same root as *charis*, \"grace\" — the verb means, essentially, \"to grace someone,\" to pursue and surround a person with favor. \"The Beloved,\" naming Christ, is a perfect participle of *agapaō*: not \"the one being loved\" but \"the one who has been loved and stands loved\" — a settled, completed, permanent state rather than an ongoing feeling. Paul reaches for the perfect tense here because he wants the reader to hear the Father's love for the Son as a fixed fact, not a fluctuating sentiment. And the location matters grammatically as much as theologically: \"in the Beloved\" is a sphere within which grace operates — outside of Christ and his finished work, this grace has nowhere to land.","translation":"To the praise of the glory of his grace, which he graced us with in the Beloved — the one who has been loved and stands loved."},{"c1":1,"v1":7,"c2":1,"v2":7,"label":"Ephesians 1:7","text":"\"We have redemption\" is present tense and durative — not \"we got redemption once\" but \"we are continuously in possession of it,\" a standing state rather than a memory. Greek supplies three separate verbs for the idea of redemption, each catching a different frame: *agorazō*, \"to buy in the marketplace\" (the purchase itself); *exagorazō*, \"to buy out of the market,\" implying the item is now permanently removed from sale; and *lutroō*, \"to set free by paying a ransom.\" The noun Paul uses here, *apolutrōsis*, carries that last sense — release secured by a price paid, not a release granted out of leniency. \"Forgiveness\" is *aphesis*, from *aphiēmi*, \"to send away\" — sin is not excused but dispatched, sent off as if it had never been filed. And \"trespasses\" is *paraptōma*, literally \"a fall to the side,\" the picture of a foot slipping off a path rather than of a target deliberately missed. The next verse describes the scale of this grace with *perisseuō*, \"to super-abound, to exist in surplus beyond what was needed\" — grace with excess built in, not grace rationed to just cover the debt.","translation":"In whom we are continually having our redemption through his blood, the sending away of our trespasses, according to the wealth of his grace, which he made to super-abound toward us in every wisdom and understanding."},{"c1":1,"v1":9,"c2":1,"v2":9,"label":"Ephesians 1:9","text":"\"Mystery\" (*mustērion*) in Paul's vocabulary is not something baffling but something formerly sealed — a plan kept back and now disclosed, plain enough once opened. The \"will\" behind it is *thelēma*, desire rooted in the emotions, not *boulē*, a plan reasoned out by deliberation; God's disclosure of this plan flows out of his heart's affection, not a committee decision. \"According to\" is *kata*, a preposition whose root sense is \"down over\" — it describes something governing or pressing down on what follows. So the mystery isn't revealed arbitrarily; it's released under the governing hand of what pleased God (*eudokia*, \"that which seemed good\").","translation":"Having made known to us the secret of his will, according to what pleased him, which he purposed within himself."},{"c1":1,"v1":10,"c2":1,"v2":10,"label":"Ephesians 1:10","text":"\"Dispensation\" translates *oikonomia* — *oikos*, \"house,\" plus *nomos*, \"law\" or \"management\" — the word for running a household's affairs, not a theological era in itself but the administrative method applied to one. The \"times\" being administered are *kairoi*, not *chronoi*: Greek distinguishes ordinary ticking duration (*chronos*) from *kairos*, the decisive joint or hinge-point where one period gives way to the next. Paul is describing history's seams, not its minutes. \"Gather together\" is *anakephalaioō* — *ana*, \"again,\" plus the root behind \"head\" or \"sum\" — picturing a return to a single head after fragmentation, the way a ledger's scattered entries get summed back to one total. Everything splintered by sin gets reheaded under Christ.","translation":"With a view to an administration of the fullness of the seasons: to head up again the all things in the Christ — the things in the heavens and the things on the earth — in him."},{"c1":1,"v1":11,"c2":1,"v2":11,"label":"Ephesians 1:11","text":"The better manuscripts read not \"we have obtained an inheritance\" but \"we were made an inheritance\" — the saints are the thing possessed, God's own portion, rather than merely heirs receiving one. *Proorizō* returns from verse 5, the same surveyor's verb marking out boundaries in advance. And \"counsel\" here is *boulē*, deliberate, reasoned purpose, standing next to \"will,\" *thelēma*, desire from the heart — Paul stacks both words to say God's choice is neither cold calculation alone nor impulse alone, but reasoned love and loving reason acting together.","translation":"In whom also we were made a heritage, having been marked out beforehand according to the purpose of the one who works the all things according to the counsel of his will."},{"c1":1,"v1":12,"c2":1,"v2":12,"label":"Ephesians 1:12","text":"\"Who first trusted\" hides an unusual compound: *proelpizō*, \"to hope beforehand\" — not the ordinary verb for believing (*pisteuō*) but one built on *elpizō*, \"to hope,\" with *pro* attached. It marks out Jewish believers specifically, those who held a Messianic hope in advance of the Messiah's actual coming — hoping ahead of the event that would confirm the hope. And \"Christ\" is simply the Greek transliteration of the word meaning \"anointed one,\" the exact rendering of the Hebrew \"Messiah\" — two words, one title, carried across two languages into the one name we now use as though it were a surname.","translation":"So that we should be to the praise of his glory — we who have hoped beforehand in the Christ."},{"c1":1,"v1":13,"c2":1,"v2":13,"label":"Ephesians 1:13","text":"\"Sealed\" is *sphragizō*, everyday commercial vocabulary — sealing a jar of produce for shipment, branding livestock, marking a finished transaction so no one tampers with it. Papyri from the period use the same verb for goods checked, certified, and closed off from further handling. Applied to the Holy Spirit, the image carries three ideas at once: a completed transaction (the price for sin has been paid, in full, no further negotiation), a mark of ownership (this belongs to someone now), and a guarantee of security (sealed goods travel safe). The Spirit himself is the seal — not a token he leaves behind, but his own presence taking up permanent residence.","translation":"In whom you also, having heard the word of the truth, the good news of your salvation — in whom also having believed — you were sealed with the Spirit of the promise, the Holy Spirit."},{"c1":1,"v1":14,"c2":1,"v2":14,"label":"Ephesians 1:14","text":"\"Earnest\" is *arrabōn*, a term straight out of the marketplace — a deposit paid up front as a pledge that the rest is coming. Papyri receipts use the word for exactly this: a down payment on a cow, an advance to a mouse-catcher for pest control, money in hand guaranteeing a fuller payment later. Applied to the Spirit, the picture is that God has already handed over a first installment of the salvation he intends to complete — his indwelling presence now is the guarantee, not a bonus, of what's still owed. \"Purchased possession\" is *peripoiēsis*, the general idea of something acquired and kept for oneself — the saints as God's own preserved property, awaiting the final payment, which Paul elsewhere identifies with the resurrection of the body.","translation":"Who is a down payment of our inheritance, until the redeeming of the possession being preserved, to the praise of his glory."},{"c1":1,"v1":17,"c2":1,"v2":17,"label":"Ephesians 1:17","text":"\"Spirit\" here has no definite article, and it's ambiguous by design: not simply the Holy Spirit as a separate gift, and not simply the human spirit, but the human spirit as it operates under the Holy Spirit's influence — a settled disposition of wisdom rather than a fresh possession, since the Ephesians already had the Spirit. Paul prays for that disposition to characterize them, not for something they lack. \"Knowledge\" is *epignōsis*, not the simple word for knowing (*gnōsis*) but the compound with *epi* attached, intensifying it toward full, precise, experiential acquaintance — knowledge that has arrived, not knowledge still gathering data.","translation":"That the God of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of the glory, may give you a spirit of wisdom and revelation in full knowledge of him."},{"c1":1,"v1":18,"c2":1,"v2":18,"label":"Ephesians 1:18","text":"\"The eyes of your understanding\" is literally \"the eyes of your heart\" — *kardia* in Greek covers not just emotion but the whole faculty of reasoning and perceiving, so Paul is praying for clarified thinking as much as clarified feeling. \"Being enlightened\" is a perfect participle: a completed past act whose light-filled result still stands, not a flickering illumination that needs relighting. The word for \"what\" (*tis*) matters too — Paul isn't asking that they grasp how great the hope is, but simply what it actually is, its content rather than its size.\n\nThe prayer's third request, spilling into the next two verses, piles up four separate Greek words for power: *dunamis*, inherent capacity; *energeia*, power actually at work; *kratos*, strength as displayed; *ischus*, power as a built-in endowment. Stacked together they describe not one attribute among many but power measured against power, the way you'd triangulate a distance from several fixed points. That power is identified concretely: it's the same force God put to work (*energeō*) raising Christ bodily out from among the dead and seating him at his right hand — resurrection power, already spent once, now aimed at believers.","translation":"The eyes of your heart having been enlightened, so that you may know what is the hope of his calling, what is the wealth of the glory of his inheritance among the saints, and what is the surpassing greatness of his power toward us who are believing, measured by the operative strength of his might, which he put to work in the Christ when he raised him out from among the dead and seated him at his right hand in the heavenlies."},{"c1":1,"v1":21,"c2":1,"v2":21,"label":"Ephesians 1:21","text":"Paul stacks four rank-words in a row — *archē*, \"first one, ruler\"; *exousia*, \"delegated authority\"; *dunamis*, \"power\"; *kuriotēs*, \"lordship\" — the standard Jewish vocabulary for tiers of angelic beings, good and fallen alike. \"Far above\" translates *huperanō*, a doubled-up preposition (\"over\" plus \"above\") that piles emphasis on emphasis, as if one \"above\" wasn't enough. \"World\" is *aiōn*, not *kosmos* — Paul means duration, an age with a beginning and end, not the created order itself; Christ outranks every power in this age and in the one still to come.\n\nThe chapter's close adds \"put in subjection,\" *hupotassō*, military language for placing troops under a commanding officer — Christ's exaltation is a formal transfer of command, not a vague honor. \"Church\" is *ekklēsia*, \"a called-out assembly.\" And \"fulness,\" *plērōma*, is deliberately double-edged: the church is what Christ fills, and simultaneously that through which his fulness is expressed to the world — container and content named with the same word.","translation":"Far above every ruler and authority and power and lordship, and every name that is named, not only in this age but also in the one about to come; and he put all things in subjection under his feet, and gave him as head over all things to the church, which is his body, the fulness of the one who is continually filling the all things with all things."},{"c1":2,"v1":2,"c2":2,"v2":2,"label":"Ephesians 2:2","text":"\"Walked\" is *peripateō* — literally \"to walk around\" — worn down by ordinary use into \"to conduct one's life, to make one's way.\" It's locative: the unsaved life is lived entirely inside the sphere of sin, the way a fish lives inside water, never once outside the boundary. The tense is aorist, viewing that whole former life as a single completed unit seen from a distance, not tracked moment by moment. \"Course of this world\" pairs two ideas that need separating: *kosmos* is humanity's entire system alienated from God across all history, while *aiōn* is the particular spirit or climate of a given age — the zeitgeist, so to speak, of one period within that longer alienation.\n\n\"Prince of the power of the air\" names Satan with *archōn*, \"the first in rank,\" ruling *exousia*, delegated authority — a term Paul elsewhere applies to demonic forces under Satan's command. \"Air\" is *aēr*, the thick lower atmosphere close to the ground, distinct from *aithēr*, the clear upper air — Satan's domain is pictured as pressing right up against human life, not remote. \"Sons of disobedience\" uses the Hebrew idiom in which a person is called \"a son of\" whatever quality defines him; \"disobedience\" itself, *apeitheia*, comes from a root meaning \"unpersuadable\" — not merely someone who disobeys once, but someone constitutionally resistant to being moved.","translation":"In which you once walked about, according to the age of this world, according to the ruler of the authority of the air, of the spirit now working in the sons of unpersuadableness."},{"c1":2,"v1":3,"c2":2,"v2":3,"label":"Ephesians 2:3","text":"\"Had our conversation\" is *anastrephō*, an old sense of \"conduct oneself\" that has nothing to do with talking — Paul is still describing behavior, not speech. \"Lusts\" is *epithumia*, a strong craving, morally neutral by itself but colored evil here by its source: *sarx*, \"flesh,\" the corrupted human nature. \"Fulfilling\" is a present participle of *poieō*, \"to do\" — durative, describing habitual practice rather than a single act, the steady, ongoing performance of what the depraved nature and \"mind\" (*dianoia*, here plural, meaning the evil thoughts themselves) demanded.\n\n\"Were\" — \"children of wrath\" — is imperfect tense, continuous action stretching backward with no starting point in view: not a status entered into at some point but one true from birth onward. \"Children\" is *teknon*, from the verb \"to give birth,\" stressing the fact of origin rather than legal standing — we didn't drift into this condition, we were born into it, by nature (*phusis*, what is innate, not merely acquired by habit).","translation":"Among whom we too all once conducted ourselves in the cravings of our flesh, continually doing the desires of the flesh and of the thoughts, and we were continually children of wrath by nature, even as the rest."},{"c1":2,"v1":4,"c2":2,"v2":6,"label":"Ephesians 2:4–6","text":"Paul pivots with a bare *de* — \"but\" — swinging from the death sentence of the previous verses straight into God's answer. He calls God *plousios* in mercy, \"rich,\" a word that pictures abundant material wealth, not bare sufficiency. The reason is marked with *dia*, \"because of\": it was his great *agapē*, the deliberate, self-giving love that goes to a cross for the one loved, not mere affection. The verb \"made alive,\" *zōopoieō*, is bound to two others by the same *syn-* prefix — made alive with, raised with, seated with Christ — so that what happened to him in his resurrection and exaltation is now credited to us as if it had happened to us directly. \"By grace you have been saved\" is a periphrastic construction, a perfect participle plus the present tense of \"to be,\" stacking two ideas at once: the perfect declares the saving act finished with its results fixed, and the present tense of the helping verb declares those results still standing right now. It is a state entered once, and never exited.","translation":"But God, being continually rich in mercy, because of his great love with which he loved us, made us — dead as we were in our trespasses — alive together with the Christ (by grace you have been in a saved state, and you remain so), and raised us up together and seated us together in the heavenly places in Christ Jesus."},{"c1":2,"v1":7,"c2":2,"v2":7,"label":"Ephesians 2:7","text":"The purpose clause looks ahead with a striking phrase — \"the ages that are coming upon one another,\" a stack of future ages rolling in wave after wave, era after era, rather than one uninterrupted stretch of time. What each age keeps displaying is the \"surpassing riches of his grace,\" and the verb \"show,\" *endeiknumi*, stands in the middle voice: God acts for his own interest, putting himself on exhibit through kindness to us. \"Kindness,\" *chrēstotēs*, paired with the preposition *epi*, \"upon, toward,\" frames the saints as the very stage on which this display keeps happening — we are the exhibit, not just the audience.","translation":"In order that in the ages continually coming upon one another he might keep on displaying, for his own glory, the surpassing riches of his grace in kindness toward us in Christ Jesus."},{"c1":2,"v1":8,"c2":2,"v2":10,"label":"Ephesians 2:8–10","text":"\"That,\" in \"this is not of yourselves,\" is neuter — *touto* — while Greek \"faith,\" *pistis*, is feminine, so the pronoun cannot be pointing narrowly back to \"faith\" as its antecedent; it sweeps up the whole preceding statement, the entire fact of salvation by grace through faith. That whole package is called a gift, sourced in God, not in \"works\" as its origin. \"Workmanship,\" *poiēma*, comes from the ordinary verb \"to make,\" but the verb used a few words later for God's own creative act is *ktizō*, \"to create\" — the word reserved for what only God does. Good works were \"prepared beforehand,\" *proetoimazō*, stacking \"before\" onto \"make ready\": God laid the works out in advance as the track believers are meant to run, not the ticket that got them onto it. \"Walk,\" *peripateō*, literally \"to walk about,\" is Paul's standing word for the ordering of daily conduct.","translation":"For by the grace you have been in a saved state through faith, and this is not from yourselves — it is God's gift — not from works, so that no one may boast; for we are his workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works, which God made ready beforehand, in order that we should be continually walking in them."},{"c1":2,"v1":13,"c2":2,"v2":13,"label":"Ephesians 2:13","text":"The contrast turns on two adverbs of distance, \"far off\" and \"near,\" the standard terms for Gentiles outside the covenant and Jews within it. \"Have become near\" renders a verb of entering a new state rather than a gradual approach — a decisive, once-for-all shift, and one accomplished specifically \"by the blood of Christ,\" naming the price paid rather than making a vaguer appeal to his life or teaching.","translation":"But now, in Christ Jesus, you who once were far off have become near ones by the blood of the Christ."},{"c1":2,"v1":14,"c2":2,"v2":14,"label":"Ephesians 2:14","text":"\"He himself is our peace\" opens with an intensive pronoun, *autos* — not \"he too\" but \"he and no one else.\" Behind \"peace,\" *eirēnē*, stands the verb \"to join,\" so peace in Paul's vocabulary means fundamentally the joining of what had been split apart, not merely the absence of hostility. What had split Jew and Gentile is pictured architecturally as \"the middle wall of the fence\" — a boundary marker attached to a barrier, the whole Mosaic system read as a dividing hedge — and Christ's act is described as tearing the wall down, not cutting a gate through it.","translation":"For he himself is our peace, the one who made the two groups one, having broken down the dividing wall of the fence."},{"c1":2,"v1":15,"c2":2,"v2":15,"label":"Ephesians 2:15","text":"\"Abolished,\" *katargeō*, means to render something inoperative, not to erase it from memory — the law's claims stopped functioning as a dividing wall, put out of commission by Christ's death. The verb behind \"make\" in \"one new man\" is not the ordinary \"make,\" *poieō*, but *ktizō*, \"create\": Paul is not describing a merger of two existing parties into a coalition but the creation of a third thing that did not exist before. And \"new,\" *kainos*, means new in kind rather than new by the calendar, while \"man,\" *anthrōpos*, is the generic human-race word, not *anēr*, \"a male individual\" — so the entity in view is a new order of humanity, not one group's rebrand.","translation":"Having in his flesh rendered inoperative the law of commandments expressed in decrees, so that he might create the two into one new man in himself, making peace."},{"c1":2,"v1":16,"c2":2,"v2":16,"label":"Ephesians 2:16","text":"\"Reconcile,\" *apokatallassō*, carries a doubled prefix: *apo*, \"back,\" stacked onto the simple verb \"to reconcile.\" The simple verb, *katallassō*, means to bring together parties who are at odds; the compound suggests not merely a fresh truce between strangers but a restoration to some prior unity that had been lost — Jew, Gentile, and God being returned to how things stood before the rupture, not just patched into a new arrangement.","translation":"And that he might reconcile the both, back to their former unity, in one body to God through the cross, having put to death the enmity by it."},{"c1":3,"v1":6,"c2":3,"v2":6,"label":"Ephesians 3:6","text":"\"Should be,\" in the underlying Greek, is simply the infinitive \"to be\" — Paul is stating an already-accomplished fact, not announcing a future goal. Three compounds built on *syn-*, \"together with,\" pile up in a row: *sygklēronoma*, \"fellow-heirs\"; *syssōma*, \"fellow-body-members,\" belonging jointly to one body; and \"fellow-partakers.\" The manuscripts include the name \"Jesus\" alongside \"Christ\" here — without it the sentence could be misread as Gentiles inheriting Israel's own Messianic promises; with \"Jesus\" attached, the promises in view are specifically those tied to the crucified and risen one, not the covenant promises made to Israel as a nation.","translation":"That the Gentiles are fellow-heirs and fellow-body-members and fellow-partakers of the promise in Christ Jesus through the good news."},{"c1":3,"v1":9,"c2":3,"v2":9,"label":"Ephesians 3:9","text":"\"Bring to light,\" *phōtizō*, is built on the noun for light — Paul's task is to shine a light on something previously dim, not to invent it. \"Fellowship\" translates *oikonomia*, the same household-management word rendered \"dispensation\" elsewhere; here it names the administrative arrangement itself, the way the mystery is being put into practice, namely Gentile and Jew admitted on equal footing. \"World,\" *aiōn*, means \"age\" — the mystery was hidden from the very start of the ages, not withheld for merely a generation or two.","translation":"And to bring to light what is the administration of the mystery that has been kept hidden away from the ages in God who created all things."},{"c1":3,"v1":10,"c2":3,"v2":10,"label":"Ephesians 3:10","text":"\"Might be known,\" *gnōrizō*, and \"manifold,\" *polupoikilos* — literally \"much-variegated,\" a word elsewhere describing richly patterned, many-colored fabric — together picture the church as a display case: God's wisdom, in all its intricate coloring, is being made known to the ranks of angelic rulers and authorities through what happens in the church, not through direct instruction handed to them. The angels are onlookers to redemption, not participants in it — they learn what grace is by watching it worked out among people.","translation":"In order that now, through the church, there might be made known to the rulers and authorities in the heavenly places the many-colored wisdom of God."},{"c1":3,"v1":13,"c2":3,"v2":13,"label":"Ephesians 3:13","text":"\"Faint,\" *egkakeō*, means to lose heart, to give out under pressure — Paul is asking the Ephesians not to let his imprisonment discourage them into giving up. \"I desire\" is *aiteō* in the middle voice, \"to ask for oneself\": Paul is not relaying a command but pressing a personal request, that his sufferings be read correctly — as their honor, not his defeat.","translation":"For this reason I am asking, on my own behalf, that you not be losing heart at my tribulations for you, which are your glory."},{"c1":3,"v1":16,"c2":3,"v2":16,"label":"Ephesians 3:16","text":"The preposition behind \"strengthened ... into the inner man\" is *eis*, a preposition of motion, \"into,\" not a static \"in\" — the strengthening is pictured as power in motion, poured toward a destination, with the inward man, the seat of a person's moral and rational self, as the target and receiver rather than the source. That inpouring happens \"through the Spirit\": the Spirit is the channel of transmission, God's own agent carrying strength from himself into the core of the believer.","translation":"That he would grant to you, according to the riches of his glory, to be continually strengthened with power, through his Spirit, into the inward man."},{"c1":3,"v1":17,"c2":3,"v2":19,"label":"Ephesians 3:17–19","text":"\"Dwell,\" *katoikeō*, joins \"down\" to \"make one's home\" — the sense is settling in permanently, not visiting, and the aorist tense points to a decisive, completed settling rather than a gradual process. \"Rooted\" and \"grounded\" are both perfect-tense participles, planted once with the results still holding firm now — one image drawn from a tree's root system, the other from a building's foundation. \"Comprehend,\" *katalambanō*, does not mean intellectual understanding so much as \"seize, lay hold of, make one's own\" — Paul is praying for something closer to a grip than a grasp. \"Know,\" *ginōskō*, in the next clause shifts registers: it is the verb for knowledge gained by lived experience rather than reasoning, so the prayer moves from a mental laying-hold of Christ's love to that same love being lived and felt. \"Passeth\" is built on *huperballō*, \"to throw beyond\": this love outruns the very knowledge just described, so no amount of experiencing it exhausts it.","translation":"That the Christ may finally settle down and be at home in your hearts through faith; you having been firmly rooted and grounded in love, so that you may be fully able to lay hold, together with all the saints, of what is the breadth and length and height and depth, and to know by experience the love of Christ that goes beyond all such knowing — so that you may be filled up to all the fulness of God."},{"c1":4,"v1":4,"c2":4,"v2":6,"label":"Ephesians 4:4–6","text":"The passage builds a run of sevenfold repetitions of \"one\" — body, Spirit, hope, Lord, faith, \"baptism,\" God — a deliberate drumbeat underlining the unity Paul has just urged. The word usually left untranslated as \"baptism,\" *baptisma*, comes from a verb meaning \"to place into\" or \"immerse.\" Read for its plain sense rather than transliterated as a technical term, it names the single act by which every believer is placed into the one body — the same placing this letter has already described as being joined to Christ — rather than pointing narrowly to a ceremony.","translation":"One body and one Spirit, even as also you were called in one hope of your calling; one Lord, one faith, one placing-into; one God and Father of all, who is over all and through all and in all."},{"c1":4,"v1":7,"c2":4,"v2":7,"label":"Ephesians 4:7","text":"Paul turns from the church's corporate oneness to what belongs to each believer individually. \"Grace\" here is *charis*, carrying the definite article — the specific enabling grace his readers already know by experience, not *charisma*, the word for the gift itself, but the empowering source behind it. The opening \"but\" (*de*) is adversative rather than merely transitional: it sets \"each one\" over against the \"all\" of the unity just described, so that the oneness of the body never erases the particularity of the member. That grace comes \"according to the measure of the gift of Christ\" — *metron*, a measured allotment, not an equal share. Christ himself, the giver, sets the size of each portion to fit the calling it serves.","translation":"But to each one of us grace was given according to the measure of the gift belonging to the Christ."},{"c1":4,"v1":8,"c2":4,"v2":8,"label":"Ephesians 4:8","text":"The \"gifts\" Christ gives here are *doma*, a plain, general word for something given — not *charisma*, the term for a specific supernatural endowment. Paul is building toward verse 11, where these gifts turn out to be gifted men handed to the church. The verb behind \"led captivity captive\" is a cognate construction in the Greek, literally \"he took captive a captivity\" — a Hebraic idiom borrowed from Psalm 68, picturing a conquering king marching his defeated foes home in procession. \"Gave\" is a simple aorist, a completed, decisive act: the ascension and the distribution of gifts are treated as one accomplished event, not an ongoing process.","translation":"Wherefore it says: Having gone up on high, he took captive a host of captives and gave gifts to men."},{"c1":4,"v1":13,"c2":4,"v2":13,"label":"Ephesians 4:13","text":"\"Till\" translates *mechri*, marking a goal line stretching out ahead — \"as far as, up to the point where.\" \"Come\" is *katantaō*, \"to arrive at, to reach\" a destination, reinforced by *eis*, \"into\": the unity of the faith is something the church is still travelling toward, not something already possessed in full. \"Knowledge\" is *epignōsis*, knowledge intensified — the prefix *epi*- sharpens *gnōsis* into full, precise, firsthand acquaintance rather than secondhand information. \"Perfect\" is *teleios*, \"mature, full-grown\" — the deliberate opposite of the \"infants\" (*nēpioi*) of the very next verse. Growth toward Christ is measured not by an arrival at intellectual consensus but by the church catching up, member by member, to the \"stature\" — the full measured height — that belongs to him.","translation":"Until we all arrive at the oneness of the faith and of the full, precise knowledge of the Son of God, at a mature man, at the measure of the stature of the fullness of the Christ."},{"c1":4,"v1":14,"c2":4,"v2":14,"label":"Ephesians 4:14","text":"\"Children\" is *nēpios* — literally \"the non-speaking one,\" an infant who hasn't yet found words — the deliberate opposite of the \"mature man\" (*teleios*) of verse 13. \"Tossed to and fro\" is *kludōnizomai*, a nautical term for being pitched about by wave-swell, applied here to a mind knocked around by every new teaching. \"Carried about\" is *peripherō*, carried in circles — going nowhere, just churned. \"Sleight\" is *kubeia*, literally \"dice-playing\" — the false teacher operates like a cheat at the gaming table, loading the outcome. \"Cunning craftiness\" is *panourgia*, built from \"all\" and \"work\" — a readiness to do absolutely anything to get the result. And \"lie in wait to deceive\" renders *methodeia*, a deliberate, systematic scheme — the ancestor of our word \"method,\" but here bent entirely toward deception.","translation":"In order that we may no longer be infants, wave-tossed and carried around in circles by every wind of teaching, by the dice-player's trickery of men, by craftiness working its system of deceit."},{"c1":4,"v1":17,"c2":4,"v2":17,"label":"Ephesians 4:17","text":"\"Testify\" translates *martureō*, language for a solemn, formal declaration — closer to swearing testimony than casual advice — and Paul adds \"in the Lord,\" making the appeal Christ's own, not merely his. \"Walk\" is *peripateō*, the standard Greek idiom for a whole pattern of conduct, not a single act. \"Vanity\" is *mataiotēs* — emptiness, resultlessness, effort that goes nowhere and produces nothing lasting. And \"mind\" is *nous*, which in Greek carries more than intellect: it's the faculty that judges, discerns, and orients a person morally. A *nous* given over to *mataiotēs* isn't just confused — it has lost the capacity to aim at anything real.","translation":"This, therefore, I am saying and solemnly declaring in the Lord: no longer are you to be walking as the Gentiles walk, in the futility of their mind."},{"c1":4,"v1":18,"c2":4,"v2":18,"label":"Ephesians 4:18","text":"\"Darkened\" is a perfect passive participle of *skotoō* — the perfect tense marks an action completed in the past whose effect has settled in and stayed: not a temporary shadow but a permanent condition. \"Alienated\" is likewise built for a settled state, describing people who have become strangers to \"the life of God\" — shut out of a relationship, not merely misinformed about one. \"Ignorance\" is *agnoia*, and Paul locates it \"in them\" — the fault sits inside the person, not in some accident of upbringing. \"Blindness\" translates *pōrōsis*, from *pōros*, the word for hardened, callused tissue: the image is of skin gone numb from repeated friction, no longer able to feel what it once felt.","translation":"Being those who have been permanently darkened in their understanding, who stand alienated from the life of God because of the ignorance that is in them, because of the hardening of their heart."},{"c1":4,"v1":19,"c2":4,"v2":19,"label":"Ephesians 4:19","text":"\"Past feeling\" renders *apalgeō*, another perfect participle, describing people who have stopped being able to feel pain or shame — the nerve has gone dead. \"Given themselves over\" is *paradidōmi*, \"to hand over\" — the same verb used of Judas handing Jesus to his enemies, or of God \"giving over\" the rebellious in Romans 1. It's a decisive, aorist act of self-surrender, not a slow drift. \"Lasciviousness\" is *aselgeia* — a temperament that recognizes no restraint and dares whatever impulse suggests. And the phrase \"to work all uncleanness\" uses *ergasia*, \"a trade, a business\": moral corruption pursued the way a man pursues a livelihood, methodically, \"with greediness\" — *pleonexia*, an appetite that never reaches enough.","translation":"Who, being of such a nature as to have become past feeling, handed themselves over to unrestrained wantonness, to the working of every kind of uncleanness in the grip of greediness."},{"c1":4,"v1":22,"c2":4,"v2":24,"label":"Ephesians 4:22–24","text":"\"Put off\" is an aorist infinitive — a decisive, once-for-all action pictured as removing a garment, not a gradual shedding. \"Old\" is *palaios*, \"worn out, used up,\" not simply \"former\" — the old self is described as worn through. That old self \"is being corrupted\" — a present participle, an ongoing decay still underway right up to the moment it's stripped off — \"according to the passionate desires of deceit,\" deceit personified as the force driving the decline. Against that, \"renewed\" is a present infinitive: a continuous process, not a single event, worked on \"the spirit of the mind\" — the deep governing disposition, not just opinions. \"Put on,\" like \"put off,\" is aorist — decisive. And \"new\" is *kainos*, new in kind and quality, not *neos*, merely new in time: this self is a different sort of thing entirely, \"created according to God\" — patterned directly on him — \"in righteousness and holiness of truth,\" truth personified as the counterweight to the deceit of verse 22.","translation":"That you once for all put off, with reference to your former way of life, the old self that is being corrupted through the passionate desires of deceit; and that you are being continually renewed in the spirit of your mind; and that you once for all put on the new self, created according to God in righteousness and holiness that belongs to truth."},{"c1":4,"v1":25,"c2":4,"v2":25,"label":"Ephesians 4:25","text":"\"Putting away lying\" is an aorist participle — a completed act, \"having once for all put off the lie,\" tied straight back to the putting off of the old self two verses earlier. What follows is a present imperative: \"be speaking truth,\" a standing practice, not a single confession. And the reason Paul gives isn't civic decency but anatomy: \"we are members belonging to one another\" — a lie between believers isn't just a moral failure, it's one organ misinforming another inside a single body.","translation":"Therefore, having once for all put off the lie, be continually speaking truth, each one with his neighbor, because we are members belonging to one another."},{"c1":4,"v1":26,"c2":4,"v2":26,"label":"Ephesians 4:26","text":"Greek has three separate words for anger here, and Paul plays them against each other. *Thumos* is a sudden boil-up of passion, quick to rise and quick to fade — forbidden outright three verses later. *Parorgismos*, \"wrath,\" is anger shot through with irritation and bitterness — also forbidden, in this very verse. *Orgē* is different: a settled, abiding disposition, capable of righteous indignation under the right conditions — and this is the word behind the command \"be angry.\" The verb is a present imperative: keep on holding this settled indignation against what deserves it. The check comes immediately after: \"sin not\" is also present tense, \"stop sinning\" — a righteous *orgē* can curdle into something else if left untended, and Paul's deadline for dealing with it is nightfall.","translation":"Be continually indignant, and stop sinning. Do not let the sun go down on your irritated, embittered anger."},{"c1":4,"v1":27,"c2":4,"v2":27,"label":"Ephesians 4:27","text":"\"Neither give place\" is a present imperative with a negative — grammar that forbids the continuation of something already happening, not merely a fresh start: \"stop giving place,\" as if the door had already been left ajar. \"Place\" is *topos*, literally a patch of ground, here \"opportunity, room to operate.\" Unchecked wrath, Paul has just said, hands the devil exactly that — real estate inside a life.","translation":"And stop giving the devil room to operate."},{"c1":4,"v1":28,"c2":4,"v2":28,"label":"Ephesians 4:28","text":"\"Let him steal no more\" is built from a present participle plus a present imperative: \"the one who is stealing, let him stop stealing\" — addressing an ongoing habit, not a single lapse, among people already in the church. The remedy Paul offers isn't merely restraint but redirection: \"rather let him labor, working with his own hands\" — the same hands once used to take are retrained to produce — \"so that he may have something to share with the one who has need.\" A taker becomes a giver; the cure for theft is generosity.","translation":"The one who is stealing, let him steal no longer, but rather let him be laboring, working with his own hands what is good, so that he may have something to be sharing with the one who has need."},{"c1":4,"v1":29,"c2":4,"v2":29,"label":"Ephesians 4:29","text":"\"Corrupt\" is *sapros* — the word for rotten fruit or spoiled food, something decayed past use. Paul pictures speech itself as capable of going bad. \"Let it not proceed\" is a present imperative: keep such words from ever leaving the mouth, an ongoing vigilance rather than a single filter. The alternative is speech fitted \"for building up as the need requires\" — practical usefulness, aimed at whoever's actually listening — with the purpose that it \"give grace,\" *charis*, to those who hear: words meant to leave the hearer better off than they found him.","translation":"Let no rotten word be proceeding out of your mouth, but only what is good for building up as the need requires, so that it may give grace to those who hear."},{"c1":4,"v1":30,"c2":4,"v2":30,"label":"Ephesians 4:30","text":"\"Stop grieving\" is a present imperative under negation — not a blanket warning against ever grieving the Spirit, but an order to break off something already in motion. The rotten talk and bitterness Paul has just been listing aren't abstract rule-breaking; they're actively wounding a Person who is capable of being grieved. \"Whereby,\" *en hō* — literally \"in whom\" — locates the seal in the Spirit himself rather than in some separate mark he stamps on. He doesn't merely apply the seal; he is the seal, God's own guarantee stamped onto the believer. And that guarantee runs \"to the day of redemption\" — not the redemption already accomplished at the cross, but its final installment, the day the purchased body itself is set free.","translation":"And stop grieving the Spirit, the Holy Spirit of God, in whom you were sealed for the day of redemption."},{"c1":4,"v1":31,"c2":4,"v2":31,"label":"Ephesians 4:31","text":"Paul stacks near-synonyms to close every exit. *Pikria*, \"bitterness,\" is the resentment that sits and sours rather than passing. *Thumos*, \"wrath,\" is the quick boil-over — anger that flares hot and burns out fast. *Orgē*, \"anger,\" is the settled, simmering kind; note that this is the very word used for the legitimate anger permitted back in 4:26, but here it's forbidden — the same emotion is fine as righteous indignation and poisonous as raw human passion. *Kraugē*, \"clamor,\" is the outward eruption — shouting, brawling, anger gone audible. *Blasphēmia* is slander, speech built to injure. And \"put away\" is *airō*, which doesn't mean quietly setting something down; it means picking it up bodily and carrying it off. Nothing here gets tolerated in small doses — the command covers every stripe of each vice, root and branch.","translation":"Let all bitterness and quick-boiling wrath and simmering anger and brawling and slander be carried off from you, together with all malice."},{"c1":4,"v1":32,"c2":4,"v2":32,"label":"Ephesians 4:32","text":"\"Be\" is *ginomai*, \"become\" — Paul isn't asking the Ephesians to maintain a state, he's commanding a change of state, and the present tense makes it a continual becoming, not a one-time turn. *Chrēstos*, \"kind,\" is the direct opposite of the harshness just condemned. *Eusplagchnos*, \"tenderhearted,\" is built on the word for inward organs — compassion felt in the gut, not just performed. The real find is \"forgiving\": Paul doesn't use *aphiēmi*, the word for God legally canceling a debt at the cross, but *charizomai* — to extend a favor, to treat someone graciously regardless of what they've earned. And \"in Christ,\" *en Christō*, is location language, not motive language — it isn't \"for Christ's sake\" but forgiveness exercised within the sphere Christ creates, the same sphere in which God forgave them.","translation":"And keep on becoming kind to one another, tenderhearted, graciously forgiving each other just as God also, in Christ, graciously forgave you."},{"c1":5,"v1":5,"c2":5,"v2":5,"label":"Ephesians 5:5","text":"\"Ye know\" doubles up two different verbs of knowing. *Oida* is absolute, self-evident knowledge — the kind that needs no argument. *Ginōskontes*, from *ginōskō*, is knowledge gained through experience, through having lived it out. Paul isn't offering a new claim; he's reminding them of something they already hold both as settled fact and as something they've watched play out. *Pornos* is the word for one who trades his body for illicit use — a fornicator in the fullest, most physical sense. *Pleonektēs*, \"covetous,\" is someone perpetually reaching for more, particularly what belongs to others. And the sentence ends by redefining that greed theologically: the covetous man is an *eidōlolatrēs*, an idolater — because grasping after more than God gives is functionally worship of something other than God.","translation":"For you know this — you know it for certain and you know it from experience — that no fornicator or unclean person or covetous man, who is an idolater, holds an inheritance in the kingdom of Christ and of God."},{"c1":5,"v1":6,"c2":5,"v2":6,"label":"Ephesians 5:6","text":"\"Vain\" is *kenos*, \"empty, hollow\" — words that sound substantial but have nothing inside them, the kind of talk that dresses up self-indulgence as harmless. And \"children of disobedience\" renders *huios*, \"sons\" — a Hebrew idiom that doesn't mean people who disobey now and then, but people defined by disobedience as their controlling trait, the way \"son of thunder\" names a character rather than a single act.","translation":"Let no one keep deceiving you with empty words, for because of these things the wrath of God is coming upon the sons of disobedience."},{"c1":5,"v1":7,"c2":5,"v2":10,"label":"Ephesians 5:7–10","text":"\"Be\" is again *ginomai*, and the command — \"stop becoming joint-partakers\" — is a present imperative with the negative, meaning this isn't a hedge against a future risk but an order to break off a partnership already forming. \"Partakers,\" *sunmetochos*, doubles the idea of togetherness: *metechō*, \"to hold with,\" plus *sun*, \"with,\" stacked on top of each other. The verb \"were\" sits in the emphatic position — you *were* darkness, past and finished, which is exactly why becoming it again would be a regression. *Phōs*, \"light,\" names light itself, not a lamp that merely holds it; the Ephesians aren't just illuminated, they're identified as light in the Lord. \"Walk,\" *peripateō*, literally means ordering one's conduct, and the present imperative makes it a standing, habitual practice, not an occasional gesture. \"Goodness,\" *agathōsunē*, is active — doing good, not merely wishing it — and \"proving,\" *dokimazō*, is the language of testing something in order to approve it once it passes.","translation":"Stop becoming joint-partakers with them; for you were once darkness, but now you are light in the Lord. Be habitually walking as children of light — for the fruit of the light consists in every kind of goodness and righteousness and truth — constantly testing and approving what is well-pleasing to the Lord."},{"c1":5,"v1":13,"c2":5,"v2":13,"label":"Ephesians 5:13","text":"\"All things\" is *ta panta*, \"the all things\" — the Greek article points back to the specific secret sins just named, not to everything in general. \"Made manifest\" is *phaneroō*, to drag into visibility what had been hidden, and it's passive: things don't manifest themselves, light does the exposing. The logic runs in a circle worth noticing — whatever gets exposed by light takes on the nature of light itself; exposure isn't just detection, it's a kind of transformation out of the category of darkness altogether.","translation":"But all these things, when they are exposed by the light, are made plainly visible, for everything that is being made visible is light."},{"c1":5,"v1":14,"c2":5,"v2":14,"label":"Ephesians 5:14","text":"\"Give light\" translates *epiphauskō*, \"to shine upon\" — the picture is of dawn breaking over someone still asleep, light arriving from outside rather than something the sleeper produces himself. The quotation stacks sleep and death as parallel pictures of the same condition, and both commands — \"wake\" and \"arise\" — are answered not by the sleeper's own effort but by Christ's light falling on him.","translation":"Therefore he says: Wake up, you who are sleeping, and rise up from among the dead, and Christ will shine upon you."},{"c1":5,"v1":17,"c2":5,"v2":17,"label":"Ephesians 5:17","text":"*Aphrōn*, \"unwise,\" is stronger than careless — it names someone acting without the rational faculty engaged at all, senseless in the moment rather than merely mistaken. \"Understanding\" is *suniēmi*, built from *syn*, \"together,\" and the verb \"to send\" — literally putting perception together with the thing perceived, seeing how pieces fit rather than just registering that they exist. And once more *ginomai*, \"become,\" carries a present prohibition: stop an already-moving drift, don't just avoid a future one.","translation":"Therefore stop becoming those without reflection, but keep on understanding what the will of the Lord is."},{"c1":5,"v1":18,"c2":5,"v2":20,"label":"Ephesians 5:18–20","text":"*Methuskō*, \"be drunk,\" pictures saturation — the same word describes hides soaked through with liquid until pliable. The state in view isn't a single lapse but being permeated all the way through by wine's influence. \"Excess,\" *asōtia*, literally means \"un-saving-ness\" (*a-* plus *sōzō*, \"to save\") — a life with no rescuing quality left in it, spending itself toward ruin with nothing to show. Against that, \"filled\" is *plēroō* in the present imperative passive: not a single completed filling but a repeated, continuous yielding to control, moment by moment. The participles that follow — speaking, singing, making melody, giving thanks — describe what that continual filling looks like in practice, not separate commands but the shape the Spirit-controlled life naturally takes.","translation":"And stop being made drunk with wine, in which is dissipation, but keep on being filled by the Spirit — speaking to one another in psalms and hymns and spiritual songs, singing and making melody in your heart to the Lord, continually giving thanks for all things in the name of our Lord Jesus Christ to God the Father."},{"c1":5,"v1":21,"c2":5,"v2":21,"label":"Ephesians 5:21","text":"\"Submitting\" is *hupotassō*, built from a military root — *tassō* meant arranging troops in battle order under a commanding officer, and the prefix *hupo* adds \"under.\" In the middle voice, as it is here, the sense is self-chosen: not a rank imposed from outside but a position a person places himself into. The present participle keeps it a standing posture rather than a single decision made once and left behind.","translation":"Continually placing yourselves in submission to one another in the fear of Christ."},{"c1":5,"v1":24,"c2":5,"v2":24,"label":"Ephesians 5:24","text":"The connecting word is *alla*, the strongest adversative in Greek — \"but,\" not \"therefore.\" That matters: the verse isn't drawing a conclusion from what precedes, it's setting up a contrast. Christ is Savior of the body in a way no husband is; that difference stands. But the difference doesn't cancel the pattern — *hupotassō* again, middle voice, a self-given submission — because the point of the analogy was never that husbands equal Christ in every respect, only that the church's relation to Christ supplies the shape wives are to follow toward their husbands.","translation":"But just as the church submits itself to Christ, so also let the wives submit themselves to their husbands in everything."},{"c1":5,"v1":25,"c2":5,"v2":27,"label":"Ephesians 5:25–27","text":"The verb for the husband's love is *agapaō* — the self-giving love God is, the love shown at the cross, set apart from the other loves a husband might also feel (passion, fond affection, simple liking) by its willingness to spend itself for another's good rather than seek its own. \"Sanctify,\" *hagiazō*, means setting apart for sacred use, and \"cleansing\" rides alongside it as the means by which that setting-apart happens. The washing itself, *loutron*, is a bath — an act, not just a substance — and \"by the word,\" *en hrēmati*, is locative: the cleansing happens within the sphere the word creates, the word doing the purifying work water only pictures. The purpose clause that follows — \"that he might present the church to himself\" — uses a decisive, one-time verb: a future, single moment of presentation, not an ongoing process, the wedding day toward which all the cleansing has been aimed. \"Without blemish,\" *amōmos*, is sacrificial language — the term for an animal offered with no defect, faultless by inspection.","translation":"Husbands, keep on loving your wives just as Christ also loved the church and gave himself up on its behalf, so that he might sanctify it, cleansing it by the washing of water in the sphere of the word, so that he might present the church to himself in glory, not having spot or wrinkle or any such thing, but that it might be holy and without blemish."},{"c1":5,"v1":28,"c2":5,"v2":30,"label":"Ephesians 5:28–30","text":"Paul's hōs (ὡς, \"as\") in \"love their wives as their own bodies\" carries qualitative force — not \"in a similar way,\" but \"as being.\" The logic runs from the one-flesh union: the wife is, in a very real sense, an extension of the husband's own self, so love for her is nature, not mere duty. Verse 30 opens in the Greek with melē (μέλη, \"members\") thrown forward for emphasis — \"Members we are of his body\" — underscoring that believers are not merely associated with Christ but organically part of him. The clause \"of his flesh and of his bones\" is missing from the earliest manuscripts and is bracketed by most modern editors as a later addition drawn from Genesis 2:23.","translation":"In this way also, husbands ought to be loving their wives as being their own bodies. The one continually loving his own wife is loving himself, for no one has ever yet hated his own flesh, but is continually nourishing and cherishing it, even as the Christ does the church, because members are we of his body."},{"c1":5,"v1":31,"c2":5,"v2":31,"label":"Ephesians 5:31","text":"\"Leave\" translates kataleípō (καταλείπω), \"to leave behind, depart from\" — a decisive break from the family of origin. \"Joined\" is proskollaō (προσκολλάω), literally \"to glue onto\"; the prefixed preposition intensifies the plain verb into an image of adhesive, permanent bonding. And \"one flesh\" carries the preposition eis (εἰς, \"into\") in the Greek — not a static fact declared but a union arrived at: the two do not simply find themselves one flesh, they become it.","translation":"Because of this, a man shall leave behind his father and his mother and shall be glued fast to his wife, and the two shall come to be one flesh."},{"c1":6,"v1":4,"c2":6,"v2":4,"label":"Ephesians 6:4","text":"\"Provoke\" translates parorgízō (παροργίζω), \"to rouse to wrath, exasperate\" — the command targets a specific parental failure, needling children into resentment through harshness or favoritism. \"Bring up\" is ektrephō (ἐκτρέφω), the same root used of feeding an infant, now extended to cover the whole process of nurturing a child to maturity. The pairing that follows separates two methods of that nurture: paideia (παιδεία) is training by action — correction, discipline, structured formation — while nouthēsia (νουθεσία) is training by word, the spoken warning that addresses the mind directly.","translation":"And you fathers, stop provoking your children to wrath, but keep on rearing them in the discipline and admonition of the Lord."},{"c1":6,"v1":5,"c2":6,"v2":8,"label":"Ephesians 6:5–8","text":"Paul calls these believers douloi (δοῦλοι), \"slaves\" — real chattel slaves with pagan owners, not household employees in any modern sense. He labels their owners kurios (κύριος, \"lord, master\") rather than the harsher despotēs used elsewhere in the New Testament, then qualifies it \"according to the flesh\" — masters only in the earthly sphere, since the slave's true Kurios is in heaven. \"Fear and trembling\" is Paul's stock phrase, used elsewhere of himself and of the Corinthians and Philippians, for earnest, careful diligence rather than literal dread. \"Singleness of heart\" is undivided sincerity, set against eye-service — service rendered only under the master's watching eye, calculated to win favor rather than flowing from real goodwill. \"Heart\" here is actually psuchē (ψυχή), \"soul\": the service is to rise from the whole inner self, done as if rendered to Christ, with reward tracking the deed and not the social rank of the one who did it.","translation":"Slaves, keep on obeying those who are your masters according to the flesh, with fear and trembling, in singleness of your heart, as to Christ; not by way of eye-service, as men-pleasers, but as slaves of Christ, doing the will of God from the soul; rendering service with good will, as to the Lord and not to men, knowing that whatever good each one does, this he will receive back from the Lord, whether he is slave or free."},{"c1":6,"v1":9,"c2":6,"v2":9,"label":"Ephesians 6:9","text":"The command to masters mirrors the one just given to slaves — \"do the same things\" does not mean serving in return, but applying the identical Christian standard. \"Forbearing\" is aniēmi (ἀνίημι), \"to let go, give up,\" aimed at \"the threatening\" — the definite article points to a habitual, expected practice, the standing threat masters used to keep order. The reasoning is theological: master and slave answer to the same Kurios in heaven, so there can be no prosōpolēmpsia (προσωπολημψία) — literally \"receiving of face,\" judging by outward rank instead of the person's actual worth.","translation":"And masters, keep on doing the same things toward them, giving up your threatening, knowing that both their Master and yours is in heaven, and there is no receiving of a face with him."},{"c1":6,"v1":10,"c2":6,"v2":10,"label":"Ephesians 6:10","text":"\"Be strong\" renders a passive verb, endunamoō (ἐνδυναμόω) — not a summons to self-generated grit but an instruction to be strengthened, to receive strength poured in from outside, the way one puts on a garment. \"Power\" (kratos, κράτος) is strength as displayed and exercised, while \"might\" (ischus, ἰσχύς) is the underlying capacity, strength held in reserve. Paul stacks both to describe not just God's raw resource of power but its active, effectual working on the believer's behalf.","translation":"Finally, be continually strengthened in the Lord and in the active working of his might."},{"c1":6,"v1":11,"c2":6,"v2":11,"label":"Ephesians 6:11","text":"\"Put on\" is enduō (ἐνδύω), \"to clothe oneself with.\" The \"whole armor\" is panoplia (πανοπλία) — pas (πᾶς, \"all\") plus hoplon (ὅπλον, \"weapon\") — a soldier's complete equipment, not any one piece of it. The genitive \"of God\" marks source, and the real weight falls on the completeness of the set: armor supplied by God but taken up only in part still leaves gaps. \"Wiles\" translates methodeia (μεθοδεία), built from a verb meaning \"to follow a fixed method or system\" — not a random assault but calculated, systematic stratagem. \"Stand\" is soldier's language for holding one's ground under attack, the opposite of retreat.","translation":"Put on the full armor of God, so that you may be able to stand your ground against the calculated stratagems of the devil."},{"c1":6,"v1":12,"c2":6,"v2":12,"label":"Ephesians 6:12","text":"\"Wrestle\" is palē (πάλη), a specific athletic term for the grappling contest in which two opponents fight to pin one another — close, personal, all-or-nothing combat, a shift from the massed-formation soldier imagery that frames the rest of the passage. Kosmokratōr (κοσμοκράτωρ, \"world-ruler\") is a compound of kosmos (\"world, order\") and krateō (\"to hold power\") — naming spirit-beings whose reach is cosmic, not petty local troublemakers. \"Heavenly places\" here is the lower atmosphere ringing the earth, the domain Paul elsewhere assigns to Satan's forces, not the highest heaven where God dwells.","translation":"Because our wrestling is not against blood and flesh, but against the rulers, against the authorities, against the world-rulers of this darkness, against the spiritual forces of wickedness in the heavenly places."},{"c1":6,"v1":13,"c2":6,"v2":13,"label":"Ephesians 6:13","text":"\"Take up\" is analambanō (ἀναλαμβάνω) in the aorist imperative — a decisive, once-for-all command rather than an ongoing process: put the armor on completely, now, and keep it on. \"Withstand\" is anthístēmi (ἀνθίστημι), \"to stand against, resist\" — military language for holding a line under direct assault. The definite article before \"day\" singles out one particular day of testing, not days in general. \"Done\" translates katergazomai (κατεργάζομαι), \"to work out to completion\" — not merely surviving the assault but fighting it through to the end.","translation":"Therefore, take up the full armor of God at once, so that you will be able to resist in the evil day, and, having fought everything through to the end, to stand."},{"c1":6,"v1":14,"c2":6,"v2":17,"label":"Ephesians 6:14–17","text":"The verbs \"having girded,\" \"having put on,\" \"having shod\" are middle voice, not passive — the armor does not fall on the believer automatically; each piece is his own to take up. The girdle is \"truth,\" and its function is structural, not ornamental: it cinches everything else into place and frees the legs for movement. \"Righteousness\" as breastplate is the ongoing, lived integrity of the believer's conduct guarding the heart — not the once-for-all righteousness credited at conversion. \"Preparation\" (hetoimazō, ἑτοιμάζω) carried in classical usage the sense of a firm, settled foundation — the gospel of peace gives solid footing, not merely readiness to move. The shield, thureos (θυρεός), is the large, oblong, door-shaped shield covering the whole body — the word is built on thura, \"door\" — designed to catch and quench arrows dipped in pitch and set alight. The sword, machaira (μάχαιρα), is the short weapon for close combat, and it is identified with rhēma (ῥῆμα) — not Scripture as an abstract whole but the specific word of God spoken and applied in the moment of need.","translation":"Stand, therefore, having girded your loins with truth, and having put on the breastplate of righteousness, and having shod your feet with the firm-footing readiness that comes from the good news of peace; in addition to all these, having taken up the shield of faith, with which you will be able to quench all the flaming arrows of the evil one; and take the helmet of salvation, and the sword of the Spirit, which is the spoken word of God."},{"c1":6,"v1":18,"c2":6,"v2":18,"label":"Ephesians 6:18","text":"\"Always\" is en panti kairō (ἐν παντὶ καιρῷ), \"at every season\" — not a vague general disposition but prayer fitted to each occasion as it arises. Paul stacks proseuchē (προσευχή), prayer in general, with deēsis (δέησις), a specific plea for a particular need. \"Watching\" is agrupneō (ἀγρυπνέω), literally \"being sleepless\" — deliberate, wide-awake vigilance, the opposite of drifting into dullness. \"Perseverance\" is proskarteréō (προσκαρτερέω), \"to give unremitting, continuous attention\" — sustained effort, not a single burst. Turning the request toward himself, Paul asks for logos (λόγος), \"utterance\" — a specific word supplied for the moment — spoken with parrēsia (παρρησία), literally \"all-speech,\" the freedom to say the whole thing without holding back, fitting for a man making his case in chains.","translation":"Praying at every season, with every prayer and supplication, in the Spirit, and staying sleeplessly watchful toward this very thing with all unremitting attention and supplication for all the saints; and on my behalf, that utterance may be given me in the opening of my mouth, to make known with full freedom of speech the mystery of the good news."},{"c1":6,"v1":21,"c2":6,"v2":24,"label":"Ephesians 6:21–24","text":"Paul sends Tychicus not merely as a messenger but explicitly \"for this very purpose\" — a purpose clause naming the reason: so the Ephesians will know Paul's circumstances, and so Tychicus can parakaleō (παρακαλέω) their hearts, a verb meaning to comfort and strengthen by coming alongside. The closing benediction stacks three words with precision — eirēnē (εἰρήνη, \"peace\"), agapē (ἀγάπη, \"love\"), and pistis (πίστις, \"faith\") — asked from Father and Son together as a single source. The letter's final phrase, \"in sincerity,\" translates en aphtharsia (ἐν ἀφθαρσίᾳ), literally \"in incorruption\" — a love for Christ that does not decay, spoil, or wear thin with time.","translation":"But in order that you also may know my circumstances, what I am doing, Tychicus, the beloved brother and faithful servant in the Lord, will make everything known to you, whom I sent to you for this very purpose, that you may know our circumstances and that he may comfort your hearts. Peace to the brothers, and love with faith, from God the Father and the Lord Jesus Christ. Grace be with all those who are loving our Lord Jesus Christ with a love that does not decay."}],"Philippians":[{"c1":1,"v1":1,"c2":1,"v2":1,"label":"Philippians 1:1","text":"Paul and Timothy call themselves *douloi* (δοῦλοι), bondslaves — the word for someone born into slavery, bound to a master by a tie only death can break, with no will left but the master's. There is no article in the Greek: not \"the servants\" but servants by nature, by category of being. \"Timothy\" is built from two Greek roots meaning roughly \"one who honors God\" — a name the man grew into. The recipients are *hagioi* (ἅγιοι), \"set-apart ones\" — the same root gives us saint, sanctify, holy. Pagan Greeks used this verb of consecrating a building as a temple; Paul applies it to people, set apart from sin for God's use. \"In Christ Jesus\" is a locative of sphere — Christ is the space believers now live inside, the way a temple precinct marks off consecrated ground. \"Bishop\" (*episkopos*, ἐπίσκοπος) literally means \"one who looks upon, an overseer,\" a term borrowed from secular project-oversight. \"Deacon\" (*diakonos*, διάκονος) is the ordinary word for a servant defined by activity rather than status — the same word rendered \"minister\" elsewhere in Paul.","translation":"Paul and Timothy, bondslaves belonging to Christ Jesus, to all the set-apart ones in Christ Jesus who are at Philippi, together with the overseers and servants."},{"c1":1,"v1":2,"c2":1,"v2":2,"label":"Philippians 1:2","text":"*Charis* (χάρις), \"grace,\" named among the Greeks a favor granted freely, with no expectation of repayment — but always from one friend to another. Paul stretches the word past its ordinary reach: God's favor at the cross went out to His enemies, not His friends, which was unheard of in the word's normal use. *Eirēnē* (εἰρήνη), \"peace,\" carries an older sense of \"binding together\" — not simply an absence of conflict but a knitting of two parties, God and the believer, into one relationship.","translation":"Grace to you, and peace, from God our Father and the Lord Jesus Christ."},{"c1":1,"v1":5,"c2":1,"v2":5,"label":"Philippians 1:5","text":"*Koinōnia* (κοινωνία), \"fellowship,\" meant a joint-participation in a shared undertaking, not mere companionship — here, the Philippians' shared stake with Paul in spreading the gospel, from their first support of him up to the present. One small, telling detail: the Greek puts a definite article before \"now\" — literally \"until the now\" — a quiet pointer to the specific gift Epaphroditus had just delivered, which Paul is too tactful to name outright so soon.","translation":"I am continually thanking my God over your joint-participation in the gospel, from the first day until the this-present-moment."},{"c1":1,"v1":6,"c2":1,"v2":6,"label":"Philippians 1:6","text":"\"Being confident\" carries a causal edge in the Greek — Paul's thanksgiving flows out of a settled conviction, not a passing hope. The verb is the one used for having been fully persuaded: Paul has weighed the matter and reached a fixed conclusion that God, who started the work in them, will carry it through to completion. \"Until the day of Christ Jesus\" treats that day as near at hand, close enough to shape present confidence.","translation":"Being one who has come to this settled persuasion, that he who began a good work in you will keep on completing it, right up to the day of Christ Jesus."},{"c1":1,"v1":7,"c2":1,"v2":7,"label":"Philippians 1:7","text":"Paul says it is only \"right\" — a word meaning \"just,\" not merely fitting — to feel this way about them. His \"thinking\" is the kind that keeps turning practically toward someone's good, not idle sentiment. \"Defense\" translates *apologia* (ἀπολογία), a courtroom term for the speech an advocate makes on a defendant's behalf; Paul pictures himself defending the gospel before Rome's own tribunal. And grammatically \"my\" belongs with \"partakers,\" not \"grace\": the Philippians are Paul's co-sharers in this grace, partners with him specifically, not simply recipients of a grace that happens to be his.","translation":"Even as it is right for me to be thinking this concerning all of you, because you are holding me in your heart, both in my bonds and in the defense and confirmation of the gospel, you all being fellow-partakers with me of the grace."},{"c1":1,"v1":8,"c2":1,"v2":8,"label":"Philippians 1:8","text":"\"Record\" translates *martys* (μάρτυς), \"witness\" — the very word behind \"martyr.\" Paul calls God to testify, something he does only in his most solemn moments. \"Long after\" is *epipotheō* (ἐπιποθέω), the ordinary word for desire strengthened by an intensifying prefix — a straining, earnest longing. He locates that longing not in his own heart but \"in the bowels of Christ Jesus\" — the upper organs the ancients regarded as the seat of tender affection, what we would call the heart. Paul's longing for the Philippians is described less as his own emotion than as Christ's affection operating through him.","translation":"For God is my witness, how I am continually longing after all of you with the tender affections of Christ Jesus."},{"c1":1,"v1":9,"c2":1,"v2":11,"label":"Philippians 1:9–11","text":"\"Pray\" is *proseuchomai* (προσεύχομαι), prayer consciously addressed to a listening Person, not words tossed into the air. Paul asks that their love \"abound\" — *perisseuō* (περισσεύω), to exceed the ordinary measure, to run over. But an overflow needs banks: \"knowledge\" is *epignōsis* (ἐπίγνωσις), full, experiential knowledge rather than mere information, and \"judgment\" translates a word for trained, sensitive moral tact — the discernment to say and do the right thing, not just mean well. \"Approve\" is *dokimazō* (δοκιμάζω), to test something and then certify it genuine — the word used of examiners passing a candidate. What they are to test and approve are \"the things that differ,\" a phrase built on a verb meaning \"to carry in different directions\" — the finer distinctions of conduct, not the obvious moral basics. The goal is to be *eilikrinēs* (εἰλικρινής), unmixed and pure, and *aproskopos* (ἀπρόσκοπος), literally \"not stumbled against\" — giving no one else occasion to trip.","translation":"And this is what I am continually praying, that your love may keep on overflowing still more and more, in full experiential knowledge and all moral tact, so that you may test and approve the things that differ, in order that you may be pure and giving no one occasion to stumble, until the day of Christ, having been filled to the full with the fruit of righteousness which comes through Jesus Christ, to the glory and praise of God."},{"c1":1,"v1":12,"c2":1,"v2":12,"label":"Philippians 1:12","text":"\"Understand\" translates a verb for knowledge gained through experience, not secondhand report — Paul wants the Philippians to learn something by watching what has happened to him. \"The things concerning me\" is literally \"the things dominating me\" in Greek — his circumstances are not background noise but the ruling fact of his present life. \"Furtherance\" is *prokopē* (προκοπή), built on a verb meaning \"to cut in front of\" — used of the pioneer crew of woodcutters who hacked a road through forest ahead of an advancing army. Paul's imprisonment has not merely failed to stop the gospel; it has cut fresh road for it into ground the message could not otherwise have reached.","translation":"Now I want you to know, brothers, that the things concerning me have come to result rather in the pioneer-advance of the gospel."},{"c1":1,"v1":13,"c2":1,"v2":13,"label":"Philippians 1:13","text":"\"Manifest\" translates a phrase meaning \"have become plainly known\" — the reason for Paul's imprisonment worked its way through the ranks by word of mouth until it was common knowledge. \"Palace\" renders *praitōrion* (πραιτώριον), the Praetorian Guard — the elite imperial regiments whose barracks held Paul's rotating chain of guards. Each soldier who took his shift heard the gospel firsthand, chained wrist to wrist with the apostle for hours at a stretch.","translation":"So that my bonds have become plainly known to be in Christ, throughout the whole Praetorian Guard and to all the rest."},{"c1":1,"v1":14,"c2":1,"v2":14,"label":"Philippians 1:14","text":"\"Many\" is literally \"the majority\" — most, not all, of the believers in Rome had found new nerve to preach. \"Waxing confident\" comes from a verb meaning \"to persuade\": watching Paul's fearlessness in chains had persuaded them into a settled boldness \"in the Lord.\" But two very different motives divided these preachers. Some spoke out of *eritheia* (ἐριθεία), factious self-seeking — a term for the hired partisan spirit of a political operator — hoping to needle Paul's chains with jealousy. Others preached from simple goodwill, knowing Paul was \"set\" — literally \"appointed\" — to the defense of the gospel, wanting to lighten, not sharpen, his burden.","translation":"And the majority of the brothers, having been persuaded by my bonds, are becoming confident in the Lord to speak the word without fear, more abundantly than before. Some indeed are proclaiming Christ even out of envy and factious self-seeking, but others out of goodwill — these out of love, knowing that I am appointed for the defense of the gospel, but those out of a partisan spirit, insincerely, thinking to stir up trouble for me in my bonds."},{"c1":1,"v1":18,"c2":1,"v2":18,"label":"Philippians 1:18","text":"Whatever the motive, Paul asks simply, \"What is my feeling about it?\" and answers that Christ is being proclaimed either way — enough to make him rejoice. He then turns to his own outcome, using *sōtēria* (σωτηρία), \"salvation,\" in its broader sense of deliverance or well-being, not only eternal rescue. \"Earnest expectation\" is *apokaradokia* (ἀποκαραδοκία), a compound built on \"head\" and \"to watch,\" picturing someone straining forward with head outstretched, eyes fixed on one point — the classic image is a watchman scanning the horizon for a signal-beacon. \"Boldness\" is *parrēsia* (παρρησία), literally \"all-speech\" or free speech — courage that holds nothing back. Paul's hope is that Christ will be \"magnified,\" *megalynō* (μεγαλύνω), made great and conspicuous, in his body whether he lives or dies.","translation":"Now what is my feeling? Only this: that in every way, whether in pretense or in truth, Christ is being proclaimed, and in this I rejoice — yes, and I will keep on rejoicing. For I know that this will turn out for my deliverance, through your prayer and the supply of the Spirit of Jesus Christ, according to my straining expectation and hope, that in nothing I shall be put to shame, but that with all boldness of speech, as always, so now, Christ shall be made conspicuous in my body, whether through life or through death."},{"c1":1,"v1":21,"c2":1,"v2":21,"label":"Philippians 1:21","text":"\"For me to live is Christ\" — Christ is not a cause Paul serves from outside but the very sphere his existence operates within, an energizing Person animating his life from the inside. The infinitive translated \"to die\" is better rendered \"to have died\" — the tense points not to the act of dying but to the resulting state afterward. Death holds no sting for Paul precisely because what lies on the other side of it, not the act itself, is what he calls gain.","translation":"For to me, to be living — that is Christ, and to have died is gain."},{"c1":1,"v1":22,"c2":1,"v2":22,"label":"Philippians 1:22","text":"The strait Paul describes — hemmed in from both sides — uses a definite article in Greek before \"two,\" pointing back to the two options just named, life and death, each pulling with equal weight and holding him motionless between them. \"Desire\" is a strengthened word for passionate longing, not a mild preference. \"To depart\" translates *analyō* (ἀναλύω), used of a ship slipping its moorings or a soldier striking his tent — fitting language from a man who made tents for a living and was, at that moment, a prisoner in a military camp. \"To abide in the flesh\" pictures clinging to this present, inconvenient body rather than simply \"continuing to live.\"","translation":"But if to go on living in the flesh — this is the fruit of my labor, then I do not know what to choose. But I am hemmed in from both sides by the two, having the passionate desire to strike my tent and be with Christ, which is far better; yet to remain on in the flesh is more necessary for your sake."},{"c1":1,"v1":25,"c2":1,"v2":25,"label":"Philippians 1:25","text":"\"Confidence\" sits in the perfect tense — a settled conviction reached only after Paul turned the matter over fully in his mind, not a snap feeling. Having weighed it, he concludes the Philippians need his ministry more than he needs to depart. \"Continue\" carries the specific sense of remaining alive, and \"furtherance\" is the same word rendered earlier as \"pioneer-advance\" — Paul expects to go on cutting fresh road into their spiritual progress.","translation":"And having come to this settled conviction, I know that I shall remain and continue on with you all, for your progress and joy in the faith."},{"c1":1,"v1":26,"c2":1,"v2":26,"label":"Philippians 1:26","text":"\"Coming\" translates *parousia* (παρουσία), literally \"being alongside\" — the same word used elsewhere of Christ's own return, His personal presence among His people. Paul's promised visit to Philippi is described in the vocabulary usually reserved for the Lord's own arrival; his presence there will be the occasion, not merely the announcement, of their increased joy.","translation":"So that your rejoicing may keep overflowing in Christ Jesus because of me, through my coming to be present with you again."},{"c1":1,"v1":27,"c2":1,"v2":27,"label":"Philippians 1:27","text":"The word behind \"let your conversation be\" is not the ordinary word for conduct but *politeuomai* (πολιτεύομαι), built on the word for a city and its citizen body — the verb for discharging the duties owed by a member of a political community to its whole and its head. Philippi was a Roman colony proud of its citizenship, and Paul reaches for a word his readers would recognize instantly: live as citizens, discharging your obligations to your commonwealth — except here the commonwealth is heaven. The verb is middle voice, meaning the subject acts on itself: the saints are to hold themselves to this standard, not merely be held to it by another. \"Becometh\" translates a word meaning literally \"weighing as much as\" — their manner of life is to carry the same weight as the gospel they claim to believe, so their words are not emptied of substance by a mismatched life.","translation":"Only, be discharging your citizen-duty in a manner that weighs as much as the gospel of Christ, so that whether I come and see you or am absent, I may keep hearing about you — that you are standing firm in one spirit, with one soul contending together as a team for the faith of the gospel."},{"c1":1,"v1":28,"c2":1,"v2":28,"label":"Philippians 1:28","text":"\"Terrified\" pictures the fright of a startled horse, a sudden spooked panic. \"Adversaries\" describes people dug in, entrenched in opposition — not casual critics but committed opponents. \"Evident token\" is a legal term for proof established by an appeal to hard facts: the Philippians' calm under this pressure functions as courtroom-grade evidence, visible to their opponents, both of the opponents' own coming ruin and of the believers' salvation.","translation":"And in nothing being frightened by those entrenched against you — which is a clear evidence to them of their destruction, but of your salvation, and that from God."},{"c1":1,"v1":29,"c2":1,"v2":29,"label":"Philippians 1:29","text":"\"It is given\" translates *charizomai* (χαρίζομαι), the verb for God's free, gracious bestowal — the same word used of the gift of salvation itself. Paul applies it here to suffering: enduring for Christ is categorized with believing in Him as a grace-gift, not a misfortune. \"In the behalf of\" renders *hyper* (ὑπέρ), a preposition that elsewhere carries the sense \"in the place of,\" used of substitutionary action — suffering for Christ's sake is described in language that echoes His own substitutionary death, though believers plainly cannot share in that atoning work itself.","translation":"Because to you it has been graciously given, on behalf of Christ, not only to be believing in him, but also to be suffering on his behalf."},{"c1":1,"v1":30,"c2":1,"v2":30,"label":"Philippians 1:30","text":"\"Conflict\" is *agōn* (ἀγών), the word for an athletic contest — the root of our word \"agony.\" Paul frames his own imprisonment, and their trials, not as senseless suffering but as a strenuous, disciplined competition — the same event the Philippians had watched him run and now hear he is still running.","translation":"Having the same contest which you saw in me, and now hear to be in me."},{"c1":2,"v1":1,"c2":2,"v2":1,"label":"Philippians 2:1","text":"The \"if\" here is not hypothetical — the Greek uses a conditional form for a condition already met, better rendered \"since.\" Paul lists four realities as accomplished fact, each a ground for his appeal to unity. \"Consolation\" is *paraklēsis* (παράκλησις), a word ranging across exhortation, encouragement, and comfort depending on context — here it is Christ's own example functioning as an appeal to the Philippians. \"Comfort\" is a different word, *paramythion*, literally \"a word alongside\" meant to stimulate or console, carrying more tenderness than the first term. \"Fellowship of the Spirit\" is again *koinōnia* — the Philippians' joint participation with the Holy Spirit in a common interest, which naturally produces unity wherever believers actually yield to it. \"Bowels and mercies\" pairs tenderheartedness with the compassionate impulses that flow from it.","translation":"Since, then, there is a certain ground of appeal in Christ, since there is a certain tender persuasion of love, since there is a certain joint-participation with the Spirit, since there are certain tenderheartednesses and compassions."},{"c1":2,"v1":2,"c2":2,"v2":2,"label":"Philippians 2:2","text":"\"Fulfil\" means to fill completely full, not merely satisfy. \"Likeminded\" is literally \"thinking the same thing.\" \"Of one accord\" is, word for word, \"soul with soul\" — a fusion of sentiment, not just agreement of opinion. \"Of one mind\" restates the first phrase in stronger terms — Paul piles up near-synonyms for emphasis, driving the same point home three times rather than distinguishing three separate ideas.","translation":"Fill full my joy, by thinking the same thing, having the same love, being soul with soul, thinking the one thing."},{"c1":2,"v1":3,"c2":2,"v2":3,"label":"Philippians 2:3","text":"\"Strife\" is *eritheia* (ἐριθεία) again, the factious self-seeking already noted in chapter one. \"Vainglory\" combines the words for \"empty\" and \"opinion\" — pride resting on nothing substantial. \"Esteem\" translates a verb for a judgment reached by weighing external facts, not a feeling or sentiment — Paul asks for a considered conclusion, not a forced emotion. \"Better\" is literally \"having above,\" to excel or surpass — regard others as genuinely ahead of yourself, on the evidence, not merely as a polite fiction.","translation":"Doing nothing according to factious self-seeking or empty pride, but in lowliness of mind considering one another as excelling yourselves."},{"c1":2,"v1":4,"c2":2,"v2":4,"label":"Philippians 2:4","text":"\"Look\" translates *skopeō* (σκοπέω), to fix the attention on something with real interest and desire — the same root gives us \"scope.\" Paul is not asking for a passing glance at others' needs but a fixed, deliberate attention equal to what people naturally give their own interests.","translation":"Not each one keeping his attention fixed on his own interests only, but each one also on the interests of others."},{"c1":2,"v1":5,"c2":2,"v2":5,"label":"Philippians 2:5","text":"\"Let this mind be in you\" translates a single verb, *phroneō* (φρονέω), meaning to direct one's thinking toward something in a practical, active way — not abstract contemplation but a settled bent of mind that shapes conduct. Tellingly, the verb \"to be\" is not in the Greek at all; it is supplied by translators. Whether \"was\" or \"is,\" the point stands: Christ's habitual orientation, described in the verses that follow, is the pattern the Philippians are to keep adopting for themselves.","translation":"Keep on directing your minds to this, which was also in Christ Jesus."},{"c1":2,"v1":6,"c2":2,"v2":6,"label":"Philippians 2:6","text":"*Morphē* (μορφή), \"form,\" is not about shape — it is a philosophical term for the outward expression that flows from, and truly represents, someone's inner nature. Applied to Christ, it means His outward expression is, and always has been, a genuine expression of the essence of Deity. The verb \"being\" is not the plain verb \"to be\" but one indicating a condition begun in the past and still continuing — Christ did not stop expressing His divine essence when He took on flesh; both were simultaneously true. \"Thought it not robbery\" translates a word with two possible senses — \"a thing seized illegitimately\" or \"a treasure clutched and held onto at all costs.\" Given that the whole passage is about humility, the second sense fits: Christ did not regard His equality with God — His right to express the divine essence — as something to be grasped and defended at all costs.","translation":"Who, continuing to subsist in the outward expression of God, did not consider equality with God a treasure to be clutched and held onto at all costs."},{"c1":2,"v1":7,"c2":2,"v2":7,"label":"Philippians 2:7","text":"The verb behind \"made himself of no reputation\" is *kenoō* (κενόω), \"emptied\" — the term behind the theological label \"kenosis.\" The participle \"having taken\" is aorist, and in Greek grammar an aorist participle's action precedes that of the main verb: the taking of a servant's form came first and brought about the emptying, not the other way around. \"Form of a servant\" reuses the same *morphē* from verse 6 — Christ's servanthood was as genuine an expression of His nature as His deity had been; He exchanged one true expression for another rather than putting on a costume. \"Was made in the likeness of men\" uses an ingressive aorist, marking entrance into a new state — He became something He had not been. And \"likeness\" (*homoiōma*, ὁμοίωμα) means a real likeness, not an illusion — Christ's humanity was actual, not a disguise.","translation":"But emptied himself, having taken the form of a bondslave, entering into the likeness of men."},{"c1":2,"v1":8,"c2":2,"v2":8,"label":"Philippians 2:8","text":"Verse 8 shifts vocabulary: \"fashion\" is *schēma* (σχῆμα), an outward form assumed from the outside and perceived by the senses — the opposite of *morphē*'s inner-to-outer expression. Christ's deity and servanthood were both genuine self-expression; His humanity, by contrast, is described as something He was \"found in,\" the way an observer perceives Him. \"Obedient unto death\" uses a preposition meaning \"up to the point of\" — obedience carried all the way to, and through, dying. There is no definite article before \"cross\" in the Greek, throwing weight onto the kind of death rather than the specific event: death by crucifixion, the shameful method reserved for slaves and criminals.\n\nGod then \"highly exalted\" Him — a compound verb meaning raised to superlative rank — and \"gave\" Him THE NAME, the definite article marking a specific, supreme title, not just any name. Every knee bows and every tongue \"confesses\" — *exomologeō* (ἐξομολογέω), to declare openly, to agree with what is already true.\n\nIn the verses that follow, \"work out\" translates *katergazomai* (κατεργάζομαι), \"to carry through to a conclusion\" — not to earn salvation, but to carry an already-given salvation forward to its full outworking, the way a student works out a problem to its answer. \"Worketh\" is *energeō* (ἐνεργέω), our \"energize\" — God supplies both the desire (\"to will,\" rooted in emotion, not bare reason) and the power to act on it. And \"murmurings\" is *gongusmos* (γογγυσμός), an onomatopoetic word that sounds like what it means, the low cooing-mutter of discontented undertones rather than open dissent — the saints are called instead to \"shine,\" a verb about appearing, being visible, as *phōstēres* (φωστῆρες), the word for heavenly bodies like the stars.","translation":"But being found in outward guise as a man, he humbled himself, becoming obedient up to the point of death, even death by a cross. For this reason God also highly exalted him, and graciously gave him THE NAME which is above every name, that at THE NAME of Jesus every knee should bow, and every tongue openly confess that Jesus Christ is Lord. So then, my beloved, as you have always obeyed, keep on working out your own salvation to its full conclusion, with fear and trembling, for it is God who is energizing in you both the willing and the doing, for his good pleasure. Do all things without muttered undertones and disputings, that you may become blameless and unmixed, children of God without blemish in the midst of a crooked and perverse generation, among whom you are appearing as stars in the world."},{"c1":2,"v1":16,"c2":2,"v2":18,"label":"Philippians 2:16–18","text":"\"Holding forth\" is a word used in secular writing for offering wine to a guest — extending the word of life the way a host extends a cup. \"Rejoice\" here is not the ordinary word for gladness but *kauchaomai* (καυχάομαι), \"to boast\" or \"glory\" — Paul wants grounds to glory, not just feel happy, at the day of Christ. \"Labored\" means to labor to the point of exhaustion. Then Paul reaches for the vocabulary of pagan sacrifice: \"offered up\" is *spendō* (σπένδω), the drink-offering poured out over the main sacrifice as its minor accompaniment. He casts his own possible martyrdom as that minor libation, poured out over the greater offering — the Philippians' faith and *leitourgia* (λειτουργία), the priestly service, described with the very word used for the Levites' temple ministry.","translation":"Holding forth the word of life, that I may have grounds to glory in the day of Christ, that I did not run in vain nor labor to exhaustion in vain. But even if I am being poured out as a libation upon the sacrifice and priestly service of your faith, I rejoice, and I rejoice together with you all; and you too, be rejoicing, and rejoice together with me."},{"c1":2,"v1":19,"c2":2,"v2":24,"label":"Philippians 2:19–24","text":"\"Good comfort\" is *eupsycheō* (εὐψυχέω), literally \"well-souled\" — Paul wants his own soul heartened by news of them. \"Likeminded\" is *isopsychos* (ἰσόψυχος), \"equal-souled\" — Paul says flatly he has no one else in Rome whose soul matches Timothy's. \"Naturally\" carries the sense of \"genuinely, without pretense\" — the real article, not an imitation. \"Proof\" is *dokimē* (δοκιμή), the tested-and-approved character already noted in chapter 1 — Timothy's worth has been established under trial, not merely claimed. \"Trust\" in the closing line is the perfect tense of the verb \"to persuade\" — a settled conviction, not a fresh hope, grounded in the Lord's own faithfulness.","translation":"But I am hoping in the Lord Jesus to send Timothy to you shortly, that I too may be encouraged when I learn of your condition. For I have no one else like-souled, who will genuinely care for your condition. But you know his tested character, that as a child serves a father, he has served with me in the gospel. And I am persuaded in the Lord that I myself will also come shortly."},{"c1":2,"v1":25,"c2":2,"v2":25,"label":"Philippians 2:25","text":"Epaphroditus's name means \"charming\" or \"lovely\" — and Paul's description matches. \"Brother\" literally means \"from the same womb,\" a word for shared origin that Paul uses to put himself on equal footing with this otherwise unknown believer. \"Messenger\" translates *apostolos* (ἀπόστολος) — the same word rendered \"apostle\" elsewhere, here used of an ambassador sent on a specific commission, lending Epaphroditus's errand real dignity. And \"ministered\" is *leitourgeō* (λειτουργέω), the priestly-service word again — his care for Paul is described with the vocabulary of temple ritual, as though his kindness carried the weight of sacred duty.","translation":"But I considered it necessary to send to you Epaphroditus, my brother and fellow-worker and fellow-soldier, but your messenger and priestly minister to my need."},{"c1":2,"v1":26,"c2":2,"v2":30,"label":"Philippians 2:26–30","text":"\"Full of heaviness\" uses the same rare word applied to Christ's own anguish in Gethsemane — not homesickness exactly, but a deep unease, literally \"not-at-home\" distress. \"Nigh unto death\" pictures death as his next-door neighbor — that close. \"Not regarding his life\" translates a gambler's term, \"to stake everything, to risk it all\" — Epaphroditus had gambled recklessly with his own life for Paul's sake. \"Reputation\" is a word meaning \"to hold dear, to prize\" — Paul asks the Philippians to treasure such men, not merely tolerate them.","translation":"For indeed he was sick, near to death, but God had mercy on him. So receive him in the Lord with all joy, and hold such men in honor, because for the work of Christ he came near to death, having gambled with his life to make up for what was lacking in your service to me."},{"c1":3,"v1":1,"c2":3,"v2":1,"label":"Philippians 3:1","text":"\"Finally\" translates a phrase meaning literally \"as for the rest\" — not a sign-off, just a shift of subject. Paul warns them three times over against \"dogs,\" using the harsher Greek word for mangy scavenging street dogs rather than the gentler diminutive Jesus once used of household pets — aimed at the Judaizers. \"Concision\" is a deliberate pun on the word for circumcision, *peritomē* (περιτομή) turned into *katatomē* (κατατομή) — \"mutilation\" instead of the sign of the covenant, since they had reduced a spiritual sign to a mere cut in the flesh. \"Worship\" is *latreuō* (λατρεύω), the word for Israel's own service of Jehovah — startling when applied, as Paul applies it here, to Gentile believers serving God by the Spirit rather than the flesh. \"Confidence\" is again that settled, deliberated persuasion — Paul has reached his conclusion only after weighing the matter, and it is that he trusts nothing in the flesh at all.","translation":"As for the rest, my brothers, keep on rejoicing in the Lord. Keep a watchful eye on the dogs, keep a watchful eye on the evil workers, keep a watchful eye on the mutilation. For we are the circumcision, who serve by the Spirit of God and glory in Christ Jesus, and have no settled confidence in the flesh."},{"c1":3,"v1":4,"c2":3,"v2":4,"label":"Philippians 3:4","text":"Paul was circumcised \"the eighth day\" — the mark of a native-born Jew, in contrast to converts to Judaism circumcised as adults. \"Of the stock of Israel\" uses a preposition of origin, \"out of\" — tracing his line from Jacob, not Esau. \"Zeal\" would have carried an almost technical ring for his readers, echoing the fervent Zealot party among the Pharisees. And \"blameless\" describes the righteousness achievable by outward law-keeping — a real accomplishment by that measure, which is exactly Paul's point: he had more grounds for confidence in the flesh than his rivals, and discarded it all anyway.","translation":"Circumcised the eighth day, of the stock of Israel, of the tribe of Benjamin, a Hebrew of Hebrews; as to the law, a Pharisee; as to zeal, a persecutor of the church; as to the righteousness which is in the law, become blameless."},{"c1":3,"v1":7,"c2":3,"v2":7,"label":"Philippians 3:7","text":"\"Counted\" is in the perfect tense — a conclusion reached after deliberation and still standing, not a passing sentiment. And \"loss\" is singular in the Greek even though \"gains\" was plural: whatever separate assets Paul once tallied have collapsed into a single write-off.","translation":"But whatever things were gains to me, these I have counted, because of Christ, as one loss."},{"c1":3,"v1":8,"c2":3,"v2":8,"label":"Philippians 3:8","text":"Paul stacks up five separate Greek particles in a row to open this verse — \"yea, indeed, therefore, at least, even\" — a pile-up that shows the sheer force of his conviction more than any single word could. The verb \"count\" shifts to the present tense here: this is not a one-time decision but an ongoing, habitual stance. \"Knowledge\" is relational, experiential knowledge of a person gained through intimacy, not information about someone. And \"suffered the loss\" is a business term meaning \"to be fined, to forfeit as a penalty\" — Paul describes his old credentials as though a court had confiscated them, willingly surrendered for what he gained in exchange.","translation":"Yes, indeed, therefore, at the very least, I am continually counting all things to be loss because of the surpassing value of knowing Christ Jesus my Lord, for whose sake I have forfeited all things, and count them refuse, that I may gain Christ."},{"c1":3,"v1":9,"c2":3,"v2":9,"label":"Philippians 3:9","text":"\"Be found\" carries the sense of \"turn out actually to be\" upon observation — Paul wants his life, not just his claim, to demonstrate that he is in Christ. \"Know\" shifts to a tense meaning \"come to know\" — an ongoing entrance into experiential knowledge of Christ, not information already possessed. \"Power\" is *dynamis* (δύναμις), the word for a force capable of overcoming resistance — the same power that raised Christ from the dead, which Paul wants surging through his own life against sin. \"Made conformable\" reuses the *morphē* root from chapter 2's kenosis passage — Paul wants his life to become a genuine outward expression, matching Christ's, of dying to self. And \"resurrection\" here is *exanastasis* (ἐξανάστασις), a compound found nowhere else in the New Testament, \"out-resurrection\" — pointing to an ongoing spiritual rising Paul is pursuing now, not the future bodily resurrection already guaranteed him.","translation":"And be found in him, not having my own righteousness which comes from the law, but that which is through faith in Christ, the righteousness which comes from God on the basis of faith; that I may come to know him, and the power of his resurrection, and the joint-participation in his sufferings, being conformed to his death, if by any means I may arrive at the out-resurrection from among the dead."},{"c1":3,"v1":12,"c2":3,"v2":14,"label":"Philippians 3:12–14","text":"\"Attained\" here uses a verb for active appropriation — grasping something and making it one's own — distinct from the \"arrive at a goal\" word used just before it. \"Perfect\" means spiritually mature, not sinless — Paul uses it elsewhere in explicit contrast to spiritual immaturity. \"Follow after\" is *diōkō* (διώκω), \"pursue\" — the image of a runner streaking down a racecourse. \"Apprehend\" repeats the same root with a prefix meaning \"down\" — not just to catch, but to seize and pull down and make one's own, the way a tackler brings down and controls his man. \"Reaching forth\" pictures a runner whose eye outruns his hand, and his hand his foot — every part of him straining toward what is ahead. \"Mark\" is *skopos* (σκοπός), a target for shooting — Christlikeness is the target Paul has fixed his aim on. \"High calling\" is literally \"the calling from above\" — a summons issued from heaven, not a vocation chosen on earth.","translation":"Not that I have already appropriated it, or am already made perfectly mature, but I am pursuing on, if indeed I may seize that for which also I was seized by Christ Jesus. Brothers, I do not count myself to have seized it; but one thing I do: forgetting the things behind, and stretching forward to the things ahead, I am pursuing on toward the target, for the prize of the calling from above of God in Christ Jesus."},{"c1":3,"v1":15,"c2":3,"v2":15,"label":"Philippians 3:15","text":"Verse 12 used the verb form of \"perfect\" for an absolute, finished maturity Paul denies having reached; here he switches to the noun, describing a relative maturity — real, but with room still to grow. It is not a contradiction, just two different words doing two different jobs. \"Otherwise minded\" uses a word for diversity in a negative sense — some in the church had drifted into a different, mistaken way of thinking, and Paul simply hands that over to God to correct.","translation":"Let us therefore, as many as are mature, keep on being of this mind; and if in anything you are otherwise minded, this too God will reveal to you."},{"c1":3,"v1":16,"c2":3,"v2":16,"label":"Philippians 3:16","text":"\"Attained\" here is a different Greek word again, meaning to arrive at a point along a road — Paul's thought is progress-based: whatever ground has already been covered. \"Walk\" translates *stoicheō* (στοιχέω), literally to march in file, rank by rank — the picture is of a column keeping formation along the same path already reached, not wandering off it.","translation":"Only, as far as we have arrived, let us keep marching in step by that same standard."},{"c1":3,"v1":17,"c2":3,"v2":17,"label":"Philippians 3:17","text":"\"Followers together\" is *symmimētēs* (συμμιμητής), \"joint-imitators\" — Paul asks them to imitate him together, as a body, reinforcing one another. \"Mark\" is again the intent-attention verb from chapter 2. Those who make their \"belly their god\" reflects Epicurean self-indulgence — appetite worshipped as ultimate good, a swing from legalism straight into license Paul had already warned against.\n\nIn the closing verses, \"conversation\" shifts to the noun form of the citizenship-word from 1:27 — *politeuma* (πολίτευμα), \"commonwealth.\" \"Is\" translates not the ordinary verb of being but the same word used in chapter 2 for Christ's continuing existence — a fixedness; our commonwealth is permanently located in heaven, not merely visiting there. \"Look for\" stacks three components meaning \"receive,\" \"away from other things,\" and an intensifying \"out\" — a picture of eager, undistracted welcome, straining toward one object only. \"Change\" reuses the *schēma* root from chapter 2's \"fashion\" — an outward transformation. \"Vile body\" is literally \"body of humiliation,\" from the same root as \"humbled himself\" in 2:8. \"Fashioned like\" reuses *morphē* once more, tying our future transformation to the same vocabulary used of Christ's own self-emptying and exaltation. \"Subdue\" is a military term for arranging troops under a commander's authority — the universe brought into orderly formation under Christ.","translation":"Be imitators together of me, brothers, and observe those who walk according to the example you have in us, whose god is their belly, and whose glory is in their shame, who mind earthly things. For our commonwealth is fixed and existing in heaven, from which also we are eagerly awaiting a Savior, the Lord Jesus Christ, who will transform the body of our humiliation, to be conformed to the body of his glory, according to the working by which he is able even to subdue all things to himself."},{"c1":4,"v1":1,"c2":4,"v2":1,"label":"Philippians 4:1","text":"\"Dearly beloved\" and \"longed for\" are both plural adjectives built on *agapē* (ἀγάπη), the self-sacrificial love that is God's own character — Paul loves each of them individually with a love not native to himself. \"Crown\" is *stephanos* (στέφανος), the victor's garland awarded at the games or at a festival, woven of leaves or flowers — not *diadēma*, the ruler's crown. The Philippians themselves are Paul's winner's wreath, the proof of a race well run.","translation":"Therefore, my brothers, beloved and longed for, my joy and victor's garland, so stand firm in the Lord, beloved."},{"c1":4,"v1":2,"c2":4,"v2":2,"label":"Philippians 4:2","text":"Euodia's name means \"prosperous journey,\" Syntyche's \"a pleasant meeting\" or \"good luck\" — both names suggesting capable, well-regarded women, which makes their public rift more notable. \"Beseech\" is a strong verb, \"I exhort, I beg\" — repeated for each woman by name, a personal, almost pleading appeal rather than an apostolic command.","translation":"Euodia I exhort, and Syntyche I exhort, to be of the same mind in the Lord."},{"c1":4,"v1":3,"c2":4,"v2":3,"label":"Philippians 4:3","text":"\"Entreat\" carries the weight of a request backed by authority — a different register from the gentle \"beseech\" used on the two women; this man, whoever he was, could be given something closer to an order. \"Yokefellow\" translates *syzygos* (σύζυγος), literally \"yoked together\" — likely functioning as a proper name, a pun on his own character as one who pulls well in double harness. \"Help\" is *syllambanō* (συλλαμβάνω), \"take hold together with\" — the same verb Martha used asking Jesus to make Mary lend a hand. \"Labored\" is *synathleō* (συναθλέω), an athletic term for teammates contending together toward one goal, the same image used elsewhere in this letter for unified striving.","translation":"Yes, I ask you also, true yokefellow, help these women who contended together with me in the gospel."},{"c1":4,"v1":4,"c2":4,"v2":4,"label":"Philippians 4:4","text":"The command to \"rejoice\" repeats the same verb twice in a row, in the present tense — a standing instruction to keep on rejoicing continually, not a one-time feeling to summon up.","translation":"Keep on rejoicing in the Lord always; again I will say, keep on rejoicing."},{"c1":4,"v1":5,"c2":4,"v2":5,"label":"Philippians 4:5","text":"\"Moderation\" translates *epieikēs* (ἐπιεικής), a word better rendered \"sweet reasonableness\" — the willingness to be satisfied with less than one's due rather than pressing every legal right. \"At hand\" is *engys* (ἐγγύς), \"near\" — the nearness of the Lord's return is offered as the very reason such graciousness makes sense; there is no need to fight for every inch when the account is about to be settled.","translation":"Let your sweet reasonableness be known to all men. The Lord is near."},{"c1":4,"v1":8,"c2":4,"v2":8,"label":"Philippians 4:8","text":"\"True\" here means true in character broadly, not merely factually accurate. \"Honest\" is *semnos* (σεμνός), a word for what is venerable, dignified, worthy of reverence — the kind of thing that produces a noble seriousness in those who dwell on it. \"Think\" is *logizomai* (λογίζομαι), careful, deliberate reflection — a rare enough discipline in any age.","translation":"Whatever things are true, whatever honorable, whatever righteous, whatever pure, whatever lovely, whatever of good report — think carefully on these things."},{"c1":4,"v1":9,"c2":4,"v2":9,"label":"Philippians 4:9","text":"\"Received\" is the standard word for receiving instruction from a teacher — Paul is citing himself as the source of a tradition, not offering a mere suggestion. \"Do\" is *prassō* (πράσσω), practicing something as an ongoing habit, not performing it once.","translation":"The things you learned and received and heard and saw in me — keep on practicing these things, and the God of peace will be with you."},{"c1":4,"v1":10,"c2":4,"v2":10,"label":"Philippians 4:10","text":"\"Flourished\" translates a word used in the Greek Old Testament of a dry tree bursting back into bloom — the Philippians' care for Paul had not died, it had simply lacked opportunity to bud again. \"Careful\" here is a different Greek word than the \"worry\" of verse 6 — this one means simply \"to have in mind, to be thoughtful of,\" with none of the anxious connotation.","translation":"But I rejoiced in the Lord greatly, that now at last you caused your care for me to bloom again — in which indeed you were thoughtful, but lacked opportunity."},{"c1":4,"v1":11,"c2":4,"v2":11,"label":"Philippians 4:11","text":"\"Content\" translates *autarkēs* (αὐτάρκης), a term borrowed straight from Stoic philosophy, where it meant self-sufficiency, independence from circumstances. Paul takes the Stoic word and redirects it — his independence is not self-generated but Christ-dependent; he is sufficient because he is not relying on himself. \"Abased\" reuses the \"humbled\" root from chapter 2, now applied to Paul's own circumstances of want. \"Instructed\" is *mueō* (μυέω), the technical term for initiation into a pagan mystery religion — literally \"I have been let in on the secret.\" \"Full\" is a strong word normally used of feeding animals, \"to be satiated\" — Paul says he knows what it is to be filled to the point of an animal's fullness, and what it is to go hungry.","translation":"Not that I speak from want, for I have learned, in whatever circumstances I am, to be self-sufficient. I know how to be brought low, and I know how to have abundance. In everything and in all things I have been let in on the secret, both to be filled and to hunger, both to abound and to be in need. I am strong for all things in the one who keeps infusing power into me."},{"c1":4,"v1":14,"c2":4,"v2":14,"label":"Philippians 4:14","text":"\"Communicate\" is *synkoinōneō* (συγκοινωνέω), \"to become a fellow-partaker with\" — the Philippians did not just send a gift, they made themselves co-participants in Paul's affliction, sharing the weight of it alongside him.","translation":"Nevertheless, you did well in becoming fellow-partakers with my affliction."},{"c1":4,"v1":17,"c2":4,"v2":17,"label":"Philippians 4:17","text":"\"Desire\" is present tense, marking habitual character rather than a single wish — Paul is not in the habit of angling for gifts. \"Abound to your account\" borrows straight from the vocabulary of the money markets — interest accruing to a depositor's ledger. Paul frames their generosity not as a gift given to him but as an investment compounding in their own favor.","translation":"Not that I am seeking the gift, but I am seeking the fruit which keeps accumulating to your account."},{"c1":4,"v1":18,"c2":4,"v2":23,"label":"Philippians 4:18–23","text":"\"I have received\" opens with a stock commercial phrase — literally a receipt, \"paid in full,\" the standard first-century formula for acknowledging payment, likely with a touch of dry humor. \"I am full\" sits in the perfect tense: filled up in the past, and still, right now, fully supplied — Greek compresses that whole idea into one word. \"An odor of a sweet smell\" and \"sacrifice\" both come straight from the Greek Old Testament's language for the Levitical offerings — Paul dresses the Philippians' gift in the vocabulary of a sacrifice pleasing to God, not merely useful to Paul. And the same root behind \"I am full\" returns in his promise that God will \"supply\" all their need — the fullness they gave him will be repaid in kind, out of the far greater reserves of God's own riches in glory.","translation":"But I have all things paid in full, and abound; I am filled full, having received from Epaphroditus the things sent from you, a fragrant odor, a sacrifice acceptable, well-pleasing to God. And my God will supply all your need according to his riches in glory in Christ Jesus."}],"Colossians":[{"c1":1,"v1":3,"c2":1,"v2":8,"label":"Colossians 1:3–8","text":"Paul's word for \"praying\" is proseuchomai, built on the preposition pros, \"toward\" — it pictures prayer offered face-to-face with God, never used elsewhere in the New Testament for requests made to a person. The Colossians' faith rests \"in\" Christ (a preposition of settled position, not motion into), while their love reaches \"into\" the other saints — the difference is a faith already at home versus a love still pressing forward to embrace others. That love and faith are fueled by a hope \"laid up\" for them in heaven: the verb is a perfect participle of a word meaning \"to be stored away,\" picturing something deposited once and kept safe ever since, beyond the reach of any thief or enemy. The gospel itself, Paul says, is \"bearing fruit and increasing\" — both verbs are middle voice, marking an energy that comes from within the plant itself rather than being applied from outside, like a seed carrying its own capacity to reproduce.\n\nPaul's petition for the church turns to a specific word for knowledge, epignosis — gnosis with the prefix epi- added, intensifying it into a full, penetrating grasp rather than a surface acquaintance. It was a favorite term of the very teachers troubling Colossae, who reserved \"true knowledge\" for an inner circle; Paul prays that all the saints be filled with it, refusing to let it stay exclusive. Their conduct is to be \"worthy\" of the Lord — axios, literally \"of equal weight,\" conduct that tips the scale to match the character of the One they represent.\n\nBeing \"strengthened with might\" pairs a verb and noun from the same root (dynamoo / dynamis) — strength that gives actual capacity to act, not just theoretical power — measured \"according to\" the manifested strength of God's glory. Two different words render as \"patience\": makrothymia is patience worn toward difficult people, staying power that refuses to blaze up in anger at provocation; hypomone is endurance under circumstances, the grit that holds up under a long siege of trouble. \"Made us meet\" translates hikanoo, \"to render sufficient\" — it describes God qualifying believers for their share in the saints' inheritance by placing them, once for all, in a standing that already qualifies them.","translation":"I am continually giving thanks to God, the Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, praying for you, since we heard of your faith in Christ Jesus and the love you continually hold toward all the saints, on account of the hope stored away for you in heaven — the hope you heard of before in the message of the gospel's truth, which has arrived and is at home among you, just as it is in all the world, continually bearing its own fruit and increasing, just as it has been doing among you from the day you heard and came to know experientially the grace of God in truth. We have not stopped praying for you and asking that you be filled with the full, penetrating knowledge of His will in every wisdom and spiritual understanding, so that you may walk in a manner weighing as much as the Lord, pleasing Him in everything, continually bearing fruit in every good work and increasing in the full knowledge of God, being made powerful with all power according to the strength of His glory, resulting in all endurance and patience-toward-people with joy, giving thanks to the Father who qualified you for a share of the portion of the saints in light."},{"c1":1,"v1":13,"c2":1,"v2":13,"label":"Colossians 1:13","text":"The verb \"delivered\" is rhyomai, which pictures rescue by force — being drawn out and away by someone stronger, not a quiet transfer. What we're delivered from is \"the power of darkness,\" exousia — not lawful, ordered authority but its opposite, an arbitrary, tyrannical grip; darkness rules by domination, not by right. What we're delivered into is a basileia, a properly ordered kingdom under a rightful king, the sharpest possible contrast to the rule just left behind. \"Translated\" renders methistemi, the word used for the wholesale relocation of conquered peoples by ancient empires — moving an entire population from one territory and reseating it in another. Paul pictures the believer's transfer out of Satan's domain into Christ's kingdom on that scale: not a change of mood but a change of citizenship.","translation":"Who rescued us out of the tyrannical dominion of the darkness and resettled us into the kingdom of the Son of His love."},{"c1":1,"v1":14,"c2":1,"v2":14,"label":"Colossians 1:14","text":"\"Redemption\" translates apolytrosis, a term for release purchased by paying a ransom price — not a favor extended but a debt settled. Paul immediately defines that redemption as \"forgiveness,\" aphesis, literally a \"sending away\" — the same idea pictured in the Day of Atonement scapegoat, which carried Israel's sins out of the camp and out of sight for good. At the cross, sin isn't overlooked; it's paid for and then sent away.","translation":"In whom we continually hold our release, secured by the payment of a ransom — the sending away of our sins."},{"c1":1,"v1":15,"c2":1,"v2":15,"label":"Colossians 1:15","text":"\"Image\" translates eikon, a word that implies derivation from an original — not an accidental resemblance, the way two eggs happen to look alike, but a copy struck from a die, the way a coin bears the sovereign's likeness or a child bears the parent's features. Applied to Christ, it means He is God's exact, derived representation. \"Firstborn,\" prototokos, carries two ideas at once in Greek usage: priority in time and primacy in rank. It doesn't put Christ inside the class of created things as the first specimen; it places Him before all creation and over it — the eternal Son, not the first thing made.","translation":"Who is the derived likeness of the unseen God, having priority over and sovereignty over all creation."},{"c1":1,"v1":16,"c2":1,"v2":16,"label":"Colossians 1:16","text":"\"The all things\" — ta panta with the article, meaning the entire, collective universe rather than a scattered list of items — \"were created,\" first in the aorist tense, marking one definite, completed act at a point in the past. The preposition rendered \"by Him\" is actually locative, \"in Him\": creation didn't merely pass through Christ as a tool, it took place within the sphere of His person, as though His very being were the space in which the universe came to exist. A second appearance of \"created\" near the end of the verse shifts to the perfect tense — the act is past, but the result stands permanently: everything remains, right now, in a state of having-been-created by Him. Everything moves toward Him too, not just from Him — the same things that owe their origin to Christ also owe Him their destiny.","translation":"Because in Him were created the all things — the ones in the heavens and the ones on the earth, the visible things and the invisible — whether thrones or lordships or rulers or authorities; the all things stand created through Him and with a view to Him."},{"c1":1,"v1":17,"c2":1,"v2":17,"label":"Colossians 1:17","text":"\"He Himself is before all things\" — the pronoun autos is thrown in for emphasis where Greek doesn't require it, underlining His personal identity, while the verb \"is\" (not \"was\" or \"became\") states plain, timeless existence prior to everything that had a beginning. \"Consist\" is synistemi in the perfect tense — literally \"hold together\" — picturing Christ as the ongoing principle of cohesion that keeps the universe an ordered system rather than a chaos of disconnected parts. Whatever holds atoms together or keeps galaxies in their courses, Paul says, is an expression of His sustaining will, continuously exercised, not a force set going once and left alone.","translation":"And He Himself is before all things, and the all things continue holding together in Him."},{"c1":1,"v1":18,"c2":1,"v2":18,"label":"Colossians 1:18","text":"\"Beginning,\" arche, again doubles as both \"point of origin\" and \"governing source\" — Christ isn't merely first in a sequence of church history, He's the one from whom the church's life originates. \"Firstborn from the dead\" pictures resurrection as a kind of birth, Christ emerging from among the dead the way an infant emerges from the womb — the same image lies behind the phrase elsewhere about God \"loosing the birth-pangs of death.\" The verb behind \"might have the preeminence\" is a form of ginomai, \"become,\" not the verb \"to be\" used of His eternal deity back in verse 15 — a careful distinction: Christ is eternally first by nature, but He became head of the church in time, through incarnation and resurrection, entering that headship as a historical event layered on top of an eternal fact.","translation":"And He Himself is the Head of the body, the church; who is the origin, firstborn out from among the dead, in order that He might become — in all things — Himself the One holding first place."},{"c1":1,"v1":19,"c2":1,"v2":19,"label":"Colossians 1:19","text":"\"Dwell\" is katoikeo, built on the word for \"home\" with the prefix kata- added for emphasis — a permanent residence, not the temporary lodging pictured by a related word for sojourning as a stranger. Paul is saying that the full weight of deity doesn't visit Christ or rest on Him temporarily; it is permanently at home in Him, as native to His being as a family living in its own house rather than borrowed lodgings. \"Fulness,\" pleroma, is a term the false teachers at Colossae liked to use for the sum-total of divine power, which they imagined as parceled out among a chain of lesser spiritual beings. Paul's point is blunt: none of it is divided or diluted — the entire pleroma, undivided, is at home in Christ alone.","translation":"Because in Him God was well pleased that all the fulness continually make its permanent home."},{"c1":1,"v1":20,"c2":1,"v2":20,"label":"Colossians 1:20","text":"\"To make peace\" translates a verb whose root sense is \"to bind together\" — Christ's death doesn't simply end hostilities, it welds back together what sin had torn apart. \"Reconcile\" is compounded with the preposition apo, \"back\" or \"away from\" — restoration to an original, prior unity, not the creation of something new. Paul extends the reach of that reconciliation to \"things on earth\" and \"things in heaven,\" cosmic in scope, all of it secured through the blood of His cross — the single price by which both the estrangement of guilty people and the disorder of a groaning creation are addressed.\n\nVerses 21–22 pick up the same thread for individual believers. \"Alienated\" is a perfect passive participle — a settled, once-established state of estrangement, not a passing mood. \"Enemies\" carries an active sense: hostility in mind, not just outward hostility suffered. And \"present you holy\" pictures a searching, penetrating gaze — the compound word for \"sight\" builds \"look down into,\" the inspection of someone examining an object up close, and Paul says believers will stand that scrutiny blameless.","translation":"And, through Him, to reconcile the all things to Himself, having made peace by the blood of His cross — whether the things on the earth or the things in the heavens. And you, who once were in a settled state of alienation and hostile in mind because of your evil deeds, He has now reconciled in the body of His flesh through death, in order to present you holy and without blemish and beyond reproach before His penetrating gaze."},{"c1":1,"v1":23,"c2":1,"v2":23,"label":"Colossians 1:23","text":"\"Assuming\" translates ei with the indicative mood — not the hypothetical \"if\" that leaves the outcome open, but a conditional particle used with a fact already assumed true, closer to \"since\" than \"if perhaps.\" Paul isn't questioning whether the Colossians are saved; continuing in the faith is the evidence, not the condition, of a salvation already secured. \"Grounded\" is a perfect participle of a verb meaning \"to lay a foundation\" — a once-for-all act with lasting results: they were set on the foundation, and they remain set on it. Genuine faith doesn't need to be topped up by the new teachers' additions; it rests, complete, on a foundation already laid.","translation":"Since, as is the case, you continue in your faith, having been placed on a foundation with the present result that you stand firmly grounded, and are not being shifted away from the hope held out by the gospel you heard — the gospel proclaimed in all creation under heaven, of which I, Paul, became a servant."},{"c1":1,"v1":24,"c2":1,"v2":24,"label":"Colossians 1:24","text":"\"Fill up\" is a compound verb, antanapleroo, whose prefix anti- signals that what's supplied comes from a corresponding, matching source, filling a gap from the opposite side. The \"afflictions of Christ\" here is not the word Paul elsewhere uses of His atoning death — that term never describes His sacrificial suffering on the cross — but the suffering He endured in His earthly ministry: opposition, persecution, exhaustion in service. That kind of suffering was necessarily cut short by His limited years on earth, and Paul pictures himself and every faithful servant since as continuing to supply what that curtailed ministry left unfinished, in the ongoing work of building up the church, His body.","translation":"I am now rejoicing in my sufferings on your behalf, and on my part filling up what is lacking of the afflictions of Christ, in my flesh, for the sake of His body, which is the church."},{"c1":1,"v1":25,"c2":1,"v2":25,"label":"Colossians 1:25","text":"\"Dispensation\" is oikonomia, built from \"house\" and \"law\" — the term for a household steward's job of administering the master's affairs, not owning them but managing them faithfully on someone else's behalf. Paul describes his apostleship the same way: a stewardship entrusted to him, not a possession he earned. \"Fulfil the word of God\" doesn't mean merely finishing a task but giving the message its complete development — preaching it out to its full intended scope, Gentile and Jew alike, so nothing of its content is left undelivered.","translation":"Of which I became a servant, according to the stewardship of God given to me for you, to give full development to the word of God."},{"c1":1,"v1":26,"c2":1,"v2":26,"label":"Colossians 1:26","text":"\"Mystery\" borrows a word familiar from the pagan mystery religions, but Paul turns its meaning inside out. Where those cults guarded their secrets for a narrow circle of initiates, the Christian \"mystery\" is a truth once hidden that is now announced to everyone — the very opposite of exclusivity. It was hidden \"from\" the ages, not \"before\" them: concealment could only begin once there were people from whom to conceal it, so the hiding starts with humanity's history, even though God's purpose was fixed before the world began.","translation":"The mystery that has been kept hidden from the ages and from the generations, but has now been made known to His saints."},{"c1":1,"v1":27,"c2":1,"v2":27,"label":"Colossians 1:27","text":"\"Would make known\" translates thelo, \"desired\" — underscoring that God's choice to reveal this mystery to the Gentiles was sheer grace, nothing they had earned. The \"wealth\" language is deliberate: unlike a national religion with boundaries, this mystery overflows every barrier of race or caste, and Paul calls that overflow itself the glory on display. \"Christ in you\" is present possession; \"the hope of glory\" is what's still ahead — believers already hold the reality in seed form, but its full unveiling waits for Christ's appearing.","translation":"To whom God desired to make known what is the wealth of the glory of this mystery among the Gentiles, which is Christ in you, the hope of the glory."},{"c1":1,"v1":28,"c2":1,"v2":28,"label":"Colossians 1:28","text":"\"Preach\" here is katangello, \"announce\" — Paul isn't peddling a philosophy but broadcasting a fact. Alongside it stand two complementary verbs: noutheteo, \"admonish,\" addressed to behavior needing correction, and didasko, \"instruct,\" addressed to truth needing to be understood — warning paired with teaching the way repentance is paired with faith. \"Every man\" appears three times in one verse, a deliberate hammering against the Colossian teachers' claim to a higher wisdom reserved for the initiated few. And \"perfect,\" teleios, borrows language from the pagan mystery cults for the fully initiated as opposed to the raw beginner — except Paul insists every believer, not a select group, is granted that full initiation in Christ.","translation":"Whom we are continually announcing, admonishing every man and instructing every man in every kind of wisdom, so that we may present every man mature in Christ."},{"c1":1,"v1":29,"c2":1,"v2":29,"label":"Colossians 1:29","text":"\"Labor\" is kopiao, a term for the exhausting training of an athlete, and it primes the next verb, agonizomai, \"to contend in the arena\" — Paul pictures gospel ministry as strenuous, sweat-soaked effort, not passive waiting. The \"working\" that powers this labor is energeia (the root of our word \"energy\"), a term Paul reserves for superhuman power at work, whether God's or the devil's — here it's Christ's own energy operating in him, not human willpower stretched thin. Paul then extends the same athletic image, agon, \"the arena of a contest,\" to describe his prayer life for churches he has never even visited in person — an inward wrestling match fought on their behalf. He wants their hearts \"knit together\" — a term for weaving or binding threads into a single fabric — resulting in the full assurance that comes from full knowledge, epignosis again, \"of the mystery of God, Christ.\" The grammar puts \"Christ\" in apposition with \"the mystery,\" meaning the two are the same thing: the mystery isn't information about Christ, it is Christ Himself, in whom every treasure of wisdom is kept — hidden not because it's withheld, but because, like buried treasure, it rewards only those willing to dig for it.","translation":"To this end I am also continually laboring to the point of exhaustion, contending according to His energy, which is working powerfully in me. For I want you to know how great a struggle I am having on your behalf and on behalf of those in Laodicea and all who have not seen my face in the flesh, that their hearts may be encouraged, being knit together in love, resulting in all the wealth of full assurance of understanding, resulting in the full knowledge of the mystery of God, Christ — in whom are hidden all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge."},{"c1":2,"v1":4,"c2":2,"v2":4,"label":"Colossians 2:4","text":"\"Beguile\" translates paralogizomai, literally \"to reason alongside\" — not an outright lie but a subtly skewed argument, reasoning that runs parallel to the truth just enough to mislead. \"Enticing words\" is pithanologia, built from \"to persuade\" and \"word\" — smooth, plausible-sounding argument, the kind that convinces by the sound of its logic rather than the substance of it.","translation":"This I am saying so that no one may lead you astray by false reasoning, using persuasive, plausible-sounding speech."},{"c1":2,"v1":5,"c2":2,"v2":5,"label":"Colossians 2:5","text":"\"Order\" and \"steadfastness\" are both military terms — Paul, writing under armed guard in Rome, pictures the Colossians' faith as a well-drilled unit standing in formation, a solid front like a phalanx that can't be broken through. \"Received,\" in the next section, is paralambano, \"to take to one's side\" — not merely hearing information but personally appropriating it, making the teaching about Christ one's own. Two participles then trace two different kinds of growth: \"rooted\" is perfect tense, describing a once-completed act, like a tree taking root, whose result now stands fixed and firm; \"built up\" is present tense, ongoing construction that never finishes. And it happens \"in Him\" rather than \"upon Him\" — Christ isn't merely the foundation the building rests on, He's the space the whole structure rises within.","translation":"For even though I am absent in body, yet I am with you in spirit, rejoicing to see your orderly ranks and the solid front of your faith in Christ. So then, just as you took Christ Jesus the Lord to yourselves, keep ordering your behavior in Him — having been rooted, with the present result that you stand firm, and continually being built up in Him, and continually being made firm with reference to your faith, just as you were taught, overflowing with it in thanksgiving."},{"c1":2,"v1":8,"c2":2,"v2":8,"label":"Colossians 2:8","text":"\"Beware\" is a present imperative, \"keep constantly on your guard\" — an ongoing watchfulness, not a one-time alert. \"Spoil\" translates a verb used of carrying someone off as plunder or a captive slave — the false teaching doesn't just confuse, it enslaves. Paul's word for \"philosophy\" appears only this once in his letters; he isn't condemning the discipline broadly but \"his philosophy\" — a specific, article-marked system, \"empty deceit\" resting on paradosis, \"handed-down tradition,\" the kind of chain-of-transmission authority the Colossian teachers claimed instead of any grounding in Scripture. He calls its content \"elementary\" — the same word for a child's basic lessons — beneath the maturity Christ actually offers.","translation":"Keep constantly on guard, so that no one carries you off as plunder through his empty, deceptive philosophy — one resting on human tradition, on the elementary teachings of the world, and not on Christ."},{"c1":2,"v1":9,"c2":2,"v2":9,"label":"Colossians 2:9","text":"\"Dwelleth\" is present tense, katoikei, marking continuous, permanent residence, not a temporary visitation. \"In bodily form\" makes the point sharper still: the full weight of deity is at home in Christ specifically as one who possesses an actual human body, from His birth through His glorified, ascended state — not an abstraction hovering near Him but deity clothed in flesh. Greek actually has two different words behind our one English \"deity\" — one describes a general divine quality that reasoning from creation can dimly detect, the other describes full, personal Godhead. Paul reaches for the stronger of the two here: not that divine qualities radiate from Christ, but that absolute Godhead itself is what indwells Him.","translation":"Because in Him, all the fulness of the Godhead continually and permanently makes its home, in bodily form."},{"c1":2,"v1":10,"c2":2,"v2":10,"label":"Colossians 2:10","text":"\"Complete\" is a perfect participle of the verb behind \"fulness\" itself — literally, \"having been filled full, with the present result that you stand in a state of fulness.\" The logic runs: believers are first in Him, and being in Him, they are filled — not filled up in order to earn entry, but filled because they're already inside. Whatever the false teachers offered through their hierarchy of angelic mediators, Paul says believers already possess in Christ, who outranks every one of those powers as their Head.","translation":"And you are in Him, having been completely filled full, with the present result that you stand in a state of fulness — in Him who is the Head of every ruler and authority."},{"c1":2,"v1":11,"c2":2,"v2":11,"label":"Colossians 2:11","text":"\"Putting off\" compounds two ideas of removal — stripping completely out of a garment and getting entirely away from it, more than simply taking off a coat; it's disposing of it for good. The best manuscripts read \"the body of the flesh,\" not \"the body of the sins of the flesh\" — meaning the physical body itself, as it had been dominated by the sinful nature. That domination is what gets stripped away at conversion, not the sin nature's mere existence, which remains, but its controlling grip, broken and rendered powerless — the same point Paul makes in Romans when he says the body ruled by sin is \"rendered inoperative.\" This, Paul says, is the real circumcision — not a cut in the flesh removing a small piece of the body, but a spiritual operation removing sin's whole controlling claim, done through Christ rather than by human hands.","translation":"In whom also you were circumcised with a circumcision not performed by human hands, in the stripping off and away from yourselves of the body of the flesh, by the circumcision that belongs to Christ."},{"c1":2,"v1":12,"c2":2,"v2":12,"label":"Colossians 2:12","text":"\"Buried with Him\" and \"raised with Him\" both build on the picture in Romans 6 — believers are placed into identification with Christ's death and resurrection through the Spirit's baptism, so that the power of sin is broken and a new, divine life is imparted. The \"rising\" in view here isn't the future bodily resurrection but a present, spiritual one — the same shift from spiritual death to spiritual life this letter has already described in chapter 1. That transfer happens through faith in the working of God — the same phrase used elsewhere for the display of power that raised Christ's actual body from the tomb, applied now to what happens inside a believing sinner.","translation":"Having been buried with Him in that placing-into, in which placing-into you were also raised with Him, through your faith in the working of the God who raised Him out from among the dead."},{"c1":2,"v1":13,"c2":2,"v2":13,"label":"Colossians 2:13","text":"\"Sins\" is paraptoma, \"a fall to the side\" — a stumble off the intended path, in the dative case here, meaning the Colossians were dead \"with reference to\" their trespasses, not merely guilty of them. Their \"uncircumcision\" is named alongside their trespasses not for its literal, physical sense but as a symbol — the outward mark of an unchastened, ungoverned inner nature. \"Quickened together\" is one compound verb, \"made alive together with\" — the timing matters: this new life wasn't given piecemeal but all at once, in identification with the same historical moment Christ Himself was raised.","translation":"And you, though dead with reference to your trespasses and the uncircumcision of your flesh, He made alive together with Him, having graciously forgiven us all our trespasses."},{"c1":2,"v1":14,"c2":2,"v2":14,"label":"Colossians 2:14","text":"\"Blotting out\" is a verb for erasing writing — wiping a wax tablet clean, not merely crossing it out but removing the record entirely. \"Handwriting\" is cheirographon, the technical term for a signed IOU, a bond of debt standing against the debtor until it's paid or cancelled. Paul pictures the law's claim on humanity as exactly this kind of legal debt-instrument, and says God didn't just discount it — He removed it \"out of the midst,\" out of the place where a debtor's obligation would otherwise sit perpetually in view, embarrassing him at every turn — by nailing the actual document to the cross alongside Christ.","translation":"Having wiped out the bond of debt consisting of decrees — the one that stood against us and was directly opposed to us — He has taken it completely out of the way, having nailed it to the cross."},{"c1":2,"v1":15,"c2":2,"v2":15,"label":"Colossians 2:15","text":"\"Stripped off\" reuses the same verb from 2:11 for putting off the old self, now applied to the powers of darkness — Christ strips them the way a man strips off and discards a garment, then puts them on public display, a verb for publicly exhibiting something in shame, all pictured as a Roman triumphal procession, with the defeated powers marched behind the victor's chariot.\n\nThe next section names \"eating,\" \"drinking,\" \"festival,\" \"new moon,\" \"Sabbath\" — the Greek nouns are the acts themselves, the eating, the drinking, rather than food and drink as substances, pointing at ascetic self-denial rather than dietary law. Paul calls the whole system \"a shadow of things to come\" against \"the body,\" meaning the substance — the reality the shadow only sketched has arrived in Christ, so going back to the shadow after the substance has appeared is backward.\n\n\"Beguile of reward\" is a term from athletic judging — an umpire disqualifying a competitor and declaring him unworthy of the prize; the false teachers were setting themselves up as judges over other believers' spiritual standing. Their self-styled \"humility\" is undercut by the very next word, \"delighting in\" — a self-conscious, self-imposed performance rather than genuine lowliness, tangled up with a fixation on visions they claimed to have scrutinized minutely. Paul's sharpest jab is grammatical: the Greek word for \"Head\" is feminine, but the pronoun that follows it is masculine — breaking normal agreement on purpose, forcing the reader to hear \"Head\" as a person, not an abstraction, and that person is Christ, from whom the whole body — through its joints and ligaments — draws nourishment and grows with a growth that comes from God, not from angelic mediators.","translation":"Having stripped off and away from Himself the rulers and authorities, He made a public display of them, leading them in triumphal procession. Therefore let no one judge you in the matter of eating or drinking, or with respect to a festival, a new moon, or a sabbath day — these are a shadow of what was coming, but the body, the substance, belongs to Christ. Let no one who delights in a self-imposed humility and the worship of angels disqualify you from your prize, going on at length about visions he has seen, being pointlessly puffed up by his fleshly mind, and not holding fast to the Head — from whom the whole body, being supplied and knit together through its joints and ligaments, grows with the growth that God supplies."},{"c1":2,"v1":20,"c2":2,"v2":23,"label":"Colossians 2:20–23","text":"\"If ye be dead\" isn't a hypothetical — the conditional particle plus indicative mood marks a fact already settled: \"since, as is the case, you died with Christ.\" Having already died to these elemental teachings, Paul asks, why submit to them as though still alive under their rule? The prohibitions themselves stack up: \"touch\" pictures a deliberate, clinging contact, not an accidental brush, while \"handle\" describes only superficial contact — even a light, passing touch is forbidden by these rules. All of it, Paul notes dryly, concerns things \"destined for corruption\" in the very act of being used up — perishable stuff, hardly worth this much anxiety. The whole system is \"self-devised worship\" — a compound word meaning worship one invents and imposes on oneself rather than what God actually requires — paired with harsh, unsparing treatment of the body that has, Paul says bluntly, no actual value as a check on sensual indulgence. Ascetic severity and self-indulgence turn out to be the same problem wearing different clothes.","translation":"Since, as is the case, you died with Christ, away from the elemental teachings of the world, why, as though still living in the world, do you let yourselves be subjected to decrees: Do not touch, do not taste, do not handle — all of which things are destined for decay in their very using — decrees according to the precepts and teachings of men? These things carry a reputation for wisdom in their self-devised worship, their affected humility, and their unsparing severity toward the body — but they hold no actual value against the indulgence of the flesh."},{"c1":3,"v1":2,"c2":3,"v2":4,"label":"Colossians 3:2–4","text":"\"Set your affection\" isn't just an emotional preference but phroneo, \"direct your mind toward\" — Paul already told them to seek things above; now he adds that they must think that way too, not merely act that way occasionally. \"Ye are dead\" is aorist, a completed past fact, not an ongoing process — the break from the old life happened, once, at conversion. Their new \"life\" is described as hidden — not absent, just currently concealed, the way a seed is fully alive underground before anything shows above the surface. And Paul doesn't merely say Christ gives that life; he says Christ is our life — identifying the believer's existence with Christ's own so closely that when He is revealed, so are they, sharing in the same glory.","translation":"Keep your mind set on the things above, not on the things on the earth. For you died, and your life has been hidden with Christ in God. Whenever Christ, who is our life, is made visible, then you also will be made visible with Him in glory."},{"c1":3,"v1":5,"c2":3,"v2":7,"label":"Colossians 3:5–7","text":"\"Mortify\" is nekroo, \"put to death\" — an obsolete-sounding English word for a blunt Greek verb meaning to drain something of all power, to kill it outright rather than merely restrain it. The list that follows names the body's members not as body parts themselves but as instruments turned toward sin — sexual immorality in general, moral impurity, the filth of an indulgent life, depraved passion, evil desire, and covetousness, which Paul flatly calls idolatry — worship redirected from God to the desire for more. All of it describes how these believers \"once walked\" — their entire manner of life used to be bounded by this list, with nothing in it that pleased God.","translation":"Put to death, once for all, your members that belong to the earth: sexual immorality, impurity, depraved passion, evil desire, and the greed that amounts to idolatry — because of these things the wrath of God is coming. In these very things you also once walked, when you were living in them."},{"c1":3,"v1":8,"c2":3,"v2":8,"label":"Colossians 3:8","text":"\"Anger\" and \"wrath\" name two different tempers, not the same sin twice: the first is the slow-burning, settled grudge that nurses a plan of revenge, while the second is the sudden boil-over, a flash flood of temper. \"Malice\" adds active ill-will, the desire to actually harm someone; \"blasphemy\" here means slander against another person's reputation, not only against God; \"filthy communication\" is specifically obscene, foul-mouthed speech. Four words, four distinct ways anger leaks out — cold, hot, malicious, and coarse.","translation":"But now put away, all of it, once for all: settled, vengeful anger, sudden bursts of wrath, malice, slander, obscene talk out of your mouth."},{"c1":3,"v1":9,"c2":3,"v2":11,"label":"Colossians 3:9–11","text":"\"Stop lying\" uses a present imperative with a negative — grammar for halting an action already in progress, not warning against a future one; these believers were still lying to each other and needed to quit. The \"old man\" gets \"stripped off\" with the same forceful verb used earlier for stripping off the body dominated by sin — a decisive, once-for-all removal, not a gradual fade. The \"new man\" is \"constantly being renewed\" — present tense, ongoing — after the image of the Creator, echoing Genesis without claiming to be a second Genesis: as the first man bore God's image at creation, the new man bears it again in re-creation. And where that renewal has taken hold, Paul says there simply \"cannot be\" distinctions of race, culture, or class — not merely that such distinctions don't matter but that they have become impossible categories in Christ: Greek, Jew, barbarian, slave, free — categories that once divided the whole ancient world into privileged and unprivileged, all rendered void.\n\n\"Put on\" then shifts to a command in the aorist — do it at once, not gradually — clothing yourselves in the temperament fitting for God's \"chosen ones,\" a word implying selection out of the crowd. The list of virtues that follows draws on a Hebrew sense of the \"inward parts\" as the seat of tender affection rather than violent passion — compassion, kindness, humility, meekness, the willingness to accept even hard treatment without fighting back, and patience toward difficult people. \"Forgiving\" shares its root with the word for grace itself — to forgive is to extend the same kind of favor God has already extended, in the same measure He extended it.","translation":"Stop lying to one another, since you have stripped off the old self with its practices, and have clothed yourselves with the new self, which is continually being renewed to a full knowledge, according to the image of the One who created it — a state in which there cannot be Greek and Jew, circumcised and uncircumcised, barbarian, Scythian, slave, free, but Christ is all things and in all. Put on, then, as God's chosen ones, holy and beloved: a heart of compassion, kindness, humility, meekness, patience, bearing with one another and freely forgiving one another, if anyone has a complaint against another — just as, and to the extent that, the Lord forgave you, so also you must forgive."},{"c1":3,"v1":14,"c2":3,"v2":14,"label":"Colossians 3:14","text":"\"Above all these\" pictures love as the outer garment thrown over everything already put on — not one virtue among many but the sash that holds the rest in place. \"Bond\" is a word for a binding cord or ligament, and \"perfectness\" describes not sinless flawlessness but completeness — the result you get only when every virtue is bound together into one working whole rather than scattered pieces. Without love, Paul implies, the other virtues stay loose and disconnected, however good each looks on its own.","translation":"And upon all these, put on love, which is the binding cord of completeness."},{"c1":3,"v1":15,"c2":3,"v2":15,"label":"Colossians 3:15","text":"\"Rule\" translates a term from athletic contests for an umpire's decision — Paul pictures \"the peace of Christ\" stepping into the tug-of-war among competing impulses in the heart and calling the decisive verdict, the way an umpire settles a disputed play. That peace isn't only personal; it's the peace \"into which you were called in one body\" — corporate, binding the whole church together, not just each believer's private calm.\n\nThe \"word of Christ\" is to \"dwell\" richly — the same word used earlier for deity being \"at home\" in Christ, now applied to Scripture taking up permanent residence in the believer, richly, not sparsely. Paul distinguishes three overlapping categories of song — one leaning toward the Psalms of David, one toward newly composed Christian praise, and a general umbrella term covering any spiritual song — while singing \"with grace,\" the definite article marking out a specific, Spirit-supplied grace rather than singing talent, since the Greek word for grace doubles as the word for thanksgiving.","translation":"And let the peace of Christ act as umpire in your hearts — the peace to which you were also called in one body. And be continually thankful. Let the word of Christ make its home in you richly, teaching and admonishing one another in all wisdom, with psalms, hymns, and spiritual songs, singing with grace in your hearts to God. And whatever you do, in word or deed, do it all in the name of the Lord Jesus, continually giving thanks to God the Father through Him."},{"c1":3,"v1":18,"c2":3,"v2":22,"label":"Colossians 3:18–22","text":"\"Fitting\" appears in the imperfect tense — \"was fitting,\" pointing back to the very moment these believers entered the Christian life, as if the obligation were self-evident from the start rather than newly imposed. For husbands, the command is to love with the word for God's own self-giving love, not the ordinary affection that brought them to the altar in the first place — this is love as self-denial, the kind shown at the cross, deliberately distinguished from mere fondness. \"Provoke\" toward children is a word for irritation that, left unchecked, breeds a settled discouragement — Paul traces cause and effect: needless exasperation produces disheartened kids. \"Eyeservice\" is one compound word for work done only under a watching eye — service calibrated to whether the boss is looking — set against \"singleness of heart,\" undivided effort whether observed or not.","translation":"Wives, keep yourselves in subjection to your husbands, as is fitting in the Lord. Husbands, love your wives with a self-giving love, and do not be embittered against them. Children, obey your parents in everything, for this is well-pleasing in the Lord. Fathers, do not provoke your children, so they do not lose heart. Slaves, obey your earthly masters in everything, not with eye-service, as people-pleasers, but with an undivided heart, fearing the Lord."},{"c1":3,"v1":23,"c2":3,"v2":25,"label":"Colossians 3:23–25","text":"\"Whatever you do\" uses two different Greek verbs for \"do\" in the same sentence — the first a generic word for performing an action, the second specifically \"labor, work hard,\" opposed to idleness. Paul isn't asking for mere compliance but diligence, done \"from the soul,\" heart-deep rather than surface-level. \"Reward\" is a requital paid back in kind, and the phrase \"of the inheritance\" functions as an equation — the reward is the inheritance, not a bonus alongside it. Masters get their own coda in the next verse: what they owe their slaves is \"equity\" — not literal sameness of social condition but the fair, even-handed treatment due a fellow believer, since the master too answers to a Master in heaven.","translation":"Whatever you do, work at it heartily, as for the Lord and not for men, knowing that from the Lord you will receive back the inheritance as your reward. It is the Lord Christ you are serving. For the wrongdoer will be paid back for the wrong he has done, and there is no favoritism. Masters, grant your slaves what is just and fair, knowing that you too have a Master in heaven."},{"c1":4,"v1":2,"c2":4,"v2":4,"label":"Colossians 4:2–4","text":"\"Continue\" is proskartereo, unremitting, persistent attention — not occasional prayer but a habit sustained without letting up. Paired with it, \"watch\" warns against the specific danger of long, sustained prayer going slack — staying spiritually alert even while praying at length, since drowsy prayer easily becomes empty repetition. Paul's request for himself is for \"a door of the word\" — a specific opening to preach, not vague success — appropriate for a man who describes his imprisonment with a perfect-tense verb: \"I have been bound, with the present result that I remain in a bound state,\" a chain that was, at the moment of writing, still literally attached to his wrist.","translation":"Devote yourselves to prayer with unremitting attention, staying alert in it with thanksgiving, praying at the same time also for us, that God would open to us a door for the word, to speak the mystery of Christ, on account of which I have been bound and remain bound, that I may make it clear, as it is necessary for me to speak."},{"c1":4,"v1":5,"c2":4,"v2":5,"label":"Colossians 4:5","text":"\"Redeeming the time\" pictures buying up an opportunity before it's gone — a marketplace verb, \"to purchase for oneself,\" and \"time\" here is kairos, not the ordinary word for duration but a strategic, ripe moment. Paul's exhortation is toward \"those without,\" outsiders to the faith — wise conduct toward them is itself a kind of shrewd buying, seizing brief windows to do good before they close.","translation":"Order your behavior wisely toward those outside, buying up for yourselves every strategic opportunity."},{"c1":4,"v1":6,"c2":4,"v2":6,"label":"Colossians 4:6","text":"\"Seasoned with salt\" carries a double sense in the ancient world — salt both flavors food, making it palatable and pleasant, and preserves it from spoiling. Paul's picture of speech \"seasoned with salt\" is speech that is both agreeable to hear and free of corruption — pleasant without being rotten underneath. The goal is knowing how to answer \"each one\" individually — not a stock response but conversation tailored to the person in front of you.","translation":"Let your speech always be gracious, seasoned throughout with salt, so that you may know how it is necessary for you to answer each person."},{"c1":4,"v1":7,"c2":4,"v2":9,"label":"Colossians 4:7–9","text":"\"Fellow-servant,\" literally \"fellow-bondslave,\" is Paul's way of placing Tychicus on the same level as himself before Christ — no hierarchy, both owned by the same Master. \"I have sent\" is what grammarians call the epistolary aorist: Paul writes from the vantage point of his readers, describing the sending as already complete because by the time they read the letter, it will be. Onesimus travels with Tychicus carrying his own quiet transformation in the sentence structure — once known to the Colossians only as a runaway slave, he's introduced here simply as \"the faithful and beloved brother, one of you,\" his old status left entirely unmentioned.","translation":"Tychicus will make known to you all my circumstances — the beloved brother, faithful servant, and fellow-bondslave in the Lord. I have sent him to you for this very purpose, that you may know how we are, and that he may encourage your hearts — together with Onesimus, the faithful and beloved brother, who is one of you. They will make known to you everything that is happening here."},{"c1":4,"v1":14,"c2":4,"v2":14,"label":"Colossians 4:14","text":"\"Physician\" is this brief mention's sole biblical note identifying Luke's profession — a detail that colors how carefully he researched and wrote his Gospel and Acts, with the trained observational habits of a doctor. Paul's warmth shows in the added word \"beloved,\" while in the very same breath Demas gets nothing but his bare name — no epithet, no praise, a silence that reads as ominous next to Luke's warm commendation, especially in light of what later happens to Demas.","translation":"Luke, the beloved physician, greets you, and so does Demas."},{"c1":4,"v1":15,"c2":4,"v2":15,"label":"Colossians 4:15","text":"\"The church in her house\" — the Greek pronoun in the best manuscripts is feminine, identifying Nympha as a woman leading a congregation that met under her own roof, at a time before any dedicated church buildings existed anywhere in the Roman world; the earliest churches were, without exception, someone's living room.","translation":"Greet the brothers in Laodicea, and Nympha, and the church in her house."},{"c1":4,"v1":16,"c2":4,"v2":16,"label":"Colossians 4:16","text":"\"The letter from Laodicea\" is one of the New Testament's small mysteries — a letter of Paul's, now lost, or possibly an early title for what we know as Ephesians, meant to circulate as a general letter among several churches rather than address only one. Whichever it was, Paul's instruction pictures early congregations trading correspondence, treating apostolic letters as shared property meant for wider circulation, not private mail to one church alone.","translation":"And when this letter has been read among you, see that it is read also in the church of the Laodiceans, and that you in turn read the letter from Laodicea."},{"c1":4,"v1":17,"c2":4,"v2":17,"label":"Colossians 4:17","text":"\"Fulfil\" is a verb meaning \"discharge completely\" — not merely perform a duty but carry it through to its full extent. The added emphasis Paul places on this one-line message to Archippus suggests a ministry of some real weight, not routine service, and a man who may have needed the nudge to see it all the way through.","translation":"And say to Archippus: Look to the ministry you received in the Lord, that you fulfill it completely."},{"c1":4,"v1":18,"c2":4,"v2":18,"label":"Colossians 4:18","text":"\"With my own hand\" marks a shift — everything before this line was dictated to a secretary; Paul takes the pen himself only for the closing greeting, a habit that also guarded against forged letters circulating under his name. \"Remember my bonds\" lands with quiet weight: as he wrote it, an actual chain was fastened to his wrist, and the simple request asks for exactly what a chained man most needs — not to be forgotten by the people he can no longer visit.","translation":"The greeting is in my own hand — Paul's. Remember my chains. Grace be with you."}],"1 Timothy":[{"c1":1,"v1":5,"c2":1,"v2":7,"label":"1 Timothy 1:5–7","text":"The word behind ‘commandment’ here isn’t entolē (the word for one of the Ten Commandments) but paraggelia, the noun cousin of the verb Paul used for ‘charge’ back in verse 3 — the definite article ties the phrase back to it, so this reads ‘the aim (telos) of that charge.’ The goal of Paul’s charge to Timothy is love — agapē, the word John uses for God’s love for the world, not the pale ‘charity’ of the old translation. It flows from a good conscience: agathos describes something whole and functioning as it should, producing a sense of well-being rather than the nagging discomfort of guilt. Faith here is anupokritos, ‘unfeigned’ — built on the verb behind ‘hypocrite,’ the actor’s word for playing a part on a Greek stage; put the alpha of negation on it and you get faith that isn’t a performance. Some have missed this mark entirely (astocheō, an archer’s term for a bad shot) and been wrenched off course — ektrepō is medical language for a dislocated limb, a joint twisted out of its socket, not a minor stumble but a real derangement. What they wanted instead was to be nomodidaskaloi, ‘law-teachers,’ one compound word — professional specialists in the Mosaic law, though by Paul’s account they don’t even understand the terms they use so confidently.","translation":"Now the aim of the charge is love out of a clean heart and a good conscience and faith not play-acted — some, having missed the mark, have been wrenched out of joint into empty talk, wanting to be law-teachers, though they understand neither what they are saying nor the matters they insist on so confidently."},{"c1":1,"v1":8,"c2":1,"v2":11,"label":"1 Timothy 1:8–11","text":"Paul calls the law kalos, ‘good’ — a word that leans toward goodness as displayed outwardly, fitting since he’s about to describe what the law is good for. It isn’t legislated for the naturally upright but for the anupotaktos, the ‘unruly’ — literally one who refuses to be brought under, still under law’s authority but resisting it, distinct from the anomos who denies having any law over him at all. ‘Profane’ is bebēlos, built on the word for a threshold: it names someone who has made himself common ground, open for anyone to walk across, because he has kept no part of himself set apart for God — the opposite of holy, not because he’s especially wicked but because he’s kept nothing back. Over against this whole list stands ‘sound teaching’ — hugiainō is the ordinary word for bodily health, so Paul is picturing doctrine as either healthy or diseased, not merely correct or incorrect.","translation":"We know that the law is good if a person uses it lawfully — realizing this, that law is not laid down for a law-abiding person but for the lawless and the unruly, for the irreverent and the sinful, for the unholy and the common, for those who strike father and strike mother, for man-killers, for the sexually immoral, for those who lie with men, for slave-traders, for liars, for those who swear falsely, and for whatever else stands opposed to healthy teaching, according to the gospel of the glory of the blessed God, with which I have been entrusted."},{"c1":1,"v1":12,"c2":1,"v2":14,"label":"1 Timothy 1:12–14","text":"‘I thank’ renders a present tense that’s really a state: ‘I have constant gratitude,’ not a one-time acknowledgment but Paul’s settled disposition. The verb behind ‘enabled’ is the same one he uses in Philippians 4:13 — literally ‘clothed with strength,’ picturing God dressing him in a capacity he didn’t have on his own, here specifically the strength to carry the gospel he’d once tried to destroy. ‘Counted’ is hēgeomai, a word for a weighed, deliberate judgment based on evidence, not a gut feeling — God looked at the zeal Paul had poured into persecuting the church and judged, correctly, that the same intensity redirected would make him a formidable minister. ‘Injurious’ is hubristēs, describing someone whose contempt for others erupts into outrageous, violent acts — not petty rudeness but active cruelty. That’s the raw material grace took hold of.","translation":"I have constant gratitude to him who clothed me with strength, Christ Jesus our Lord, because he judged me trustworthy, appointing me to service — though I was formerly a blasphemer and a persecutor and a man of violent, outrageous contempt. But I was shown mercy, because I acted in ignorance, in unbelief; and the grace of our Lord overflowed all the more, with the faith and love that are in Christ Jesus."},{"c1":1,"v1":15,"c2":1,"v2":17,"label":"1 Timothy 1:15–17","text":"‘Faithful’ is pistos, a word papyrus letters of the period use for someone reliable enough to trust with money or a message — Paul is staking that same reliability on the sentence he’s about to state as fact. ‘Chief’ translates an intensive pronoun: not that Paul ranks worst among sinners in some cosmic tally, but that he stands, in his own case, foremost — the extreme test case proving Christ’s patience has no ceiling. That patience is makrothumia, built from ‘long’ and thumos, the word for a violent, seething passion stronger even than ordinary anger — makrothumia is the long, sustained holding-back of that eruption, restraint stretched out over time rather than a single moment of calm. Paul reaches for ‘eternal,’ not ‘everlasting,’ deliberately: the life given the believer had no beginning, because it’s God’s own life extended to us, while what’s often called ‘everlasting’ punishment has a beginning but supposedly no end — two different shapes of endlessness. And ‘immortal,’ describing God, is better rendered ‘incorruptible’ — not exemption from death so much as freedom from decay, from any process of wearing down.","translation":"Trustworthy is the saying and worthy of full acceptance: that Christ Jesus came into the world to save sinners, of whom I am foremost. Yet for this reason I was shown mercy, so that in me, the foremost, Christ Jesus might display his utmost patience, as an example to those who were going to be believing on him for life eternal. Now to the King of the ages, incorruptible, invisible, the only God, be honor and glory forever and ever. Amen."},{"c1":1,"v1":18,"c2":1,"v2":20,"label":"1 Timothy 1:18–20","text":"‘Son’ is teknon, ‘child’ — a term Paul uses as an endearment for grown men too, not a note on Timothy’s age. ‘Put away’ understates the Greek, which is literally ‘thrust from themselves’ — a violent, deliberate shove against one’s own conscience, not a passive drifting. And ‘faith’ carries the definite article: not their personal trust broken, but the Faith, the body of Christian doctrine, which they wrecked against like a ship. ‘Delivered to Satan’ is a perfect tense — a completed act with a result still standing at the moment Paul writes: Hymenaeus and Alexander are still under that sentence, not merely something Paul once did to them.","translation":"This charge I am entrusting to you, child Timothy, in keeping with the prophecies once made about you, so that by them you may be waging the good warfare, holding on to faith and a good conscience — which some, having thrust from themselves, have suffered shipwreck concerning the Faith; among them are Hymenaeus and Alexander, whom I have handed over to Satan, so that they may be taught not to go on blaspheming."},{"c1":2,"v1":3,"c2":2,"v2":5,"label":"1 Timothy 2:3–5","text":"‘Our Savior, God’ plays off a title claimed by the reigning Caesar in the imperial cult — ‘savior’ in the sense of the one who held the empire together and kept the peace. Paul sets God’s saviorhood against that: not political preservation but rescue from sin. ‘Will’ is thelō, a wish rooted in desire rather than cold decree — God’s longing that all be saved arises from something like emotion, though it doesn’t override the human will he built into people. ‘Knowledge’ is epignōsis, ‘full’ or precise knowledge, a compound the New Testament reserves for grasping things ethical and divine, never applied to God’s own knowledge. ‘Mediator’ is mesitēs, the technical word for someone who steps between two parties to broker peace or seal a covenant — and the word for ‘man’ here isn’t anēr, a male individual, but anthrōpos, the racial term for humanity. Christ didn’t merely become a man; in taking on anthrōpos, he identified himself with the whole human race.","translation":"This is good and acceptable before God our Savior, whose will it is that all people be saved and come into a full, precise knowledge of the truth. For God is one, and there is one mediator between God and mankind — himself mankind, Christ Jesus."},{"c1":2,"v1":8,"c2":2,"v2":8,"label":"1 Timothy 2:8","text":"‘I will’ switches verbs from the thelō of verse 4 to boulomai — a desire that proceeds from deliberate judgment rather than feeling; Paul isn’t wishing, he’s directing. ‘Holy hands’ uses hosios, not the more common hagios — this is holiness as lived experience, a life actually conformed to what’s right, not positional or ceremonial purity. And ‘doubting’ is dialogismos, disputatious, skeptical reasoning — the kind of prayer Paul rules out isn’t uncertainty about outcomes but an argumentative, second-guessing spirit turned either on God’s character or on the people being prayed for.","translation":"I want, then, the men to be praying in every place, lifting up hands that are holy, apart from anger and skeptical arguing."},{"c1":2,"v1":13,"c2":2,"v2":15,"label":"1 Timothy 2:13–15","text":"‘Formed’ is plassō, the potter’s word — molding clay or wax by hand, the same verb the Greek Old Testament uses for God shaping Adam from the dust. Eve’s deception is intensified: Adam simply wasn’t ‘deceived’ (apataō), but Eve was exapataō’d — the compound piles on a prefix meaning ‘thoroughly, completely,’ marking hers as the deeper deception. ‘Was’ in transgression is a perfect tense of ginomai, ‘to become’ — not a static state but a falling into a condition that then held. And ‘saved’ (sōzō) covers a wide range in the New Testament — healed, rescued from a wreck, kept safe — it isn’t limited to eternal salvation; here, describing a woman Paul has already assumed to be a believer, the sense is being kept safe and brought through, not converted, in the ordinary work of childbearing.","translation":"For Adam was formed first, then Eve; and Adam was not deceived, but the woman, having been thoroughly deceived, came to be in transgression. Yet she will be kept safe through her childbearing, if they continue in faith and love and holiness, with sound judgment."},{"c1":3,"v1":2,"c2":3,"v2":2,"label":"1 Timothy 3:2","text":"‘Blameless’ compounds ‘to lay hold of’ with a negating alpha — literally ‘un-grabbable’: a life with no handle on it for accusation to catch. ‘Husband of one wife’ is, word for word, ‘a one-woman man’ — both nouns lack the definite article, a Greek construction that emphasizes quality of character over bare fact. It’s less a marital headcount than a description of temperament: a man whose love, by nature, fixes on one woman, the way we’d call a dog ‘a one-man dog’ to describe its loyalty, not its history. ‘Vigilant’ (nēphalios) pictures someone calm, level-headed, and clear-eyed rather than simply sober from drink. ‘Hospitable’ compounds ‘fond of’ with ‘stranger’ — literally someone drawn toward the outsider, the traveler, the person with no other place to go.","translation":"The overseer, then, must be a man on whom nothing can be laid hold of — a one-woman kind of man, level-headed, sound-minded, orderly, hospitable to the stranger, skilled at teaching."},{"c1":3,"v1":3,"c2":3,"v2":3,"label":"1 Timothy 3:3","text":"‘Not given to wine’ compounds ‘wine’ with ‘beside’ — one who lingers too long at the cup and turns quarrelsome under it. ‘No striker’ is literally ‘a bruiser,’ someone quick with a fist. ‘Patient’ is epieikēs, a word better rendered ‘sweetly reasonable’ — someone who won’t press his legal rights to their absolute limit even when he’s entitled to, because strict justice pushed too far becomes its own injustice. ‘No brawler’ builds on machē, ‘battle’ — not a man who goes looking for a fight. And ‘not covetous’ is literally ‘not fond of silver.’","translation":"Not one who lingers over wine and turns quarrelsome, not a striker, but sweetly reasonable, not a fighter, not a lover of silver."},{"c1":3,"v1":6,"c2":3,"v2":6,"label":"1 Timothy 3:6","text":"‘Novice’ is neophutos, ‘newly sprung up’ — the word used of a palm tree just planted, roots not yet down. ‘Lifted up with pride’ is tuphoō, literally ‘to raise smoke’ — pride here isn’t just inflation but a fog that clouds the mind, leaving a man stupefied by his own self-regard, unable to see straight. And ‘condemnation’ (krima) is the specific judicial sentence under which the devil already stands, the verdict passed on him for the original sin of pride — precisely the trap Paul warns a hasty appointment could walk a new believer into.","translation":"Not a newly planted one, lest, being clouded with the smoke of pride, he fall into the condemnation of the devil."},{"c1":3,"v1":7,"c2":3,"v2":7,"label":"1 Timothy 3:7","text":"‘Good report’ is marturia kalē, an ‘excellent testimony’ — Paul wants the outside world’s read on this man’s character to be positive, not merely the church’s, on the theory that outsider opinion can catch what insider loyalty misses.","translation":"It is also necessary that he have an excellent testimony from those outside, so that he not fall into reproach and the snare of the devil."},{"c1":3,"v1":10,"c2":3,"v2":10,"label":"1 Timothy 3:10","text":"‘Proved’ is dokimazō — testing aimed at approval, the assumption being the candidate passes. ‘Blameless’ is anegklētoi, a courtroom word: ‘unaccused,’ no charge standing against him. The participle carrying it is conditional in force — ‘let them serve, provided they remain unaccused’ — approval isn’t a one-time hurdle but an ongoing state.","translation":"And let these also first be tested; then let them serve as deacons, provided they are found unaccused."},{"c1":3,"v1":11,"c2":3,"v2":11,"label":"1 Timothy 3:11","text":"‘Wives’ has no possessive pronoun in the Greek and simply reads ‘women’ — likely a third office in this list (the connective hōsautōs, ‘likewise,’ is Paul’s marker for adding another item in a series), women deacons such as Phoebe in Romans 16, not the spouses of the men just described. ‘Slanderers’ is diabolos, the very word used elsewhere for ‘the devil’ — from a verb meaning ‘to throw across, to falsely accuse’; a slanderer does, in miniature, exactly what Satan’s name describes him doing at large.","translation":"Women, likewise, must be dignified, not slanderers, level-headed, faithful in all things."},{"c1":3,"v1":16,"c2":3,"v2":16,"label":"1 Timothy 3:16","text":"‘Without controversy’ is homologoumenōs, ‘confessedly’ — everyone concedes the point, from the verb ‘to agree with.’ ‘Manifest in the flesh’ uses phaneroō, ‘made visible’: the invisible one becomes visible, not by acquiring existence but by taking on a body that could be seen. ‘Justified in the Spirit’ doesn’t use dikaioō in its usual sense of a sinner declared righteous — here it means vindicated, endorsed, proved to be what he claimed. The whole line balances flesh against spirit: flesh is his humanity, openly on display; spirit is his deity, usually cloaked beneath that humanity but breaking through at moments like the Transfiguration, each such moment vindicating who he really was. ‘Received up’ translates the technical term Luke uses for the ascension itself in Acts 1.","translation":"And confessedly great is the mystery of godliness: he was made visible in flesh, vindicated in spirit, seen by angels, proclaimed among the nations, believed on in the world, taken up in glory."},{"c1":4,"v1":6,"c2":4,"v2":6,"label":"1 Timothy 4:6","text":"‘Brethren’ is adelphos, literally ‘from the same womb’ — Christians as a single family sharing one Father. ‘Put in remembrance’ is a milder verb than direct exhortation, literally ‘to put under,’ i.e. to suggest, to plant an idea rather than hammer it home. ‘Minister’ is diakonos, emphasizing activity rather than office — a servant seen in the act of serving, not yet the technical title ‘clergyman’ it would later become. ‘Nourished up’ is a present participle: not a meal once eaten but continual feeding, an ongoing diet. And ‘attained’ is a perfect tense — ‘have closely followed and still hold the results,’ a finished course of instruction whose effect persists into the present.","translation":"By constantly putting these things before the brothers, you will be a good servant of Christ Jesus, continually nourishing yourself on the words of the faith and of the good teaching which you have closely followed and now hold."},{"c1":4,"v1":9,"c2":4,"v2":11,"label":"1 Timothy 4:9–11","text":"‘Labor’ (kopiaō) means work to the point of exhaustion; its partner is agōnizomai, the word for competing in the Greek games, the root of our ‘agony’ — together they picture strenuous, painful effort, not casual service. ‘Trust’ is a perfect tense of elpizō: ‘we have set our hope,’ with the sense that the hope, once fixed, now stands settled and fixed in place. Calling God ‘Savior of all men, especially of believers’ plays on two senses of the word at once — he is Savior of everyone in that he has provided salvation available to all at the cross, but Savior in the fullest, realized sense only for those who’ve actually placed their faith in it.","translation":"This saying is trustworthy and deserving of full acceptance. For it is to this end that we labor to exhaustion and are contending, because we have set, and still have set, our hope on the living God, who is Savior of all men, especially of those who believe. These things keep commanding and teaching."},{"c1":4,"v1":12,"c2":4,"v2":12,"label":"1 Timothy 4:12","text":"‘Despise’ isn’t a passive feeling — kataphroneō is contempt that shows itself in action, and the prohibition is a present imperative, which in Greek forbids the continuing of something already underway: Paul isn’t warning against a hypothetical, he’s telling Timothy to stop letting a thing that’s already happening keep happening. ‘Be’ an example is likewise not the ordinary verb ‘to be’ but ginomai, ‘to become’ — ‘keep on becoming,’ an ongoing process rather than a fixed state achieved once. ‘Example’ is tupos, the mark left by a blow or the impression stamped by a die — the pattern other lives get shaped against, not just a good instance among many.","translation":"Stop letting anyone look down on your youth, but keep on becoming a pattern for the believers, in speech, in conduct, in love, in faith, in purity."},{"c1":4,"v1":13,"c2":4,"v2":13,"label":"1 Timothy 4:13","text":"‘Reading’ is anagnōsis, technical for the public reading of Scripture aloud in the gathered assembly, sometimes with running comment attached. ‘Give attendance to’ is prosechō, literally ‘hold toward’ — keep your mind fastened on this. The order Paul lists — reading, then exhortation, then teaching — likely reflects logical priority as much as sequence: exhortation needs teaching under it as a foundation, so instruction has to come first even where exhortation is named first.","translation":"Until I come, keep holding your attention on the public reading, on exhortation, on teaching."},{"c1":4,"v1":14,"c2":4,"v2":14,"label":"1 Timothy 4:14","text":"‘Gift’ is charisma, Paul’s technical term for a Spirit-given capacity that equips someone for a specific service in the church, not a natural talent but a direct impartation. It came ‘through prophecy’ — prophetic words spoken over Timothy, apparently repeated at his ordination when the elders (presbuterion, the formal body of elders) laid hands on him, an act of identification marking him as now one of their number. ‘Do not neglect’ is a present imperative in a prohibition, which in Greek forbids continuing something already in motion — Timothy, by temperament diffident, seems to have actually been letting this gift go unused, and Paul is telling him to stop.","translation":"Do not keep neglecting the gift that is in you, which was given you through prophecy, along with the laying on of the hands of the elders."},{"c1":4,"v1":15,"c2":4,"v2":15,"label":"1 Timothy 4:15","text":"‘Meditate’ is meletaō, the word for an orator’s rehearsal — not quiet reflection but active practice, drilling a skill. ‘Give yourself wholly to them’ is literally ‘be constantly in these things’ — total immersion, not periodic attention. And ‘profiting’ is prokopē, ‘to cut forward’ — a pioneer’s word for blazing a trail through brush no one has cleared yet, picturing Timothy’s growth as visible, forward progress rather than passive maturing.","translation":"Practice these things; be constantly in them, so that your advance may be evident to all."},{"c1":4,"v1":16,"c2":4,"v2":16,"label":"1 Timothy 4:16","text":"‘Take heed’ is epechō, literally ‘to hold upon’ — fasten your attention and don’t let go. ‘Continue’ is epimenō, ‘to stay by the side of’ something, sticking close rather than checking in occasionally. The ‘salvation’ in view can’t be the sinner’s conversion or the saint’s preservation, since both of those are God’s work, not the fruit of careful teaching; given the immediate context about demon-inspired error, it’s rescue from getting tangled in false doctrine — Timothy protects himself and his hearers from heresy by staying glued to the reading, exhortation, and teaching of the Word.","translation":"Keep holding your attention on yourself and on the teaching. Stay right by these things — for in doing this you will save both yourself and those who are listening to you."},{"c1":5,"v1":3,"c2":5,"v2":3,"label":"1 Timothy 5:3","text":"‘Honor’ is timao, ‘to fix the value of’ something and then pay it the respect that value warrants — here the context folds in the practical sense of financial support as part of that honor. ‘Widows indeed’ uses ontōs, ‘truly, genuinely’ — Paul is marking off a specific class: women left completely without family support, as the following verses go on to define.","translation":"Be constantly showing honor to widows who are truly widows."},{"c1":5,"v1":4,"c2":5,"v2":4,"label":"1 Timothy 5:4","text":"‘Show piety’ is eusebeō, the same root elsewhere translated ‘godliness’ — Paul treats family duty as itself an act of reverence, not a separate category from religion. ‘At home’ is literally ‘their own private household’ (idios doubling down on ‘own’), marking this as a family obligation before it’s a church one. ‘Requite’ is apodidōmi, ‘to give off from oneself’ — the image is of discharging a debt, throwing off a weight one owes, rather than doing a favor. And ‘parents’ translates progonos, literally ‘those who came into being before’ — broader than just mother and father, covering grandparents and forebears generally.","translation":"But if a widow has children or grandchildren, let them learn first to show piety toward their own household and to discharge what they owe their forebears — for this is pleasing in the sight of God."},{"c1":5,"v1":5,"c2":5,"v2":7,"label":"1 Timothy 5:5–7","text":"‘Desolate’ is a perfect participle of a verb meaning ‘left utterly alone’ — not just presently by herself but settled into that condition. ‘Trusteth’ is a perfect tense of elpizō, ‘to hope’: a past act whose result has become permanent, so ‘has fixed her hope, and it stays fixed.’ ‘Liveth in pleasure’ translates a verb closer to ‘lives wastefully, wantonly’ — squandering rather than merely enjoying. And the line about being ‘dead while she liveth’ pairs a present participle (‘living’) with a perfect verb (‘has died’): she is living, having died, with the abiding result that she is, right now, a dead woman walking.","translation":"But the widow who has been left utterly alone has fixed her hope on God, and continues in petitions and prayers night and day. But the one who lives wastefully — she is living, having died, so that she is dead. Keep commanding these things as well, so that they may be beyond reproach."},{"c1":5,"v1":8,"c2":5,"v2":8,"label":"1 Timothy 5:8","text":"‘Provide’ is pronoeō, literally ‘to think of in advance’ — providing here starts with foresight, not just handing over money when asked. ‘Infidel’ is apistos, built by negating pistos, ‘faith’ — the very word for ‘unbeliever.’ Paul’s point bites: a man who won’t do what even an unbeliever does by natural instinct has, in practice, denied the faith he claims — faith that produces no natural fruit isn’t functioning as faith at all.","translation":"But if anyone does not think ahead for his own, and especially for those of his own household, he has denied the faith and is worse than an unbeliever."},{"c1":5,"v1":13,"c2":5,"v2":13,"label":"1 Timothy 5:13","text":"‘Wandering about’ is perierchomai, the word for aimless strollers and wanderers. ‘Tattlers’ is phluareō, ‘to talk nonsense’ — and pointedly, to bring false, idle charges against someone. ‘Busybodies’ is periergos, the same word used in Acts 19 for the ‘curious arts’ of pagan sorcery — prying into what’s concealed, meddling in business that isn’t theirs.","translation":"And at the same time they also learn to be idle, going about from house to house — and not only idle, but talkers of nonsense and prying into others’ affairs, saying what they ought not to say."},{"c1":5,"v1":16,"c2":5,"v2":16,"label":"1 Timothy 5:16","text":"‘Church’ (ekklēsia) is built on ‘to call out from’ — a body summoned out of the general population into an assembly. Paul’s point turns on the word’s plain meaning: family obligations exist precisely so the called-out assembly isn’t burdened with debts that belong to a household first.","translation":"If any believer has widows, let him keep assisting them, and let the assembly not be burdened, so that it may assist those who are truly widows."},{"c1":5,"v1":19,"c2":5,"v2":19,"label":"1 Timothy 5:19","text":"‘Accusation’ is katēgoria, specifically a formal charge brought before a tribunal — not gossip or complaint, a legal-sounding word. And ‘before’ two or three witnesses is epi, ‘upon’ — the charge must stand upon their authority, requiring their testimony as its actual basis rather than mere hearsay.","translation":"Against an elder, do not accept an accusation except upon the testimony of two or three witnesses."},{"c1":5,"v1":20,"c2":5,"v2":20,"label":"1 Timothy 5:20","text":"‘Rebuke’ is elegchō — not a scolding but the wielding of truth with such force that it brings the guilty party to an actual conviction of the wrong, whether or not he confesses it. ‘Others’ who are meant to fear are specifically ‘the rest’ of the elders, hoi loipoi — this is a check aimed at the leadership class itself.","translation":"Those who are sinning, keep rebuking in the presence of all, so that the rest may have fear."},{"c1":5,"v1":21,"c2":5,"v2":21,"label":"1 Timothy 5:21","text":"‘I charge’ is diamarturomai, a classical word for calling gods and men to witness a solemn testimony — the strongest form of adjuration available, which tells us something about how seriously Paul needed Timothy to take this. The Greek grammar behind ‘God, and the Lord Jesus Christ’ actually identifies the two as one: ‘our God, even Christ Jesus’ — a direct, pointed claim of Christ’s deity, aimed like a spear at the imperial cult that called Caesar ‘lord’ and ‘god.’","translation":"I solemnly charge you before our God, even Christ Jesus, and the elect angels, that you guard these things without prejudging, doing nothing out of favoritism."},{"c1":5,"v1":22,"c2":5,"v2":22,"label":"1 Timothy 5:22","text":"Laying on hands signals identification — restoring the repentant sinner back into visible fellowship with the assembly, not a ritual gesture. ‘Keep’ is tēreō, watchful, ongoing custody — the verb assumes you already possess the thing you’re guarding. And ‘pure’ here is hagnos in the sense of ‘upright, honorable,’ not narrowly ‘chaste’ — Paul’s concern is Timothy’s integrity in these restoration decisions broadly, not just sexual purity.","translation":"Lay hands hastily on no one, and do not share in the sins of others. Keep watchful guard over yourself, staying pure."},{"c1":5,"v1":23,"c2":5,"v2":23,"label":"1 Timothy 5:23","text":"‘Drink no longer water’ is really ‘stop being a water-drinker’ — Timothy’s habit, not a single glass, and Paul isn’t banning water so much as prescribing wine as medicine for a specific ailment. The word for wine, oinos, means the fermented drink throughout, not a modern grape-juice substitute — Paul is recommending a period remedy, not laying down a beverage policy.","translation":"No longer be a water-drinker only, but be using a little wine, because of your stomach and your frequent illnesses."},{"c1":5,"v1":24,"c2":5,"v2":24,"label":"1 Timothy 5:24","text":"‘Open beforehand’ misreads the pro- prefix as a time marker; it’s actually just intensifying — ‘openly manifest to all eyes,’ plainly obvious, not ‘obvious ahead of time.’ Paul pictures some sins marching ahead of the sinner like heralds, announcing the verdict before any court convenes, while other sins trail behind, only surfacing at the judgment itself.","translation":"The sins of some people are openly manifest, going ahead to judgment; the sins of others, they follow after."},{"c1":5,"v1":25,"c2":5,"v2":25,"label":"1 Timothy 5:25","text":"‘Likewise’ signals an antithesis, not a repetition: just as some sins are plain and some stay hidden until judgment, so some good works are obvious and some aren’t — but even the hidden good can’t stay hidden forever. ‘Otherwise’ doesn’t mean ‘bad’; it means ‘not yet visible.’","translation":"In the same way, good works too are openly manifest; and those that are not, are not able to remain hidden."},{"c1":6,"v1":2,"c2":6,"v2":2,"label":"1 Timothy 6:2","text":"‘Despise’ is kataphroneō, ‘to think down on’ — contempt shown in dismissive treatment. The verb for ‘do them service’ shares its root with doulos, ‘slave’ — literally ‘slave for them all the more.’ ‘Faithful’ here doesn’t describe a virtue but a status: pistos means ‘believers,’ brothers in the faith, not simply ‘trustworthy.’ And ‘benefit’ is euergesia in its ordinary secular sense of ‘kindly service’ — the kind acts these Christian masters do for their slaves, not favors the slaves owe back.","translation":"And those who have believing masters, let them not look down on them because they are brothers, but let them serve them all the more, because those who benefit by their work are believers and beloved."},{"c1":6,"v1":3,"c2":6,"v2":5,"label":"1 Timothy 6:3–5","text":"‘Wholesome’ teaching is again hugiainō, ‘healthy’ — Paul keeps this whole letter’s controversy in medical terms, sound doctrine versus disease. ‘Proud’ is tuphoo, the same ‘smoke-blinded’ word from 3:6, here in the perfect tense: not a passing flare of ego but a settled, permanent fog. ‘Knowing nothing’ is epistamai plus ‘not even one thing’ — total incapacity for real, fixed thought, not mere ignorance of facts. And ‘destitute of the truth’ is apostereo, ‘defrauded, robbed’ — the tense implies they once actually held the truth and have since been stripped of it, whether by their own doing or another’s.","translation":"If anyone teaches otherwise and does not agree with the healthy words of our Lord Jesus Christ and the teaching that fits godliness, he has been permanently clouded with pride, understanding not even one thing, but sick with a craving for controversies and word-battles, out of which come envy, strife, slander, evil suspicions, and constant friction among men whose minds have been robbed of the truth, who reckon godliness a means of gain."},{"c1":6,"v1":6,"c2":6,"v2":10,"label":"1 Timothy 6:6–10","text":"‘Contentment’ is autarkeia, a Stoic technical term for inward self-sufficiency — needing nothing from outside circumstances to stay steady. The logic linking verses 6 and 7 runs on ‘because,’ not ‘and’: we brought nothing in because we can carry nothing out, one fact proving the other. The ‘content’ of verse 8 shares that root but drops the ‘self’ prefix — arkeo, ‘to have unfailing strength against a threat,’ the same word behind ‘my grace is sufficient for thee.’ ‘Will be rich’ uses boulomai, the reasoned, deliberated desire, not a passing impulse — this is calculated ambition, not a stray temptation. And ‘coveted’ is orego, ‘to stretch oneself out to grasp something,’ the same reaching gesture used positively elsewhere of desiring a good work — here bent toward money instead.","translation":"But godliness with self-sufficiency is great gain, because we brought nothing into the world, since we are not able to carry anything out either; and having food and covering, with these we shall be sufficiently strengthened. But those who have settled on wanting to be rich fall into temptation and a trap and many senseless and harmful cravings, which plunge men into ruin and destruction. For the love of money is a root of all the evils; reaching for it, some have been led away from the faith and have pierced themselves through with many consuming griefs."},{"c1":6,"v1":11,"c2":6,"v2":11,"label":"1 Timothy 6:11","text":"‘But thou’ emphatically sets Timothy apart from the money-lovers just described — ‘you, in contrast to them.’ ‘Flee’ is a present imperative: not a single dash for the exit but a lifelong habit of continual fleeing. ‘Follow’ (diōkō) is the same verb for a hunter’s pursuit, running hard after something desired. And ‘patience’ is hupomonē, built from ‘remain’ and ‘under’ — literally the stance of remaining under a weight rather than throwing it off, endurance as a settled posture, not a single act of grit.","translation":"But you, man of God, keep fleeing from these things; and keep pursuing righteousness, godliness, faith, love, patient endurance, gentleness."},{"c1":6,"v1":12,"c2":6,"v2":12,"label":"1 Timothy 6:12","text":"‘Fight’ is agōnizomai, a present imperative — the ongoing, no-let-up struggle of the Greek athletic games, not a single skirmish. ‘Good’ is kalos, not agathos — goodness as a spectator sees it, the beauty of technique on display, not just moral rightness. ‘Lay hold’ switches to an aorist imperative, viewing the habitual grasping-after eternal life as one decisive, summed-up act rather than a process — Timothy already has eternal life as a gift; Paul is urging him to grip it, to lay hold of what it means in experience, not to acquire it for the first time.","translation":"Keep fighting the good fight of the faith; take hold of the eternal life to which you were called, and about which you spoke the good confession in the presence of many witnesses."},{"c1":6,"v1":13,"c2":6,"v2":16,"label":"1 Timothy 6:13–16","text":"‘Quickeneth’ is zōogoneō, ‘preserves alive’ — Timothy is reminded he answers to the One who keeps everything, including him, in existence. ‘Keep’ the commandment is an aorist imperative, a sharp, military-style order given all at once, fitting for a Paul who knew Timothy’s naturally hesitant temperament needed a jolt now and then. ‘Appearing’ is epiphaneia, the word pagan Greek used for a god’s glorious manifestation to help his people — Paul redirects it to Christ’s return. ‘Times’ is kairos, not chronos — not clock-time but God’s own strategically appointed seasons, known to him alone. And ‘immortality’ is athanasia, built by simply negating ‘death’ — ‘no-death,’ the flat opposite of what every mortal faces, reserved here for God alone.","translation":"I charge you, in the presence of God who is constantly keeping all things alive, and of Christ Jesus who testified the good confession before Pontius Pilate: keep this command spotless, beyond reproach, until the appearing of our Lord Jesus Christ, which he will show at his own appointed seasons — he who is the blessed and only Sovereign, the King of those who reign as kings and Lord of those who rule as lords, who alone has no-death, dwelling in unapproachable light, whom no one has seen or is able to see. To him be honor and eternal power. Amen."},{"c1":6,"v1":17,"c2":6,"v2":19,"label":"1 Timothy 6:17–19","text":"‘World’ is aiōn, literally ‘the now age’ — not the physical globe but the whole floating atmosphere of values and assumptions people currently breathe, the present age’s spirit rather than kosmos, the ordered universe. ‘High-minded’ compounds ‘think’ with ‘high’ — thinking of oneself as elevated. ‘Trust’ uses a perfect infinitive of elpizō: ‘have set, and still have, their hope fixed’ on wealth’s instability rather than on God. And ‘giveth’ is parechō, ‘holds forth, continually offers’ — God as the one perpetually extending good things for enjoyment, not a distant benefactor but an active, ongoing giver.","translation":"Charge those who are rich in the present age not to be high-minded, nor to have set their hope on the uncertainty of riches, but on God, who is constantly holding out to us all things richly to enjoy — to be doing good, to be rich in good works, generous, ready to share, storing up for themselves a good foundation for the future, so that they may take hold of what is truly life."}],"2 Timothy":[{"c1":1,"v1":3,"c2":1,"v2":7,"label":"2 Timothy 1:3–7","text":"Paul's 'I thank God' is present tense — an ongoing habit of gratitude, not a single act. 'Serve' translates latreuō, the word for the priest's sacred duty at the altar; Paul frames his whole life, inherited from his forebears, as an act of worship. 'I am reminded' renders hupomnēsis, recollection triggered by an outside prompt rather than anamnēsis, memory that simply surfaces on its own — something jogged Paul's memory of Timothy's tears. 'Unfeigned' is anupokritos, built from the same root that gives us 'hypocrite,' the word for a Greek stage actor performing behind a mask; the negating alpha in front describes faith with no mask at all, no performance. 'Dwelt in' is enoikeō, 'to live at home in' — the faith that first lived in Lois and Eunice has settled residence in Timothy too. 'Stir up' is anazōpureō, literally 'kindle the fire again,' built from words for 'again,' 'life,' and 'fire' — and it's a present-tense infinitive, so the command isn't to strike one spark but to keep tending the flame continuously. The 'gift' is a charisma, Spirit-given, bundled with 'power, love, and a sound mind' set directly against deilia, cowardly timidity — the very opposite of what God handed Timothy.","translation":"I am continually thankful to God, whom I continually serve as my forefathers did, with a clean conscience, as I hold you unceasingly in my petitions, yearning night and day to see you, remembering your tears, so that I may be filled with joy — having been reminded of the unmasked faith that is in you, which was at home first in your grandmother Lois and your mother Eunice, and, I am persuaded, is at home in you also. For this reason I am reminding you: keep on fanning into flame the gift of God that is in you through the laying on of my hands. For God did not give us a spirit of cowardice, but of power and love and a sound mind."},{"c1":1,"v1":8,"c2":1,"v2":8,"label":"2 Timothy 1:8","text":"'Be not ashamed' uses the aorist subjunctive with the negative, not the present imperative — the grammar forbids an action not yet begun rather than commanding Timothy to stop something already underway. Paul isn't rebuking existing shame; he's heading off a temptation that hasn't yet taken hold. 'His prisoner' doesn't mean a man Christ has locked up, but a man who belongs to Christ and wears chains for that reason — the genitive marks possession, not agency. 'Be partaker of the afflictions' translates sugkakopatheō, a compound stacking 'with' onto 'suffer' onto 'evil' — literally, to suffer hardship together with someone. Timothy isn't asked to suffer alone; he's asked to share Paul's and the Lord's own sufferings. And the strength for that sharing is qualified — 'according to the power of God' — so the endurance asked of him is never presented as self-generated grit.","translation":"Therefore, do not begin to be ashamed of the testimony that belongs to our Lord, nor of me, his prisoner — but suffer hardship together with us for the sake of the gospel, according to the power of God."},{"c1":1,"v1":9,"c2":1,"v2":9,"label":"2 Timothy 1:9","text":"'Who saved' pins the verb to a specific historical moment — an aorist participle, not an ongoing process — because Paul's argument is that a settled, one-time act of God back then guarantees his continued power now. Salvation is 'not according to our works but according to his own purpose and grace': kata carries the sense of 'down,' of domination or control, so Paul is saying our works exercise no control whatsoever over how salvation is given. That grace, he adds, was granted 'before eternal times' — a phrase reaching back of any reckoned time at all, lodged in God's purpose before creation existed to receive it. Then, moving into the next two verses, 'made manifest' (phaneroō) means bringing into visibility something that had been hidden; 'appearing' is epiphaneia, the word Greek writers used for a god's glorious self-disclosure, here applied to Christ's incarnation. 'Abolished' translates katargeō, 'rendered inoperative' — not erased, since believers still die, but stripped of its power to terrify or hold. Paul calls himself a kērux, 'herald' — the term for the Emperor's own officer, who proclaimed the sovereign's message with a formality and authority that demanded a hearing.","translation":"The One who saved us — at that decisive moment — and called us with a holy calling, not according to our works but according to his own purpose and grace, which was given us in Christ Jesus before times eternal, but has now been made visible through the appearing of our Savior, Christ Jesus, who rendered death inoperative and brought life and incorruption to light through the gospel — the gospel for which I was appointed a herald, an ambassador, and a teacher."},{"c1":1,"v1":12,"c2":1,"v2":12,"label":"2 Timothy 1:12","text":"'I know' is oida, not ginōskō — not knowledge gained through experience but flat, settled certainty about who God is. Both 'I have believed' and 'am persuaded' sit in the perfect tense: not 'I believed once' but 'I came to believe, and that belief still stands, fixed.' 'Able' is dunatos, a word that carries more than bare capability — it points to power actually resident in the one described. 'Keep' is phulassō, a soldier's term for standing guard. What's guarded — 'that which I have committed to him' — reads best in context as the deposit of gospel truth entrusted to Paul's care as herald and teacher, not his own soul's safekeeping; the surrounding verses are all about service, not salvation. 'That day' points ahead to the reckoning where that stewardship gets evaluated.","translation":"For this reason I am also suffering these things. But I am not ashamed, for I know — with absolute certainty — the One in whom I have placed my trust, and I have come to a settled persuasion that he is powerful enough to guard, until that day, what has been entrusted to me."},{"c1":1,"v1":13,"c2":1,"v2":13,"label":"2 Timothy 1:13","text":"'Pattern' is hupotupōsis, built on tupos, the word for the mark a stamp or seal leaves behind — an impression that reproduces the original exactly. Paul isn't asking Timothy to preserve a vague memory of the gospel's gist; he's asking him to keep the precise wording he was taught, the way a die keeps the shape it was cut to. The phrase matters for how the New Testament regards its own words — not just its ideas, but its actual vocabulary, treated as worth guarding letter for letter. 'In faith and love' names the spirit in which that guarding is to be done — not rigid formalism, but conviction wrapped in warmth.","translation":"Keep holding fast the pattern of sound words which you heard from me personally, in faith and love which is in Christ Jesus."},{"c1":1,"v1":14,"c2":1,"v2":14,"label":"2 Timothy 1:14","text":"'That good thing committed to you' is literally 'the beautiful deposit' — Paul keeps returning to deposit-language for the gospel entrusted to Timothy, treating doctrine like something banked, to be guarded rather than improved on. 'Keep' again is phulassō, standing guard. And the guarding isn't a solo effort — it happens 'through the Holy Spirit who dwells in us,' enoikeō, the same 'at home' word used earlier of Timothy's faith. The Spirit isn't a visitor lodging temporarily; he's meant to be fully at home, free to do his sanctifying work without resistance.","translation":"That beautiful trust which was deposited with you, guard through the Holy Spirit who is at home in us."},{"c1":1,"v1":15,"c2":1,"v2":15,"label":"2 Timothy 1:15","text":"'Asia' here is the Roman province in western Asia Minor, not a continent — a compact region, which makes the scope of the defection sharper: a whole province's believers turning from Paul. The turning wasn't personal pique; the same two names, Phygellus and Hermogenes, resurface later tied to doctrinal error, so this was a theological break, not a falling-out. Then, moving into the next two verses, Onesiphorus stands in deliberate contrast. 'Refreshed' is anapsuchō, 'to cool off' — a vivid, almost physical word for the relief he brought Paul. 'Chain' is halusis, a manacle; Paul was literally handcuffed to a Roman guard around the clock, and visiting him carried real risk. 'Very diligently' uses a comparative form — Onesiphorus searched with more effort than anyone could reasonably have expected.","translation":"You know this — that all those in Asia turned away from me, among them Phygellus and Hermogenes. May the Lord grant mercy to the household of Onesiphorus, because he often cooled my spirit and was not ashamed of my chain, but when he arrived in Rome he kept seeking me out with more-than-expected diligence, and found me."},{"c1":1,"v1":18,"c2":1,"v2":18,"label":"2 Timothy 1:18","text":"'Better' is a comparative — Timothy, as Ephesus's own pastor, actually knew more about Onesiphorus's service there than Paul did; a small, human touch. Chapter two opens with the pronoun 'you' placed for emphasis — 'as for you' — as Paul turns urgently from his own situation to Timothy's future conduct. 'Be strong' is endunamoō, a passive form: Timothy isn't told to grit his teeth but to be strengthened, the power coming from outside him, 'in the grace that is in Christ Jesus.' 'Commit' is paratithēmi, the deposit-word again — Timothy is to bank the gospel with men whose defining trait is pistos, not 'believing' so much as 'trustworthy,' reliable enough to be handed something valuable.\n\nThe soldier image that follows sharpens the point: 'endure hardship' is sunkakopatheō, built with the same 'with' prefix as in 1:8 — Timothy suffers hardship alongside Paul, not alone. A soldier on campaign doesn't get 'entangled' (emplekō, literally 'inwoven') in civilian business, because his whole purpose is to satisfy the one who enlisted him. The image trades on the plain fact that a man can't serve two masters with divided attention.","translation":"And how much he served me in Ephesus, you know better than I do. You, then, my child, be strengthened by the grace that is in Christ Jesus; and the things you heard from me personally, in the presence of many witnesses, deposit as a trust with trustworthy men, men who will be sufficient to teach others also. Take your share in enduring hardship together with me, as a good soldier of Christ Jesus. No one serving as a soldier gets himself entangled in civilian affairs, so that he may please the one who enlisted him."},{"c1":2,"v1":5,"c2":2,"v2":5,"label":"2 Timothy 2:5","text":"'Strive for the mastery' is athleō, the verb behind our word 'athlete' — the term for competing in the public games. Greek contestants underwent months of mandated training beforehand, under strict diet and discipline, before they were even allowed to compete; break the rules and you were disqualified — adokimos, 'failing the test,' the same word Paul uses of himself elsewhere. Paul's point rides on that detail: the wreath doesn't go to whoever crosses the line first, but to whoever competed inside the rules the whole way through.","translation":"And if anyone competes as an athlete, he is not crowned unless he has competed according to the rules."},{"c1":2,"v1":6,"c2":2,"v2":6,"label":"2 Timothy 2:6","text":"'Husbandman' is geōrgos, a plain tiller of the soil, and 'laboureth' is kopiaō, work pushed to the point of exhaustion — not light effort but wearing toil. 'Must' is dei, 'it is necessary in the nature of the case' — not a suggestion but a built-in principle: the one who does the exhausting work has the first claim on what it produces. Paul is laying groundwork for the plain fact that gospel labor, done with that kind of effort, rightly supports the laborer.","translation":"It is necessary, in the nature of the case, that the farmer who toils to the point of exhaustion be the first to share in the crops."},{"c1":2,"v1":7,"c2":2,"v2":7,"label":"2 Timothy 2:7","text":"'Consider' is noeō, 'grasp with the mind' — Paul isn't asking Timothy to file away three pleasant pictures but to work out what each one demands of him: the soldier's undivided focus, the athlete's disciplined obedience to the rules, the farmer's willingness to labor hard before he can expect a return.","translation":"Grasp the meaning of what I am saying; for the Lord will give you understanding in everything."},{"c1":2,"v1":8,"c2":2,"v2":10,"label":"2 Timothy 2:8–10","text":"'Raised from the dead' is a perfect participle — not simply 'he rose' but 'he was raised, and remains, permanently, alive.' 'From the dead' is literally 'out from among the dead ones': Christ rose out of that company while the rest stayed in it, which is exactly why his resurrection functions as the guarantee, not just an example, of ours. 'Evildoer' is kakourgos, a technical legal term for a criminal — the specific charge under which Paul was chained, 'to the extent of bonds.' But 'the word of God is not bound' uses the perfect tense too: it 'has not been shackled, and remains unshackled' — Paul's imprisonment can restrain his body but not the message. 'The elect' comes from eklegō, 'to pick out'; those picked out are chosen not merely for their own benefit but with a view to the salvation of others through them, which is why Paul can call his own suffering something endured for their sake.","translation":"Keep remembering Jesus Christ, raised out from among the dead, from the offspring of David, according to my gospel, for which I am suffering hardship to the point of chains, as a criminal. But the word of God has not been shackled — it remains unshackled. For this reason I am enduring all things for the sake of the chosen ones, so that they too may obtain the salvation that is in Christ Jesus, together with eternal glory."},{"c1":2,"v1":11,"c2":2,"v2":13,"label":"2 Timothy 2:11–13","text":"'If' throughout this saying is the particle for a fulfilled condition — not a hypothetical 'suppose we died with him' but 'since we did die with him,' an aorist pointing to an accomplished fact, the believer's identification with Christ's death and resurrection. 'We shall live with him' pairs 'with' with the instrumental case — life not merely alongside Christ but by means of him, he himself being the life. 'Believe not' doesn't describe honest doubt; in this context it means unfaithfulness — proving untrue to him in how one actually lives, not failing to intellectually assent. And the last line turns on God's own nature rather than ours: he remains faithful because faithfulness is what he is — for him to abandon that would be to deny himself, which his own character rules out.","translation":"Trustworthy is this statement: since we died together with him, we shall also live by means of him; since we are enduring, we shall also reign as kings with him; since we are denying him, that One will deny us; since we are being unfaithful, that One remains faithful — for he is not able to deny himself."},{"c1":2,"v1":14,"c2":2,"v2":14,"label":"2 Timothy 2:14","text":"'Strive about words' is logomacheō, a compound built straight from 'word' and 'fight' — literally 'word-fighting,' wrangling over phrasing rather than substance. Paul says it produces nothing chrēsimos, 'fit for use' — not that it's merely unhelpful, but that it yields nothing usable at all. 'Subverting' is katastrephō, the root of our word 'catastrophe' — the picture is of ground plowed under or a city leveled, not a minor setback but a ruin.","translation":"Keep reminding them of these things, solemnly charging them before God not to be endlessly word-fighting — a practice that produces not one useful thing, but ruins those who listen."},{"c1":2,"v1":15,"c2":2,"v2":15,"label":"2 Timothy 2:15","text":"'Study' is spoudazō, a verb of urgency — 'make haste, exert full effort,' not leisurely diligence but hustle. 'Shew thyself approved' pairs paristēmi, 'present, exhibit,' with dokimos, the word for something tested and found up to standard — Paul pictures a workman whose work has passed inspection, with nothing to be ashamed of when it's examined. 'Rightly dividing' is orthotomeō, 'cut straight' — a compound that suggests the careful, exact work of a mason squaring stone or a craftsman cutting cloth true to the line, applied here to handling the truth honestly rather than twisting it to score points.\n\nPaul then turns to what to avoid: 'shun' is periistēmi, 'turn yourself around and away from' — an active sidestep, not passive avoidance. 'Profane' is bebēlos, built from the word for a threshold anyone may tread — common ground, as opposed to what is set apart. 'Vain babblings' is kenophōnia, 'empty sound' — and Paul's point is that emptiness never stays empty; since nature won't tolerate a vacuum, hollow talk fills itself with something, and that something is corruption. 'Canker' is gangraina, our own word gangrene — the picture of infection that, left untreated, keeps spreading and eating away at healthy tissue, exactly what false teaching does to a church body.","translation":"Make every effort to present yourself to God approved — a workman with nothing to be ashamed of, cutting a straight path through the word of truth. But turn yourself away from unhallowed, empty talk — for it will progress further into ungodliness, and their word will spread like gangrene."},{"c1":2,"v1":19,"c2":2,"v2":19,"label":"2 Timothy 2:19","text":"'The foundation of God standeth sure' actually reads 'the firm foundation of God stands' — 'firm,' meaning immovable, modifies foundation directly rather than asserting its stability as a separate claim. 'Standeth' is a perfect tense: it has stood, and by virtue of that, stands now, permanently. The seal that follows carries two inscriptions functioning as one unit — one describing God's own certain knowledge of who belongs to him, the other calling those who claim that name to a matching holiness. 'Nameth' is onomazō, not casual reference but a pronouncing of the name as a claim — invoking it is itself a confession of allegiance, and Paul ties that confession directly to an obligation: depart from unrighteousness or the naming is empty.","translation":"Nevertheless, God's firm foundation has stood, and stands now, bearing this seal: The Lord knows those who are his, and, Let everyone who names the name of the Lord depart from unrighteousness."},{"c1":2,"v1":22,"c2":2,"v2":22,"label":"2 Timothy 2:22","text":"'Flee' and 'follow' are set as opposites in force: 'follow' is diōkō, 'pursue' — the same word Paul uses elsewhere for chasing down a goal with real exertion, not casual imitation. 'Faith' here is better read as 'faithfulness,' in the sense of fidelity and reliability, since Paul is listing virtues rather than talking about belief. 'Charity' is agapē — not almsgiving, as the English word suggests today, but the deliberate, self-giving love the Spirit produces, the kind willing to spend itself for someone else's good.","translation":"Keep fleeing the passions of youth, but keep pursuing righteousness, faithfulness, love, and peace, together with those who call on the Lord out of a clean heart."},{"c1":2,"v1":23,"c2":2,"v2":23,"label":"2 Timothy 2:23","text":"'Unlearned' is apaideutos — the negative of the verb 'to train a child' — describing not a lack of book-knowledge but a mind that's never been disciplined, never drilled in how to think. 'Avoid' is a flat refusal, and 'gender' is 'beget' — Paul pictures these undisciplined questions as literally fathering quarrels, a natural offspring rather than an occasional side effect.","translation":"But refuse foolish and undisciplined questionings, knowing that they constantly beget quarrels."},{"c1":2,"v1":24,"c2":2,"v2":26,"label":"2 Timothy 2:24–26","text":"'Servant' is doulos, 'bondslave' — the Lord's, and by extension his people's, complete possession. 'Gentle' describes outward demeanor rather than inner disposition, and 'instructing' is better rendered 'correcting,' since the verb here carries the sense of setting someone straight rather than merely informing them. The verb 'will give' is optative — a mood marking an outcome as undetermined, a remote possibility rather than a guarantee, which fits Paul's carefully tentative 'if perhaps.' 'Recover themselves' is literally 'sober up' — the same image used elsewhere for waking from a drunken stupor, matched against 'taken captive,' a perfect participle describing people who have been and remain held by the devil's snare. Correction, on this picture, isn't punishing an enemy; it's trying to rouse someone out of an intoxication they didn't fully choose.","translation":"And the Lord's bondslave must not, in the nature of the case, quarrel, but must be gentle toward all, skilled in teaching, patient of wrongs, correcting his opponents with gentleness — perhaps God may grant them repentance leading to a precise knowledge of the truth, and they may sober up out of the devil's snare, where they have been held captive, to do that One's will."},{"c1":3,"v1":8,"c2":3,"v2":8,"label":"2 Timothy 3:8","text":"'Withstood' is anistēmi, 'to set oneself against' — deliberate, active opposition, not passive disagreement. Paul draws the comparison in degree, not method: these teachers oppose the truth the way Jannes and Jambres opposed Moses — not necessarily by magic, but with the same stubborn intensity. 'Reprobate' is adokimos, the same word used elsewhere for failing a test — men who have been examined and found wanting, disqualified with respect to the very faith they claim to teach.","translation":"Now, just as Jannes and Jambres set themselves against Moses, so these also set themselves against the truth — men corrupted in mind, disqualified concerning the faith, having been tested and found wanting."},{"c1":3,"v1":9,"c2":3,"v2":9,"label":"2 Timothy 3:9","text":"'Proceed' is prokoptō, literally 'hammer forward' — the picture of a smith forging metal by repeated blows, here used of advancing in a course of action. Paul says that forward motion will stop; their anoia, 'folly' — a word carrying overtones of derangement, not mere mistake — will become as publicly obvious as it did with Jannes and Jambres, whose tricks collapsed in front of Pharaoh's court.","translation":"But they will make no further progress, for their derangement will become obvious to everyone, just as that of those two became obvious."},{"c1":3,"v1":10,"c2":3,"v2":11,"label":"2 Timothy 3:10–11","text":"'Hast fully known' is parakoloutheō, a compound meaning literally 'to follow alongside' — the word for a disciple who attaches himself to a teacher and stays close, conforming his own life to the pattern. It's stronger than 'you learned facts about me'; it describes discipleship, the aorist tense pointing back to the specific moment Timothy threw in his lot with Paul. The pronoun 'you' is placed for emphasis — 'you, in contrast to others' — as Paul singles Timothy out from those in Asia who had turned away.","translation":"But as for you, you attached yourself as a disciple to me — because of my teaching, conduct, purpose, faith, patience, love, endurance, the persecutions and sufferings that happened to me in Antioch, Iconium, and Lystra, such persecutions as I endured; and out of them all the Lord rescued me."},{"c1":3,"v1":12,"c2":3,"v2":12,"label":"2 Timothy 3:12","text":"'Will live godly' isn't a flat future tense — the verb is thelō, 'to desire, to be bent on.' Paul isn't predicting that godly living happens to attract persecution; he's saying that anyone whose will is actually set on a godly life in Christ Jesus will be persecuted, as a matter of course, not as an unlucky exception.","translation":"And all, in fact, who are determined to live a godly life in Christ Jesus will be persecuted."},{"c1":3,"v1":13,"c2":3,"v2":13,"label":"2 Timothy 3:13","text":"'Seducers' is goēs, originally the word for a wailing incantation-chanter — a sorcerer who works by spells — narrowed here to mean an outright impostor. 'Wax worse and worse' is prokoptō again, the same 'hammer forward' word from the previous verse — these men make progress too, but in the wrong direction, deceiving others while sliding further into their own self-deception, the same verb working both ways at once.","translation":"But pernicious men and impostors will progress further toward the worst, deceiving and being deceived."},{"c1":4,"v1":2,"c2":4,"v2":2,"label":"2 Timothy 4:2","text":"'Preach' is kērussō, the verb for a herald's formal proclamation of an emperor's message — delivered with an authority that expects to be heeded, not a speaker's private opinion offered for consideration. The command form itself is sharp and summary, almost military. 'Be instant in season, out of season' uses a word meaning 'stand ready, be at hand' — Timothy is to hold himself in constant readiness whether the moment is favorable or unfavorable; there's no closed season on the task.\n\nTwo different words for correction follow. 'Reprove' is elegchō, a rebuke aimed at bringing the hearer to actually acknowledge guilt. 'Rebuke' is epitimaō, sharper and more severe, used whether or not the person ever admits fault — it carries a hint of consequences to come. 'Exhort' softens the tone again: it means 'urge, plead, beg,' and it's to be done with makrothumia, a temper that absorbs provocation without lashing back, paired with solid teaching rather than mere emotional pressure.","translation":"Herald the word with full authority. Stand ready with it whether the time is opportune or inopportune. Reprove so as to bring conviction; rebuke sharply; plead and urge — doing all this with a temper that will not retaliate, and with careful, patient instruction."},{"c1":4,"v1":5,"c2":4,"v2":5,"label":"2 Timothy 4:5","text":"'Watch' is nēphō, 'be sober, alert, clear-headed' — the opposite of drifting through the work in a fog. 'Endure hardships' is a sharp aorist imperative, a crisp command rather than a gentle suggestion — fitting, since Timothy's temperament tended toward caution rather than grit. 'Evangelist' has no definite article in the Greek, which shifts the weight from office to character: not 'go take up the office of evangelist' but 'let your work carry an evangelistic quality' — every pastor's ministry should have that outward reach built in. 'Make full proof' means 'carry a thing through to completion, perform it fully' — nothing done by halves.","translation":"But as for you, be sober-minded in all things, endure hardship, let your work carry the character of one who brings good news, and carry your ministry through to its full completion."},{"c1":4,"v1":6,"c2":4,"v2":6,"label":"2 Timothy 4:6","text":"'I am now ready to be offered' uses spendomai, the word for a drink-offering poured out at a pagan altar — the same image Paul reaches for in Philippians, of his own blood as a libation poured over the more important sacrifice, the church's service. 'Departure' is analuō, literally 'to unloose, break up' — a word soldiers used for striking camp and sailors used for weighing anchor. Paul the tentmaker reaches for the soldier's picture: he's not ending, he's breaking camp. 'Is at hand' means 'stands ready, present' — as though death itself were already standing at the tent flap.","translation":"For as for me, my blood is already being poured out as a drink offering, and the time for me to break camp has arrived."},{"c1":4,"v1":7,"c2":4,"v2":7,"label":"2 Timothy 4:7","text":"All three verbs here sit in the perfect tense — not 'I fought, ran, kept' as isolated past events, but 'I have fought, and the fight is settled; I have run, and the race is over; I have guarded, and the trust stands secure' — completed action with a present, ongoing result. 'The good fight' carries the definite article in Greek, 'the fight,' the specific one set before him, not a generic contest; and 'good' here points to external, visible beauty of execution rather than moral goodness — the aesthetics of a fight well fought, the way a wrestler's form can be called beautiful. 'Course' is a runner's track. 'Kept' is 'guard' — the same stewardship language Paul has used throughout the letter for the gospel deposit.","translation":"I have fought the good fight to its finish, and I am resting now in its victory. I have run the course to its end, and I am resting now at the finish line. I have guarded the trust, and it stands secure."},{"c1":4,"v1":8,"c2":4,"v2":8,"label":"2 Timothy 4:8","text":"'Crown' is stephanos, the athlete's victory wreath, not a royal diadem — Paul is still standing at the finish line from the previous verse, now looking toward the awards stand. 'Judge' is kritēs, which in this athletic picture reads as 'umpire,' the official who scores the contest fairly, not a courtroom figure. 'Give' means 'award' — the vocabulary of handing out a prize that's been earned within the rules. 'Loved his appearing' uses a perfect-tense verb: a love that was fixed on Christ's return at some point and has stayed fixed there ever since, a settled orientation of life rather than a passing sentiment.","translation":"From now on, there is reserved for me the victor's wreath of righteousness, which the Lord, the umpire who judges rightly, will award me on that day — and not only to me, but to all who have set their love, and kept it set, on his appearing."},{"c1":4,"v1":9,"c2":4,"v2":9,"label":"2 Timothy 4:9","text":"'Do thy diligence' is spoudazō again, the same urgent 'make every effort' verb Paul used earlier in this letter — this isn't a casual invitation but a request pressed with real urgency. Paul is alone and running out of time.","translation":"Make every effort to come to me quickly."},{"c1":4,"v1":10,"c2":4,"v2":12,"label":"2 Timothy 4:10–12","text":"'Forsaken' is egkataleipō, a compound built from 'leave,' 'down,' and 'in' — stronger than plain desertion, it pictures leaving someone down in circumstances stacked against him, helpless. It's the same verb behind our expression 'let down.' 'World' is aiōn, not the physical earth but the present age — its whole climate of values, the pull Demas apparently found more attractive than standing by a chained old man. A small grammatical hinge matters in verse 12: the connecting word is adversative, 'but,' distinguishing Tychicus, whom Paul himself dispatched to Ephesus, from Crescens and Titus, who had left on their own initiative. 'Take Mark' means literally 'pick up' — a practical, en-route instruction, not a formal summons.","translation":"For Demas let me down — he set his heart on this present age and came to love it — and he went off to Thessalonica; Crescens to Galatia, Titus to Dalmatia. Luke alone is with me. Pick up Mark and bring him with you, for he is useful to me for the work. But Tychicus I sent off to Ephesus."},{"c1":4,"v1":13,"c2":4,"v2":13,"label":"2 Timothy 4:13","text":"'Cloak' names a specific garment — a circular, felt travel-cape reaching to the knees, the kind woven from the same tough goat's hair Paul had handled for years as a tentmaker, made to shed wind and rain. 'Books' are papyrus rolls; 'parchments' are animal-skin manuscripts, more durable and valuable than papyrus. Even facing execution, Paul is still asking for his library. In the verses that follow about Alexander, 'did me much evil' points to actual deeds, not just words. 'The Lord will reward him' is a plain future tense, a statement of fact rather than a curse invoked in anger. 'Beware' turns the guarding word toward self-protection — a term that assumes the threat comes from outside, an assault to be defended against rather than provoked.","translation":"Bring, when you come, the cloak I left at Troas with Carpus, and the papyrus rolls — above all, the parchments. Alexander the coppersmith did me great harm; the Lord will pay him back according to his deeds. Be on guard against him yourself, for he strongly opposed our words."},{"c1":4,"v1":16,"c2":4,"v2":16,"label":"2 Timothy 4:16","text":"'Answer' is apologia, literally 'a talking of oneself off from' a charge — the technical term for a formal legal defense. 'No man stood with me' uses the word for someone appearing in court on the accused's behalf, as advocate or character witness. Nobody showed up. 'Forsook' is the same word used of Demas earlier in the chapter — the whole church in Rome, in that moment, let him down.","translation":"At my first defense, no one stood at my side — everyone let me down. May it not be counted against them."},{"c1":4,"v1":17,"c2":4,"v2":17,"label":"2 Timothy 4:17","text":"Where no human advocate appeared, 'the Lord stood with me' uses a verb meaning 'stand alongside' — the same one Paul uses elsewhere for standing by someone and taking responsibility for their needs. The Lord filled the role no one else would. 'Strengthened' pictures being clothed with strength from outside, not summoning it from within. 'Delivered out of the mouth of the lion' echoes the language of the Psalms — not a claim that Paul escaped execution, since he's already spoken of his blood being poured out, but that he wasn't defeated by fear into silence; he got to finish proclaiming the gospel before the whole imperial court.","translation":"But the Lord stood at my side and clothed me with strength, so that through me the proclamation might be fully made and all the Gentiles might hear it. And I was pulled out of the lion's mouth."},{"c1":4,"v1":18,"c2":4,"v2":22,"label":"2 Timothy 4:18–22","text":"'Deliver' is ruomai, a tender word meaning 'to draw to oneself, out of harm's way' — rescue pictured as being pulled close rather than merely being spared. And 'every evil work' most likely points inward rather than outward: not protection from some external villain, but from the one failure Paul would have counted truly evil — collapsing into silence instead of testifying when it mattered most. He closes with the ordinary business of any letter: greetings, news of where his coworkers are, a last request to come before winter.","translation":"And the Lord will draw me to himself, away from every pernicious deed, and will keep me safe for his heavenly kingdom — to him be the glory forever and ever. Amen. Greet Prisca and Aquila, and the household of Onesiphorus. Erastus remained in Corinth, and Trophimus I left behind in Miletus, sick. Make every effort to come before winter. Eubulus greets you, and Pudens, and Linus, and Claudia, and all the brothers. The Lord be with your spirit. Grace be with you."}],"Titus":[{"c1":1,"v1":5,"c2":1,"v2":5,"label":"Titus 1:5","text":"\"Left\" translates apoleipō (ἀπολείπω), a verb for a temporary leaving-behind, not the permanent departure implied by its cousin kataleipō — Paul's stay in Crete with Titus was meant to end, with Titus staying on only to finish the job. \"Set in order\" is epidiorthoō (ἐπιδιορθόω), a term doctors used for splinting a broken limb or straightening a crooked one; the churches in Crete weren't broken beyond repair, just still bent, needing the adjustments Paul hadn't gotten to. Those remaining tasks are literally \"the things left\" (ta leiponta), unfinished business rather than failures. \"Ordain\" is kathistēmi (καθίστημι), literally \"set down\" — to install someone into a standing office — and \"appoint\" near the end is diatassō (διατάσσω), \"give a charge,\" the word for issuing a formal directive. Titus wasn't left with vague suggestions; he had marching orders.","translation":"For this reason I left you behind temporarily in Crete, so that you might set straight the things still left undone, and might appoint elders in every city, as I directed you."},{"c1":1,"v1":6,"c2":1,"v2":9,"label":"Titus 1:6–9","text":"\"Blameless\" is anegklētos (ἀνέγκλητος): egkaleō means to bring someone up on formal charges, and the alpha-prefix negates it — not sinless, but a life offering no legal opening for an accusation to stick. \"Riot\" translates asōtia (ἀσωτία), built from the negative of sōzō, \"to save\" — it names a life that can't be salvaged, money and character both poured out past recovery. \"Unruly\" is anupotaktos (ἀνυπότακτος), \"un-subjectable,\" a child who won't come under anyone's control. \"Steward\" is oikonomos (οἰκονόμος), from oikos (\"house\") and the root behind nomos (\"law, management\") — the overseer runs God's household the way a steward runs an estate, accountable for what isn't his own. \"Self-willed\" is authadēs (αὐθάδης), literally \"self-pleasing\" (autos + hēdomai) — a man whose own satisfaction is his final court of appeal. \"Soon angry\" is orgilos (ὀργίλος), the adjective form of orgē, \"anger\" — a short fuse built in, not just occasional flashes. \"Temperate\" is egkratēs (ἐγκρατής), \"having power over\" — the same self-mastery Paul lists among the Spirit's fruit in Galatians 5. \"Holding fast\" the trustworthy word is antechō (ἀντέχω), a grip strong enough to resist being pried loose. The overseer must be able both to \"exhort\" (parakaleō, literally \"call alongside,\" urging rather than commanding) and to \"convince\" or \"convict\" (elegchō), a word for cross-examining an opponent until he is forced to admit he is wrong.","translation":"If anyone is beyond reproach, husband of one wife, with children who are believers, not open to the charge of reckless living or rebellion — for as God's household manager, the overseer must be beyond reproach — not self-pleasing, not quick-tempered, not given to wine, not violent, not greedy for money, but hospitable, a lover of what is good, sound-minded, just, holy, self-controlled, holding fast to the trustworthy word as it has been taught, so that he may be able both to encourage with sound teaching and to refute those who contradict it."},{"c1":1,"v1":10,"c2":1,"v2":11,"label":"Titus 1:10–11","text":"\"Vain talkers\" is mataiologos (ματαιολόγος), pairing mataios (\"empty, resultless\") with logos (\"word\") — talk that produces nothing, noise without substance. \"Deceivers\" is phrenapatēs (φρεναπάτης), literally a \"mind-deceiver\" (phrēn, \"mind,\" plus apataō, \"deceive\") — subtler than an outright liar, someone who plants a false impression in another person's thinking. \"To stop the mouth\" is epistomizō (ἐπιστομίζω), built on stoma, \"mouth\" — the image is a bit forced into a horse's mouth to shut it. \"Subvert\" is anatrepō (ἀνατρέπω), \"to overturn, capsize\" — these teachers aren't just misguiding individuals, they're flipping entire households (oikos) over.","translation":"For there are many rebellious people, empty talkers and deceivers of the mind, especially those of the circumcision, whose mouths must be stopped — men who are overturning whole households, teaching things they should not, for the sake of dishonest gain."},{"c1":1,"v1":12,"c2":1,"v2":12,"label":"Titus 1:12","text":"Paul quotes a Cretan poet turning his own countryman's words against the false teachers. \"Always\" is aei (ἀεί), \"perpetually, without exception\" — the charge of lying is constitutional, not occasional. \"Slow bellies\" literally reads gastēr argos (γαστὴρ ἀργός), \"an idle stomach\" — argos is built from a- privative plus ergon, \"work,\" so \"without work.\" The image isn't slowness of digestion but appetite that never does anything productive — men defined by their stomachs rather than their labor.","translation":"A certain one of their own, a prophet of their own people, has said: Cretans are always liars, evil beasts, idle bellies."},{"c1":1,"v1":13,"c2":1,"v2":14,"label":"Titus 1:13–14","text":"\"Sharply\" is apotomōs (ἀποτόμως), from apotemnō, \"to cut off\" — the rebuke should be a clean, decisive cut, not a lingering scolding. \"Giving heed\" is prosechō (προσέχω), literally \"to hold toward\" — more than listening, it's granting consent, letting something take hold of you. The false teachers \"turn themselves away\" (anastrephō in the middle voice) from the truth — the verb puts the responsibility on them; nobody dragged them off, they turned themselves.","translation":"This testimony is true. For this reason, rebuke them sharply, so that they may be healthy in the faith, not holding on to Jewish myths and commandments of men who are turning themselves away from the truth."},{"c1":1,"v1":15,"c2":1,"v2":16,"label":"Titus 1:15–16","text":"The purity in view here is ceremonial, the kind bound up in man-made rules about clean and unclean, not moral purity — the same category Jesus addresses when he tells the Pharisees that unwashed hands don't defile a meal. \"Profess\" is homologeō (ὁμολογέω), \"to say the same thing\" — an agreement in word. \"Reprobate\" is adokimos (ἀδόκιμος): dokimos describes metal that has passed assay by fire; the alpha-prefix flips it to metal tested and rejected. These men claim to know God in word while failing the one test that matters, their own conduct.","translation":"To the pure, all things are pure; but to those who are defiled and unbelieving, nothing is pure — rather, both their mind and their conscience have been defiled. They claim to know God, but by their deeds they deny him, being detestable and disobedient, and disqualified for any good work."},{"c1":2,"v1":1,"c2":2,"v2":1,"label":"Titus 2:1","text":"\"Thou\" translates the emphatic pronoun su (σύ), set in sharp contrast to the false teachers just described — you, unlike them. \"Become\" is prepō (πρέπω), \"to stand out, be conspicuously fitting\" — Titus's teaching should visibly match sound doctrine, the way a garment properly fits its wearer.","translation":"But as for you — go on speaking the things that are fitting for sound teaching."},{"c1":2,"v1":2,"c2":2,"v2":2,"label":"Titus 2:2","text":"\"Aged men\" is presbutēs (πρεσβύτης), a matter of years, not church office. \"Sober\" is nēphalios (νηφάλιος), tied to nēphō, \"to be free of wine, clear-headed.\" \"Grave\" is semnos (σεμνός), \"dignified, worthy of reverence.\" \"Temperate\" is sōphrōn (σώφρων), built from sōs (\"sound\") and phrēn (\"mind\") — a mind kept healthy and in control of itself. \"Sound\" in \"sound in faith\" is hugiainō (ὑγιαίνω), the ordinary Greek word for physical health, applied here to doctrine free of any diseased admixture. \"Patience\" renders hupomonē, literally \"remaining under\" — not passive waiting but staying put under pressure without buckling.","translation":"That aged men be clear-headed, dignified, sound-minded, healthy in the faith, in the love, in the patient endurance."},{"c1":2,"v1":3,"c2":2,"v2":5,"label":"Titus 2:3–5","text":"\"Behavior\" is katastēma (κατάστημα), broader than conduct — it's bearing, demeanor, the whole way a person carries herself. \"Holiness\" here is hieroprepēs (ἱεροπρεπής), built from hieron (\"temple, sacred place\") and prepō (\"to be fitting\") — behavior appropriate to something set apart for God, temple-grade reverence. \"False accusers\" is diabolos (διάβολος) — the same word rendered \"devil\" elsewhere, a slanderer who hurls accusations across at someone. \"Given to much wine\" uses the perfect participle of douloō, \"to enslave\" — the perfect tense marks a settled condition, not a bad habit but a completed enslavement. \"Teachers of good things\" is one compound word, kalodidaskalos (καλοδιδάσκαλος), kalos (\"good\") fused to didaskalos (\"teacher\"). What follows, \"they may teach\" the young women, is actually sōphronizō (σωφρονίζω), a causative verb meaning \"to bring someone to their senses, train them into soundness of mind\" — coaching, not mere instruction. \"Keepers at home\" is oikourgos (οἰκουργός), \"one who works at the house\" — active management, not confinement. \"Obedient\" is hupotassō (ὑποτάσσω), a military term for ranking soldiers under a commanding officer — voluntary, ordered submission, not coercion.","translation":"Older women likewise are to be reverent in bearing, not slanderers, not enslaved to much wine, teachers of what is good, so that they may train the young women to be fond of their husbands, fond of their children, sound-minded, pure, workers at home, kind, putting themselves in subjection to their own husbands, so that the word of God may not be spoken against."},{"c1":2,"v1":6,"c2":2,"v2":8,"label":"Titus 2:6–8","text":"\"Sober minded\" translates sōphroneō (σωφρονέω), the verb form of the same sound-mindedness word used of the older men and women — self-mastery is the one virtue Paul asks of every group in this chapter, worded a little differently each time. \"Exhort\" remains parakaleō, urging rather than commanding — Titus is to call alongside, not bark orders.","translation":"Urge the younger men likewise to keep themselves under control; in everything showing yourself a model of good works, in your teaching showing integrity and dignity, sound speech that cannot be condemned, so that the opponent may be put to shame, having nothing bad to say about us."},{"c1":2,"v1":9,"c2":2,"v2":10,"label":"Titus 2:9–10","text":"\"Be obedient\" is again hupotassō, but here in the middle voice — the slave is to place himself under his master's authority, an act of the will rather than a status forced on him. \"Master\" is despotēs (δεσπότης), our word \"despot,\" absolute ownership and authority. \"Answering again\" is antilegō (ἀντιλέγω), \"to speak against\" — more than a smart remark, it covers quietly working against the master's stated wishes. \"Purloining\" is nosphizō (νοσφίζω), skimming off for oneself a portion of what was entrusted to one's care — embezzlement in miniature. \"Adorn\" is kosmeō (κοσμέω), from kosmos, \"an ordered, harmonious system\" — good conduct doesn't just avoid embarrassing the gospel, it makes the teaching about God attractive, gives it order and beauty in the world's eyes.","translation":"Urge slaves to put themselves in subjection to their own masters in everything, to give them satisfaction, not talking back, not pilfering, but showing all good faith, so that they may make the teaching of God our Savior attractive in every way."},{"c1":2,"v1":11,"c2":2,"v2":15,"label":"Titus 2:11–15","text":"\"Grace\" is charis (χάρις): in ordinary Greek ethics it named a favor given freely, expecting nothing back, but always between friends. The gospel breaks that boundary — God's charis lands on people who were his enemies, not his friends, which is what makes it grace rather than reciprocity. \"Hath appeared\" is epiphainō (ἐπιφαίνω), built on phainō, \"to shine\" — grace didn't arrive quietly, it broke into visibility. \"Teaching\" is paideuō (παιδεύω), the root of \"pedagogy\" — training that includes correction and discipline, not just information handed over. The grammar of \"that blessed hope\" and \"the glorious appearing\" matters: one article governs both phrases joined by \"and,\" which in Greek means they describe a single reality, not two — \"the appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior, Jesus Christ\" is one event, and one person is both God and Savior in that clause. \"For\" in \"gave himself for us\" is huper (ὑπέρ), a preposition that can mean \"instead of\" as much as \"on behalf of\" — substitution, not just benefit. \"Redeem\" is lutroō (λυτρόω), freedom purchased by paying a ransom price. \"Peculiar,\" finally, is periousios (περιούσιος), \"over and above\" the ordinary — not odd, but specially set aside, a people God claims as his own private possession beyond the general run of things.","translation":"For the grace of God that brings salvation to all men has appeared, training us so that, denying ungodliness and worldly desires, we may live self-controlled and righteous and godly lives in the present age, continually looking for the blessed hope — the appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior, Jesus Christ — who gave himself for us, in order that he might ransom us from all lawlessness and cleanse for himself a people set apart as his own possession, eager for good works. Keep speaking these things and exhorting and rebuking with full authority. Let no one look down on you."},{"c1":3,"v1":1,"c2":3,"v2":3,"label":"Titus 3:1–3","text":"\"Put in mind\" is hupomimniskō (ὑπομιμνήσκω), a repeated, ongoing reminder, not a one-time announcement. \"No brawlers\" is amachos (ἄμαχος), \"not a fighter\" (a- + machē, \"battle\"). \"Gentle\" is epieikeia (ἐπιείκεια), the quality of someone who has a right and doesn't press it to the limit — reasonableness that yields rather than insisting. \"Foolish\" is anoētos (ἀνόητος), lacking understanding, and \"disobedient\" is apeithēs (ἀπειθής), literally \"unpersuadable\" (a- + peithō, \"persuade\") — not just rule-breaking but immune to reason. \"Living\" is diagō (διάγω), \"to lead [one's days] through\" — the whole span of a life, not a single act.","translation":"Keep reminding them to put themselves in subjection to rulers and authorities, to be obedient, to be ready for every good work, to speak evil of no one, to be peaceable, reasonable, showing all gentleness to all men. For we too were once without understanding, unpersuadable, going astray, slaves to various desires and pleasures, spending our days in malice and envy, hateful, hating one another."},{"c1":3,"v1":4,"c2":3,"v2":7,"label":"Titus 3:4–7","text":"\"Love toward man\" is philanthrōpia (φιλανθρωπία), phileō (\"to love, be fond of\") fused to anthrōpos (\"man, mankind\") — our word \"philanthropy.\" \"Regeneration\" is palingenesia (παλιγγενεσία), palin (\"again\") plus the verb \"to become\" — a becoming-again, a fresh start at the root rather than a patch-up. \"Washing\" uses the noun from louō, which means a full bath, the whole body — distinct from the verb used of washing just the feet in John 13, which covers only a part. The regeneration Paul describes is total, not partial. \"Renewing\" is anakainōsis (ἀνακαίνωσις), kainos (\"new\") plus ana (\"again, up\") — where regeneration marks the single moment of new birth, renewing describes the ongoing, repeated remaking that follows it, the difference between being born and growing up.","translation":"But when the kindness and love for mankind of God our Savior appeared, he saved us — not by works done in righteousness that we ourselves performed, but according to his mercy — through the washing of new birth and the continual renewing of the Holy Spirit, whom he poured out on us richly through Jesus Christ our Savior, so that, having been justified by his grace, we might become heirs according to the hope of eternal life."},{"c1":3,"v1":8,"c2":3,"v2":8,"label":"Titus 3:8","text":"\"Faithful\" is pistos (πιστός), \"trustworthy,\" describing the saying just given as reliable rather than merely believed. \"Affirm\" carries an intensive prefix in the Greek, marking a steadfast, forceful assertion rather than a passing comment. \"Be careful\" is phrontizō (φροντίζω), \"to give careful thought to,\" and \"maintain\" is proistēmi (προΐστημι), literally \"to stand before\" — to take the lead in something, be out front doing it, not just approve of it from a distance. Believers are to be out in front of good works, not trailing behind them.","translation":"Trustworthy is the saying, and concerning these things I want you to speak with steadfast confidence, so that those who have believed God may give careful thought to taking the lead in good works."},{"c1":3,"v1":9,"c2":3,"v2":9,"label":"Titus 3:9","text":"\"Avoid\" is periistēmi (περιΐστημι) in the middle voice, \"to turn oneself around\" to get away from something — deliberate, active avoidance, not passive disinterest. \"Foolish\" is mōros (μωρός), the root of our word \"moron.\" \"Vain\" is mataios (μάταιος), \"empty, without result\" — arguments that go nowhere and produce nothing.","translation":"But turn yourself away from stupid controversies and genealogies and quarrels and fights about the law, for they are unprofitable and empty."},{"c1":3,"v1":10,"c2":3,"v2":11,"label":"Titus 3:10–11","text":"\"Heretick\" is hairetikos (αἱρετικός), from haireō, \"to take for oneself, choose\" — originally naming someone who picks his own doctrine rather than receives what's handed down, a self-appointed authority, a factionalist. \"Subverted\" is ektrepō (ἐκτρέπω), a medical term for a limb wrenched out of its joint — not just off the path but twisted out of true. \"Condemned of himself\" is autokatakritos (αὐτοκατάκριτος), auto (\"self\") plus the verb \"to judge down\" — his own conduct hands down the verdict against him; no outside witness is needed.","translation":"Reject a factious person after one or two warnings, knowing that such a one has been twisted out of joint and goes on sinning, standing self-condemned."},{"c1":3,"v1":12,"c2":3,"v2":12,"label":"Titus 3:12","text":"\"Be diligent\" is spoudazō (σπουδάζω), \"make every effort, hurry up and do it.\" \"Determined\" renders krinō in the perfect tense — a decision reached in the past that still stands, settled and not being reconsidered. Paul isn't musing about where to winter; he's already decided and the decision holds.","translation":"When I send Artemas or Tychicus to you, make every effort to come to me at Nicopolis, for I have determined to spend the winter there."},{"c1":3,"v1":13,"c2":3,"v2":13,"label":"Titus 3:13","text":"\"Lawyer\" translates nomikos (νομικός), which in the New Testament regularly means an expert in the Mosaic law, not a member of a Roman legal profession — Zenas's expertise was in Scripture, not statute.","translation":"Diligently send Zenas the lawyer and Apollos on their way, so that nothing may be lacking to them."},{"c1":3,"v1":14,"c2":3,"v2":14,"label":"Titus 3:14","text":"\"Maintain\" is again proistēmi, \"take the lead in\" — the same word used in verse 8, applied now to meeting real, pressing needs rather than staying unproductive.","translation":"And let our own people also learn to take the lead in good works for cases of urgent need, so that they may not be unfruitful."},{"c1":3,"v1":15,"c2":3,"v2":15,"label":"Titus 3:15","text":"\"Salute\" is aspazomai (ἀσπάζομαι), a warm greeting, a wish of well-being extended to someone, closing the letter on a personal note.","translation":"All who are with me greet you. Greet those who love us in the faith. Grace be with you all."}],"Hebrews":[{"c1":1,"v1":2,"c2":1,"v2":2,"label":"Hebrews 1:2","text":"The phrase behind \"these last days\" reads literally \"the last of these days\" — eschatos, the outermost point, the final edge of a stretch of time already underway. God had been speaking through the prophets across that whole span; now, at its very end, he speaks differently — not \"through a prophet\" but \"in Son.\" The Greek keeps the article before \"prophets,\" marking them out as a class of individuals, but drops it before \"Son,\" throwing the weight onto quality of nature rather than mere identity: this one speaks not as an instrument pressed into service but as one who by nature belongs to God's own being.\n\nHe is appointed \"heir of all things\" — the dominion Adam forfeited at the fall, regained for the race through the last Adam. And the world itself came into being dia (\"through\") him, a preposition for mediate agency, naming him active co-agent in creation rather than a passive tool. The word for \"worlds,\" aionas, doesn't mean simply the physical universe — it names the ages, the unfolding stretches of time through which God's purposes work themselves out. The Son isn't only the original craftsman of matter; he steers history's ages toward their goal.","translation":"In the last of these days, God spoke to us in one who is, in nature, Son — whom he appointed heir of all things, and through whom he also made the ages."},{"c1":1,"v1":5,"c2":1,"v2":5,"label":"Hebrews 1:5","text":"The writer strings together seven Old Testament quotations to prove the Son ranks above the angels, opening with Psalm 2:7, where the Greek word order throws \"Son\" to the front for emphasis: \"Son of mine — you are.\" Angels do get called \"sons of God\" in the Old Testament, but only collectively, never as individuals singled out by name; that title elsewhere belongs to Israel as a nation. \"Today I have begotten you\" isn't a statement about the Son's eternal existence or about his human birth — the verb marks an installation into office, not an entrance into life. It points to the resurrection, where God publicly enacted the Son's royal standing in a formal, official sense.\n\nThe second citation, from 2 Samuel 7, promises \"I will be to him a Father, and he will be to me a Son\" — words never once spoken over an angel. Angels are sons of God only by virtue of having been created; this claim to sonship rests on something no angel can share.","translation":"For to which of the angels did he ever say: Son of mine you are — today I have begotten you? And again: I will be to him a Father, and he himself will be to me a Son?"},{"c1":1,"v1":11,"c2":1,"v2":11,"label":"Hebrews 1:11","text":"The \"they\" that will perish is not the earth mentioned just before but the heavens — the Greek pronoun is intensive, \"they themselves,\" singling the heavens out for a fate set against what follows. \"You remain\" translates diameno, built from meno (\"remain\") with dia prefixed for intensifying force — not just staying but staying permanently — and it stands in the present tense, describing what is simply, continuously true of God rather than a future promise still to be kept.\n\n\"Wax old\" is palaioo, \"to become worn out,\" a different idea from the ordinary Greek word for age (archaios, \"ancient,\" old merely by the passage of time). The heavens don't simply get old the way a document ages sitting in an archive — they wear thin the way a garment wears out from use.","translation":"They themselves will perish, but as for you, you continually remain. And they will all wear out like a garment."},{"c1":1,"v1":12,"c2":1,"v2":12,"label":"Hebrews 1:12","text":"\"Vesture\" translates peribolaion, literally \"that which is thrown around\" — a cloak or wrap, something draped over oneself and eventually discarded, not formal dress. The verb behind the King James's \"shalt fold\" should actually read \"roll up\" — an old copying error produced \"fold\" instead — and the picture is of a worn mantle rolled up and set aside, exactly what happens to clothing no longer wanted.\n\n\"Shall not fail\" renders a verb, leipo, that means \"leave off, cease.\" The heavens get rolled up and exchanged like worn-out clothes, but God's years simply never cease. The angels belong to that same category of created, changeable things; the Son, unchanging, stands above them all.","translation":"And like a mantle you will roll them up; like a garment they will be changed. But as for you, you are the same, and your years will not cease."},{"c1":1,"v1":14,"c2":1,"v2":14,"label":"Hebrews 1:14","text":"The writer closes his angel-argument with a question expecting the answer \"yes.\" \"Ministering\" translates leitourgika, the same root used earlier of priestly service — angels serve in a quasi-sacred capacity, but as agents carrying out duty, not as principals in their own right. \"Sent forth\" is a present passive participle built on the same root as apostello, the verb behind \"apostle\" — angels are continually being dispatched on commission, ongoing service under orders rather than a fixed rank held on their own account.\n\nThat commission runs \"for the sake of those who are about to inherit salvation\" — the inheritance is still ahead, not yet possessed, and angels exist to serve the heirs, not to be heirs themselves. That is their entire job description, set against a Son who rules rather than runs errands.","translation":"Are they not all spirits engaged in sacred service, continually being sent out on commission for the sake of those who are about to inherit salvation?"},{"c1":2,"v1":3,"c2":2,"v2":3,"label":"Hebrews 2:3","text":"\"How\" translates pos used rhetorically — \"how is it even possible\" — a question that expects the answer \"there is no way.\" The pronoun \"we\" is emphatic in the Greek, throwing weight onto the readers themselves: it is precisely to them that God has spoken in the Son, which makes any negligence on their part all the more inexcusable. \"Escape\" is ekpheugo, \"to flee out from\" — the image is of a fugitive slipping free of a trap, and the writer insists there simply is no exit.\n\nThe word rendered \"which\" is qualitative rather than merely relative — not just \"a salvation that,\" but \"a salvation of this very character,\" one whose pedigree runs from the Lord himself (kurios, the word the Greek Old Testament uses for the divine name) straight to eyewitnesses. Salvation announced first by the Lord in person, not relayed through an intermediary.","translation":"How will we possibly escape if we disregard so great a salvation — a salvation of such a character as to have been spoken at the first by the Lord, and confirmed to us by those who heard him?"},{"c1":2,"v1":4,"c2":2,"v2":4,"label":"Hebrews 2:4","text":"The writer piles up four words for the confirming miracles, each carrying its own shade of meaning. Semeion (\"signs\") points to a miracle's meaning — a mark pointing beyond itself. Teras (\"wonders\") points to its strangeness — something so unusual it stops people in their tracks. Dunamis (\"miracles,\" literally \"power\") points to its source — an act carrying God's own inherent power, the root behind our word \"dynamite.\" \"Gifts\" translates merismos, \"distributions\" or \"impartations,\" and the grammar makes the Holy Spirit the active giver rather than a passive attribute being handed around.\n\nFour separate angles on the same body of evidence: what it signified, how strange it was, where its power came from, and who was doing the giving.","translation":"God also bearing them witness, both with signs and wonders and various works of power, and with distributions from the Holy Spirit according to his own will."},{"c1":2,"v1":7,"c2":2,"v2":7,"label":"Hebrews 2:7","text":"The question \"what is man\" carries, in its Hebrew background, the force of \"how small, how insignificant\" rather than \"how great.\" \"Son of man\" reflects a Hebrew idiom, \"son of Adam\" — pointing to man's earthy origin, formed out of dust. The Greek word chosen for \"man\" here is anthropos, the generic term for mankind, not aner, which would mean a male individual specifically — the whole race is in view.\n\n\"A little\" is better read as \"a little while\" than \"a little degree\" — the same word turns up two verses later describing the incarnation itself, so the sense is temporal: humanity, in Adam, was made lower than the angels for a limited time, not lower by some smaller measure. \"Crowned\" translates stephanoo, the word for the victor's wreath given for achievement and rank, not diadema, the royal crown of an office — Adam's glory was a crown earned by his standing as head of creation, not merely a badge of authority.","translation":"You made him, for a little while, lower than the angels; you crowned him, as a victor, with glory and honor."},{"c1":2,"v1":8,"c2":2,"v2":8,"label":"Hebrews 2:8","text":"The psalm's claim is total — \"all things\" placed under man's feet, with nothing held back. But the writer immediately sets that totality against plain observation: \"we do not yet see all things subjected to him.\" The tension is the point. Adam was given comprehensive dominion, and comprehensive dominion is exactly what present experience shows we no longer have.\n\nThe gap between the declared grant and the observed reality is where the fall shows itself: rule lost, not merely rule diminished. That gap is precisely what the verse that follows exists to close.","translation":"You put all things in subjection under his feet — for in subjecting all things to him, he left nothing that is not put under him. But now we do not yet see all things subjected to him."},{"c1":2,"v1":12,"c2":2,"v2":12,"label":"Hebrews 2:12","text":"This is a direct citation from Psalm 22, brought in purely as proof from Scripture for the claim just made — that the Son is not ashamed to call believers his brothers. The word rendered \"church\" is simply ekklesia, \"assembly\" — the Son is pictured singing praise in the middle of the gathered community, not standing apart from it.\n\nThe quotation does double duty: it supplies scriptural warrant for calling believers \"brethren,\" and it places that praise-song in a corporate, congregational setting rather than a private one.","translation":"I will announce your name to my brothers; in the middle of the assembly I will sing your praise."},{"c1":2,"v1":13,"c2":2,"v2":13,"label":"Hebrews 2:13","text":"This second citation comes from Isaiah, where the prophet's own children carried names that functioned as living prophecies — meanings tied to a coming judgment and a returning remnant. The bond Isaiah claims with those children, both of them standing as signs to the nation, becomes the model here for the bond between Christ and believers.\n\nIt's a household picture: a father naming his own as evidence of a shared mission, borrowed to show how completely the Son identifies with those he calls his brothers.","translation":"And again: I will put my trust in him. And again: Here am I, and the children God has given me."},{"c1":2,"v1":14,"c2":2,"v2":14,"label":"Hebrews 2:14","text":"Two different Greek verbs do careful work here, both usually flattened in English into \"share\" or \"partake.\" The children \"hold in common\" (koinoneo) flesh and blood — a nature they possess jointly with one another simply by being born. But Christ \"took hold of\" (metecho, literally \"hold with\") that same flesh and blood — a different root, describing something not natural to him but deliberately laid hold of and attached to himself. Humanity was never his by nature; he added it. The Greek order of the nouns, \"blood and flesh,\" reflects a set phrase for human nature as distinct from God's.\n\n\"Destroy\" translates katargeo, \"render inoperative, bring to nothing\" — not annihilate. The devil isn't erased; his grip is broken. And \"power\" is kratos, meaning dominion or sovereign reach rather than mere strength — death's hold over humanity was a kingdom, and that kingdom has been overturned.","translation":"Since, then, the children hold in common blood and flesh, he himself likewise took hold of the same, in order that through death he might render inoperative the one holding the dominion of death — that is, the devil."},{"c1":2,"v1":15,"c2":2,"v2":15,"label":"Hebrews 2:15","text":"\"Deliver\" translates apallasso, a word used in everyday Greek documents of a wife securing release from a marriage contract — a formal, legal setting-free, not a vague rescue. \"Subject to bondage\" is enochoi, built from echo (\"hold\") with en (\"in\") prefixed — literally \"held in.\"\n\nDeath, before Christ's victory, held its grip on people across their entire span of life, not as an occasional dread but as a constant, low-grade captivity of fear.","translation":"And release those who, through fear of death, were being held in bondage through the whole course of their lives."},{"c1":2,"v1":16,"c2":2,"v2":16,"label":"Hebrews 2:16","text":"\"Verily\" is dēpou, \"as is well known, doubtless\" — a throwaway aside assuming common ground with the reader. \"Took hold of\" renders epilambanetai, a verb pictured from someone grasping a person to pull them back from danger — laying hold for the purpose of rescue. The King James inserts the italicized words \"the nature of,\" which aren't in the Greek at all and actually blur the sense; the verb isn't about assuming a nature but about reaching out to help.\n\nChrist doesn't reach out to rescue angels; he reaches for Abraham's descendants.","translation":"For, as is well known, it is not angels he takes hold of to rescue, but he takes hold of Abraham's descendants, to bring them help."},{"c1":2,"v1":18,"c2":2,"v2":18,"label":"Hebrews 2:18","text":"The Greek word order shifts the emphasis from the English: \"in that which he suffered, having himself been tempted\" — the suffering explains what the temptation actually consisted of. \"Tempted\" is peirazomai, a word carrying two senses at once: putting something to the test to see what's really in it, and — because so many fail such tests — solicitation toward evil. Both are live here; Christ's sufferings were themselves the proving ground and the point of pressure toward sin.\n\n\"Succor\" translates boetheo, a compound built from \"cry\" and \"run\" — literally, to run at someone's cry for help. It isn't passive sympathy; it's an active response to a call.","translation":"For in that which he himself suffered, being put to the test, he is able to run to the cry of those who are being tested, and bring them help."},{"c1":3,"v1":1,"c2":3,"v2":1,"label":"Hebrews 3:1","text":"\"Holy\" here is positional rather than a report card on behavior — hagios, \"set apart,\" naming a status before God rather than a level of moral achievement. \"Partakers\" is metochoi, elsewhere translated \"associates\" — people bound together in a shared calling, here a heavenly one specifically, distinguishing this group from Old Testament saints who could equally be called \"holy brethren\" but whose calling and destiny were earthly.\n\n\"Consider\" is katanoeo, \"to fix the mind on, examine attentively\" — not a glance but a sustained, deliberate look. And \"apostle,\" applied to Christ here, is apostolos, the noun from apostello, the verb used throughout the Greek Old Testament for God dispatching Moses on a commission, and elsewhere for God sending the Son — the same verb, the same kind of formal, credentialed mission, for both men. \"Profession\" is better rendered \"confession\" — homologia, literally \"speaking the same thing,\" agreeing with a report already given rather than volunteering fresh testimony.","translation":"Therefore, holy brothers, associates in a heavenly calling, fix your minds attentively on the Apostle and High Priest of our confession — Jesus."},{"c1":3,"v1":2,"c2":3,"v2":2,"label":"Hebrews 3:2","text":"\"Faithful\" is present tense in the Greek, not past — not \"was faithful\" on some occasion but \"is faithful\" as a matter of settled, ongoing character. The comparison to Moses draws on God's own testimony in Numbers, where Moses is called faithful \"in all my house\" — the highest standard of human fidelity these readers knew.\n\nThe Son matches that same standard of faithfulness, but, as the argument that follows will show, in an entirely different capacity.","translation":"Who is faithful to the one who appointed him — as Moses also was faithful in all his house."},{"c1":3,"v1":3,"c2":3,"v2":3,"label":"Hebrews 3:3","text":"The logic runs on a plain analogy: a builder outranks the house he builds. Since the Son is the one who built the household — Israel, and by extension the household of faith — and Moses is himself only a member of that household, the Son necessarily outranks Moses.\n\nThe builder's glory always exceeds the glory of what he made.","translation":"For this one has been counted worthy of more glory than Moses, by as much as the one who built the house has more honor than the house itself."},{"c1":3,"v1":4,"c2":3,"v2":4,"label":"Hebrews 3:4","text":"The writer heads off a possible misreading. Crediting the Son with building the house doesn't set him up as an independent builder alongside God — every house has a builder, and the one who built everything is God.\n\nThe Son's specific role as builder of Israel's house is folded into, not separate from, God's role as builder of all things.","translation":"For every house is built by someone, but the one who built all things is God."},{"c1":3,"v1":6,"c2":3,"v2":6,"label":"Hebrews 3:6","text":"The preposition shift here carries the whole point: Moses was faithful en (\"in\") the house, a servant working within it; the Son is faithful epi (\"over\") the house, ruling above it as owner's son rather than staff. \"If\" translates ean, introducing a genuinely open, future condition — not a threat to a settled salvation, but a test that reveals whether the profession was ever real to begin with.\n\n\"Hold fast\" is katecho, a word used in nautical Greek for a ship holding its course toward land — the picture is of steady, sustained direction, not a single decisive grip. \"Confidence\" is parrēsia, built from \"all\" and \"speech\" — literally unrestrained, all-out speech, the boldness that comes from having nothing to hide and nothing to fear.","translation":"But Messiah, as a Son, is faithful over his house — whose house we are, if indeed we go on holding our course with the boldness and exultant confidence of our hope, firm until the end."},{"c1":3,"v1":9,"c2":3,"v2":9,"label":"Hebrews 3:9","text":"The word translated \"when\" actually means \"where\" — hou, a place-marker, tying the unbelief to the specific location of the wilderness testing. Two different words for \"test\" sit behind this verse: peirazo, testing to discover what's really there, whether good or evil, and dokimazo, testing in order to approve someone who passes.\n\nIsrael's fathers put God through the first kind — probing him with suspicion, as though he might fail — rather than approaching him with the confidence that assumes he will pass. That's the insult buried in the verse: testing God as though he were the one on trial.","translation":"Where your fathers tested me, to see what was in me, and saw my works forty years."},{"c1":3,"v1":11,"c2":3,"v2":11,"label":"Hebrews 3:11","text":"\"So\" translates hos, \"in keeping with which\" — tying this verse to the preceding testing as cause to consequence. The oath itself preserves a Hebrew idiom: literally \"if they shall enter,\" a self-imprecating formula meaning something like \"may I no longer be God if they enter\" — the strongest form a vow could take.\n\n\"Rest\" is katapausis, built from pauo (\"cease, stop\") with a prefix adding a sense of permanence — not a pause but a settled, lasting cessation from labor, here specifically Israel's promised tranquility in Canaan, forfeited by that generation's unbelief.","translation":"So I swore in my wrath: If they shall enter into my rest —"},{"c1":3,"v1":13,"c2":3,"v2":13,"label":"Hebrews 3:13","text":"\"Exhort\" is parakaleo, built from kaleo (\"call\") intensified by para — \"call urgently,\" beg, press. The same verb kaleo shows up again in \"today,\" meaning \"proclaim aloud\" — the phrase pictures someone crying out the word \"Today!\" for as long as it remains true. The definite article in front of \"Today\" ties this straight back to the earlier citation, keeping the same specific day in view.\n\n\"Deceitfulness\" translates apate, naming a trick or stratagem rather than a general quality of being deceptive — sin here isn't described as vaguely misleading but as running an active con. And \"the sin\" carries the definite article, pointing to one particular sin already named in context: apostasy, not sin in general.","translation":"But keep on exhorting one another, daily, as long as it is still called \"Today,\" so that none of you may be hardened by the trickery of sin."},{"c1":3,"v1":14,"c2":3,"v2":14,"label":"Hebrews 3:14","text":"\"Partakers\" is metochoi again, but the genitive that follows flips the relationship found in verse 6: there, believers are Christ's house, his possession; here, Christ is the believers' shared possession — \"partakers of Christ\" means co-holders of him. \"Are made\" translates gegonamen, the perfect tense of ginomai (\"become\") — grammar that reaches into the past and holds its result open into the present: \"we became, and as a result now are.\"\n\n\"Confidence\" is hupostasis, literally \"that which stands under\" — the same word used elsewhere in this letter for a \"title deed,\" borrowed from legal documents that proved ownership of property. Faith functions here as the paperwork underwriting the claim to salvation.","translation":"For we have become — and so now are — sharers together in Messiah, if indeed we go on holding the beginning of our confident ground firm until the end."},{"c1":3,"v1":16,"c2":3,"v2":16,"label":"Hebrews 3:16","text":"The Greek here hinges on a single accent mark distinguishing an indefinite pronoun (\"some\") from an interrogative one (\"who?\"). Read as a question, the verse becomes a pointed challenge rather than a vague statement: \"who, having heard, provoked?\" — followed immediately by its own answer.\n\nNot a few stragglers, but the entire generation that left Egypt under Moses. No one gets to imagine the failure belonged to someone else.","translation":"For who, having heard, provoked him? Was it not all who came out of Egypt through Moses?"},{"c1":3,"v1":17,"c2":3,"v2":17,"label":"Hebrews 3:17","text":"\"Carcases\" translates a Greek word whose singular form literally means \"a limb\" — the image underneath is dismemberment, bodies scattered rather than simply \"dead.\" Paul draws on the same picture in writing to Corinth, describing that generation as \"strewn down\" across the wilderness.\n\nNot a quiet burial, but bodies left where they fell, generation after generation, across forty years.","translation":"And with whom was he angry forty years? Was it not with those who sinned, whose bodies fell scattered in the wilderness?"},{"c1":3,"v1":18,"c2":3,"v2":18,"label":"Hebrews 3:18","text":"The verb for \"did not believe\" isn't pisteuo, the ordinary New Testament word for \"believe\" — it's apeitheo, \"to refuse to be persuaded, to be non-compliant, disobedient.\" The word choice matters: this wasn't simple doubt or an honest failure to be convinced.\n\nIt names a stubborn, willful refusal to comply, obstinacy dressed up as skepticism.","translation":"And to whom did he swear that they would not enter into his rest, except to those who refused to be persuaded?"},{"c1":3,"v1":19,"c2":3,"v2":19,"label":"Hebrews 3:19","text":"\"Unbelief\" is apistia, simply \"faith\" with a negating prefix attached — grammatically the bare opposite of trust. But the sequence in this short passage runs disobedience first, unbelief following as its result.\n\nA reminder that unbelief is rarely a neutral intellectual verdict; it grows out of a will already set against compliance.","translation":"So we see that they were not able to enter, because of unbelief."},{"c1":4,"v1":1,"c2":4,"v2":1,"label":"Hebrews 4:1","text":"\"Being left\" is a present participle — \"there being left behind and still remaining\" — stressing that the offer of rest hasn't expired; it remains open to this generation of readers. \"Come short of\" translates a verb that could mean actually falling short, being judged to have fallen short, or merely thinking one has fallen short.\n\nContext favors the last sense: the writer's fear is that his readers will talk themselves into believing it's too late for them, when the promise is, in fact, still on the table.","translation":"Let us therefore fear, lest, while a promise of entering into his rest still remains open, any of you should come to think that he has fallen short of it."},{"c1":4,"v1":2,"c2":4,"v2":2,"label":"Hebrews 4:2","text":"The verb behind \"not being mixed with faith\" is sugkerannumi, \"to blend together, commingle\" — a chemical picture, of two substances failing to combine into a single compound. The message reached their ears but never fused with faith in their minds, so it stayed inert, undissolved, doing nothing.\n\n\"Was preached\" reflects a periphrastic perfect construction — grammar built to stress not just that the message was delivered once, but that it was delivered so thoroughly its imprint persists to this day in the hearers' memory. There was no excuse of unclear delivery; the failure was entirely on the side of absorption.","translation":"For indeed we have had good news thoroughly announced to us — and it remains with us still — just as they also did; but the message they heard did not profit them, because it was not commingled by faith with those who heard it."},{"c1":4,"v1":3,"c2":4,"v2":3,"label":"Hebrews 4:3","text":"\"We... enter\" sits in the present tense — entering rest is described as an ongoing mark of believers, not a one-time past event or a future hope only. The rest itself was ready from the very foundation of the world, already prepared in God's completed work of creation.\n\nThe unbelieving wilderness generation didn't fail to enter because the rest wasn't ready; it was ready and waiting the whole time.","translation":"For we who have believed are entering into that rest, just as he said: As I swore in my wrath, they shall not enter into my rest — though his works were finished from the foundation of the world."},{"c1":4,"v1":4,"c2":4,"v2":4,"label":"Hebrews 4:4","text":"This verse simply supplies the writer's proof text from Genesis, grounding the whole discussion of \"rest\" in God's own rest on the seventh day of creation — the pattern every later promise of rest, for Israel and for believers, traces back to.","translation":"For he has said somewhere concerning the seventh day: And God rested on the seventh day from all his works."},{"c1":4,"v1":5,"c2":4,"v2":5,"label":"Hebrews 4:5","text":"The writer simply repeats the earlier quotation from Psalm 95, and the repetition itself is the argument — driving home, a second time, that this generation was formally excluded from the rest by God's own oath.","translation":"And again in this place: They shall not enter into my rest."},{"c1":4,"v1":6,"c2":4,"v2":6,"label":"Hebrews 4:6","text":"\"It remains\" is apoleipo, \"left over from earlier times\" — the offer of rest didn't expire with the wilderness generation; it carried forward, unclaimed. Those who first heard it are described with a passive participle from euaggelizomai — \"those who were the subjects of the good-news announcement\" — the emphasis falling on their having received it, not on how they responded.\n\n\"Unbelief\" here is again the \"non-persuasible\" word, apeitheia, matching the verb used two verses earlier — the same stubborn refusal to be won over, not a passive lack of information.","translation":"Since therefore it remains over for some to enter it, and those who first received the good news did not enter because of their refusal to be persuaded —"},{"c1":4,"v1":7,"c2":4,"v2":7,"label":"Hebrews 4:7","text":"\"He designates\" is horizo, \"to mark out the boundaries of, to fix precisely\" — God pins down a specific day, not a vague future opportunity. The writer flags that this fresh offer came five hundred years after Moses, spoken through David — proof by itself that the rest under Joshua's conquest of Canaan hadn't exhausted the promise, or God wouldn't have needed to reissue it centuries later.\n\n\"As it is said\" uses a perfect tense — \"as it has been said, and stands said still\" — the record isn't a fading memory; it's permanently on the books.","translation":"He again marks out a certain day, Today, saying through David after so long a time, just as it has been said before and stands said still: Today, if you hear his voice, do not go on hardening your hearts."},{"c1":4,"v1":8,"c2":4,"v2":8,"label":"Hebrews 4:8","text":"The name behind \"Joshua\" is the identical Greek word rendered \"Jesus\" everywhere else in the New Testament — Iesous — used of the actual man Joshua in only a couple of spots in the whole New Testament, this being one of them. The wordplay isn't incidental to the writer's argument: the earlier Joshua could only lead Israel into a temporary, physical rest.\n\nIf that rest had been final, there would have been no reason for God, five hundred years later, to speak of \"another day\" still to come. The very fact that a later offer was made proves the first was incomplete.","translation":"For if Joshua had given them rest, God would not have spoken of another day after that."},{"c1":4,"v1":10,"c2":4,"v2":10,"label":"Hebrews 4:10","text":"The writer returns here to katapausis, the general word for \"rest\" as cessation, after using a more specific word for Sabbath-keeping just before — a shift from the ideal, Sabbath-patterned rest to the plainer language of ceasing from labor.\n\n\"His own\" translates idios, a word marking private, personal ownership rather than shared or generic possession — God's works are peculiarly his, and the rest that follows is likewise the believer's own cessation, patterned after God's, not a borrowed or generic stopping-point.","translation":"For the one who has entered into his rest has himself also rested from his works, just as God rested from his own."},{"c1":4,"v1":11,"c2":4,"v2":11,"label":"Hebrews 4:11","text":"\"Let us labor\" is spoudazo, a verb combining urgency of purpose with urgency of effort — not calm diligence but hurried, intent exertion, the sort of word used for someone racing to finish before a deadline.\n\nThe warning that follows recycles the wilderness generation's failure as a cautionary \"example\" — the Greek word names a pattern or mold nobody should let themselves be poured into twice.","translation":"Let us therefore make every effort to enter into that rest, so that no one may fall into the same pattern of disobedience."},{"c1":4,"v1":12,"c2":4,"v2":12,"label":"Hebrews 4:12","text":"\"Living\" translates the present participle of zao — not a past-tense description but an ongoing state: the word is alive right now, actively so, not a static text. \"Powerful\" is energes, the root behind our word \"energy\" — active, energizing, doing work rather than merely existing. \"Sharper\" comes from temno, \"to cut\" — the comparative form measures the word's edge against the sharpest weapon its readers knew, a two-edged sword.\n\n\"Dividing asunder\" is easy to misread as one thing separated from another — soul from spirit, joint from marrow. The Greek genitive actually describes a piercing through each one internally; the sword doesn't cut soul away from spirit, it cuts through the soul, and through the spirit, laying bare what's inside each. \"Discerner\" is kritikos, built on krino (\"judge, sift, separate\"), the root behind our word \"critic\" — a capacity to sift and analyze, not merely observe.","translation":"For the word of God is living and active, and sharper than any two-edged sword, piercing through even to the dividing of soul and spirit, of joints and marrow, and is a sifter and analyzer of the reflections and conceptions of the heart."},{"c1":4,"v1":13,"c2":4,"v2":13,"label":"Hebrews 4:13","text":"\"Laid bare\" translates trachelizo, a verb built from trachelos, \"neck\" or \"throat\" — used of a wrestler seizing an opponent by the throat, or of a sacrificial victim's neck bent back to expose it to the knife. The image is graphic and specific: total, forced exposure, the kind inflicted on something that cannot resist.\n\n\"With whom we have to do\" carries the word logos in a commercial sense familiar from everyday Greek business language — \"to render account,\" to settle a ledger. Every created thing stands exposed like a sacrifice bared to the blade, and every person owes God a reckoning like an account come due.","translation":"And there is no creature hidden from his sight, but all things are naked and laid bare, throats exposed, to the eyes of him to whom we must render our account."},{"c1":4,"v1":15,"c2":4,"v2":15,"label":"Hebrews 4:15","text":"\"Touched with the feeling of\" translates sunpatheo — patheo (\"suffer,\" from the same root as pascho) with sun (\"with\") prefixed — literally, \"to suffer along with.\" This isn't abstract awareness of human weakness; it's experiential, a fellow-feeling built from actually having gone through the same pressure.\n\n\"In all points\" pushes the comparison as far as it will go — full identification with human temptation, with the one stated exception that in his case it never issued in sin.","translation":"For we do not have a high priest unable to feel with us in our weaknesses, but one who has been tempted in every way just as we are — yet without sin."},{"c1":4,"v1":16,"c2":4,"v2":16,"label":"Hebrews 4:16","text":"\"Let us come\" is proserchomai, the standard verb for a worshiper's approach to the altar under the old system — the writer reuses temple-approach language for approaching Christ directly. Two nouns follow, mercy and grace, and they aren't redundant: mercy answers for sins already committed, while grace supplies what's needed going forward — pardon for the past, provision for what's ahead.\n\n\"In time of need\" carries the sense of well-timed help, aid that arrives exactly when it's needed rather than early or late.","translation":"Let us therefore approach the throne of grace with boldness, so that we may receive mercy and find grace for well-timed help."},{"c1":5,"v1":3,"c2":5,"v2":3,"label":"Hebrews 5:3","text":"\"Ought\" here is opheilo, marking a moral obligation rooted in the priest's own condition — distinct from the sheer logical necessity expressed by a different verb elsewhere in this letter, and from the language of simple \"fittingness\" used of Christ's sufferings.\n\nBecause the priest himself is bound up in the same moral weakness as those he serves, he's morally obligated to offer sacrifice for himself first — an obligation that exposes exactly what's missing in a priest who has no sin of his own to atone for.","translation":"And because of this, he is under obligation — just as he offers for the people, so also for his own sins — to make offering."},{"c1":5,"v1":4,"c2":5,"v2":4,"label":"Hebrews 5:4","text":"The point is procedural and deliberately unglamorous: priestly office is never self-appointed. Aaron didn't nominate himself; the honor came from outside, by call.\n\nAnyone claiming the office on his own authority disqualifies himself by the very act of claiming it.","translation":"And no one takes this honor for himself, but only when he is called by God, just as Aaron also was."},{"c1":5,"v1":5,"c2":5,"v2":5,"label":"Hebrews 5:5","text":"The writer quotes the same words used earlier to establish Christ's sonship — \"you are my Son, today I have begotten you\" — reusing them here to ground the priestly call in the sonship itself, not in personal ambition or self-promotion.\n\nThe sentence is actually left grammatically unfinished in the Greek, trailing off after the quotation, trusting the reader to supply the obvious conclusion: the one who spoke those words is the one who conferred the honor.","translation":"So also the Messiah did not glorify himself to become a high priest, but it was the one who said to him: You are my Son, today I have begotten you —"},{"c1":5,"v1":8,"c2":5,"v2":8,"label":"Hebrews 5:8","text":"The Greek has no article before \"Son\" here, and the missing article does the same work it did earlier in the letter — it isn't naming an identity so much as a quality: \"though he was Son by nature,\" full deity, not merely one son among others. The point isn't that deity needed disciplining out of some tendency to disobey; obedience, as an experience, was simply unavailable to him before the incarnation — there was no one greater to obey.\n\nOnly by taking on a human situation did he come to know obedience firsthand, learned the way anyone learns anything: by living it, specifically by living it under suffering.","translation":"Though he was Son by nature, he learned obedience from the things he suffered."},{"c1":5,"v1":9,"c2":5,"v2":9,"label":"Hebrews 5:9","text":"\"Made perfect\" is teleios, which doesn't describe moral improvement but arrival at an intended goal — reaching the completion for which something was designed. Christ's sufferings brought him to the very consummation his priestly office required.\n\n\"Author\" translates aitios, \"that in which a cause resides\" — the point of origin from which something proceeds; his finished obedience is the actual source salvation flows from, not merely its occasion. And \"those who obey him\" describes who the saved turn out to be, not the basis on which they are saved.","translation":"And having been brought to completeness, he became, to all those who obey him, the source of eternal salvation."},{"c1":5,"v1":10,"c2":5,"v2":10,"label":"Hebrews 5:10","text":"\"Called\" translates prosagoreuo, a verb used for the formal, public act of styling someone with a title — a solemn designation, not an offhand label.\n\nGod publicly proclaimed him high priest in the specific order of Melchizedek, and the timing matters: the title comes only after the discipline of suffering described in the verse before had run its full course.","translation":"Publicly designated by God as a high priest after the order of Melchizedek."},{"c1":6,"v1":7,"c2":6,"v2":8,"label":"Hebrews 6:7–8","text":"The picture is agricultural: the same rain, falling repeatedly and generously, produces radically different results depending on the soil. One plot yields useful crops and receives blessing for it; the other, watered identically, yields only thorns and briers and ends up fit only for burning.\n\nThe point isn't a difference in what's given — the rain is identical — but a difference in the ground itself, in what each soil does with the same gift.","translation":"For land that drinks in the rain that often falls on it, and produces vegetation useful to those for whose sake it is cultivated, receives a blessing from God. But if it bears thorns and thistles, it is worthless, and near to a curse — and its end is to be burned."},{"c1":6,"v1":14,"c2":6,"v2":14,"label":"Hebrews 6:14","text":"The doubled phrasing — \"blessing I will bless,\" \"multiplying I will multiply\" — reflects a Hebrew construction carried over into the Greek, where repeating a verb alongside its own root intensifies it.\n\nIt isn't redundancy; it's emphasis, the strongest way Hebrew had of saying it would happen without fail.","translation":"Saying: Blessing, I will bless you; and multiplying, I will multiply you."},{"c1":6,"v1":15,"c2":6,"v2":15,"label":"Hebrews 6:15","text":"\"Obtained\" is epitugchano, \"to hit upon, attain\" — and what Abraham actually attained in his lifetime was only the seed of the promise, not its full harvest.\n\nIsaac's birth was the down payment, not the completed transaction; the promise's ultimate fulfillment reaches forward past Abraham's own lifetime.","translation":"And so, having patiently waited, he obtained the promise."},{"c1":6,"v1":17,"c2":6,"v2":17,"label":"Hebrews 6:17","text":"\"Willing\" is boulomai, marking a desire rooted in reasoned decision rather than a different word for desire rooted in feeling — God's oath is a considered resolve, not an emotional gesture. \"Immutability\" is ametathetos, built on metatithemi (\"to transpose, change position\") with a negating prefix — a related noun from the same root means \"turncoat,\" so the point is pointed: God will not be a turncoat regarding his own promise.\n\n\"Confirmed\" translates mesiteuo, \"to act as mediator, to pledge oneself as surety\" — from the same root as \"mediator.\" God is pictured stepping in as his own guarantor, interposing himself between the promise and its heirs as witness to his own word.","translation":"In this, God, wanting even more abundantly to show to the heirs of the promise the unchangeableness of his purpose, guaranteed it with an oath —"},{"c1":6,"v1":18,"c2":6,"v2":18,"label":"Hebrews 6:18","text":"\"Things\" translates pragma, from prasso (\"to do, accomplish\") — not vague objects but concrete, completed acts: the promise made and the oath sworn, both done deeds, both unchangeable. \"Strong\" is ischuros, describing indwelling strength like that of an army or a fortress — a bulwark, not a mild comfort.\n\nAnd \"fled for refuge\" is katapheugo, the exact word used in the Greek Old Testament for the manslayer running to a city of refuge to escape the avenger of blood — an image the readers would already have known, of a fugitive with nowhere else to go, given one designated place of safety.","translation":"So that, through two unchangeable acts in which it is impossible for God to lie, we who have fled for refuge might have strong encouragement to lay hold of the hope set before us."},{"c1":6,"v1":20,"c2":6,"v2":20,"label":"Hebrews 6:20","text":"\"Forerunner\" introduces a concept entirely foreign to the old system — the Aaronic high priest went into the Holy of Holies as a substitute, standing in for people who themselves could never follow him there. Christ instead enters as one who blazes the trail, opening a way others will actually walk after him. The Greek carries no definite article before \"forerunner,\" so translations that make the phrase sound like an already-familiar idea overstate the case slightly — this really is a new idea, introduced here for the first time.\n\nAnd \"Jesus\" is, once again, simply the Greek form of the name for Israel's God — a quiet, constant reminder running through the letter that Israel's own Jehovah is the one who has gone ahead.","translation":"Where Jesus, as a forerunner, entered on our behalf, having become a high priest forever, after the order of Melchizedek."},{"c1":7,"v1":2,"c2":7,"v2":2,"label":"Hebrews 7:2","text":"\"Gave\" is merizo, \"to divide into portions, distribute\" — Abraham's tithe was a deliberate, calculated apportioning, not an impulsive gift. The name Melchizedek itself breaks down to \"king of righteousness,\" while \"king of Salem\" adds \"king of peace.\"\n\nTwo titles stacked on top of each other, one drawn from his name, the other from the place he ruled, together sketching a portrait of righteousness and peace united in a single figure.","translation":"To whom also Abraham apportioned a tenth of everything — his name meaning, first, king of righteousness, and then also king of Salem, that is, king of peace."},{"c1":7,"v1":3,"c2":7,"v2":3,"label":"Hebrews 7:3","text":"\"Without father, without mother, without genealogy\" describes an absence of record, not a miraculous birth — significant because Israel's priesthood ran entirely on documented lineage; a priest without a paper trail would ordinarily be disqualified. \"Made like\" translates aphomoioo, \"to produce a facsimile\" — the likeness lies in how Melchizedek's story is written, in what Scripture leaves out about him, not in some literal claim that he was himself eternal.\n\nIt's the silence of the record, not the man himself, that supplies the type. And the likeness specifically points to the Son as Son of God, not Son of Man — the eternal side of Christ's identity, since as Son of Man he plainly was born and did die.","translation":"Without father, without mother, without genealogy, having neither beginning of days nor end of life, but made like the Son of God — he remains a priest continually."},{"c1":7,"v1":5,"c2":7,"v2":5,"label":"Hebrews 7:5","text":"The Levitical priests collected tithes strictly \"according to the law\" — under legal commandment, even from their own kinsmen descended from the same Abraham.\n\nThat's an important contrast the writer is setting up: compliance with a legal requirement carries no admission of personal inferiority on the part of those who pay it, which sharpens, by contrast, just how remarkable Abraham's voluntary tithe to Melchizedek was.","translation":"And those descendants of Levi who receive the priestly office have a commandment, according to the law, to collect tithes from the people — that is, from their brothers, though these have come from the loins of Abraham."},{"c1":7,"v1":7,"c2":7,"v2":7,"label":"Hebrews 7:7","text":"\"Dispute\" translates antilogia, literally \"word spoken against\" — a word-for-word contradiction. The adjectives \"lesser\" and \"greater\" appear in the neuter gender in Greek, casting the statement in its widest, most general form.\n\nNot this particular blessing only, but the principle itself, stated as an axiom nobody would think to argue against: the inferior is blessed by the superior, never the reverse.","translation":"But beyond all dispute, the lesser is blessed by the greater."},{"c1":7,"v1":13,"c2":7,"v2":13,"label":"Hebrews 7:13","text":"The writer's word choice for \"another\" tribe is heteros, \"another of a different kind,\" rather than allos, \"another of the same kind\" — Christ doesn't simply belong to a different tribe than Levi's, he belongs to a tribe of an entirely different category, one with no connection whatsoever to altar service.\n\n\"Altar\" itself is thusiasterion, specifically the altar where sacrificial victims were offered — not a generic word for altar, but the one tied directly to blood sacrifice, the very thing no member of Judah had any legal standing to perform.","translation":"For he of whom these things are said belonged to a different kind of tribe, from which no one has ever attended at the altar."},{"c1":7,"v1":14,"c2":7,"v2":14,"label":"Hebrews 7:14","text":"\"Evident\" is prodelos, \"openly plain, obvious to everyone\" — no dispute possible about the facts. \"Sprang\" translates anatello, the verb used for the sun or a star rising, or a cloud forming, or a plant coming up out of the ground — an image of emergence, of something breaking into visibility.\n\nAnd \"Lord\" here is kurios, the standard Greek rendering of the divine name Jehovah throughout the Greek Old Testament — a title with weight, quietly asserting deity even while the sentence's actual subject is Christ's very human tribal origin.","translation":"For it is plainly evident that our Lord has sprung from Judah — a tribe about which Moses said nothing regarding priests."},{"c1":7,"v1":15,"c2":7,"v2":15,"label":"Hebrews 7:15","text":"\"Far more evident\" uses a different, stronger compound — katadelos, \"thoroughly, unmistakably evident\" — distinct from the plainer word used one verse earlier. That earlier word marked a simple historical fact, Christ's tribal origin.\n\nThis one marks something even more inescapable: the logical necessity, staring the reader in the face, that an entirely different kind of priesthood had to arise.","translation":"And it is even more thoroughly evident that, after the likeness of Melchizedek, a different kind of priest arises."},{"c1":7,"v1":16,"c2":7,"v2":16,"label":"Hebrews 7:16","text":"\"Law\" is nomos, a general norm or standard, narrowed here by entole, \"commandment\" — a specific precept, and that precept is called sarkinos, \"fleshly\": rules keyed to physical, bodily qualifications — the right family line, physical wholeness, ceremonial cleanness.\n\nAgainst that stands akatalutos, \"indissoluble\" — built from luo (\"loosen, dissolve\") with a negating prefix — describing a life that cannot be broken apart into disunited pieces. Christ doesn't hold his priesthood by satisfying a checklist of physical requirements; he holds it by the sheer, unbreakable power of a life that even death couldn't dissolve.","translation":"Who has become a priest, not according to the norm of a fleshly commandment, but according to the power of an indissoluble life."},{"c1":7,"v1":17,"c2":7,"v2":17,"label":"Hebrews 7:17","text":"This verse is simply the scriptural anchor for the claim just made — Psalm 110 again, cited to certify in Scripture's own words that the new priest's life, and therefore his priesthood, is permanent by divine testimony, not merely by the writer's own argument.","translation":"For it is testified: You are a priest forever, after the order of Melchizedek."},{"c1":7,"v1":22,"c2":7,"v2":22,"label":"Hebrews 7:22","text":"The writer's argument up to this point converges here: because God backed this priesthood with an oath — something never done for the Levitical line — Jesus becomes the guarantee, the pledge underwriting a better covenant.\n\nThe strength of the guarantee rests entirely on the strength of what backs it: an oath God cannot go back on.","translation":"By as much, then, Jesus has become the pledge of a better covenant."},{"c1":7,"v1":27,"c2":7,"v2":27,"label":"Hebrews 7:27","text":"The position of \"daily\" in the Greek sentence is doing careful work — it attaches to Christ's ongoing intercession, not to any need to repeat his sacrifice; his intercession runs daily, but the sacrifice behind it never needs renewing, unlike the Aaronic high priest's yearly repetition on the Day of Atonement. \"Once for all\" translates ephapax, a compound stressing finality — done, and done permanently.\n\nAnd unlike every Aaronic priest, Christ never had to offer first for sins of his own before he could offer for anyone else's — there was nothing of his own to atone for.","translation":"Who has no daily need, as those high priests did, to offer sacrifices first for his own sins and then for those of the people — for this he did once for all, when he offered up himself."},{"c1":7,"v1":28,"c2":7,"v2":28,"label":"Hebrews 7:28","text":"\"Weakness\" here is astheneia, describing weakness as a defining, general characteristic rather than isolated lapses — the law's appointees are, by their very constitution, weak men.\n\nAgainst that, the oath — which came later than the law and so supersedes it — appoints the Son, described with teleioo, \"brought to completion, perfected\": not a man patched up despite weakness, but one made complete, permanently, without that built-in defect.","translation":"For the law appoints as high priests men who are, by nature, weak; but the word of the oath, which came after the law, appoints a Son, made complete forever."},{"c1":8,"v1":2,"c2":8,"v2":2,"label":"Hebrews 8:2","text":"\"Minister\" translates *leitourgos* (leitourgos), built from an old word for \"belonging to the people\" plus *ergon* (ergon), \"work\" — it named someone in public office serving the state, and both the Greek Old Testament and the New Testament use it for service rendered to God as well as to people. Christ's priesthood is public, official work, not a private religious feeling. \"Sanctuary\" renders *ta hagia* (ta hagia), plural, \"the holy places\" — Christ ministers in the heavenly holies themselves. \"Tabernacle\" is simply the ordinary word for a tent. \"True\" is *alēthinos* (alethinos), and here it doesn't mean \"genuine\" as opposed to \"fake\" — it means \"the real one\" as opposed to a mere copy or representation. The earthly tent Moses built was never the counterfeit; it was the model. The tent the Lord himself pitched is the reality behind it, heaven's own Holy of Holies, and Christ's priesthood outranks Aaron's precisely because he serves there instead.","translation":"A minister of the holy places, and of the tabernacle — the genuine one, which the Lord pitched, not man."},{"c1":8,"v1":3,"c2":8,"v2":3,"label":"Hebrews 8:3","text":"Every high priest exists to offer something — that is the job description. Priesthood without an offering is a contradiction in terms. So if Christ holds the office of high priest, the logic is airtight: he too must have something to present. The verse doesn't yet say what; it simply establishes the necessity, setting up the case the writer builds through the rest of the chapter that Christ's offering is himself.","translation":"For every high priest is appointed to offer both gifts and sacrifices; therefore it is necessary that this one also have something to offer."},{"c1":8,"v1":4,"c2":8,"v2":4,"label":"Hebrews 8:4","text":"The argument runs by elimination. The Levitical priesthood was fixed by the Law of Moses to men of Aaron's line functioning at an earthly altar — that post was already staffed, and staffed exclusively, by others, under legal right. If Christ's priestly work were an earthly function, he would have no priesthood left to hold, since the earthly slot belonged to a different order by law, not by merit. The very fact that he is a priest at all requires a sanctuary the Law never touched — a heavenly one.","translation":"For if he were on earth, he would not be a priest at all, since there are those who offer the gifts according to law."},{"c1":8,"v1":5,"c2":8,"v2":5,"label":"Hebrews 8:5","text":"\"Example\" is *hupodeigma* (hupodeigma), a rough sketch or outline suggestive of the real thing, and \"shadow\" is *skia* (skia) — literally a shadow cast by a solid object, with no substance of its own. A shadow proves something real is standing behind it, but it isn't that thing; it has no independent existence. That's the relationship the writer draws between the earthly tabernacle and the heavenly one: the Aaronic priests spent their whole careers serving a shadow, doing real work inside an unreal building.\n\nThe \"better covenant\" of verse 6 is said to be \"established\" — *nomotheteō* (nomotheteo), literally \"to enact as law,\" sharing a root with *nomos* (nomos), \"law.\" It isn't a handshake agreement; it's legislated, put on the books with full authority. Verse 7 then explains why a second covenant was needed at all: \"sought\" is *zēteō* (zeteo) in the imperfect tense, describing action that kept happening across a stretch of past time — not \"a place was sought\" once, but \"a place kept on being sought.\" Dissatisfaction with the first covenant wasn't a later verdict handed down after the fact; it was live and ongoing while that covenant was still in force.\n\nWhen God announces through Jeremiah that he will \"make\" a new covenant, the verb is *sunteleō* (sunteleo), \"to bring to completion, to consummate\" — a stronger word than the ordinary *poieō* (poieo), \"to make,\" chosen to stress that this covenant finishes what the first one left undone. \"Covenant\" itself, *diathēkē* (diatheke), comes from *diatithēmi* (diatithemi) — *tithēmi* (tithemi), \"to place,\" with a prefix carrying the sense \"between\" — literally something placed between two parties. The same noun does double duty later in the letter, naming both an arrangement between two and a will that only takes effect at the death of the one who made it.\n\nIsrael's failure is described with *emmenō* (emmeno), \"to remain true to, abide by,\" which they didn't do, met by God's *ameleō* (ameleo) — *melō* (melo), \"to care for,\" with a negating prefix, \"to stop caring for.\" It marks a real shift, concern giving way to disregard. The promise that under the new covenant nobody will need a teacher uses a double negative in Greek, the strongest possible denial available — and it switches verbs for \"know\" partway through: *ginōskō* (ginosko), knowledge gained by being introduced to something you didn't know before, then *oida* (oida), simply and fully knowing. Everyone under the new covenant moves from having God explained to them, to just knowing him.\n\nFinally, \"old\" is *palaios* (palaios), not *archaios* (archaios) — the difference between \"worn out, obsolete\" and merely \"existing since long ago.\" The first covenant isn't retired for its age; it's retired for being used up. \"Made old\" is a perfect-tense verb, *palaioō* (palaioo), an action completed in the past whose effect still stands: the covenant was rendered obsolete the moment a new one was promised, and it has stayed obsolete ever since. \"Waxeth old,\" *gēraskō* (gerasko), pictures the specific weakening that comes with age — decay headed toward an end, not neutral aging.","translation":"They serve a sketch and shadow of the heavenly things — just as Moses, when about to construct the tabernacle, was warned: \"See that you make everything according to the pattern that was shown you on the mountain.\" But now he has obtained a more excellent ministry, by as much as he is also mediator of a better covenant, one that has been enacted on the basis of better promises."},{"c1":9,"v1":3,"c2":9,"v2":3,"label":"Hebrews 9:3","text":"Exodus describes two veils in the tabernacle — one at the entrance to the Holy Place, a second separating the Holy Place from the Holy of Holies. The writer's phrase \"the second veil\" assumes his readers know that layout: pass through it, and you're standing where only the high priest ever stood, and only once a year.","translation":"And behind the second veil, a tabernacle called the Holy of Holies."},{"c1":9,"v1":4,"c2":9,"v2":4,"label":"Hebrews 9:4","text":"Naming the golden censer among the furnishings of the Holy of Holies raises a real question, since the incense altar itself stood in the Holy Place, just outside the veil. The verb \"had,\" *echō* (echo), doesn't have to mean \"permanently contained\" — it can mean simply \"belonged to, pertained to.\" On the Day of Atonement a special golden censer, different from the one used the rest of the year, was carried past the veil by the high priest, filled with coals, so the incense cloud covered the mercy seat before he brought in the blood. That one-day use is likely what's in view. \"Ark\" is *kibōtos* (kibotos), simply \"box\" or \"chest\" — the same plain word used for any storage container, dignified here only by what it held: the tables of the law, and, on the tradition the writer follows, the jar of manna and Aaron's rod as well.","translation":"having a golden censer, and the ark of the covenant covered on every side with gold, in which was a golden jar holding the manna, and Aaron's rod that budded, and the tablets of the covenant."},{"c1":9,"v1":5,"c2":9,"v2":5,"label":"Hebrews 9:5","text":"\"Mercy seat\" is *hilastērion* (hilasterion), the Greek Old Testament's word for the gold cover over the ark where atonement was made — the same word Paul uses of Christ in Romans 3:25, usually rendered \"propitiation.\" It's a word aimed at sin, not at anger. It doesn't describe an offering that talks an angry God down; it describes the removing of the barrier sin throws up between God and man, a covering-over that clears the way for fellowship. The blood sprinkled there stood between the broken law resting in the ark and the priest who approached on the people's behalf — exactly what Christ's blood does, permanently.\n\nThe next verse adds that the tabernacle's furnishings had been \"prepared,\" *kataskeuazō* (kataskeuazo), in the perfect tense — set in place once, with the arrangement still standing — after which the priests entered the outer tent \"continually,\" day after day, to carry out their service, *epiteleō* (epiteleo), a verb used elsewhere of completing a religious rite. The picture is one of routine access to the outer room set directly against the sealed, once-a-year access to the inner one.","translation":"And above it, cherubim of glory, overshadowing the mercy seat — about which we cannot now speak in detail. These things having been set in place, the priests enter the first tent continually, performing the services."},{"c1":9,"v1":7,"c2":9,"v2":7,"label":"Hebrews 9:7","text":"The high priest's entry into the Holy of Holies was tightly restricted: once a year, alone, and never without blood. \"Errors\" translates *agnoēma* (agnoema), sin committed in ignorance or without full deliberation — even unintentional sin needed covering. \"For\" is *huper* (huper), the preposition of substitution, the same word used of one person dying in another's place. The blood wasn't a gesture; it stood in for the sinner, exactly as Christ's blood would.","translation":"But into the second, only the high priest enters, once a year, and not without blood, which he offers on behalf of himself and for the sins of ignorance of the people."},{"c1":9,"v1":8,"c2":9,"v2":8,"label":"Hebrews 9:8","text":"The point here turns on timing, not vocabulary: as long as the tabernacle's two-room structure stood — an outer room anyone could enter, an inner room sealed off from everyone but the high priest, and him only once a year — the arrangement itself announced that the way into God's presence was not yet open. The building was the message. Its architecture, not a sermon, told Israel that direct access to God still lay in the future. When the veil tore at the cross, that architecture became obsolete on the spot, God's own comment on what had just been accomplished.","translation":"The Holy Spirit making this clear: the way into the Holy Place had not yet been laid open, while the first tent was still standing."},{"c1":9,"v1":9,"c2":9,"v2":9,"label":"Hebrews 9:9","text":"\"Figure\" is *parabolē* (parabole) — literally something \"thrown alongside\" something else to explain it, the same root that gives us \"parable.\" The tabernacle functioned exactly that way: a visual aid set alongside the reality, teaching by illustration rather than being the thing itself. \"Perfect,\" *teleios* (teleios), doesn't mean sinless — it means complete, lacking nothing further needed to finish the job. No ritual, gift, or sacrifice under the old system could bring a worshiper's conscience to that state of completeness; at most it addressed the ceremony, never the conscience underneath it.","translation":"This was a symbol for the present time, according to which gifts and sacrifices are being offered that are not able to make the worshiper complete as to his conscience."},{"c1":9,"v1":10,"c2":9,"v2":10,"label":"Hebrews 9:10","text":"The old system's regulations rested, literally, \"upon\" (*epi*, epi) food and drink and various washings — its whole operating basis was bodily, not spiritual. \"Meats\" is *brōma* (broma), simply \"food\" in general, wider than the narrowed modern sense of animal flesh. These rules are called \"carnal,\" *sarx* (sarx), \"flesh\" — meaning human, tied to the physical conditions of human life. They stood only \"until the time of reformation\" — *diorthōsis* (diorthosis), a word used elsewhere of straightening a crooked limb or setting a broken bone back into normal alignment. The old system wasn't evil; it was a splint, holding a fracture in place until the day it could be properly set right.\n\nWhen Christ \"came\" as high priest, the verb is *paraginomai* (paraginomai), \"to arrive on the scene, to come alongside\" — the language of an advent, someone entering from outside rather than simply growing up within the system he was joining. He ministers \"through,\" *dia* (dia), a greater tabernacle — the preposition of the means by which something is accomplished, making the sanctuary itself the channel of his priestly work. That tabernacle is explicitly not \"of this creation,\" *ktisis* (ktisis) — it doesn't belong to the made, material universe at all.\n\n\"His own blood\" uses *idios* (idios), not the ordinary possessive *autos* (autos) — *idios* marks personal, private, unique ownership, the same intensity John uses when he reports Jesus calling God his own Father in a sense no one else could claim. Christ entered \"once for all,\" *ephapax* (ephapax), a single decisive act set against the priest's yearly repetition. And what he obtained he \"found,\" *heuriskō* (heurisko) — not merely received, *lambanō* (lambano), but sought out and secured, the language of active procurement, matching \"redemption,\" *lutrōsis* (lutrosis), from *lutron* (lutron), the ransom price paid to free a slave.\n\nCeremonial defilement is *koinoō* (koinoo), \"to make common, unhallowed\"; the cure is *hagiazō* (hagiazo), \"to set apart for God\" — restoration to a status, which is exactly the limit animal blood had. Christ, by contrast, offered himself \"through eternal Spirit\" — the Greek has no definite article before \"Spirit,\" pointing not to an outside force acting on him but to his own eternal divine nature as the moving power behind the offering. That's what animals never brought to the altar: a will of their own, freely given.\n\n\"Mediator,\" *mesitēs* (mesites), names someone who stands between two parties to make or ratify an agreement — Christ standing between a holy God and sinful people, removing the obstacle between them. The writer then leans on the double sense of *diathēkē* (diatheke): the same word means both \"covenant\" and \"will, testament,\" and a testament only takes legal effect once its maker has died. That's exactly why Christ's death was necessary to activate a covenant God had already promised — a will is only words on paper until the testator is gone.\n\nEverything connected to the old covenant's inauguration — the book, the people, the tabernacle and its vessels — was sprinkled with blood, because without blood shed there is no forgiveness. Even heaven's own sanctuary needed cleansing before Christ could enter it, though with a better sacrifice than animal blood could supply. The earthly tabernacle is called *antitypon* (antitupon), a \"corresponding type\" of the heavenly one, while the heavenly is called *alēthinos* (alethinos) — genuinely, fully what the earthly one only pointed toward.","translation":"These were only regulations for the body — foods and drinks and various washings — imposed until the time for setting things right. Not through the blood of goats and calves, but through his own blood, he entered once for all into the Holy Place, having sought out and secured eternal redemption."},{"c1":9,"v1":26,"c2":9,"v2":26,"label":"Hebrews 9:26","text":"The phrase rendered \"in the end of the world\" is literally \"at the consummation of the ages\" — not the world's final moment but history's ages reaching their intended completion, the point everything before had been building toward. \"Appeared\" is *phaneroō* (phaneroo) in the perfect passive: not simply \"he appeared and the moment passed,\" but \"he has been manifested\" — an action finished that leaves a permanent, standing result. One sacrifice, offered once, settles the matter for every age it touches; that's the whole point of contrasting it with sacrifices that had to be repeated endlessly because none of them actually finished anything.\n\nThat logic carries straight into the next chapter, where the Law is said to hold only a \"shadow,\" *skia* (skia), of the good things to come, not the \"image,\" *eikōn* (eikon), of the realities themselves. A shadow is a vague outline cast by something solid — useful for telling you a shape is there, useless for showing you what it actually looks like. An *eikōn* is a true likeness, faithful in its details. That's why the sacrifices \"make perfect,\" *teleioō* (teleioo), no one — not because they were badly performed, but because a shadow, however many times you retrace it, never becomes the object that cast it. The repetition itself was the tell: something that actually finished the job wouldn't need to be re-offered every year.","translation":"But now, once, at the consummation of the ages, he has been manifested for the putting away of sin through the sacrifice of himself. For the law, holding only a shadow of the good things to come, and not the very image of the realities, is never able, by the same sacrifices continually offered year after year, to make complete those who approach."},{"c1":10,"v1":2,"c2":10,"v2":2,"label":"Hebrews 10:2","text":"The logic of the verse is a kind of test: if the yearly sacrifices had actually finished the job of cleansing, the ritual would have stopped, because a worshipper who is truly clean carries no more consciousness of sin needing to be dealt with. The fact that the offering had to be repeated every single year is itself the proof that nothing final had happened there — the problem persisted, so the remedy kept being reapplied. That doesn't mean Old Testament believers were left stranded in the meantime; a worshipper who brought his sacrifice in genuine faith, trusting God to one day provide the real payment for sin, was reckoned righteous on that basis, in full, from the moment he believed. The repetition exposed the limits of the animal blood itself, not the sincerity of the faith it was meant to picture.","translation":"Would they not then have stopped being offered, since the worshippers, having once been cleansed, would no longer be carrying even one troubled conscience about sins?"},{"c1":10,"v1":3,"c2":10,"v2":3,"label":"Hebrews 10:3","text":"\"Remembrance\" renders *anamnesis* (anamnesis), a noun meaning \"a calling back to mind\" rather than a removal. Each year's Day of Atonement didn't erase guilt from the worshippers' memory — paradoxically, it revived it. The very ceremony meant to deal with sin ended up rehearsing it annually, which told the worshippers, whether they admitted it to themselves or not, that the sacrifice hadn't actually settled the account.","translation":"But in them there kept occurring, year after year, a calling back to mind of sins."},{"c1":10,"v1":4,"c2":10,"v2":4,"label":"Hebrews 10:4","text":"The claim needs no elaborate defense because the mismatch is self-evident: an animal's blood is a physical substance, and sin is a moral offense against God. There is no natural bridge between those two categories, and no quantity of repeated animal sacrifice manufactures one. That is precisely why the whole system could only ever point forward to something else — it was never built to close the gap on its own.","translation":"For it is impossible for the blood of bulls and goats to take away sins."},{"c1":10,"v1":6,"c2":10,"v2":6,"label":"Hebrews 10:6","text":"This isn't a blanket statement that God despised the Levitical offerings. The Israelites who brought them were obeying a command He Himself had given, and their obedience genuinely pleased Him. What He took no pleasure in was the offerings' capacity to function as an actual payment for sin — as a transaction meant to settle guilt, they accomplished nothing, however faithfully they were carried out.","translation":"In whole burnt offerings and offerings for sin you took no pleasure."},{"c1":10,"v1":7,"c2":10,"v2":7,"label":"Hebrews 10:7","text":"\"It stands written of me\" reaches back to Scripture already on record — the Messiah's assignment wasn't drawn up after the sacrificial system proved inadequate; it was there in the text from the start, waiting for Him to step into it. The phrase functions almost like a legal citation: the terms were filed long before the moment of fulfillment arrived. When Messiah does step in, the passage continues, He does more than simply add a better sacrifice alongside the old ones — He sets aside the first order of things specifically to make room for the second, so that what could never satisfy God's will gives way to what actually does.","translation":"Then I said, Behold, I have come — in the roll of the book it stands written of me — to do your will, O God."},{"c1":10,"v1":10,"c2":10,"v2":10,"label":"Hebrews 10:10","text":"\"Sanctified\" translates *hagiazo* (hagiazo), \"to set apart for God\" — here describing not a gradual ethical improvement but the once-for-all placement of a believing sinner into the status of one who belongs to God, with everything that status carries. The Greek underlines the permanence of it: \"we are sanctified\" pairs a perfect participle with a present-tense verb, a construction built specifically to say that the state was entered decisively and continues without interruption. \"Once for all\" belongs with \"the offering of the body of Jesus Christ\" — a single, unrepeatable act accomplishing what years of repeated offerings never could.\n\nThe verses that follow press the same contrast physically. Every Levitical priest is pictured standing, day after day, offering the same sacrifices that never actually remove sin — the posture is the point: nobody sits down in the middle of unfinished work. Messiah, by contrast, sat down after His one offering, and stayed seated. A finished sacrifice gets a seated priest; an unfinished one keeps its priest on his feet indefinitely.","translation":"By which will we have been made holy, and stand holy still, through the offering of the body of Jesus Christ, once for all."},{"c1":10,"v1":16,"c2":10,"v2":18,"label":"Hebrews 10:16–18","text":"The old covenant carved its law onto stone tablets handed down from outside. The new covenant, quoted here from Jeremiah, has God writing the same law directly onto mind and heart — an internalized instinct rather than an external code to consult. The second marker of the new covenant is even more striking: God says He will no longer remember the sins and lawless acts at all. Once that is true, nothing is left for a sacrifice to do — the writer's argument closes right there: no more remembrance of sin means no more need of an offering for it.\n\nThe road into God's presence that follows is called, in the Greek, *prosphatos* (prosphatos) — built from a root meaning \"near\" combined with the word behind \"to kill.\" Its plain sense is \"freshly slain,\" not merely \"new\" the way we'd use the word today. It's a road opened by a killing and sprinkled in blood, set against the older road to the tabernacle's mercy seat, itself opened only by animal blood but now superseded. The curtain that once blocked entry is identified with Messiah's own body — torn, as the veil was torn, so that what had barred access now stands open.\n\nThe priest who leads the way in is described in the Greek not as one compound title but as two separate words: \"a priest, a great one\" — the greatness gets its own emphasis, distinct from the office itself. Believers are told to approach with a heart called *alethinos* (alethinos), \"genuine\" — the kind of heart that actually matches what it claims to be — sprinkled clean and washed, borrowing the picture of a priest ceremonially prepared before entering to serve.\n\nHolding on to that confession gets its own vocabulary too. \"Hold fast\" is *katecho* (katecho), \"to hold down\" — a grip firm enough to master what it holds, not a loose grasp. \"Wavering\" is *aklines* (aklines), built from the verb \"to lean\" with a negating prefix attached — literally \"not leaning,\" the picture of something built to stay upright rather than tip backward under pressure. And believers are told to give each other *katanoeo* (katanoeo), sustained, attentive observation, aimed at stirring up love and good works — the same root that gives English \"paroxysm\" describes that stirring: a deliberate provocation, meant here in the best possible sense.","translation":"This is the covenant that I will decree for them after those days, says the Lord: I will keep putting my laws upon their hearts, and upon their minds I will keep inscribing them; and their sins and their lawless deeds I will remember no more, not ever. Now where release from these has been granted, there is no longer any offering for sin."},{"c1":10,"v1":25,"c2":10,"v2":25,"label":"Hebrews 10:25","text":"\"Forsaking\" is *egkataleipo* (egkataleipo), \"to let down, to abandon\" — stronger than simply missing a meeting; it pictures dropping something you had been holding up. \"Assembling together\" is *episunagoge* (episunagoge), built on the same root that gives us \"synagogue\" (a going-together), with an added preposition that sharpens it toward the actual, concrete gathering rather than the abstract idea of a congregation. Some among the readers, under pressure, had quietly begun dropping out; the exhortation runs the other direction — show up for each other, and do it more, not less, as the day visibly draws nearer.\n\nThe sin described next is qualified by *hekousios* (hekousios), \"voluntarily, of one's own accord\" — set in deliberate contrast to sins committed out of ignorance or weakness. The Greek present tense behind \"if we go on sinning\" points to a settled, habitual course rather than a single lapse. And the knowledge in view isn't the bare, surface-level *gnosis* (gnosis); it's *epignosis* (epignosis), a fuller, more personal grasp of the truth — someone who had genuinely understood, not merely heard, and turned away with eyes wide open.\n\nWhat is left for such a person is only \"a fearful expectation of judgment\" — closer to \"a kind of dreadful looking-for\" — because the one available sacrifice has already been consciously rejected. The comparison then drops back to the Mosaic law itself: a person who set that law aside died without mercy, on the testimony of two or three witnesses. If that was the penalty under the lesser system, the argument presses, what penalty fits the far greater offense described next?\n\nThree actions define that greater offense, one against each person of the Godhead: trampling the Son of God underfoot, treating His blood as ordinary, and insulting the Spirit of grace. \"Ordinary\" renders *koinos* (koinos), whose basic sense is \"shared by all, common property\" — the direct opposite of what is set apart for sacred use. To call the covenant blood *koinos* is to strip it of any special worth and treat it as no different from anyone else's. That is the substance of the apostasy this passage is warning against — not ignorance, but a deliberate downgrade of what deserves the highest reverence.","translation":"Not abandoning the gathering of ourselves together, as the custom of some is, but continually encouraging one another — and all the more as you see the day drawing near."},{"c1":10,"v1":30,"c2":10,"v2":30,"label":"Hebrews 10:30","text":"The certainty behind the warning rests on God's own word, not on a threat of petty payback. \"Vengeance\" can mislead a modern reader — the underlying idea is the full, impartial execution of justice, giving every party exactly what is due. The citation draws two lines from the Song of Moses: one declaring that the administering of justice belongs to God alone, the other that the Lord will judge His own people. Together they rule out any assumption that covenant privilege buys an exemption — belonging to God's people places a person squarely under His judgment, never beyond it.","translation":"For we know the one who said, Justice belongs to me — I will repay. And again, The Lord will judge his people."},{"c1":10,"v1":31,"c2":10,"v2":31,"label":"Hebrews 10:31","text":"The statement stands as a summary verdict on everything just argued: someone who has trampled the Son, cheapened His blood, and insulted the Spirit has stepped out from under grace and directly into the path of God's own justice. Nothing abstract is meant by it — the \"living God\" is not a passive judge but an active one, and nothing made by Him escapes His hands.","translation":"It is a fearful thing to fall into the hands of the living God."},{"c1":10,"v1":33,"c2":10,"v2":33,"label":"Hebrews 10:33","text":"\"Made a spectacle\" translates *theatrizo* (theatrizo), the root behind our word \"theater\" — to be put on public display for contempt, the very word later used to describe Christians literally thrown to wild animals in the Roman arena. Here the arena was social rather than literal: reproach and pressure aimed at those who had left the synagogue system for the new covenant. \"Afflictions\" in this verse is a different word than the one used just before it — *thlipsis* (thlipsis), which pictures pressure applied from outside, a squeezing. The persecution reached further than belief alone: it extended to anyone who stood in solidarity with the persecuted. The word for that solidarity, *koinonia* (koinonia), means active partnership — these believers shared materially in others' suffering, and were targeted for the sharing as much as for the faith itself.","translation":"On the one hand, you were being made a public spectacle through both insults and pressures; on the other, you were making yourselves partners with those who were being treated this way."},{"c1":10,"v1":34,"c2":10,"v2":34,"label":"Hebrews 10:34","text":"The \"partnership\" of the previous verse gets defined here in concrete terms: sympathy toward those in prison, and a striking response to having their own property seized. \"Accepted\" is *prosdechomai* (prosdechomai), \"to receive to oneself\" — a word implying willing acceptance rather than mere passive endurance. What made joy possible in the face of real loss was the knowledge of a possession that couldn't be confiscated: an asset outside the reach of any seizure, permanent where the earthly one was not.","translation":"For you sympathized with those in bonds, and you accepted the plundering of your possessions with joy, knowing that you yourselves possess something better, and one that continues to remain."},{"c1":10,"v1":35,"c2":10,"v2":35,"label":"Hebrews 10:35","text":"\"Confidence\" translates *parresia* (parresia), built from the words for \"all\" and \"speech\" — literally the freedom to say everything, unguarded and unreserved. It names the bold, unashamed posture these believers had already shown under real pressure. The exhortation isn't to acquire some new virtue but to stop discarding one they already had — and the reason given is blunt: that confidence carries a payoff worth protecting.","translation":"So do not throw away your confidence, which carries a great reward."},{"c1":10,"v1":36,"c2":10,"v2":36,"label":"Hebrews 10:36","text":"\"Receive\" is *komizo* (komizo), which means more than simply obtaining something — it pictures carrying an item away for one's own use and benefit, the way one collects what is owed. \"Patience\" translates *hupomone* (hupomone), built from \"to remain\" plus \"under\" — literally staying put beneath a weight rather than trying to escape it. The exhortation isn't to feel patient; it's to stay under the pressure without bolting, because the promised reward only reaches the one still standing there when it arrives.","translation":"For you have need of endurance — of remaining under — so that, having done the will of God, you may carry away the promise for yourselves."},{"c1":10,"v1":37,"c2":10,"v2":37,"label":"Hebrews 10:37","text":"The wording intensifies the wait rather than shortening it: not just \"a little while\" but something closer to \"a very little — however little — while,\" the smallness doubled for emphasis. The line draws on Habakkuk's prophecy of a coming vision that would not fail, language already applied by Jewish interpreters to the Messiah's arrival before this writer picked it up the same way.","translation":"For yet a very little — how very little — while, and the one who is coming will come, and will not delay."},{"c1":10,"v1":39,"c2":10,"v2":39,"label":"Hebrews 10:39","text":"The sentence opens with an emphatic personal pronoun, drawing a hard line between the writer's own company and the hypothetical person just described: \"But we — we are not of the shrinking-back kind.\" Both \"those who draw back\" and \"those who believe\" are phrased in the Greek as descriptions of a class of person, not as isolated, one-time acts — this is about which kind of person someone fundamentally is, not a single bad decision made under strain. \"Destruction\" translates *apoleia* (apoleia), the same word used elsewhere for utter, final ruin — not a setback but the loss of the soul itself. Set against it is the \"preserving of the soul,\" the outcome that belongs, by nature, to the believing kind.","translation":"But as for us, we are not of the shrinking-back kind, who shrink back to destruction, but of the believing kind, who believe unto the preserving of the soul."},{"c1":11,"v1":2,"c2":11,"v2":2,"label":"Hebrews 11:2","text":"\"Obtained a good report\" renders *emartyrēthēsan* (emartyrethesan), the aorist passive of *martyreō* (martyreo), \"to bear witness.\" The direction of the verb matters: this isn't the elders testifying to something, it's witness being borne to them — God himself put his stamp of approval on their lives. \"By it\" translates *en tautē* (en taute), a phrase that sits on the border between two Greek cases: it can mean \"in the sphere of\" faith and \"by means of\" faith at once, so faith is named as both the arena these people lived in and the tool that got results for them. And \"elders\" (not the more usual \"fathers\") is a deliberately broad label, reaching for every Old Testament saint the chapter is about to parade past.","translation":"For by means of this the elders had witness borne to them."},{"c1":11,"v1":3,"c2":11,"v2":3,"label":"Hebrews 11:3","text":"\"Understand\" translates *noeō* (noeo), the verb for perceiving with the reflective mind rather than the eye. In the New Testament it's never used of plain physical sight — it names the mental grasp that follows seeing and draws a conclusion from it. Faith, in this verse, isn't opposed to reason; it's reason correctly applied to what's in front of you, concluding a Maker from what's made.\n\n\"Worlds\" is *aiōn* (aion), not the ordinary word for the physical globe. *Aiōn* reaches past matter to include the ages themselves — the created order understood as space and time together, both of them God's product rather than a backdrop he happened to build inside of.\n\n\"Framed\" is *katartizō* (katartizo), the verb used for equipping or fitting something out for the purpose it's meant to serve — a craftsman assembling parts so the whole works as intended. The universe wasn't just produced; it was outfitted, tuned to its purpose.\n\nAnd the \"word\" by which it was framed is *hrēma* (hrema), the word for an uttered statement, not *logos* (logos), the title John gives the Son in his Gospel's opening verse. This verse is about God's spoken command bringing the ages into being, not about the person of the Word. The visible, in other words, came out of the invisible — which is exactly why grasping it takes faith and not observation.","translation":"By faith we perceive that the ages have been fitted together by the spoken word of God, so that what is seen has not come to exist out of things that are visible."},{"c1":11,"v1":5,"c2":11,"v2":5,"label":"Hebrews 11:5","text":"\"Translated\" is *metatithēmi* (metatithemi), a compound built on the verb \"to place\" plus a prefix meaning change — literally \"to transpose,\" to put one thing where another had been. The same word describes moving Jacob's bones to a new burial site and swapping one legal arrangement for another elsewhere in the New Testament. Applied to Enoch, it pictures a straight substitution: heaven put in the place earth had occupied, with no interval of death between the two.\n\nThe verse doesn't actually credit Enoch with faith to be translated. God did the translating because Enoch's life pleased him, and it was faith that produced that life in the first place. Genesis' own commentary on the man is simply that he walked with God — ongoing, close company, the kind faith alone sustains.\n\n\"Had witness borne to him\" sits in the perfect tense — not a single compliment paid once, but a testimony still standing on record. The verdict on Enoch's life was settled and filed before the transfer to heaven ever happened.","translation":"By faith Enoch was transferred, so that he did not see death, and he was not found, because God had transferred him. For before his transfer, he had been attested — the record still stands — that he was one who pleased God."},{"c1":11,"v1":6,"c2":11,"v2":6,"label":"Hebrews 11:6","text":"The infinitive behind \"to please\" is aorist, and here that marks a flat, timeless axiom rather than one occasion — without faith, pleasing God isn't merely difficult, it's impossible, no exceptions carved out.\n\nTwo different verbs sit behind the sentence's two English \"is\"-es. The first, that God \"is,\" comes from the plain verb of existence: he simply exists. The second, that he \"is a rewarder,\" comes from *ginomai* (ginomai), the verb of becoming — not a static fact to assent to, but an outcome God proves true in the seeker's own experience. Faith isn't stopping at \"God exists\"; it's trusting that he will show himself faithful to the one who comes to him.\n\n\"Diligently seek\" carries a prefix that sharpens the ordinary verb for seeking into searching something out, tracking it to the end rather than glancing at it once. Faith keeps looking.","translation":"But without faith it is impossible to please him at all, for the one who comes to God must believe that he exists, and that he proves himself a rewarder to those who are earnestly searching him out."},{"c1":11,"v1":7,"c2":11,"v2":7,"label":"Hebrews 11:7","text":"\"Moved with fear\" softens a word that isn't about fright at all. *Eulabeomai* (eulabeomai) means to act with reverent caution — the wary, careful respect of a man taking seriously a warning about something he can't yet see. Noah wasn't spooked into building; he was sobered into it.\n\n\"Prepared\" is *kataskeuazō* (kataskeuazo), the verb for equipping and outfitting something for use, not merely nailing boards together. Noah didn't just build a hull, he fitted it out to actually keep his household alive. \"Ark\" (*kibōtos*, kibotos) is the same word later used for the ark of the covenant — a wooden chest built to carry something precious safely through danger.\n\n*Kosmos* (kosmos), \"world,\" can mean either the physical earth or the human race, and the ambiguity is worth keeping rather than resolving: Noah's faith condemned both at once — it announced coming judgment on the earth itself, and by sheer contrast it exposed the unbelief of everyone around him. The righteousness he's said to have \"become heir of\" is righteousness in conduct, the fruit his faith produced in a life actually lived, not the righteousness that first declares a sinner right with God.","translation":"By faith Noah, having been warned about things not yet seen, with reverent caution equipped an ark for the saving of his household, through which he condemned the world and became an heir of the righteousness that comes according to faith."},{"c1":11,"v1":8,"c2":11,"v2":8,"label":"Hebrews 11:8","text":"\"When he was called\" translates a present participle running alongside the main verb \"obeyed\" — the two actions overlap in the Greek. Abraham's obedience wasn't a response that came later; it happened in the very moment of the call, obedience and summons landing together.\n\n\"Not knowing\" whither he went uses *epistamai* (epistamai), not the ordinary verbs for knowing facts. *Epistamai* is about fixing the mind's attention on something. So this isn't ignorance Abraham stumbled into — it's that he refused to let the unknown destination occupy his thoughts. He didn't need the itinerary to obey the command.","translation":"By faith Abraham, at the very time he was being called, obeyed by going out to a place he was going to receive as an inheritance, and he went out without fixing his mind on where he was going."},{"c1":11,"v1":9,"c2":11,"v2":9,"label":"Hebrews 11:9","text":"\"Sojourned\" is *paroikeō* (paroikeo), the standard word for a resident alien — someone living inside a community's borders without a citizen's rights. Paired with it is the preposition *eis* (eis), which ordinarily marks motion toward a place rather than settled rest. The grammar itself does the work: Abraham moved into the land, and then stayed there as a permanent outsider — at home nowhere and everywhere at once.\n\n\"Strange\" (*allotria*, allotria) simply means \"belonging to another.\" Abraham lived in the very land God had promised him as though it were someone else's property, housed in tents (*skēnē*, skene), the shelter of a man who expects to move again. Isaac and Jacob are named beside him because between the three of them they cover the entire span of that unsettled life in Canaan, all three still waiting on the one promise.","translation":"By faith he went and settled as a foreigner without citizen's rights in the land of the promise, as in a land belonging to another, living in tents together with Isaac and Jacob, fellow heirs of that same promise."},{"c1":11,"v1":10,"c2":11,"v2":10,"label":"Hebrews 11:10","text":"Both \"city\" and \"foundations\" carry the definite article in the Greek — not a city, but the city, the one with the foundations. Abraham had a specific destination in view, not a vague heavenly notion.\n\nThe two words for God's role here, \"builder\" (*technitēs*, technites) and \"maker\" (*dēmiourgos*, demiourgos), are trade-guild language. *Technitēs* is the skilled architect who designs; *dēmiourgos* originally named a workman who builds for the public — for a whole community, not a private patron. Abraham wasn't waiting on real estate. He was waiting on a city God had designed and built for a people.","translation":"For he was continually looking forward to the city that has the foundations, the city whose architect and builder is God."},{"c1":11,"v1":11,"c2":11,"v2":11,"label":"Hebrews 11:11","text":"The intensive pronoun \"herself\" carries real weight — this is the very Sarah who had laughed at the promise in disbelief, now singled out as the one who received power to conceive. Faith didn't erase her history of doubt; it overtook it.\n\nThe phrase behind \"to conceive seed\" uses *katabolē* (katabole), literally \"a throwing down\" — the same word used elsewhere for the founding of the world. It's blunt, physical language for conception, but the shared vocabulary links Sarah's body to the same divine capability that laid the world's foundations: the God who founds worlds can found a family in a womb long past its season.\n\nHer faith rests on a specific judgment about God's character. \"Considered him faithful\" is a verb of reasoned conclusion, not a feeling — she weighed the evidence of who God is and decided he could be trusted to keep his word, whatever her own body told her.","translation":"By faith Sarah herself received power to conceive offspring, even though she was past the proper age, because she had judged him faithful who had made the promise."},{"c1":11,"v1":12,"c2":11,"v2":12,"label":"Hebrews 11:12","text":"\"Sprang\" renders an aorist passive of *gennaō* (gennao), \"to beget\" — one decisive event, not a slow buildup. From a single man an entire nation was born, and the writer underlines just how unlikely that was by adding the emphatic phrase \"and that too,\" practically leaning on the word \"one.\"\n\nThat one man is described with a perfect passive participle usually rendered \"as good as dead\" — the verb means \"rendered dead, deadened,\" and the perfect tense marks this as a settled condition rather than a passing low point: Abraham's body had, for every practical purpose, already given out for fathering children. Paul reaches for the identical participle describing Abraham's body in Romans 4:19.\n\n\"Innumerable\" stacks a negating prefix onto the verb \"to count\" — not simply a great many, but a multitude that has outrun counting, matched against the writer's two pictures of scale: the stars overhead and the sand at the sea's edge.","translation":"And therefore there were born, from one man — and him as good as dead — descendants like the stars of heaven in number, and like the sand along the seashore, beyond counting."},{"c1":11,"v1":13,"c2":11,"v2":13,"label":"Hebrews 11:13","text":"\"In faith\" is *kata pistin* (kata pistin), literally \"according to faith\" — these people didn't merely happen to have faith when death came; they died under faith's rule, governed by it rather than by sight.\n\n\"Embraced\" translates *aspazomai* (aspazomai), the ordinary verb for greeting or waving at someone. The picture is of sailors saluting a coastline they can see on the horizon but haven't yet reached — they saw the fulfillment only from a distance and hailed it as certain, without ever setting foot on it.\n\nCalling themselves \"strangers and pilgrims\" wasn't self-pity; it was a confession they made willingly, accepting a homeless life now because faith had already secured them a home later.","translation":"These all died governed by faith, not having received the promises, but having seen them from a distance and having waved them a greeting, and having confessed that they were strangers and temporary residents on the earth."},{"c1":11,"v1":14,"c2":11,"v2":14,"label":"Hebrews 11:14","text":"\"Say\" is a present participle, *legontes* (legontes) — not something said once, but something kept on saying, a settled confession repeated as a matter of course. Their admission of homelessness wasn't a passing remark; it was their standing testimony about themselves.\n\n\"Fatherland\" (*patris*, patris) is built directly on the word for father — not merely \"a country,\" but the ancestral home that belongs to one's own line. And \"seeking\" carries an intensifying prefix that sharpens the ordinary verb into an active, deliberate search. They weren't drifting toward somewhere vague; they were pursuing a specific home they knew was theirs by right.","translation":"For those who keep saying such things make it plain that they are earnestly seeking a fatherland."},{"c1":11,"v1":15,"c2":11,"v2":15,"label":"Hebrews 11:15","text":"\"Been mindful\" renders an imperfect verb — not one backward glance but a habitual, ongoing remembering. The logic runs: if they had kept dwelling on the country they'd left, the door back would have stood open the whole time.\n\n\"Opportunity\" is *kairos* (kairos), not the word for clock-time but for a fitting, seizable occasion. Turning back was a live option available to them at every stage, not a door slammed shut behind them. Their not returning was a continual choice against a continual possibility — which is exactly what makes it evidence of faith rather than mere circumstance.","translation":"And if indeed they had kept remembering that country from which they had come out, they would have kept having opportunity to turn back."},{"c1":11,"v1":16,"c2":11,"v2":16,"label":"Hebrews 11:16","text":"\"Called\" translates *epikaleisthai* (epikaleisthai), a compound that means specifically \"to be surnamed\" — adding a name onto one someone already bears. God isn't merely willing to be associated with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob; he takes their names onto his own as a surname he's glad to wear — \"the God of Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob.\"\n\n\"Prepared\" is a simple aorist, a completed and settled act. God has already built the city these patriarchs were reaching for, and that finished provision is the very ground given for why he isn't ashamed to carry their name. The relationship isn't tentative on his side.","translation":"But now, as the case stands, they are reaching for a better country, that is, a heavenly one. That is why God is not ashamed to be called by their name, as their God — for he prepared a city for them."},{"c1":11,"v1":17,"c2":11,"v2":19,"label":"Hebrews 11:17–19","text":"The verb behind \"offered up\" stands in the perfect tense while \"being tested\" runs alongside it as a present participle — grammatically, Abraham had already offered Isaac before the test ever reached its conclusion. The sacrifice was complete in his will well before any hand stopped his; the perfect tense marks it as a settled, finished act of the heart, not a last-second reversal.\n\nIsaac is called \"only begotten\" (*monogenēs*, monogenes) even though Abraham had another son in Ishmael. The word marks him as unique with respect to the promise — the one specific son through whom the line was to run, irreplaceable for that purpose whether or not he was literally the only child.\n\n\"Received him back in a figure\" (*en parabolē*, en parabole) signals that the resurrection in view here was symbolic, not literal — Isaac never actually died. The ram substituted in the thicket enacted death and restoration in Isaac's place, a parable acted out, and Abraham's reasoning that God could raise the dead proved sound within that acted picture, long before it was tested on the real thing at an empty tomb.","translation":"By faith Abraham, while being tested, had already offered up Isaac — yes, he who had received the promises was offering up his one-of-a-kind son, the one about whom it had been said, In Isaac shall your seed be called — having reasoned that God was able to raise him even from among the dead, from which, figuratively speaking, he did receive him back."},{"c1":11,"v1":20,"c2":11,"v2":20,"label":"Hebrews 11:20","text":"The Greek carries a small \"and\" that some English versions drop: \"by faith, and that concerning things to come, Isaac blessed...\" The extra word throws emphasis onto the blessing's reach — this wasn't a wish for the next harvest, it reached forward to events neither son would live to see fulfilled.","translation":"By faith, and that regarding things still to come, Isaac blessed Jacob and Esau."},{"c1":11,"v1":21,"c2":11,"v2":21,"label":"Hebrews 11:21","text":"*Hekaston* (hekastos), \"each,\" rather than a vague \"both,\" marks Jacob's blessing as two distinct, deliberate acts — he blessed Manasseh and Ephraim individually, discerning between them, much as he had already shown discernment in placing the younger son ahead of the elder. The detail \"leaning on the top of his staff\" reproduces the Greek Old Testament's rendering of Genesis 47:31, where the unpointed Hebrew consonants could be read either \"staff\" or \"bed\" depending on which vowels were supplied. The Septuagint translators chose \"staff,\" and the writer to the Hebrews simply follows that established Greek wording rather than working back to the Hebrew himself. Whichever object is actually meant, the picture stands: a dying man too weak to stand upright, still bowing in worship.","translation":"By faith Jacob, when he was dying, blessed each one of the sons of Joseph, and worshiped, leaning on the top of his staff."},{"c1":11,"v1":22,"c2":11,"v2":22,"label":"Hebrews 11:22","text":"\"Made mention\" translates *emnēmoneusen* (mnēmoneuō), \"to call to mind, to remember\" — but here the memory runs forward rather than back. Joseph is dying in Egypt with no exodus yet in sight; his faith consists in remembering God's ancient promise to Abraham clearly enough to stake his own burial arrangements on it centuries in advance. The instruction concerning his bones, fulfilled only when Joshua finally carried them into Canaan, is the tangible proof: a man ordering his own skeleton moved because he trusted a promise he would never live to see kept.","translation":"By faith Joseph, when he was coming to his end, called to mind the exodus of the sons of Israel, and gave command concerning his bones."},{"c1":11,"v1":23,"c2":11,"v2":23,"label":"Hebrews 11:23","text":"\"Proper\" translates *asteion* (asteios), a word that originally described someone with the polish of the city — \"urbane, refined\" — before it broadened to mean simply \"beautiful, comely.\" The phrase is a dative of respect: the child was comely with respect to God, meaning in God's own estimation, not merely his parents' fond judgment. Stephen uses the identical word of the infant Moses in Acts 7:20. \"Commandment\" renders *diatagma* (diatagma), a formal royal edict rather than an informal directive — Pharaoh's order carried the full weight of state law, which makes the parents' three-month concealment a calculated act of civil disobedience grounded in a higher allegiance than the throne.","translation":"By faith Moses, after he was born, was hidden three months by his parents, because they saw that the child was comely; and they did not fear the king's edict."},{"c1":11,"v1":24,"c2":11,"v2":26,"label":"Hebrews 11:24–26","text":"\"Choosing\" is an aorist middle participle — a single decisive act of the will, not a gradual drift away from Egypt. The \"pleasures of sin\" Moses walked away from translate *apolausin* (apolausis), a word for full, satisfied enjoyment rather than crude vice; the phrase covers the ambition and refined comforts available to him in Pharaoh's court, not merely sensual indulgence. \"He esteemed\" translates *hēgēsamenos* (hēgeomai), the language of calculation and weighing — an aorist that pictures Moses doing the math once and reckoning Christ's reproach worth more than Egypt's treasure. \"He had respect unto\" renders *aphorōn* (aphoraō), a compound verb meaning literally \"to look away from\" one thing in order to fix the eyes on another; it is present tense, a sustained, ongoing gaze, not a single glance. And \"recompense\" is *misthapodosian* (misthapodosia), built from *misthos* (\"wages\") and the verb \"to pay back\" — not a gift graciously bestowed but a wage owed, the debt God guarantees to settle.","translation":"By faith Moses, when he had become great, refused to be called a son of Pharaoh's daughter, having chosen rather to be mistreated together with the people of God than to have the temporary enjoyment of sin, having counted the reproach of the Messiah greater wealth than the treasures of Egypt; for he was continually looking away to the reward."},{"c1":11,"v1":27,"c2":11,"v2":27,"label":"Hebrews 11:27","text":"\"He endured\" translates *ekarteresen* (kartereō), built on *karteros*, \"strong, steadfast\" — the verb pictures holding out with a firm grip. The tense is a constative aorist: it scoops up all forty years in Midian into a single summary snapshot of persistent faith rather than narrating them year by year. The apparent tension with Exodus 2:15, where fear drives Moses out of Egypt, dissolves once the two fears in view are distinguished: one fear — of being exposed as the Egyptian's killer — sent him running; a different fear he refused to feel, cowering before Pharaoh's continued wrath once his flight had already declared him committed to his own people. \"As seeing\" is a present participle, not a single glance but a sustained, ongoing gaze fixed on the invisible God — the thing that kept him steady through four decades of obscurity.","translation":"By faith he left Egypt behind, not fearing the anger of the king; for he held out with a steadfast grip, as one continually seeing him who is invisible."},{"c1":11,"v1":28,"c2":11,"v2":28,"label":"Hebrews 11:28","text":"\"Kept\" translates a perfect tense of *poieō*, \"to make, institute\" — *pepoiēken* — and the perfect tense is the whole point: it doesn't just report a past founding, it insists the significance of what Moses instituted still held at the moment of writing. The Passover ritual itself was always temporary, a shadow due to expire once the reality it pointed to arrived; but as a picture of Christ's sacrifice, its meaning remained fully in force for the letter's first readers. \"Sprinkling\" is *proschysin* (proschysis), from a verb meaning \"to pour toward\" or \"dash against\" — describing a forceful action rather than a light mist, closer to pouring blood against a doorpost than dabbing it on with a brush.","translation":"By faith he has instituted the Passover and the pouring out of the blood, so that the destroyer of the firstborn should not touch them."},{"c1":11,"v1":29,"c2":11,"v2":29,"label":"Hebrews 11:29","text":"The commentary tradition has little to add here, but the Greek text itself repays a glance. \"Passed through\" is *diebēsan* (diabainō), an aorist that treats the whole crossing as one accomplished act rather than a slow process. \"Taking a trial of it\" (*peiran labontes*) is a vivid idiom — the Egyptians didn't simply attempt the same crossing, they \"took a taste\" or \"made an experiment\" of the sea, as though testing whether it would hold for them too. It did not. \"Were drowned\" translates *katepothēsan* (katapinō), \"to gulp down, swallow\" — the sea doesn't merely drown the pursuers in this picture, it swallows them whole.","translation":"By faith they passed through the Red Sea as through dry land; and when the Egyptians attempted the same, they were swallowed up."},{"c1":11,"v1":30,"c2":11,"v2":30,"label":"Hebrews 11:30","text":"Another bare entry, but two grammatical facts stand out. \"Fell\" (*epesan*, from piptō) is a plain aorist — one completed collapse, not a gradual crumbling. \"Having been encircled\" (*kyklōthenta*, from kykloō) is an aorist passive participle: the walls did not fall because Israel stormed them, but only after they had first been marched around. The order of the Greek clause preserves the logic of Joshua 6 — circling in obedience, faith exercised in the waiting, and only then the collapse.","translation":"By faith the walls of Jericho fell, after they had been encircled for seven days."},{"c1":11,"v1":31,"c2":11,"v2":31,"label":"Hebrews 11:31","text":"\"Believed not\" translates *apeithēsasin* (apeitheō), not the ordinary word for unbelief but one built on the idea of disobedience — the sin of Jericho's residents was not simple doubt but a refusal to act on news of Israel's God that had already reached them. Rahab's contrasting verb, \"received,\" is *dexamenē* (dechomai), the word for welcoming a guest as a friend rather than merely admitting a stranger into one's house — she did not just let the spies in, she took their side. \"With peace\" underscores the same point: she received them without enmity or treachery, at genuine risk to herself.","translation":"By faith Rahab the harlot did not perish along with those who were disobedient, having welcomed the spies as friends, in peace."},{"c1":11,"v1":32,"c2":11,"v2":32,"label":"Hebrews 11:32","text":"The writer breaks off the roll call with a rhetorical question built on a striking verb: *epileipsei* (epileipō), \"to fail, run short, give out\" — the word used of a spring running dry or supplies giving out. Time itself, he says, will fail him before the stories do; the participle *diēgoumenon* (diēgeomai), \"narrating, recounting in detail,\" pictures him cut off mid-story, not for want of material but for want of hours. The six names that follow — Gideon, Barak, Samson, Jephthah, David, Samuel — are simply piled up with no interest in chronological or moral ranking; the effect wanted is sheer accumulated weight of testimony.","translation":"And what more shall I say? For time will fail me if I go on narrating about Gideon, Barak, Samson, Jephthah, David also, and Samuel, and the prophets."},{"c1":11,"v1":33,"c2":11,"v2":33,"label":"Hebrews 11:33","text":"\"Subdued\" translates *katēgōnisanto* (katagōnizomai), a compound built on the same root as our word \"agony\" — literally \"to fight down, wrestle to the ground.\" It is the language of a desperate, close-quarters struggle, not an easy walkover, and it fits kings like David wrestling entire kingdoms into submission. \"Wrought righteousness\" pairs these conquests with just governance: the phrase has in view not merely private virtue but the public exercise of power, faith showing itself in these men by binding conquest to justice rather than to tyranny.","translation":"Who through faith wrestled down kingdoms, worked righteousness, obtained the fulfillment of promises, shut the mouths of lions."},{"c1":11,"v1":34,"c2":11,"v2":34,"label":"Hebrews 11:34","text":"The list piles up short, punchy aorists with no connecting particles — a rhetorical burst rather than a smooth sentence. \"Quenched the power of fire\" uses *dynamin* (dynamis) in its raw sense of \"force, violence,\" not abstract potency. \"Escaped the edge of the sword\" is literally \"escaped the mouths of the sword\" (*stomata machairēs*) — the same word used for a beast's mouth, picturing the blade as something that devours rather than merely cuts. \"Out of weakness were made strong\" is a passive, *edynamōthēsan* (dynamoō) — strength given, not achieved, sharing its root with *dynamis* above. \"Turned to flight armies of aliens\" uses *eklinan* (klinō), \"to bend, incline\" — a technical military term for routing an enemy line until it buckles and breaks.","translation":"Quenched the force of fire, escaped the mouths of the sword, out of weakness were made powerful, became mighty in war, made the armies of foreigners bend and break."},{"c1":11,"v1":35,"c2":11,"v2":35,"label":"Hebrews 11:35","text":"\"Were tortured\" translates *etympanisthēsan* (tympanizō), a verb built directly on *tympanon*, the wheel-shaped rack over which a victim was stretched like a drumhead and then beaten. The word is brutally literal, not a generic term for suffering. These martyrs are offered a way off the rack — recant, and live — and the text marks that offer with the definite article, \"the deliverance,\" the specific release actually on the table, which they refuse. They hold out instead for \"a better resurrection,\" *kreittonos* (kreittōn), a comparative that measures this hope against mere restoration to ordinary life — like the widow's son raised earlier in the same verse — and finds it wanting; they wanted resurrection to glory, not simply resumed existence.","translation":"Women received back their dead by resurrection; and others were racked and beaten, refusing to accept the release offered them, so that they might obtain a better resurrection."},{"c1":11,"v1":36,"c2":11,"v2":36,"label":"Hebrews 11:36","text":"\"Others\" shifts from *allos* (used elsewhere in the chapter for \"another of the same kind\") to *heteroi* (heteros), \"others of a different kind\" — signaling a change of category, from martyrs killed outright to those who endured a slower ordeal. \"Yea, moreover\" translates *eti de*, a climactic formula that ratchets the list upward: after mockings and scourgings comes something the writer treats as worse still — chains and prison, the kind of confinement Jeremiah endured, a drawn-out torment of the mind rather than a single blow to the body.","translation":"And still others of a different kind received trial by mockings and scourgings, and, what is more, by chains and imprisonment."},{"c1":11,"v1":37,"c2":11,"v2":37,"label":"Hebrews 11:37","text":"The rapid, unconnected rhythm continues. \"They died by slaughter of the sword\" points to mass killings rather than isolated executions — the kind of scenes the letter's first Jewish readers would already know well from the Maccabean persecutions. The sheepskins and goatskins were not a prophet's professional uniform; they are mentioned because these fugitives had nothing else to wear. \"Afflicted\" translates *thlibomenoi* (thlibō), \"to press hard upon, to squeeze\" — the image is of being crushed from outside; \"tormented\" is *kakouchoumenoi* (kakoucheō), \"to treat badly, maltreat,\" a catch-all word for abuse covering whatever specific cruelty is not already named.","translation":"They were stoned, they were tempted, they were sawn in two, they died by slaughter of the sword; they went about in sheepskins, in goatskins, being in want, pressed hard, treated with every kind of abuse."},{"c1":11,"v1":38,"c2":11,"v2":38,"label":"Hebrews 11:38","text":"\"World\" here is *kosmos*, but not the corrupt system the word usually names elsewhere in the New Testament — it is the world considered as an ordered economy, a whole way of valuing and organizing life. That economy runs on sight and self-interest; the martyrs ran on faith, operating on an entirely different plane, so the world that drove them out into deserts and mountains turns out to be the one found wanting, not them. \"Caves\" translates *opais* (opē), a word that originally meant simply \"a hole\" or an opening one can see through — the same word the Greek Old Testament uses for the cleft in the rock where God hid Moses to let His glory pass by. These heroes of faith ended their lives in the same kind of hole God once used as a shelter for His own glory.\n\nThe passage insists in the same breath that this whole company \"received not the promise.\" The verb is a plain aorist of *lambanō*, \"to take, receive\" — they died without the object of the promise in hand, holding only the certainty that it was coming. That gap between promise given and promise fulfilled is not a mark of failure; it is structural to the whole chapter. Every hero named here saw the goal from a distance and died still reaching toward it.\n\nThe reason supplied is striking: God had \"provided some better thing for us,\" so that the Old Testament faithful should not be made perfect apart from us. \"Made perfect\" translates a form of *teleioō*, a verb this letter uses repeatedly for the finishing work accomplished by Christ's better sacrifice and better covenant — not moral improvement but the reaching of a designed goal. The grammar binds Old and New Testament believers into a single, jointly-completed company: their perfecting waits on ours, so that no stage of God's redemptive plan reaches its finish line alone.\n\nThat roll call then feeds straight into the exhortation that follows, where these figures are called a \"cloud of witnesses\" — *nephos*, not the sharply outlined *nephelē* but a vast, formless mass filling the whole visible sky, the very image classical writers used for an overwhelming multitude of people. \"Witnesses\" is *martyrōn* (martys), the term for one who testifies to what he has personally seen — these figures are less spectators in a grandstand than a bank of testimony the letter's readers are urged to draw strength from as they run their own race.","translation":"Men of whom the world was not worthy — wandering in deserts and mountains, and in caves and holes of the earth. And these all, though they were borne witness to through their faith, did not receive the promise, since God had provided some better thing for us, so that apart from us they should not be made perfect."},{"c1":12,"v1":2,"c2":12,"v2":2,"label":"Hebrews 12:2","text":"The roll call of Old Testament faith in chapter 11 was building to this: past every witness in that great cloud, the writer points his readers to one supreme example. \"Looking\" translates *aphorao* (aphoraō), a verb built on \"away from\" fused to \"see\" — it pictures a runner wrenching his eyes off the grandstand and welding them onto the finish line, sight and mind together locked on a single point. Let that focus drift toward the crowd, and the pace slackens; the Christian life runs the same way. \"Author\" renders *archēgos* (archēgos), built from \"to lead\" and \"first\": not merely the originator of faith but the trailblazer, the one who runs the course first and so becomes the pattern every runner after him follows. \"Finisher\" is *teleioō* (teleioō), \"to carry through to completion\" — Jesus did not simply possess faith, He lived it out to its fullest possible expression, so that His life stands as the finished, perfect specimen of what faith looks like from start to end. The preposition behind \"for the joy set before him\" is *anti* (anti), whose ordinary first-century meaning is \"instead of,\" not \"in order to obtain.\" The joy was already His — the glory shared with the Father before the world began — and the cross was what He accepted in exchange for it, not a prize He was chasing. \"Set\" is *prokeimenēs* (prokeimenēs), \"lying before,\" a joy already present and possessed, surrendered rather than pursued. And \"is set down\" is a perfect tense: He sat down once, His work finished, and He remains seated — no repeat performance, no further offering required. Having set Jesus before them like this, the writer immediately urges his readers to \"consider\" him — *analogizomai* (analogizomai), \"to reckon up, weigh side by side\" — the opposition he endured, called *antilogia* (antilogia), \"speaking against,\" so that these persecuted believers might measure their own suffering against his and refuse to grow faint of heart.","translation":"Looking off and away, our eyes fixed on Jesus, the pioneer and perfecter of the faith, who instead of the joy then lying before him endured a cross, despising its shame, and has sat down and remains seated at the right hand of the throne of God."},{"c1":12,"v1":4,"c2":12,"v2":4,"label":"Hebrews 12:4","text":"The readers had suffered under persecution, but not yet to the point of bloodshed, unlike Messiah, whose obedience carried him all the way to a bloody death on a cross. \"Resist\" is *antikathistēmi* (antikathistēmi), a compound meaning literally \"to stand against\" — a posture of opposition. \"Striving\" is *antagōnizomai* (antagōnizomai), \"to fight agonizingly against,\" the word behind our \"agonize\": not a light skirmish but a brutal, exhausting match against the pull toward apostasy. The Proverbs quotation that follows supplies the key term for the rest of the passage — \"chastening,\" *paideia* (paideia). This is not primarily a word for punishment; it names the whole process of raising and training a child, correcting faults and cultivating virtue toward maturity. Reading their persecution through this word reframes it entirely: not retribution for wrongdoing, but the discipline that shapes a son into what he is meant to become.","translation":"Not yet have you resisted to the point of shedding blood, in your struggle against sin."},{"c1":12,"v1":7,"c2":12,"v2":7,"label":"Hebrews 12:7","text":"The word rendered \"endure\" is *hupomenō* (hupomenō), literally \"to remain under\" — the readers are pictured staying put beneath a weight rather than shrugging it off. The grammar here describes an ongoing fact, not a conditional: it is for training, not despite it, that they are remaining under these pressures. \"Dealeth\" translates *prospherō* (prospherō) in its passive form, \"to be treated, to be behaved toward\" — the very fact of God's afflictive dealing with them is itself the proof that they belong to him. This letter went out to a mixed congregation, saved and unsaved alike sitting under the same persecution. What separated the two was precisely this: who would keep remaining under the discipline, and who would bolt back to the old sacrifices to escape it.","translation":"It is for training that you are remaining under it; God is dealing with you as with sons. For what son is there whom a father does not discipline?"},{"c1":12,"v1":8,"c2":12,"v2":8,"label":"Hebrews 12:8","text":"Old Testament Israel had learned to read any national hardship — drought, famine, invasion — as a sign of God's displeasure. It would have been natural for these first-century Hebrews to read their persecution the same way. The writer flips the logic: far from proving God's disfavor, the discipline proves the opposite. Every genuine son gets disciplined; a household that leaves a child untouched by correction has not treated him as a son at all. Absence of discipline, not its presence, is the danger sign here.","translation":"But if you are without the discipline in which all sons have shared, then you are illegitimate, and not sons."},{"c1":12,"v1":9,"c2":12,"v2":9,"label":"Hebrews 12:9","text":"The connective \"furthermore\" signals a shift in the argument: up to this point the comparison has run between God's fatherhood and human fatherhood generally; now the writer narrows it to make the human side look small. Our earthly fathers disciplined us according to \"the flesh\" — their claim on us is limited, physical, temporary — while God is called \"Father of spirits,\" the one who generates and sustains our essential, eternal being. Human fathers we respected out of natural affection or plain fear; how much more, then, does the Father of spirits deserve our full submission? \"Live\" is not pushed off into some future reward — it names real, present vitality now, the difference between merely existing and actually having life.","translation":"Furthermore, we have had fathers of our flesh as those who disciplined us, and we kept paying them reverence. Shall we not much more be subjecting ourselves to the Father of spirits, and live?"},{"c1":12,"v1":10,"c2":12,"v2":10,"label":"Hebrews 12:10","text":"Two things mark human discipline as imperfect. First, it is confined to childhood — it stops at manhood whether or not it finished its work. Second, human parents are short-sighted and fallible; the word behind \"pleasure\" is *dokeō* (dokeō), \"to be of the opinion, to suppose\" — it names subjective judgment, guesswork that may or may not land on what is actually best. What seemed good to a parent was not always what was good for the child. God's discipline carries no such limitation. It is not guesswork but purpose, aimed at one clear end: that we come to share, in actual experience, in his own holiness.","translation":"For they, on the one hand, disciplined us for a few days according to what seemed good to them; but he disciplines us for our profit, so that we may share in his holiness."},{"c1":12,"v1":11,"c2":12,"v2":11,"label":"Hebrews 12:11","text":"The negation in this verse falls on \"seems,\" not on \"chastening\" — no discipline, of any kind, seems pleasant while it is happening. \"Yieldeth\" is *apodidōmi* (apodidōmi), \"to give back\" — carrying a hint of repayment, as though the harvest is compensation for what the discipline cost. \"Exercised\" is *gumnazō* (gumnazō), the verb behind \"gymnasium,\" used of athletes training their bodies through repeated strain. The picture is of spiritual muscle built the same way physical muscle is built — through resistance, not comfort. What these readers were going through was, in that sense, training: the internal struggle between retreating to the old system for relief and pressing on in a faith not yet fully proven to them.","translation":"Now all discipline, for the present, does not seem to be joyful but painful; yet afterward it keeps on yielding back the peaceable fruit of righteousness to those who have been trained by it."},{"c1":12,"v1":12,"c2":12,"v2":12,"label":"Hebrews 12:12","text":"\"Lift up\" translates *anorthoō* (anorthoō), a term used by medical writers for setting a dislocated joint back into place and restoring it to strength — far more forceful than a simple \"raise.\" These are not tired hands that need encouragement; they are joints that need to be reset. \"Hang down\" is *pariēmi* (pariēmi), originally \"to let pass, to disregard,\" and by extension \"to go slack\" — describing hands that have simply stopped functioning, most likely in prayer, through neglect. \"Feeble\" is *paraluō* (paraluō), the very word behind \"paralytic,\" the same term used of the palsied man in Matthew 8. These are not merely weary limbs; they are limbs gone numb and unresponsive, in need of active reinvigoration, not gentle encouragement.","translation":"Therefore, set straight again the hands that have gone slack and the knees that have become paralyzed."},{"c1":12,"v1":13,"c2":12,"v2":13,"label":"Hebrews 12:13","text":"\"Paths\" is *trochia* (trochia), literally \"wheel-track\" — the rut a cart wears into a road. \"Straight\" here is *orthos* (orthos), used not in the sense of straight-versus-crooked but of level, smooth, passable — clear the ruts so nothing catches a wheel. \"Lame\" is *cholos* (cholos), used elsewhere of physical limping but applied here to a spiritual stagger, describing those among the readers most shaken by persecution and closest to turning back. \"Turned out of the way\" translates *ektrepō* (ektrepō), another term drawn from medicine, meaning \"to wrench or twist out of joint\" — the image is of a limping limb further injured by rough ground rather than helped over it. The exhortation is aimed at the already-faithful: live steadily enough that your example smooths the road rather than laming further those still finding their footing.","translation":"And keep making straight wheel-tracks for your feet, so that what is lame may not be wrenched out of joint, but rather be healed."},{"c1":12,"v1":14,"c2":12,"v2":14,"label":"Hebrews 12:14","text":"\"Follow\" is *diōkō* (diōkō), \"to run swiftly in pursuit, to press hard after\" — the same verb Paul uses of straining toward the finish line in Philippians 3:12. There is nothing passive in it; it describes a racer's urgency applied to the pursuit of peace and holiness. Given the historical setting, the exhortation carries real bite: persecution breeds exactly the kind of resentment and division that make peace hard to chase, especially toward believers who look weak or wavering. The holiness in view is not abstract piety but the specific standing this letter has already described — a cleansed conscience, real access to God through Christ's sacrifice. Without it, no one sees the Lord.","translation":"Keep constantly pursuing peace with everyone, and the holiness apart from which no one will see the Lord."},{"c1":12,"v1":15,"c2":12,"v2":15,"label":"Hebrews 12:15","text":"\"Looking diligently\" translates *episkopeō* (episkopeō), the verb behind our word \"bishop\" and the same root used of elders exercising oversight in 1 Peter 5:2 and Acts 20:28. The prefix turns a simple \"look\" into \"watch over, keep guard\" — the readers are told to police their own condition the way an overseer watches a congregation. \"Fail\" is *hustereō* (hustereō) combined with \"away from,\" giving \"to fall back from\" something already attained — and it is a present-tense participle, describing a drift already underway rather than a hypothetical danger. This pictures precisely the unsaved Jew who has been carried along by the Spirit's convicting work up to the very edge of faith, and who now risks slipping back rather than crossing over. \"Root of bitterness\" also carries a present participle — \"springing up\" — a process caught mid-motion, still revealing what kind of root it is as it grows, with the power to poison not just one person but, as the definite article specifies, \"the many\": the majority of the congregation.\n\nThe same watchfulness applies to the danger of scandal. \"Profane,\" describing Esau, is *bebēlos* (bebēlos), a word originally used of ground open to any foot to walk on — common, unconsecrated — standing opposite *hagios* (hagios), \"set apart, holy.\" Esau was profane not primarily in the moral sense but in this deeper sense: nothing in him was reserved for God. His birthright carried the family priesthood, and he traded it for a single meal, treating what should have been sacred as ordinary currency. The parallel to these readers is exact and uncomfortable: like Esau, they only professed to hold something — salvation — that had not yet, in the case of the unconverted among them, actually been secured, and they stood in danger of trading that profession for relief from persecution just as cheaply as Esau traded his birthright for soup.\n\nWhen Esau later wanted the blessing back, Scripture records that he \"found no place of repentance.\" Greek supplies two distinct words for repentance, and the choice here matters. *Metamelomai* (metamelomai) means regret or remorse over the consequences of an act — it is the word used of Judas. *Metanoeō* (metanoeō) means an actual reversal of moral purpose, a genuine change of mind and direction. Esau could work himself into tears of remorse; he could never work himself into that deeper reversal. The warning to the readers follows the same line as the sin of chapter 6: a hardness that forecloses on its own future, not because God's mercy runs out, but because the heart that produced it no longer wants what mercy offers.\n\nThe passage that follows sets this warning against the backdrop of Sinai itself. \"Might be touched\" is *psēlaphaō* (psēlaphaō), a present participle describing groping or feeling after something rather than confirmed contact — the mountain was material and reachable, but only just, and only through fear. \"Blackness\" and \"darkness\" render two different Greek words for gloom, the kind of half-dark that swallows color without erasing it entirely — dusk, or the shadow of death. \"Tempest\" is *thuella* (thuella), from a root meaning \"to boil or foam\" — a sudden, violent, upward-tearing gale, not a gentle wind. This is the old covenant's own self-portrait: a mountain wrapped in fear. The readers are being told plainly what they are not returning to.","translation":"Keep exercising oversight over yourselves, so that no one may be falling back from the grace of God; so that no root of bitterness, springing up, may trouble you, and through it the many be defiled."},{"c1":12,"v1":19,"c2":12,"v2":19,"label":"Hebrews 12:19","text":"The writer keeps piling up the sensory details of Sinai — trumpet blast, an audible voice speaking actual words — to press home just how overwhelming and material that revelation was. The people who heard it did not ask for more; they begged that not one further word be added. That reaction alone tells the story: this was a revelation the hearers could not endure directly, in sharp contrast to what the readers are being invited into instead.","translation":"And to a blast of a trumpet, and to a sound of words being spoken — a sound of such a kind that those who heard it begged that no further word be added to them."},{"c1":12,"v1":20,"c2":12,"v2":20,"label":"Hebrews 12:20","text":"\"Touch\" here is *thinganō* (thinganō), \"to touch, to handle\" in a way that affects the object touched — in classical usage it often describes laying hands on something sacred, an act capable of desecrating it. To touch Sinai at all, even accidentally, was to profane it, and the penalty was severe enough that it applied even to a wandering animal. The command reveals just how absolute the boundary around the old covenant's holy ground was — untouchable, on pain of death, even for a beast that could not know better.","translation":"For they could not bear what was commanded: \"If even a beast touches the mountain, it is to be stoned.\""},{"c1":12,"v1":21,"c2":12,"v2":21,"label":"Hebrews 12:21","text":"\"Fear\" here is intensified by its prefix — *ekphobos* (ekphobos), built on the preposition \"out of\" attached to \"fear,\" meaning something closer to \"frightened right out of oneself.\" Even Moses, who spoke with God face to face, was reduced to this by the sight at Sinai. If the mediator of the old covenant reacted this way, the writer's point about what that revelation actually felt like from the inside needs no further argument.","translation":"And so terrifying was the sight that Moses said, \"I am frightened out of myself, and trembling.\""},{"c1":12,"v1":22,"c2":12,"v2":24,"label":"Hebrews 12:22–24","text":"Against that terrifying backdrop, the writer swings the readers toward the opposite picture entirely: not a mountain wrapped in dread but a city alive with celebration. \"General assembly\" translates *panēguris* (panēguris), a classical term for a festival gathering of an entire people turned out for public games or a national celebration — the word itself carries a note of festivity that has nothing in common with Sinai's gloom. \"Church\" is *ekklēsia* (ekklēsia), \"a called-out assembly\" — here naming the whole company of the redeemed, and \"the spirits of just men made perfect\" names specifically those already gone ahead into glory. Every phrase in this list functions as a deliberate counterweight to the fire, blackness, and trumpet-blast just described: angels beyond counting instead of terror, festival instead of dread, access instead of a boundary line no one dared cross.\n\nBest of all, this whole gathering centers on Jesus himself, called here \"mediator of a new covenant\" and connected to \"the blood of sprinkling.\" The comparison is not between Christ's blood and the blood that literally flowed from Abel's own body — it is between Christ's blood and the animal blood Abel offered in sacrifice. The entire letter has been building toward exactly this claim: that the blood of the new covenant does not merely equal but supersedes and replaces every drop shed under the old sacrificial system. Abel's offering could only point forward; this one actually accomplishes what it points to.","translation":"But you have come to Mount Zion, and to the city of the living God, heavenly Jerusalem, and to countless myriads of angels in festal gathering, and to the assembly of the firstborn enrolled in heaven, and to God, judge of all, and to the spirits of righteous ones made perfect, and to Jesus, mediator of a new covenant, and to blood that speaks something better than Abel's."},{"c1":12,"v1":26,"c2":12,"v2":26,"label":"Hebrews 12:26","text":"At Sinai, God's voice shook the physical earth — a single, historical, localized event. The promise the writer now quotes from Haggai points to something larger still on the horizon: a coming shaking that will not stop at the earth but will reach the heavens themselves. What was once a mountain trembling under a voice becomes, in this prophecy, the entire created order set trembling under the same voice — the same God who spoke through angels at Sinai now speaks through his own Son, and one day that voice will unmake and remake the whole universe.","translation":"Whose voice then shook the earth; but now he has given a promise, on record, saying, \"Yet once more I will shake not only the earth but also the heaven.\""},{"c1":12,"v1":27,"c2":12,"v2":27,"label":"Hebrews 12:27","text":"The writer zeroes in on one phrase from the Haggai quotation — \"yet once more\" — because of what it implies: this final shaking is final, a last event before the eternal order is established. \"Removing\" translates *metatithēmi* (metatithēmi), \"to transfer to a new basis, to transpose\" — not a word for annihilation but for exchange, moving something from one footing onto another. What gets shaken is everything that was ever made, the entire created order under the weight of the curse; what remains is whatever cannot be shaken in the first place. The point is not destruction for its own sake but clarification: stripping away the temporary reveals what was eternal all along.\n\nOn the strength of that permanence, the readers are told to hold onto grace and offer God worship marked by *eulabeia* (eulabeia) — not timidity but careful, deliberate reverence — and by *deos* (deos), a wholesome, sobered awe, deliberately distinct from *phobos* (phobos), the cringing terror of someone facing raw danger. \"Consuming fire\" is not decoration; it names the same holiness that once wrapped Sinai in smoke, a holiness that will not coexist indefinitely with persistent, unrepented evil. For a congregation being tempted to trade the reality of Christ's sacrifice for the shadow of the old one, the warning and the comfort arrive in the very same sentence.","translation":"And this phrase, \"yet once more,\" signals the transposing onto a new basis of the things that are shaken, as of things that have been made, so that the things not shaken may remain."},{"c1":13,"v1":2,"c2":13,"v2":2,"label":"Hebrews 13:2","text":"\"Hospitality\" translates *philoxenia* (philoxenia), literally \"love of strangers\" — *phileo* (to love, be fond of) fused with *xenos* (a stranger or foreign guest). The command \"be not forgetful\" uses a negated present imperative, which in Greek prohibits an ongoing lapse rather than a single slip: not \"don't forget this once\" but \"stop letting it drop — keep it up.\" The reward clause trades on a verb meaning \"to escape notice\": some, without realizing what they were doing, ended up hosting angels as houseguests. The point rests on real uncertainty — you can't always tell, from the outside, who you're feeding.","translation":"Do not keep on forgetting hospitality to strangers, for through this some, without knowing it, entertained angels as guests."},{"c1":13,"v1":3,"c2":13,"v2":3,"label":"Hebrews 13:3","text":"\"Be mindful\" is a present imperative — a standing habit of attention, not a passing glance. \"As bound with them\" comes from a perfect passive participle built on *deo* (deo, \"to bind\") with the prefix *syn* (\"together with\"): readers are told to picture themselves chained alongside the prisoner, not merely sympathizing from outside the cell. The final phrase, \"in the body,\" grounds the whole appeal in something unavoidably physical — you too wear flesh that can be seized and starved, so the sufferer's body is to be treated as continuous with your own.","translation":"Keep on being mindful of those in prison, as though bound together with them; of those being mistreated, since you yourselves also are in a body."},{"c1":13,"v1":4,"c2":13,"v2":4,"label":"Hebrews 13:4","text":"Greek often drops the verb \"to be,\" and this verse is a case in point — the writer supplies none, leaving translators to decide whether he states a fact or gives an order. Context favors a command: a present imperative, \"let marriage be held...\" \"Honor\" is *timios* (timios), literally \"of great price\" — the word for something so valuable it is prized rather than merely respected. The purity of the marriage bed follows as the natural consequence of that valuation, not as a separate rule bolted on.\n\nThe next verses pivot to money and trust. \"Without covetousness\" is the compound *aphilarguros* (aphilarguros) — an alpha-negative plus *phileo* (\"to love\") plus *arguros* (\"silver\"): \"without any fondness for silver.\" \"Content\" translates *arkeo* (arkeo), a verb meaning to possess enough strength in oneself to lack nothing needed; its cognate noun gave the Stoics their word for self-sufficiency, but here the sufficiency is not self-generated — it rests on dependence on God rather than willpower.\n\nThe promise that follows stacks up negatives for emphasis, since Greek grammar lets multiple negatives intensify a denial rather than cancel it out — the line reads less like a flat future tense and more like \"I will absolutely not, not ever.\" \"Forsake\" is built from *leipo* (\"to leave\") with two prefixes, *kata* (\"down\") and *en* (\"in\") — the compound pictures abandoning someone helpless in the middle of a bad situation, not simply walking away from them.","translation":"Let marriage be held as precious among all, and let the marriage bed be kept undefiled; for God will judge sexually immoral persons and adulterers."},{"c1":13,"v1":7,"c2":13,"v2":7,"label":"Hebrews 13:7","text":"\"Remember\" is again a present imperative — an ongoing discipline of calling former leaders to mind, not a one-time memorial. \"Follow\" renders a verb meaning \"imitate,\" aimed specifically at their faith, while \"considering\" is a compound built on *theoreo* (\"to look at\") with the prefix *ana* (\"up, thoroughly\") — a careful, sustained inspection, not a glance in passing. \"The end of their conversation\" is better read as the outcome of their way of life: readers are told to watch how a life anchored in that faith actually finished, and to copy the parts of it that matched what was taught.","translation":"Keep remembering those leading you, who spoke to you the word of God; and, carefully observing the outcome of their way of life, keep imitating their faith."},{"c1":13,"v1":8,"c2":13,"v2":8,"label":"Hebrews 13:8","text":"No verb \"is\" appears in the Greek, so the natural reading pairs \"Jesus\" directly with \"Christ\" as a claim of identity: Jesus is the Messiah. \"Christ\" simply transliterates the Greek word for \"anointed,\" carrying over the sense of the Hebrew \"Messiah\" — not a title tacked onto a name but a translated claim about who this man is. Since the leaders just mentioned had staked their lives on exactly that identification, the writer is holding up their unwavering conviction as much as he is asserting Christ's own changelessness.","translation":"Jesus is the Messiah — yesterday and today the same, and forever."},{"c1":13,"v1":9,"c2":13,"v2":9,"label":"Hebrews 13:9","text":"\"Be not carried about\" is a negated present passive imperative — a command to stop something already in progress: readers were actively being swept along by these teachings as the letter reached them. \"Divers and strange\" pairs a word for \"many-colored, variegated\" with a word for \"foreign\" — the danger isn't one clean, identifiable error but a shifting patchwork of them, none of it native to the gospel. The heart is \"established\" by a verb built on the idea of firmness — grace makes it steady in a way food regulations never could, since those who relied on the latter got no lasting benefit from it.","translation":"Stop being carried away by teachings that are many-colored and foreign to the faith. For it is good for the heart to keep being made steady by grace, not by foods, which never profited those devoted to them."},{"c1":13,"v1":10,"c2":13,"v2":10,"label":"Hebrews 13:10","text":"\"Right\" translates *exousia* (exousia), a legal term for delegated authority — the standing to do something because it has been granted, not mere ability to do it. The claim is pointed: those still serving at the Levitical tabernacle have no legal claim on this altar's food, because the two systems simply don't overlap. \"Serve\" here is the technical verb for cultic ministry, covering priest and worshiper alike — anyone still operating inside the old system is shut out of the new one's table by definition, not as a penalty.","translation":"We have an altar from which those who serve the tabernacle have no right to eat."},{"c1":13,"v1":11,"c2":13,"v2":11,"label":"Hebrews 13:11","text":"The verbs describing the Day of Atonement ritual sit in the present tense, marking a fixed, repeated pattern rather than a single past event: the blood \"is brought\" in by the high priest, the carcasses \"are burned\" outside the camp. That habitual present matters for the argument — this was never a one-off but the standing rule of the old system, and the rule itself excluded any sacrificial meal for priest or people alike.","translation":"For the bodies of those animals whose blood is brought by the high priest into the sanctuary as a sin offering are burned up outside the camp."},{"c1":13,"v1":12,"c2":13,"v2":12,"label":"Hebrews 13:12","text":"\"Sanctify\" is *hagiazo* (hagiazo), \"to set apart for God,\" and here it stands as an aorist subjunctive inside a purpose clause — a single, decisive act aimed at a specific goal, not a gradual process. \"His own blood\" uses the possessive *idios* (idios), stressing that the blood was peculiarly his, personally his own, not merely available to him. \"Suffered\" is likewise aorist, a completed historical event: Jesus was crucified outside Jerusalem's gate, matching the disposal of the Day of Atonement carcasses outside the camp, so that his death would set apart a people cut loose from the old system's boundaries.\n\nThe verses that follow press the application: believers are to keep going out to him outside the camp, carrying his reproach, because \"here\" — on earth, inside the old order — there is no city that remains. The earthly city is described with a present participle meaning \"the one presently remaining,\" set against a future participle meaning \"the one about to come.\" The old order is temporary by grammar as well as by fact.","translation":"Therefore Jesus also, in order that he might set apart the people through his own blood, suffered outside the gate."},{"c1":13,"v1":17,"c2":13,"v2":17,"label":"Hebrews 13:17","text":"The verse issues two present imperatives, \"obey\" and \"submit\" — a habitual posture, not a single act of compliance. \"Submit\" is itself a compound meaning literally \"to retreat under,\" picturing a voluntary yielding of ground rather than a forced defeat. The leaders \"watch\" using a verb built from \"without sleep\" — the image is of someone keeping vigil through the night, the way a shepherd or a nurse stays alert over a case that cannot be left unattended. Their eventual accounting is described with *apodidomi* (apodidomi), a verb at home in commerce and bookkeeping, \"to pay back what is owed\" — cast here as a future participle: they are people who will one day have to render this account, and that expectation shapes how closely they watch now.\n\nThe writer wants that watch kept \"with joy, not with groaning\" — the groan he names is the particular sigh of someone shouldering a thankless, resisted task. And the closing clause turns on a rare compound, *alysiteles* (alysiteles), built on the negative of a word meaning \"profitable\" or \"paying its toll\" — resisting these leaders, the writer says, is a losing transaction, unprofitable to the very people doing the resisting.","translation":"Keep obeying your leaders and keep submitting to them, for they keep watch, sleepless, over your souls, as those who will have to render an account — so that they may do this with joy and not with groaning, for that would be no profit to you."},{"c1":13,"v1":22,"c2":13,"v2":22,"label":"Hebrews 13:22","text":"\"Suffer\" here means \"bear with\" — the verb is *anechomai* (anechomai), literally \"to hold oneself up under\" something. The writer is asking for patient endurance of his exhortation, not mere passive tolerance. \"I have written\" is *epistello* (epistello), the verb for sending formal instructions by letter, and it's the root behind the word \"epistle\" — it names the whole document as a dispatched message, not casual correspondence. Calling the letter \"few words\" is itself a small apology, asking readers not to hold its brevity against the weight of what it says.","translation":"And I urge you, brothers, bear with this word of exhortation, for indeed I have written to you in few words."},{"c1":13,"v1":23,"c2":13,"v2":23,"label":"Hebrews 13:23","text":"There's little grammar to mine in these closing lines, but one detail stands out: \"set at liberty\" is a perfect passive participle of *apolyo* (apolyo, \"to release\") — the perfect tense marks a completed action whose result still holds, so the writer isn't only reporting that Timothy was once freed but that he remains free as this is written. Beyond that, this is plain personal news — travel plans and a hoped-for reunion, nothing coded in the tenses.","translation":"Know that our brother Timothy has been set free and remains so; if he comes soon, I will see you along with him."}]};